const Map<String, Map<String, String>> embeddedSkillFiles = {
  "mobile-accessibility": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-accessibility\ndescription: \"Audits and fixes accessibility in Android, iOS and Flutter apps against WCAG 2.2 level AA and EN 301 549, and checks whether the European Accessibility Act applies. Covers Compose semantics, TalkBack, SwiftUI modifiers, VoiceOver, Dynamic Type, Flutter Semantics, touch targets, contrast, font scaling and automated checks. Engineering guidance, not legal advice. Use for accessibility reviews, EAA readiness, screen reader bugs or a11y tests.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile accessibility\n\nMake an Android, iOS or Flutter app usable with a screen reader, large text, switch or keyboard access and\nreduced motion, and show it: decide whether the European Accessibility Act (EAA) applies, test the critical\nuser journeys automatically and by hand, and report each failure against a WCAG 2.2 success criterion with\nevidence and a code fix.\n\n**This is engineering guidance, not legal advice.** The agent reports what the app does and which success\ncriterion or clause it relates to. Whether the EAA applies, whether an exemption or the disproportionate\nburden clause can be used, and what goes into the accessibility statement are decisions for the team's\ncompliance or legal owner. Where a point cannot be verified, the agent says so instead of guessing.\n\n## When to use\n\n- \"Is our app in scope of the European Accessibility Act?\" or \"Are we EAA ready?\"\n- Accessibility review of a screen, a pull request or a whole app against WCAG 2.2 AA or EN 301 549.\n- TalkBack or VoiceOver bug reports: unlabeled buttons, wrong reading order, focus lost after a dialog.\n- Text cut off at large font sizes, small touch targets, low contrast, animations that ignore Reduce Motion.\n- Adding automated accessibility checks to Compose, Espresso, XCUITest or Flutter widget tests.\n- Writing the accessibility information a service provider has to publish (EAA Annex V).\n\nNot for: general code review (use `mobile-code-review`, which calls out basic accessibility), CRA, RED or GDPR\n(use `mobile-eu-compliance`), or store listing and review rules (use `mobile-release`).\n\n## Verified key facts\n\nChecked against the Official Journal text of Directive (EU) 2019/882\n(https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/882/oj/eng), EN 301 549 V4.1.1 from the ETSI deliverables listing and\nthe WCAG 2.2 Recommendation. Re-check before quoting them in customer-facing documents.\n\n| Topic | Fact |\n|---|---|\n| EAA application date | National measures apply from 28 June 2025 (Art. 31(2)); covers products placed on the market and services provided to consumers after that date (Art. 2(1) and (2)) |\n| Services in scope (Art. 2(2)) | Electronic communications services; services providing access to audiovisual media services; listed elements of air, bus, rail and waterborne passenger transport (websites, mobile apps, e-tickets, travel information); consumer banking services; e-books and dedicated software; e-commerce services |\n| E-commerce (Art. 3(30), recital 43) | Services at a distance, through websites and mobile device-based services, at the individual request of a consumer, with a view to concluding a consumer contract; recital 43 says this applies to the online sale of any product or service |\n| Mobile app requirement (Annex I, Section III(c)) | Make \"mobile device-based services, including mobile applications, accessible in a consistent and adequate way by making them perceivable, operable, understandable and robust\" |\n| Microenterprise exemption (Art. 4(5), Art. 3(23)) | Microenterprises providing services are exempt from the service requirements. Microenterprise: fewer than 10 persons and annual turnover or balance sheet total not exceeding EUR 2 million. Product manufacturers are not exempt (Art. 14(4) only lightens documentation) |\n| Content not covered (Art. 2(4)) | Pre-recorded time-based media and office files published before 28 June 2025, some online maps, third-party content not funded, developed or controlled by the operator, archives not updated after 28 June 2025 |\n| Transitional measures (Art. 32(1)) | Until 28 June 2030 services may keep using products lawfully used before that date; service contracts agreed before 28 June 2025 may continue until they expire, at most five years |\n| Information duty (Art. 13(2), Annex V) | Service providers publish how the service meets the requirements, in the general terms and conditions or an equivalent document, in accessible form |\n| Exceptions (Art. 14) | Fundamental alteration or disproportionate burden only with a documented assessment, kept for five years |\n| EN 301 549 | V4.1.1 (2026-09) is the harmonised standard prepared for the EAA and aligned with WCAG 2.2; it gives presumption of conformity only once cited in the Official Journal under the EAA (check the citation status). Clause 11 (non-web software) applies to mobile apps, including web views inside them |\n| WCAG 2.2 | W3C Recommendation (current version dated 12 December 2024). New at AA: 2.4.11, 2.5.7, 2.5.8, 3.3.8; new at A: 3.2.6, 3.3.7; 4.1.1 Parsing removed |\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Scope.** Decide whether the EAA applies before rating anything as a legal gap.\n   - Is the app part of a service in Art. 2(2), provided to consumers in the EU? An IoT companion app,\n     a game or a B2B tool is not listed as such, but an in-app shop or a checkout can make it e-commerce.\n   - Microenterprise providing services (Art. 4(5))? Record the facts, not a conclusion. Public sector app:\n     Directive (EU) 2016/2102 applies instead or as well.\n   - Use the decision table in [EAA, EN 301 549 and WCAG 2.2](references/eaa-wcag.md). Open scope points go\n     to the compliance owner. WCAG 2.2 AA is still the engineering target when the EAA does not apply.\n2. **Critical journeys.** List the screens a user must be able to finish: onboarding, sign-in, the core\n   task, purchase or payment, settings, help and error states. Audit these first.\n3. **Load the platform reference** for the code under review:\n   [Android](references/android.md), [iOS](references/ios.md), [Flutter](references/flutter.md).\n4. **Static review.** Walk the rules below over each screen's code. Note file:line for every candidate.\n5. **Automated checks.** Run what the project has, add what it lacks:\n   - Android: Compose `enableAccessibilityChecks()`, Espresso `AccessibilityChecks.enable()`, Accessibility\n     Scanner on a device, the Play pre-launch report.\n   - iOS: `XCUIApplication().performAccessibilityAudit()` in a UI test (iOS 17 or later), Accessibility Inspector.\n   - Flutter: `meetsGuideline(...)` widget tests with the four built-in guidelines.\n   - Automated checks catch only part of the issues. A clean run is not a pass.\n6. **Manual test.** Run the [manual screen reader test script](references/manual-test-script.md) with\n   TalkBack and VoiceOver, at 200 percent text, with Reduce Motion and with a hardware keyboard or switch.\n7. **Map and rate.** Each finding gets one WCAG 2.2 success criterion (number and name), a severity (see\n   Output format), the screen, platform, evidence and a fix. For EN 301 549, mobile apps use clause 11: for\n   most criteria the clause number is 11 followed by the WCAG number (1.4.3 is 11.1.4.3).\n8. **Report** in the output format below. Fix code only when asked; add a regression test for every fix.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Every control and meaningful image has a name; decorative content is hidden (1.1.1, 4.1.2, 2.5.3)\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: icon-only button, TalkBack announces only \"Button\"\nIconButton(onClick = onDelete) { Icon(Icons.Default.Delete, contentDescription = null) }\n\n// GOOD: localized name on the actionable icon; decorative image explicitly null\nIconButton(onClick = onDelete) {\n    Icon(Icons.Default.Delete, contentDescription = stringResource(R.string.delete_message))\n}\nImage(painterResource(R.drawable.header_wave), contentDescription = null)\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD: no label, VoiceOver falls back to whatever it can derive from the image\nButton(action: delete) { Image(systemName: \"trash\") }\n\n// GOOD: the title is the accessibility label, the icon is what sighted users see\nButton(action: delete) { Label(\"Delete message\", systemImage: \"trash\") }\n    .labelStyle(.iconOnly)\nImage(decorative: \"header-wave\")\n```\n\n```dart\n// BAD\nIconButton(icon: const Icon(Icons.delete), onPressed: onDelete);\n\n// GOOD: tooltip becomes the semantics label; decorative image excluded\nIconButton(icon: const Icon(Icons.delete), tooltip: l10n.deleteMessage, onPressed: onDelete);\nImage.asset('assets/header_wave.png', excludeFromSemantics: true);\n```\n\n- The visible text must be part of the accessible name (2.5.3 Label in Name), so voice control works.\n- Names describe the action or content, not the widget type: \"Delete message\", not \"Delete button\".\n\n### 2. Roles and states are exposed, preferably by native controls (4.1.2)\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: two focus stops, no role on the row, state announced separately\nRow(Modifier.clickable { onToggle() }) {\n    Text(\"Wi-Fi\")\n    Switch(checked = on, onCheckedChange = { onToggle() })\n}\n\n// GOOD: one focus stop with role Switch and its on/off state\nRow(Modifier.toggleable(value = on, role = Role.Switch, onValueChange = { onToggle() }).padding(16.dp)) {\n    Text(\"Wi-Fi\", Modifier.weight(1f))\n    Switch(checked = on, onCheckedChange = null)\n}\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD: looks like a toggle, VoiceOver hears static text\nHStack { Text(\"Wi-Fi\"); Spacer(); if on { Image(systemName: \"checkmark\") } }\n    .onTapGesture { on.toggle() }\n\n// GOOD: native control carries role and value\nToggle(\"Wi-Fi\", isOn: \$on)\n```\n\n```dart\n// BAD\nGestureDetector(onTap: toggle, child: Row(children: [const Text('Wi-Fi'), if (on) const Icon(Icons.check)]));\n\n// GOOD\nSwitchListTile(title: const Text('Wi-Fi'), value: on, onChanged: (_) => toggle());\n```\n\n### 3. Related content is one focus stop and the reading order follows the layout (1.3.1, 1.3.2, 2.4.3)\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: avatar, name, time and preview are four swipes each\nRow { Avatar(user); Column { Text(name); Text(time); Text(preview) } }\n\n// GOOD: one node; clickable rows merge automatically\nRow(Modifier.semantics(mergeDescendants = true) {}) { Avatar(user); Column { Text(name); Text(time); Text(preview) } }\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD\nHStack { Avatar(user); VStack { Text(name); Text(time); Text(preview) } }\n    .accessibilityElement(children: .combine)\n```\n\n```dart\n// GOOD\nMergeSemantics(child: ListTile(leading: Avatar(user), title: Text(name), subtitle: Text(preview)));\n```\n\n- Fix reading order with the layout first. Override only when needed: `isTraversalGroup` and `traversalIndex`\n  (Compose), `accessibilitySortPriority` (SwiftUI), `OrdinalSortKey` (Flutter).\n\n### 4. Touch targets are at least 48dp on Android and 44pt on iOS (2.5.8)\n\nWCAG 2.5.8 sets a 24 by 24 CSS pixel floor at AA. Material guidance (48dp) and the Apple HIG default\ncontrol size (44x44 pt) are stricter; use them as the engineering target.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: 24dp clickable icon with neighbours close by\nIcon(Icons.Default.Close, stringResource(R.string.close), Modifier.size(24.dp).clickable(onClick = onClose))\n\n// GOOD: IconButton reserves 48dp; custom clickables add Modifier.minimumInteractiveComponentSize()\nIconButton(onClick = onClose) { Icon(Icons.Default.Close, stringResource(R.string.close)) }\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD: visual size stays small, hit area grows\nButton(action: close) { Image(systemName: \"xmark\") }\n    .frame(minWidth: 44, minHeight: 44)\n    .contentShape(Rectangle())\n    .accessibilityLabel(\"Close\")\n```\n\n```dart\n// BAD: shrinks Material tap targets below 48\nIconButton(style: IconButton.styleFrom(tapTargetSize: MaterialTapTargetSize.shrinkWrap), ...);\n\n// GOOD: keep the default padded size, or enforce it for custom widgets\nConstrainedBox(constraints: const BoxConstraints(minWidth: 48, minHeight: 48), child: child);\n```\n\n### 5. Text follows the system size up to 200 percent without loss of content (1.4.4, 1.4.10, 1.4.12)\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: fixed height cuts text at large font scale; dividing by fontScale cancels the user's setting\nBox(Modifier.height(40.dp)) { Text(label, maxLines = 1, fontSize = (14 / LocalDensity.current.fontScale).sp) }\n\n// GOOD: sp from the theme, minimum height instead of fixed height, wraps\nBox(Modifier.heightIn(min = 40.dp)) { Text(label, style = MaterialTheme.typography.bodyLarge) }\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD\nText(label).font(.system(size: 15)).frame(height: 40)\n\n// GOOD: text style scales with Dynamic Type; switch layout at accessibility sizes\n@Environment(\\.dynamicTypeSize) private var typeSize\n@ScaledMetric(relativeTo: .body) private var iconSize: CGFloat = 24\nvar body: some View {\n    let layout = typeSize.isAccessibilitySize ? AnyLayout(VStackLayout()) : AnyLayout(HStackLayout())\n    layout { Image(systemName: \"bolt\").frame(width: iconSize, height: iconSize); Text(label).font(.body) }\n}\n```\n\n```dart\n// BAD: switches off text scaling for the whole app\nMediaQuery(data: MediaQuery.of(context).copyWith(textScaler: TextScaler.noScaling), child: app);\n\n// GOOD: let text scale; clamp only a widget that truly cannot grow, and never below 2.0\nMediaQuery.withClampedTextScaling(maxScaleFactor: 2.0, child: const BottomBarLabels());\n```\n\n### 6. Contrast is sufficient and colour is never the only signal (1.4.3, 1.4.11, 1.4.1)\n\n- Text: at least 4.5:1, large text 3:1. Icons, focus indicators and control borders: 3:1 against adjacent colours.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: #9E9E9E on white is about 2.7:1; the error is shown only as a red border\nText(hint, color = Color(0xFF9E9E9E))\nOutlinedTextField(value, onValueChange, isError = invalid)\n\n// GOOD: #616161 on white is about 6.2:1; the error is also text, read by TalkBack\nText(hint, color = Color(0xFF616161))\nOutlinedTextField(value, onValueChange, isError = invalid,\n    supportingText = { if (invalid) Text(errorText) },\n    modifier = Modifier.semantics { if (invalid) error(errorText) })\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD: semantic colours adapt to dark mode and Increase Contrast\nText(hint).foregroundStyle(.secondary)\nif invalid { Label(errorText, systemImage: \"exclamationmark.triangle\").foregroundStyle(.red) }\n```\n\n```dart\n// GOOD: errorText is shown and announced, not only the red outline\nTextField(decoration: InputDecoration(labelText: l10n.email, errorText: invalid ? l10n.emailInvalid : null));\n```\n\n### 7. Screens have titles and sections have headings (2.4.6, 1.3.1; EN 301 549 11.2.4.2)\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD\nText(\"Payment method\", style = MaterialTheme.typography.titleMedium)\n\n// GOOD\nText(\"Payment method\", style = MaterialTheme.typography.titleMedium, modifier = Modifier.semantics { heading() })\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD\nText(\"Payment method\").font(.headline).accessibilityAddTraits(.isHeader)\n```\n\n```dart\n// GOOD\nSemantics(header: true, child: Text(l10n.paymentMethod, style: Theme.of(context).textTheme.titleMedium));\n```\n\n### 8. Status changes are announced without moving focus (4.1.3, 3.3.1)\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: \"Saved\" appears visually only\nif (saved) Text(stringResource(R.string.saved))\n\n// GOOD\nText(statusText, Modifier.semantics { liveRegion = LiveRegionMode.Polite })\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD (iOS 17 and later; use UIAccessibility.post(notification: .announcement, argument:) before that)\nAccessibilityNotification.Announcement(\"Saved\").post()\n```\n\n```dart\n// GOOD: live region on the status widget; Android discourages announcement events\nSemantics(liveRegion: true, child: Text(statusText));\n```\n\n- Use assertive or high-priority announcements only for time-critical information. No live region on timers.\n\n### 9. Gestures have a single-tap alternative exposed to assistive technology (2.5.1, 2.5.7)\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: swipe-to-delete is the only way to delete\nSwipeToDismissBox(state, backgroundContent = { DeleteBackground() }) { MessageRow(m) }\n\n// GOOD: same action as a custom accessibility action (and a visible menu item)\nMessageRow(m, Modifier.semantics {\n    customActions = listOf(CustomAccessibilityAction(deleteLabel) { onDelete(m); true })\n})\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD\nMessageRow(message).accessibilityAction(named: \"Delete\") { delete(message) }\n```\n\n```dart\n// GOOD\nSemantics(\n  customSemanticsActions: {CustomSemanticsAction(label: l10n.delete): () => onDelete(m)},\n  child: MessageRow(m),\n);\n```\n\n- Drag to reorder, sliders and maps need buttons or an equivalent control that works without dragging.\n\n### 10. Motion respects Reduce Motion; nothing flashes (2.2.2, 2.3.1; EAA Annex I Section VII(i))\n\n```kotlin\n// GOOD: skip decorative animation when the user removed animations (API 26+)\nval animate = ValueAnimator.areAnimatorsEnabled()\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD\n@Environment(\\.accessibilityReduceMotion) private var reduceMotion\nwithAnimation(reduceMotion ? nil : .default) { expanded.toggle() }\n```\n\n```dart\n// GOOD\nfinal duration = MediaQuery.disableAnimationsOf(context) ? Duration.zero : const Duration(milliseconds: 300);\n```\n\n- Auto-playing carousels and looping animations longer than five seconds need pause, stop or hide (2.2.2).\n- Nothing flashes more than three times per second (2.3.1).\n\n### 11. Sign-in and forms work with password managers, autofill and labels (3.3.8, 3.3.2, 1.3.5, 3.3.7)\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: placeholder as the only label, paste blocked, no autofill type\nTextField(value, onValueChange, placeholder = { Text(\"Password\") })\n\n// GOOD: persistent label, autofill hint (Compose 1.8+), paste allowed\nOutlinedTextField(value, onValueChange, label = { Text(stringResource(R.string.password)) },\n    visualTransformation = PasswordVisualTransformation(),\n    modifier = Modifier.semantics { contentType = ContentType.Password })\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD\nSecureField(\"Password\", text: \$password).textContentType(.password)\n```\n\n```dart\n// GOOD\nAutofillGroup(child: TextField(\n  obscureText: true,\n  autofillHints: const [AutofillHints.password],\n  decoration: InputDecoration(labelText: l10n.password),\n));\n```\n\n- Offer an alternative to memory or puzzle tests: passkeys, biometrics, magic links, copy and paste of codes.\n- Do not ask for data already entered in the same flow (3.3.7 Redundant Entry).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Declaring the app \"EAA compliant\" or \"WCAG conformant\". The report states what was tested and found.\n- Assuming every app is in EAA scope, or that none is. Scope follows Art. 2 and the provider's role.\n- Treating a clean Accessibility Scanner, audit or `meetsGuideline` run as a pass. Manual testing is required.\n- Labels that repeat the role (\"Button, delete button\") or describe the icon (\"trash can\") instead of the action.\n- Hiding content from screen readers to \"reduce noise\" when it carries information.\n- Disabling or clamping font scaling app-wide; fixed heights on text containers; `maxLines = 1` on labels.\n- Custom gesture-only interactions with no accessibility action.\n- Locking portrait for convenience (1.3.4). Android 16 ignores orientation locks on large screens anyway.\n- Overlay \"accessibility widgets\" or SDKs that claim to fix an app automatically.\n- Inventing article numbers, success criterion numbers or deadlines. If a citation cannot be confirmed, say so.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] EAA scope recorded: service category, consumer-facing, provider role, microenterprise facts, open questions\n- [ ] Critical journeys listed and each one tested end to end with TalkBack and VoiceOver\n- [ ] Every actionable element has a localized, action-based name; decorative images hidden\n- [ ] Custom controls expose role and state; native controls preferred\n- [ ] Related content merged; reading and focus order match the visual order; focus moves into dialogs and back\n- [ ] Touch targets at least 48dp / 44pt, or at minimum 24 by 24 with spacing\n- [ ] Text scales to 200 percent without clipping or overlap; no app-wide scaling override\n- [ ] Contrast 4.5:1 for text, 3:1 for large text, icons and control borders; no colour-only meaning\n- [ ] Headings marked; each screen has a title\n- [ ] Status messages and errors announced; errors also shown as text\n- [ ] Gestures and dragging have alternatives exposed as accessibility actions\n- [ ] Reduce Motion respected; no flashing; auto-playing motion can be paused\n- [ ] Sign-in supports password managers, autofill and paste; fields have persistent labels\n- [ ] Automated checks run in CI: Compose or Espresso checks, XCUITest audit, Flutter guideline tests\n- [ ] Accessibility information for Annex V drafted if the EAA applies\n- [ ] Report says \"engineering guidance, not legal advice\" and lists unverified points\n\n## Output format\n\n~~~markdown\n## Accessibility report: <app / version / build>\n\nEngineering assessment, not legal advice. Scope and conformity decisions belong to <compliance owner>.\n\nTarget: WCAG 2.2 level AA (EN 301 549 V4.1.1 clause 11 for mobile). Devices: <model, OS and screen reader versions>. Journeys tested: <sign-in, checkout, settings>.\nEAA: <in scope as e-commerce service | not in scope | open question>. Microenterprise: <facts or unknown>.\n\n| ID | WCAG 2.2 SC | Severity | Screen | Platform | Evidence | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A11Y-01 | 1.1.1 Non-text Content, 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value | Blocker | Checkout | Android | Pay button is an Icon with `contentDescription = null` (`CheckoutScreen.kt:88`); TalkBack: \"Button\" | Add localized contentDescription (rule 1) |\n| A11Y-02 | 1.4.4 Resize Text | Major | Settings | iOS | At AX3 the row title is truncated; `.frame(height: 44)` in `SettingsRow.swift:21` | Replace with `minHeight`, use text styles (rule 5) |\n| A11Y-03 | 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum) | Minor | Feed | Flutter | `meetsGuideline(androidTapTargetGuideline)` fails: like button 32x32 (`feed_item.dart:54`) | Remove `shrinkWrap`, 48x48 min (rule 4) |\n\n### Automated checks\n- <tool, version, result, link to CI run>\n\n### Open questions for the compliance owner\n- <for example: does the in-app subscription purchase make the app an e-commerce service?>\n\n### Not tested\n- <screens, platforms, assistive technologies, third-party content not under the team's control>\n~~~\n\nSeverity: Blocker -- a user of assistive technology cannot complete a critical journey (sign-in, payment,\ncore task). Major -- the journey is possible but a criterion fails on a critical screen or app-wide.\nMinor -- a criterion fails on a secondary screen, or the issue slows users down without blocking them.\n\nEvery finding has exactly one primary success criterion (add a second only when both fail for the same cause),\nevidence a reviewer can reproduce, and a fix. Order by severity.\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-code-review` -- general review that flags basic accessibility issues in a diff\n- `mobile-testing` -- test setup for the Compose, XCUITest and Flutter accessibility checks\n- `mobile-eu-compliance` -- CRA, RED and GDPR assessment for the same product\n- `mobile-release` -- store listing, release notes and the published accessibility information\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- running accessibility tests on every pull request\n- `mobile-kotlin`, `mobile-swift`, `mobile-flutter` -- language and UI framework rules the fixes follow\n",
    "references/android.md":
        "# Android accessibility: Compose, Views, TalkBack and tests\n\nLoad this when reviewing or fixing accessibility in an Android app. APIs checked against\ndeveloper.android.com (Compose semantics, accessibility defaults and testing pages, the material3 and\nui-test references, Android 14 and 16 platform notes).\n\n## Compose semantics cookbook\n\n| Need | Compose API | WCAG 2.2 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Name for an icon or image | `contentDescription` parameter, or `Modifier.semantics { contentDescription = ... }` | 1.1.1, 4.1.2 |\n| Decorative image | `contentDescription = null` | 1.1.1 |\n| Hide a node that carries no information | `Modifier.semantics { hideFromAccessibility() }` (`invisibleToUser()` is deprecated) | 1.3.1 |\n| One focus stop for a group | `Modifier.semantics(mergeDescendants = true) {}`; `clickable` and `toggleable` merge by default | 1.3.1 |\n| Replace children's semantics | `Modifier.clearAndSetSemantics { contentDescription = ... }` | 1.3.1 |\n| Role and state | `toggleable(value, role = Role.Switch, ...)`, `selectable(..., role = Role.RadioButton)`, `triStateToggleable` | 4.1.2 |\n| Custom state text | `Modifier.semantics { stateDescription = ... }` | 4.1.2 |\n| Click hint | `clickable(onClickLabel = ...)`, `combinedClickable(onLongClickLabel = ...)` | 4.1.2 |\n| Heading | `Modifier.semantics { heading() }` | 1.3.1, 2.4.6 |\n| Reading order | `Modifier.semantics { isTraversalGroup = true }` and `traversalIndex = ...` | 1.3.2, 2.4.3 |\n| Status message | `Modifier.semantics { liveRegion = LiveRegionMode.Polite }` | 4.1.3 |\n| Error text | `Modifier.semantics { error(message) }`, plus visible `supportingText` | 3.3.1 |\n| Gesture alternative | `Modifier.semantics { customActions = listOf(CustomAccessibilityAction(label) { ...; true }) }` | 2.5.1, 2.5.7 |\n| Autofill type | `Modifier.semantics { contentType = ContentType.Password }` (`androidx.compose.ui.autofill`, Compose 1.8+) | 1.3.5, 3.3.8 |\n| Pane or dialog title | `Modifier.semantics { paneTitle = ... }` | 2.4.2 |\n| Touch target | `Modifier.minimumInteractiveComponentSize()` (material3, reserves at least 48.dp) or `sizeIn(minWidth = 48.dp, minHeight = 48.dp)` | 2.5.8 |\n\n### List row with secondary actions\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: row has a clickable body, a bookmark icon and a share icon: three focus stops,\n// and the icons have no names\nRow(Modifier.clickable(onClick = open)) {\n    Text(article.title)\n    Icon(Icons.Default.BookmarkBorder, null, Modifier.clickable(onClick = bookmark))\n    Icon(Icons.Default.Share, null, Modifier.clickable(onClick = share))\n}\n\n// GOOD: one focus stop that reads the title, with the secondary actions as custom actions;\n// the visible icons stay tappable for touch users\nRow(\n    Modifier\n        .clickable(onClickLabel = stringResource(R.string.open_article), onClick = open)\n        .semantics {\n            customActions = listOf(\n                CustomAccessibilityAction(bookmarkLabel) { bookmark(); true },\n                CustomAccessibilityAction(shareLabel) { share(); true },\n            )\n        }\n) {\n    Text(article.title, Modifier.weight(1f))\n    IconButton(onClick = bookmark, modifier = Modifier.clearAndSetSemantics {}) {\n        Icon(Icons.Default.BookmarkBorder, contentDescription = null)\n    }\n    IconButton(onClick = share, modifier = Modifier.clearAndSetSemantics {}) {\n        Icon(Icons.Default.Share, contentDescription = null)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Selection controls\n\n```kotlin\n// GOOD: the row is the control; the Checkbox has no own click handler so it is not a second focus stop\nRow(\n    Modifier\n        .toggleable(value = checked, role = Role.Checkbox, onValueChange = onCheckedChange)\n        .padding(16.dp)\n        .fillMaxWidth()\n) {\n    Text(label, Modifier.weight(1f))\n    Checkbox(checked = checked, onCheckedChange = null)\n}\n```\n\nMaterial components add the 48dp minimum only when they are interactive: `Checkbox(onCheckedChange = null)`\nhas no padding, which is correct when the parent row handles the click.\n\n## Text scaling\n\n- Always `sp` for text (through `MaterialTheme.typography`). Never `dp` for text size, never divide by\n  `LocalDensity.current.fontScale`.\n- Android 14 scales fonts up to 200 percent with a nonlinear curve. Do not compute sizes from\n  `Configuration.fontScale` or `DisplayMetrics.scaledDensity`; for Views use `TypedValue.applyDimension()`\n  and `TypedValue.deriveDimension()`.\n- Do not use `sp` for padding or fixed heights: with nonlinear scaling, 4sp + 20sp might not equal 24sp.\n- Containers with text use `heightIn(min = ...)`, not `height(...)`; avoid `maxLines = 1` on labels and buttons.\n- Preview large text with `@PreviewFontScale` (a multipreview of font scales) or `@Preview(fontScale = 2f)`.\n- Views: `android:textSize` and `android:lineHeight` both in `sp`.\n\n## Views (legacy screens)\n\n```xml\n<!-- BAD: no label, 32dp target -->\n<ImageButton android:layout_width=\"32dp\" android:layout_height=\"32dp\" android:src=\"@drawable/ic_delete\" />\n\n<!-- GOOD -->\n<ImageButton\n    android:layout_width=\"wrap_content\"\n    android:layout_height=\"wrap_content\"\n    android:minWidth=\"48dp\"\n    android:minHeight=\"48dp\"\n    android:contentDescription=\"@string/delete_message\"\n    android:src=\"@drawable/ic_delete\" />\n```\n\n- Decorative: `android:importantForAccessibility=\"no\"`. Headings: `android:accessibilityHeading=\"true\"`\n  or `ViewCompat.setAccessibilityHeading(view, true)`. Labels for inputs: `android:labelFor`, or\n  `TextInputLayout` with a hint.\n- Live status: `android:accessibilityLiveRegion=\"polite\"` or `ViewCompat.setAccessibilityLiveRegion(...)`.\n\n## Motion\n\n```kotlin\n// GOOD: respects \"Remove animations\" (animator duration scale 0); API 26+\nval animationsOn = ValueAnimator.areAnimatorsEnabled()\nval spec: AnimationSpec<Float> = if (animationsOn) tween(300) else snap()\n```\n\n## Orientation and large screens\n\n- Android 16 (targetSdk 36): orientation, resizability and aspect-ratio restrictions are ignored on displays\n  with smallest width 600dp or more (games excepted). The opt-out property\n  `android.window.PROPERTY_COMPAT_ALLOW_RESTRICTED_RESIZABILITY` is temporary and will not apply when\n  targeting API 37. Build layouts that work in both orientations (WCAG 1.3.4).\n\n## Automated checks\n\n### Compose UI tests (Compose 1.8+)\n\n```kotlin\n// build.gradle.kts: same version as the other Compose ui-test artifacts, declared in libs.versions.toml\nandroidTestImplementation(\"androidx.compose.ui:ui-test-junit4-accessibility\")\n```\n\n```kotlin\n@get:Rule val composeTestRule = createAndroidComposeRule<ComponentActivity>()\n\n@Test fun checkoutScreen_passesAccessibilityChecks() {\n    composeTestRule.setContent { CheckoutScreen(state = previewState) }\n    composeTestRule.enableAccessibilityChecks()        // requires API 34+ on the test device\n    composeTestRule.onRoot().tryPerformAccessibilityChecks()\n}\n```\n\n- After `enableAccessibilityChecks()`, every action such as `performClick()` also runs the checks.\n- Pass an `AccessibilityValidator` to tune the checks, for example\n  `AccessibilityValidator().setThrowExceptionFor(AccessibilityCheckResult.AccessibilityCheckResultType.WARNING)`.\n- The checks cover missing labels, contrast, touch target size and traversal order.\n\n### Semantics assertions (any API level)\n\n```kotlin\ncomposeTestRule.onNodeWithContentDescription(\"Delete message\").assertHasClickAction()\ncomposeTestRule.onNode(hasText(\"Payment method\") and isHeading()).assertExists()\ncomposeTestRule.onNode(SemanticsMatcher.expectValue(SemanticsProperties.Role, Role.Switch))\n    .performClick().assertIsOn()\ncomposeTestRule.onNodeWithTag(\"closeButton\").assertHeightIsAtLeast(48.dp).assertWidthIsAtLeast(48.dp)\n```\n\n### Espresso (Views)\n\n```kotlin\n// androidTestImplementation(\"androidx.test.espresso:espresso-accessibility:<version>\")\n@Before fun enableChecks() { AccessibilityChecks.enable() }   // runs on every ViewAction\n```\n\n### Outside tests\n\n- Accessibility Scanner (Google Play): Snapshot for one screen, Record for a flow. Checks content labels,\n  touch target size, clickable items, text and image contrast.\n- Google Play pre-launch report: Accessibility tab with touch target, contrast, labeling and implementation results.\n- Android Studio Compose UI Check mode on a preview audits accessibility issues such as low contrast.\n\n## TalkBack quick reference\n\n- Enable: Settings > Accessibility > TalkBack, or volume-key shortcut. Developer setting \"Log output level:\n  VERBOSE\" helps debugging.\n- Swipe right or left: next or previous element. Double tap: activate. Two-finger swipe: scroll.\n- The TalkBack menu lists custom actions (\"Actions\") and reading controls. Its gesture differs between\n  TalkBack versions and devices; check it on the test device.\n- Switch Access: check that every action is reachable and each item is highlighted once.\n\nThe full manual procedure is in the manual test script reference.\n",
    "references/eaa-wcag.md":
        "# EAA, EN 301 549 and WCAG 2.2 for mobile apps\n\nLoad this when deciding whether the European Accessibility Act applies to an app, when mapping findings to\nEN 301 549 or WCAG 2.2, or when drafting the accessibility information a service provider has to publish.\nQuotes are from the Official Journal text of Directive (EU) 2019/882\n(https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/882/oj/eng), EN 301 549 V4.1.1 (2026-09) and the WCAG 2.2\nRecommendation (https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/). Engineering guidance, not legal advice.\n\n## Dates\n\n| Date | What happens | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| 28 June 2022 | Member States adopt and publish national transposition measures | Art. 31(1) |\n| 28 June 2025 | National measures apply; products placed on the market and services provided to consumers after this date are covered | Art. 31(2), Art. 2(1) and (2) |\n| 28 June 2027 | Latest date for the 112 emergency-number answering obligations (Art. 4(8)) if a Member State uses the derogation | Art. 31(3) |\n| 28 June 2030 | End of the transitional period for services using products lawfully used before 28 June 2025; first Commission report | Art. 32(1), Art. 33(1) |\n\nThe EAA is a directive: obligations reach companies through each Member State's national law, which can add\ndetails (enforcement body, penalties under Art. 30). Check the national law of each market the app serves.\n\n## Scope: products\n\nArt. 2(1) covers products placed on the market after 28 June 2025: consumer general purpose computer hardware\nand their operating systems, payment terminals and certain self-service terminals (ATMs, ticketing and\ncheck-in machines, interactive information terminals), consumer terminal equipment for electronic\ncommunications or for audiovisual media services, and e-readers. A mobile app on its own is not one of these\nproducts. A phone maker's preinstalled operating system is.\n\n## Scope: services (where most apps fall)\n\nArt. 2(2) covers these services provided to consumers after 28 June 2025:\n\n| Art. 2(2) | Service | Typical mobile apps |\n|---|---|---|\n| (a) | Electronic communications services, except transmission services used for machine-to-machine services | Operator apps, calling and messaging services that are electronic communications services under Directive (EU) 2018/1972 |\n| (b) | Services providing access to audiovisual media services | Streaming and TV apps, including electronic programme guides |\n| (c) | Air, bus, rail and waterborne passenger transport: websites, mobile device-based services including mobile applications, e-tickets and e-ticketing, transport information, interactive self-service terminals | Airline, rail and ferry apps. Urban, suburban and regional transport: only the self-service terminals element (point (v)) applies |\n| (d) | Consumer banking services (Art. 3(28): credit agreements, certain investment services, payment services, payment-account services, e-money) | Banking, payment and e-money apps |\n| (e) | E-books and dedicated software (Art. 3(41)) | E-book reader apps and stores |\n| (f) | E-commerce services (Art. 3(30)) | Shopping apps, marketplaces, apps with an in-app shop or checkout that concludes a consumer contract |\n\nKey points from the text:\n\n- \"Consumer\" (Art. 3(22)) is a natural person acting outside their trade, business, craft or profession. A\n  service offered only to businesses is not a consumer service.\n- \"E-commerce services\" (Art. 3(30)): \"services provided at a distance, through websites and mobile\n  device-based services by electronic means and at the individual request of a consumer with a view to\n  concluding a consumer contract\". Recital 43: the e-commerce obligations \"should apply to the online sale of\n  any product or service\".\n- Recital 47: the four principles (perceivable, operable, understandable, robust) are those used in\n  Directive (EU) 2016/2102. Public sector apps fall under that directive.\n\n### Decision table\n\n| Situation | Typical position | Record as an open question |\n|---|---|---|\n| Shopping app or marketplace selling to consumers in the EU | E-commerce service, in scope | Which entity is the service provider |\n| Mobile banking, payment or e-money app for consumers | Consumer banking service, in scope | - |\n| Airline, long-distance rail or ferry app | Passenger transport element, in scope | Whether the operator is a regional or urban service (then only terminals) |\n| City public transport app | Urban, suburban and regional transport: only Art. 2(2)(c)(v) terminals apply | Online ticket sales: recital 35 relies on the e-commerce obligations for them |\n| Streaming or TV app | Access to audiovisual media services, in scope | - |\n| IoT companion app that only controls the user's device | Not a listed service on its face | Does it sell subscriptions, spare parts or services in-app (e-commerce)? |\n| Game, fitness or productivity app with in-app purchases | Not listed as such; the purchase flow may be e-commerce | Whether in-app purchases through the store make the publisher an e-commerce service provider |\n| Internal or B2B app (employees, fleet, field service) | Not a consumer service | - |\n| App of a public sector body | Directive (EU) 2016/2102 | Whether a listed service in the app also falls under the EAA |\n\nDo not decide the open questions. List the facts that bear on them.\n\n## Microenterprises (Art. 3(23), Art. 4(5), Art. 14(4))\n\n- Definition: \"an enterprise which employs fewer than 10 persons and which has an annual turnover not exceeding\n  EUR 2 million or an annual balance sheet total not exceeding EUR 2 million\".\n- Art. 4(5): \"Microenterprises providing services shall be exempt from complying with the accessibility\n  requirements referred to in paragraph 3 of this Article and any obligations relating to the compliance with\n  those requirements.\"\n- Recital 53: the enterprise must genuinely fulfil Commission Recommendation 2003/361/EC.\n  That Recommendation takes partner and linked enterprises into account, so a small subsidiary of a group may\n  not qualify. Not checked here against the Recommendation text; confirm with the legal owner.\n- Microenterprises dealing with products are not exempt. They only need not document the Art. 14\n  assessment, but must give the facts to the market surveillance authority on request.\n- Art. 4(6): Member States provide guidelines and tools to microenterprises.\n\n## What services must do\n\n- Art. 13(1): design and provide the service in line with the accessibility requirements.\n- Annex I Section III: accessible information about the service and its accessibility features (more than one\n  sensory channel, understandable, text that can be turned into other formats, adequate fonts, contrast and\n  adjustable spacing, alternatives for non-text content), accessible websites and mobile apps (point (c)),\n  and accessible support services where they exist (point (d)).\n- Annex I Section IV adds service-specific requirements, for example:\n  - (e) consumer banking: identification methods, electronic signatures, security and payment services that are\n    perceivable, operable, understandable and robust; information not more complex than CEFR level B2.\n  - (g) e-commerce: accessibility information about the products sold when the responsible operator provides\n    it; accessible identification, security and payment functions.\n  - (b) audiovisual: accessible programme guides; subtitles, audio description and similar access services\n    transmitted with adequate quality and synchronised, with user control.\n  - (f) e-books: no blocking of assistive technology, including by digital rights management.\n- Art. 13(2) and Annex V: publish the information on how the service meets the requirements in the general\n  terms and conditions or an equivalent document, in written and oral format and accessibly; keep it for as\n  long as the service is in operation.\n- Art. 13(3) and (4): procedures to stay conformant when the service or standards change; corrective measures\n  and information to the competent authorities in case of non-conformity.\n\n## Exceptions and transition\n\n- Art. 2(4): content not covered: pre-recorded time-based media and office files published before\n  28 June 2025; online maps if essential navigational information is provided accessibly; third-party content\n  \"neither funded, developed by, or under the control of\" the operator; archives not updated after\n  28 June 2025.\n- Art. 14: requirements apply only where they do not cause a fundamental alteration or a disproportionate burden\n  (criteria in Annex VI). The assessment must be documented, kept for five years and, for disproportionate\n  burden, renewed at least every five years. Operators that receive funding for improving accessibility cannot\n  rely on disproportionate burden. Except for microenterprises, operators relying on Art. 14 inform the\n  authorities.\n- Art. 32(1): a transitional period until 28 June 2030 for services using products lawfully used before\n  28 June 2025; service contracts agreed before 28 June 2025 continue until they expire, at most five years.\n  How this applies to a given app release is a legal question; do not use it to rate a finding as acceptable.\n\n## EN 301 549\n\n- Current version: V4.1.1 (2026-09), \"Harmonised European Standard\", adopted 24 August 2026, latest\n  announcement date 30 November 2026, withdrawal of conflicting national standards 31 May 2028 (from the\n  standard's foreword; listing at https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_en/301500_301599/301549/).\n- Prepared under standardisation request C(2022) 6456 \"to provide one voluntary means of conforming to the\n  essential requirements of Directive (EU) 2019/882\". \"Once the present document is cited in the Official\n  Journal\" under that directive, conformance with the clauses in Tables A.1 to A.5 gives a presumption of\n  conformity. Whether and when it has been cited must be checked in the Official Journal; this skill could not\n  confirm a citation.\n- V3.2.1 (2021-03) was prepared for Directive (EU) 2016/2102 (public sector websites and apps). V4.1.1 has\n  clauses 9, 10 and 11 updated to WCAG 2.2.\n- Annex ZB maps the standard to the EAA: Tables ZB.1 to ZB.3 for products, ZB.4 for all services, ZB.5 for\n  specific services.\n\n### Clause 11 (non-web software) for mobile apps\n\n- Clause 11.0 lists \"mobile applications\" among the software types covered. Web views inside an app are\n  assessed with clause 11, not clause 9 (\"Where there is any doubt, clause 11 requirements have precedence\").\n- Numbering: 11 plus the WCAG number for most criteria (11.1.4.3 Contrast (minimum), 11.2.5.8 Target size\n  (minimum), 11.3.3.8 Accessible authentication (minimum), 11.4.1.2 Name, role, value).\n- Void for non-web software (no requirement): 2.4.1 Bypass Blocks, 2.4.5 Multiple Ways, 3.1.2 Language of\n  Parts, 3.2.3 Consistent Navigation, 3.2.6 Consistent Help, and 4.1.1 (removed). Report these as best\n  practice, not as failures, when the target is EN 301 549.\n- 11.1.4.4 Resize text is modified: up to 200 percent or, where the platform's text resizing does not reach\n  200 percent for all text, up to the platform's text sizing capabilities.\n- 11.2.4.2 Non-web software titled: screens have titles that describe their name, topic or purpose where the\n  platform supports titles.\n- 11.3.3.8 Note 2 lists password manager support and copy and paste as mechanisms that satisfy the criterion.\n- 11.5.2 Accessibility services: use the platform accessibility services (11.5.2.3, a recommendation) so that\n  name, role, state, value, actions and focus are exposed (11.5.2.5 to 11.5.2.17).\n- 11.7 User preferences: follow the platform's user preference settings for accessibility features (text size,\n  contrast, reduce motion) unless not following them is essential to the function.\n- Clause 4 functional performance criteria (usage without vision, with limited vision, without colour\n  perception, without hearing, limited manipulation, limited cognition, privacy, and others) mirror EAA\n  Annex I Section VII.\n\n## WCAG 2.2 level A and AA on mobile\n\nAll 55 level A and AA success criteria, with the usual mobile check. Use the number and name exactly in\nfindings.\n\n| SC | Name | Level | Mobile check |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1.1.1 | Non-text Content | A | Icons, images, charts have names; decorative ones hidden |\n| 1.2.1 | Audio-only and Video-only (Prerecorded) | A | Transcript or audio alternative |\n| 1.2.2 | Captions (Prerecorded) | A | Captions in the player, user can switch on |\n| 1.2.3 | Audio Description or Media Alternative (Prerecorded) | A | Audio description or text alternative |\n| 1.2.4 | Captions (Live) | AA | Live streams have captions |\n| 1.2.5 | Audio Description (Prerecorded) | AA | Audio description track |\n| 1.3.1 | Info and Relationships | A | Headings, lists, groups, form labels exposed in semantics |\n| 1.3.2 | Meaningful Sequence | A | Screen reader order matches visual order |\n| 1.3.3 | Sensory Characteristics | A | No \"tap the round button on the right\" only instructions |\n| 1.3.4 | Orientation | AA | Works in portrait and landscape unless essential |\n| 1.3.5 | Identify Input Purpose | AA | Autofill content types on personal data fields |\n| 1.4.1 | Use of Color | A | Errors, states and links not colour-only |\n| 1.4.2 | Audio Control | A | Auto-playing audio over 3 seconds can be paused or muted |\n| 1.4.3 | Contrast (Minimum) | AA | 4.5:1 text, 3:1 large text (18 pt, or 14 pt bold) |\n| 1.4.4 | Resize Text | AA | 200 percent (EN 301 549: up to platform maximum) without loss |\n| 1.4.5 | Images of Text | AA | No text baked into images |\n| 1.4.10 | Reflow | AA | Content reflows at large text and narrow widths, no two-way scrolling |\n| 1.4.11 | Non-text Contrast | AA | 3:1 for icons, control borders, focus indicators |\n| 1.4.12 | Text Spacing | AA | No clipping when line, letter or word spacing grows |\n| 1.4.13 | Content on Hover or Focus | AA | Tooltips and popovers dismissible and persistent |\n| 2.1.1 | Keyboard | A | Everything works with an external keyboard and switch access |\n| 2.1.2 | No Keyboard Trap | A | Focus can leave every component, including bottom sheets and web views |\n| 2.1.4 | Character Key Shortcuts | A | Single-key shortcuts can be turned off or remapped |\n| 2.2.1 | Timing Adjustable | A | Session and OTP timeouts can be extended or warned |\n| 2.2.2 | Pause, Stop, Hide | A | Carousels and animations over 5 seconds can be paused |\n| 2.3.1 | Three Flashes or Below Threshold | A | No flashing more than three times per second |\n| 2.4.1 | Bypass Blocks | A | Void in EN 301 549 clause 11 |\n| 2.4.2 | Page Titled | A | Each screen has a title (EN 301 549 11.2.4.2) |\n| 2.4.3 | Focus Order | A | Focus moves into dialogs and returns after they close |\n| 2.4.4 | Link Purpose (In Context) | A | \"Learn more\" links have context |\n| 2.4.5 | Multiple Ways | AA | Void in EN 301 549 clause 11 |\n| 2.4.6 | Headings and Labels | AA | Headings and labels describe topic or purpose |\n| 2.4.7 | Focus Visible | AA | Keyboard focus indicator visible |\n| 2.4.11 | Focus Not Obscured (Minimum) | AA | Focused field not hidden by the keyboard, sticky bars or sheets |\n| 2.5.1 | Pointer Gestures | A | Pinch, multi-finger and path gestures have single-tap alternatives |\n| 2.5.2 | Pointer Cancellation | A | Actions fire on release, can be aborted by sliding off |\n| 2.5.3 | Label in Name | A | Accessible name contains the visible text |\n| 2.5.4 | Motion Actuation | A | Shake or tilt features have a UI alternative and can be disabled |\n| 2.5.7 | Dragging Movements | AA | Reorder, sliders, maps work without dragging |\n| 2.5.8 | Target Size (Minimum) | AA | At least 24 by 24 (platform target: 48dp / 44pt) |\n| 3.1.1 | Language of Page | A | App and web view language set so the screen reader voice is correct |\n| 3.1.2 | Language of Parts | AA | Void in EN 301 549 clause 11 |\n| 3.2.1 | On Focus | A | Focus alone does not navigate or submit |\n| 3.2.2 | On Input | A | Changing a field does not change context unexpectedly |\n| 3.2.3 | Consistent Navigation | AA | Void in EN 301 549 clause 11 |\n| 3.2.4 | Consistent Identification | AA | Same function, same name and icon across screens |\n| 3.2.6 | Consistent Help | A | Void in EN 301 549 clause 11; still good practice |\n| 3.3.1 | Error Identification | A | Errors described in text and announced |\n| 3.3.2 | Labels or Instructions | A | Persistent labels, not placeholder only |\n| 3.3.3 | Error Suggestion | AA | Error text says how to fix it |\n| 3.3.4 | Error Prevention (Legal, Financial, Data) | AA | Review, confirm or undo for payments and contracts |\n| 3.3.7 | Redundant Entry | A | No re-entry of data already given in the same flow |\n| 3.3.8 | Accessible Authentication (Minimum) | AA | Password managers, paste, passkeys or biometrics allowed |\n| 4.1.2 | Name, Role, Value | A | Custom controls expose name, role, state and value |\n| 4.1.3 | Status Messages | AA | Toasts, snackbars, loading and result messages announced |\n\n## Accessibility information template (Annex V)\n\nDraft for the compliance owner; the final wording and where it is published are their decision.\n\n```markdown\n## Accessibility of <service name>\n\n<Service name> is <general description of the service, in plain language>.\n\nHow the service works: <main functions, how to sign in, buy, pay, get help>.\n\nHow we meet the accessibility requirements:\n- Mobile apps for Android and iOS are designed and tested against EN 301 549 V4.1.1 clause 11 (WCAG 2.2 level AA).\n- Supported assistive technologies: TalkBack, Switch Access, VoiceOver, Voice Control, external keyboards.\n- The apps follow system text size, bold text, contrast and reduce motion settings.\n- Known limitations: <list, with the planned fix date, or the Art. 14 assessment reference>.\n- How we keep it conformant: accessibility checks on every release, manual screen reader tests of <journeys>.\n\nContact for accessibility questions and feedback: <accessible channels: email, phone, relay service>.\nLast reviewed: <date>.\n```\n\n## Open points this skill could not verify\n\n- Whether EN 301 549 V4.1.1 has been cited in the Official Journal under Directive (EU) 2019/882, and from when.\n- Whether in-app purchases processed by an app store make the app publisher an e-commerce service provider.\n- National transposition details (enforcement body, penalties, any extensions) for each Member State.\n",
    "references/flutter.md":
        "# Flutter accessibility: Semantics, text scaling and guideline tests\n\nLoad this when reviewing or fixing accessibility in a Flutter app. APIs checked against api.flutter.dev\n(widgets, semantics, painting and flutter_test libraries).\n\n## Semantics cookbook\n\n| Need | Flutter API | WCAG 2.2 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Name for an icon button | `IconButton(tooltip: ...)`; `Icon(semanticLabel: ...)` for a meaningful standalone icon | 1.1.1, 4.1.2 |\n| Name for an image | `Image(semanticLabel: ...)`; decorative: `excludeFromSemantics: true` | 1.1.1 |\n| Custom widget semantics | `Semantics(label:, hint:, value:, button: true, onTap:, child:)` | 4.1.2 |\n| Toggle, check and selection state | `Semantics(toggled:, checked:, selected:, enabled:)`, or Material `Switch`, `Checkbox`, `Radio` | 4.1.2 |\n| Heading | `Semantics(header: true)`; `headingLevel` for a level | 1.3.1, 2.4.6 |\n| One focus stop for a group | `MergeSemantics(child: ...)` | 1.3.1 |\n| Replace children's semantics | `Semantics(excludeSemantics: true, label: ..., child: ...)` | 1.3.1 |\n| Hide decoration | `ExcludeSemantics(child: ...)` | 1.1.1 |\n| Hide content behind a modal | `BlockSemantics` (Material dialogs and sheets do this already) | 2.4.3 |\n| Reading order | Fix the widget order first; otherwise `Semantics(sortKey: OrdinalSortKey(1))` on the siblings | 1.3.2 |\n| Status message | `Semantics(liveRegion: true, child: Text(status))` | 4.1.3 |\n| Announcement | `SemanticsService.sendAnnouncement(View.of(context), message, TextDirection.ltr)` (`announce` is deprecated since 3.35) | 4.1.3 |\n| Gesture alternative | `Semantics(customSemanticsActions: {CustomSemanticsAction(label: ...): handler})` | 2.5.1, 2.5.7 |\n| Autofill | `TextField(autofillHints: const [AutofillHints.email])` inside `AutofillGroup` | 1.3.5, 3.3.8 |\n| Stable id for tests | `Semantics(identifier: 'checkout_pay')` | - |\n| Touch target | Material default `MaterialTapTargetSize.padded` (48x48); `ConstrainedBox` for custom widgets | 2.5.8 |\n\n### Custom tappable card\n\n```dart\n// BAD: GestureDetector has no role, no label; the image is read as \"image\"\nGestureDetector(\n  onTap: () => open(item),\n  child: Column(children: [Image.network(item.imageUrl), Text(item.title), Text(item.price)]),\n);\n\n// GOOD: one button node with a meaningful label, decorative image excluded, favourite as an action\nSemantics(\n  button: true,\n  label: '\${item.title}, \${item.price}',\n  onTapHint: l10n.openDetails,\n  excludeSemantics: true,\n  customSemanticsActions: {\n    CustomSemanticsAction(label: item.isFavourite ? l10n.removeFavourite : l10n.addFavourite):\n        () => toggleFavourite(item),\n  },\n  child: InkWell(\n    onTap: () => open(item),\n    child: Column(children: [\n      Image.network(item.imageUrl, excludeFromSemantics: true),\n      Text(item.title),\n      Text(item.price),\n    ]),\n  ),\n);\n```\n\nKeep a visible favourite button for touch users too; the custom action is the screen reader path.\n\n## Text scaling\n\n- Read the scaler with `MediaQuery.textScalerOf(context)`. `MediaQuery.textScaleFactorOf` and\n  `TextScaler.textScaleFactor` exist for backward compatibility only; do not build new code on them.\n- Never wrap the app in `MediaQuery(data: ...copyWith(textScaler: TextScaler.noScaling))`.\n- `MediaQuery.withClampedTextScaling(maxScaleFactor: ...)` and `MediaQuery.withNoTextScaling` exist for a\n  subtree that truly cannot grow. Use them locally, keep the maximum at 2.0 or more, and justify each use.\n- No fixed `SizedBox(height: ...)` around text; use `ConstrainedBox(constraints: BoxConstraints(minHeight: ...))`.\n  Let `Row`s wrap (`Wrap`, or switch to `Column` when `textScaler.scale(14) > 28`).\n- Test at a scale of 2.0 in widget tests (see below) and on a device at the largest system setting.\n\n## Other user preferences (EN 301 549 11.7)\n\n```dart\nfinal reduceMotion = MediaQuery.disableAnimationsOf(context); // Reduce Motion / Remove animations\nfinal boldText = MediaQuery.boldTextOf(context);               // iOS Bold Text\nfinal highContrast = MediaQuery.highContrastOf(context);       // iOS Increase Contrast\n```\n\n- Respect them: shorter or no animations, heavier weights, a high-contrast `ThemeData` (`MaterialApp` has\n  `highContrastTheme` and `highContrastDarkTheme`).\n\n## Automated checks\n\n### Guideline tests\n\n```dart\nimport 'package:flutter_test/flutter_test.dart';\n\nvoid main() {\n  testWidgets('checkout screen meets accessibility guidelines', (tester) async {\n    final SemanticsHandle handle = tester.ensureSemantics();\n    await tester.pumpWidget(const MaterialApp(home: CheckoutScreen()));\n\n    await expectLater(tester, meetsGuideline(androidTapTargetGuideline)); // 48x48\n    await expectLater(tester, meetsGuideline(iOSTapTargetGuideline));     // 44x44\n    await expectLater(tester, meetsGuideline(labeledTapTargetGuideline)); // tappable nodes have labels\n    await expectLater(tester, meetsGuideline(textContrastGuideline));     // WCAG text contrast\n    handle.dispose();\n  });\n}\n```\n\n- `textContrastGuideline` samples pixels around each text node with a simple light and dark partition; it can\n  miss text on images and gradients. Check those by hand.\n- Run the same test with large text:\n\n```dart\ntestWidgets('checkout works at 200 percent text', (tester) async {\n  tester.platformDispatcher.textScaleFactorTestValue = 2.0;\n  addTearDown(tester.platformDispatcher.clearTextScaleFactorTestValue);\n  await tester.pumpWidget(const MaterialApp(home: CheckoutScreen()));\n  expect(tester.takeException(), isNull); // no overflow errors\n});\n```\n\n### Semantics assertions\n\n```dart\nfinal handle = tester.ensureSemantics();\nexpect(\n  tester.getSemantics(find.byTooltip('Delete message')),\n  matchesSemantics(label: 'Delete message', isButton: true, hasTapAction: true,\n      hasEnabledState: true, isEnabled: true, isFocusable: true),\n);\nexpect(find.bySemanticsLabel('Payment method'), findsOneWidget);\nhandle.dispose();\n```\n\n`matchesSemantics` matches all flags exactly; when only some flags matter, `containsSemantics` is less brittle.\n\n### On device\n\n- Flutter renders its own pixels, so platform tools see only what the semantics tree exposes. Run Android\n  Accessibility Scanner and the iOS Accessibility Inspector on a release or profile build as well.\n- `showSemanticsDebugger: true` on `MaterialApp` shows the semantics tree over the UI during development.\n",
    "references/ios.md":
        "# iOS accessibility: SwiftUI, UIKit, VoiceOver and tests\n\nLoad this when reviewing or fixing accessibility in an iOS app. APIs and availability checked against\ndeveloper.apple.com documentation (SwiftUI, Accessibility, UIKit, XCUIAutomation) and the Human Interface\nGuidelines accessibility page.\n\n## SwiftUI cookbook\n\n| Need | SwiftUI API | Availability | WCAG 2.2 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Name | `.accessibilityLabel(_:)`, or a `Label` / `Button` title | iOS 14 | 1.1.1, 4.1.2 |\n| Usage hint | `.accessibilityHint(_:)` (short, optional, not a second label) | iOS 14 | 3.3.2 |\n| Value of a custom control | `.accessibilityValue(_:)` | iOS 14 | 4.1.2 |\n| Role and state | Native `Toggle`, `Button`, `Picker`, `Slider`; else `.accessibilityAddTraits(.isButton)`, `.isSelected`, `.isHeader` | iOS 14 | 4.1.2 |\n| Heading | `.accessibilityAddTraits(.isHeader)` or `.accessibilityHeading(.h2)` | iOS 14 / 15 | 1.3.1 |\n| Group | `.accessibilityElement(children: .combine)`, `.ignore` with own label, `.contain` | iOS 13 | 1.3.1 |\n| Hide decoration | `Image(decorative:)`, `.accessibilityHidden(true)` | iOS 14 | 1.1.1 |\n| Custom control semantics | `.accessibilityRepresentation { Slider(...) }` | iOS 15 | 4.1.2 |\n| Gesture alternative | `.accessibilityAction(named:_:)`, `.accessibilityAdjustableAction` | iOS 14 | 2.5.1, 2.5.7 |\n| Reading order | `.accessibilitySortPriority(_:)` | iOS 14 | 1.3.2 |\n| Move focus | `@AccessibilityFocusState` with `.accessibilityFocused(_:)` | iOS 15 | 2.4.3 |\n| Voice Control names | `.accessibilityInputLabels(_:)` | iOS 14 | 2.5.3 |\n| Announcement | `AccessibilityNotification.Announcement(\"...\").post()` | iOS 17 | 4.1.3 |\n| Screen or layout change | `AccessibilityNotification.ScreenChanged`, `.LayoutChanged` | iOS 17 | 2.4.3 |\n| Scaled size | `@ScaledMetric(relativeTo: .body)` | iOS 14 | 1.4.4 |\n| Text size category | `@Environment(\\.dynamicTypeSize)`, `.isAccessibilitySize` | iOS 15 | 1.4.4, 1.4.10 |\n| Reduce Motion | `@Environment(\\.accessibilityReduceMotion)` | iOS 13 | 2.2.2, 2.3.1 |\n| Increase Contrast | `@Environment(\\.colorSchemeContrast)` | iOS 13 | 1.4.3, 1.4.11 |\n| Bold Text | `@Environment(\\.legibilityWeight)` | iOS 13 | 11.7 user preferences |\n| Differentiate Without Color | `@Environment(\\.accessibilityDifferentiateWithoutColor)` | iOS 13 | 1.4.1 |\n| Reduce Transparency | `@Environment(\\.accessibilityReduceTransparency)` | iOS 13 | 1.4.3 |\n\n### Card with a custom label and actions\n\n```swift\n// BAD: five VoiceOver stops; the price is read before the title; favourite is unlabeled\nVStack {\n    AsyncImage(url: item.imageURL)\n    Text(item.price)\n    Text(item.title)\n    Button { toggleFavourite() } label: { Image(systemName: item.isFavourite ? \"heart.fill\" : \"heart\") }\n}\n\n// GOOD: one element with a meaningful label; favourite exposed as a named action and a trait\nVStack {\n    AsyncImage(url: item.imageURL).accessibilityHidden(true)\n    Text(item.title)\n    Text(item.price)\n    Button { toggleFavourite() } label: {\n        Label(\"Favourite\", systemImage: item.isFavourite ? \"heart.fill\" : \"heart\").labelStyle(.iconOnly)\n    }\n}\n.accessibilityElement(children: .ignore)\n.accessibilityLabel(\"\\(item.title), \\(item.price)\")\n.accessibilityValue(item.isFavourite ? \"Favourite\" : \"\")\n.accessibilityAddTraits(.isButton)\n.accessibilityAction { open(item) }\n.accessibilityAction(named: item.isFavourite ? \"Remove from favourites\" : \"Add to favourites\") { toggleFavourite() }\n```\n\n### Focus after a sheet or error\n\n```swift\n@AccessibilityFocusState private var errorFocused: Bool\n\nVStack {\n    if let error { Text(error).accessibilityFocused(\$errorFocused) }\n    Button(\"Pay\") { Task { await pay() } }\n}\n.onChange(of: error) { _, newValue in errorFocused = newValue != nil }\n```\n\n## Dynamic Type\n\n- Use text styles: `.font(.body)`, `.font(.headline)`. A custom font scales with\n  `.font(.custom(\"Brand\", size: 17, relativeTo: .body))`.\n- No fixed `frame(height:)` on text; use `minHeight`. Let text wrap; avoid `.lineLimit(1)` on labels.\n- Switch to a vertical layout at accessibility sizes (`dynamicTypeSize.isAccessibilitySize`) with `AnyLayout`\n  (iOS 16) or `ViewThatFits`.\n- Do not cap with `.dynamicTypeSize(...DynamicTypeSize.large)` app-wide. A cap on a single element that\n  truly cannot grow should still allow accessibility sizes, and the content must stay reachable (for example\n  through the Large Content Viewer for tab bar items).\n- The HIG asks for text that can be enlarged by at least 200 percent (140 percent on watchOS).\n- UIKit: `UIFont.preferredFont(forTextStyle:)` or `UIFontMetrics(forTextStyle:).scaledFont(for:)`, plus\n  `adjustsFontForContentSizeCategory = true` on labels, text fields and text views.\n\n## Touch targets and contrast (HIG)\n\n- Default control size on iOS and iPadOS: 44x44 pt; minimum 28x28 pt. Use 44x44 for anything a user taps\n  often; WCAG 2.5.8 sets the 24 by 24 floor.\n- Enlarge hit areas without changing visuals: `.frame(minWidth: 44, minHeight: 44)` plus\n  `.contentShape(Rectangle())`.\n- HIG contrast guidance follows WCAG AA: 4.5:1 up to 17 pt text, 3:1 at 18 pt or bold text. Provide a\n  higher-contrast variant when Increase Contrast is on (asset catalog \"High Contrast\" appearance or\n  `colorSchemeContrast`).\n\n## Motion\n\n```swift\n@Environment(\\.accessibilityReduceMotion) private var reduceMotion\n\n// BAD: parallax and zoom regardless of the setting\n.scaleEffect(isPressed ? 1.2 : 1).animation(.spring, value: isPressed)\n\n// GOOD: replace motion with a cross-fade or no animation\n.scaleEffect(reduceMotion ? 1 : (isPressed ? 1.2 : 1))\n.animation(reduceMotion ? nil : .default, value: isPressed)\n```\n\nUIKit: `UIAccessibility.isReduceMotionEnabled` and its change notification.\n\n## UIKit essentials\n\n```swift\n// BAD\nlet button = UIButton(type: .custom)\nbutton.setImage(UIImage(systemName: \"trash\"), for: .normal)\n\n// GOOD\nbutton.accessibilityLabel = String(localized: \"Delete message\")\nheaderLabel.accessibilityTraits.insert(.header)\ndecorativeImageView.isAccessibilityElement = false\nUIAccessibility.post(notification: .announcement, argument: String(localized: \"Saved\"))\n```\n\n## Automated checks\n\n### XCUITest accessibility audit (iOS 17 and later)\n\n```swift\nfinal class AccessibilityAuditTests: XCTestCase {\n    @MainActor\n    func testCheckoutScreen() throws {\n        let app = XCUIApplication()\n        app.launch()\n        app.buttons[\"Checkout\"].tap()\n\n        try app.performAccessibilityAudit()   // fails the test for every issue found\n    }\n\n    @MainActor\n    func testSettingsDynamicTypeAndContrast() throws {\n        let app = XCUIApplication()\n        app.launch()\n        try app.performAccessibilityAudit(for: [.dynamicType, .contrast, .textClipped]) { issue in\n            // Return true only to ignore a reviewed, documented false positive\n            issue.auditType == .contrast && issue.element?.identifier == \"brandLogo\"\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Audit types (`XCUIAccessibilityAuditType`): `.all`, `.contrast`, `.dynamicType`, `.elementDetection`,\n  `.hitRegion`, `.parentChild`, `.sufficientElementDescription`, `.textClipped`, `.trait`, `.action`.\n  Not every type applies to every platform; check the documentation for the Xcode version in CI.\n- Issues expose `auditType`, `element`, `compactDescription` and `detailedDescription`; put them in the\n  report's evidence.\n- The audit checks the current screen only: navigate to each critical screen and state, then audit.\n- The current documentation lists the API under the XCUIAutomation framework. The test target needs an iOS 17\n  or later simulator or device.\n\n### Accessibility Inspector\n\nXcode > Open Developer Tool > Accessibility Inspector: inspect labels, traits and values on a simulator or\ndevice, and run an audit on the current screen. Use Environment Overrides in the Xcode debug bar to switch\ntext size, Increase Contrast and other settings while the app runs.\n\n### Previews\n\n```swift\n#Preview(\"AX5\") { CheckoutView().environment(\\.dynamicTypeSize, .accessibility5) }\n```\n\n## VoiceOver quick reference\n\n- Enable: Settings > Accessibility > VoiceOver, or the Accessibility Shortcut (triple-click the side button).\n- Swipe right or left: next or previous element. Double tap: activate. Three-finger swipe: scroll.\n- Rotor (rotate two fingers): headings, actions, links, form controls. Custom actions appear under \"Actions\".\n- Two-finger scrub (\"Z\" gesture): go back or dismiss; make sure custom modals support it\n  (`.accessibilityAction(.escape)` or UIKit `accessibilityPerformEscape()`).\n- Also test Voice Control (names spoken match visible labels) and Full Keyboard Access with a hardware keyboard.\n\nThe full manual procedure is in the manual test script reference.\n",
    "references/manual-test-script.md":
        "# Manual screen reader and settings test script\n\nLoad this for step 6 of the workflow. Automated checks cannot tell whether a label makes sense, whether focus\nlands in the right place after an action, or whether a journey can be finished at all. This script can.\nRun it per critical journey, on a real device where possible, once per platform.\n\n## Setup\n\n- Record: device model, OS version, screen reader version, app build, date, tester.\n- Use a test account with realistic data (long names, long addresses, many items) and a payment sandbox.\n- Learn the gestures before testing; a tester fighting the screen reader produces false findings.\n- Android: TalkBack on; in TalkBack's developer settings set \"Log output level\" to VERBOSE if debugging.\n- iOS: VoiceOver on via the Accessibility Shortcut (triple-click the side button) so it can be toggled fast.\n- Turn on the screen reader's caption or speech output panel if available, so the spoken text can be copied\n  into findings as evidence.\n\n## Pass 1: screen reader, linear navigation\n\nFor each screen in the journey:\n\n1. Open the screen. Expected: focus starts at the top (title or first element), or at the most relevant\n   element after navigation. The screen title is announced (2.4.2).\n2. Swipe right through every element to the end, then left back to the start.\n   - Every element is reachable; nothing visible is skipped (1.3.1, 2.1.1).\n   - Each element is announced once, in visual reading order (1.3.2, 2.4.3).\n   - Each announcement has a name, role and state that match what is on screen: \"Wi-Fi, switch, on\"\n     (4.1.2). No \"button\", \"unlabeled\", file names, or icon names like \"ic_delete_24\".\n   - Decorative images are skipped (1.1.1). Related text (title, price, rating) is one stop, not five.\n   - Headings are announced as headings (1.3.1).\n3. Use heading navigation (TalkBack reading controls set to Headings; VoiceOver rotor set to Headings).\n   Expected: the screen's sections can be reached by heading.\n4. Activate each control with a double tap. Expected: it does what its label says.\n5. Open the actions menu on rows that have swipe, long-press or drag behaviour (TalkBack \"Actions\",\n   VoiceOver rotor \"Actions\"). Expected: each gesture-only action is available (2.5.1, 2.5.7).\n\n## Pass 2: screen reader, task completion\n\nComplete the journey from start to finish using only the screen reader, for example:\n\n| Step | Check | SC |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sign in | Fields announce label and type (\"Email, edit box\"); password manager or autofill offers credentials; paste works | 3.3.2, 1.3.5, 3.3.8 |\n| Enter invalid data | Error is announced when it appears, or focus moves to it; the text says how to fix it | 3.3.1, 3.3.3, 4.1.3 |\n| Open a dialog or bottom sheet | Focus moves into it; content behind it is not reachable; closing returns focus to the trigger | 2.4.3, 2.1.2 |\n| Loading and results | \"Loading\" and the result (\"3 results\", \"Saved\") are announced without moving focus | 4.1.3 |\n| Payment or contract | Summary is readable before confirming; there is a review, confirm or undo step | 3.3.4 |\n| One-time code | Code field supports autofill or paste; timeout can be extended or is long enough | 3.3.8, 2.2.1 |\n| Web view (terms, help, 3-D Secure) | Content is readable and operable with the screen reader; focus does not get stuck | 2.1.2, 4.1.2 |\n| Back navigation | Back gesture or button works (TalkBack back; VoiceOver two-finger scrub) | 2.1.1 |\n\nA journey that cannot be finished is a Blocker. Record the exact step and the spoken text.\n\n## Pass 3: text size and display settings\n\n1. Set the largest text size: Android Settings > Display > Font size at maximum (Android 14 and later go to\n   200 percent) plus Display size at maximum; iOS Settings > Accessibility > Display and Text Size > Larger\n   Text with the largest accessibility size.\n2. Walk the journey again. Expected: no truncated or overlapping text, no text cut off inside buttons, all\n   content and controls still reachable by scrolling, no horizontal scrolling for reading text (1.4.4, 1.4.10).\n3. Turn on Bold Text (iOS) or Bold text / High contrast text (Android, where available). Expected: the app\n   follows the setting (EN 301 549 11.7).\n4. Turn on Increase Contrast (iOS) or a high-contrast option (Android). Check text and icons against\n   backgrounds with a contrast checker on screenshots: 4.5:1 text, 3:1 large text and icons (1.4.3, 1.4.11).\n5. Turn on colour filters or grayscale. Expected: errors, states and charts are still understandable (1.4.1).\n6. Rotate the device. Expected: the journey works in landscape unless orientation is essential (1.3.4).\n\n## Pass 4: motion\n\n1. iOS: Settings > Accessibility > Motion > Reduce Motion on. Android: Settings > Accessibility > Remove\n   animations on (wording varies by device).\n2. Expected: parallax, zoom and large transitions are removed or replaced by fades; auto-playing carousels and\n   animations longer than five seconds can be paused (2.2.2); nothing flashes more than three times per\n   second (2.3.1).\n\n## Pass 5: switch, keyboard and voice\n\n1. Connect a Bluetooth keyboard. Android: Tab and arrow keys; iOS: Settings > Accessibility > Keyboards >\n   Full Keyboard Access. Expected: every control can be reached and activated, focus is visible, focus is\n   not trapped, and the focused control is not hidden behind the soft keyboard or a sticky bar\n   (2.1.1, 2.1.2, 2.4.7, 2.4.11).\n2. Android Switch Access or iOS Switch Control: each actionable item is highlighted once; gesture actions are\n   available as menu items.\n3. iOS Voice Control (\"Show names\") or Android Voice Access: saying the visible label activates the control\n   (2.5.3).\n\n## Recording findings\n\nFor each failure write down, at the moment it happens:\n\n- Journey and step, screen name, platform and assistive technology.\n- What was expected and what happened, with the spoken text in quotes:\n  `TalkBack: \"Button\" on the pay icon; expected \"Pay 24.90 euros, button\"`.\n- A screenshot or screen recording with the screen reader caption visible.\n- The WCAG 2.2 success criterion (number and name) and a first severity guess.\n\nThen map each one to the report table in the skill's output format.\n",
  },
  "mobile-ble": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-ble\ndescription: \"Designs, implements and reviews Bluetooth Low Energy code in Android, iOS and Flutter apps: Android 12+ permissions, filtered scans, GATT operation queues, MTU, bonding, status 133, CoreBluetooth threading and state restoration, flutter_blue_plus, framing, CRC, safe firmware updates (DFU) and connection state machines. Use when writing or reviewing BLE scan, connect, read, write or notify code, reconnect logic or device protocols.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile BLE\n\nBuild and review the app side of a Bluetooth Low Energy link the way a senior connected-device engineer\nwould: correct permissions per OS version, one GATT operation at a time, no assumptions about threads,\nexplicit connection states, framed and checked messages, firmware updates that cannot brick the device,\nand logs that leak nothing. A good result works on real phones, recovers from drops, and survives\nhostile or corrupt input.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Writing or changing scan, connect, discover, read, write, notify or disconnect code.\n- Android BLE permissions, foreground services or background work for a connected device.\n- CoreBluetooth central code, background mode, state restoration or reconnect logic.\n- flutter_blue_plus code, or a Flutter plugin that talks to a peripheral.\n- Designing or reviewing a packet protocol on top of GATT: framing, chunking, sequence numbers, CRC.\n- Firmware update (DFU) flows driven by the app.\n- \"Status 133\", \"GATT error\", \"writes get lost\", \"notifications stop\", \"works on Pixel but not on X\".\n\nNot for: a full MASVS audit (use `mobile-security`), CRA or EN 18031 mapping (use `mobile-eu-compliance`),\ngeneral Kotlin or Swift style (use `mobile-kotlin`, `mobile-swift`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Read the device contract first.** Find the firmware protocol document: services, characteristics,\n   opcodes, packet layout, CRC variant, timing, DFU procedure. The firmware document is authoritative.\n   If a packet format, opcode or timing is not written down, ask; never guess it.\n2. **Read the project setup.**\n   - Android: `minSdk`, `targetSdk`, manifest permissions, foreground service declarations.\n   - iOS: deployment target, `Info.plist` usage strings, `UIBackgroundModes`, restoration identifier.\n   - Flutter: `pubspec.yaml` BLE and permission plugins and their versions.\n3. **Load the platform reference** for the code being touched:\n   [Android](references/android.md), [iOS](references/ios.md), [Flutter](references/flutter.md).\n4. **Model the link** as a sealed connection state machine before writing callbacks (rule 9).\n5. **Put every GATT operation behind one queue** with a timeout per operation (rule 3).\n6. **Design or check the wire protocol**: framing above MTU, sequence numbers, CRC, bounds checks.\n   Use [Protocol and DFU](references/protocol-and-dfu.md).\n7. **Plan background behaviour** per platform: foreground service or Companion Device Manager on\n   Android, `bluetooth-central` and state restoration on iOS.\n8. **Test** with fakes behind an interface, then on real hardware. See [Testing](references/testing.md).\n9. **Report** permissions, `Info.plist` keys, entitlements, foreground service types and background\n   modes the change adds. These affect store review and signing and must be called out explicitly.\n\n## Platform facts (verified)\n\n| Area | Fact |\n|---|---|\n| Android 12+ (API 31) | `BLUETOOTH_SCAN`, `BLUETOOTH_CONNECT`, `BLUETOOTH_ADVERTISE` are runtime permissions; legacy `BLUETOOTH` and `BLUETOOTH_ADMIN` get `maxSdkVersion=\"30\"` |\n| `neverForLocation` | Lets API 31+ scan without location; some beacons are filtered from results |\n| Android 11 and lower | Scanning needs `ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION` |\n| Android 13 (API 33) | `writeCharacteristic(c, value, writeType)` returns an `Int` status; byte-array callbacks; old forms deprecated |\n| Android 14 (API 34) | Stack requests ATT MTU 517 on the first `requestMtu` and ignores later requests |\n| Android 14 (API 34) | Every foreground service declares a type; `connectedDevice` needs `FOREGROUND_SERVICE_CONNECTED_DEVICE` |\n| Android 16 (API 36) | Bond loss: system keeps the bond and shows a dialog; `ACTION_KEY_MISSING` and `ACTION_ENCRYPTION_CHANGE` intents (OEM behaviour varies) |\n| Android 17 (API 37) | `connectGatt(BluetoothGattConnectionSettings, Executor, BluetoothGattCallback)` added; the `Context` overloads are deprecated; autonomous re-pairing after bond loss |\n| iOS 13+ | `NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription` is required to use Bluetooth |\n| iOS | `connect(_:options:)` never times out; `queue: nil` delivers delegate calls on the main queue |\n| iOS | Background scanning needs `bluetooth-central` and explicit service UUIDs |\n| iOS 26 | Per TN3115, some relaunch cases are limited to apps that set up accessories with AccessorySetupKit |\n| flutter_blue_plus 2.x | `connect` takes a required `license:` argument; commercial use needs a paid license |\n\nRe-check these against the official pages before quoting them in a design document. Versions move.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Declare permissions per API level and check them before every call\n\n```xml\n<!-- BAD: location requested on Android 12+ for no reason, no legacy caps -->\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.BLUETOOTH\" />\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION\" />\n\n<!-- GOOD: app never derives location from scans -->\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.BLUETOOTH\" android:maxSdkVersion=\"30\" />\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.BLUETOOTH_ADMIN\" android:maxSdkVersion=\"30\" />\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION\" android:maxSdkVersion=\"30\" />\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.BLUETOOTH_SCAN\"\n    android:usesPermissionFlags=\"neverForLocation\" />\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.BLUETOOTH_CONNECT\" />\n<uses-feature android:name=\"android.hardware.bluetooth_le\" android:required=\"true\" />\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD: CBCentralManager created at launch, before the user knows why; no usage string\nlet central = CBCentralManager(delegate: self, queue: nil)\n\n// GOOD: Info.plist has NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription; create the manager when the feature starts\nif CBManager.authorization == .denied { state = .bluetoothDenied; return }\ncentral = CBCentralManager(delegate: self, queue: bleQueue)\n```\n\n- If the app uses scan results for location, drop `neverForLocation` and keep `ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION`.\n- Request runtime permissions from a screen that explains the feature. Handle \"denied\" with a path to settings.\n\n### 2. Scan with filters, a time limit and the cheapest mode that works\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: unfiltered, low latency, never stopped\nscanner.startScan(callback)\n\n// GOOD\nval filters = listOf(ScanFilter.Builder().setServiceUuid(ParcelUuid(SERVICE_UUID)).build())\nval settings = ScanSettings.Builder().setScanMode(ScanSettings.SCAN_MODE_BALANCED).build()\nscanner.startScan(filters, settings, callback)\nscanTimeout = scope.launch { delay(10.seconds); scanner.stopScan(callback) }\n```\n\n- Stop as soon as the device is found. Never scan in a loop.\n- Unfiltered scans stop on screen off. Apps not in the foreground get `SCAN_MODE_LOW_POWER`.\n- Starting scans too often gets throttled: `SCAN_FAILED_SCANNING_TOO_FREQUENTLY`, or silently no\n  results on some devices. The exact limit is not documented; do not restart scans in a tight loop.\n- iOS: pass service UUIDs to `scanForPeripherals(withServices:options:)`; `nil` does not work in background.\n\n### 3. One GATT operation at a time, each with a timeout\n\nAndroid and iOS both drop or reject overlapping requests. Android returns `false` or\n`ERROR_GATT_WRITE_REQUEST_BUSY`; the callback for the first request may never come.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: second call fails while the first is in flight\ngatt.writeCharacteristic(cmd, bytes, WRITE_TYPE_DEFAULT)\ngatt.readCharacteristic(status)\n\n// GOOD: serialized queue, completes on the callback, times out if the callback never comes\nsuspend fun write(c: BluetoothGattCharacteristic, value: ByteArray) =\n    queue.run(timeout = 5.seconds) { gatt.writeCharacteristic(c, value, WRITE_TYPE_DEFAULT) == BluetoothStatusCodes.SUCCESS }\n```\n\n- Service discovery, MTU request, enabling notifications (CCCD write), reads and writes all go through the queue.\n- A timeout fails the operation and usually the connection: disconnect, close, reconnect.\n- Full queue implementation: [Android](references/android.md).\n\n### 4. Never assume the main thread in BLE callbacks\n\nAndroid GATT callbacks arrive on a binder thread unless you pass a `Handler` or `Executor`.\niOS delegate calls arrive on the queue passed to `CBCentralManager`, or main when `nil`.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: mutates UI-facing state and a plain list from a binder thread\noverride fun onCharacteristicChanged(g: BluetoothGatt, c: BluetoothGattCharacteristic, value: ByteArray) {\n    buffer.add(value); uiState.items += parse(value)\n}\n\n// GOOD: hand off to one serial context; StateFlow exposes the result\noverride fun onCharacteristicChanged(g: BluetoothGatt, c: BluetoothGattCharacteristic, value: ByteArray) {\n    inbound.trySend(value.copyOf())   // Channel consumed on a limitedParallelism(1) dispatcher\n}\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD: BLE work on the main queue blocks UI during bursts of notifications\ncentral = CBCentralManager(delegate: self, queue: nil)\n\n// GOOD: dedicated serial queue; hop to the main actor only for UI state\ncentral = CBCentralManager(delegate: self, queue: DispatchQueue(label: \"ble.central\"))\nTask { @MainActor in model.apply(event) }\n```\n\n### 5. Use the value-carrying Android 13+ APIs; never read `characteristic.value` on 33+\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: deprecated in API 33, not memory safe, value can change under you\ncharacteristic.value = bytes\ngatt.writeCharacteristic(characteristic)\n\n// GOOD\nval status = if (Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= 33) {\n    gatt.writeCharacteristic(characteristic, bytes, BluetoothGattCharacteristic.WRITE_TYPE_DEFAULT)\n} else {\n    legacyWrite(gatt, characteristic, bytes)   // @Suppress(\"DEPRECATION\") helper: sets value, calls old API\n}\n```\n\n- Override both `onCharacteristicChanged` overloads when `minSdk < 33`; use the `value` parameter on 33+.\n- API 37: prefer `connectGatt(BluetoothGattConnectionSettings, Executor, callback)` behind a version check.\n\n### 6. Always close the GATT, and treat status 133 as \"close, back off, retry\"\n\nStatus 133 (0x85) is a generic stack error with no public constant. It shows up on connect failures,\nstale caches and too many open clients.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: reconnect on the same object, never closed, leaks a client interface per attempt\nif (status != BluetoothGatt.GATT_SUCCESS) gatt.connect()\n\n// GOOD\noverride fun onConnectionStateChange(gatt: BluetoothGatt, status: Int, newState: Int) {\n    if (status != BluetoothGatt.GATT_SUCCESS || newState == BluetoothProfile.STATE_DISCONNECTED) {\n        gatt.close()\n        events.trySend(LinkEvent.Dropped(status))   // state machine schedules retry with backoff\n        return\n    }\n    if (newState == BluetoothProfile.STATE_CONNECTED) events.trySend(LinkEvent.Connected)\n}\n```\n\n- Backoff: for example 1 s, 2 s, 4 s, capped, with a maximum attempt count, then surface an error.\n- `disconnect()` is asynchronous. Call `close()` once you no longer need the object, including after timeouts.\n\n### 7. Size every write from the negotiated MTU\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: assumes 512-byte packets because MTU was \"requested\"\ngatt.requestMtu(517); send(chunk512)\n\n// GOOD: payload per ATT write is MTU - 3, taken from onMtuChanged\noverride fun onMtuChanged(gatt: BluetoothGatt, mtu: Int, status: Int) {\n    maxPayload = if (status == BluetoothGatt.GATT_SUCCESS) mtu - 3 else 20\n}\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD: iOS negotiates MTU itself; ask per write type\nlet maxPayload = peripheral.maximumWriteValueLength(for: .withoutResponse)\n```\n\n- Default ATT MTU is 23 bytes (20 bytes of payload). A single attribute value is at most 512 bytes.\n- Request a high connection priority only for bulk transfer, then go back to `CONNECTION_PRIORITY_BALANCED`.\n\n### 8. Respect flow control on writes without response\n\n```swift\n// BAD: loop floods the stack; packets are dropped with no error\nfor chunk in chunks { peripheral.writeValue(chunk, for: tx, type: .withoutResponse) }\n\n// GOOD\nfunc pump() {\n    while peripheral.canSendWriteWithoutResponse, let chunk = outbox.popFirst() {\n        peripheral.writeValue(chunk, for: tx, type: .withoutResponse)\n    }\n}\nfunc peripheralIsReady(toSendWriteWithoutResponse peripheral: CBPeripheral) { pump() }\n```\n\n- Android: keep no-response writes in the queue too; treat `ERROR_GATT_WRITE_REQUEST_BUSY` as back-pressure.\n- Use writes with response, or an application-level ACK, for anything that must not be lost.\n\n### 9. Model the connection as a sealed state machine\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: booleans that drift apart\nvar isConnected = false; var isConnecting = false; var servicesReady = false\n\n// GOOD\nsealed interface Link {\n    data object Idle : Link\n    data class Connecting(val attempt: Int) : Link\n    data object Discovering : Link\n    data class Ready(val maxPayload: Int) : Link\n    data class Backoff(val attempt: Int, val reason: Int) : Link\n    data class Failed(val reason: FailReason) : Link\n}\n```\n\n```swift\nenum Link: Sendable { case idle, connecting(attempt: Int), discovering, ready(maxPayload: Int), backoff(attempt: Int), failed(FailReason) }\n```\n\n- One function computes the next state from (state, event). Illegal transitions are logged and ignored.\n- Commands are only accepted in `Ready`. UI observes the state; it never drives callbacks directly.\n\n### 10. Frame, number and check every message; never trust device input\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: length byte from the device used as-is\nval len = data[1].toInt()\nval payload = data.copyOfRange(2, 2 + len)\n\n// GOOD: bounds-check every length, verify CRC, reject and count bad frames\nfun parse(frame: ByteArray): Frame? {\n    if (frame.size < HEADER + CRC_SIZE) return null\n    val len = frame[2].toInt() and 0xFF\n    if (len > MAX_PAYLOAD || HEADER + len + CRC_SIZE != frame.size) return null\n    if (crc16(frame, 0, HEADER + len) != frame.readU16(HEADER + len)) return null\n    return Frame(seq = frame[1].toInt() and 0xFF, payload = frame.copyOfRange(HEADER, HEADER + len))\n}\n```\n\n- CRC16 or CRC32 detects corruption, not tampering. Authenticity needs a signature or MAC (rule 13).\n- Sequence numbers detect loss, duplicates and reordering across chunked transfers.\n- Layout, CRC variant and limits come from the firmware document. See [Protocol and DFU](references/protocol-and-dfu.md).\n\n### 11. Reconnect the platform way\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: background scan loop to find a known device again\nwhile (true) { scanner.startScan(cb); delay(5_000); scanner.stopScan(cb) }\n\n// GOOD: autoConnect for a known device; the stack waits for it to come back in range (API 37: settings overload)\ngatt = device.connectGatt(context, /* autoConnect = */ true, callback, BluetoothDevice.TRANSPORT_LE)\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD: retrieve by stored identifier and connect; the request does not time out\nif let p = central.retrievePeripherals(withIdentifiers: [savedID]).first {\n    p.delegate = self\n    central.connect(p)\n}\n```\n\n- Use direct connect (`autoConnect = false`) for the first, user-initiated connection; it is faster.\n- iOS identifiers are per-app UUIDs, not MAC addresses. Store them, do not try to derive them.\n\n### 12. Keep long-lived links alive with the supported mechanism\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: start a service from a BLE callback while in the background (throws on Android 12+)\noverride fun onCharacteristicChanged(...) { context.startForegroundService(intent) }\n\n// GOOD: start the connectedDevice service while the app is visible, or use Companion Device Manager\nstartForeground(id, notification, ServiceInfo.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_TYPE_CONNECTED_DEVICE)\n```\n\n```xml\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE\" />\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_CONNECTED_DEVICE\" />\n<service android:name=\".DeviceLinkService\" android:exported=\"false\"\n    android:foregroundServiceType=\"connectedDevice\" />\n```\n\n- For devices that stay paired for months, prefer Companion Device Manager presence over a permanent service.\n- iOS: `UIBackgroundModes` with `bluetooth-central` plus `CBCentralManagerOptionRestoreIdentifierKey`.\n  A user force quit stops relaunch. Details: [Android](references/android.md), [iOS](references/ios.md).\n\n### 13. Firmware updates must never brick the device\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: blind stream, no checks, phone at 5 %, image trusted because the CRC matched\ndfu.send(image)\n\n// GOOD: preconditions, signed image verified by the bootloader, resumable, verified after reboot\nrequire(battery.phone >= 30 && deviceInfo.battery >= 50) { \"battery too low\" }   // thresholds from firmware team\nrequire(sha256(image).contentEquals(release.sha256))     // download integrity\ndfu.send(image, resumeFrom = device.confirmedOffset())   // device verifies the vendor signature\ncheck(device.reportedVersion() == release.version)\n```\n\n- Dual-bank or swap with rollback on the device; the old image stays bootable until the new one is confirmed.\n- Never downgrade unless the firmware explicitly allows it (anti-rollback).\n\n### 14. Pair securely and keep keys and personal data out of logs\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD\nLog.d(\"BLE\", \"connected \${device.address} key=\${sessionKey.toHex()} payload=\${value.toHex()}\")\n\n// GOOD: redacted identifiers, no keys, payload sizes not contents\nlog.debug { \"connected dev=\${device.address.redact()} mtu=\$mtu len=\${value.size}\" }\n```\n\n- Require LE Secure Connections with Numeric Comparison, Passkey Entry or OOB. \"Just Works\" has no\n  protection against man-in-the-middle during pairing.\n- Encrypt and authenticate application data end to end when the link alone is not trusted.\n- MAC addresses, serial numbers and device names can be personal data (GDPR). Do not ship them in analytics.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Calling `gatt.connect()` again after an error instead of `close()` and a fresh `connectGatt`.\n- `Thread.sleep` or fixed delays between GATT calls instead of a queue that waits for callbacks.\n- Assuming the MTU you requested is the MTU you got.\n- Catching every failure as \"device not found\" so status codes never reach logs or the state machine.\n- Scanning without filters, without a timeout, or restarting scans every few seconds.\n- Reading characteristic values from shared objects on Android 13+ instead of the callback parameter.\n- Treating CRC as security, or a device's length field as trusted.\n- A DFU flow that can start on low battery, cannot resume, or accepts unsigned images.\n- Storing the Android MAC address as the cross-platform device ID; iOS never exposes it.\n- Building a protocol from sniffed traffic and guesses instead of the firmware document.\n- Declaring `ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION`, `BLUETOOTH_PRIVILEGED` or extra permissions \"just in case\".\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Firmware protocol document located; every opcode, length and CRC variant traced to it\n- [ ] Manifest: API 31+ permissions, legacy ones capped at `maxSdkVersion=\"30\"`, location only if needed\n- [ ] Runtime permission and Bluetooth-off states handled with a user-visible path\n- [ ] `Info.plist`: `NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription`; `bluetooth-central` only if background is required\n- [ ] Scans use filters, a time limit and stop on match\n- [ ] Every GATT operation goes through one queue with a timeout\n- [ ] No callback touches UI or unsynchronised state; one serial context owns the link\n- [ ] Android 13+ value-carrying APIs used, with fallbacks below API 33\n- [ ] `close()` on every disconnect, failure and timeout; status 133 retried with capped backoff\n- [ ] Payload size taken from the negotiated MTU or `maximumWriteValueLength(for:)`\n- [ ] Write-without-response respects flow control\n- [ ] Connection modelled as sealed states; commands only in `Ready`\n- [ ] Every inbound length bounds-checked; CRC verified; sequence numbers checked\n- [ ] Reconnect uses `autoConnect`, CDM or `retrievePeripherals(withIdentifiers:)`, not scan loops\n- [ ] Foreground service type `connectedDevice` declared with its permission, or CDM used\n- [ ] DFU: battery check, signed image verified on device, resume, rollback, version check after reboot\n- [ ] Pairing uses LE Secure Connections with an authenticated method where the device supports it\n- [ ] Logs and analytics contain no keys, tokens, raw payloads or unredacted device identifiers\n- [ ] BLE layer behind an interface with fakes; tested on real phones from more than one vendor\n\n## Output format\n\nFor a review or design check:\n\n~~~markdown\n## BLE review: <app / module / branch>\n\nScope: <files>. Platforms: <Android | iOS | Flutter>. Firmware doc: <name and version, or \"missing\">.\n\n### Findings\n\n1. [Blocker] Android -- ble/GattClient.kt:88 -- reconnect calls gatt.connect() after status 133, gatt never closed\n   Why: leaks a GATT client per attempt; after a few failures every connect returns 133 until Bluetooth restarts\n   Fix:\n   ```kotlin\n   gatt.close(); stateMachine.on(LinkEvent.Dropped(status))\n   ```\n\n2. [Major] iOS -- BLE/Central.swift:41 -- writes without response in a loop, no canSendWriteWithoutResponse check\n   Why: chunks are dropped silently during DFU\n   Fix: pump from peripheralIsReady(toSendWriteWithoutResponse:)\n\n### Protocol\n| Check | Status | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Length fields bounds-checked | Missing | `FrameParser.kt:22` trusts `data[1]` |\n| CRC verified on every frame | Met | `Crc16.kt`, CRC-16/CCITT-FALSE per firmware doc 2.3 |\n\n### Manifest, Info.plist and background changes\n- <permission / key / service type added or required, with store-review impact>\n\n### Questions\n- <anything the firmware document does not define>\n\n### Verdict\nRequest changes -- 1 Blocker, 1 Major.\n~~~\n\nSeverity: Blocker (bricked device, data corruption, security or privacy breach, crash), Major (lost data,\nstuck connection, battery drain, wrong behaviour on common phones), Minor, Nit. For a new protocol, the\nagent fills the protocol checklist from [Protocol and DFU](references/protocol-and-dfu.md) instead.\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-security` -- MASVS checks for pairing, key storage, logging and exported components\n- `mobile-eu-compliance` -- CRA and EN 18031 duties for companion apps, secure update evidence\n- `mobile-kotlin`, `mobile-swift`, `mobile-flutter` -- language rules for the BLE layer code\n- `mobile-testing` -- fakes, virtual time and device-lab strategy\n- `mobile-performance` -- battery cost of scans, connection priority and background work\n- `mobile-code-review` -- general review dimensions alongside the BLE-specific findings\n",
    "references/android.md":
        "# Android BLE\n\nLoad this for any Kotlin or Java BLE code on Android. Facts below were checked against the\ndeveloper.android.com reference and behaviour-change pages; re-check them when `targetSdk` moves.\n\n## Permissions by API level\n\n| API level | Scan | Connect, read, write | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 31 and higher | `BLUETOOTH_SCAN` (runtime) | `BLUETOOTH_CONNECT` (runtime) | `neverForLocation` removes the location need; some beacons are filtered |\n| 29-30 | `ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION` + `BLUETOOTH_ADMIN` | `BLUETOOTH` | Background scanning from a service also needs `ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION` |\n| 28 and lower | `ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION` or fine + `BLUETOOTH_ADMIN` | `BLUETOOTH` | |\n\n- `BLUETOOTH_ADVERTISE` only if the phone advertises (peripheral role).\n- `startScan` returns no results without the location permission on API 30 and lower, with no error.\n- `<uses-feature android:name=\"android.hardware.bluetooth_le\" android:required=\"false\"/>` keeps the app\n  installable on devices without BLE; then check `PackageManager.FEATURE_BLUETOOTH_LE` at runtime.\n\n```kotlin\nprivate val blePermissions: Array<String> =\n    if (Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= Build.VERSION_CODES.S) {\n        arrayOf(Manifest.permission.BLUETOOTH_SCAN, Manifest.permission.BLUETOOTH_CONNECT)\n    } else {\n        arrayOf(Manifest.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION)\n    }\n\nprivate val launcher = registerForActivityResult(ActivityResultContracts.RequestMultiplePermissions()) { result ->\n    viewModel.onPermissionsResult(granted = result.values.all { it })\n}\n\nfun requestBle() = launcher.launch(blePermissions)\n```\n\nBluetooth off is a separate state. Ask the user to enable it with `BluetoothAdapter.ACTION_REQUEST_ENABLE`\n(needs `BLUETOOTH_CONNECT` on API 31+), and listen for `BluetoothAdapter.ACTION_STATE_CHANGED`.\n\n## Scanning\n\n```kotlin\nclass Scanner(private val adapter: BluetoothAdapter, private val scope: CoroutineScope) {\n    @RequiresPermission(Manifest.permission.BLUETOOTH_SCAN)\n    fun scan(service: UUID, timeout: Duration = 10.seconds): Flow<ScanResult> = callbackFlow {\n        val scanner = adapter.bluetoothLeScanner ?: run { close(BluetoothOffException()); return@callbackFlow }\n        val cb = object : ScanCallback() {\n            override fun onScanResult(callbackType: Int, result: ScanResult) { trySend(result) }\n            override fun onScanFailed(errorCode: Int) { close(ScanFailedException(errorCode)) }\n        }\n        val filters = listOf(ScanFilter.Builder().setServiceUuid(ParcelUuid(service)).build())\n        val settings = ScanSettings.Builder().setScanMode(ScanSettings.SCAN_MODE_BALANCED).build()\n        scanner.startScan(filters, settings, cb)\n        val timer = scope.launch { delay(timeout); close() }\n        awaitClose { timer.cancel(); scanner.stopScan(cb) }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `getBluetoothLeScanner()` returns null while Bluetooth is off.\n- Scan modes: `SCAN_MODE_LOW_POWER` (default, and forced when the app is not in the foreground),\n  `SCAN_MODE_BALANCED`, `SCAN_MODE_LOW_LATENCY` (foreground only), `SCAN_MODE_OPPORTUNISTIC`.\n- `onScanFailed` codes include `SCAN_FAILED_ALREADY_STARTED` (1) and `SCAN_FAILED_SCANNING_TOO_FREQUENTLY` (6).\n  Throttling can also show up as a scan that simply returns nothing. The limit is not documented; the widely\n  reported figure is five starts in 30 seconds. Debounce user-triggered rescans.\n- Process not running: `startScan(filters, settings, pendingIntent)` delivers results through the\n  `PendingIntent` with `EXTRA_LIST_SCAN_RESULT`. The peripheral must be advertising. From a receiver, start\n  short work with WorkManager; Android 12+ blocks starting a foreground service from the background.\n\n## The GATT operation queue\n\n```kotlin\nsealed interface GattResult {\n    data class Ok(val value: ByteArray = ByteArray(0)) : GattResult\n    data class Error(val status: Int) : GattResult\n    data object NotStarted : GattResult\n    data object Timeout : GattResult\n}\n\nclass GattQueue {\n    private val mutex = Mutex()\n    private val pending = AtomicReference<CompletableDeferred<GattResult>?>(null)\n\n    // start() issues the platform call and returns true if it was accepted.\n    suspend fun run(timeout: Duration, start: () -> Boolean): GattResult = mutex.withLock {\n        val deferred = CompletableDeferred<GattResult>()\n        pending.set(deferred)\n        try {\n            if (!start()) return GattResult.NotStarted\n            withTimeoutOrNull(timeout) { deferred.await() } ?: GattResult.Timeout\n        } finally {\n            pending.set(null)\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Called from the binder thread by BluetoothGattCallback.\n    fun complete(result: GattResult) { pending.get()?.complete(result) }\n}\n```\n\n```kotlin\nprivate val callback = object : BluetoothGattCallback() {\n    override fun onCharacteristicWrite(g: BluetoothGatt, c: BluetoothGattCharacteristic, status: Int) =\n        queue.complete(if (status == BluetoothGatt.GATT_SUCCESS) GattResult.Ok() else GattResult.Error(status))\n\n    override fun onCharacteristicRead(g: BluetoothGatt, c: BluetoothGattCharacteristic, value: ByteArray, status: Int) =\n        queue.complete(if (status == BluetoothGatt.GATT_SUCCESS) GattResult.Ok(value.copyOf()) else GattResult.Error(status))\n\n    override fun onDescriptorWrite(g: BluetoothGatt, d: BluetoothGattDescriptor, status: Int) =\n        queue.complete(if (status == BluetoothGatt.GATT_SUCCESS) GattResult.Ok() else GattResult.Error(status))\n\n    override fun onMtuChanged(g: BluetoothGatt, mtu: Int, status: Int) {\n        if (status == BluetoothGatt.GATT_SUCCESS) negotiatedMtu.set(mtu)   // AtomicInteger, default 23\n        queue.complete(if (status == BluetoothGatt.GATT_SUCCESS) GattResult.Ok() else GattResult.Error(status))\n    }\n\n    override fun onServicesDiscovered(g: BluetoothGatt, status: Int) =\n        queue.complete(if (status == BluetoothGatt.GATT_SUCCESS) GattResult.Ok() else GattResult.Error(status))\n}\n```\n\n- A disconnect must fail the pending operation: call `queue.complete(GattResult.Error(status))` from\n  `onConnectionStateChange` before closing, so the caller does not wait for the timeout.\n- Timeouts: 5-10 s for reads and writes and 15-30 s for service discovery are common starting points.\n  Agree values with the firmware team; a slow device is a protocol fact, not a guess.\n- Never call the next GATT method from inside a callback directly. Return to the queue.\n\n## Android 13+ signatures (API 33)\n\n| Deprecated in 33 | Use instead |\n|---|---|\n| `boolean writeCharacteristic(c)` | `int writeCharacteristic(c, byte[] value, int writeType)` returning `BluetoothStatusCodes` |\n| `boolean writeDescriptor(d)` | `int writeDescriptor(d, byte[] value)` |\n| `onCharacteristicChanged(gatt, c)` | `onCharacteristicChanged(gatt, c, byte[] value)` |\n| `onCharacteristicRead(gatt, c, status)` | `onCharacteristicRead(gatt, c, byte[] value, int status)` |\n| `onDescriptorRead(gatt, d, status)` | `onDescriptorRead(gatt, d, int status, byte[] value)` |\n\nReturn values of the new write: `SUCCESS`, `ERROR_MISSING_BLUETOOTH_CONNECT_PERMISSION`,\n`ERROR_PROFILE_SERVICE_NOT_BOUND`, `ERROR_GATT_WRITE_NOT_ALLOWED`, `ERROR_GATT_WRITE_REQUEST_BUSY` (201),\n`ERROR_UNKNOWN`.\n\n```kotlin\n// Enable notifications: local flag plus the CCCD write on the peripheral\n@RequiresPermission(Manifest.permission.BLUETOOTH_CONNECT)\nsuspend fun enableNotifications(c: BluetoothGattCharacteristic): GattResult {\n    if (!gatt.setCharacteristicNotification(c, true)) return GattResult.NotStarted\n    val cccd = c.getDescriptor(CCCD_UUID) ?: return GattResult.NotStarted   // 00002902-0000-1000-8000-00805f9b34fb\n    val value = BluetoothGattDescriptor.ENABLE_NOTIFICATION_VALUE\n    return queue.run(5.seconds) {\n        if (Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= 33) {\n            gatt.writeDescriptor(cccd, value) == BluetoothStatusCodes.SUCCESS\n        } else {\n            @Suppress(\"DEPRECATION\") run { cccd.value = value; gatt.writeDescriptor(cccd) }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nUse `ENABLE_INDICATION_VALUE` for indications. Notifications are not queued operations once enabled, but\nthe CCCD write is.\n\n## Threading\n\n- With `connectGatt(context, autoConnect, callback, transport)` callbacks run on an unspecified\n  background thread (binder). The overload with a `Handler` lets you choose; API 37 adds an `Executor`.\n- Pick one serial context to own link state, for example `Dispatchers.Default.limitedParallelism(1)` or a\n  `HandlerThread`. Push callback data into a `Channel` and process it there.\n- Copy byte arrays from callbacks before handing them off (`value.copyOf()`).\n- `MutableStateFlow` is safe to update from any thread, but read-modify-write sequences are not; use\n  `update { }` or the serial context.\n\n## Connecting, closing and status 133\n\n```kotlin\n@RequiresPermission(Manifest.permission.BLUETOOTH_CONNECT)\nfun open(device: BluetoothDevice, auto: Boolean): BluetoothGatt =\n    if (Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= 37) {\n        // API 37: build BluetoothGattConnectionSettings (check the builder API for your compileSdk)\n        device.connectGatt(settingsFor(auto), executor, callback)\n    } else {\n        @Suppress(\"DEPRECATION\")\n        device.connectGatt(context, auto, callback, BluetoothDevice.TRANSPORT_LE)\n    }\n```\n\n- Always pass `TRANSPORT_LE` for BLE peripherals; dual-mode phones otherwise may try classic first.\n- `connectGatt` returns a new client. Exactly one live `BluetoothGatt` per device; close the old one first.\n- Status values in `onConnectionStateChange`: `GATT_SUCCESS` (0), `GATT_INSUFFICIENT_AUTHENTICATION` (5),\n  `GATT_INSUFFICIENT_ENCRYPTION` (15), `GATT_CONNECTION_CONGESTED` (143), `GATT_CONNECTION_TIMEOUT` (147, API 35),\n  `GATT_FAILURE` (257). 133 (0x85) and other values outside this list are stack or controller codes with no\n  public constant. Log the\n  number; do not map it to a user message beyond \"could not connect\".\n- Retry policy for 133 and other connect failures: `close()`, wait with exponential backoff (for example\n  1, 2, 4, 8 s, cap 30 s), retry up to a limit, then report failure and stop.\n- Too many open clients exhaust the stack. Close on every path: disconnect, failure, timeout, scope cancel.\n\n```kotlin\nclass DeviceLink(...) : AutoCloseable {\n    override fun close() {\n        queue.complete(GattResult.Error(-1))\n        gatt?.disconnect()\n        gatt?.close()\n        gatt = null\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## autoConnect\n\n- `autoConnect = false`: direct connect, fails if the device is absent, no automatic reconnect. Use for the\n  first, user-initiated connection.\n- `autoConnect = true`: the stack reconnects when the device is available again, including after a\n  peripheral-initiated disconnect or loss of range. Slower to establish. Use for known devices.\n- Below Android 10 only one connection request runs at a time; from Android 10 requests are batched.\n- The connection is closed if the process is killed. Reconnect after restart from a service or CDM event.\n\n## MTU, connection priority, PHY\n\n- `requestMtu(517)` once, after connecting and before heavy traffic. From Android 14 the stack requests 517\n  on the first request from any client and ignores later requests. Use the `mtu` in `onMtuChanged`.\n- Payload per write or notification is `mtu - 3`.\n- `requestConnectionPriority(CONNECTION_PRIORITY_HIGH)` for bulk transfer such as DFU, then\n  `CONNECTION_PRIORITY_BALANCED`. `CONNECTION_PRIORITY_LOW_POWER` for idle links. `CONNECTION_PRIORITY_DCK`\n  (API 34) is for digital car keys only.\n- `setPreferredPhy` (2M PHY) can speed up transfers if both sides support it; result in `onPhyUpdate`.\n\n## Bonding\n\n- Many peripherals trigger bonding themselves when a protected characteristic is accessed\n  (`GATT_INSUFFICIENT_AUTHENTICATION` or `_ENCRYPTION`). Prefer that to calling `createBond()` blindly.\n- `createBond()` is asynchronous. Listen for `BluetoothDevice.ACTION_BOND_STATE_CHANGED` and `EXTRA_BOND_STATE`\n  (`BOND_BONDING`, `BOND_BONDED`, `BOND_NONE`). Queue no GATT work while `BOND_BONDING`.\n- Android 16: when a bonded device cannot be authenticated on reconnect, the system disconnects, keeps the\n  bond and shows a dialog. Apps targeting 16 can receive `ACTION_KEY_MISSING` and `ACTION_ENCRYPTION_CHANGE`.\n  OEM behaviour varies; keep the older bond-loss handling when `ACTION_KEY_MISSING` is not broadcast.\n- Android 16: `CompanionDeviceManager.removeBond(int)` removes the bond for a CDM-associated device.\n- Android 17: the system may re-pair automatically after bond loss. `ACTION_PAIRING_REQUEST` carries\n  `EXTRA_PAIRING_CONTEXT`, and `ACTION_KEY_MISSING` is sent only if automatic re-pairing fails.\n- `BluetoothDevice.setPin` is deprecated in API 37 and needs `BLUETOOTH_PRIVILEGED` for apps targeting 37.\n\n## Background and foreground services\n\n- Android 12+: no foreground service start from the background except listed exemptions, one of which is\n  an app using Companion Device Manager that declares `REQUEST_COMPANION_START_FOREGROUND_SERVICES_FROM_BACKGROUND`\n  (preferred) or `REQUEST_COMPANION_RUN_IN_BACKGROUND`.\n- Android 14+: declare `android:foregroundServiceType=\"connectedDevice\"` and `FOREGROUND_SERVICE_CONNECTED_DEVICE`.\n  The runtime prerequisite is one of: a granted `BLUETOOTH_CONNECT`, `BLUETOOTH_ADVERTISE` or `BLUETOOTH_SCAN`\n  (or the other options listed for this type, such as `CHANGE_NETWORK_STATE`).\n- Android 15: `connectedDevice` is not in the list of types a `BOOT_COMPLETED` receiver may not launch\n  (that list is `dataSync`, `camera`, `mediaPlayback`, `phoneCall`, `mediaProjection`, `microphone`).\n  The 6-hour limit applies to `dataSync` and `mediaProcessing`, not to `connectedDevice`.\n- Android 16: jobs that run while a foreground service is running now count against the job runtime quota.\n  Do not rely on WorkManager running long while your BLE service is up.\n\n```kotlin\nclass DeviceLinkService : LifecycleService() {\n    override fun onStartCommand(intent: Intent?, flags: Int, startId: Int): Int {\n        super.onStartCommand(intent, flags, startId)\n        ServiceCompat.startForeground(\n            this, NOTIFICATION_ID, buildNotification(),\n            ServiceInfo.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_TYPE_CONNECTED_DEVICE,\n        )\n        return START_STICKY\n    }\n}\n```\n\nStart it from a visible activity (for example when the user taps \"Connect\"), not from a BLE callback.\n\n## Companion Device Manager\n\n- `associate(AssociationRequest, Executor, Callback)` shows a system picker; the user selects the device.\n- `startObservingDevicePresence(ObservingDevicePresenceRequest)` (API 36; the `String` address overload,\n  API 31, is deprecated in 36) wakes your `CompanionDeviceService` when the device comes in or out of range.\n  Requires `REQUEST_OBSERVE_COMPANION_DEVICE_PRESENCE`.\n- Limits from the docs: limited filtering and no support for random MAC addresses. Peripherals with rotating\n  addresses should use a Resolvable Private Address and be bonded so the OS can resolve it.\n- Android 16: the app no longer gets `RESULT_DISCOVERY_TIMEOUT`; the user sees a dialog and the app receives\n  `RESULT_USER_REJECTED` when it is dismissed.\n\n## Logging\n\n- `BluetoothDevice.getAddress()` is a stable identifier on Android. Redact it in logs (`AA:BB:..:EF`) and do\n  not send it to analytics.\n- Use a debug-only HCI snoop log (Developer options) for packet-level debugging, never production logging.\n",
    "references/flutter.md":
        "# Flutter BLE (flutter_blue_plus)\n\nLoad this for Dart BLE code. API details were checked against the flutter_blue_plus 2.3.x pages and API\nreference on pub.dev, and permission_handler 13.x. Check `pubspec.lock` for the version the project uses;\nnames changed between 1.x and 2.x.\n\n## Licensing and supply chain (read first)\n\n- flutter_blue_plus 2.x uses its own FlutterBluePlus License, not BSD. Nonprofit, personal and educational\n  use is free; for-profit use needs a paid commercial license.\n- `connect` has a required `license:` argument: `License.nonprofit` or `License.commercial`\n  (`License.free` is deprecated, renamed to `nonprofit`).\n- The changelog (2.3.5) adds a license ping during the build that includes the package name and app name.\n- The Android package moved from `com.lib.flutter_blue_plus` to `com.jmx.flutter_blue_plus` in 2.3.9.\n  Update ProGuard or R8 keep rules that name the old package.\n- Flag these to the owner: licence cost, a build-time network call to a third party, and an SBOM entry with\n  a non-OSI licence. Do not pick the `license:` value on the owner's behalf.\n\n## Setup\n\nAndroid manifest (same rules as native, see [Android](android.md)):\n\n```xml\n<uses-feature android:name=\"android.hardware.bluetooth_le\" android:required=\"false\" />\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.BLUETOOTH_SCAN\" android:usesPermissionFlags=\"neverForLocation\" />\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.BLUETOOTH_CONNECT\" />\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.BLUETOOTH\" android:maxSdkVersion=\"30\" />\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.BLUETOOTH_ADMIN\" android:maxSdkVersion=\"30\" />\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION\" android:maxSdkVersion=\"30\" />\n```\n\nIf the app derives location from scans, drop `neverForLocation`, keep `ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION` uncapped and\npass `androidUsesFineLocation: true` to `startScan`.\n\niOS `Info.plist`: `NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription`; `UIBackgroundModes` with `bluetooth-central` only if\nneeded, plus `FlutterBluePlus.setOptions(restoreState: true)` before any other call.\n\n## Runtime permissions with permission_handler\n\n```dart\nFuture<bool> ensureBlePermissions() async {\n  if (Platform.isAndroid) {\n    final sdk = (await DeviceInfoPlugin().androidInfo).version.sdkInt;\n    final wanted = sdk >= 31\n        ? [Permission.bluetoothScan, Permission.bluetoothConnect]\n        : [Permission.locationWhenInUse];\n    final result = await wanted.request();\n    return result.values.every((s) => s.isGranted);\n  }\n  // iOS: the system prompt appears on first CoreBluetooth use; permission_handler reports the status.\n  return (await Permission.bluetooth.request()).isGranted;\n}\n```\n\n- On Android, `permanentlyDenied` is only reported by `request()`, not by `status`. Offer `openAppSettings()`.\n- iOS with CocoaPods: permission_handler needs the `PERMISSION_BLUETOOTH=1` macro in the Podfile\n  `post_install` block; with Swift Package Manager the `Info.plist` key is enough. Check the plugin README\n  for the exact macro name for your version.\n- `DeviceInfoPlugin` is from device_info_plus; any way of reading the SDK level works.\n\n## Adapter state and scanning\n\n```dart\nif (await FlutterBluePlus.isSupported == false) return BleUnavailable();\n\nawait FlutterBluePlus.adapterState\n    .where((s) => s == BluetoothAdapterState.on)\n    .first\n    .timeout(const Duration(seconds: 5));      // iOS starts in `unknown`\n\nfinal sub = FlutterBluePlus.onScanResults.listen(\n  (results) { if (results.isNotEmpty) _onFound(results.last); },\n  onError: (Object e) => _onScanError(e),\n);\nFlutterBluePlus.cancelWhenScanComplete(sub);\n\nawait FlutterBluePlus.startScan(\n  withServices: [Guid(serviceUuid)],\n  timeout: const Duration(seconds: 10),\n  androidScanMode: AndroidScanMode.balanced,  // default is lowLatency\n);\nawait FlutterBluePlus.isScanning.where((s) => s == false).first;\n```\n\n- `onScanResults` clears between scans; `scanResults` keeps previous results.\n- Filters combine with OR (`withServices`, `withNames`, `withRemoteIds`, `withMsd`, `withServiceData`).\n- `FlutterBluePlus.turnOn()` is Android only.\n\n## Connecting\n\n```dart\nfinal device = result.device;\n\nfinal stateSub = device.connectionState.listen((s) {\n  if (s == BluetoothConnectionState.disconnected) {\n    _link.add(LinkEvent.dropped(device.disconnectReason?.code));\n  }\n});\ndevice.cancelWhenDisconnected(stateSub, delayed: true, next: true);\n\nawait device.connect(\n  license: License.commercial,                  // chosen by the owner, see Licensing\n  timeout: const Duration(seconds: 15),\n  mtu: 512,                                     // Android only; ignored on iOS\n);\nfinal services = await device.discoverServices();   // after every connection\n```\n\n- `connect(autoConnect: true, mtu: null)` reconnects whenever the device is found and returns immediately;\n  `autoConnect` cannot be combined with `mtu`, so call `requestMtu` after connecting. Listen to\n  `connectionState` to know when it actually connects. Calling `disconnect()` turns auto connect off.\n- `remoteId` is the MAC address on Android and a system UUID on iOS. Never use it as a cross-platform key.\n- `FlutterBluePlus.events.onConnectionStateChanged` observes every device; useful for a single link manager.\n\n## MTU, writes and notifications\n\n```dart\n// Android: request explicitly if not done in connect(); iOS negotiates on its own\nif (Platform.isAndroid) await device.requestMtu(512);\nfinal maxPayload = device.mtuNow - 3;\n\nfor (final chunk in frames(bytes, maxPayload)) {\n  await rx.write(chunk, withoutResponse: false, timeout: 5);\n}\n\nawait tx.setNotifyValue(true);\nfinal notifySub = tx.onValueReceived.listen(_onBytes);\ndevice.cancelWhenDisconnected(notifySub);\n```\n\n- `requestMtu(desiredMtu, {predelay, timeout})` is Android only. `device.mtu` is a stream; `mtuNow` the value.\n- `write(value, {withoutResponse = false, allowLongWrite = false, timeout = 15})`. `withoutResponse: true`\n  always returns success immediately; delivery is not guaranteed. `allowLongWrite` allows up to 512 bytes\n  with response but can leave partial data if interrupted; prefer your own framing.\n- `onValueReceived` fires for reads and notifications only. `lastValueStream` also fires on writes and\n  re-emits the last value on listen, which makes it easy to process a stale packet twice.\n- `createBond`, `removeBond`, `bondState`, `requestConnectionPriority` and `clearGattCache` are Android only.\n- `FlutterBluePlus.setOperationQueueMode` (2.3.0) controls how operations are queued across devices. The\n  plugin serialises calls per device, but your protocol still needs one outstanding command at a time.\n\n## Structure\n\n- Wrap flutter_blue_plus in a `BleClient` interface in the data layer. Widgets and view models never import\n  the plugin. This is also the seam for fakes; see [Testing](testing.md).\n- Cancel every `StreamSubscription` (use `cancelWhenDisconnected` and `cancelWhenScanComplete`), and dispose\n  the link manager with its owner.\n- Platform channels deliver results on the main isolate. Heavy parsing of large transfers (DFU images,\n  logs) goes to `Isolate.run`.\n\n```dart\nabstract interface class BleClient {\n  Stream<LinkState> get state;\n  Future<void> connect(String deviceId);\n  Future<Uint8List> request(Uint8List command, {Duration timeout});\n  Stream<Uint8List> get notifications;\n  Future<void> disconnect();\n}\n```\n\n## Platform differences to design for\n\n| Topic | Android | iOS |\n|---|---|---|\n| Device id | MAC address, stable | System UUID per phone, can change |\n| MTU | Requested by the app | Negotiated by the system |\n| Bonding API | `createBond`, `removeBond` | None; triggered by encrypted characteristics |\n| Turn adapter on | `turnOn()` | Not possible; ask the user |\n| Background | Foreground service or CDM (native code) | `bluetooth-central` + `restoreState` |\n| Location permission | API 30 and lower, or if not `neverForLocation` | Never needed for BLE |\n\nBackground work on Android (foreground service type `connectedDevice`, Companion Device Manager) is native\ncode; add it in the Android host project and follow [Android](android.md).\n",
    "references/ios.md":
        "# iOS BLE (CoreBluetooth central)\n\nLoad this for Swift code that uses `CBCentralManager` and `CBPeripheral`. Facts were checked against\ndeveloper.apple.com (CoreBluetooth reference, `Info.plist` keys, TN3115).\n\n## Info.plist, capabilities and review\n\n| Key or setting | When | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription` | Always, iOS 13+ | Required if the app uses Bluetooth. A deployment target below 13 also needs `NSBluetoothPeripheralUsageDescription` |\n| `UIBackgroundModes` = `bluetooth-central` | Only if BLE work continues in the background | Added via Background Modes capability. App Review asks why; explain the user-visible feature |\n| `UIBackgroundModes` = `bluetooth-peripheral` | Only if the phone advertises or serves GATT in the background | Rare for companion apps |\n\n- Write the usage string around the user benefit (\"Connect to your thermostat to change settings\"),\n  not \"This app uses Bluetooth\".\n- Every new key or background mode is an `Info.plist` change with App Review impact. Call it out in the MR.\n- iOS 18+: AccessorySetupKit offers a system picker for accessories with app-provided names and images.\n  Consider it for new products; per TN3115, iOS 26 limits some background relaunch cases to apps that\n  set up their accessories with AccessorySetupKit.\n\n## Authorization and power state\n\n```swift\nswitch CBManager.authorization {          // iOS 13.1+, class property\ncase .notDetermined: break                // creating a CBCentralManager triggers the prompt\ncase .denied, .restricted: state = .bluetoothDenied\ncase .allowedAlways: break\n@unknown default: break\n}\n```\n\n- `centralManagerDidUpdateState(_:)` is the gate. Issue no scans or connects until `.poweredOn`.\n- On `.poweredOff`, `.resetting` or `.unauthorized` every peripheral is invalid. Move the state machine to\n  `Idle` and rebuild from stored identifiers when power returns.\n\n## Threading\n\n- `CBCentralManager(delegate:queue:options:)`: `queue` is where all central and peripheral delegate calls\n  arrive. `nil` means the main queue.\n- Use one serial `DispatchQueue` for the manager and every peripheral it owns. Confine all BLE state to it.\n- Swift 6: the delegate is an `NSObject` subclass. Either keep it a `final class` whose mutable state is only\n  touched on the BLE queue (and say so in a comment where you mark it `@unchecked Sendable`), or forward\n  events into an `AsyncStream` consumed by an actor. Hop to `@MainActor` only for UI state.\n\n```swift\nfinal class BleCentral: NSObject, @unchecked Sendable {   // all mutable state confined to `queue`\n    private let queue = DispatchQueue(label: \"com.example.ble\")\n    private lazy var central = CBCentralManager(\n        delegate: self, queue: queue,\n        options: [CBCentralManagerOptionRestoreIdentifierKey: \"com.example.ble.central\"]\n    )\n    private let continuation: AsyncStream<LinkEvent>.Continuation\n    let events: AsyncStream<LinkEvent>\n\n    override init() {\n        let (stream, cont) = AsyncStream.makeStream(of: LinkEvent.self)\n        events = stream\n        continuation = cont\n        super.init()\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Scanning\n\n```swift\ncentral.scanForPeripherals(withServices: [serviceUUID],\n                           options: [CBCentralManagerScanOptionAllowDuplicatesKey: false])\n```\n\n- Pass service UUIDs. `nil` returns everything in the foreground and does not work in the background.\n- In the background, scan options have no effect; duplicates are coalesced.\n- Allowing duplicates costs battery; use it only for live RSSI or advertisement payload updates.\n- Stop with `stopScan()` once found and on a timer you own.\n\n## Connecting and reconnecting\n\n- `connect(_:options:)` never times out. Keep a strong reference to the `CBPeripheral`; deallocating it\n  cancels the connection. Add your own timeout for user-initiated connects and cancel with\n  `cancelPeripheralConnection(_:)`.\n- A pending `connect` to a known peripheral is the iOS way to reconnect when it comes back in range. It also\n  survives suspension and, with restoration, termination by the system.\n- Known devices: `retrievePeripherals(withIdentifiers:)` with the stored `UUID`. Devices connected by the\n  system or other apps: `retrieveConnectedPeripherals(withServices:)`, then `connect` locally.\n- `peripheral.identifier` is assigned by the system the first time this device sees the peer. It is not the\n  MAC address and differs between phones. Store it per phone; map it to a device serial read over GATT.\n- iOS 17+: `CBConnectPeripheralOptionEnableAutoReconnect` asks the system to reconnect after a drop;\n  disconnects then arrive in `centralManager(_:didDisconnectPeripheral:timestamp:isReconnecting:error:)`.\n\n```swift\nfunc reconnectKnown() {\n    guard central.state == .poweredOn else { return }\n    for p in central.retrievePeripherals(withIdentifiers: store.knownIDs) {\n        p.delegate = self\n        peripherals[p.identifier] = p          // strong reference\n        central.connect(p)\n    }\n}\n\nfunc centralManager(_ central: CBCentralManager, didConnect peripheral: CBPeripheral) {\n    continuation.yield(.connected(peripheral.identifier))\n    peripheral.discoverServices([serviceUUID])\n}\n\nfunc centralManager(_ central: CBCentralManager, didDisconnectPeripheral peripheral: CBPeripheral, error: Error?) {\n    continuation.yield(.dropped(peripheral.identifier, error.map(String.init(describing:))))\n    central.connect(peripheral)                // pending reconnect, no timeout\n}\n```\n\n## Discovery, notifications and serialization\n\n- Discover only the services and characteristics you need: `discoverServices([uuid])`,\n  `discoverCharacteristics([uuids], for:)`. After a reconnect, discover again unless restoration provided them.\n- `setNotifyValue(true, for:)`; confirm in `peripheral(_:didUpdateNotificationStateFor:error:)`.\n- CoreBluetooth queues requests internally, but your protocol usually needs request-response pairing.\n  Keep an application-level queue: one outstanding command, a timeout, then the next.\n- Handle `peripheral(_:didModifyServices:)`: the GATT table changed (for example after DFU). Rediscover.\n\n## Writes and MTU\n\n- `writeValue(_:for:type:)` with `.withResponse` calls `peripheral(_:didWriteValueFor:error:)`;\n  `.withoutResponse` gives no result and no error.\n- Flow control for `.withoutResponse`: send while `canSendWriteWithoutResponse` is true, resume in\n  `peripheralIsReady(toSendWriteWithoutResponse:)`.\n- `maximumWriteValueLength(for:)` is the largest single write for that write type, and the two types can\n  return different values. Ask for the type you actually use, after connect, and size chunks from it.\n- The app cannot request an MTU. iOS negotiates it; read the size after connect, not at launch.\n\n## State restoration and background\n\n- Opt in with `CBCentralManagerOptionRestoreIdentifierKey`. The string must be identical on every launch.\n- Scene-based apps get `nil` launch options, so you cannot read restored identifiers from them. Create the\n  central with the same identifier early on every launch (for example in the app delegate's launch method).\n- `centralManager(_:willRestoreState:)` delivers `CBCentralManagerRestoredStatePeripheralsKey` with\n  connected or pending peripherals, including discovered services and notification state when possible.\n  Set delegates and strong references there. If keys are missing, rebuild from your own stored state.\n- TN3115 relaunch rules (summarised; read the note for your case):\n  - Relaunched: removed from memory by the system, crashed, device restarted (after first unlock).\n  - Not relaunched: user force quit, Bluetooth power toggled in Settings.\n  - iOS 26 and later: for the cases marked with note 5 (force quit, Control Center Bluetooth toggle,\n    Airplane Mode), only apps that set up accessories with AccessorySetupKit are relaunched.\n  - Relaunch happens only for a pending event: an ongoing scan, a pending connect, or a subscribed\n    notification that fires.\n- In the background, keep work short. Each wake-up gets limited execution time; do not start long uploads\n  from a notification without a background task.\n\n```swift\nfunc centralManager(_ central: CBCentralManager, willRestoreState dict: [String: Any]) {\n    let restored = dict[CBCentralManagerRestoredStatePeripheralsKey] as? [CBPeripheral] ?? []\n    for p in restored {\n        p.delegate = self\n        peripherals[p.identifier] = p\n    }\n    continuation.yield(.restored(restored.map(\\.identifier)))\n}\n```\n\n## Pairing on iOS\n\n- Apps cannot start pairing directly. iOS pairs when the app accesses a characteristic that requires\n  encryption or authentication; the system shows the dialog.\n- Outside AccessorySetupKit (which supports removing an accessory's pairing), only the user can remove a\n  pairing in Settings. If the device lost its keys, the app must guide the user to \"Forget This Device\"\n  and pair again. Detect this from repeated encryption errors after connect.\n\n## Logging\n\n- Do not log `peripheral.identifier` together with account data in production. Redact payloads; log length,\n  opcode and status. Use `os.Logger` with `privacy: .private` for anything identifying.\n\n```swift\nlogger.debug(\"rx opcode=\\(opcode) len=\\(data.count) dev=\\(peripheral.identifier, privacy: .private)\")\n```\n",
    "references/protocol-and-dfu.md":
        "# Protocol design and firmware updates\n\nLoad this when designing, implementing or reviewing the byte-level protocol on top of GATT, or a firmware\nupdate (DFU) flow. The firmware specification is authoritative. Everything below marked \"example\" is a\npattern to compare against, not a format to assume. If the device document does not define a field,\ntiming or opcode, the agent asks instead of inventing one.\n\n## Example frame layout\n\n```text\nExample only. Real layout comes from the firmware document.\n\n 0        1        2        3 .. 3+len-1     3+len   3+len+1\n+--------+--------+--------+---------------+--------+--------+\n| opcode |  seq   |  len   |   payload     |  crc16 (LE)     |\n+--------+--------+--------+---------------+--------+--------+\ncrc16 = CRC-16/IBM-3740 (CCITT-FALSE) over bytes 0 .. 3+len-1\n```\n\nQuestions the firmware document must answer before code is written:\n\n| Topic | Question |\n|---|---|\n| Byte order | Little or big endian for every multi-byte field? |\n| Length | What does `len` count? Maximum value? Is a zero-length payload legal? |\n| CRC | Which variant (poly, init, reflect, xorout)? Over which bytes? Byte order of the CRC itself? |\n| Sequence | Width, wrap-around, per direction or shared, reset on reconnect? |\n| Chunking | Is a frame allowed to span several ATT packets? How is the first and last chunk marked? |\n| Acknowledgement | ACK per frame, per window, or none? What does a NACK carry? |\n| Timing | Response timeout per opcode, inter-packet gap, maximum time to apply a command |\n| Errors | Error codes and which ones are retryable |\n| Versioning | How the app learns the protocol version and what it does on mismatch |\n\n## Chunking above the MTU\n\n- Payload per ATT packet is `ATT_MTU - 3`. Default ATT_MTU is 23, so the safe minimum is 20 bytes.\n- A single attribute value is at most 512 bytes (Core spec, ATT). Anything larger must be framed by the\n  application.\n- Put a header on each chunk (example: frame id, chunk index, last-chunk flag) or a length prefix on the\n  frame so the receiver knows when it is complete.\n- Reassembly limits: maximum frame size, maximum chunks, and a timeout after which a partial frame is\n  dropped. Without limits a broken or hostile device can exhaust memory.\n\n```kotlin\nclass Reassembler(private val maxFrame: Int, private val timeout: Duration, private val clock: () -> Long) {\n    private var buffer = ByteArrayOutputStream()\n    private var expected = -1\n    private var startedAt = 0L\n\n    // Example chunk header: [0] = total length low byte, [1] = high byte on the first chunk only.\n    fun accept(chunk: ByteArray, first: Boolean): ByteArray? {\n        if (first) {\n            if (chunk.size < 2) return reset()\n            expected = (chunk[0].toInt() and 0xFF) or ((chunk[1].toInt() and 0xFF) shl 8)\n            if (expected == 0 || expected > maxFrame) return reset()\n            buffer = ByteArrayOutputStream(expected)\n            buffer.write(chunk, 2, chunk.size - 2)\n            startedAt = clock()\n        } else {\n            if (expected < 0 || clock() - startedAt > timeout.inWholeMilliseconds) return reset()\n            if (buffer.size() + chunk.size > expected) return reset()\n            buffer.write(chunk)\n        }\n        if (buffer.size() < expected) return null\n        return buffer.toByteArray().also { reset() }\n    }\n\n    private fun reset(): ByteArray? { expected = -1; buffer = ByteArrayOutputStream(); return null }\n}\n```\n\n## Never trust device input\n\n- Bounds-check every length, count and offset against both the received size and a fixed maximum.\n- Mask bytes before arithmetic: `b.toInt() and 0xFF` in Kotlin, `Int(b)` from `UInt8` in Swift,\n  `b & 0xFF` in Dart.\n- Reject unknown opcodes and versions; count rejects in a metric without logging payloads.\n- Strings from the device (names, versions) are untrusted: limit length, validate the charset, and never\n  build file paths, SQL or HTML from them.\n- Fuzz the parser in unit tests: random bytes, truncated frames, maximum lengths, wrong CRCs.\n\n```swift\n// Safe reads from Data without traps\nextension Data {\n    func u8(at i: Int) -> UInt8? { indices.contains(startIndex + i) ? self[startIndex + i] : nil }\n    func u16le(at i: Int) -> UInt16? {\n        guard let lo = u8(at: i), let hi = u8(at: i + 1) else { return nil }\n        return UInt16(lo) | (UInt16(hi) << 8)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nNote: `Data` slices keep the parent's indices. Always offset from `startIndex`, as above.\n\n## CRC implementations\n\nCRC detects accidental corruption. It does not detect a deliberate change: anyone can recompute it.\nUse a signature (firmware images) or a MAC or AEAD (commands, data) for authenticity.\n\nVerify any implementation with the standard check input `\"123456789\"` (ASCII):\n\n| Variant | Parameters | Check value |\n|---|---|---|\n| CRC-16/IBM-3740 (CCITT-FALSE) | poly 0x1021, init 0xFFFF, no reflection, xorout 0 | `0x29B1` |\n| CRC-16/XMODEM | poly 0x1021, init 0x0000, no reflection, xorout 0 | `0x31C3` |\n| CRC-16/KERMIT | poly 0x1021, init 0x0000, reflected, xorout 0 | `0x2189` |\n| CRC-16/MODBUS | poly 0x8005, init 0xFFFF, reflected, xorout 0 | `0x4B37` |\n| CRC-32 (ISO-HDLC, zlib) | poly 0x04C11DB7, init 0xFFFFFFFF, reflected, xorout 0xFFFFFFFF | `0xCBF43926` |\n\nKotlin, CRC-16/IBM-3740:\n\n```kotlin\nfun crc16Ccitt(data: ByteArray, from: Int = 0, to: Int = data.size): Int {\n    var crc = 0xFFFF\n    for (i in from until to) {\n        crc = crc xor ((data[i].toInt() and 0xFF) shl 8)\n        repeat(8) { crc = if (crc and 0x8000 != 0) (crc shl 1) xor 0x1021 else crc shl 1 }\n        crc = crc and 0xFFFF\n    }\n    return crc\n}\n\n// CRC-32: use the JDK\nfun crc32(data: ByteArray): Long = java.util.zip.CRC32().apply { update(data) }.value\n\n@Test fun crcCheckValues() {\n    val input = \"123456789\".toByteArray(Charsets.US_ASCII)\n    assertEquals(0x29B1, crc16Ccitt(input))\n    assertEquals(0xCBF43926L, crc32(input))\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n\n```swift\nfunc crc16Ccitt(_ data: some Sequence<UInt8>) -> UInt16 {\n    var crc: UInt16 = 0xFFFF\n    for byte in data {\n        crc ^= UInt16(byte) << 8\n        for _ in 0..<8 { crc = (crc & 0x8000) != 0 ? (crc << 1) ^ 0x1021 : crc << 1 }\n    }\n    return crc\n}\n// XCTAssertEqual(crc16Ccitt(Array(\"123456789\".utf8)), 0x29B1)\n```\n\nDart:\n\n```dart\nint crc16Ccitt(List<int> data) {\n  var crc = 0xFFFF;\n  for (final b in data) {\n    crc ^= (b & 0xFF) << 8;\n    for (var i = 0; i < 8; i++) {\n      crc = (crc & 0x8000) != 0 ? ((crc << 1) ^ 0x1021) & 0xFFFF : (crc << 1) & 0xFFFF;\n    }\n  }\n  return crc;\n}\n// expect(crc16Ccitt(ascii.encode('123456789')), 0x29B1);\n```\n\n## Sequence numbers, timeouts and retries\n\n- Each command carries a sequence number; the response echoes it. Drop responses whose number does not\n  match the outstanding command.\n- Duplicates (same number twice) are acknowledged again but not applied again. Commands that change state\n  should be idempotent or carry the sequence number so the device can deduplicate.\n- Wrap-around: compare with modular arithmetic, never with `<` on raw values.\n- Per-operation policy, written down with the firmware team:\n\n| Operation | Example timeout | Retries | On final failure |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Read device info | 5 s | 2 | Disconnect, reconnect |\n| Command with ACK | 3 s | 3, same sequence number | Report error to user |\n| DFU chunk | 2 s | 5, resend from last confirmed offset | Pause, resume later |\n| Service discovery | 20 s | 1 | Close, reconnect |\n\nValues in this table are examples. Use the timings from the firmware document.\n\n## Security of the link\n\n- Pairing: LE Secure Connections (Bluetooth 4.2+, P-256 ECDH). Only Numeric Comparison, Passkey Entry and\n  Out of Band give protection against man-in-the-middle during pairing. Just Works protects against\n  passive eavesdropping only (with LE Secure Connections) and offers no MITM protection.\n- If the product cannot use an authenticated pairing method, protect the application layer: a\n  per-device key provisioned at the factory or during onboarding, authenticated encryption (for example\n  AES-GCM) with a counter or nonce that never repeats, and replay protection on the device.\n- Never ship a shared key in the app binary. Store per-device secrets in the Android Keystore or iOS Keychain.\n- Characteristics that change device state require an encrypted link (permission level on the device side).\n- Logs: opcode, length, sequence number and status only. No keys, tokens, passwords, raw payloads, or\n  unredacted MAC addresses and serial numbers in production logs or analytics.\n\n## Firmware update (DFU) safety\n\nGoal: no sequence of user actions, radio drops or battery events leaves the device unbootable.\n\nDevice side (confirm with the firmware team; the app cannot compensate for missing pieces):\n- Bootloader verifies the image signature with a public key in protected storage before it boots it.\n- Dual-bank or swap-based update; the current image stays bootable until the new one is confirmed.\n- Rollback if the new image does not confirm itself after boot (watchdog or test-boot flag).\n- Anti-rollback: version counter so older, vulnerable images are rejected.\n- Resume: device reports the last confirmed offset so the app can continue after a drop.\n\nApp side:\n1. Download over HTTPS from your backend; verify the hash or signature metadata of the download.\n2. Check compatibility: hardware revision, current version, bootloader version, minimum app version.\n3. Preconditions: phone battery or charging, device battery above the firmware team's threshold,\n   Bluetooth on, user consent, screen-on or foreground service for the transfer on Android.\n4. Request high connection priority (Android) and the largest MTU; chunk from the negotiated size.\n5. Send chunks with flow control and per-chunk ACK or windowed ACK; persist the confirmed offset.\n6. On disconnect: back off, reconnect, query the offset, resume. Never restart from zero without asking\n   the device.\n7. After the device reboots: reconnect, rediscover services (the GATT table may change), read the version,\n   and confirm success only when it matches the release.\n8. Report progress and errors to the user in plain words. Do not let the user start a second update in\n   parallel.\n\n```kotlin\nsealed interface DfuState {\n    data object Checking : DfuState\n    data class Blocked(val reason: BlockReason) : DfuState      // low battery, incompatible, no consent\n    data class Transferring(val sent: Long, val total: Long) : DfuState\n    data class Paused(val confirmedOffset: Long) : DfuState\n    data object Validating : DfuState\n    data object Rebooting : DfuState\n    data class Done(val version: String) : DfuState\n    data class Failed(val error: DfuError, val deviceBootable: Boolean) : DfuState\n}\n```\n\nVendor DFU libraries (for example the SoC vendor's DFU or SMP/MCUmgr client) implement the transfer. The\nsafety properties above still have to be checked in the device design; a library cannot add signature\nverification to a bootloader that lacks it.\n\n## Protocol checklist\n\n- [ ] Firmware protocol document identified, with version, and every field traced to it\n- [ ] Byte order, length semantics, maximum lengths and CRC variant written down\n- [ ] Frames larger than `MTU - 3` chunked with a defined first and last marker or length prefix\n- [ ] Reassembly has a maximum size, a chunk limit and a timeout\n- [ ] Every length and offset bounds-checked; parser fuzzed\n- [ ] CRC verified with the standard check value in a unit test\n- [ ] Sequence numbers match responses to requests; duplicates not applied twice; wrap-around handled\n- [ ] Timeout and retry policy per operation agreed with the firmware team\n- [ ] Authenticity: signatures for firmware, MAC or AEAD for sensitive commands; CRC not used as security\n- [ ] Pairing method and link security level documented; no shared keys in the app\n- [ ] DFU: signed images verified by the bootloader, dual bank or rollback, anti-rollback, resume, battery check\n- [ ] Logs contain no keys, payloads or unredacted identifiers\n",
    "references/testing.md":
        "# Testing BLE code\n\nLoad this when adding tests for a BLE layer or planning device testing. Most BLE bugs are timing,\nordering and error-path bugs; they can be reproduced with fakes and virtual time long before a phone\ntouches real hardware.\n\n## What simulators and emulators can do\n\n| Environment | BLE support | Use it for |\n|---|---|---|\n| iOS Simulator | No Core Bluetooth radio; the central never reaches `.poweredOn` | UI and logic with a fake `BleClient` only |\n| Android Emulator, API 31+ images | Simulated Bluetooth (classic and BLE profiles) through the emulator's virtual radio | Permission flows, emulator-to-emulator experiments; not a substitute for your peripheral |\n| JVM / Swift / Dart unit tests | None | State machine, queue, parser, retry and backoff logic with fakes |\n| Real phones | Full | Everything that touches the stack: MTU, bonding, 133, background, DFU |\n\nRe-check the emulator documentation for your emulator version; Bluetooth support has changed across\nreleases. Treat any emulator result as \"logic works\", never as \"works on phones\".\n\n## Put the platform behind an interface\n\nThe app talks to a small interface. The platform implementation is thin; the logic above it (state\nmachine, queue, protocol, DFU) is tested against a fake.\n\nKotlin:\n\n```kotlin\ninterface GattPort {\n    val events: Flow<GattEvent>                       // Connected, Dropped(status), Notified(bytes), MtuChanged(mtu)\n    suspend fun connect(address: String)\n    suspend fun write(char: UUID, value: ByteArray, withResponse: Boolean): GattResult\n    suspend fun close()\n}\n\nclass FakeGattPort : GattPort {\n    private val _events = MutableSharedFlow<GattEvent>(extraBufferCapacity = 64)\n    override val events: Flow<GattEvent> = _events\n    val writes = mutableListOf<ByteArray>()\n    var connectStatuses = ArrayDeque(listOf(133, 133, 0))   // fail twice, then succeed\n    var closeCount = 0\n\n    override suspend fun connect(address: String) {\n        val status = connectStatuses.removeFirst()\n        _events.emit(if (status == 0) GattEvent.Connected else GattEvent.Dropped(status))\n    }\n    override suspend fun write(char: UUID, value: ByteArray, withResponse: Boolean): GattResult {\n        writes += value.copyOf(); return GattResult.Ok()\n    }\n    override suspend fun close() { closeCount++ }\n    suspend fun notify(bytes: ByteArray) = _events.emit(GattEvent.Notified(bytes))\n}\n```\n\n```kotlin\n@Test fun retriesStatus133WithBackoffAndClosesEachTime() = runTest {\n    val port = FakeGattPort()\n    val link = DeviceLink(port, backoff = listOf(1.seconds, 2.seconds), dispatcher = StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler))\n\n    link.connect(\"AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF\")\n    advanceTimeBy(3.seconds); runCurrent()\n\n    assertEquals(Link.Ready::class, link.state.value::class)\n    assertEquals(2, port.closeCount)\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n\n```swift\nprotocol PeripheralPort: Sendable {\n    var events: AsyncStream<PeripheralEvent> { get }\n    func connect(_ id: UUID) async\n    func write(_ data: Data, withResponse: Bool) async throws\n    func maximumWriteLength(withResponse: Bool) -> Int\n}\n\nfinal class FakePeripheralPort: PeripheralPort, @unchecked Sendable {\n    let (events, continuation) = AsyncStream.makeStream(of: PeripheralEvent.self)\n    var written: [Data] = []\n    var maxLength = 20\n    func connect(_ id: UUID) async { continuation.yield(.connected) }\n    func write(_ data: Data, withResponse: Bool) async throws { written.append(data) }\n    func maximumWriteLength(withResponse: Bool) -> Int { maxLength }\n}\n\n@Test func chunksRespectMaximumWriteLength() async throws {\n    let port = FakePeripheralPort()\n    port.maxLength = 20\n    let sender = FrameSender(port: port)\n    try await sender.send(Data(repeating: 0xAB, count: 100))\n    #expect(port.written.allSatisfy { \$0.count <= 20 })\n}\n```\n\nDart:\n\n```dart\nclass FakeBleClient implements BleClient {\n  final _state = StreamController<LinkState>.broadcast();\n  final _notifications = StreamController<Uint8List>.broadcast();\n  final sent = <Uint8List>[];\n\n  @override Stream<LinkState> get state => _state.stream;\n  @override Stream<Uint8List> get notifications => _notifications.stream;\n  @override Future<void> connect(String id) async => _state.add(LinkState.ready);\n  @override Future<Uint8List> request(Uint8List c, {Duration timeout = const Duration(seconds: 3)}) async {\n    sent.add(c);\n    return Uint8List.fromList([0x00]);\n  }\n  @override Future<void> disconnect() async => _state.add(LinkState.idle);\n\n  void dropLink() => _state.add(LinkState.dropped);\n}\n\ntest('command times out when the device never answers', () {\n  fakeAsync((async) {\n    final client = SilentBleClient();               // request() never completes\n    Object? error;\n    DeviceRepository(client).readVersion().catchError((Object e) { error = e; return ''; });\n    async.elapse(const Duration(seconds: 4));\n    expect(error, isA<TimeoutException>());\n  });\n});\n```\n\n## What to test with fakes\n\n- State machine: every (state, event) pair, including illegal ones (notification while `Connecting`).\n- Queue: one operation at a time; timeout fails the operation; a drop fails the pending operation.\n- Status 133 and other connect failures: close is called each time; backoff timing; give-up after N attempts.\n- Chunking: every chunk fits `MTU - 3` or `maximumWriteValueLength(for:)`; reassembly limits; out-of-order chunks.\n- Parser: truncated frames, maximum lengths, length fields larger than the buffer, wrong CRC, unknown opcode.\n- CRC: the standard check value for `\"123456789\"` (see [Protocol and DFU](protocol-and-dfu.md)).\n- DFU: resume from offset after a drop, refusal on low battery, version check after reboot.\n- Logging: assert redaction (no raw MAC, no key material) in the formatted log lines.\n\n## Device testing\n\nManual tools:\n- nRF Connect for Mobile (Android and iOS): scan, inspect advertisement data and RSSI, connect, read and\n  write characteristics, toggle notifications and indications, view a log, and run a Nordic DFU.\n  Use it to confirm the peripheral behaves as documented before debugging the app.\n- Android HCI snoop log (Developer options) to see packets on the phone; open it with a protocol analyser.\n- A hardware sniffer for link-layer problems the phone cannot show.\n\nDevice matrix (minimum):\n- Android: a Pixel on the latest release, a Samsung, one more OEM, and the oldest supported API level.\n  Include one device below API 31 if `minSdk` allows it (location permission path).\n- iOS: oldest supported iOS and the latest release.\n- Peripheral: current firmware and the oldest firmware still in the field.\n\nScenarios to run by hand on every release that touches BLE:\n- [ ] First pairing, permission denied, Bluetooth off, then on\n- [ ] Walk out of range and back; peripheral power cycle; phone Bluetooth toggle\n- [ ] App backgrounded for 10 minutes with an active link; screen off; Doze on Android\n- [ ] App killed by the system (iOS restoration) and force quit (expected: no relaunch)\n- [ ] Bond removed on the phone, then on the peripheral (Android 16 and 17 bond-loss dialogs)\n- [ ] DFU with a drop in the middle, with low battery, and with an incompatible image\n- [ ] Two devices connected at once, if the product supports it\n",
  },
  "mobile-ci-cd": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-ci-cd\ndescription: \"Writes and hardens CI/CD pipelines for Android, iOS and Flutter on GitHub Actions and GitLab CI: Gradle and xcodebuild builds, lint and tests, signing from secrets, fastlane, Play and TestFlight upload, Firebase App Distribution, caching and SHA-pinned actions. Use when setting up, fixing, speeding up or reviewing a mobile pipeline, workflow YAML, .gitlab-ci.yml or Fastfile.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile CI/CD\n\nBuild pipelines that a senior mobile engineer would trust with the signing keys: fast feedback on every\npull request, reproducible signed release builds from tags, and no path for untrusted code to reach a\nsecret or a store account.\n\n## When to use\n\n- The user asks to set up, fix, speed up or review CI for an Android, iOS, Flutter or KMP app.\n- A workflow file (`.github/workflows/*.yml`), `.gitlab-ci.yml`, `Fastfile`, `Matchfile` or Gradle\n  signing config is being added or changed.\n- Release automation: Play Console upload, TestFlight, Firebase App Distribution, version numbering.\n- A security review of an existing pipeline (secret exposure, unpinned actions, broad token permissions).\n\nNot for: app-level security such as storage or network hardening (use `mobile-security`), or writing the\nmerge request that ships the pipeline (use `mobile-merge-request`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Inventory the project before writing YAML.\n   - Platforms, build system, flavors or schemes, module layout, existing lint and test tasks.\n   - Existing CI files, `Gemfile` / `Fastfile`, `libs.versions.toml`, `Package.resolved`, `pubspec.lock`.\n   - Where releases go today (Play track, TestFlight, Firebase App Distribution) and who approves them.\n2. Agree the pipeline shape with the user (see table below). Ask before choosing store tracks,\n   distribution groups or approval rules; do not guess them.\n3. Load the matching reference and adapt its workflow instead of writing from memory:\n   [Android on GitHub Actions](references/android-github-actions.md),\n   [iOS on GitHub Actions](references/ios-github-actions.md),\n   [Flutter on GitHub Actions](references/flutter-github-actions.md),\n   [GitLab CI](references/gitlab-ci.md).\n4. Write the verify job first (lint, static analysis, unit tests, coverage gate). It runs on every PR and\n   never sees a secret.\n5. Write the release job second: tag or manual trigger only, protected `environment:`, signing from\n   secrets into the runner temp dir, upload to the store or distribution service.\n6. Harden: top-level `permissions: contents: read`, SHA-pinned actions, concurrency group, timeouts,\n   secrets passed through `env:` only, untrusted event data never interpolated into `run:`.\n7. Validate locally where possible: `actionlint` for syntax and expression errors, `zizmor` for\n   workflow security findings, and a dry run on a branch with the release job disabled.\n8. Deliver the files plus the list of required secrets and the manual setup steps (see Output format).\n   Never ask the user to paste secret values into the conversation.\n\n## Pipeline shape\n\n| Trigger | Jobs | Secrets |\n|---|---|---|\n| `pull_request` | verify (lint, detekt/ktlint/SwiftLint/analyze, unit tests, coverage gate), debug build | None |\n| `push` to `main` | verify, QA build to Firebase App Distribution or TestFlight internal | Distribution only, via `environment:` |\n| `push` tag `v*` or `workflow_dispatch` | verify, signed release build, store upload | Signing and store, via `environment:` with required reviewers |\n\nRun iOS jobs only when iOS files change (`paths:` filters). macOS runner minutes cost several times more\nthan Linux minutes.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Every action is pinned to a full commit SHA\n\nTags are mutable. A compromised tag runs attacker code with your secrets.\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: tag can be moved to a malicious commit\n- uses: actions/checkout@v7\n- uses: some-org/upload-thing@main\n\n# GOOD: immutable commit, human-readable version in the comment\n# (the SHA below is a placeholder, resolve the real one before use)\n- uses: actions/checkout@<full-40-char-commit-sha> # vX.Y.Z\n```\n\n- Resolve a tag to its commit: `git ls-remote https://github.com/actions/checkout 'refs/tags/vX.Y.Z*'`\n  (use the `^{}` line for annotated tags) or `gh api repos/actions/checkout/commits/vX.Y.Z --jq .sha`.\n- Keep pins fresh with Dependabot, which updates the SHA and the comment together:\n\n```yaml\n# .github/dependabot.yml\nversion: 2\nupdates:\n  - package-ecosystem: github-actions\n    directory: /\n    schedule:\n      interval: weekly\n```\n\n- Container images are pinned by digest (`image@sha256:<digest>`), tools by exact version\n  (`Gemfile.lock`, `firebase-tools@<x.y.z>`).\n\n### 2. The token gets the least privilege, granted per job\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: default token may have write access to the whole repo\non: pull_request\njobs:\n  test: ...\n\n# GOOD: read-only by default, escalate only where needed\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\njobs:\n  distribute:\n    permissions:\n      contents: read\n      id-token: write   # OIDC to a cloud provider, nothing else\n```\n\n- Set `persist-credentials: false` on `actions/checkout` unless a later step pushes with the token.\n\n### 3. Secrets never reach untrusted code\n\nSecrets are not passed to `pull_request` runs from forks. Do not \"fix\" that by switching triggers.\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: runs fork code with secrets and a write token\non: pull_request_target\njobs:\n  build:\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # vX.Y.Z\n        with:\n          ref: \${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}\n      - run: ./gradlew bundleRelease\n\n# GOOD: PRs build unsigned; signing happens only on trusted refs\non:\n  pull_request:\n  push:\n    tags: ['v*']\njobs:\n  release:\n    if: startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/v')\n    environment: production\n```\n\n- `workflow_run` and `issue_comment` triggers carry the same risk: secrets plus someone else's input.\n\n### 4. Release jobs run behind a protected environment\n\n- Store and signing secrets are environment secrets, not repository secrets.\n- The environment has required reviewers and a deployment branch or tag rule (`v*` tags, `main`).\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: any workflow on any branch can read the Play key\nenv:\n  PLAY_JSON: \${{ secrets.PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON }}   # repository secret, workflow level\n\n# GOOD: only this job, only after approval, only on allowed refs\njobs:\n  release:\n    environment: play-production\n    steps:\n      - run: bundle exec fastlane android release\n        env:\n          PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON: \${{ secrets.PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON }}\n```\n\n### 5. Signing material is decoded into the runner temp dir, never committed\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: keystore committed as app/release.jks, password in gradle.properties,\n# both visible to every fork and every past clone forever\n- run: ./gradlew bundleRelease\n\n# GOOD: decoded at build time, outside the workspace\n- name: Decode upload keystore\n  env:\n    KEYSTORE_BASE64: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_UPLOAD_KEYSTORE_BASE64 }}\n  run: |\n    echo \"\$KEYSTORE_BASE64\" | base64 --decode > \"\$RUNNER_TEMP/upload.jks\"\n    echo \"ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PATH=\$RUNNER_TEMP/upload.jks\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n```\n\n- Gradle reads path and passwords from environment variables (see the Android reference).\n- With Play App Signing, CI holds only the upload key. A leaked upload key can be reset in Play Console;\n  a leaked app signing key cannot.\n- iOS: certificates and profiles come from `fastlane match` in `readonly` mode into a temporary keychain\n  created by `setup_ci`. Never export a `.p12` into the repo.\n- `.gitignore` covers `*.jks`, `*.keystore`, `*.p12`, `*.p8`, `*.mobileprovision`, `google-services.json`\n  if it is environment specific, and any `*-service-account.json`.\n\n### 6. Secrets and untrusted input go through `env:`, never inline\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: secret ends up in the process list and possibly the log; PR title is a script injection\n- run: |\n    set -x\n    curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer \${{ secrets.API_TOKEN }}\" ...\n    echo \"Building \${{ github.event.pull_request.title }}\"\n\n# GOOD\n- env:\n    API_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.API_TOKEN }}\n    PR_TITLE: \${{ github.event.pull_request.title }}\n  run: |\n    curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer \$API_TOKEN\" ...\n    echo \"Building \$PR_TITLE\"\n```\n\n- No `set -x`, `env`, `printenv` or `--debug` flags in steps that hold secrets.\n- Mask values derived from a secret: `echo \"::add-mask::\$DERIVED\"`.\n- `--dart-define` values and `BuildConfig` fields are compiled into the app. They are configuration,\n  never secrets.\n\n### 7. One run per ref; cancel PR runs, never cancel releases\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: every push queues another full run, and a new tag can kill an upload half way\nconcurrency:\n  group: ci\n  cancel-in-progress: true\n\n# GOOD\nconcurrency:\n  group: \${{ github.workflow }}-\${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: \${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' }}\n```\n\n- Every job has `timeout-minutes`. A hung simulator should not burn six hours of macOS minutes.\n\n### 8. Caches are keyed on lockfiles and written only from trusted branches\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: hand-rolled Gradle cache, keyed on nothing useful, written by every PR\n- uses: actions/cache@<sha> # vX.Y.Z\n  with:\n    path: ~/.gradle\n    key: gradle\n\n# GOOD: setup-gradle caches and only writes from the default branch by default\n- uses: gradle/actions/setup-gradle@<sha> # vX.Y.Z\n```\n\n- iOS: cache `SourcePackages` (passed via `-clonedSourcePackagesDirPath`) keyed on\n  `hashFiles('**/Package.resolved')`, and resolve with `-disableAutomaticPackageResolution` so CI uses\n  the committed versions.\n- Flutter: `subosito/flutter-action` with `cache: true`. Release jobs restore caches read-only.\n\n### 9. Version numbers come from CI and only go up\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: versionCode hand-edited in build.gradle.kts, forgotten, rejected by Play\nversionCode = 42\n\n# BAD: GitHub expressions have no arithmetic, this is not a number\nrun: ./gradlew bundleRelease -PversionCode=\${{ github.run_number + 1000 }}\n\n# GOOD: offset computed in the shell, passed as a Gradle property\n- run: echo \"VERSION_CODE=\$(( GITHUB_RUN_NUMBER + \${VERSION_CODE_OFFSET:-0} ))\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n  env:\n    VERSION_CODE_OFFSET: \${{ vars.VERSION_CODE_OFFSET }}\n- run: ./gradlew bundleRelease -PversionCode=\"\$VERSION_CODE\"\n```\n\n- iOS: pass `CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION=\$BUILD_NUMBER` to the build so `CFBundleVersion` follows the run.\n- Flutter: `--build-number=\"\$VERSION_CODE\"` sets both Android `versionCode` and iOS `CFBundleVersion`.\n- `run_number` is per workflow and does not change on re-run. Set an offset above the highest number\n  already uploaded when migrating, and never rename the release workflow file without bumping the offset.\n- Marketing version (`versionName`, `CFBundleShortVersionString`, `--build-name`) comes from the tag.\n\n### 10. Quality gates fail the build before anything is signed\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: lint runs but never fails; release job does not depend on tests\n- run: ./gradlew lint || true\nrelease:\n  runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n\n# GOOD\nverify:\n  steps:\n    - run: ./gradlew ktlintCheck detekt lint testDebugUnitTest koverVerify --continue\nrelease:\n  needs: verify\n```\n\n- Coverage thresholds live in the build (`koverVerify` rules) or a short script over `xccov` / `lcov`\n  output, and the threshold is a number in the repo, not in someone's head.\n- Lint baselines are fine for legacy debt; new issues fail the build. `verify` is a required check on `main`.\n- Upload test reports with `if: always()` so failures can be diagnosed.\n\n### 11. Obfuscation symbols and mapping files are kept per build\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: obfuscated release with nothing to symbolicate crashes\n- run: flutter build appbundle --obfuscate --split-debug-info=build/symbols\n# (build/symbols discarded with the runner)\n\n# GOOD: symbols archived and sent to the crash reporter\n- run: flutter build appbundle --release --obfuscate --split-debug-info=build/symbols --build-number=\"\$VERSION_CODE\"\n- uses: actions/upload-artifact@<sha> # vX.Y.Z\n  with:\n    name: symbols-\${{ env.VERSION_CODE }}\n    path: build/symbols\n    retention-days: 90\n```\n\n- Android R8 `mapping.txt`, iOS `dSYM`s and Flutter `--split-debug-info` output are archived with the\n  build number in the name, and uploaded to Crashlytics or the crash reporter in use.\n- Retention for crash symbols matches how long the release is supported.\n\n### 12. Apple authentication uses an App Store Connect API key\n\n```ruby\n# BAD: Apple ID password and 2FA session cookies in CI\nENV[\"FASTLANE_PASSWORD\"] = \"...\"\nENV[\"FASTLANE_SESSION\"] = \"...\"\n\n# GOOD: .p8 API key from secrets, scoped by role, revocable\napi_key = app_store_connect_api_key(\n  key_id: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_KEY_ID\"),\n  issuer_id: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_ISSUER_ID\"),\n  key_content: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64\"),\n  is_key_content_base64: true\n)\nupload_to_testflight(api_key: api_key, skip_waiting_for_build_processing: true)\n```\n\n- Lowest role that covers the lane; a dedicated CI key, never a personal one.\n- `match(readonly: true)` in CI. Creating or renewing certificates is a manual, reviewed operation.\n\n### 13. Prefer short-lived credentials over stored keys\n\n- Where the target supports it (Google Cloud and Firebase, AWS, Azure), use OIDC from the workflow\n  (`id-token: write` plus the provider's auth action) instead of a long-lived JSON key.\n- Where a stored key is unavoidable (Play service account JSON, App Store Connect `.p8`), scope it to one\n  app, grant the minimum console role, and record a rotation date.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- `pull_request_target` or `workflow_run` that checks out and builds PR code with secrets available.\n- Third-party actions referenced by tag or branch, or `curl ... | bash` installers in release jobs.\n- Workflow-level `permissions: write-all` or no `permissions:` block at all.\n- Keystores, `.p12`, `.p8`, `google-services.json` with production keys, or passwords committed \"temporarily\".\n- Signing release builds on every PR \"to make sure signing works\".\n- `continue-on-error: true` or `|| true` on lint and test steps.\n- Release jobs that re-run the whole verify pipeline in parallel instead of `needs:` on it.\n- Uploading to the production track directly from CI without a staged rollout or a human approval.\n- Caches shared between PR and release jobs without lockfile keys.\n- Debug output (`set -x`, `--verbose`, `--debug`) left on in jobs that handle secrets.\n- Hand-maintained version numbers that drift between Android, iOS and the tag.\n- One giant job doing lint, tests, build and upload, so a flaky test blocks diagnosis of everything else.\n\n## Checklist\n\nSecure pipeline:\n- [ ] Top-level `permissions: contents: read`; any extra scope granted per job with a comment\n- [ ] Every third-party action pinned to a full commit SHA with a version comment; Dependabot enabled\n- [ ] Container images pinned by digest; CLI tools pinned by exact version or lockfile\n- [ ] No `pull_request_target`, `workflow_run` or comment-triggered job builds untrusted code with secrets\n- [ ] Signing and store secrets are environment secrets behind required reviewers and ref rules\n- [ ] Keystore and keys decoded into `\$RUNNER_TEMP`, never written into the workspace or committed\n- [ ] Secrets and event data reach scripts only through `env:`; no `set -x` or debug flags near them\n- [ ] `persist-credentials: false` on checkout unless the job pushes\n- [ ] App Store Connect API key used; no Apple ID password or session; `match` is `readonly`\n- [ ] OIDC used where the provider supports it; remaining stored keys scoped and rotation-dated\n\nPipeline quality:\n- [ ] Verify job runs lint, static analysis, unit tests and a coverage threshold, and fails on violations\n- [ ] Release job `needs:` the verify job and runs only on tags, `main` or manual dispatch\n- [ ] Concurrency group per workflow and ref; `cancel-in-progress` only for PRs\n- [ ] `timeout-minutes` on every job; `paths:` filters on expensive macOS jobs\n- [ ] Caches keyed on lockfiles; release jobs do not write caches from PR content\n- [ ] versionCode and build number derived from the run number with a documented offset\n- [ ] R8 mapping, dSYMs and Flutter split debug info archived per build and uploaded to the crash reporter\n- [ ] Test and lint reports uploaded with `if: always()`\n- [ ] `actionlint` and `zizmor` pass on the workflow files\n\n## Output format\n\nDeliver files, then a setup sheet. Do not invent secret values; name them and say how to create them.\n\n~~~markdown\n## CI/CD: <project> -- <platforms>\n\n### Files\n- `.github/workflows/android.yml` -- verify on PR, Firebase QA build on main, Play internal on tag\n- `fastlane/Fastfile` -- lanes `android release`, `ios beta`\n- `app/build.gradle.kts` -- signing config and versionCode from Gradle properties (diff below)\n\n```yaml\n<full workflow file>\n```\n\n### Required secrets\n| Name | Scope | What it is | How to create |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ANDROID_UPLOAD_KEYSTORE_BASE64 | env `play-production` | Upload keystore, base64 | `base64 -i upload.jks` (macOS) or `base64 -w0 upload.jks` (Linux) |\n| PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON | env `play-production` | Play Console service account key | Google Cloud service account, invited in Play Console with release permission for this app only |\n| ASC_KEY_ID / ASC_ISSUER_ID / ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64 | env `testflight` | App Store Connect API key | Users and Access > Integrations, lowest role that can upload |\n\n### Repository variables\n| Name | Example | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| VERSION_CODE_OFFSET | 1000 | Keeps versionCode above previously uploaded builds |\n\n### Manual setup\n1. Create environments `play-production`, `testflight` with required reviewers and tag rule `v*`.\n2. Add the secrets above as environment secrets.\n3. Branch protection on `main`: require the `verify` check.\n4. Enable Dependabot for `github-actions`.\n5. <store-side steps: Play App Signing enrolled, first AAB uploaded manually, TestFlight groups created>\n\n### Verification\n- `actionlint` and `zizmor` output\n- Which jobs were run on a branch, and which were not run because they need secrets\n\n### Open questions\n- <tracks, groups, approvers or thresholds that need the owner's decision>\n~~~\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-security` -- secret storage, MASVS and supply-chain review beyond the pipeline\n- `mobile-merge-request` -- describing pipeline changes and required manual steps in the MR\n- `mobile-code-review` -- reviewing Gradle, Fastfile and workflow changes alongside app code\n- `mobile-kotlin`, `mobile-swift`, `mobile-flutter` -- build configuration conventions per platform\n- `mobile-release` -- store readiness, tracks, phased rollout and rollback\n- `mobile-testing` -- which test layers the pipeline runs and how to shard them\n",
    "references/android-github-actions.md":
        "# Android on GitHub Actions\n\nLoad this when the pipeline builds a native Android app (Kotlin or Java, Gradle Kotlin DSL). Adapt module\nnames, flavors, tasks and package names to the project.\n\nPinning convention used in every file below: `@<sha>` is a placeholder for the full 40-character commit\nSHA of the release named in the comment. Resolve it before committing (see Rule 1 in SKILL.md). Versions\nin comments were valid at the time of writing; use the latest release when pinning.\n\n## Workflow: `.github/workflows/android.yml`\n\n```yaml\nname: Android\n\non:\n  pull_request:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n    tags: ['v*']\n  workflow_dispatch:\n\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\nconcurrency:\n  group: \${{ github.workflow }}-\${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: \${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' }}\n\njobs:\n  verify:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 30\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v7\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n\n      - uses: gradle/actions/wrapper-validation@<sha> # v6\n\n      - uses: actions/setup-java@<sha> # v6\n        with:\n          distribution: temurin\n          java-version: '21'\n\n      - uses: gradle/actions/setup-gradle@<sha> # v6\n\n      - name: Static analysis\n        run: ./gradlew ktlintCheck detekt lint --continue\n\n      - name: Unit tests and coverage gate\n        run: ./gradlew testDebugUnitTest koverVerify --continue\n\n      - name: Upload reports\n        if: always()\n        uses: actions/upload-artifact@<sha> # v7\n        with:\n          name: verify-reports\n          path: |\n            **/build/reports/\n            **/build/test-results/\n          retention-days: 14\n\n  instrumented:\n    needs: verify\n    if: github.event_name == 'push' && github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 45\n    strategy:\n      fail-fast: false\n      matrix:\n        api-level: [29, 35]\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v7\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n      - uses: actions/setup-java@<sha> # v6\n        with:\n          distribution: temurin\n          java-version: '21'\n      - uses: gradle/actions/setup-gradle@<sha> # v6\n      - name: Enable KVM\n        run: |\n          echo 'KERNEL==\"kvm\", GROUP=\"kvm\", MODE=\"0666\", OPTIONS+=\"static_node=kvm\"' | sudo tee /etc/udev/rules.d/99-kvm4all.rules\n          sudo udevadm control --reload-rules\n          sudo udevadm trigger --name-match=kvm\n      - uses: reactivecircus/android-emulator-runner@<sha> # v2\n        with:\n          api-level: \${{ matrix.api-level }}\n          arch: x86_64\n          target: google_apis\n          script: ./gradlew connectedDebugAndroidTest\n\n  firebase-qa:\n    needs: verify\n    if: github.event_name == 'push' && github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 30\n    environment: firebase-qa\n    permissions:\n      contents: read\n      id-token: write # OIDC to Google Cloud, no stored JSON key\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v7\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n      - uses: actions/setup-java@<sha> # v6\n        with:\n          distribution: temurin\n          java-version: '21'\n      - uses: gradle/actions/setup-gradle@<sha> # v6\n        with:\n          cache-read-only: true\n\n      - name: Compute versionCode\n        env:\n          VERSION_CODE_OFFSET: \${{ vars.VERSION_CODE_OFFSET }}\n        run: echo \"VERSION_CODE=\$(( GITHUB_RUN_NUMBER + \${VERSION_CODE_OFFSET:-0} ))\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n\n      - name: Build QA APK\n        run: ./gradlew assembleQa -PversionCode=\"\$VERSION_CODE\"\n\n      - uses: google-github-actions/auth@<sha> # v3\n        with:\n          workload_identity_provider: \${{ vars.GCP_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_PROVIDER }}\n          service_account: \${{ vars.GCP_DISTRIBUTION_SERVICE_ACCOUNT }}\n\n      - name: Distribute to testers\n        env:\n          FIREBASE_APP_ID: \${{ vars.FIREBASE_ANDROID_APP_ID }}\n        run: |\n          npx --yes firebase-tools@<x.y.z> appdistribution:distribute \\\n            app/build/outputs/apk/qa/app-qa.apk \\\n            --app \"\$FIREBASE_APP_ID\" \\\n            --groups \"qa\" \\\n            --release-notes \"main @ \${GITHUB_SHA::8}, build \$VERSION_CODE\"\n\n  release:\n    needs: verify\n    if: startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/v')\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 45\n    environment: play-production # required reviewers + tag rule v*\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v7\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n      - uses: actions/setup-java@<sha> # v6\n        with:\n          distribution: temurin\n          java-version: '21'\n      - uses: gradle/actions/setup-gradle@<sha> # v6\n        with:\n          cache-read-only: true\n      - uses: ruby/setup-ruby@<sha> # v1\n        with:\n          bundler-cache: true\n\n      - name: Compute versions\n        env:\n          VERSION_CODE_OFFSET: \${{ vars.VERSION_CODE_OFFSET }}\n        run: |\n          echo \"VERSION_CODE=\$(( GITHUB_RUN_NUMBER + \${VERSION_CODE_OFFSET:-0} ))\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n          echo \"VERSION_NAME=\${GITHUB_REF_NAME#v}\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n\n      - name: Decode upload keystore\n        env:\n          KEYSTORE_BASE64: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_UPLOAD_KEYSTORE_BASE64 }}\n        run: |\n          echo \"\$KEYSTORE_BASE64\" | base64 --decode > \"\$RUNNER_TEMP/upload.jks\"\n          echo \"ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PATH=\$RUNNER_TEMP/upload.jks\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n\n      - name: Build signed AAB\n        env:\n          ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD }}\n          ANDROID_KEY_ALIAS: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_KEY_ALIAS }}\n          ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD }}\n        run: ./gradlew bundleRelease -PversionCode=\"\$VERSION_CODE\" -PversionName=\"\$VERSION_NAME\"\n\n      - name: Archive AAB and R8 mapping\n        uses: actions/upload-artifact@<sha> # v7\n        with:\n          name: release-\${{ env.VERSION_CODE }}\n          path: |\n            app/build/outputs/bundle/release/app-release.aab\n            app/build/outputs/mapping/release/mapping.txt\n          retention-days: 90\n\n      - name: Upload to Play internal track\n        env:\n          PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON: \${{ secrets.PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON }}\n        run: bundle exec fastlane android internal\n```\n\nNotes:\n- `instrumented` and `firebase-qa` run on `main` only; the PR path stays fast and secret-free.\n- The `qa` build type is assumed to exist and to be signed with a non-production key (or the debug key).\n  Firebase App Distribution accepts AABs only when the Firebase project is linked to Play, so APK is the\n  simpler default for QA.\n- `environment:` on `firebase-qa` and `release` holds the secrets and variables those jobs need.\n- If the Play account cannot use OIDC with the upload tool, the JSON key is an environment secret, not a\n  repository secret.\n\n## Gradle: signing and version from CI (`app/build.gradle.kts`)\n\n```kotlin\nandroid {\n    defaultConfig {\n        versionCode = providers.gradleProperty(\"versionCode\").orNull?.toInt() ?: 1\n        versionName = providers.gradleProperty(\"versionName\").orNull ?: \"0.0.0-dev\"\n    }\n\n    signingConfigs {\n        create(\"release\") {\n            val keystorePath = providers.environmentVariable(\"ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PATH\").orNull\n            if (keystorePath != null) {\n                storeFile = file(keystorePath)\n                storePassword = providers.environmentVariable(\"ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD\").get()\n                keyAlias = providers.environmentVariable(\"ANDROID_KEY_ALIAS\").get()\n                keyPassword = providers.environmentVariable(\"ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD\").get()\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    buildTypes {\n        release {\n            isMinifyEnabled = true\n            isShrinkResources = true\n            signingConfig = signingConfigs.getByName(\"release\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- No passwords in `gradle.properties`, `local.properties` or the build file.\n- A local `bundleRelease` without the environment variables fails at signing. That is intended: release\n  builds come from CI.\n- `versionCode` must stay below 2100000000. Keep the offset small.\n\n## Coverage threshold (Kover)\n\n```kotlin\n// build.gradle.kts of the module (or the root, if Kover merges modules)\nkover {\n    reports {\n        verify {\n            rule {\n                minBound(80)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Exclude generated code (Hilt, Room, Compose previews, DI modules) through Kover filters so the number\n  measures real logic.\n- Raise the threshold over time; never lower it to make a PR pass without a recorded decision.\n- JaCoCo with a `JacocoCoverageVerification` task is an equivalent alternative if the project already uses it.\n\n## Fastlane: Play upload (`fastlane/Fastfile`)\n\n```ruby\ndefault_platform(:android)\n\nplatform :android do\n  lane :internal do\n    upload_to_play_store(\n      package_name: \"com.example.app\",\n      track: \"internal\",\n      aab: ENV.fetch(\"AAB_PATH\", \"app/build/outputs/bundle/release/app-release.aab\"),\n      json_key_data: ENV.fetch(\"PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON\"),\n      skip_upload_metadata: true,\n      skip_upload_images: true,\n      skip_upload_screenshots: true,\n      release_status: \"completed\"\n    )\n  end\n\n  lane :promote_production do\n    upload_to_play_store(\n      package_name: \"com.example.app\",\n      track: \"internal\",\n      track_promote_to: \"production\",\n      rollout: \"0.1\",\n      json_key_data: ENV.fetch(\"PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON\"),\n      skip_upload_changelogs: true,\n      skip_upload_metadata: true,\n      skip_upload_images: true,\n      skip_upload_screenshots: true\n    )\n  end\nend\n```\n\n- `upload_to_play_store` is the action behind `fastlane supply`.\n- Use `release_status: \"draft\"` while the app has never been published; Play rejects completed releases\n  for draft apps.\n- Commit `Gemfile` and `Gemfile.lock` so the fastlane version is pinned.\n- The Play service account is invited in Play Console with release permissions for this app only, not\n  admin on the developer account.\n- Production promotion is a separate, manually triggered lane with a staged rollout, not part of the tag job.\n\n## Secrets and variables\n\n| Name | Kind | Scope |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ANDROID_UPLOAD_KEYSTORE_BASE64` | secret | env `play-production` |\n| `ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD`, `ANDROID_KEY_ALIAS`, `ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD` | secret | env `play-production` |\n| `PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON` | secret | env `play-production` |\n| `VERSION_CODE_OFFSET` | variable | repository |\n| `GCP_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_PROVIDER`, `GCP_DISTRIBUTION_SERVICE_ACCOUNT`, `FIREBASE_ANDROID_APP_ID` | variable | env `firebase-qa` |\n\nCreate the keystore secret with `base64 -i upload.jks | pbcopy` (macOS) or `base64 -w0 upload.jks` (Linux)\nand paste it into the environment secret form. Delete any local plaintext copies of passwords afterwards.\n",
    "references/flutter-github-actions.md":
        "# Flutter on GitHub Actions\n\nLoad this when the pipeline builds a Flutter app for Android and iOS. Platform-specific signing and store\nupload follow the [Android](android-github-actions.md) and [iOS](ios-github-actions.md) references; this\nfile covers what changes for Flutter.\n\nPinning convention: `@<sha>` is a placeholder for the full 40-character commit SHA of the release named\nin the comment. Resolve it before committing. Versions in comments were valid at the time of writing.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- Flavors `staging` and `prod`, with entry points `lib/main_staging.dart` and `lib/main_prod.dart`, and\n  matching Android product flavors and iOS schemes. Ask the owner if the project differs.\n- The Flutter version is pinned in `pubspec.yaml` (`environment: flutter:`) or in the workflow, and\n  `pubspec.lock` is committed.\n- Android Gradle reads signing from environment variables exactly as in the Android reference. Do not set\n  `versionCode` in Gradle; Flutter passes `--build-number` through.\n\n## Workflow: `.github/workflows/flutter.yml`\n\n```yaml\nname: Flutter\n\non:\n  pull_request:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n    tags: ['v*']\n  workflow_dispatch:\n\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\nconcurrency:\n  group: \${{ github.workflow }}-\${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: \${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' }}\n\njobs:\n  verify:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 20\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v7\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n\n      - uses: subosito/flutter-action@<sha> # v2\n        with:\n          channel: stable\n          flutter-version-file: pubspec.yaml\n          cache: true\n\n      - run: flutter pub get --enforce-lockfile\n      - run: dart format --output=none --set-exit-if-changed .\n      - run: flutter analyze --fatal-infos\n      - run: flutter test --coverage\n\n      - name: Coverage gate\n        env:\n          MIN_COVERAGE: '70'\n        run: |\n          awk -F: -v min=\"\$MIN_COVERAGE\" '\n            /^LF:/ { found += \$2 }\n            /^LH:/ { hit += \$2 }\n            END {\n              pct = found ? hit * 100 / found : 0\n              printf \"Line coverage: %.1f%%\\n\", pct\n              exit (pct < min)\n            }' coverage/lcov.info\n\n  android:\n    needs: verify\n    if: startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/v')\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 40\n    environment: android-release # required reviewers + tag rule v*\n    permissions:\n      contents: read\n      id-token: write # OIDC for Firebase App Distribution\n    strategy:\n      fail-fast: false\n      matrix:\n        include:\n          - flavor: staging\n            target: apk\n          - flavor: prod\n            target: appbundle\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v7\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n      - uses: actions/setup-java@<sha> # v6\n        with:\n          distribution: temurin\n          java-version: '21'\n      - uses: subosito/flutter-action@<sha> # v2\n        with:\n          channel: stable\n          flutter-version-file: pubspec.yaml\n          cache: true\n\n      - name: Compute versions\n        env:\n          VERSION_CODE_OFFSET: \${{ vars.VERSION_CODE_OFFSET }}\n        run: |\n          echo \"VERSION_CODE=\$(( GITHUB_RUN_NUMBER + \${VERSION_CODE_OFFSET:-0} ))\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n          echo \"VERSION_NAME=\${GITHUB_REF_NAME#v}\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n\n      - name: Decode upload keystore\n        env:\n          KEYSTORE_BASE64: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_UPLOAD_KEYSTORE_BASE64 }}\n        run: |\n          echo \"\$KEYSTORE_BASE64\" | base64 --decode > \"\$RUNNER_TEMP/upload.jks\"\n          echo \"ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PATH=\$RUNNER_TEMP/upload.jks\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n\n      - name: Build\n        env:\n          ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD }}\n          ANDROID_KEY_ALIAS: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_KEY_ALIAS }}\n          ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD: \${{ secrets.ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD }}\n          FLAVOR: \${{ matrix.flavor }}\n          TARGET: \${{ matrix.target }}\n        run: |\n          flutter build \"\$TARGET\" --release \\\n            --flavor \"\$FLAVOR\" \\\n            -t \"lib/main_\${FLAVOR}.dart\" \\\n            --dart-define-from-file=\"config/\${FLAVOR}.json\" \\\n            --obfuscate \\\n            --split-debug-info=\"build/symbols/android-\${FLAVOR}\" \\\n            --build-number=\"\$VERSION_CODE\" \\\n            --build-name=\"\$VERSION_NAME\"\n\n      - name: Keep binaries, symbols and mapping\n        uses: actions/upload-artifact@<sha> # v7\n        with:\n          name: android-\${{ matrix.flavor }}-\${{ env.VERSION_CODE }}\n          path: |\n            build/app/outputs/flutter-apk/*.apk\n            build/app/outputs/bundle/\${{ matrix.flavor }}Release/*.aab\n            build/app/outputs/mapping/\${{ matrix.flavor }}Release/mapping.txt\n            build/symbols/android-\${{ matrix.flavor }}\n          retention-days: 90\n\n      # staging: testers via Firebase App Distribution\n      - if: matrix.flavor == 'staging'\n        uses: google-github-actions/auth@<sha> # v3\n        with:\n          workload_identity_provider: \${{ vars.GCP_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_PROVIDER }}\n          service_account: \${{ vars.GCP_DISTRIBUTION_SERVICE_ACCOUNT }}\n      - if: matrix.flavor == 'staging'\n        env:\n          FIREBASE_APP_ID: \${{ vars.FIREBASE_ANDROID_STAGING_APP_ID }}\n        run: |\n          npx --yes firebase-tools@<x.y.z> appdistribution:distribute \\\n            build/app/outputs/flutter-apk/app-staging-release.apk \\\n            --app \"\$FIREBASE_APP_ID\" --groups \"qa\"\n\n      # prod: Play internal track, lane as in the Android reference with the Flutter AAB path\n      - if: matrix.flavor == 'prod'\n        uses: ruby/setup-ruby@<sha> # v1\n        with:\n          bundler-cache: true\n          working-directory: android\n      - if: matrix.flavor == 'prod'\n        working-directory: android\n        env:\n          PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON: \${{ secrets.PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON }}\n          AAB_PATH: \${{ github.workspace }}/build/app/outputs/bundle/prodRelease/app-prod-release.aab\n        run: bundle exec fastlane android internal\n```\n\nMatrix notes:\n- A job-level `if:` is evaluated before the matrix expands and cannot read `matrix`. Filter per entry\n  with step-level `if: matrix.flavor == '...'`, or split into separate jobs.\n- `fail-fast: false` so a staging failure does not cancel an in-flight prod upload.\n- `upload-artifact` warns, not fails, on path patterns that match nothing, so one path list can serve\n  both targets.\n- For a staging build on every push to `main`, add a second job with the same steps and only the\n  staging entry; keep prod on tags.\n\n## iOS job\n\n```yaml\n  ios:\n    needs: verify\n    if: startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/v')\n    runs-on: macos-15\n    timeout-minutes: 60\n    environment: testflight\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v7\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n      - uses: subosito/flutter-action@<sha> # v2\n        with:\n          channel: stable\n          flutter-version-file: pubspec.yaml\n          cache: true\n      - uses: ruby/setup-ruby@<sha> # v1\n        with:\n          bundler-cache: true\n          working-directory: ios\n\n      - name: Compute build number\n        env:\n          BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET: \${{ vars.BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET }}\n        run: echo \"BUILD_NUMBER=\$(( GITHUB_RUN_NUMBER + \${BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET:-0} ))\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n\n      - name: Signing, build and upload\n        working-directory: ios\n        env:\n          ASC_KEY_ID: \${{ secrets.ASC_KEY_ID }}\n          ASC_ISSUER_ID: \${{ secrets.ASC_ISSUER_ID }}\n          ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64: \${{ secrets.ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64 }}\n          MATCH_PASSWORD: \${{ secrets.MATCH_PASSWORD }}\n          MATCH_GIT_BASIC_AUTHORIZATION: \${{ secrets.MATCH_GIT_BASIC_AUTHORIZATION }}\n        run: bundle exec fastlane ios beta\n\n      - name: Keep symbols\n        uses: actions/upload-artifact@<sha> # v7\n        with:\n          name: ios-prod-\${{ env.BUILD_NUMBER }}\n          path: |\n            build/symbols/ios-prod\n            build/ios/archive/*.xcarchive/dSYMs\n          retention-days: 90\n```\n\n`ios/fastlane/Fastfile`:\n\n```ruby\ndefault_platform(:ios)\n\nplatform :ios do\n  lane :beta do\n    setup_ci\n    api_key = app_store_connect_api_key(\n      key_id: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_KEY_ID\"),\n      issuer_id: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_ISSUER_ID\"),\n      key_content: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64\"),\n      is_key_content_base64: true\n    )\n    match(type: \"appstore\", readonly: true, api_key: api_key)\n\n    Dir.chdir(\"../..\") do\n      sh(\n        \"flutter\", \"build\", \"ipa\", \"--release\",\n        \"--flavor\", \"prod\",\n        \"-t\", \"lib/main_prod.dart\",\n        \"--dart-define-from-file=config/prod.json\",\n        \"--obfuscate\",\n        \"--split-debug-info=build/symbols/ios-prod\",\n        \"--build-number=#{ENV.fetch('BUILD_NUMBER')}\",\n        \"--export-options-plist=ios/ExportOptions.plist\"\n      )\n    end\n\n    ipa = Dir[File.join(File.expand_path(\"../..\"), \"build/ios/ipa/*.ipa\")].first\n    UI.user_error!(\"No IPA produced\") if ipa.nil?\n    upload_to_testflight(api_key: api_key, ipa: ipa, skip_waiting_for_build_processing: true)\n  end\nend\n```\n\n- Plain Ruby in a Fastfile runs with `ios/fastlane` as the working directory, while actions resolve\n  paths from `ios/`. Absolute paths (as above) avoid mixing the two up.\n- `ios/ExportOptions.plist` sets `method` to App Store distribution, manual signing, and maps the bundle\n  id to the `match AppStore <bundle id>` profile.\n- The Xcode project's Release configuration for the `prod` scheme uses manual signing with that profile.\n\n## Obfuscation symbols\n\n- `--obfuscate` requires `--split-debug-info`. The directory holds the symbol files needed to read Dart\n  stack traces; without them, production crashes are unreadable.\n- Archive them per flavor and build number, and upload them to the crash reporter. For Crashlytics:\n  `firebase crashlytics:symbols:upload --app=<firebase-app-id> build/symbols/<dir>`.\n- Symbolicate a stored trace manually with\n  `flutter symbolize -i <stack-trace-file> -d <symbols-dir>/<file>.symbols`.\n- Never ship the symbols directory inside the app or commit it.\n\n## Configuration and secrets\n\n- `--dart-define` and `--dart-define-from-file` values are compiled into the binary and are readable by\n  anyone with the APK or IPA. Use them for environment URLs and feature flags, never for API secrets.\n- `config/<flavor>.json` files hold non-secret configuration and can be committed. Anything secret stays\n  server side.\n- Secrets and variables are the union of the Android and iOS tables, scoped to the same environments.\n",
    "references/gitlab-ci.md":
        "# GitLab CI\n\nLoad this when the project uses GitLab CI instead of GitHub Actions. The rules in SKILL.md apply\nunchanged; this file maps them to GitLab keywords and shows a short Android pipeline. iOS and Flutter\njobs follow the same shape on a macOS runner and reuse the fastlane lanes from the\n[iOS](ios-github-actions.md) and [Flutter](flutter-github-actions.md) references.\n\n## Concept mapping\n\n| GitHub Actions | GitLab CI |\n|---|---|\n| `uses: owner/action@<sha>` | `image: <image>@sha256:<digest>`, `include: project: ... ref: <commit-sha>` |\n| `permissions:` | `CI_JOB_TOKEN` allowlist and project token access settings |\n| Environment secrets with required reviewers | Protected and masked CI/CD variables, protected environments with deployment approvals |\n| Secrets withheld from fork PRs | Protected variables are exposed only to pipelines on protected branches and tags |\n| `concurrency` + `cancel-in-progress` | `interruptible: true` + auto-cancel redundant pipelines; `resource_group` for deploys |\n| `github.run_number` | `CI_PIPELINE_IID` (per project, increasing) |\n| OIDC `id-token: write` | `id_tokens:` keyword |\n| `actions/upload-artifact` | `artifacts:` with `expire_in` |\n| `paths:` filter | `rules: - changes:` |\n\n## Variables\n\n- Signing and store credentials are CI/CD variables that are protected (only on protected refs) and\n  masked (or masked and hidden). Mark `v*` tags as protected tags so release pipelines receive them.\n- Binary files (keystore, `.p8`) are stored base64-encoded and decoded at runtime. A File-type variable\n  can hold text files such as the Play service account JSON; the variable then contains a path.\n- Merge request pipelines from forks run in the fork project by default. A parent-project member can\n  choose to run them in the parent project, which exposes its non-protected variables; review the fork's\n  code, including `.gitlab-ci.yml`, before doing that.\n\n## Pipeline: `.gitlab-ci.yml` (Android)\n\n```yaml\nworkflow:\n  rules:\n    - if: \$CI_PIPELINE_SOURCE == \"merge_request_event\"\n    - if: \$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH == \$CI_DEFAULT_BRANCH\n    - if: \$CI_COMMIT_TAG =~ /^v/\n\nstages: [verify, build, deploy]\n\ndefault:\n  image: <android-sdk-image>@sha256:<digest> # placeholder: JDK + Android SDK image, pinned by digest\n  interruptible: true\n\nvariables:\n  GRADLE_USER_HOME: \$CI_PROJECT_DIR/.gradle\n\n.gradle-cache: &gradle-cache\n  key:\n    files:\n      - gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.properties\n      - gradle/libs.versions.toml\n  paths:\n    - .gradle/caches/\n    - .gradle/wrapper/\n  policy: pull\n\nverify:\n  stage: verify\n  timeout: 30m\n  cache:\n    - <<: *gradle-cache   # read-only for MRs and tags\n  rules:\n    - if: \$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH != \$CI_DEFAULT_BRANCH\n  script:\n    - ./gradlew ktlintCheck detekt lint testDebugUnitTest koverVerify --continue\n  artifacts:\n    when: always\n    expire_in: 14 days\n    reports:\n      junit: \"**/build/test-results/**/TEST-*.xml\"\n    paths:\n      - \"**/build/reports/\"\n\nverify-main:\n  extends: verify\n  cache:\n    - <<: *gradle-cache\n      policy: pull-push   # only the default branch writes the cache\n  rules:\n    - if: \$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH == \$CI_DEFAULT_BRANCH\n\nrelease:\n  stage: build\n  timeout: 45m\n  interruptible: false\n  needs: [verify]\n  rules:\n    - if: \$CI_COMMIT_TAG =~ /^v/\n  environment:\n    name: play-production   # protected environment with deployment approval\n  resource_group: play-store\n  cache:\n    - <<: *gradle-cache\n  script:\n    - export VERSION_CODE=\$(( CI_PIPELINE_IID + \${VERSION_CODE_OFFSET:-0} ))\n    - export ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PATH=\"\$(mktemp -d)/upload.jks\"\n    - echo \"\$ANDROID_UPLOAD_KEYSTORE_BASE64\" | base64 -d > \"\$ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PATH\"\n    - ./gradlew bundleRelease -PversionCode=\"\$VERSION_CODE\" -PversionName=\"\${CI_COMMIT_TAG#v}\"\n    - bundle install\n    - bundle exec fastlane android internal\n  artifacts:\n    expire_in: 90 days\n    paths:\n      - app/build/outputs/bundle/release/app-release.aab\n      - app/build/outputs/mapping/release/mapping.txt\n```\n\nNotes:\n- Only `verify-main` writes the Gradle cache, so merge request code cannot poison what release jobs\n  restore. `cache:key:files` accepts at most two files.\n- The release job assumes Ruby and Bundler are available in the image; otherwise use a separate,\n  digest-pinned image for the upload step.\n- Enable \"Auto-cancel redundant pipelines\" in the project CI/CD settings so `interruptible: true` jobs are\n  cancelled by newer pipelines on the same ref. The release job sets `interruptible: false`.\n- `resource_group` serialises store uploads so two tags never upload at the same time.\n- Shell expansion works in `script:` lines, so arithmetic for `VERSION_CODE` is done there.\n- `ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD`, `ANDROID_KEY_ALIAS`, `ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD` and\n  `PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON` are protected, masked variables scoped to the `play-production` environment.\n- Never enable `CI_DEBUG_TRACE` on jobs with protected variables; it prints every variable.\n\n## iOS and Flutter on GitLab\n\n```yaml\nios-testflight:\n  stage: deploy\n  tags: [macos]   # placeholder: tag of a GitLab-hosted or self-hosted macOS runner\n  timeout: 60m\n  interruptible: false\n  needs: [verify]\n  rules:\n    - if: \$CI_COMMIT_TAG =~ /^v/\n  environment:\n    name: testflight\n  resource_group: testflight\n  script:\n    - export BUILD_NUMBER=\$(( CI_PIPELINE_IID + \${BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET:-0} ))\n    - export MARKETING_VERSION=\"\${CI_COMMIT_TAG#v}\"\n    - bundle install\n    - bundle exec fastlane ios beta\n  artifacts:\n    expire_in: 90 days\n    paths:\n      - build/*.dSYM.zip\n```\n\n- Self-hosted macOS runners keep state between jobs. `setup_ci` uses a temporary keychain, but also clean\n  derived data and never leave decoded keys on disk.\n- Use `rules: - changes:` to run macOS jobs only when iOS or shared code changed.\n",
    "references/ios-github-actions.md":
        "# iOS on GitHub Actions\n\nLoad this when the pipeline builds a native iOS app (Swift, Xcode project or workspace, SPM dependencies).\nAdapt workspace, scheme, bundle id and simulator names to the project.\n\nPinning convention: `@<sha>` is a placeholder for the full 40-character commit SHA of the release named\nin the comment. Resolve it before committing. Versions in comments were valid at the time of writing.\n\n## Before writing the workflow\n\n- Check the runner image's installed Xcode versions and simulators in the `actions/runner-images`\n  documentation for the chosen macOS label. Do not assume a path or device name exists.\n- App Store uploads must be built with the minimum Xcode and SDK Apple currently requires. Pick the\n  runner label and Xcode version from that, not from what is cheapest.\n- Release signing uses manual signing in the Release configuration with the `match` profile names, or\n  `match` updates the project with `update_code_signing_settings`. Decide which before writing lanes.\n- Any `Info.plist`, entitlement or capability change needed for CI (for example a new app group) is\n  called out to the owner, because the provisioning profiles must be regenerated.\n\n## Workflow: `.github/workflows/ios.yml`\n\n```yaml\nname: iOS\n\non:\n  pull_request:\n    paths:\n      - 'ios/**'\n      - 'fastlane/**'\n      - 'Gemfile.lock'\n      - '.github/workflows/ios.yml'\n  push:\n    tags: ['v*']\n  workflow_dispatch:\n\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\nconcurrency:\n  group: \${{ github.workflow }}-\${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: \${{ github.event_name == 'pull_request' }}\n\nenv:\n  XCODE_VERSION: '<xcode-version-on-runner>' # placeholder, for example the value listed in the image README\n  WORKSPACE: ios/App.xcworkspace\n  SCHEME: App\n\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: macos-15 # choose the image that ships the required Xcode\n    timeout-minutes: 40\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v7\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n\n      - name: Select Xcode\n        run: sudo xcode-select -s \"/Applications/Xcode_\${XCODE_VERSION}.app\"\n\n      - uses: actions/cache@<sha> # v6\n        with:\n          path: SourcePackages\n          key: spm-\${{ runner.os }}-\${{ env.XCODE_VERSION }}-\${{ hashFiles('**/Package.resolved') }}\n          restore-keys: spm-\${{ runner.os }}-\${{ env.XCODE_VERSION }}-\n\n      - name: Lint\n        run: swiftlint lint --strict # assumes SwiftLint is provided (SPM plugin, Mint or Homebrew)\n\n      - name: Unit tests\n        run: |\n          set -o pipefail\n          xcodebuild test \\\n            -workspace \"\$WORKSPACE\" \\\n            -scheme \"\$SCHEME\" \\\n            -destination 'platform=iOS Simulator,name=<simulator-on-runner>,OS=latest' \\\n            -clonedSourcePackagesDirPath SourcePackages \\\n            -disableAutomaticPackageResolution \\\n            -enableCodeCoverage YES \\\n            -resultBundlePath \"\$RUNNER_TEMP/Tests.xcresult\" \\\n            CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO\n\n      - name: Coverage gate\n        env:\n          MIN_COVERAGE: '0.70'\n        run: |\n          COVERAGE=\$(xcrun xccov view --report --json \"\$RUNNER_TEMP/Tests.xcresult\" | jq '.lineCoverage')\n          echo \"Line coverage: \$COVERAGE\"\n          awk -v c=\"\$COVERAGE\" -v min=\"\$MIN_COVERAGE\" 'BEGIN { exit (c + 0 < min + 0) }'\n\n      - name: Upload result bundle\n        if: failure()\n        uses: actions/upload-artifact@<sha> # v7\n        with:\n          name: test-results\n          path: \${{ runner.temp }}/Tests.xcresult\n          retention-days: 7\n\n  testflight:\n    needs: test\n    if: startsWith(github.ref, 'refs/tags/v')\n    runs-on: macos-15\n    timeout-minutes: 60\n    environment: testflight # required reviewers + tag rule v*\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v7\n        with:\n          persist-credentials: false\n\n      - name: Select Xcode\n        run: sudo xcode-select -s \"/Applications/Xcode_\${XCODE_VERSION}.app\"\n\n      - uses: actions/cache/restore@<sha> # v6\n        with:\n          path: SourcePackages\n          key: spm-\${{ runner.os }}-\${{ env.XCODE_VERSION }}-\${{ hashFiles('**/Package.resolved') }}\n\n      - uses: ruby/setup-ruby@<sha> # v1\n        with:\n          bundler-cache: true\n\n      - name: Compute build number\n        env:\n          BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET: \${{ vars.BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET }}\n        run: |\n          echo \"BUILD_NUMBER=\$(( GITHUB_RUN_NUMBER + \${BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET:-0} ))\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n          echo \"MARKETING_VERSION=\${GITHUB_REF_NAME#v}\" >> \"\$GITHUB_ENV\"\n\n      - name: Build and upload to TestFlight\n        env:\n          ASC_KEY_ID: \${{ secrets.ASC_KEY_ID }}\n          ASC_ISSUER_ID: \${{ secrets.ASC_ISSUER_ID }}\n          ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64: \${{ secrets.ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64 }}\n          MATCH_PASSWORD: \${{ secrets.MATCH_PASSWORD }}\n          MATCH_GIT_BASIC_AUTHORIZATION: \${{ secrets.MATCH_GIT_BASIC_AUTHORIZATION }}\n        run: bundle exec fastlane ios beta\n\n      - name: Archive dSYMs\n        uses: actions/upload-artifact@<sha> # v7\n        with:\n          name: dsyms-\${{ env.BUILD_NUMBER }}\n          path: build/*.dSYM.zip\n          retention-days: 90\n```\n\nNotes:\n- The release job uses `actions/cache/restore` only, so a PR cannot poison what the release build links.\n- `-disableAutomaticPackageResolution` makes the build fail if `Package.resolved` is out of date instead\n  of silently resolving new versions.\n- `paths:` keeps Android-only PRs off macOS runners. If the iOS check is required on `main`, add a\n  lightweight always-running job with the same name or the PR will wait for a check that never starts.\n\n## Fastlane (`fastlane/Fastfile`)\n\n```ruby\ndefault_platform(:ios)\n\nplatform :ios do\n  lane :beta do\n    setup_ci # temporary keychain on CI, no changes to the login keychain\n\n    api_key = app_store_connect_api_key(\n      key_id: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_KEY_ID\"),\n      issuer_id: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_ISSUER_ID\"),\n      key_content: ENV.fetch(\"ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64\"),\n      is_key_content_base64: true\n    )\n\n    match(type: \"appstore\", readonly: true, api_key: api_key)\n\n    build_app(\n      workspace: \"ios/App.xcworkspace\",\n      scheme: \"App\",\n      configuration: \"Release\",\n      export_method: \"app-store\",\n      cloned_source_packages_path: \"SourcePackages\",\n      output_directory: \"build\",\n      xcargs: \"CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION=#{ENV.fetch('BUILD_NUMBER')} MARKETING_VERSION=#{ENV.fetch('MARKETING_VERSION')}\"\n    )\n\n    upload_to_testflight(\n      api_key: api_key,\n      skip_waiting_for_build_processing: true\n    )\n  end\nend\n```\n\n`fastlane/Matchfile`:\n\n```ruby\ngit_url(\"https://github.com/<org>/<certificates-repo>.git\")\nstorage_mode(\"git\")\ntype(\"appstore\")\napp_identifier([\"com.example.app\"])\n```\n\n- `MATCH_GIT_BASIC_AUTHORIZATION` is base64 of `user:token` for a token that can only read the\n  certificates repository. A deploy key or a cloud storage backend for `match` is equally fine.\n- `MATCH_PASSWORD` decrypts the repository. Losing it means regenerating certificates; store it in a\n  password manager as well as in CI.\n- `CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION` and `MARKETING_VERSION` overrides work when `Info.plist` uses\n  `\$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)` and `\$(MARKETING_VERSION)`, which is the default for current Xcode templates.\n  Older projects with hardcoded values need that change first.\n- Build numbers must increase for each upload of the same marketing version. Set `BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET`\n  above the highest build already in App Store Connect when migrating.\n- Upload dSYMs to the crash reporter in the same lane (for example `upload_symbols_to_crashlytics`) when\n  the project uses one.\n\n## App Store Connect API key\n\n- Created in App Store Connect under Users and Access, Integrations. The `.p8` file can be downloaded only once.\n- Store as `ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64` (`base64 -i AuthKey_XXXX.p8`), with `ASC_KEY_ID` and `ASC_ISSUER_ID`, as\n  environment secrets of `testflight`.\n- Use a team key with the lowest role that can upload and manage TestFlight for the lanes in use. Revoke\n  it when the pipeline is retired or the key may have leaked.\n- Never use `FASTLANE_USER` / `FASTLANE_PASSWORD`, app-specific passwords or `FASTLANE_SESSION` in CI.\n\n## Firebase App Distribution (ad hoc builds)\n\n- Needs an `adhoc` (or enterprise) profile from `match(type: \"adhoc\", readonly: true)` and tester devices\n  registered in the profile. TestFlight internal testing is usually simpler for iOS QA.\n- Upload with the Firebase CLI (`firebase appdistribution:distribute <ipa> --app <id> --groups <group>`)\n  after authenticating with OIDC via `google-github-actions/auth`, as in the Android reference.\n\n## Secrets and variables\n\n| Name | Kind | Scope |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ASC_KEY_ID`, `ASC_ISSUER_ID`, `ASC_KEY_P8_BASE64` | secret | env `testflight` |\n| `MATCH_PASSWORD`, `MATCH_GIT_BASIC_AUTHORIZATION` | secret | env `testflight` |\n| `BUILD_NUMBER_OFFSET` | variable | repository |\n",
  },
  "mobile-code-review": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-code-review\ndescription: \"Reviews Kotlin, Java, Swift and Dart code and diffs in Android, iOS and Flutter apps for correctness, concurrency, lifecycle leaks, UI performance, architecture, error handling, tests, accessibility and i18n. Reports Blocker, Major, Minor and Nit findings with file:line and fix snippets, then an Approve or Request changes verdict. Use when reviewing a pull request, merge request, diff, branch or file before merging mobile code.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile code review\n\nReview mobile code the way a senior Android, iOS and Flutter engineer would: find the defects that will crash,\nleak, hang or mislead users in production, explain why each one matters, and show the fix.\n\n## When to use\n\n- A pull request, merge request, branch, commit range or single file needs review.\n- The user asks \"review this\", \"is this safe to merge\", \"what is wrong with this code\".\n- Before merging changes in Kotlin, Java, Swift, Objective-C interop or Dart code.\n- After the agent itself wrote mobile code and should check it before handing it over.\n\nNot for: a full security audit (use `mobile-security`), or designing an architecture from scratch\n(use `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Establish scope.\n   - Diff against the merge base, not the tip of main: `git diff --merge-base origin/main`.\n   - Read the MR description and linked ticket so findings are judged against intent.\n2. Identify platforms and load the matching reference before reviewing:\n   [Kotlin](references/kotlin.md), [Java](references/java.md), [Swift](references/swift.md),\n   [Dart and Flutter](references/dart.md).\n3. Read every changed file in full, plus direct callers and callees of changed functions.\n   A hunk out of context hides most lifecycle and threading bugs.\n4. Pass over the change once per dimension, in this order:\n   correctness, concurrency, lifecycle and memory, security and privacy, UI performance,\n   architecture, error handling, tests, accessibility, i18n.\n5. Verify each candidate finding before reporting it.\n   - Trace the code path. Check it is not already handled elsewhere (base class, interceptor, wrapper).\n   - If it cannot be confirmed from the code, report it as a question, not a defect.\n6. Assign a severity (see below) and write a minimal fix snippet for every Blocker and Major.\n7. Give the verdict. Do not edit files unless the user asks for fixes.\n\n## Severity levels\n\n| Severity | Meaning | Merge impact |\n|---|---|---|\n| Blocker | Crash, data loss or corruption, security or privacy breach, broken build, store rejection | Must fix before merge |\n| Major | Wrong behaviour on a realistic path, leak, race, ANR or hang, swallowed error, missing test for new logic, broken layer boundary | Fix before merge, or defer only with a ticket |\n| Minor | Maintainability, small inefficiency, unclear naming that misleads, missing edge-case test | Should fix, may be a follow-up |\n| Nit | Preference or polish. Never blocks | Optional |\n\nRules for severity:\n- Rate by consequence and likelihood, not by how easy the fix is.\n- Do not report what the formatter or linter already enforces.\n- One root cause is one finding. List extra locations inside it.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Coroutines and tasks are scoped to an owner and cancellable\n\nWork outlives its screen when launched in a global scope. It leaks memory and writes to dead UI.\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD\nGlobalScope.launch { repo.sync() }\n\n// GOOD\nviewModelScope.launch { repo.sync() }\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: unstructured task keeps running after the view disappears\n.onAppear { Task { await model.load() } }\n\n// GOOD: cancelled automatically when the view disappears\n.task { await model.load() }\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD\n_sub = stream.listen(_onData); // never cancelled\n\n// GOOD\n@override\nvoid dispose() {\n  _sub?.cancel();\n  super.dispose();\n}\n```\n\nJava:\n```java\n// BAD: executor never shut down, callbacks hit a destroyed Activity\nexecutor.execute(() -> textView.setText(load()));\n\n// GOOD: work in a ViewModel, result exposed as LiveData, executor shut down in onCleared()\nexecutor.execute(() -> result.postValue(load()));\n```\n\n### 2. Cancellation is never swallowed\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: runCatching also catches CancellationException and breaks structured concurrency\nval user = runCatching { api.user() }.getOrNull()\n\n// GOOD\nval user = try {\n    api.user()\n} catch (e: IOException) {\n    null\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: loop ignores cancellation\nfor id in ids { await upload(id) }\n\n// GOOD\nfor id in ids {\n    try Task.checkCancellation()\n    await upload(id)\n}\n```\n\n### 3. UI state is touched only on the main thread or actor\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: hardcoded dispatcher, untestable and easy to misuse\nwithContext(Dispatchers.IO) { dao.insert(item) }\n\n// GOOD: injected dispatcher\nclass Repo(private val io: CoroutineDispatcher) {\n    suspend fun save(item: Item) = withContext(io) { dao.insert(item) }\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: UI state mutated from an arbitrary executor\nfinal class FeedModel: ObservableObject { @Published var items: [Item] = [] }\n\n// GOOD\n@MainActor @Observable\nfinal class FeedModel { var items: [Item] = [] }\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD: 5 MB JSON parsed on the UI isolate, drops frames\nfinal data = jsonDecode(body);\n\n// GOOD\nfinal data = await Isolate.run(() => jsonDecode(body));\n```\n\n### 4. No long-lived references to short-lived objects\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: Activity leaked for the lifetime of the process\nobject Analytics { lateinit var context: Context }\n\n// GOOD\nclass Analytics @Inject constructor(@ApplicationContext private val context: Context)\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: self -> timer -> closure -> self\ntimer = Timer.scheduledTimer(withTimeInterval: 1, repeats: true) { _ in self.tick() }\n\n// GOOD\ntimer = Timer.scheduledTimer(withTimeInterval: 1, repeats: true) { [weak self] _ in self?.tick() }\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD: controller created in build, never disposed\nWidget build(BuildContext context) => TextField(controller: TextEditingController());\n\n// GOOD: owned by the State object, disposed in dispose()\nlate final _controller = TextEditingController();\n```\n\n### 5. Async UI code re-checks lifecycle after every suspension point\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD\nawait repo.save();\nNavigator.of(context).pop();\n\n// GOOD\nawait repo.save();\nif (!context.mounted) return;\nNavigator.of(context).pop();\n```\n\nKotlin (Compose):\n```kotlin\n// BAD: collects while the app is in the background\nval state by viewModel.state.collectAsState()\n\n// GOOD\nval state by viewModel.state.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()\n```\n\n### 6. UI recomposes and rebuilds only what changed\n\nKotlin (Compose):\n```kotlin\n// BAD: no key, item state jumps on reorder\nLazyColumn { items(messages) { MessageRow(it) } }\n\n// GOOD\nLazyColumn { items(messages, key = { it.id }) { MessageRow(it) } }\n```\n\nSwift (SwiftUI):\n```swift\n// BAD: index identity, wrong rows animate on insert\nForEach(items.indices, id: \\.self) { i in Row(item: items[i]) }\n\n// GOOD: Item is Identifiable with a stable id\nForEach(items) { item in Row(item: item) }\n```\n\nDart (Flutter):\n```dart\n// BAD: whole screen rebuilds on every tick\nsetState(() => _seconds++);\n\n// GOOD: only the label listens\nValueListenableBuilder<int>(\n  valueListenable: _seconds,\n  builder: (context, value, _) => Text('\$value'),\n)\n```\n\n### 7. Errors are handled where they can be acted on, and never silently dropped\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD\ntry { upload() } catch (e: Exception) { }\n\n// GOOD\ntry {\n    upload()\n} catch (e: IOException) {\n    _state.update { it.copy(error = UiError.Network) }\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD\nlet data = try? await api.fetch()\n\n// GOOD\ndo {\n    state = .loaded(try await api.fetch())\n} catch {\n    state = .failed(error)\n}\n```\n\n### 8. Layer boundaries hold\n\n- ViewModels and domain code do not import `android.view`, `UIKit` views or `BuildContext`.\n- Domain models are not network DTOs or database entities. Map at the data-layer edge.\n- UI does not call repositories or APIs directly.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: ViewModel holds a View and returns a Retrofit DTO\nclass ProfileViewModel(private val view: TextView, private val api: Api)\n\n// GOOD\nclass ProfileViewModel(private val getProfile: GetProfileUseCase) : ViewModel()\n```\n\n### 9. New logic ships with tests, and tests are deterministic\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: real delay, flaky and slow\nThread.sleep(500)\n\n// GOOD: virtual time, runs instantly\n@Test fun retriesAfterBackoff() = runTest {\n    val repo = Repo(api = failingOnceApi, io = StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler))\n    val result = async { repo.load() }\n    advanceTimeBy(500)\n    assertEquals(expected, result.await())\n}\n```\n\nSwift: use `await` on the operation under test, not `sleep`. Dart: use `fakeAsync` or `tester.pump(duration)`.\n\n### 10. Accessibility and i18n are part of correctness\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD\nIcon(Icons.Default.Delete, contentDescription = null) // actionable icon, no label\n\n// GOOD\nIcon(Icons.Default.Delete, contentDescription = stringResource(R.string.delete))\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD: concatenated String is shown verbatim, never localized, no plural rules\nText(\"You have \" + String(count) + \" messages\")\n\n// GOOD: localized key with a plural variation in the String Catalog\nText(\"You have \\(count) messages\", comment: \"Inbox count\")\n```\n\n- Touch targets at least 48dp (Android) or 44pt (iOS).\n- Text scales with system font size. No fixed heights on text containers.\n- Use start/end, not left/right, so RTL layouts work.\n- Never build sentences by concatenating translated fragments.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Reviewing only the diff hunks without opening the full file.\n- Reporting style issues the linter already catches.\n- \"Consider refactoring\" with no reason and no snippet.\n- Twenty Nits hiding one Blocker. Order by severity, cap Nits at five.\n- Guessing at a bug the code does not show. Ask instead.\n- Approving with an open Blocker or Major because \"it was already like that\". Pre-existing issues in\n  touched lines are in scope; elsewhere, mention them once as out of scope.\n- Rewriting the author's whole function in the fix snippet. Show the smallest change.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Read the MR description, ticket and every changed file in full\n- [ ] Coroutines, Tasks, Futures and Streams have an owner and are cancelled\n- [ ] Cancellation is propagated (no catch-all over suspending or async code)\n- [ ] UI state mutated only on main thread or `@MainActor`\n- [ ] No Activity, Context, View or BuildContext stored beyond its lifecycle\n- [ ] Closures capturing `self` checked for cycles; controllers and subscriptions disposed\n- [ ] Lists have stable keys or identity; no heavy work in composition, body or build\n- [ ] No swallowed errors; user-facing failure state exists\n- [ ] No secrets, tokens or personal data in code or logs (escalate to `mobile-security`)\n- [ ] Layer boundaries respected; DTOs mapped at the data layer\n- [ ] New logic has deterministic tests\n- [ ] Accessibility labels, touch targets, dynamic text size\n- [ ] User-visible strings localized, plurals handled, RTL safe\n- [ ] Every Blocker and Major has a fix snippet\n\n## Output format\n\n~~~markdown\n## Review: <MR title or scope>\n\nScope: <files / commits reviewed>. Platforms: <Android | iOS | Flutter>.\n\n### Findings\n\n1. [Blocker] path/to/File.kt:42 -- <one-line issue>\n   Why: <consequence for users or the codebase>\n   Fix:\n   ```kotlin\n   <minimal snippet>\n   ```\n\n2. [Major] path/to/View.swift:88 -- <issue>\n   Why: ...\n   Fix: ...\n\n### Questions\n- path/to/file.dart:17 -- <thing that could not be confirmed from the code>\n\n### Clean\n- <dimensions checked with no findings, for example \"Concurrency: no issues\">\n\n### Verdict\nRequest changes -- 1 Blocker, 1 Major.\n~~~\n\nVerdict rule: any Blocker or Major means Request changes. Otherwise Approve, listing Minor and Nit items as optional.\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-security` -- deep MASVS audit when the change touches storage, network, auth or crypto\n- `mobile-kotlin`, `mobile-java`, `mobile-swift`, `mobile-flutter` -- language rules the review applies\n- `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`, `mobile-design-patterns` -- architecture expectations\n- `mobile-merge-request` -- MR description, commits and reviewer etiquette\n- `mobile-testing` -- what test coverage a change should come with\n- `mobile-performance` -- measuring a suspected jank, startup or memory regression\n- `mobile-accessibility` -- full accessibility audit against WCAG 2.2\n",
    "references/dart.md":
        "# Dart and Flutter review reference\n\nLoad this when the change contains `.dart` files. Assume Dart 3 with sound null safety.\n\n## Quick scan\n\n| Pattern | Why | Usual severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| `context` used after `await` without a `mounted` check | Uses a dead element, wrong navigator, crash | Major |\n| `setState` after an `await` without `if (!mounted) return;` | Exception on disposed State | Major |\n| `.listen(` with the subscription not stored and cancelled | Leak, callbacks on disposed widget | Major |\n| `TextEditingController(`, `AnimationController(`, `ScrollController(`, `FocusNode(` without `dispose` | Leak | Major |\n| `FutureBuilder(future: fetch()` in `build` | Refetches on every rebuild | Major |\n| `!` on values from network, platform or JSON | Crash | Major |\n| `catch (e) {}` / `catch (_) {}` | Swallowed error | Major |\n| Widget-returning helper methods (`Widget _buildHeader()`) in large widgets | Loses const and rebuild granularity | Minor |\n| `ListView(children: list.map(...).toList())` for long lists | Builds every item up front | Major for unbounded lists |\n| Missing `const` on constructors and widgets | Extra rebuild work | Nit (the linter usually catches it) |\n| `print(` / `debugPrint(` with tokens, emails, device IDs | Personal data in logs | Major, see `mobile-security` |\n\n## Async and lifecycle\n\n```dart\n// BAD\nFuture<void> _save() async {\n  await repo.save(form);\n  ScaffoldMessenger.of(context).showSnackBar(const SnackBar(content: Text('Saved')));\n}\n\n// GOOD\nFuture<void> _save() async {\n  await repo.save(form);\n  if (!mounted) return; // inside State; use context.mounted when only a BuildContext is available\n  ScaffoldMessenger.of(context).showSnackBar(const SnackBar(content: Text('Saved')));\n}\n```\n\n- Every controller, `FocusNode`, `StreamSubscription`, `Timer` and `AnimationController` created by a\n  `State` is disposed or cancelled in `dispose()`.\n- `StreamController`s are closed by their owner.\n- Fire-and-forget futures are explicit: wrap in `unawaited(...)` and enable the `unawaited_futures` or\n  `discarded_futures` lint.\n\n## Concurrency\n\n- CPU-heavy work (large JSON, image processing, crypto) runs in `Isolate.run` or `compute`.\n- Data sent to isolates is sendable; closures passed to `Isolate.run` do not capture large objects or\n  `BuildContext`.\n\n## Widgets and performance\n\n- `build` is pure and cheap: no network calls, no controller creation, no future creation.\n\n```dart\n// BAD: new future each rebuild, request fires repeatedly\n@override\nWidget build(BuildContext context) => FutureBuilder(future: api.fetchUser(), builder: _builder);\n\n// GOOD: future created once\nlate final Future<User> _user = api.fetchUser();\n\n@override\nWidget build(BuildContext context) => FutureBuilder(future: _user, builder: _builder);\n```\n\n- Split large widgets into small `StatelessWidget` classes with `const` constructors rather than private\n  methods returning widgets.\n- Keys: `ValueKey(item.id)` on stateful list items that can reorder. `GlobalKey` only when cross-tree access\n  is really needed.\n- Long or unbounded lists use `ListView.builder` / `SliverList.builder`.\n- Large images are decoded at display size (`cacheWidth` / `cacheHeight` or `ResizeImage`).\n- State is scoped as low as possible; a `setState` at the top of a screen rebuilds everything below it.\n\n## State management\n\n- Riverpod: `ref.watch` in `build`, `ref.read` in callbacks, `ref.listen` for side effects such as\n  navigation and snackbars. Providers that hold screen state use `autoDispose` unless they must persist.\n- Bloc: no `emit` after the handler completes or after `close`; long streams use `emit.forEach` /\n  `emit.onEach`. UI side effects go through `BlocListener`, not `BlocBuilder`.\n- Business logic does not live in widgets.\n\n## Errors and null safety\n\n- Catch specific types (`on SocketException`, `on FormatException`, domain exceptions). Never catch `Error`\n  subclasses such as `StateError` or `TypeError` to keep going; they indicate bugs.\n- JSON parsing validates types instead of `as` casts on untrusted data.\n- Sealed classes plus exhaustive `switch` for result and state types; the compiler then flags missing cases.\n\n## Platform integration\n\n- Platform channels use Pigeon or a typed wrapper; channel names and method names are not stringly duplicated.\n- Native side handlers run on the platform main thread unless a background task queue is configured;\n  heavy native work is moved off it.\n- New permissions, `Info.plist` keys or `AndroidManifest.xml` entries are called out in the MR.\n\n## Accessibility and i18n\n\n- Icon-only buttons have a `tooltip` or `Semantics(label: ...)`.\n- Text uses theme styles and respects text scaling; no fixed-height containers around text.\n- Strings come from `AppLocalizations` (gen-l10n) with ICU plurals, not string concatenation.\n- `EdgeInsetsDirectional` and `AlignmentDirectional` for RTL support.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Unit tests with `package:test`; mocks with `mocktail`; prefer fakes for repositories.\n- Widget tests use `pump(duration)` for timed behaviour; `pumpAndSettle` never returns with infinite\n  animations such as a looping progress indicator.\n- Golden tests run on one pinned platform in CI because font rendering differs.\n- Time-dependent logic uses `fakeAsync` or an injected clock.\n",
    "references/java.md":
        "# Java (Android) review reference\n\nLoad this when the change contains `.java` files. Java on Android is usually legacy code, so focus on leaks,\nthreading and Kotlin interop rather than asking for a rewrite.\n\n## Quick scan\n\n| Pattern | Why | Usual severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| `static Context`, `static Activity`, `static View` | Leaks the Activity for the process lifetime | Major |\n| Non-static inner `Handler`, `Runnable`, `AsyncTask`, listener in an Activity | Implicit outer reference leaks the Activity | Major |\n| `new Handler()` with no Looper | Deprecated, binds to whatever thread calls it | Minor |\n| `AsyncTask` | Deprecated since API 30, leaks and loses results on rotation | Major in new code |\n| `Executors.new...` with no `shutdown()` | Thread leak | Major |\n| `catch (InterruptedException e) {}` | Interrupt status lost | Major |\n| `catch (Exception e) {}` / `e.printStackTrace()` | Swallowed error | Major |\n| `notifyDataSetChanged()` | Full rebind, no animations | Minor |\n| `observe(this, ...)` in a Fragment | Duplicate observers after view recreation | Major |\n| `equals` without `hashCode` (or the reverse) | Broken in sets, maps, DiffUtil | Major |\n| `Stream`, `Cursor` or `InputStream` not closed | Resource leak | Major |\n\n## Leaks and lifecycle\n\n```java\n// BAD: inner class holds MainActivity, message queue holds the Handler\nprivate final Handler handler = new Handler() {\n    @Override public void handleMessage(Message msg) { textView.setText(\"done\"); }\n};\n\n// GOOD: no outer reference, explicit Looper, callbacks removed in onDestroy\nprivate final Handler handler = new Handler(Looper.getMainLooper());\n\n@Override protected void onDestroy() {\n    handler.removeCallbacksAndMessages(null);\n    super.onDestroy();\n}\n```\n\n- Fragments observe with `getViewLifecycleOwner()` and null the binding in `onDestroyView()`.\n- Listeners registered in `onStart` / `onResume` are removed in the matching `onStop` / `onPause`.\n\n## Threading\n\n- Background work belongs in a ViewModel or repository using an `ExecutorService` that is shut down in\n  `onCleared()`, not in the Activity.\n- `LiveData.setValue` on the main thread only; `postValue` from background threads. `postValue` coalesces:\n  if called twice quickly only the last value is delivered. Flag it when every value matters.\n- Shared mutable fields accessed from multiple threads are `volatile`, guarded by `synchronized`, or use\n  `java.util.concurrent.atomic` types. Check-then-act on a `ConcurrentHashMap` still needs `computeIfAbsent`\n  or similar.\n\n```java\n// BAD: interrupt swallowed, thread cannot be stopped cleanly\ntry { Thread.sleep(1000); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }\n\n// GOOD\ntry {\n    Thread.sleep(1000);\n} catch (InterruptedException e) {\n    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n    return;\n}\n```\n\n## Resources\n\n```java\n// BAD\nCursor c = db.query(...);\nwhile (c.moveToNext()) { ... }\n\n// GOOD\ntry (Cursor c = db.query(...)) {\n    while (c.moveToNext()) { ... }\n}\n```\n\n## Value objects and collections\n\n- `equals` and `hashCode` are overridden together, over the same fields.\n- `DiffUtil.ItemCallback.areItemsTheSame` compares ids; `areContentsTheSame` compares content\n  (usually via `equals`).\n- Getters do not expose internal mutable collections:\n\n```java\n// BAD\npublic List<Item> getItems() { return items; }\n\n// GOOD\npublic List<Item> getItems() { return Collections.unmodifiableList(items); }\n```\n\n## Kotlin interop\n\n- Public API used from Kotlin carries nullability annotations. Prefer JSpecify\n  (`org.jspecify.annotations.Nullable`, `@NullMarked` on the package) or AndroidX `@Nullable` / `@NonNull`.\n  Without them Kotlin sees platform types and null checks disappear.\n- Static utilities that Kotlin callers use often are candidates for migration, but migration is out of scope\n  for a review unless the MR is about it.\n\n## Testing\n\n- JUnit 4 or 5 with Mockito for unit tests; `InstantTaskExecutorRule` when LiveData is involved.\n- Espresso tests use `IdlingResource` or test executors, never `Thread.sleep`.\n",
    "references/kotlin.md":
        "# Kotlin and Android review reference\n\nLoad this when the change contains `.kt` or `.kts` files. Each item lists what to look for and the usual severity.\n\n## Quick scan\n\nSearch the diff for these first. Each hit needs a closer look, not an automatic finding.\n\n| Pattern | Why | Usual severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| `GlobalScope`, `CoroutineScope(` in a class with no `cancel()` | Unowned work, leaks | Major |\n| `runBlocking` outside tests and `main` | Blocks the calling thread, ANR on main | Major |\n| `runCatching {` around suspend calls | Swallows `CancellationException` | Major |\n| `catch (e: Exception)` / `catch (e: Throwable)` in suspend code | Same, unless it rethrows cancellation | Major |\n| `Dispatchers.IO` / `Dispatchers.Default` hardcoded in a class | Untestable, hidden threading | Minor |\n| `!!` | Crash on unexpected null | Major if input is external |\n| `lateinit var` read before guaranteed init | `UninitializedPropertyAccessException` | Major |\n| `collectAsState()` | Collects while app is backgrounded | Minor |\n| `launchWhenStarted`, `launchWhenResumed` | Deprecated, suspends instead of cancelling | Minor |\n| `fallbackToDestructiveMigration` | Deletes user data on schema change | Blocker in release builds |\n| `allowMainThreadQueries` | Jank and ANR | Major |\n| `registerReceiver(` without an export flag | Crash when targeting Android 14+ | Blocker |\n| `Log.d(` / `println(` with tokens, emails, device IDs | Personal data in logs | Major, see `mobile-security` |\n\n## Coroutines and Flow\n\n- Every `launch` and `async` runs in a scope tied to a lifecycle: `viewModelScope`, `lifecycleScope`,\n  or an injected application scope for work that must outlive a screen.\n- Prefer `suspend` functions over functions that launch internally and return `Unit`. The caller owns the scope.\n- Injected dispatchers, so tests can replace them.\n- `CancellationException` is rethrown. A generic `catch` in suspend code must rethrow it:\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD\ntry { repo.refresh() } catch (e: Exception) { log(e) }\n\n// GOOD\ntry {\n    repo.refresh()\n} catch (e: CancellationException) {\n    throw e\n} catch (e: IOException) {\n    log(e)\n}\n```\n\n- Mutable state is private; the public type is read-only.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: anyone can push state, and read-modify-write races\nval state = MutableStateFlow(UiState())\nfun select(id: String) { state.value = state.value.copy(selected = id) }\n\n// GOOD\nprivate val _state = MutableStateFlow(UiState())\nval state: StateFlow<UiState> = _state.asStateFlow()\nfun select(id: String) = _state.update { it.copy(selected = id) }\n```\n\n- Cold flows exposed to UI are converted once, not per collector:\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: new upstream per collector, restarts on rotation\nval items: Flow<List<Item>> get() = repo.observe()\n\n// GOOD\nval items: StateFlow<List<Item>> = repo.observe()\n    .stateIn(viewModelScope, SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5_000), emptyList())\n```\n\n- `flowOn` changes the upstream context only. Check it is placed after the operators that need it.\n- One-off events: prefer modelling them as state the UI consumes and acknowledges. If a `Channel` is used,\n  check it is collected with `repeatOnLifecycle` so events are not delivered to a stopped UI.\n- Fragments collect with `viewLifecycleOwner.lifecycleScope` plus `repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED)`,\n  never `lifecycleScope` alone.\n\n## Jetpack Compose\n\n- Composables do not perform side effects in the body. Use `LaunchedEffect`, `DisposableEffect`,\n  or event callbacks.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: runs on every recomposition\n@Composable fun Screen(vm: ScreenViewModel) {\n    vm.load()\n}\n\n// GOOD\n@Composable fun Screen(vm: ScreenViewModel) {\n    LaunchedEffect(Unit) { vm.load() }\n}\n```\n\n- `LaunchedEffect` keys match the inputs the effect reads. `LaunchedEffect(Unit)` that reads a changing\n  parameter is a bug; use the parameter as key or `rememberUpdatedState`.\n- `remember` keys include every input of the calculation, otherwise the cached value is stale.\n- High-frequency state (scroll, animation) is read in a lambda modifier or `derivedStateOf`:\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: recomposes on every scroll pixel\nval showFab = listState.firstVisibleItemIndex > 0\n\n// GOOD\nval showFab by remember { derivedStateOf { listState.firstVisibleItemIndex > 0 } }\n```\n\n- Stability: with strong skipping (default since Kotlin 2.0.20), unstable parameters are compared by\n  instance. A list rebuilt on every recomposition defeats skipping. Hoist it, `remember` it, or use\n  `kotlinx.collections.immutable`.\n- Lazy lists use `key` and, for mixed rows, `contentType`.\n- Reusable composables take `modifier: Modifier = Modifier` as the first optional parameter and apply it\n  to the root element only.\n- Composables below the screen level receive state and lambdas, not the ViewModel.\n- `rememberSaveable` for user input that must survive process death and rotation.\n\n## Lifecycle and Android components\n\n- No `Activity`, `Fragment`, `View` or `Context` (other than application context) in singletons, companion\n  objects or ViewModels.\n- Fragment view bindings are cleared in `onDestroyView`.\n- Broadcast receivers registered at runtime use `ContextCompat.registerReceiver` with\n  `RECEIVER_EXPORTED` or `RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED`, and are unregistered.\n- Foreground services declare `android:foregroundServiceType` and the matching permission (required when\n  targeting Android 14+). Check the type matches what the service actually does.\n- Deferrable background work uses WorkManager, not a service or a coroutine in `Application`.\n- New permissions in the manifest are justified in the MR and requested at the point of use.\n\n## Hilt and dependency injection\n\n- `@ApplicationContext` for context in singletons.\n- Scope annotations match lifetime: no `@Singleton` holding screen state.\n- No service locator calls (`EntryPoints.get`) inside business logic.\n\n## Room and persistence\n\n- DAO functions are `suspend` or return `Flow`.\n- Every schema version bump has a `Migration` or `AutoMigration`, and `exportSchema = true`.\n- Transactions wrap multi-step writes (`@Transaction` or `withTransaction`).\n\n## Error handling and nullability\n\n- `!!` only where the invariant is local and obvious; otherwise `requireNotNull(x) { \"reason\" }` or a\n  safe call with a fallback.\n- Network and IO results are mapped to a domain error type the UI can render, not raw exceptions.\n- `Result` or sealed result types are not mixed with thrown exceptions in the same layer.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `runTest` for coroutines; `StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler)` injected; `Dispatchers.setMain` in a\n  rule or extension when `viewModelScope` is used.\n- Turbine (`flow.test { ... }`) for Flow assertions instead of `toList()` with timing hacks.\n- MockK: `coEvery` / `coVerify` for suspend functions. Prefer fakes for repositories.\n- Compose UI tests find nodes by semantics (text, content description, test tag), not by position.\n\n## Build and dependencies\n\n- New dependencies are added to `libs.versions.toml`, not hardcoded in module build files.\n- KSP preferred over kapt for new annotation processors.\n- R8 keep rules added for new reflection, serialization or JNI code.\n- `minSdk`, `targetSdk` or `compileSdk` changes are called out explicitly in the MR.\n",
    "references/swift.md":
        "# Swift and iOS review reference\n\nLoad this when the change contains `.swift` files. Assume Swift 6 language mode with strict concurrency\nunless the project settings say otherwise.\n\n## Quick scan\n\n| Pattern | Why | Usual severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| `!` force unwrap, `try!`, `as!` | Crash on unexpected input | Major, Blocker on external data |\n| Implicitly unwrapped `var x: T!` outside IBOutlets | Crash if read early | Minor |\n| Escaping closure using `self` without `[weak self]`, stored by `self` | Retain cycle | Major |\n| `unowned self` | Crash if the owner is gone first | Major unless lifetime is guaranteed |\n| `@unchecked Sendable` | Silences data-race checking | Major unless a lock guards every field |\n| `nonisolated(unsafe)` | Same | Major unless justified |\n| `DispatchQueue.main.async` inside `async` code | Mixes models, loses isolation checking | Minor |\n| `Task.detached` | Drops actor context and priority | Minor, needs a reason |\n| `DispatchSemaphore.wait()` / `.sync` on main | Deadlock or hang | Blocker |\n| `@ObservedObject var model = Model()` | Model recreated on every parent redraw | Major |\n| `ForEach(..., id: \\.self)` over non-unique values | Wrong rows update, crashes on duplicates | Major |\n| `AnyView` in lists | Defeats diffing | Minor |\n| `print(` with tokens, emails, device IDs | Personal data in logs | Major, see `mobile-security` |\n\n## Concurrency\n\n- UI-facing models are `@MainActor`. View models built with `@Observable` are usually `@MainActor final class`.\n- Types crossing isolation boundaries are `Sendable`. Prefer value types; make reference types final with\n  immutable state or protect them with an actor.\n- Actor reentrancy: state read before an `await` may have changed after it.\n\n```swift\n// BAD: two concurrent calls both see nil and both download\nactor ImageCache {\n    var images: [URL: Image] = [:]\n    func image(for url: URL) async throws -> Image {\n        if let cached = images[url] { return cached }\n        let image = try await download(url)\n        images[url] = image\n        return image\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: cache the in-flight task, not just the result\nactor ImageCache {\n    private var tasks: [URL: Task<Image, Error>] = [:]\n    func image(for url: URL) async throws -> Image {\n        if let task = tasks[url] { return try await task.value }\n        let task = Task { try await download(url) }\n        tasks[url] = task\n        return try await task.value\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Long-running `for await` loops inside a `Task` stored by a class capture `self` until the loop ends,\n  so `deinit` never runs. Use `[weak self]` inside the loop body, or tie the work to `.task` in the view.\n\n```swift\n// BAD: self is retained by the task forever, deinit never cancels it\ntask = Task {\n    for await value in stream { self.handle(value) }\n}\n\n// GOOD\ntask = Task { [weak self] in\n    for await value in stream {\n        guard let self else { return }\n        self.handle(value)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Cancellation is checked in long loops (`try Task.checkCancellation()` or `Task.isCancelled`).\n- `withTaskGroup` / `withThrowingTaskGroup` instead of manually spawning many `Task {}` and joining.\n\n## Memory\n\n- Delegates are `weak var delegate: SomeDelegate?` with a class-constrained protocol.\n- Combine: `sink` closures capture `self` weakly; `AnyCancellable` stored and released with the owner.\n- Block-based `NotificationCenter` observers are removed, or replaced by\n  `for await _ in NotificationCenter.default.notifications(named: ...)` inside `.task`.\n\n## SwiftUI\n\n- Ownership: `@State private var model = Model()` for `@Observable` models the view creates;\n  `@StateObject` for legacy `ObservableObject`. Passed-in models are plain properties (`@Observable`) or\n  `@ObservedObject` (legacy).\n- `body` is cheap and pure: no formatting with new `DateFormatter()` per call, no I/O, no sorting large arrays.\n- Async work starts in `.task` or `.task(id:)`, which cancels on disappear or id change.\n- Identity: `ForEach` uses stable `Identifiable` ids. `.id(x)` on a view resets its state when `x` changes;\n  check that is intended.\n- `NavigationStack` with a typed path for programmatic navigation; no deprecated `NavigationView` in new code.\n- Accessibility: images that convey meaning have `.accessibilityLabel`; decorative images use\n  `Image(decorative:)`; custom controls set `.accessibilityAddTraits(.isButton)`; text uses Dynamic Type\n  styles, not fixed sizes.\n\n## Errors and safety\n\n- `try?` only where failure is truly irrelevant; otherwise `do / catch` with a user-visible state.\n- Typed throws (`throws(MyError)`) where the error set is closed and callers switch over it.\n- `guard let` early exits instead of nested `if let` pyramids.\n- `Codable` models tolerate missing optional fields from the server (`decodeIfPresent` or optional properties).\n\n## Testing\n\n- Swift Testing (`@Test`, `#expect`, `#require`) for new tests; XCTest is fine in existing suites.\n- Async tests `await` the operation. In XCTest async contexts use `await fulfillment(of:timeout:)`,\n  not `wait(for:)`.\n- No `sleep` in tests. Inject clocks (`any Clock<Duration>`) or schedulers.\n- Protocols at boundaries (network, storage) so fakes can be injected.\n\n## Project and dependencies\n\n- New SPM packages pinned to a version range, `Package.resolved` committed for apps.\n- New `Info.plist` keys, entitlements, background modes or privacy usage strings are called out in the MR.\n- New required-reason API usage is reflected in `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`.\n",
  },
  "mobile-design-patterns": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-design-patterns\ndescription: \"Chooses and implements design patterns in Kotlin, Java, Swift and Dart mobile code: Repository, Factory, Strategy, Observer, Adapter, Decorator, Facade, State machine, Command, Coordinator, Result types and Delegation, with a selection table and over-engineering warnings. Use when picking a pattern, refactoring god classes or flag-driven state, wrapping an SDK, or when a design feels over-abstracted.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile design patterns\n\nPick the smallest structure that solves a real problem in Android, iOS or Flutter code, implement it\nidiomatically for the language, and remove patterns that only add indirection.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Structuring a new feature, data source, SDK wrapper, device protocol or navigation flow.\n- Refactoring long `when` / `switch` chains, boolean state flags, god classes or copy-pasted wrappers.\n- Reviewing code full of `*Manager`, `*Factory`, `*Builder`, `*Impl` names and deciding which ones earn their place.\n- Modelling a lifecycle (BLE connection, upload, onboarding, checkout) as an explicit state machine.\n- Deciding between thrown exceptions and typed results at a layer boundary.\n\nNot for: whole-app architecture (use `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`, `mobile-mvc`) or security design\n(use `mobile-security`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Write the problem in one sentence before naming a pattern: \"X changes independently of Y because Z\".\n   No sentence, no pattern.\n2. Check whether the language already solves it: named and default arguments, `by` delegation, function\n   types, sealed types, `Flow` / `AsyncSequence` / `Stream`, protocol extensions. Prefer the language feature.\n3. Find the problem in the selection table and read the matching rule, including its \"Skip when\" line.\n4. Load the language reference for full implementations: [Kotlin](references/kotlin.md),\n   [Swift](references/swift.md), [Dart](references/dart.md), [Java](references/java.md).\n5. Implement the smallest version. Add an interface or protocol only for a second implementation,\n   a test seam or a module boundary.\n6. Wire collaborators through constructor injection, never through globals.\n7. Unit test what the pattern isolates: transitions, strategies, mappers, decorators, command ordering.\n8. When reviewing, name the cost of a misused pattern (extra files, indirection, test burden) and show the\n   simpler alternative.\n\n## Pattern selection\n\n| Problem | Pattern | Mobile example | Skip when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| UI must not know if data comes from network, database or cache | Repository | Room or Core Data as source of truth, API refreshes it | One read-only source, no mapping, no caching |\n| Concrete type depends on runtime input | Factory | Exporter per format, message type from JSON | Only one type, or DI already builds it |\n| Many optional parameters | Named / default args (Builder only in Java) | Notification spec, request options | Kotlin, Swift, Dart: almost always skip Builder |\n| One shared instance | DI scope (`@Singleton`, app container) | HTTP client, database, BLE manager | Never hand-roll a mutable global |\n| Interchangeable algorithm | Strategy, often just a function | Retry backoff, sort order, image compression | Only one algorithm and no test need |\n| React to changing values | Observer via platform streams | Connection state, database queries | One-shot value: use a suspend / async function |\n| Foreign API does not fit your interface | Adapter | Callback SDK to suspend / async, DTO to domain | You own both sides: change the interface |\n| Add caching, logging, retry or metrics without editing a class | Decorator | Timed or caching repository | It belongs on every call: put it in the class or an HTTP interceptor |\n| Multi-step subsystem used from many places | Facade | `DeviceSession.open()` hides connect, MTU, discovery, notifications | It starts holding UI state: split it |\n| Behaviour depends on phase; some transitions are illegal | State machine | BLE connection, upload, onboarding | Two states a single `Boolean` models correctly |\n| Operations must be queued, serialized, retried or undone | Command | GATT operation queue, undoable edits | Fire-and-forget call with no ordering needs |\n| Flow logic spread across screens | Coordinator / Router | Onboarding, deep links, auth gate | One push from list to detail |\n| Expected failures are part of the API | Result / sealed errors / typed throws | Network, validation, pairing errors | Programmer errors: let them crash |\n| Reuse behaviour or call back to an owner | Delegation | Kotlin `by`, `CBCentralManagerDelegate` | Inheritance is not involved and a lambda suffices |\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. An abstraction needs a reason\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: interface + Impl with one implementation, no fake, same module\ninterface DateFormatter { fun format(i: Instant): String }\nclass DateFormatterImpl : DateFormatter { ... }\n\n// GOOD: a plain class until a second implementation, a test fake or a module boundary appears\nclass DateFormatter(private val zone: ZoneId) { fun format(i: Instant): String = ... }\n```\n\nValid reasons for an interface or protocol: a fake in ViewModel tests, a domain module that must not see\nthe data module, multiple real implementations, a platform split (KMP `expect` / `actual`).\n\n### 2. Repository: one source of truth, domain types out\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: ViewModel talks to Retrofit and Room directly and exposes DTOs\nclass DevicesViewModel(private val api: DeviceApi, private val dao: DeviceDao) : ViewModel()\n\n// GOOD: database is the source of truth, network only refreshes it, mapping happens here\nclass DeviceRepository @Inject constructor(private val api: DeviceApi, private val dao: DeviceDao) {\n    fun observeDevices(): Flow<List<Device>> = dao.observeAll().map { rows -> rows.map { it.toDomain() } }\n    suspend fun refresh() = dao.upsertAll(api.devices().map { it.toEntity() })\n}\n```\n\nSkip when: one repository per table that forwards DAO calls 1:1. Group by feature or aggregate instead.\n\n### 3. Factory: one place decides which type to build\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: construction logic duplicated at every call site\nval exporter = if (format == \"pdf\") PdfExporter(fonts) else CsvExporter(separator = ';')\n\n// GOOD\nclass ExporterFactory @Inject constructor(private val fonts: FontProvider) {\n    fun create(format: ExportFormat): Exporter = when (format) {\n        ExportFormat.Pdf -> PdfExporter(fonts)\n        ExportFormat.Csv -> CsvExporter(separator = ';')\n    }\n}\n```\n\nDart (factory constructor with pattern matching on untrusted JSON):\n```dart\nsealed class Message {\n  const Message();\n  factory Message.fromJson(Map<String, Object?> json) => switch (json) {\n        {'type': 'text', 'body': String body} => TextMessage(body),\n        {'type': 'image', 'url': String url} => ImageMessage(Uri.parse(url)),\n        _ => throw FormatException('Unknown message type: \${json['type']}'),\n      };\n}\n```\n\nSkip when: there is one concrete type, or the DI container already provides it. No factory of factories.\n\n### 4. Builder: use named and default arguments instead\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: hand-written builder in a language with named arguments\nval spec = NotificationSpec.Builder().title(\"Low battery\").channel(\"alerts\").build()\n\n// GOOD\ndata class NotificationSpec(val title: String, val channel: String = \"default\", val silent: Boolean = false)\nval spec = NotificationSpec(title = \"Low battery\", channel = \"alerts\")\n```\n\nSwift: a struct with defaulted `var` properties gets a memberwise initializer with default arguments.\nDart: `const NotificationSpec({required this.title, this.channel = 'default'})` plus `copyWith`.\nJava: a Builder is the right tool; see [Java](references/java.md).\n\n### 5. Singleton: scope it in DI, do not hand-roll it\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: global mutable state, leaks Context, cannot be replaced in tests\nobject Session { lateinit var context: Context; var token: String? = null }\n\n// GOOD\n@Singleton\nclass SessionStore @Inject constructor(@ApplicationContext private val context: Context)\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: hidden dependency\nfinal class FeedModel { func load() async throws { items = try await APIClient.shared.feed() } }\n\n// GOOD: injected, `.shared` may be the default only at the composition root\nfinal class FeedModel {\n    private let api: FeedAPI\n    init(api: FeedAPI) { self.api = api }\n}\n```\n\nFine to keep: stateless Kotlin `object` utilities and `data object` cases of sealed types.\n\n### 6. Strategy: often just a function\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: algorithm switches on a mode flag\nfun delayFor(attempt: Int, mode: String): Long = if (mode == \"exp\") 500L shl attempt else 1_000L\n\n// GOOD: the policy is a value the caller injects\nfun interface BackoffPolicy { fun delayMillis(attempt: Int): Long }\n\nval exponential = BackoffPolicy { attempt -> 500L shl attempt.coerceAtMost(6) }\nval fixed = BackoffPolicy { 1_000L }\nclass Uploader(private val backoff: BackoffPolicy)\n```\n\nSwift: `let backoff: (Int) -> Duration`. Dart: `typedef BackoffPolicy = Duration Function(int attempt);`.\nSkip when: only one algorithm exists and tests do not need to swap it.\n\n### 7. Observer: use the platform stream type, not listener lists\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: listeners never removed, callbacks on arbitrary threads\nprivate val listeners = mutableListOf<(ConnState) -> Unit>()\n\n// GOOD\nprivate val _state = MutableStateFlow<ConnState>(ConnState.Disconnected)\nval state: StateFlow<ConnState> = _state.asStateFlow()\n```\n\nSwift: `@Observable` for UI models, `AsyncStream` for single-consumer event streams, Combine\n`CurrentValueSubject` when several subscribers need the latest value.\nDart: `Stream` from a `StreamController.broadcast()` closed by its owner, or `ValueNotifier` for UI.\nSkip when: the value is produced once. Return it from a suspend or async function.\n\n### 8. Adapter: wrap foreign APIs at the edge\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: SDK callback types leak into the ViewModel\nprinter.print(job, object : PrintCallback { ... })\n\n// GOOD: one adapter turns the callback API into a cancellable suspend function\nsuspend fun LegacyPrinter.printAwait(job: PrintJob): PrintResult =\n    suspendCancellableCoroutine { cont ->\n        val handle = print(job, object : PrintCallback {\n            override fun onDone(result: PrintResult) = cont.resume(result)\n            override fun onError(error: Throwable) = cont.resumeWithException(error)\n        })\n        cont.invokeOnCancellation { handle.cancel() }\n    }\n```\n\nSwift: `withCheckedThrowingContinuation`, resumed exactly once. Dart: a `Completer`.\nDTO-to-domain mappers are adapters too; keep them in the data layer.\n\n### 9. Decorator: add cross-cutting behaviour by wrapping\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: timing code copy-pasted into every method, or a subclass of the concrete client\n// GOOD: delegate everything, override only what changes\nclass TimedDeviceApi(private val inner: DeviceApi, private val metrics: Metrics) : DeviceApi by inner {\n    override suspend fun firmwareInfo(id: String): FirmwareInfo {\n        val mark = TimeSource.Monotonic.markNow()\n        try { return inner.firmwareInfo(id) } finally { metrics.record(\"firmwareInfo\", mark.elapsedNow()) }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nSkip when: the concern is HTTP-level (use an OkHttp `Interceptor` or `URLSession` configuration), or the\nstack of decorators has an order nobody documented.\n\n### 10. Facade: hide a multi-step subsystem behind intent-level calls\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: every screen repeats connect -> request MTU -> discover services -> enable notifications\n// GOOD\nclass DeviceSession @Inject constructor(private val gatt: GattClient, private val queue: GattQueue) {\n    suspend fun open(address: String): Result<Unit> = ...   // runs the whole sequence in order\n    fun telemetry(): Flow<Telemetry> = ...\n    suspend fun close() = ...\n}\n```\n\nSkip when: the facade starts holding UI state, analytics and persistence. Split it by capability.\n\n### 11. State machine: sealed states, explicit transitions\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: flags that can contradict each other (connected && connecting)\nvar isConnecting = false; var isConnected = false; var lastError: Throwable? = null\n\n// GOOD: states carry their own data; one pure function decides transitions\nsealed interface ConnState {\n    data object Disconnected : ConnState\n    data class Connecting(val attempt: Int) : ConnState\n    data class Connected(val mtu: Int) : ConnState\n    data class Failed(val error: ConnError) : ConnState\n}\n\nfun reduce(state: ConnState, event: ConnEvent): ConnState = when (state) {\n    is ConnState.Connecting -> when (event) {\n        is ConnEvent.LinkUp -> ConnState.Connected(event.mtu)\n        is ConnEvent.LinkLost -> if (state.attempt < 3) ConnState.Connecting(state.attempt + 1) else ConnState.Failed(event.error)\n        else -> state // illegal here: ignore and log\n    }\n    // ... one branch per state\n}\n\n// GATT callbacks arrive on binder threads: funnel every event through one atomic update\nfun onEvent(event: ConnEvent) = _state.update { reduce(it, event) }\n```\n\nSwift: `enum ConnectionState` with associated values and a `mutating func handle(_ event:)` inside an actor.\nDart: `sealed class` plus an exhaustive `switch`. Full versions with tests are in the references.\nSkip when: two states that one `Boolean` models without contradictions.\n\n### 12. Command: operations as values, executed in order\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: Android GATT allows one outstanding operation; the second call is rejected while the first runs\ngatt.writeCharacteristic(config, payload, BluetoothGattCharacteristic.WRITE_TYPE_DEFAULT)\ngatt.readCharacteristic(status)\n\n// GOOD: commands queued and run one at a time by a single consumer\nsealed interface GattCommand {\n    class Write(val characteristic: UUID, val value: ByteArray) : GattCommand\n    class Read(val characteristic: UUID) : GattCommand\n}\n\nclass GattQueue(scope: CoroutineScope, private val executor: GattExecutor) {\n    private val commands = Channel<GattCommand>(Channel.UNLIMITED)\n    init { scope.launch { for (command in commands) executor.executeAndAwait(command) } }\n    fun enqueue(command: GattCommand) { commands.trySend(command) }\n}\n```\n\nAlso fits: undo / redo (`apply` and `revert`), offline actions persisted and replayed.\nSkip when: calls are independent and fire-and-forget.\n\n### 13. Coordinator / Router: views raise intents, one owner decides the flow\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: each view builds the next screen and its dependencies\nNavigationLink { PairingView(service: PairingService(api: .shared)) } label: { Text(\"Continue\") }\n\n// GOOD: typed routes, one owner of the path\nenum OnboardingRoute: Hashable { case permissions, pairing, done }\n\n@MainActor @Observable\nfinal class OnboardingCoordinator {\n    var path: [OnboardingRoute] = []\n    func permissionsGranted() { path.append(.pairing) }\n    func paired() { path.append(.done) }\n}\n```\n\nKotlin (Compose): screens take callbacks (`onDeviceClick: (String) -> Unit`); the nav host owns the back\nstack. Dart: `go_router` with `redirect` for auth gates. Skip when: the app has a handful of linear screens.\n\n### 14. Expected errors are typed results; bugs still crash\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: every failure collapses to null; runCatching also swallows CancellationException\nsuspend fun pair(id: String): Device? = runCatching { api.pair(id) }.getOrNull()\n\n// GOOD\nsealed interface PairResult {\n    data class Paired(val device: Device) : PairResult\n    data object Timeout : PairResult\n    data class Rejected(val reason: String) : PairResult\n}\n```\n\nSwift: `func pair(id: String) async throws(PairingError) -> Device` when the error set is closed.\nDart: `sealed class Result<T>` with `Ok` and `Err`, matched with an exhaustive `switch`.\nDo not mix thrown exceptions and result types in the same layer.\n\n### 15. Delegation: compose behaviour, keep back-references weak\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: inheriting a concrete class to reuse one method\nclass AuditedStore : PrefsStore() { override fun put(k: String, v: String) { audit(k); super.put(k, v) } }\n\n// GOOD: class delegation through an interface\nclass AuditedStore(private val inner: KeyValueStore, private val audit: Audit) : KeyValueStore by inner {\n    override fun put(key: String, value: String) { audit.record(key); inner.put(key, value) }\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: strong delegate, owner <-> child retain cycle\nvar delegate: ScannerDelegate?\n\n// GOOD: class-constrained protocol, weak reference\nprotocol ScannerDelegate: AnyObject { func scanner(_ s: Scanner, didFind device: Device) }\nweak var delegate: ScannerDelegate?\n```\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- `interface Foo` + `FooImpl` with one implementation and no fake, in the same module.\n- A pattern chosen before the problem was stated. \"We might need it later\" is not a reason.\n- `*Manager` and `*Helper` classes that mix networking, persistence and UI state: a god object, not a facade.\n- Builders in Kotlin, Swift or Dart; hand-rolled singletons with `lateinit` or `static var shared` state.\n- Use cases that only forward one repository call, added \"for consistency\".\n- Boolean flags modelling a lifecycle, or a state machine where transitions happen in many places.\n- Listener lists, `NotificationCenter` or event buses used where a typed stream would do.\n- Generic `BaseRepository<T>`, `BaseViewModel<S, E, A>` hierarchies that every feature must bend to.\n- Strategy hierarchies for an algorithm that has exactly one implementation.\n- Wrapping every third-party type \"in case we switch libraries\" when nothing is planned.\n- Decorator chains or command queues with undocumented ordering assumptions.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Problem stated in one sentence before the pattern was chosen\n- [ ] Language feature considered first (named args, `by`, function types, sealed types, streams)\n- [ ] Every interface or protocol has a second implementation, a test fake or a module boundary\n- [ ] Repositories expose domain types and a single source of truth\n- [ ] Shared instances come from DI scopes, not mutable globals\n- [ ] Observables use `Flow` / `AsyncSequence` / Combine / `Stream`, and subscriptions are scoped\n- [ ] Foreign APIs adapted at the edge; callbacks resume exactly once and support cancellation\n- [ ] State machines: sealed states, one transition function, illegal transitions handled, events serialized\n- [ ] Commands that touch serialized resources (GATT, files) run one at a time\n- [ ] Navigation decisions live in one owner per flow\n- [ ] Expected errors typed; cancellation not swallowed; programmer errors not caught\n- [ ] Swift delegates are `weak` with `AnyObject` protocols\n- [ ] Unit tests cover transitions, strategies, mappers and decorator behaviour\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`, `mobile-mvc` -- the architecture these patterns plug into\n- `mobile-kotlin`, `mobile-java`, `mobile-swift`, `mobile-flutter` -- language idioms behind each implementation\n- `mobile-code-review` -- reviewing pattern misuse as part of a change\n- `mobile-security` -- when a pattern wraps storage, crypto or network trust decisions\n- `mobile-ble` -- the connection state machine and GATT command queue in practice\n",
    "references/dart.md":
        "# Dart and Flutter pattern implementations\n\nLoad this when implementing or reviewing patterns in Dart. Assumes Dart 3 (sealed classes, records,\npatterns, class modifiers) and Flutter. DI examples use Riverpod; the patterns are the same with\n`get_it` or plain constructor injection.\n\n## Repository\n\n```dart\n// Interface because widget and notifier tests override it with a fake.\nabstract interface class DeviceRepository {\n  Stream<List<Device>> watchDevices();\n  Future<void> refresh();\n}\n\nfinal class OfflineFirstDeviceRepository implements DeviceRepository {\n  OfflineFirstDeviceRepository(this._api, this._dao);\n\n  final DeviceApi _api;\n  final DeviceDao _dao;\n\n  @override\n  Stream<List<Device>> watchDevices() =>\n      _dao.watchAll().map((rows) => [for (final row in rows) row.toDomain()]);\n\n  @override\n  Future<void> refresh() async {\n    final dtos = await _api.devices();\n    await _dao.upsertAll([for (final dto in dtos) dto.toRow()]);\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- The UI listens to the local database stream; `refresh()` only writes into it.\n- JSON DTOs and database rows never reach widgets.\n\n## Factory\n\n```dart\nsealed class Message {\n  const Message();\n\n  factory Message.fromJson(Map<String, Object?> json) => switch (json) {\n        {'type': 'text', 'body': String body} => TextMessage(body),\n        {'type': 'image', 'url': String url} => ImageMessage(Uri.parse(url)),\n        _ => throw FormatException('Unknown message type: \${json['type']}'),\n      };\n}\n\nfinal class TextMessage extends Message {\n  const TextMessage(this.body);\n  final String body;\n}\n\nfinal class ImageMessage extends Message {\n  const ImageMessage(this.url);\n  final Uri url;\n}\n```\n\n- Map patterns check key presence and value types, so malformed JSON fails at the edge instead of\n  with a cast error deep in the UI.\n- Named constructors (`Device.placeholder()`) cover simple alternative constructions without a factory.\n\n## Builder replaced by named parameters\n\n```dart\nfinal class ScanOptions {\n  const ScanOptions({\n    this.timeout = const Duration(seconds: 10),\n    this.serviceFilter,\n    this.lowLatency = false,\n  });\n\n  final Duration timeout;\n  final String? serviceFilter;\n  final bool lowLatency;\n\n  ScanOptions copyWith({Duration? timeout, String? serviceFilter, bool? lowLatency}) => ScanOptions(\n        timeout: timeout ?? this.timeout,\n        serviceFilter: serviceFilter ?? this.serviceFilter,\n        lowLatency: lowLatency ?? this.lowLatency,\n      );\n}\n\nconst options = ScanOptions(lowLatency: true);\n```\n\n- A hand-written `copyWith` cannot reset a nullable field to `null`. Code generation such as `freezed`\n  handles that, or use a sentinel.\n\n## Singleton replaced by DI scopes\n\n```dart\nfinal httpClientProvider = Provider<http.Client>((ref) {\n  final client = http.Client();\n  ref.onDispose(client.close);\n  return client;\n});\n\nfinal deviceRepositoryProvider = Provider<DeviceRepository>(\n  (ref) => OfflineFirstDeviceRepository(ref.watch(deviceApiProvider), ref.watch(deviceDaoProvider)),\n);\n\n// Test\nfinal container = ProviderContainer(\n  overrides: [deviceRepositoryProvider.overrideWithValue(FakeDeviceRepository())],\n);\naddTearDown(container.dispose);\n```\n\n- No top-level mutable variables holding clients or tokens.\n- `late final` globals initialized in `main()` are a service locator in disguise; tests cannot replace them.\n\n## Strategy\n\n```dart\ntypedef BackoffPolicy = Duration Function(int attempt);\n\nDuration fixedBackoff(int attempt) => const Duration(seconds: 1);\nDuration exponentialBackoff(int attempt) => Duration(milliseconds: 500 * (1 << (attempt > 6 ? 6 : attempt)));\n\nfinal class Uploader {\n  Uploader({BackoffPolicy backoff = exponentialBackoff}) : _backoff = backoff;\n  final BackoffPolicy _backoff;\n}\n```\n\nA `typedef` of a function type covers most strategies. Use an interface when the strategy has several methods.\n\n## Observer\n\n```dart\nfinal class ConnectionMonitor {\n  final _controller = StreamController<ConnState>.broadcast();\n  ConnState _state = const Disconnected();\n\n  ConnState get state => _state;\n  Stream<ConnState> get states => _controller.stream;\n\n  void _emit(ConnState next) {\n    _state = next;\n    _controller.add(next);\n  }\n\n  Future<void> dispose() => _controller.close();\n}\n```\n\n- Broadcast streams do not replay the latest value. Expose a `state` getter, or start the stream with it.\n- Listeners store the `StreamSubscription` and cancel it in `dispose()`.\n- In widgets prefer the state-management tool's observer (`ref.watch`, `BlocBuilder`) or `ValueListenableBuilder`\n  over raw `StreamBuilder` plumbing.\n\n## Adapter\n\n```dart\n// Callback API -> Future.\nFuture<PrintReceipt> printReceipt(LegacyPrinter printer, PrintJob job) {\n  final completer = Completer<PrintReceipt>();\n  printer.submit(job, onDone: completer.complete, onError: completer.completeError);\n  return completer.future;\n}\n\n// Listener API -> Stream, registered only while someone listens.\nStream<bool> adapterEnabled(BluetoothAdapterWrapper adapter) {\n  late final StreamController<bool> controller;\n  void listener(bool enabled) => controller.add(enabled);\n  controller = StreamController<bool>(\n    onListen: () => adapter.addListener(listener),\n    onCancel: () => adapter.removeListener(listener),\n  );\n  return controller.stream;\n}\n```\n\n- Guard against the callback firing twice: `if (!completer.isCompleted) completer.complete(value);`.\n- Platform channels are adapters too: generate them with Pigeon instead of hand-written method names.\n\n## Decorator\n\n```dart\nfinal class TimedDeviceApi implements DeviceApi {\n  TimedDeviceApi(this._inner, this._metrics);\n\n  final DeviceApi _inner;\n  final Metrics _metrics;\n\n  @override\n  Future<FirmwareInfo> firmwareInfo(String id) async {\n    final watch = Stopwatch()..start();\n    try {\n      return await _inner.firmwareInfo(id);\n    } finally {\n      _metrics.record('firmwareInfo', watch.elapsed);\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Dart has no automatic forwarding: every other member is forwarded by hand.\n  @override\n  Future<List<DeviceDto>> devices() => _inner.devices();\n}\n```\n\nFor HTTP concerns (auth headers, logging, retry) use the client's interceptor mechanism (for example `dio`\ninterceptors) rather than decorating every API class.\n\n## Facade\n\n```dart\nfinal class DeviceSession {\n  DeviceSession(this._ble, this._queue, this._machine);\n\n  final BleClient _ble;\n  final CommandQueue _queue;\n  final ConnectionMachine _machine;\n\n  Future<void> open(String deviceId) async {\n    _machine.send(const Connect());\n    await _ble.connect(deviceId);\n    final mtu = await _queue.enqueue(() => _ble.requestMtu(deviceId, 247));\n    await _queue.enqueue(() => _ble.discoverServices(deviceId));\n    await _queue.enqueue(() => _ble.enableNotifications(deviceId, telemetryCharacteristic));\n    _machine.send(LinkUp(mtu));\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`BleClient` is the app's own adapter over whichever BLE plugin the project uses. MTU requests are an\nAndroid concept; on iOS the plugin typically reports the negotiated value instead.\n\n## State machine (connection)\n\n```dart\nenum ConnError { timeout, rejected, bluetoothOff }\n\nsealed class ConnState { const ConnState(); }\nfinal class Disconnected extends ConnState { const Disconnected(); }\nfinal class Connecting extends ConnState { const Connecting(this.attempt); final int attempt; }\nfinal class Connected extends ConnState { const Connected(this.mtu); final int mtu; }\nfinal class Disconnecting extends ConnState { const Disconnecting(); }\nfinal class Failed extends ConnState { const Failed(this.error); final ConnError error; }\n\nsealed class ConnEvent { const ConnEvent(); }\nfinal class Connect extends ConnEvent { const Connect(); }\nfinal class LinkUp extends ConnEvent { const LinkUp(this.mtu); final int mtu; }\nfinal class LinkLost extends ConnEvent { const LinkLost(this.error); final ConnError error; }\nfinal class Disconnect extends ConnEvent { const Disconnect(); }\nfinal class Closed extends ConnEvent { const Closed(); }\n\nConnState reduce(ConnState state, ConnEvent event, {int maxAttempts = 3}) => switch ((state, event)) {\n      (Disconnected() || Failed(), Connect()) => const Connecting(1),\n      (Connecting(), LinkUp(:final mtu)) => Connected(mtu),\n      (Connecting(:final attempt), LinkLost(:final error)) =>\n        attempt < maxAttempts ? Connecting(attempt + 1) : Failed(error),\n      (Connected(), LinkLost()) => const Connecting(1),\n      (Connecting() || Connected(), Disconnect()) => const Disconnecting(),\n      (Disconnecting(), Closed() || LinkLost()) => const Disconnected(),\n      _ => state, // illegal transition: ignored, logged by the caller\n    };\n\nfinal class ConnectionMachine {\n  final _states = StreamController<ConnState>.broadcast();\n  ConnState state = const Disconnected();\n  Stream<ConnState> get states => _states.stream;\n\n  void send(ConnEvent event) {\n    final next = reduce(state, event);\n    if (identical(next, state)) return;\n    state = next;\n    _states.add(next);\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- An isolate is single-threaded, so `send` cannot race. It must stay synchronous: an `await` inside it\n  would let another event interleave.\n- These classes do not override `==`. Tests use matchers, or add `==` / `hashCode` (or `equatable`, `freezed`).\n\n```dart\ntest('link lost while connected starts reconnect', () {\n  final next = reduce(const Connected(185), const LinkLost(ConnError.timeout));\n  expect(next, isA<Connecting>().having((s) => s.attempt, 'attempt', 1));\n});\n```\n\n## Command\n\n```dart\nfinal class CommandQueue {\n  Future<void> _tail = Future.value();\n\n  // Runs commands strictly one after another; a failing command does not block the next.\n  Future<T> enqueue<T>(Future<T> Function() command) {\n    final result = _tail.then((_) => command());\n    _tail = result.then<void>((_) {}, onError: (Object _, StackTrace __) {});\n    return result;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- Add a timeout per command (`command().timeout(...)`) when the underlying operation can hang.\n- Undo / redo: commands with `apply` and `revert`, two stacks, redo cleared on every new command.\n\n## Router (go_router)\n\n```dart\nGoRouter buildRouter(AuthState auth) => GoRouter(\n      initialLocation: '/devices',\n      refreshListenable: auth,\n      redirect: (context, state) {\n        final loggingIn = state.matchedLocation == '/login';\n        if (!auth.isLoggedIn) return loggingIn ? null : '/login';\n        if (loggingIn) return '/devices';\n        return null;\n      },\n      routes: [\n        GoRoute(path: '/login', builder: (context, state) => const LoginScreen()),\n        GoRoute(\n          path: '/devices',\n          builder: (context, state) => const DeviceListScreen(),\n          routes: [\n            GoRoute(\n              path: ':id',\n              builder: (context, state) => DeviceDetailScreen(id: state.pathParameters['id']!),\n            ),\n          ],\n        ),\n      ],\n    );\n```\n\n- Auth gating and deep-link handling live in `redirect`, not in each screen.\n- Screens take callbacks or call `context.go(...)` with paths built in one place, not string literals\n  scattered across widgets.\n\n## Result type\n\n```dart\nsealed class Result<T> {\n  const Result();\n}\n\nfinal class Ok<T> extends Result<T> {\n  const Ok(this.value);\n  final T value;\n}\n\nfinal class Err<T> extends Result<T> {\n  const Err(this.error);\n  final AppError error;\n}\n\nString label(Result<Device> r) => switch (r) {\n      Ok(:final value) => value.name,\n      Err(:final error) => error.message,\n    };\n```\n\n- Return `Result` for expected failures (network, validation, pairing). Let `Error` subclasses\n  (`StateError`, `ArgumentError`) propagate; they are bugs.\n\n## Delegation\n\nDart has no `by` keyword. Options, in order of preference:\n\n- Composition with explicit forwarding (as in the decorator above).\n- Callbacks passed to the constructor for owner notifications, instead of a delegate interface.\n- `mixin` for small reusable behaviour that needs no state from a specific superclass.\n\n```dart\nmixin Disposables {\n  final _subscriptions = <StreamSubscription<Object?>>[];\n  void track(StreamSubscription<Object?> s) => _subscriptions.add(s);\n  Future<void> disposeAll() => Future.wait(_subscriptions.map((s) => s.cancel()));\n}\n```\n",
    "references/java.md":
        "# Java pattern implementations (Android)\n\nLoad this when implementing or reviewing patterns in Java Android code. Java lacks named arguments,\nclass delegation and (on many toolchains) pattern matching for `switch`, so some patterns that are\nover-engineering in Kotlin are the right tool here. Examples use Java 17 language features that D8\ndesugars for all API levels (records, `instanceof` patterns, switch expressions).\n\n## Builder (justified in Java)\n\n```java\npublic final class NotificationSpec {\n    private final String title;\n    private final String channel;\n    private final boolean silent;\n\n    private NotificationSpec(Builder b) {\n        this.title = b.title;\n        this.channel = b.channel;\n        this.silent = b.silent;\n    }\n\n    public static Builder builder(String title) { return new Builder(title); }\n\n    public static final class Builder {\n        private final String title;        // required values go in the builder constructor\n        private String channel = \"default\";\n        private boolean silent;\n\n        private Builder(String title) { this.title = Objects.requireNonNull(title); }\n\n        public Builder channel(String channel) { this.channel = channel; return this; }\n        public Builder silent(boolean silent) { this.silent = silent; return this; }\n        public NotificationSpec build() { return new NotificationSpec(this); }\n    }\n}\n\nNotificationSpec spec = NotificationSpec.builder(\"Low battery\").channel(\"alerts\").build();\n```\n\n- Two or three fields: a `record` or a constructor is enough. Builders pay off with many optional fields.\n- When the class moves to Kotlin, delete the builder and use default arguments plus `@JvmOverloads`\n  for remaining Java callers.\n\n## Singleton through DI (Hilt)\n\n```java\n@Singleton\npublic final class SessionStore {\n    private final Context context;\n\n    @Inject\n    public SessionStore(@ApplicationContext Context context) { this.context = context; }\n}\n\n@Module\n@InstallIn(SingletonComponent.class)\npublic abstract class DataModule {\n    @Binds\n    abstract DeviceRepository bindDeviceRepository(DefaultDeviceRepository impl);\n}\n```\n\n```java\n// BAD: global lazy singleton, not thread-safe, keeps whatever Context the first caller passed\npublic static Analytics getInstance(Context c) {\n    if (instance == null) instance = new Analytics(c);\n    return instance;\n}\n```\n\n## Factory\n\n```java\npublic final class Exporters {\n    private Exporters() {}\n\n    public static Exporter forFormat(ExportFormat format, FontProvider fonts) {\n        return switch (format) {\n            case PDF -> new PdfExporter(fonts);\n            case CSV -> new CsvExporter(';');\n        };\n    }\n}\n```\n\nA switch expression over an enum is exhaustive at compile time; a new enum constant breaks the build\ninstead of falling through silently.\n\n## Strategy\n\n```java\n@FunctionalInterface\npublic interface BackoffPolicy {\n    long delayMillis(int attempt);\n\n    BackoffPolicy FIXED = attempt -> 1_000L;\n    BackoffPolicy EXPONENTIAL = attempt -> 500L << Math.min(attempt, 6);\n}\n\npublic final class Uploader {\n    private final BackoffPolicy backoff;\n    public Uploader(BackoffPolicy backoff) { this.backoff = backoff; }\n}\n```\n\nKotlin callers can pass a lambda directly because the interface has a single abstract method.\n\n## Observer (LiveData)\n\n```java\npublic final class ConnectionViewModel extends ViewModel {\n    private final MutableLiveData<ConnState> state = new MutableLiveData<>(new ConnState.Disconnected());\n\n    public LiveData<ConnState> getState() { return state; } // read-only type outward\n}\n\n// Fragment\nviewModel.getState().observe(getViewLifecycleOwner(), this::render);\n```\n\n- `postValue` from background threads keeps only the latest value. Fine for state, wrong for events.\n\n## Adapter\n\n```java\n// DTO -> domain at the data-layer edge.\nfinal class DeviceMapper {\n    private DeviceMapper() {}\n\n    static @Nullable Device toDomain(DeviceDto dto) {\n        if (dto.serial == null) return null;\n        return new Device(dto.serial, dto.name != null ? dto.name : \"\", dto.firmware);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Decorator\n\n```java\npublic final class TimedDeviceApi implements DeviceApi {\n    private final DeviceApi inner;\n    private final Metrics metrics;\n\n    public TimedDeviceApi(DeviceApi inner, Metrics metrics) {\n        this.inner = inner;\n        this.metrics = metrics;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public FirmwareInfo firmwareInfo(String id) throws IOException {\n        long start = SystemClock.elapsedRealtime();\n        try {\n            return inner.firmwareInfo(id);\n        } finally {\n            metrics.record(\"firmwareInfo\", SystemClock.elapsedRealtime() - start);\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public List<DeviceDto> devices() throws IOException { return inner.devices(); } // manual forwarding\n}\n```\n\n## State machine (connection)\n\n```java\npublic interface ConnState {\n    record Disconnected() implements ConnState {}\n    record Connecting(int attempt) implements ConnState {}\n    record Connected(int mtu) implements ConnState {}\n    record Failed(ConnError error) implements ConnState {}\n}\n\npublic interface ConnEvent {\n    record Connect() implements ConnEvent {}\n    record LinkUp(int mtu) implements ConnEvent {}\n    record LinkLost(ConnError error) implements ConnEvent {}\n    record Disconnect() implements ConnEvent {}\n}\n\npublic final class ConnectionReducer {\n    private static final int MAX_ATTEMPTS = 3;\n\n    private ConnectionReducer() {}\n\n    public static ConnState reduce(ConnState state, ConnEvent event) {\n        if (state instanceof ConnState.Disconnected || state instanceof ConnState.Failed) {\n            return event instanceof ConnEvent.Connect ? new ConnState.Connecting(1) : state;\n        }\n        if (state instanceof ConnState.Connecting c) {\n            if (event instanceof ConnEvent.LinkUp up) return new ConnState.Connected(up.mtu());\n            if (event instanceof ConnEvent.LinkLost lost) {\n                return c.attempt() < MAX_ATTEMPTS ? new ConnState.Connecting(c.attempt() + 1) : new ConnState.Failed(lost.error());\n            }\n            if (event instanceof ConnEvent.Disconnect) return new ConnState.Disconnected();\n            return state;\n        }\n        if (state instanceof ConnState.Connected) {\n            if (event instanceof ConnEvent.LinkLost) return new ConnState.Connecting(1);\n            if (event instanceof ConnEvent.Disconnect) return new ConnState.Disconnected();\n            return state;\n        }\n        throw new IllegalStateException(\"Unhandled state \" + state);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Records give `equals` / `hashCode`, so tests compare states directly.\n- If the project's AGP and D8 support sealed types, declare `sealed interface ConnState permits ...`.\n  Pattern matching for `switch` is a Java 21 feature; confirm toolchain support before using it.\n- GATT callbacks arrive on binder threads. Apply events on one thread: a single-thread executor, or a\n  `synchronized` method that reduces and then calls `postValue`.\n\n## Command (serialized GATT queue)\n\n```java\npublic final class GattQueue {\n    private final ExecutorService worker = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();\n    private final GattExecutor executor;\n\n    public GattQueue(GattExecutor executor) { this.executor = executor; }\n\n    // One command at a time: executeBlocking issues the call and waits (with a timeout) for its callback.\n    public Future<byte[]> submit(GattCommand command) {\n        return worker.submit(() -> executor.executeBlocking(command, 5, TimeUnit.SECONDS));\n    }\n\n    public void close() { worker.shutdownNow(); }\n}\n```\n\n- `executeBlocking` typically uses a `CountDownLatch` or `CompletableFuture` completed from the\n  `BluetoothGattCallback`. Always wait with a timeout.\n- The owner calls `close()` when the connection ends, or the worker thread leaks.\n\n## Delegation\n\nJava has no class delegation. Prefer composition with explicit forwarding (as in the decorator) over\nextending a concrete class. For callbacks to an owner, pass a listener interface and clear it when the\nowner's lifecycle ends:\n\n```java\npublic void setListener(@Nullable ScanListener listener) { this.listener = listener; }\n\n@Override public void onDestroyView() {\n    scanner.setListener(null);\n    super.onDestroyView();\n}\n```\n",
    "references/kotlin.md":
        "# Kotlin pattern implementations\n\nLoad this when implementing or reviewing patterns in Kotlin (Android or KMP). Assumes coroutines, Flow and\nHilt. Adapt the DI annotations if the project uses Koin or manual injection.\n\n## Repository (offline-first)\n\n```kotlin\n// Interface only because ViewModel tests use a fake and the domain module must not see Room or Retrofit.\ninterface DeviceRepository {\n    fun observeDevices(): Flow<List<Device>>\n    suspend fun refresh(): RefreshResult\n}\n\nclass OfflineFirstDeviceRepository @Inject constructor(\n    private val api: DeviceApi,\n    private val dao: DeviceDao,\n    @IoDispatcher private val io: CoroutineDispatcher,\n) : DeviceRepository {\n\n    override fun observeDevices(): Flow<List<Device>> =\n        dao.observeAll().map { rows -> rows.map(DeviceEntity::toDomain) }\n\n    override suspend fun refresh(): RefreshResult = withContext(io) {\n        try {\n            dao.upsertAll(api.devices().map(DeviceDto::toEntity))\n            RefreshResult.Updated\n        } catch (e: IOException) {\n            RefreshResult.Offline\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n@Module\n@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)\nabstract class DataModule {\n    @Binds abstract fun deviceRepository(impl: OfflineFirstDeviceRepository): DeviceRepository\n}\n```\n\n- The UI observes the database; `refresh()` only writes into it. No second in-memory copy that can drift.\n- `HttpException` from Retrofit is not an `IOException`; map it separately if the server can reject requests.\n\n## Factory\n\n```kotlin\n// Factory function named after the type, the Kotlin convention (like MutableStateFlow(...)).\nfun BackoffPolicy(config: RetryConfig): BackoffPolicy = when (config.mode) {\n    RetryMode.Fixed -> BackoffPolicy { config.baseMillis }\n    RetryMode.Exponential -> BackoffPolicy { attempt -> config.baseMillis shl attempt.coerceAtMost(6) }\n}\n\n// Companion factory for parsing; the constructor stays private so invalid instances cannot exist.\n@JvmInline\nvalue class SerialNumber private constructor(val value: String) {\n    companion object {\n        private val format = Regex(\"[A-Z0-9]{12}\")\n        fun parse(raw: String): SerialNumber? = raw.trim().uppercase().takeIf(format::matches)?.let(::SerialNumber)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAssisted injection when a ViewModel or worker needs runtime arguments plus injected dependencies:\nuse Hilt `@AssistedInject` / `@AssistedFactory` rather than a hand-written factory class.\n\n## Builder replaced by named and default arguments\n\n```kotlin\ndata class ScanSettingsSpec(\n    val timeout: Duration = 10.seconds,\n    val serviceFilter: UUID? = null,\n    val lowLatency: Boolean = false,\n)\n\nval spec = ScanSettingsSpec(serviceFilter = TELEMETRY_SERVICE, lowLatency = true)\nval longer = spec.copy(timeout = 30.seconds)\n```\n\n- `require(...)` in `init` validates combinations a builder would have checked in `build()`.\n- A type-safe DSL (`buildList { }`, `buildString { }`, a custom `@DslMarker` builder) is justified for\n  nested structures such as test fixtures or menus, not for flat option bags.\n- Java callers of a class with defaults need `@JvmOverloads` on the constructor.\n\n## Singleton through DI\n\n```kotlin\n@Module\n@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)\nobject NetworkModule {\n    @Provides @Singleton\n    fun okHttp(): OkHttpClient = OkHttpClient.Builder().build()\n}\n\n@Singleton\nclass BleManager @Inject constructor(@ApplicationContext private val context: Context)\n```\n\n- Scope matches lifetime: `@Singleton` for process-wide, `@ActivityRetainedScoped` or `@ViewModelScoped`\n  for screen-level state. Never put screen state in a `@Singleton`.\n- An application `CoroutineScope` for work that outlives screens is also injected, not created ad hoc.\n\n## Strategy\n\n```kotlin\nfun interface ChecksumStrategy { fun compute(bytes: ByteArray): Long }\n\nclass FrameValidator(private val checksum: ChecksumStrategy) {\n    fun isValid(payload: ByteArray, expected: Long): Boolean = checksum.compute(payload) == expected\n}\n\n// java.util.zip.CRC32 is available on every API level.\nval crc32 = ChecksumStrategy { bytes -> CRC32().apply { update(bytes) }.value }\n```\n\nWhich checksum a device protocol uses is defined by the firmware spec. Do not guess it.\n\n## Observer\n\n```kotlin\nclass ConnectionMonitor @Inject constructor(private val bluetooth: BluetoothAdapterWrapper) {\n\n    // Hot state: latest value always available, deduplicated.\n    private val _state = MutableStateFlow<ConnState>(ConnState.Disconnected)\n    val state: StateFlow<ConnState> = _state.asStateFlow()\n\n    // Adapting a listener API into a cold Flow: unregister in awaitClose.\n    fun adapterEnabled(): Flow<Boolean> = callbackFlow {\n        val listener = AdapterListener { enabled -> trySend(enabled) }\n        bluetooth.register(listener)\n        awaitClose { bluetooth.unregister(listener) }\n    }.distinctUntilChanged()\n}\n```\n\n- `StateFlow` for state, `SharedFlow` for broadcasts where every subscriber needs every value.\n- Expose cold flows to the UI through `stateIn(viewModelScope, SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5_000), initial)`.\n- Collect in UI with `collectAsStateWithLifecycle()` or `repeatOnLifecycle`.\n\n## Adapter\n\n```kotlin\n// DTO -> domain at the data-layer edge. The domain type never sees nullable wire fields.\nfun DeviceDto.toDomain(): Device? {\n    val serial = SerialNumber.parse(serial ?: return null) ?: return null\n    return Device(serial = serial, name = name.orEmpty(), firmware = firmware ?: \"unknown\")\n}\n\n// Callback API -> suspend. Resume exactly once; a resume after cancellation is ignored.\nsuspend fun LocationClient.currentLocation(): Location =\n    suspendCancellableCoroutine { cont ->\n        val request = requestSingle(\n            onResult = { cont.resume(it) },\n            onError = { cont.resumeWithException(it) },\n        )\n        cont.invokeOnCancellation { request.cancel() }\n    }\n```\n\n## Decorator\n\n```kotlin\nclass CachingForecastSource(\n    private val inner: ForecastSource,\n    private val time: TimeSource = TimeSource.Monotonic,\n    private val ttl: Duration = 10.minutes,\n) : ForecastSource by inner {\n\n    private val mutex = Mutex()\n    private var cached: Pair<TimeMark, Forecast>? = null\n\n    override suspend fun forecast(): Forecast = mutex.withLock {\n        cached?.takeIf { it.first.elapsedNow() < ttl }?.second\n            ?: inner.forecast().also { cached = time.markNow() to it }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `TimeSource` is injected so tests pass a `TestTimeSource` and advance it with `+=`.\n- The `Mutex` makes concurrent callers share one fetch instead of racing.\n\n## Facade\n\n```kotlin\nclass DeviceSession @Inject constructor(\n    private val client: GattClient,\n    private val queue: GattQueue,\n    private val machine: ConnectionStateMachine,\n) {\n    suspend fun open(address: String, desiredMtu: Int): OpenResult {\n        machine.onEvent(ConnEvent.Connect)\n        val link = client.connect(address) ?: return OpenResult.Unreachable\n        val mtu = (queue.execute(GattCommand.RequestMtu(desiredMtu)) as? GattResponse.Mtu)?.value\n            ?: return OpenResult.Failed\n        queue.execute(GattCommand.DiscoverServices)\n        queue.execute(GattCommand.EnableNotifications(TELEMETRY_CHARACTERISTIC))\n        machine.onEvent(ConnEvent.LinkUp(mtu = mtu))\n        return OpenResult.Ready(link)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe facade sequences calls; it does not own UI state or persistence.\n\n## State machine (connection)\n\n```kotlin\nsealed interface ConnState {\n    data object Disconnected : ConnState\n    data class Connecting(val attempt: Int) : ConnState\n    data class Connected(val mtu: Int) : ConnState\n    data object Disconnecting : ConnState\n    data class Failed(val error: ConnError) : ConnState\n}\n\nsealed interface ConnEvent {\n    data object Connect : ConnEvent\n    data class LinkUp(val mtu: Int) : ConnEvent\n    data class LinkLost(val error: ConnError) : ConnEvent\n    data object Disconnect : ConnEvent\n    data object Closed : ConnEvent\n}\n\nfun reduce(state: ConnState, event: ConnEvent, maxAttempts: Int = 3): ConnState = when (state) {\n    ConnState.Disconnected, is ConnState.Failed -> when (event) {\n        ConnEvent.Connect -> ConnState.Connecting(attempt = 1)\n        else -> state\n    }\n    is ConnState.Connecting -> when (event) {\n        is ConnEvent.LinkUp -> ConnState.Connected(event.mtu)\n        is ConnEvent.LinkLost ->\n            if (state.attempt < maxAttempts) ConnState.Connecting(state.attempt + 1) else ConnState.Failed(event.error)\n        ConnEvent.Disconnect -> ConnState.Disconnecting\n        ConnEvent.Connect, ConnEvent.Closed -> state\n    }\n    is ConnState.Connected -> when (event) {\n        is ConnEvent.LinkLost -> ConnState.Connecting(attempt = 1)\n        ConnEvent.Disconnect -> ConnState.Disconnecting\n        ConnEvent.Connect, is ConnEvent.LinkUp, ConnEvent.Closed -> state\n    }\n    ConnState.Disconnecting -> when (event) {\n        ConnEvent.Closed, is ConnEvent.LinkLost -> ConnState.Disconnected\n        else -> state\n    }\n}\n\nclass ConnectionStateMachine @Inject constructor(private val log: Logger) {\n    private val _state = MutableStateFlow<ConnState>(ConnState.Disconnected)\n    val state: StateFlow<ConnState> = _state.asStateFlow()\n\n    // Safe from GATT binder threads: update is an atomic compare-and-set over a pure function.\n    fun onEvent(event: ConnEvent) {\n        val (old, new) = _state.updateAndGetPair { reduce(it, event) }\n        if (old == new) log.debug(\"Ignored \$event in \$old\")\n    }\n}\n\nprivate inline fun <T> MutableStateFlow<T>.updateAndGetPair(transform: (T) -> T): Pair<T, T> {\n    while (true) {\n        val old = value\n        val new = transform(old)\n        if (compareAndSet(old, new)) return old to new\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `reduce` is pure, so it is trivially unit tested and safe to retry inside `compareAndSet`.\n- Side effects (reconnect timers, `gatt.close()`) run in a collector of `state`, not inside `reduce`.\n- Prefer listing events explicitly over `else` where the compiler should flag new events.\n\n```kotlin\nclass ReduceTest {\n    @Test fun `link lost while connected starts reconnect`() {\n        assertEquals(ConnState.Connecting(1), reduce(ConnState.Connected(mtu = 247), ConnEvent.LinkLost(ConnError.Timeout)))\n    }\n\n    @Test fun `gives up after max attempts`() {\n        assertEquals(\n            ConnState.Failed(ConnError.Timeout),\n            reduce(ConnState.Connecting(3), ConnEvent.LinkLost(ConnError.Timeout), maxAttempts = 3),\n        )\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Command (GATT queue with results and timeout)\n\n```kotlin\nsealed interface GattCommand {\n    class Read(val characteristic: UUID) : GattCommand\n    class Write(val characteristic: UUID, val value: ByteArray) : GattCommand\n    class RequestMtu(val mtu: Int) : GattCommand\n    data object DiscoverServices : GattCommand\n    class EnableNotifications(val characteristic: UUID) : GattCommand\n}\n\nsealed interface GattResponse {\n    class Value(val bytes: ByteArray) : GattResponse\n    data class Mtu(val value: Int) : GattResponse\n    data object Done : GattResponse\n}\n\nclass GattQueue(scope: CoroutineScope, private val executor: GattExecutor) {\n\n    private class Pending(val command: GattCommand, val result: CompletableDeferred<GattResponse>)\n\n    private val pending = Channel<Pending>(Channel.UNLIMITED)\n\n    init {\n        scope.launch {\n            for (p in pending) {\n                // withTimeoutOrNull: a plain withTimeout throws a CancellationException subtype.\n                val response = try {\n                    withTimeoutOrNull(5.seconds) { executor.executeAndAwait(p.command) }\n                } catch (e: GattException) {\n                    p.result.completeExceptionally(e)\n                    continue\n                }\n                if (response != null) p.result.complete(response) else p.result.completeExceptionally(GattTimeoutException(p.command))\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    suspend fun execute(command: GattCommand): GattResponse {\n        val p = Pending(command, CompletableDeferred())\n        pending.send(p)\n        return p.result.await()\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `Write` holds a `ByteArray`, so it is a plain class: a `data class` would compare arrays by reference.\n- `GattExecutor.executeAndAwait` issues the platform call and suspends until the matching\n  `BluetoothGattCallback` fires (bridged with `suspendCancellableCoroutine` or a `CompletableDeferred`).\n- Payload size, write type and retry policy come from the firmware protocol document.\n\nUndo / redo variant:\n\n```kotlin\ninterface EditCommand {\n    fun apply(doc: Document): Document\n    fun revert(doc: Document): Document\n}\n\nclass History(private var doc: Document) {\n    private val undo = ArrayDeque<EditCommand>()\n    private val redo = ArrayDeque<EditCommand>()\n    fun run(c: EditCommand) { doc = c.apply(doc); undo.addLast(c); redo.clear() }\n    fun undo() { undo.removeLastOrNull()?.let { doc = it.revert(doc); redo.addLast(it) } }\n}\n```\n\n## Router (Compose)\n\n```kotlin\n@Composable\nfun DeviceListScreen(state: DeviceListState, onDeviceClick: (String) -> Unit) { ... }\n\n// The nav host owns the back stack; screens only raise intents.\n// With Navigation 3 the back stack is a list of typed keys you own, rendered by NavDisplay.\nDeviceListScreen(state = state, onDeviceClick = { id -> backStack.add(DeviceDetailKey(id)) })\n```\n\n- ViewModels do not hold a `NavController`. Navigation triggered by a result is modelled as state the\n  screen reacts to, or a callback from the screen after it observes success.\n- Deep links and auth gating are decided in one place (the nav host or a small router class).\n\n## Result types\n\n```kotlin\nsealed interface Outcome<out T, out E> {\n    data class Ok<T>(val value: T) : Outcome<T, Nothing>\n    data class Err<E>(val error: E) : Outcome<Nothing, E>\n}\n\ninline fun <T, E, R> Outcome<T, E>.map(f: (T) -> R): Outcome<R, E> = when (this) {\n    is Outcome.Ok -> Outcome.Ok(f(value))\n    is Outcome.Err -> this\n}\n```\n\n- `kotlin.Result` is fine inside a layer, but its error is an untyped `Throwable` and `runCatching`\n  catches `CancellationException`. At layer boundaries prefer a sealed domain result.\n- Exceptions for programmer errors (`require`, `check`, `error`) are not wrapped.\n\n## Delegation\n\n```kotlin\n// Class delegation: override only what changes.\nclass ReadOnlySettings(private val inner: Settings) : Settings by inner {\n    override fun set(key: String, value: String) = throw UnsupportedOperationException(\"read-only\")\n}\n\n// Property delegation: lifecycle-aware or lazy values without boilerplate.\nprivate val viewModel: DevicesViewModel by viewModels()\nprivate val parser by lazy(LazyThreadSafetyMode.NONE) { FrameParser() } // main-thread only\n```\n\n- `by inner` forwards calls made on the wrapper only. If `inner` calls its own methods internally,\n  the override is not used (unlike inheritance). Check this before relying on it.\n",
    "references/swift.md":
        "# Swift pattern implementations\n\nLoad this when implementing or reviewing patterns in Swift for iOS. Assumes Swift 6 language mode,\nasync/await, `@Observable` (iOS 17+) and SwiftUI. Older targets can swap `@Observable` for\n`ObservableObject` without changing the patterns.\n\n## Repository\n\n```swift\n// Protocol because view models are tested with a fake and the feature module must not see URLSession.\nprotocol DeviceRepository: Sendable {\n    func devices() async throws -> [Device]\n    func refresh() async throws\n}\n\nactor CachedDeviceRepository: DeviceRepository {\n    private let api: DeviceAPI\n    private let store: DeviceStore           // disk cache, the source of truth\n    private var refreshTask: Task<Void, Error>?\n\n    init(api: DeviceAPI, store: DeviceStore) {\n        self.api = api\n        self.store = store\n    }\n\n    func devices() async throws -> [Device] {\n        try await store.load().map(Device.init(record:))\n    }\n\n    // Concurrent callers share one in-flight refresh (actor reentrancy safe).\n    func refresh() async throws {\n        if let refreshTask { return try await refreshTask.value }\n        let task = Task { try await store.save(api.devices().map(DeviceRecord.init(dto:))) }\n        refreshTask = task\n        defer { refreshTask = nil }\n        try await task.value\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- DTOs (`Decodable`) and persistence records stay in the data layer; the protocol returns domain types.\n\n## Factory\n\n```swift\nenum ExportFormat { case pdf, csv }\n\nprotocol Exporter { func export(_ report: Report) throws -> Data }\n\nstruct ExporterFactory {\n    let fonts: FontProvider\n\n    func make(_ format: ExportFormat) -> any Exporter {\n        switch format {\n        case .pdf: PDFExporter(fonts: fonts)\n        case .csv: CSVExporter(separator: \";\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// Failable static factory for validated values.\nstruct SerialNumber: Hashable, Sendable {\n    let value: String\n    private init(_ value: String) { self.value = value }\n\n    static func parse(_ raw: String) -> SerialNumber? {\n        let s = raw.trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespaces).uppercased()\n        guard s.count == 12, s.allSatisfy({ \$0.isASCII && (\$0.isLetter || \$0.isNumber) }) else { return nil }\n        return SerialNumber(s)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Builder replaced by memberwise initializers\n\n```swift\nstruct ScanOptions: Sendable {\n    var timeout: Duration = .seconds(10)\n    var serviceFilter: CBUUID? = nil\n    var allowDuplicates = false\n}\n\nlet options = ScanOptions(serviceFilter: telemetryService) // defaulted members may be omitted\nvar longer = options\nlonger.timeout = .seconds(30)                               // value semantics replace copy()\n```\n\n- Memberwise initializers are internal. Public types need an explicit `public init` with default arguments.\n- Result builders (`@resultBuilder`) are for nested declarative structures, not option bags.\n\n## Singleton replaced by injection\n\n```swift\n@main\nstruct GatewayApp: App {\n    // Composition root: the only place that knows concrete types.\n    @State private var devices = DevicesModel(repository: CachedDeviceRepository(api: .live, store: .disk))\n\n    var body: some Scene {\n        WindowGroup { DevicesView().environment(devices) }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct DevicesView: View {\n    @Environment(DevicesModel.self) private var model\n    var body: some View { List(model.items) { DeviceRow(device: \$0) } }\n}\n```\n\n- `static let shared` is acceptable for truly stateless or system-owned services, and only referenced at the\n  composition root, never inside view models.\n- Tests construct `DevicesModel(repository: FakeDeviceRepository())` directly.\n\n## Strategy\n\n```swift\nstruct BackoffPolicy: Sendable {\n    let delay: @Sendable (_ attempt: Int) -> Duration\n\n    static let fixed = BackoffPolicy { _ in .seconds(1) }\n    static let exponential = BackoffPolicy { attempt in .milliseconds(500 * (1 << min(attempt, 6))) }\n}\n\nfinal class Uploader {\n    private let backoff: BackoffPolicy\n    init(backoff: BackoffPolicy = .exponential) { self.backoff = backoff }\n}\n```\n\nA protocol is the better fit when the strategy has several methods or its own state.\n\n## Observer\n\n```swift\n// UI state: Observation framework.\n@MainActor @Observable\nfinal class DevicesModel {\n    private(set) var items: [Device] = []\n    private let repository: any DeviceRepository\n    init(repository: any DeviceRepository) { self.repository = repository }\n    func load() async { items = (try? await repository.devices()) ?? [] } // real code maps errors to state\n}\n\n// Event stream with a single consumer: AsyncStream.\nfinal class TelemetryFeed: Sendable {\n    let readings: AsyncStream<Reading>\n    private let continuation: AsyncStream<Reading>.Continuation\n\n    init() {\n        (readings, continuation) = AsyncStream.makeStream(of: Reading.self, bufferingPolicy: .bufferingNewest(64))\n    }\n\n    func publish(_ reading: Reading) { continuation.yield(reading) }\n    deinit { continuation.finish() }\n}\n\n// Several subscribers that each need the latest value: Combine.\nfinal class BatteryMonitor {\n    private let subject = CurrentValueSubject<Int?, Never>(nil)\n    var level: AnyPublisher<Int?, Never> { subject.eraseToAnyPublisher() }\n    func update(_ level: Int) { subject.send(level) }\n}\n```\n\n- One `AsyncStream` is meant for one consumer; two loops iterating it split the elements between them.\n- Combine subscribers keep their `AnyCancellable` and capture `self` weakly in `sink`.\n- Consume streams in `.task { for await r in feed.readings { ... } }` so the loop cancels with the view.\n\n## Adapter\n\n```swift\n// Completion-handler API -> async. Resume exactly once on every path.\nextension LegacyPrinter {\n    func printReceipt(_ job: PrintJob) async throws -> PrintReceipt {\n        try await withCheckedThrowingContinuation { continuation in\n            submit(job) { result in continuation.resume(with: result) }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// Delegate API -> ordered AsyncStream. CBCentralManager keeps its delegate weakly; the owner retains this object.\nfinal class CentralEvents: NSObject, CBCentralManagerDelegate {\n    let events: AsyncStream<CentralEvent>\n    private let continuation: AsyncStream<CentralEvent>.Continuation\n    private var central: CBCentralManager?\n\n    override init() {\n        (events, continuation) = AsyncStream.makeStream(of: CentralEvent.self)\n        super.init()\n        central = CBCentralManager(delegate: self, queue: DispatchQueue(label: \"ble.central\"))\n    }\n\n    func centralManagerDidUpdateState(_ central: CBCentralManager) {\n        continuation.yield(.powered(central.state == .poweredOn))\n    }\n\n    func centralManager(_ central: CBCentralManager, didConnect peripheral: CBPeripheral) {\n        continuation.yield(.connected(peripheral.identifier))\n    }\n\n    func centralManager(_ central: CBCentralManager, didDisconnectPeripheral peripheral: CBPeripheral, error: (any Error)?) {\n        continuation.yield(.disconnected(peripheral.identifier))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Add cancellation with `withTaskCancellationHandler` when the wrapped API can be cancelled.\n- Yielding into one stream keeps callback order. Spawning a `Task { await ... }` per callback does not.\n\n## Decorator\n\n```swift\nstruct TimedDeviceAPI: DeviceAPI {\n    let inner: any DeviceAPI\n    let metrics: Metrics\n\n    func firmwareInfo(id: Device.ID) async throws -> FirmwareInfo {\n        let start = ContinuousClock.now\n        defer { metrics.record(\"firmwareInfo\", ContinuousClock.now - start) }\n        return try await inner.firmwareInfo(id: id)\n    }\n\n    // Swift has no automatic forwarding: every other requirement is forwarded by hand.\n    func devices() async throws -> [DeviceDTO] { try await inner.devices() }\n}\n```\n\nLarge protocols make decorators expensive in Swift. Keep the decorated protocol small.\n\n## Facade\n\n```swift\nactor DeviceSession {\n    private let central: CentralClient\n    private let machine: ConnectionStateMachine\n\n    init(central: CentralClient, machine: ConnectionStateMachine) {\n        self.central = central\n        self.machine = machine\n    }\n\n    func open(_ id: UUID) async throws(SessionError) -> PeripheralLink {\n        await machine.send(.connect)\n        do {\n            let link = try await central.connect(id)\n            try await link.discoverServices([telemetryService])\n            try await link.enableNotifications(for: telemetryCharacteristic)\n            await machine.send(.linkUp(mtu: link.maximumWriteLength))\n            return link\n        } catch {\n            throw SessionError.setupFailed(String(describing: error))\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`maximumWriteLength` here stands for a wrapper over `CBPeripheral.maximumWriteValueLength(for:)`.\n\n## State machine (connection)\n\n```swift\nenum ConnectionError: Error, Equatable, Sendable { case timeout, rejected, poweredOff }\n\nenum ConnectionState: Equatable, Sendable {\n    case disconnected\n    case connecting(attempt: Int)\n    case connected(mtu: Int)\n    case disconnecting\n    case failed(ConnectionError)\n}\n\nenum ConnectionEvent: Sendable {\n    case connect, disconnect, closed\n    case linkUp(mtu: Int)\n    case linkLost(ConnectionError)\n}\n\nextension ConnectionState {\n    func next(on event: ConnectionEvent, maxAttempts: Int = 3) -> ConnectionState {\n        switch (self, event) {\n        case (.disconnected, .connect), (.failed, .connect):\n            .connecting(attempt: 1)\n        case (.connecting, .linkUp(let mtu)):\n            .connected(mtu: mtu)\n        case (.connecting(let attempt), .linkLost(let error)):\n            attempt < maxAttempts ? .connecting(attempt: attempt + 1) : .failed(error)\n        case (.connected, .linkLost):\n            .connecting(attempt: 1)\n        case (.connecting, .disconnect), (.connected, .disconnect):\n            .disconnecting\n        case (.disconnecting, .closed), (.disconnecting, .linkLost):\n            .disconnected\n        default:\n            self // illegal transition: ignored, logged by the caller\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nactor ConnectionStateMachine {\n    private(set) var state: ConnectionState = .disconnected\n    let states: AsyncStream<ConnectionState>\n    private let continuation: AsyncStream<ConnectionState>.Continuation\n\n    init() {\n        (states, continuation) = AsyncStream.makeStream(of: ConnectionState.self, bufferingPolicy: .bufferingNewest(1))\n    }\n\n    func send(_ event: ConnectionEvent) {\n        let next = state.next(on: event)\n        guard next != state else { return }\n        state = next\n        continuation.yield(next)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Feed delegate events in order: one task iterates `CentralEvents.events` and calls `await machine.send(...)`.\n- `next(on:)` is pure; test it without Bluetooth:\n\n```swift\n@Test func linkLostWhileConnectedReconnects() {\n    #expect(ConnectionState.connected(mtu: 185).next(on: .linkLost(.timeout)) == .connecting(attempt: 1))\n}\n```\n\n## Command\n\n```swift\nfinal class CommandQueue<Command: Sendable>: Sendable {\n    private let continuation: AsyncStream<Command>.Continuation\n    private let worker: Task<Void, Never>\n\n    init(execute: @escaping @Sendable (Command) async -> Void) {\n        let (stream, continuation) = AsyncStream.makeStream(of: Command.self)\n        self.continuation = continuation\n        worker = Task { for await command in stream { await execute(command) } }\n    }\n\n    func enqueue(_ command: Command) { continuation.yield(command) }\n\n    deinit {\n        continuation.finish()\n        worker.cancel()\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- CoreBluetooth queues individual read and write requests itself. On iOS a command queue is about\n  app-level sequences (write config, wait for acknowledgement, then read status) and undo, not about the stack.\n- Writes without response use flow control: check `canSendWriteWithoutResponse` and resume on\n  `peripheralIsReady(toSendWriteWithoutResponse:)`.\n- Undo in document-style apps: `UndoManager` from the environment rather than a custom stack.\n\n## Coordinator / Router\n\n```swift\nenum OnboardingRoute: Hashable { case permissions, pairing, done }\n\n@MainActor @Observable\nfinal class OnboardingCoordinator {\n    var path: [OnboardingRoute] = []\n    func start() { path.append(.permissions) }\n    func permissionsGranted() { path.append(.pairing) }\n    func paired() { path.append(.done) }\n    func finish() { path.removeAll() }\n}\n\nstruct OnboardingFlow: View {\n    @State private var coordinator = OnboardingCoordinator()\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack(path: \$coordinator.path) {\n            WelcomeView(onContinue: { coordinator.start() })\n                .navigationDestination(for: OnboardingRoute.self) { route in\n                    switch route {\n                    case .permissions: PermissionsView(onGranted: { coordinator.permissionsGranted() })\n                    case .pairing: PairingView(onPaired: { coordinator.paired() })\n                    case .done: DoneView(onFinish: { coordinator.finish() })\n                    }\n                }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Child views receive closures, not the coordinator, so they stay previewable and reusable.\n- UIKit apps use the classic coordinator owning a `UINavigationController`; keep child coordinators in an\n  array and remove them when their flow finishes, or they leak.\n\n## Result and typed throws\n\n```swift\nenum PairingError: Error { case timeout, rejected(reason: String), bluetoothOff }\n\nfunc pair(id: Device.ID) async throws(PairingError) -> Device { ... }\n\nfunc onPairTapped() async {\n    do throws(PairingError) {\n        state = .paired(try await pair(id: selected))\n    } catch {\n        switch error { // error is PairingError, exhaustive switch\n        case .timeout: state = .retryable\n        case .rejected(let reason): state = .failed(reason)\n        case .bluetoothOff: state = .needsBluetooth\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Typed throws only where the error set is closed and callers switch over it; otherwise `throws` (any Error).\n- `Result<Success, Failure>` when an outcome must be stored or passed around, not for normal control flow.\n\n## Delegation\n\n```swift\nprotocol ScannerDelegate: AnyObject {\n    func scanner(_ scanner: Scanner, didFind device: Device)\n}\n\nfinal class Scanner {\n    weak var delegate: ScannerDelegate?\n}\n```\n\n- Prefer closures or `AsyncStream` for new APIs with one callback; delegates suit many related callbacks.\n- `unowned` only when the delegate provably outlives the delegator; otherwise `weak`.\n",
  },
  "mobile-eu-compliance": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-eu-compliance\ndescription: \"Assesses Android, iOS, Flutter and connected-device companion apps against the EU Cyber Resilience Act, the Radio Equipment Directive delegated act with EN 18031-1, -2 and -3, and GDPR with ePrivacy: inventory, requirement mapping, evidence, gaps and a remediation plan in a gap report table. Engineering guidance, not legal advice. Use for CRA or EN 18031 readiness, SBOM and vulnerability handling, BLE or Wi-Fi companion apps, or GDPR reviews of mobile apps.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile EU compliance\n\nTurn EU product rules into engineering work for a mobile app or a connected device's companion app: find out\nwhich rules apply, map the code and processes to each requirement, collect evidence, and list the gaps with\nconcrete fixes and owners.\n\n**This is engineering guidance, not legal advice.** The agent states what the code and documents show and\nwhich requirement they relate to. Scope decisions, conformity routes and anything contractual go to the\nteam's compliance or legal owner. Where a point cannot be verified, the agent says so instead of guessing.\n\n## When to use\n\n- \"Is our BLE companion app ready for the CRA and EN 18031?\"\n- CRA readiness: security updates, vulnerability handling, SBOM, coordinated disclosure, support period.\n- A Wi-Fi, BLE or cellular device is sold in the EU and its app handles pairing, accounts, firmware updates\n  or device data (RED delegated act, EN 18031).\n- GDPR or ePrivacy review of an app: telemetry, analytics and ad SDKs, consent, deletion, retention.\n- Preparing technical documentation or answering a customer or notified-body questionnaire.\n\nNot for: a general security audit with MASVS findings (use `mobile-security`, then map its findings here),\nstore submission rules (use `mobile-release`), or accessibility law (use `mobile-accessibility`).\n\n## Verified key facts\n\nChecked against the Official Journal texts listed in `docs/SOURCES.md` of this pack. Re-check before\nquoting them in customer-facing documents.\n\n| Rule | Fact |\n|---|---|\n| Cyber Resilience Act, Regulation (EU) 2024/2847 | Applies from 11 December 2027. Article 14 (reporting) applies from 11 September 2026; Chapter IV (notified bodies) from 11 June 2026 (Art. 71) |\n| CRA reporting (Art. 14) | Actively exploited vulnerability and severe incident: early warning within 24 hours, notification within 72 hours, to the coordinating CSIRT and ENISA via the single reporting platform. Final report: 14 days after a fix is available (vulnerability), one month after the notification (incident) |\n| CRA support period (Art. 13(8)) | At least five years, or the expected use time if the product is expected to be in use for less |\n| CRA SBOM (Annex I Part II(1)) | \"commonly used and machine-readable format covering at the very least the top-level dependencies\" |\n| RED delegated act, Regulation (EU) 2022/30 | Applies from 1 August 2025 (date set by Delegated Regulation (EU) 2023/2444) |\n| EN 18031-1 / -2 / -3:2024 | Cited with restrictions by Implementing Decision (EU) 2025/138 of 28 January 2025 for RED Art. 3(3)(d), (e) and (f) |\n| GDPR breach notification (Art. 33) | To the supervisory authority without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours |\n| ePrivacy Directive Art. 5(3) | Storing or reading information on the user's device needs consent, unless strictly necessary for the service the user explicitly requested |\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Scope and roles.** Establish what is placed on the EU market and by whom.\n   - Products: the app, the device, the cloud backend. Under the CRA a \"product with digital elements\"\n     includes its remote data processing solutions: backend software designed by the manufacturer without\n     which the product cannot perform one of its functions.\n   - Role: manufacturer, importer or distributor. Most app teams are the manufacturer of the app.\n   - Use the decision table in [CRA](references/cra.md) and [RED and EN 18031](references/red-en18031.md).\n     If the scope cannot be settled from facts, record it as an open question for the legal owner.\n2. **Inventory.** Build it from the code and build files before reading any policy document.\n   - Data: what is collected, where it is stored, where it is sent, retention. Third-party SDKs and what they collect.\n   - Components: dependencies from lock files (`Package.resolved`, `pubspec.lock`, Gradle dependencies).\n   - Interfaces: network endpoints, BLE services and characteristics, deep links, exported components.\n   - Security functions: authentication, pairing, update path (app and firmware), logging, deletion.\n   - Template: [evidence templates](references/evidence-templates.md).\n3. **Map to requirements.** For each applicable rule, walk its list in order:\n   - CRA Annex I Part I points (a) to (m) and Part II points (1) to (8): [CRA](references/cra.md).\n   - EN 18031 mechanisms for the device and the app side: [RED and EN 18031](references/red-en18031.md).\n   - GDPR articles and ePrivacy Art. 5(3): [GDPR for mobile](references/gdpr.md).\n4. **Collect evidence.** Every \"Met\" needs a file, config, test, document or process reference. No evidence\n   means \"Partial\" or \"Missing\", even if the team says it is done.\n5. **Gaps.** Describe each gap as a missing capability, not a missing document. Rate it by the date it must\n   be met (11 September 2026, 11 December 2027, already applicable).\n6. **Remediation plan.** One fix per gap, with an owner role and a target date. Code fixes get a snippet;\n   process fixes get a short template.\n7. **Report** in the output format below. Do not edit code unless asked.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Security updates must reach every user (CRA Annex I Part I(c), Part II(7) and (8))\n\nThe team must be able to ship a fix and get it installed. A store update alone is not enough if old builds\nkeep working with a known vulnerability.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: no way to stop a vulnerable build; users stay on it for years\nclass App : Application()\n\n// GOOD: server-driven minimum version, checked on start, plus Play In-App Updates for the immediate flow\nif (BuildConfig.VERSION_CODE < remoteConfig.minSupportedVersionCode) {\n    appUpdateManager.startUpdateFlowForResult(info, launcher, AppUpdateOptions.newBuilder(AppUpdateType.IMMEDIATE).build())\n}\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD: compare against a minimum build served by your backend and block with an update prompt\nlet build = Int(Bundle.main.object(forInfoDictionaryKey: \"CFBundleVersion\") as? String ?? \"\") ?? 0\nif build < config.minimumSupportedBuild {\n    state = .updateRequired(appStoreURL)\n}\n```\n\n- Ship security fixes separately from feature releases where technically feasible (Part II(2)).\n- Firmware updates delivered through the app count too: see rule 3.\n\n### 2. No default or skippable passwords (CRA Annex I Part I(d); EN 18031 restriction on 6.2.5.1 and 6.2.5.2)\n\nImplementing Decision (EU) 2025/138 removes the presumption of conformity for all three EN 18031 parts if\nthe user is allowed not to set and use any password.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: every device ships with the same PIN, and onboarding offers \"Skip\"\nconst val DEFAULT_PAIRING_PIN = \"000000\"\nfun onSkipPasswordClicked() = finishOnboarding()\n\n// GOOD: per-device secret printed on the label or set during onboarding; the step cannot be skipped\nfun onSetPassword(password: String) {\n    require(passwordPolicy.isAcceptable(password)) { \"rejected by policy\" }\n    device.setAdminPassword(password)\n}\n```\n\n- Brute-force protection on PIN and password entry (rate limit or lockout) on the device side.\n\n### 3. Firmware updates are verified on the device, not only checksummed (CRA Annex I Part I(c) and (f); EN 18031 secure update)\n\nCRC16 or CRC32 detects transmission errors. It does not stop a modified image.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: app downloads over HTTP and the device only checks a CRC\nval image = httpClient.get(\"http://fw.example.com/latest.bin\")\ndfu.send(image, crc32(image))\n\n// GOOD: HTTPS download, signed image, signature verified by the bootloader with a key the app never holds\nval image = api.downloadFirmware(release.id)           // TLS, pinned to your backend if required\nrequire(sha256(image).contentEquals(release.sha256))   // integrity of the download\ndfu.send(image)                                          // device verifies the vendor signature before applying\n```\n\n- EN 18031-3 (financial assets): the decision says clause 6.3.2.4's secure-update assessment criteria do not\n  confer a presumption of conformity. Treat secure update for payment-capable devices as needing extra justification.\n\n### 4. Collect only what the purpose needs (CRA Annex I Part I(g); GDPR Art. 5(1)(c) and Art. 25)\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD\nTelemetry(email = user.email, mac = device.macAddress, gps = location, rssi = rssi)\n\n// GOOD\nTelemetry(installId = rotatingInstallId, firmware = device.firmwareVersion, rssi = rssi)\n```\n\n```dart\n// BAD\nanalytics.log('pair_ok', {'serial': device.serial, 'user': user.email});\n\n// GOOD\nanalytics.log('pair_ok', {'model': device.model});\n```\n\n- MAC addresses, serial numbers, push tokens and advertising IDs are personal data when they can be linked\n  to a person. Hashing them is pseudonymisation, not anonymisation.\n\n### 5. No non-essential device storage or SDK start before consent (ePrivacy Art. 5(3); GDPR Art. 6 and 7)\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: analytics and ad SDKs start in Application.onCreate for every user\noverride fun onCreate() { super.onCreate(); Analytics.init(this); Ads.init(this) }\n\n// GOOD: start only after the user agreed; strictly necessary storage (session, settings) needs no consent\nfun onConsentChanged(consent: Consent) {\n    if (consent.analytics) Analytics.init(context) else Analytics.disable(context)\n}\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD: same rule on iOS; App Tracking Transparency is an additional Apple requirement, not GDPR consent\nif consentStore.analyticsAllowed { Analytics.start() }\n```\n\n### 6. Users can delete all their data and settings (CRA Annex I Part I(m); GDPR Art. 17)\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: \"Delete account\" only clears local preferences\nfun deleteAccount() = prefs.edit { clear() }\n\n// GOOD: server-side deletion, device unpairing and factory reset command, then local wipe\nsuspend fun deleteAccount() {\n    api.deleteAccount()                 // backend deletes account and device records\n    device.sendFactoryResetIfConnected()\n    localStore.wipe()                   // databases, files, Keystore aliases, caches\n}\n```\n\n### 7. Security-relevant events are logged without personal data, with a user opt-out (CRA Annex I Part I(l))\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: no record of security events, or a record full of personal data\nLog.d(\"Auth\", \"login ok for \$email token=\$token\")\n\n// GOOD: structured security events, no identifiers beyond what is needed, opt-out respected\nsecurityLog.record(SecurityEvent.PairingFailed(reason = Reason.WrongPin, attempt = 3))\n```\n\n### 8. Every release has an SBOM (CRA Annex I Part II(1))\n\n```kotlin\n// GOOD: build.gradle.kts, CycloneDX Gradle plugin (pin the version in libs.versions.toml)\nplugins { id(\"org.cyclonedx.bom\") }\n// ./gradlew cyclonedxBom  -> archive the generated BOM with the release\n```\n\n- iOS and Flutter: generate from `Package.resolved`, `Podfile.lock` or `pubspec.lock` with an SBOM tool\n  that reads them, in CI, per release tag. Keep the SBOM with the build, not only in a dashboard.\n\n### 9. A vulnerability contact and disclosure policy exist and are findable (CRA Annex I Part II(5) and (6); Annex II(2))\n\n```markdown\n<!-- BAD: no SECURITY.md, no contact in the app or on the website -->\n\n<!-- GOOD: SECURITY.md in the repository, the same contact on the product website and in the app's About screen -->\n## Reporting a vulnerability\nEmail security@example.com or use https://example.com/security. We acknowledge within 5 working days.\nSupported versions and the support end date: see https://example.com/support-period.\n```\n\n- Publish information about fixed vulnerabilities once the update is available (Part II(4)).\n\n### 10. Actively exploited vulnerabilities are reported on time (CRA Art. 14, from 11 September 2026)\n\nThis is a process, so the deliverable is a runbook, not code.\n\n```text\nBAD:  \"Security issues go to the backlog.\"\nGOOD: Runbook: triage owner on call; if exploitation is confirmed, early warning to the coordinating CSIRT\n      and ENISA via the single reporting platform within 24 h, notification within 72 h, final report\n      14 days after the fix ships; inform affected users about the issue and mitigations.\n```\n\n### 11. The support period is declared (CRA Art. 13(8); Annex II(7))\n\n- The end date of security support is stated where users see it before and after purchase (store listing,\n  website, About screen). At least five years unless the expected use time is shorter.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Declaring the app \"compliant\". The report states what was found; conformity is a manufacturer decision.\n- Treating the CRA as a document exercise. Most Annex I items are product capabilities that need code.\n- Assuming a BLE-only device is out of RED delegated-act scope. Article 1(1) covers equipment that can\n  communicate over the internet \"directly or via any other equipment\"; whether a phone link counts for a\n  given product is not settled by official guidance, so record it as a scope question.\n- Using EN 18031 \"guidance\" or \"rationale\" sections as evidence. The decision says they confer no presumption.\n- Relying on CRC for update integrity, or on obfuscation for confidentiality.\n- Hashing identifiers and calling the data anonymous.\n- Inventing article numbers, dates or clause numbers. If a citation cannot be confirmed, say so.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Scope written down: products, roles, which of CRA, RED delegated act, GDPR and ePrivacy apply, open questions\n- [ ] Inventory built from code and lock files: data, SDKs, components, interfaces, security functions\n- [ ] Every CRA Annex I Part I point (a)-(m) and Part II point (1)-(8) has a status and evidence\n- [ ] EN 18031 part(s) identified for the device; app-side mechanisms mapped\n- [ ] No default or skippable passwords; brute-force protection exists\n- [ ] Update path for app and firmware: forced minimum version, signed firmware, verified on device\n- [ ] Data minimisation applied to telemetry; consent before non-essential SDKs and device storage\n- [ ] Full deletion path: account, backend data, device reset, local data\n- [ ] SBOM generated per release; vulnerability contact, disclosure policy and support end date published\n- [ ] Article 14 reporting runbook exists with owners\n- [ ] Report says \"engineering guidance, not legal advice\" and lists unverified points\n\n## Output format\n\n~~~markdown\n## EU compliance gap report: <product / app / version>\n\nEngineering assessment, not legal advice. Scope and conformity decisions belong to <compliance owner>.\n\nScope: <app, device model, backend>. Role: <manufacturer | importer | distributor>.\nApplies: CRA <yes | open question>, RED delegated act <yes | no | open question>, GDPR <yes>, ePrivacy 5(3) <yes>.\nKey dates: CRA reporting 11 Sep 2026; CRA full application 11 Dec 2027; RED delegated act applies since 1 Aug 2025.\n\n| Requirement | Status | Evidence | Gap | Fix | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CRA Annex I Part I(c) security updates | Partial | Play In-App Updates in `UpdateManager.kt:31`; no minimum-version gate | Old builds keep working after a security fix | Server-driven minimum version check (rule 1) | Mobile lead |\n| EN 18031-1 6.2.5 passwords / CRA I.I(d) | Missing | Onboarding has a Skip button (`OnboardingScreen.kt:88`) | User can keep no password; no presumption of conformity | Remove Skip, enforce password policy | Product + firmware |\n| CRA Annex I Part II(1) SBOM | Missing | No SBOM step in `.github/workflows/release.yml` | No SBOM per release | Add CycloneDX generation and archive | DevOps |\n| GDPR Art. 5(1)(c) minimisation | Met | `Telemetry.kt` sends model, firmware, RSSI only | - | - | - |\n| ePrivacy Art. 5(3) consent | Not applicable | No analytics or ad SDKs in `build.gradle.kts` | - | - | - |\n\n### Open questions for the compliance owner\n- Is the device \"internet-connected radio equipment\" when it reaches the cloud only through the app?\n\n### Remediation plan\n1. <fix> -- owner, target date, deadline driver (for example CRA Art. 14 from 11 Sep 2026)\n\n### Not assessed\n- Backend and cloud infrastructure, device firmware internals, contracts with SDK vendors, <other>\n~~~\n\nStatus values: Met, Partial, Missing, Not applicable. \"Met\" always has evidence.\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-security` -- technical MASVS audit whose findings feed this gap report\n- `mobile-ble` -- pairing, secure update and protocol work on the app side of a device\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- SBOM generation, dependency scanning and signed release pipelines\n- `mobile-release` -- store privacy declarations, account deletion and release process\n- `mobile-merge-request` -- describing compliance-relevant changes in the MR\n- `mobile-accessibility` -- the European Accessibility Act, which this skill does not cover\n",
    "references/cra.md":
        "# Cyber Resilience Act for mobile and companion apps\n\nRegulation (EU) 2024/2847. Load this when mapping an app or a device's app to CRA requirements.\nQuotes are from the Official Journal text; article and annex numbers refer to that text. Engineering\nguidance, not legal advice.\n\n## Timeline (Art. 71)\n\n| Date | What applies |\n|---|---|\n| 10 December 2024 | Entry into force |\n| 11 June 2026 | Chapter IV (Articles 35 to 51): notification of conformity assessment bodies |\n| 11 September 2026 | Article 14: reporting of actively exploited vulnerabilities and severe incidents |\n| 11 December 2027 | Everything else, including Annex I essential requirements and conformity assessment |\n\nReporting applies to products already on the market, so the Article 14 process is needed before 2027.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Art. 2(1): applies to \"products with digital elements made available on the market, the intended purpose\n  or reasonably foreseeable use of which includes a direct or indirect logical or physical data connection\n  to a device or network\". A mobile app and a connected device both meet this.\n- Art. 3(1): a \"product with digital elements\" is \"a software or hardware product and its remote data\n  processing solutions\".\n- Art. 3(2): \"remote data processing\" is data processing at a distance whose software is designed by or\n  under the responsibility of the manufacturer, \"the absence of which would prevent the product with digital\n  elements from performing one of its functions\". A backend the app needs to work is usually in scope as part\n  of the product; a general-purpose website or unrelated SaaS is not.\n- Excluded by Art. 2(2)-(4): products covered by the medical device regulations (EU) 2017/745 and 2017/746,\n  vehicle type approval (EU) 2019/2144, civil aviation (EU) 2018/1139 certification, and marine equipment\n  Directive 2014/90/EU. Art. 2(5) allows further limitation for sectors with equivalent rules.\n- Free and open-source software that is not supplied in the course of a commercial activity is treated\n  differently (open-source software stewards have lighter duties). Whether a given app is \"commercial\" is a\n  legal question; record it if unclear.\n\n### Decision table\n\n| Situation | Typical CRA position | Check with the compliance owner |\n|---|---|---|\n| Paid or monetised app on Google Play or the App Store in the EU | Product with digital elements; the publisher is the manufacturer | Monetisation model, who places it on the market |\n| Free app of a company that sells a device or service | Usually in scope as part of a commercial activity | Whether the app is a separate product or part of the device |\n| Companion app required to operate a connected device | Device and app both in scope; backend may be remote data processing | Whether the device maker or the app publisher is the manufacturer of each part |\n| Medical device app under (EU) 2017/745 | Excluded from the CRA | Medical device classification |\n\n## Important and critical products (Art. 7, Annex III, Annex IV)\n\nProducts whose core functionality matches Annex III are \"important\" and need a stricter conformity route if\nharmonised standards are not fully applied (Art. 32(2) and (3)). Examples relevant to mobile and IoT from\nAnnex III class I: identity management and access control including biometric readers, password managers,\nVPN products, smart home general purpose virtual assistants, smart home products with security functionalities\n(smart door locks, security cameras, baby monitoring systems, alarm systems), internet-connected toys with\nsocial interactive or location tracking features, and personal wearables with a health monitoring purpose\nnot covered by the medical device rules.\n\n- Art. 7(1): \"The integration of a product with digital elements which has the core functionality of a product\n  category set out in Annex III shall not in itself render the product in which it is integrated subject to the\n  conformity assessment procedures\" for important products. Whether a companion app shares the classification\n  of the device it controls is not settled by the text; flag it as a scope question.\n- Default products use internal control (module A, Art. 32(1)(a)): the manufacturer self-assesses.\n\n## Annex I Part I mapped to mobile work\n\n| Point | Requirement (shortened) | Mobile evidence to look for |\n|---|---|---|\n| (1) | Appropriate level of cybersecurity based on risks | Written risk assessment (Art. 13(2)) referenced in the technical documentation |\n| (2)(a) | No known exploitable vulnerabilities at release | Dependency scan results per release, triage records |\n| (2)(b) | Secure by default configuration, with reset to original state | Defaults: TLS only, no debug flags, no default passwords, factory reset |\n| (2)(c) | Vulnerabilities addressed through security updates, automatic where applicable, with opt-out and user notification | Forced update / minimum version, Play In-App Updates, firmware update path |\n| (2)(d) | Protection from unauthorised access, report possible unauthorised access | Authentication, pairing, session handling, lockout, login notifications |\n| (2)(e) | Confidentiality of stored, transmitted, processed data, encryption at rest and in transit | Keystore / Keychain storage, TLS, BLE link encryption plus app-layer protection |\n| (2)(f) | Integrity of data, commands, programs and configuration; report corruptions | Signed firmware, authenticated BLE commands, integrity checks |\n| (2)(g) | Data minimisation | Telemetry field list with purposes |\n| (2)(h) | Availability of essential functions, resilience against denial of service | Offline behaviour, retry and backoff, rate limits |\n| (2)(i) | Limit negative impact on other devices or networks | Scan and reconnect throttling, no network flooding |\n| (2)(j) | Limited attack surface including external interfaces | Exported components, deep links, open ports, debug endpoints removed |\n| (2)(k) | Exploitation mitigation | Platform hardening, R8, no dynamic code loading, input validation |\n| (2)(l) | Recording and monitoring of security-relevant activity, with user opt-out | Security event log design, no personal data in it |\n| (2)(m) | Users can securely and permanently remove all data and settings | Account deletion, device reset, local wipe |\n\n## Annex I Part II vulnerability handling\n\n| Point | Requirement (shortened) | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| (1) | Identify and document vulnerabilities and components, SBOM \"covering at the very least the top-level dependencies\" | SBOM per release in a commonly used machine-readable format (CycloneDX or SPDX) |\n| (2) | Remediate without delay; security updates separate from feature updates where feasible | Hotfix process, release history |\n| (3) | Effective and regular security tests and reviews | Test reports, review records, pentest scope |\n| (4) | Publicly disclose fixed vulnerabilities once an update is available | Security advisories or release notes |\n| (5) | Coordinated vulnerability disclosure policy | Published CVD policy |\n| (6) | Facilitate information sharing, provide a contact address for reports | `SECURITY.md`, website security page, `security.txt` |\n| (7) | Secure distribution of updates, automatic where applicable | Signed builds, store distribution, signed firmware |\n| (8) | Security updates disseminated without delay, free of charge, with advisory messages | Release policy, user-facing advisory text |\n\n## Manufacturer obligations most relevant to app teams (Art. 13)\n\n- Cybersecurity risk assessment, documented and kept up to date (Art. 13(2) and (3)).\n- Due diligence for third-party components, including open source (Art. 13(5)).\n- Support period: \"at least five years. Where the product with digital elements is expected to be in use for\n  less than five years, the support period shall correspond to the expected use time\" (Art. 13(8)).\n- Technical documentation (Annex VII) and EU declaration of conformity; CE marking for products placed on the market.\n- User information (Annex II): manufacturer contact, single point of contact for vulnerability reports and\n  the CVD policy, intended purpose, security properties, end date of the support period, how to install\n  security updates, how to securely decommission and remove data.\n\n## Reporting (Art. 14), from 11 September 2026\n\n| Event | Early warning | Notification | Final report |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Actively exploited vulnerability | Within 24 hours of becoming aware | Within 72 hours | No later than 14 days after a corrective or mitigating measure is available |\n| Severe incident affecting the product's security | Within 24 hours | Within 72 hours | Within one month after the incident notification |\n\n- Sent simultaneously to the CSIRT designated as coordinator and to ENISA via the single reporting platform.\n- An incident is severe if it affects, or can affect, the protection of availability, authenticity, integrity\n  or confidentiality of sensitive or important data or functions, or has led or can lead to malicious code\n  in the product or the user's systems (Art. 14(5)).\n- Impacted users are informed about the vulnerability or incident and the mitigations they can take.\n\n## What an app team can do now\n\n1. Name an owner for vulnerability handling and the Article 14 runbook.\n2. Publish a vulnerability contact and CVD policy; add `SECURITY.md` and a website security page.\n3. Generate and archive an SBOM per release; scan dependencies in CI.\n4. Add a forced-update mechanism to every app, and a signed, verified firmware update path to every device.\n5. Decide and publish the support period.\n6. Start the risk assessment and technical documentation; feed in `mobile-security` audit findings.\n",
    "references/evidence-templates.md":
        "# Evidence templates\n\nLoad this when building the inventory, collecting evidence or writing the process documents the gap report\nasks for. Copy, fill and keep with the technical documentation.\n\n## 1. Product inventory\n\n```markdown\n## Products\n| Product | Type | Placed on EU market by | Version / model | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| <App name> Android | App | <company> | 3.4.0 | Google Play |\n| <App name> iOS | App | <company> | 3.4.0 | App Store |\n| <Device model> | Radio equipment (BLE, Wi-Fi) | <company> | HW rev C, FW 2.1.0 | |\n| <Backend> | Remote data processing | <company> | | Needed for remote control and updates |\n\n## Data\n| Field | Source | Purpose | Lawful basis | Stored where | Sent to | Retention |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n## Third-party components and SDKs\n| Component | Version | Purpose | Data it receives | Role (processor/controller) | Contract |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n## Interfaces\n| Interface | Direction | Authentication | Encryption | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| HTTPS API | app -> backend | OAuth 2.0 PKCE | TLS 1.2+ | |\n| MQTT | device -> broker | per-device cert | TLS | port 8883 |\n| BLE GATT | app <-> device | LE Secure Connections + app-layer token | link + AEAD | commands authenticated |\n\n## Security functions\n| Function | Implemented in | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pairing / onboarding | app + device | `OnboardingFlow.kt`, firmware spec section 4 |\n| App update enforcement | app + backend | `UpdateGate.kt`, remote config `min_version_code` |\n| Firmware update | app + device + backend | `DfuManager.kt`, bootloader signature check |\n| Deletion | app + backend + device | `AccountDeletion.kt`, API `DELETE /me`, reset command 0x7F |\n```\n\n## 2. Evidence rules\n\n| Status | Needs |\n|---|---|\n| Met | A file and line, a config, a test, a CI job, a published URL or a named process document |\n| Partial | Evidence for part of the requirement and a clear statement of what is missing |\n| Missing | Nothing found. Say where the agent looked |\n| Not applicable | The reason, for example \"no payment function, EN 18031-3 does not apply\" |\n\nStatements from the team without evidence are recorded as \"Partial: stated, not evidenced\".\n\n## 3. Coordinated vulnerability disclosure policy (CRA Annex I Part II(5) and (6))\n\n```markdown\n# Security policy\n\n## Reporting a vulnerability\nReport security issues to security@<domain> or through <web form>. Please do not open public issues.\nInclude the product, version, steps to reproduce and impact.\n\n## What happens next\n- We acknowledge your report within <N> working days.\n- We keep you informed while we investigate and fix.\n- We publish an advisory once a fix is available, and credit you if you wish.\n\n## Supported versions\nSecurity updates are provided until <support end date> for <product and versions>.\n```\n\nPublish the same contact as `/.well-known/security.txt` on the product website (RFC 9116) and in the app's\nAbout or Help screen.\n\n## 4. Article 14 reporting runbook (from 11 September 2026)\n\n```markdown\n# Exploited vulnerability and severe incident runbook\n\nOwner: <role>. Deputy: <role>. On-call contact: <channel>.\n\n1. Triage: confirm active exploitation or a severe incident (Art. 14(5) criteria).\n2. Within 24 h of awareness: early warning to the CSIRT designated as coordinator and ENISA via the single\n   reporting platform. Record the timestamp of awareness.\n3. Within 72 h: full notification with general information, nature of the exploit or incident, and\n   mitigations.\n4. Fix: ship the security update (separate from feature work where feasible).\n5. Final report: vulnerability, no later than 14 days after the fix is available; incident, within one month\n   after the notification.\n6. Inform impacted users about the issue and the measures they can take (Art. 14(8)).\n7. Post-mortem and update the risk assessment.\n```\n\n## 5. Support period statement (CRA Art. 13(8), Annex II(7))\n\n```markdown\n<Product> receives security updates until <date>. Updates are delivered through <Google Play / App Store /\nin-app firmware update>. After that date, known vulnerabilities may not be fixed.\n```\n\n## 6. SBOM per release (CRA Annex I Part II(1))\n\n```yaml\n# CI step sketch; pin every action by commit SHA and every tool version.\n- name: SBOM (Android, Gradle)\n  run: ./gradlew cyclonedxBom          # CycloneDX Gradle plugin, id org.cyclonedx.bom\n- name: Archive SBOM with the release\n  run: cp \"\$BOM_PATH\" \"dist/sbom-\${VERSION}.cdx.json\"   # BOM_PATH: the file the plugin version you pin writes\n```\n\n- iOS and Flutter: use an SBOM generator that reads `Package.resolved`, `Podfile.lock` or `pubspec.lock`, and\n  confirm in its output that the app's direct dependencies are listed. The CRA minimum is \"at the very least\n  the top-level dependencies\".\n- Store the SBOM with each release build; regenerate on every release, not once.\n",
    "references/gdpr.md":
        "# GDPR and ePrivacy for mobile apps\n\nRegulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR) and Directive 2002/58/EC as amended (ePrivacy). Load this for any app that\nprocesses personal data of people in the EU. Engineering guidance, not legal advice: lawful basis, legitimate\ninterest balancing and transfer mechanisms are decisions for the controller's privacy owner.\n\n## What counts as personal data in an app\n\n- Personal data includes online identifiers. Recital 30: people \"may be associated with online identifiers\n  provided by their devices, applications, tools and protocols, such as internet protocol addresses, cookie\n  identifiers or other identifiers\".\n- In practice: account IDs, emails, IP addresses, advertising IDs, push tokens, `identifierForVendor`, install\n  IDs, device MAC addresses and serial numbers linked to an account, precise location, BLE scan results that\n  identify nearby devices.\n- Pseudonymised data (hashed IDs) is still personal data. Only truly anonymous data is outside the GDPR.\n- Health data (Art. 9 special category) includes data revealing physical or mental health, for example heart\n  rate or sleep data from a wearable. It needs an Art. 9(2) condition, usually explicit consent.\n\n## Article map for app teams\n\n| Article | Topic | What to check in the app |\n|---|---|---|\n| 5(1)(b), (c), (e) | Purpose limitation, data minimisation, storage limitation | Each collected field has a purpose; retention periods exist and are enforced |\n| 6 | Lawful basis | Basis documented per purpose (contract, consent, legitimate interest, legal obligation) |\n| 7 | Conditions for consent | Consent is a separate, unticked choice; refusing is as easy as accepting; withdrawal is possible in the app |\n| 8 | Child's consent for information society services | Age 16 by default, Member States may lower to 13; parental authorisation below that |\n| 9 | Special categories (health, biometrics) | Explicit consent or other Art. 9(2) condition; extra protection |\n| 12-13 | Transparency | Privacy notice reachable in the app before and after sign-up |\n| 15, 16, 17, 18, 20, 21 | Access, rectification, erasure, restriction, portability, objection | In-app or documented request path; export in a machine-readable format; full deletion |\n| 25 | Data protection by design and by default | Optional data collection off by default; least-privilege permissions |\n| 28 | Processor | Contract (data processing agreement) with every SDK vendor or backend that processes data on your behalf |\n| 30 | Records of processing | Each SDK and data flow appears in the record |\n| 32 | Security of processing | Encryption in transit and at rest, access control (see `mobile-security`) |\n| 33, 34 | Breach notification | Supervisory authority \"without undue delay and, where feasible, not later than 72 hours\"; users when high risk |\n| 35 | Data protection impact assessment | Likely needed for large-scale location tracking, health data, children, or systematic monitoring |\n| 44 onwards | Transfers outside the EEA | SDKs and backends sending data to third countries have a transfer mechanism |\n\n## ePrivacy Art. 5(3): storage and access on the device\n\n\"The storing of information, or the gaining of access to information already stored, in the terminal\nequipment of a subscriber or user is only allowed on condition that the subscriber or user concerned has given\nhis or her consent\", except where strictly necessary for transmission or \"for the provider of an information\nsociety service explicitly requested by the subscriber or user to provide the service\".\n\nFor apps this covers more than cookies:\n- Reading the advertising ID, writing analytics IDs, SDK caches used for tracking, device fingerprinting.\n- Strictly necessary (no consent needed): login session, user settings, offline data of the feature the user uses.\n- Consent must be collected before the SDK starts, not after.\n\n## Third-party SDKs\n\n| SDK type | Typical role | What to verify |\n|---|---|---|\n| Crash reporting | Processor, sometimes controller for its own purposes | DPA, data fields, opt-out, retention, region |\n| Product analytics | Processor or joint controller | Consent before start (if not strictly necessary), IP handling, user ID mapping |\n| Advertising, attribution | Often independent controller | Consent under ePrivacy 5(3) and a lawful basis; disclosure in the notice |\n| Push notifications | Processor | Token handling, deletion on logout |\n| Customer support chat | Processor | What is sent with each message (device info, logs) |\n\nCheck what each SDK collects by default. Many send device identifiers and IP addresses on first start.\n\n## Store declarations\n\nGoogle Play's Data safety form and Apple's App Privacy details are store requirements, not GDPR, but they\nmust match what the code and the privacy notice say. A mismatch is both a store rejection risk and evidence\nof poor transparency. See `mobile-release`.\n\n## Practical checks\n\n- [ ] Data inventory lists every field, purpose, lawful basis, retention, recipients and region\n- [ ] No non-essential SDK starts before consent; consent can be withdrawn in settings\n- [ ] Account deletion removes data from app, backend, processors and connected devices\n- [ ] Export of the user's data exists or a documented process covers it\n- [ ] Logs and crash reports contain no direct identifiers, tokens or location\n- [ ] Breach response process names who notifies the supervisory authority within 72 hours\n- [ ] DPIA considered for location, health, children or large-scale monitoring\n",
    "references/red-en18031.md":
        "# Radio Equipment Directive delegated act and EN 18031\n\nLoad this when a Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, cellular or other radio device is placed on the EU market and the app\npairs with it, controls it, updates it or handles its data. Engineering guidance, not legal advice.\n\nWhat could be verified: the delegated act (EU) 2022/30, its amendment (EU) 2023/2444 and Implementing\nDecision (EU) 2025/138 from the Official Journal. The EN 18031 standards are paid documents; clause numbers\nbelow are only those quoted in the Implementing Decision. For any other clause, the agent must ask for the\nteam's copy of the standard instead of citing from memory.\n\n## Dates\n\n- Delegated Regulation (EU) 2022/30 applies from 1 August 2025 (originally 1 August 2024, moved by\n  Delegated Regulation (EU) 2023/2444).\n- EN 18031-1:2024, EN 18031-2:2024 and EN 18031-3:2024 were cited, with restrictions, by Implementing\n  Decision (EU) 2025/138 of 28 January 2025. Presumption of conformity starts from publication of the reference.\n- The CRA recitals expect the Commission to repeal or amend Delegated Regulation (EU) 2022/30 for products\n  that the CRA covers. Until that happens, both apply to a radio device. Check the current status before\n  planning beyond 2027.\n\n## Which requirement applies (Art. 1 of 2022/30)\n\n| RED requirement | Applies to | Harmonised standard |\n|---|---|---|\n| Art. 3(3)(d) no harm to the network, no misuse of network resources | Internet-connected radio equipment: equipment that \"can communicate itself over the internet, whether it communicates directly or via any other equipment\" | EN 18031-1 |\n| Art. 3(3)(e) protection of personal data and privacy | If it can process personal data, traffic data or location data: internet-connected radio equipment, childcare equipment, toys (Directive 2009/48/EC), and equipment worn on the body or clothing | EN 18031-2 |\n| Art. 3(3)(f) protection from fraud | Internet-connected radio equipment that lets the user transfer money, monetary value or virtual currency | EN 18031-3 |\n\nExclusions (Art. 2 of 2022/30):\n- None of the three apply to equipment covered by the medical device regulations (EU) 2017/745 or 2017/746.\n- (e) and (f) do not apply to equipment covered by (EU) 2018/1139 (civil aviation), (EU) 2019/2144 (vehicle\n  type approval) or Directive (EU) 2019/520 (electronic road tolling).\n\n### BLE devices that reach the internet through the phone\n\nThe text covers equipment that communicates over the internet \"directly or via any other equipment\". Whether\na BLE accessory whose data reaches a cloud only through the companion app falls under that phrase is not\nsettled by an official Commission statement that this pack could verify. Industry papers and law firms read\nit differently. The agent records this as an open scope question, lists the facts (does the device originate\nor receive internet traffic through the phone, for example firmware downloads or cloud commands relayed by\nthe app?), and does not decide it.\n\n## Restrictions in Implementing Decision (EU) 2025/138\n\n| Standard | Restriction |\n|---|---|\n| All three | The \"rationale\" and \"guidance\" sections do not confer a presumption of conformity |\n| All three | No presumption of conformity if, when applying clauses 6.2.5.1 and 6.2.5.2, \"the user is allowed not to set and use any password\" |\n| EN 18031-2 | For equipment covered by clauses 6.1.3 to 6.1.6 (toys and childcare), no presumption of conformity if, applying clauses 6.1.3.4.2, 6.1.4.4.2, 6.1.5.4.2 and 6.1.6.4.2, \"parental or guardian access control is not ensured\" |\n| EN 18031-3 | The assessment criteria of clause 6.3.2.4 (secure updates) do not confer a presumption of conformity |\n\nWhere a restriction applies, the manufacturer cannot rely on the standard alone for that point; the\nconformity route then needs a different justification or a notified body. That decision belongs to the\ncompliance owner.\n\n## What the app side usually has to provide\n\nThe standards are written for the radio equipment, but a companion app often implements the device's\nsecurity functions. Map each to the device's mechanisms and to evidence in the app.\n\n| Area | App-side capability | Evidence in an app codebase |\n|---|---|---|\n| Authentication, no default passwords | Pairing requires a per-device secret or a user-set password; no skip; brute-force protection | Onboarding flow, pairing code, lockout handling |\n| Access control | Only the owner (and invited users) can control the device; roles enforced on device or backend | Device sharing flow, backend authorization checks |\n| Secure update | App delivers firmware over TLS; image is signed and verified by the device; no downgrade to vulnerable versions | DFU code, signature verification on device, version checks |\n| Secure storage | Device credentials and keys stored in Keystore / Keychain; never in logs or plain files | Storage layer, key handling |\n| Secure communication | TLS to backend and brokers; BLE LE Secure Connections pairing plus app-layer authentication or encryption for sensitive commands | Network config, BLE protocol spec, command authentication |\n| Resilience | Reconnect with backoff, no flooding of the device or network, safe behaviour on malformed input | Reconnect logic, input validation |\n| Logging | Security events recorded without personal data | Event log design |\n| Deletion | User can remove personal data from app, backend and device (factory reset) | Delete-account and reset flows |\n| User notification | User is told about security-relevant changes, for example a new device owner or a failed update | Notification flows |\n\n## ETSI EN 303 645 (consumer IoT baseline)\n\nETSI EN 303 645 V3.1.3 (published September 2024) is a widely used consumer IoT baseline; ETSI TS 103 701\nV2.1.1 describes how to assess against it. It is not the harmonised standard for the RED delegated act, but\nits 13 provisions overlap with EN 18031 and the CRA and are useful as a checklist:\n\n1. No universal default passwords\n2. Implement a means to manage reports of vulnerabilities\n3. Keep software updated\n4. Securely store sensitive security parameters\n5. Communicate securely\n6. Minimise exposed attack surfaces\n7. Ensure software integrity\n8. Ensure that personal data is secure\n9. Make systems resilient to outages\n10. Examine system telemetry data\n11. Make it easy for users to delete user data\n12. Make installation and maintenance of devices easy\n13. Validate input data\n",
  },
  "mobile-flutter": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-flutter\ndescription: \"Writes idiomatic modern Dart 3 and Flutter: records, patterns and sealed classes, async and Streams, Isolate.run, small const widgets, keys, context.mounted after await, disposing controllers, Riverpod or Bloc, go_router, Pigeon platform channels, flavors, lints and unit, widget, golden and integration tests. Use when writing, refactoring or reviewing Dart or Flutter code, screens, providers, blocs, routes or tests.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Dart and Flutter\n\nWrite Dart and Flutter code the way a senior Flutter engineer would: Dart 3 types that make illegal states\nunrepresentable, widgets that rebuild only what changed, async code that respects the widget lifecycle,\nand one consistent state management approach per app.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Writing a new screen, widget, provider, bloc, repository, route or platform channel in a Flutter app.\n- Refactoring pre-Dart 3 code (enums plus nullable fields, `Map<String, dynamic>` everywhere, `is` chains).\n- Fixing jank, leaks, \"setState() called after dispose()\" or \"Looking up a deactivated widget's ancestor\".\n- Setting up `analysis_options.yaml`, flavors, `--dart-define-from-file`, Pigeon or the test pyramid.\n\nNot for: reviewing a whole MR (use `mobile-code-review`), a security audit (use `mobile-security`),\nor choosing an architecture from scratch (use `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Read the project setup before writing code.\n   - `pubspec.yaml`: SDK constraint, state management package, router, codegen (`build_runner`, `freezed`).\n   - `analysis_options.yaml`: which lint set is active.\n   - Match the existing patterns. Never add Bloc to a Riverpod app or the other way round.\n2. Model the data and state first with sealed classes, records and extension types (rules 1 to 3).\n3. Write the logic outside widgets: repository, then Notifier or Bloc. Load\n   [state management](references/state-management.md) for Riverpod and Bloc details.\n4. Write the UI as small `const` widget classes. Check every `await` in UI code for a `mounted` check,\n   and every controller, subscription and timer for a `dispose`.\n5. Native code goes through Pigeon. Load [platform channels](references/platform-channels.md).\n6. Add tests at the lowest level that proves the behaviour. Load [testing](references/testing.md).\n7. If performance is in scope, measure in profile mode first. Load [performance](references/performance.md).\n8. Run `dart format .`, `flutter analyze` and `flutter test`. Fix every warning before handing over.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Closed state is a sealed class, matched with an exhaustive switch\n\nThe compiler flags every `switch` that misses a new subtype. No `default`, no stale branches.\n\n```dart\n// BAD: impossible combinations compile (isLoading && error != null)\nclass DeviceState { bool isLoading = false; Device? device; String? error; }\n\n// GOOD\nsealed class DeviceState {}\nfinal class DeviceLoading extends DeviceState {}\nfinal class DeviceReady extends DeviceState { DeviceReady(this.device); final Device device; }\nfinal class DeviceFailed extends DeviceState { DeviceFailed(this.error); final Object error; }\n\nWidget build(BuildContext context) => switch (state) {\n  DeviceLoading() => const CircularProgressIndicator(),\n  DeviceReady(:final device) => DeviceCard(device: device),\n  DeviceFailed(:final error) => ErrorView(error: error),\n};\n```\n\n- Add `when` guards for sub-cases: `Connecting(:final attempt) when attempt > 3 => ...`.\n- Do not add `_ =>` to a switch over a sealed type. It hides the next missing case.\n\n### 2. Records and patterns replace throwaway classes and unchecked maps\n\n```dart\n// BAD: unchecked casts on untrusted JSON, crash on a wrong type\nfinal lat = json['lat'] as double;\nfinal lng = json['lng'] as double;\n\n// GOOD: destructure and validate in one step\n({double lat, double lng}) parseLocation(Map<String, Object?> json) {\n  if (json case {'lat': final double lat, 'lng': final double lng}) {\n    return (lat: lat, lng: lng);\n  }\n  throw const FormatException('Invalid location');\n}\n```\n\n- Use records for multiple return values: `final (count, name) = pair();`.\n- Records are fine for private, local data. Public domain models stay named classes.\n- Use dot shorthands (`mainAxisAlignment: .center`) and null-aware elements (`[a, ?maybeB]`) when the\n  SDK constraint allows them and the codebase already uses them.\n\n### 3. Class modifiers and extension types state the API contract\n\n```dart\n// BAD: any String goes, device and user IDs are interchangeable\nFuture<void> connect(String deviceId, String userId);\n\n// GOOD: zero-cost wrappers, mixing them up is a compile error\nextension type const DeviceId(String value) {}\nextension type const UserId(String value) {}\nFuture<void> connect(DeviceId device, UserId user);\n```\n\n| Modifier | Use for |\n|---|---|\n| `final class` | Value and state classes; no subclassing or implementing outside the library |\n| `abstract interface class` | Repository and service contracts that are only implemented |\n| `base class` | Classes with invariants that subclasses must inherit, never re-implement |\n| `sealed class` | Closed hierarchies matched with `switch` |\n| `mixin class` | Rare; only when a type must be both a mixin and a class |\n\n- Extension types are compile-time only. They are not a validation or security boundary: `id as String`\n  still works. Validate in a factory if the format matters.\n\n### 4. Every stream has one owner who closes it\n\n```dart\n// BAD: single-subscription stream exposed to many listeners -> \"Stream has already been listened to\"\nfinal _controller = StreamController<ScanResult>();\n\n// GOOD: broadcast for fan-out, closed by the owner\nfinal _results = StreamController<ScanResult>.broadcast();\nStream<ScanResult> get results => _results.stream;\nFuture<void> dispose() => _results.close();\n```\n\n- Single-subscription: one listener, buffers until listened (file reads, HTTP bodies, `async*`).\n- Broadcast: many listeners, drops events nobody hears (connection state, scan results).\n- Prefer `async*` generators. Cancelling the subscription stops the generator at the next `yield`.\n- Fire-and-forget futures are explicit: `unawaited(analytics.log(event));`.\n\n### 5. CPU-heavy work leaves the UI isolate\n\n```dart\n// BAD: 5 MB JSON decoded on the UI isolate, frames drop\nfinal data = jsonDecode(body);\n\n// GOOD\nfinal data = await Isolate.run(() => jsonDecode(body) as Map<String, Object?>);\n```\n\n- The closure must not capture `BuildContext`, widgets or large objects; everything captured is copied.\n- `Isolate.run` is not available on web. Use `compute` in code that must run on web too.\n- `async` alone does not move work off the UI thread. Only isolates do.\n\n### 6. Widgets are small const classes, not helper methods\n\n```dart\n// BAD: rebuilt with the parent every time, no const, no own Element\nWidget _buildHeader() => Padding(padding: const EdgeInsets.all(16), child: Text(title));\n\n// GOOD\nclass DeviceHeader extends StatelessWidget {\n  const DeviceHeader({super.key, required this.title});\n  final String title;\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) =>\n      Padding(padding: const EdgeInsets.all(16), child: Text(title));\n}\n```\n\n- `build` is pure and cheap: no futures, no controllers, no I/O.\n- Create futures once (`late final _user = repo.fetchUser();`), never inside `FutureBuilder(future: ...)` in `build`.\n- Read only what you need: `MediaQuery.sizeOf(context)` instead of `MediaQuery.of(context).size`.\n\n### 7. Keys only where identity matters\n\n```dart\n// BAD: stateful rows lose or swap state on reorder and delete\nListView.builder(itemBuilder: (context, i) => DeviceTile(device: devices[i]));\n\n// GOOD\nListView.builder(\n  itemCount: devices.length,\n  itemBuilder: (context, i) => DeviceTile(key: ValueKey(devices[i].id), device: devices[i]),\n);\n```\n\n- `ValueKey(id)` for list items that can reorder, insert or delete. Never `ValueKey(index)`.\n- `GlobalKey` only for real cross-tree access (`Form` validation, a `Navigator` key for go_router).\n  It is expensive and a widget with a `GlobalKey` must not appear twice in the tree.\n- Never create keys in `build` (`GlobalKey()` or `UniqueKey()`); the state resets on every rebuild.\n\n### 8. Check `mounted` after every `await` before using context or state\n\n```dart\n// BAD: the widget may be gone; wrong navigator or a crash\nonPressed: () async {\n  await repo.save(form);\n  context.pop();\n},\n\n// GOOD\nonPressed: () async {\n  await repo.save(form);\n  if (!context.mounted) return;\n  context.pop();\n},\n```\n\n- Inside a `State`, use `if (!mounted) return;` before `setState` or `context`.\n- In a Riverpod notifier, use `if (!ref.mounted) return;`. In a Cubit, `if (isClosed) return;`.\n- Keep `use_build_context_synchronously` enabled; it is in `flutter_lints`.\n\n### 9. Whatever a State creates, it disposes\n\n```dart\n// BAD: created in build, never disposed, text lost on rebuild\nWidget build(BuildContext context) => TextField(controller: TextEditingController());\n\n// GOOD\nfinal _controller = TextEditingController();\nlate final StreamSubscription<int> _sub;\nTimer? _poll;\n\n@override\nvoid initState() {\n  super.initState();\n  _sub = widget.battery.listen(_onLevel);\n}\n\n@override\nvoid dispose() {\n  _poll?.cancel();\n  _sub.cancel();\n  _controller.dispose();\n  super.dispose();\n}\n```\n\n- Covers `TextEditingController`, `ScrollController`, `PageController`, `FocusNode`, `AnimationController`,\n  `StreamSubscription`, `Timer`, `ChangeNotifier`s the State created.\n- Do not dispose objects passed in from outside; their owner does.\n- Avoid `late final _sub = stream.listen(...)`: it subscribes lazily, possibly for the first time in `dispose`.\n\n### 10. One state management approach per app, used the same way everywhere\n\nRiverpod:\n```dart\n// BAD: read in build never rebuilds; watch in a callback rebuilds for nothing\nfinal todos = ref.read(todosProvider);\nonPressed: () => ref.watch(todosProvider.notifier).add(todo),\n\n// GOOD\nfinal todos = ref.watch(todosProvider);                     // build: rebuild on change\nonPressed: () => ref.read(todosProvider.notifier).add(todo), // callbacks: one-off read\nref.listen(todosProvider, (prev, next) { /* snackbar, navigation */ });\n```\n\nBloc:\n```dart\n// BAD: side effect inside builder, runs on every rebuild\nBlocBuilder<LoginBloc, LoginState>(builder: (context, state) {\n  if (state is LoginSuccess) context.go('/home');\n  return const LoginForm();\n})\n\n// GOOD\nBlocListener<LoginBloc, LoginState>(\n  listenWhen: (previous, current) => current is LoginSuccess,\n  listener: (context, state) => context.go('/home'),\n  child: const LoginForm(),\n)\n```\n\n- Riverpod: screen state uses `autoDispose` (default with `@riverpod` codegen). Narrow rebuilds with\n  `ref.watch(provider.select((s) => s.count))`.\n- Bloc: `Cubit` for simple method-driven state, `Bloc` when events need transformers (debounce, restartable).\n- No business logic, API calls or repositories in widgets.\n\n### 11. Routing is declarative, auth lives in one redirect, deep link input is untrusted\n\n```dart\n// BAD: auth check copied into every screen, the protected screen flashes before it runs\n@override\nvoid initState() {\n  super.initState();\n  if (!auth.isLoggedIn) WidgetsBinding.instance.addPostFrameCallback((_) => context.go('/login'));\n}\n\n// GOOD: one redirect, re-evaluated whenever auth notifies\nGoRouter(\n  refreshListenable: auth,\n  redirect: (context, state) {\n    final atLogin = state.matchedLocation == '/login';\n    if (!auth.isLoggedIn) return atLogin ? null : '/login';\n    if (atLogin) return '/';\n    return null;\n  },\n  routes: [...],\n);\n```\n\n- `redirect` returns `null` for \"stay\". Always guard the target route to avoid redirect loops.\n- Path and query parameters arrive from outside the app via deep links. Parse and validate them; a\n  malformed ID shows an error screen, never a crash or a privileged action.\n- App Links (Android `assetlinks.json`, `autoVerify`) and Universal Links (Associated Domains\n  entitlement) are platform config. Call out every manifest, `Info.plist` and entitlement change.\n- Typed routes with `go_router_builder` are optional; adopt them app-wide or not at all.\n\n### 12. Build config comes from flavors and dart-defines, secrets do not\n\n```dart\n// BAD: shipped in the binary, readable with `strings`, obfuscation does not hide it\nconst apiKey = String.fromEnvironment('PAYMENT_SECRET_KEY');\n\n// GOOD: only non-secret config at build time\nconst apiBaseUrl = String.fromEnvironment('API_BASE_URL');\n// flutter run --flavor staging --dart-define-from-file=config/staging.json\n```\n\n- `--flavor` selects the Android product flavor and the Xcode scheme; both must exist natively.\n- `String.fromEnvironment` only works in a `const` context. Fail fast if a required value is empty.\n- Secrets that must stay secret live on a server; the app gets short-lived tokens at runtime and stores\n  them in the Keychain or Android Keystore backed storage.\n- Release builds: `--obfuscate --split-debug-info=<dir>`; keep the symbol files to symbolize crashes.\n\n### 13. Platform channels are generated with Pigeon\n\n```dart\n// BAD: stringly typed, untyped Map, typos fail at runtime\nfinal level = await const MethodChannel('device').invokeMethod('getBatery');\n\n// GOOD: pigeons/device_api.dart, generated Dart, Kotlin and Swift stay in sync\n@HostApi()\nabstract class DeviceApi {\n  DeviceInfo getDeviceInfo();\n  @async\n  bool requestPairing(String deviceId);\n}\n```\n\n- Native handlers run on the platform main thread by default. Move heavy work off it.\n- Native code validates every argument from Dart like any other external input.\n\n### 14. Lints are strict and warnings are not ignored\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: default template, never touched, casts from dynamic unchecked\ninclude: package:flutter_lints/flutter.yaml\n\n# GOOD\ninclude: package:flutter_lints/flutter.yaml\nanalyzer:\n  language:\n    strict-casts: true\n    strict-inference: true\n    strict-raw-types: true\nlinter:\n  rules:\n    - prefer_const_constructors\n    - prefer_final_locals\n    - unawaited_futures\n    - discarded_futures\n    - cancel_subscriptions\n    - close_sinks\n    - avoid_dynamic_calls\n```\n\n- `very_good_analysis` is a reasonable stricter alternative. Pick one set, do not mix.\n- `// ignore:` needs a reason comment next to it.\n- Use `package:logging` or a logger behind an interface, not `print`. Never log tokens or device IDs.\n\n### 15. Tests are deterministic and at the lowest useful level\n\n```dart\n// BAD: real time, slow and flaky\nawait Future<void>.delayed(const Duration(seconds: 2));\nexpect(cubit.state, 2);\n\n// GOOD: virtual time\nfakeAsync((async) {\n  final values = <int>[];\n  countdown(2).listen(values.add);\n  async.elapse(const Duration(seconds: 3));\n  expect(values, [2, 1, 0]);\n});\n```\n\n- Unit tests for notifiers, blocs and repositories; widget tests for screens; few `integration_test` flows.\n- `pumpAndSettle` times out on endless animations (a looping progress indicator). Use `pump(duration)`.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- `setState` at the top of a screen for a value one small widget shows. Push state down or use a listenable.\n- `Widget _buildX()` helper methods in place of widget classes.\n- `!` on values from JSON, platform channels or route parameters.\n- `catch (e) {}` or catching `Error` (`StateError`, `TypeError`) to keep going; those are bugs.\n- `late` fields that may never be initialized on some path.\n- `dynamic` and `Map<String, dynamic>` leaking past the data layer. Map to typed models at the edge.\n- A provider or bloc that holds a `BuildContext`, a widget or a controller.\n- Mixing Riverpod, Bloc, Provider and GetX in one app.\n- `Opacity` animated every frame; use `FadeTransition` or `AnimatedOpacity`.\n- `RepaintBoundary` sprinkled everywhere \"for performance\" without a profile showing it helps.\n- Secrets, API keys or signing material in `--dart-define`, `.env` assets or source.\n- Golden tests run on every developer machine; font rendering differs across platforms.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Matches the app's existing state management, router and codegen choices\n- [ ] Closed state modelled as sealed classes; switches exhaustive without `_`\n- [ ] Untrusted JSON and route parameters parsed with patterns or validated, no blind `as` or `!`\n- [ ] Every `StreamController` closed; broadcast only where there are many listeners\n- [ ] CPU-heavy work in `Isolate.run` or `compute`\n- [ ] Widgets are classes with `const` constructors; `build` has no side effects or futures\n- [ ] `ValueKey(id)` on reorderable list items; no keys created in `build`\n- [ ] `mounted` / `context.mounted` / `ref.mounted` / `isClosed` checked after each `await`\n- [ ] Controllers, focus nodes, subscriptions and timers disposed or cancelled\n- [ ] `ref.watch` in build, `ref.read` in callbacks, side effects in `ref.listen` or `BlocListener`\n- [ ] Auth redirect central, guarded against loops; deep link parameters validated\n- [ ] No secrets in dart-defines or assets; manifest, plist and entitlement changes called out\n- [ ] Platform channels generated with Pigeon; native work off the main thread\n- [ ] `flutter analyze` clean with strict lints; `dart format` applied\n- [ ] New logic covered by deterministic unit or widget tests; `flutter test` passes\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-code-review` -- review a Flutter MR against these rules\n- `mobile-security` -- secure storage, certificate pinning, deep link and logging audits\n- `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi` -- architecture behind Riverpod notifiers and Blocs\n- `mobile-design-patterns` -- repository, adapter and strategy patterns in Dart\n- `mobile-kotlin`, `mobile-swift` -- native side of Pigeon channels and plugins\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- flavors, signing, obfuscation symbols and golden tests in CI\n- `mobile-ble` -- Bluetooth Low Energy in Flutter and on the native side\n- `mobile-testing` -- cross-platform test strategy and goldens\n- `mobile-performance` -- DevTools profiling, raster and UI thread jank\n",
    "references/performance.md":
        "# Performance reference\n\nLoad this when fixing jank, slow startup, memory growth or app size, or when writing lists, images and\nanimations. Measure first; most \"optimizations\" without a profile add complexity and change nothing.\n\n## Measure\n\n1. Run on a real, preferably low-end, device in profile mode: `flutter run --profile`.\n   Debug mode is many times slower and its numbers are meaningless.\n2. Open DevTools:\n   - Performance view: frame chart, UI vs raster thread time, \"Track widget builds\".\n   - CPU profiler: hot Dart functions on the UI isolate.\n   - Memory view: heap snapshots and diffs to find leaked State objects and controllers.\n3. Find which thread is slow.\n\n| Slow thread | Usual cause | Fix direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| UI (Dart) | Too many rebuilds, heavy `build`, sync parsing | Narrow rebuilds, `const`, isolates |\n| Raster | `saveLayer` (opacity, clips, shader masks), huge images, blur | Cheaper effects, decode at size |\n\n4. Fix one thing, re-measure, keep the change only if the numbers improved.\n\n## Rebuild scope\n\n```dart\n// BAD: the whole screen rebuilds every second for one label\nsetState(() => _elapsed++);\n\n// GOOD: only the label listens\nValueListenableBuilder<int>(\n  valueListenable: _elapsed,\n  builder: (context, value, _) => Text('\$value s'),\n)\n```\n\n- `const` widgets are skipped on rebuild. Enable `prefer_const_constructors`.\n- Pass a `child` to `AnimatedBuilder`, `ValueListenableBuilder` and `Consumer` for the part that does\n  not change.\n- Select narrowly: `ref.watch(p.select((s) => s.count))`, `context.select((C c) => c.state.count)`.\n- `MediaQuery.sizeOf(context)`, `MediaQuery.paddingOf(context)` instead of `MediaQuery.of(context)`,\n  which rebuilds on any media change (keyboard, text scale, insets).\n\n## Lists\n\n```dart\n// BAD: builds all 5 000 rows up front\nListView(children: [for (final d in devices) DeviceTile(device: d)]);\n\n// GOOD: builds only visible rows\nListView.builder(\n  itemCount: devices.length,\n  itemExtent: 72, // or prototypeItem, when rows have a fixed height\n  itemBuilder: (context, i) => DeviceTile(key: ValueKey(devices[i].id), device: devices[i]),\n);\n```\n\n- `SliverList.builder` inside `CustomScrollView` for mixed scrolling content.\n- Never `shrinkWrap: true` on a long list inside another scrollable; it lays out every child.\n- Paginate network data; do not hold 10 000 models in memory to show 20.\n\n## Images\n\n```dart\n// BAD: 4000x3000 photo decoded at full size for a 48 px avatar (about 48 MB of pixels)\nImage.network(url, width: 48, height: 48);\n\n// GOOD: decoded at display size in physical pixels\nfinal dpr = MediaQuery.devicePixelRatioOf(context);\nImage.network(url, width: 48, height: 48, cacheWidth: (48 * dpr).round());\n```\n\n- `ResizeImage` does the same for any `ImageProvider`.\n- Precache hero or first-screen images with `precacheImage` when the screen is about to appear.\n- Prefer vector assets or correctly sized raster variants (`2.0x`, `3.0x`) over one huge PNG.\n\n## Animations and effects\n\n```dart\n// BAD: Opacity rebuilt every frame, saveLayer each frame\nAnimatedBuilder(\n  animation: _controller,\n  builder: (context, _) => Opacity(opacity: _controller.value, child: const Card()),\n);\n\n// GOOD: opacity handled in the render layer, child not rebuilt\nFadeTransition(opacity: _controller, child: const Card());\n```\n\n- Implicit animations (`AnimatedOpacity`, `AnimatedContainer`) for simple state changes.\n- Expensive: `Opacity` on large subtrees, `ShaderMask`, `ColorFilter`, `BackdropFilter`,\n  `Clip.antiAliasWithSaveLayer`. Use them on small areas.\n- For a translucent image, set the colour or opacity on the image or decoration instead of wrapping it.\n- `RepaintBoundary` isolates a frequently repainting widget (a progress ring, a live chart) from static\n  neighbours. Add it only when DevTools shows repaints spreading; it costs memory for an extra layer.\n\n## Work off the UI isolate\n\n- Parse large JSON, decode files, compute hashes and run crypto in `Isolate.run`.\n- Long-lived background processing (a stream of BLE packets to decode) can use a long-lived isolate with\n  `Isolate.spawn` and ports. Most apps never need it.\n- Avoid synchronous file I/O (`readAsStringSync`) on the UI isolate.\n\n## Startup\n\n- Keep `main()` small: initialize only what the first frame needs, defer the rest.\n- Do not `await` analytics, remote config or non-critical SDK init before `runApp`.\n- Measure with `flutter run --profile --trace-startup` and compare against the previous release.\n\n## Memory and leaks\n\n- Dispose controllers, cancel subscriptions and timers (see the main skill, rule 9).\n- `autoDispose` providers and `BlocProvider(create:)` scope state to the screen that needs it.\n- Image cache size is limited by `PaintingBinding.instance.imageCache`; raising it hides problems.\n- Use DevTools memory diffs: navigate into and out of a screen several times; a growing count of its\n  `State` class is a leak.\n\n## App size\n\n- `flutter build appbundle --analyze-size --target-platform android-arm64` (or `ipa`) and inspect in DevTools.\n- Remove unused packages and fonts; subset icon fonts (tree shaking of icons is on by default in release).\n- `--split-debug-info` moves symbols out of the binary; keep them for crash symbolication.\n\n## Rendering engine\n\n- Impeller is the default renderer on iOS and on modern Android devices; shader compilation jank warm-up\n  steps written for the old Skia backend are not needed there. Check the Flutter release notes before\n  copying older advice.\n",
    "references/platform-channels.md":
        "# Platform channels reference\n\nLoad this when Dart needs to call Kotlin or Swift, or native code needs to call or stream into Dart.\nUse `pigeon` (dev dependency) to generate type-safe channels. Hand-written `MethodChannel` code is only\nacceptable for a single trivial call in a legacy plugin.\n\n## Why Pigeon\n\n| Hand-written `MethodChannel` | Pigeon |\n|---|---|\n| Method names and argument keys are strings, typos fail at runtime | Generated, compile-time checked on all sides |\n| Arguments are `Map<Object?, Object?>`, casts everywhere | Typed data classes |\n| Dart and native drift apart silently | One definition file, regenerate both sides |\n\n## Definition file\n\n```dart\n// pigeons/device_api.dart -- not part of the app build, only input to the generator\nimport 'package:pigeon/pigeon.dart';\n\n@ConfigurePigeon(PigeonOptions(\n  dartOut: 'lib/src/platform/device_api.g.dart',\n  kotlinOut: 'android/app/src/main/kotlin/com/example/app/DeviceApi.g.kt',\n  kotlinOptions: KotlinOptions(package: 'com.example.app'),\n  swiftOut: 'ios/Runner/DeviceApi.g.swift',\n))\nclass DeviceInfo {\n  DeviceInfo({required this.model, required this.batteryLevel});\n  String model;\n  int batteryLevel;\n}\n\n@HostApi() // Dart calls native\nabstract class DeviceApi {\n  DeviceInfo getDeviceInfo();\n\n  @async\n  bool requestPairing(String deviceId);\n\n  @TaskQueue(type: TaskQueueType.serialBackgroundThread)\n  String hashFile(String path);\n}\n\n@FlutterApi() // native calls Dart\nabstract class DeviceEvents {\n  void onConnectionChanged(bool connected);\n}\n```\n\nGenerate, then commit the generated files so builds do not need the generator:\n\n```bash\ndart run pigeon --input pigeons/device_api.dart\n```\n\n- Dart `int` is a 64-bit integer: `Long` in Kotlin, `Int64` in Swift.\n- Change the definition, never the `.g.` files. Regenerate on every Pigeon version bump.\n\n## Android (Kotlin)\n\n```kotlin\nclass DeviceApiImpl(private val context: Context) : DeviceApi {\n    override fun getDeviceInfo(): DeviceInfo =\n        DeviceInfo(model = Build.MODEL, batteryLevel = readBatteryLevel(context).toLong())\n\n    // Generated handlers launch this on the main dispatcher: switch for blocking work\n    override suspend fun requestPairing(deviceId: String): Boolean =\n        withContext(Dispatchers.IO) { pairingManager.pair(deviceId) }\n\n    // Runs on the background task queue requested with @TaskQueue\n    override fun hashFile(path: String): String = sha256Of(File(path))\n}\n\nclass MainActivity : FlutterActivity() {\n    override fun configureFlutterEngine(flutterEngine: FlutterEngine) {\n        super.configureFlutterEngine(flutterEngine)\n        DeviceApi.setUp(flutterEngine.dartExecutor.binaryMessenger, DeviceApiImpl(applicationContext))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Pass `applicationContext`, never the `Activity`, to long-lived implementations.\n- Recent Pigeon versions generate `suspend fun` for `@async` methods; older versions generate a\n  `callback: (Result<T>) -> Unit` parameter. Follow the generated signature.\n\n## iOS (Swift)\n\n```swift\nfinal class DeviceApiImpl: DeviceApi {\n    func getDeviceInfo() throws -> DeviceInfo {\n        DeviceInfo(model: UIDevice.current.model, batteryLevel: Int64(currentBatteryLevel()))\n    }\n\n    // Generated code calls this from a main-actor Task: keep heavy work off the main actor\n    func requestPairing(deviceId: String) async throws -> Bool {\n        try await pairingService.pair(deviceId)\n    }\n\n    func hashFile(path: String) throws -> String { try sha256(of: URL(fileURLWithPath: path)) }\n}\n\n// AppDelegate, after GeneratedPluginRegistrant.register(with: self)\nlet controller = window?.rootViewController as! FlutterViewController\nDeviceApiSetup.setUp(binaryMessenger: controller.binaryMessenger, api: DeviceApiImpl())\n```\n\n- Throw `PigeonError(code:message:details:)` (Swift) or `FlutterError` (Kotlin) for expected failures;\n  Dart receives a `PlatformException` with that code.\n- Apps using the scene-based lifecycle register in the plugin or engine setup hook their template\n  provides; follow the generated project, not an old snippet.\n\n## Dart side\n\n```dart\n// BAD: generated API called straight from widgets, PlatformException leaks into UI\nfinal info = await DeviceApi().getDeviceInfo();\n\n// GOOD: wrapped in a repository that maps errors to domain types\nfinal class PlatformDeviceRepository implements DeviceRepository {\n  PlatformDeviceRepository(this._api);\n  final DeviceApi _api;\n\n  @override\n  Future<bool> pair(DeviceId id) async {\n    try {\n      return await _api.requestPairing(id.value);\n    } on PlatformException catch (e) {\n      throw PairingException(e.code);\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- The generated class is a detail of the data layer. Tests replace the repository, not the channel.\n- Native-to-Dart calls (`@FlutterApi`) are registered in Dart with the generated `setUp` and must be\n  invoked from the platform main thread on the native side.\n\n## Streams from native\n\n- For continuous data (BLE notifications, sensor readings, download progress) use `@EventChannelApi()`\n  in the definition file. Pigeon generates a Dart `Stream` function and a native stream handler class.\n- Cancel the Dart subscription when the screen or provider goes away; native `onCancel` must stop the\n  underlying work (scan, sensor listener) or it keeps draining the battery.\n- Throttle on the native side when the source emits faster than the UI can use (more than 60 per second).\n\n## Threading rules\n\n| Side | Default thread | Move off it with |\n|---|---|---|\n| Kotlin handler | Android main thread | `@TaskQueue` or `withContext(Dispatchers.IO)` |\n| Swift handler | Main thread / main actor | `@TaskQueue`, or async work on another actor |\n| Native -> Dart call | Must start on the platform main thread | Post to main before calling |\n| Dart handler | UI isolate | `Isolate.run` for heavy processing of the result |\n\nBLE callbacks arrive on binder threads (Android) or the central manager queue (iOS). Hop to the main\nthread before calling a `@FlutterApi` or emitting on an event sink.\n\n## Security\n\n- Native code treats every argument from Dart as untrusted input: validate paths, IDs, lengths.\n- Do not expose a generic \"run this command\" or \"read this file path\" API to Dart.\n- Do not send secrets, tokens or raw device identifiers through channels when an opaque handle works.\n- New permissions, `AndroidManifest.xml` entries, `Info.plist` usage strings or entitlements needed by\n  native code are listed in the MR.\n",
    "references/state-management.md":
        "# State management reference\n\nLoad this when writing or changing providers, notifiers, blocs or cubits. Examples target Riverpod 3\nand flutter_bloc 9. Pick one approach per app and follow what `pubspec.yaml` already uses.\n\n## Choosing\n\n| Need | Riverpod | Bloc |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dependency injection built in | Yes, providers are the DI graph | No, pair with `RepositoryProvider` or get_it |\n| Async loading, error, data | `AsyncNotifier` and `AsyncValue` | Your own sealed state classes |\n| Event debounce, cancel previous | Manual | `transformer:` with `bloc_concurrency` |\n| Strict event log and replay | No | Yes, every change starts from an event |\n| Boilerplate | Low, lower with codegen | Medium |\n\nBoth work. Switching mid-project costs more than either choice.\n\n## Riverpod\n\n### Provider types\n\n| Type | Use for |\n|---|---|\n| `Provider` | Dependencies: repositories, API clients, config |\n| `NotifierProvider` | Synchronous state with methods |\n| `AsyncNotifierProvider` | State loaded asynchronously, with methods to change it |\n| `FutureProvider` / `StreamProvider` | Read-only async values without methods |\n\n### AsyncNotifier\n\n```dart\nfinal todosProvider =\n    AsyncNotifierProvider.autoDispose<TodosNotifier, List<Todo>>(TodosNotifier.new);\n\nclass TodosNotifier extends AsyncNotifier<List<Todo>> {\n  @override\n  Future<List<Todo>> build() => ref.watch(todoRepositoryProvider).fetch();\n\n  Future<void> add(Todo todo) async {\n    final repo = ref.read(todoRepositoryProvider);\n    state = const AsyncLoading();\n    final result = await AsyncValue.guard(() async {\n      await repo.add(todo);\n      return repo.fetch();\n    });\n    if (!ref.mounted) return; // provider disposed while awaiting\n    state = result;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- `build` uses `ref.watch` for dependencies so the notifier rebuilds when they change.\n- Methods use `ref.read`. Never `ref.watch` inside a method.\n- `AsyncValue.guard` turns exceptions into `AsyncError` instead of try/catch boilerplate.\n\n### Consuming\n\n```dart\nclass TodosScreen extends ConsumerWidget {\n  const TodosScreen({super.key});\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context, WidgetRef ref) {\n    ref.listen(todosProvider, (previous, next) {\n      if (next case AsyncError(:final error)) {\n        ScaffoldMessenger.of(context).showSnackBar(SnackBar(content: Text('\$error')));\n      }\n    });\n\n    return switch (ref.watch(todosProvider)) {\n      AsyncData(:final value) => TodoList(todos: value),\n      AsyncError(:final error) => ErrorView(error: error),\n      _ => const Center(child: CircularProgressIndicator()),\n    };\n  }\n}\n```\n\n| Call | Where | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ref.watch(p)` | `build` of widgets and providers | Rebuilds when `p` changes |\n| `ref.watch(p.select((s) => s.x))` | `build` | Rebuilds only when `x` changes |\n| `ref.read(p)` / `ref.read(p.notifier)` | Callbacks, notifier methods | One-off read, no subscription |\n| `ref.listen(p, cb)` | `build` or notifier `build` | Side effects: snackbar, navigation, analytics |\n\n- `ConsumerStatefulWidget` when the widget also owns controllers; `ref` is available on the State.\n- Use the `_ =>` branch for `AsyncValue` loading only. Data and error are matched explicitly.\n\n### Lifetime\n\n```dart\n// BAD: screen state kept forever, stale data on the next visit\nfinal searchProvider = NotifierProvider<SearchNotifier, SearchState>(SearchNotifier.new);\n\n// GOOD: disposed when the last listener goes away\nfinal searchProvider =\n    NotifierProvider.autoDispose<SearchNotifier, SearchState>(SearchNotifier.new);\n```\n\n- Generated providers (`@riverpod`) are auto-dispose by default; `@Riverpod(keepAlive: true)` opts out.\n- Keep a provider alive conditionally with `final link = ref.keepAlive();` and `link.close()` later.\n- Clean up in `ref.onDispose(...)`: close sockets, cancel timers, stop BLE scans.\n\n### Parameters (family)\n\n```dart\nfinal deviceProvider = AsyncNotifierProvider.autoDispose\n    .family<DeviceNotifier, Device, DeviceId>(DeviceNotifier.new);\n\nclass DeviceNotifier extends AsyncNotifier<Device> {\n  DeviceNotifier(this.id);\n  final DeviceId id;\n\n  @override\n  Future<Device> build() => ref.watch(deviceRepositoryProvider).byId(id);\n}\n\n// usage\nref.watch(deviceProvider(DeviceId('A1')));\n```\n\n- The parameter needs value equality (`==` and `hashCode`). Records, extension types over `String`\n  and `int`, and `final class` with `==` work. A new `List` each build creates a new provider each build.\n\n## Bloc\n\n### Cubit or Bloc\n\n```dart\n// Cubit: methods emit states directly\nclass CounterCubit extends Cubit<int> {\n  CounterCubit() : super(0);\n  void increment() => emit(state + 1);\n}\n\n// Bloc: events in, states out, with concurrency control\nclass SearchBloc extends Bloc<SearchEvent, SearchState> {\n  SearchBloc(this._api) : super(SearchIdle()) {\n    on<QueryChanged>(_onQueryChanged, transformer: restartable());\n  }\n  final SearchApi _api;\n\n  Future<void> _onQueryChanged(QueryChanged event, Emitter<SearchState> emit) async {\n    emit(SearchLoading());\n    try {\n      emit(SearchResults(await _api.search(event.query)));\n    } on Exception {\n      emit(SearchFailure());\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- `bloc_concurrency` transformers: `restartable()` (search as you type), `droppable()` (ignore taps while\n  busy), `sequential()` (queue writes). Default is concurrent.\n- Streams inside a handler: `await emit.forEach(stream, onData: (v) => State(v));`. It cancels when the\n  bloc closes. Do not `listen` manually in the constructor.\n- Cubit methods that await check `if (isClosed) return;` before `emit`. Emitting after close throws.\n\n### State equality\n\n```dart\n// BAD: list mutated in place, new state == old state, nothing rebuilds\nstate.items.add(item);\nemit(state);\n\n// GOOD: new immutable state\nemit(state.copyWith(items: [...state.items, item]));\n```\n\n- Bloc skips a state equal (`==`) to the current one. Give states value equality (`equatable`,\n  `freezed` or hand-written `==`) and never mutate them.\n\n### Widgets\n\n| Widget | Use for |\n|---|---|\n| `BlocProvider(create: ...)` | Creates and closes the bloc with the subtree |\n| `BlocProvider.value(value: ...)` | Passes an existing bloc; does not close it |\n| `BlocBuilder` | Builds UI from state; pure, no side effects |\n| `BlocSelector` / `context.select` | Rebuilds only when a derived value changes |\n| `BlocListener` | Side effects: navigation, snackbars, dialogs |\n| `BlocConsumer` | Builder and listener on the same bloc |\n\n```dart\n// BAD: bloc created with .value inside build, never closed\nBlocProvider.value(value: SearchBloc(api), child: const SearchView());\n\n// GOOD\nBlocProvider(create: (_) => SearchBloc(api), child: const SearchView());\n```\n\n- `context.read<T>()` in callbacks, `context.watch<T>()` or `context.select` in `build`. Same rule as Riverpod.\n- Pass `listenWhen` and `buildWhen` to cut noise instead of filtering inside the callback.\n\n## Both\n\n- State classes are immutable. Use `final` fields and `copyWith`.\n- Notifiers and blocs never hold `BuildContext`, widgets, controllers or `GlobalKey`s.\n- Navigation is a side effect: trigger it from `ref.listen` or `BlocListener`, not from the notifier.\n- Repositories are injected so tests can replace them. See [testing](testing.md).\n",
    "references/testing.md":
        "# Testing reference\n\nLoad this when adding or fixing Dart or Flutter tests. Packages: `flutter_test` (bundled), `test`,\n`mocktail`, `fake_async`, `bloc_test` for Bloc apps, `integration_test` (bundled with the SDK).\n\n## Pyramid\n\n| Level | Tool | Tests | Speed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Unit | `package:test` or `flutter_test` | Repositories, mappers, notifiers, blocs, pure Dart | ms |\n| Widget | `testWidgets` + `WidgetTester` | One screen or widget with faked dependencies | ms to s |\n| Golden | `matchesGoldenFile` | Visual regressions of design-system widgets | s |\n| Integration | `integration_test` on device or emulator | A few critical user flows end to end | min |\n\nMost tests are unit and widget tests. Integration tests cover login, pairing, purchase: flows where a\nbreak costs real money or support tickets.\n\n## Fakes and mocks\n\n```dart\nclass MockTodoRepository extends Mock implements TodoRepository {}\nclass FakeTodo extends Fake implements Todo {}\n\nsetUpAll(() => registerFallbackValue(FakeTodo())); // needed for any() on custom types\n\nlate MockTodoRepository repo;\nsetUp(() => repo = MockTodoRepository());\n\ntest('loads todos once', () async {\n  when(() => repo.fetch()).thenAnswer((_) async => [Todo('1', 'Pair device')]);\n  // ...\n  verify(() => repo.fetch()).called(1);\n  verifyNever(() => repo.add(any()));\n});\n```\n\n- Stub futures with `thenAnswer((_) async => ...)`, never `thenReturn(Future...)`.\n- Prefer a hand-written fake (an in-memory repository) when many tests need the same behaviour. Mocks\n  are for verifying interactions.\n- Mock your own interfaces, not third-party classes such as `http.Client` or `Dio` directly. Wrap them.\n\n## Time\n\n```dart\n// BAD: real delay\nawait Future<void>.delayed(const Duration(seconds: 30));\n\n// GOOD: virtual time with package:fake_async\ntest('retries after backoff', () {\n  fakeAsync((async) {\n    final values = <int>[];\n    countdown(2).listen(values.add);\n    async.elapse(const Duration(seconds: 3));\n    expect(values, [2, 1, 0]);\n  });\n});\n```\n\n- Inside `testWidgets`, time is already fake: advance it with `tester.pump(duration)`.\n- Inject a clock (`DateTime Function() now`) instead of calling `DateTime.now()` in logic.\n\n## Riverpod\n\n```dart\ntest('notifier loads todos', () async {\n  when(() => repo.fetch()).thenAnswer((_) async => [Todo('1', 'Pair device')]);\n  final container = ProviderContainer.test(\n    overrides: [todoRepositoryProvider.overrideWithValue(repo)],\n  );\n  final sub = container.listen(todosProvider, (_, _) {}); // keeps autoDispose providers alive\n  await expectLater(container.read(todosProvider.future), completion(hasLength(1)));\n  sub.close();\n});\n```\n\n- `ProviderContainer.test` disposes the container automatically at the end of the test.\n- Widget tests wrap the tree in `ProviderScope(overrides: [...], child: ...)`.\n\n## Bloc\n\n```dart\nblocTest<SearchBloc, SearchState>(\n  'emits loading then results',\n  setUp: () => when(() => api.search('ble')).thenAnswer((_) async => ['Sensor A']),\n  build: () => SearchBloc(api),\n  act: (bloc) => bloc.add(QueryChanged('ble')),\n  expect: () => [isA<SearchLoading>(), isA<SearchResults>()],\n);\n```\n\n- With value equality on states, compare whole states: `expect: () => [SearchLoading(), SearchResults(['Sensor A'])]`.\n- Use `seed:` to start from a given state and `wait:` for debounced transformers.\n\n## Widget tests\n\n```dart\ntestWidgets('shows spinner then list', (tester) async {\n  when(() => repo.fetch()).thenAnswer((_) async => [Todo('1', 'Pair device')]);\n  await tester.pumpWidget(\n    ProviderScope(\n      overrides: [todoRepositoryProvider.overrideWithValue(repo)],\n      child: const MaterialApp(home: Scaffold(body: TodosScreen())),\n    ),\n  );\n\n  expect(find.byType(CircularProgressIndicator), findsOneWidget);\n  await tester.pump(); // let the future complete and rebuild\n  expect(find.text('Pair device'), findsOneWidget);\n});\n```\n\n| Call | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `pump()` | One frame; microtasks and completed futures are processed |\n| `pump(duration)` | Advances fake time, then one frame |\n| `pumpAndSettle()` | Pumps until no frames are scheduled; fails with a timeout on endless animations |\n| `runAsync(() => ...)` | Real async I/O (rare; files, real isolates) |\n\n- Material widgets need `MaterialApp` and usually `Scaffold` (or `Material`) above them.\n- Find by semantics a user sees (`find.text`, `find.bySemanticsLabel`, `find.byTooltip`) before `find.byKey`.\n- `tester.enterText`, `tester.tap`, then `pump`; nothing rebuilds until a frame is pumped.\n\n## Golden tests\n\n```dart\ntestWidgets('device card', (tester) async {\n  await tester.pumpWidget(const MaterialApp(home: Scaffold(body: DeviceCard(device: sampleDevice))));\n  await expectLater(find.byType(DeviceCard), matchesGoldenFile('goldens/device_card.png'));\n}, tags: 'golden');\n```\n\n- Update with `flutter test --update-goldens`; review the PNG diff in the MR.\n- Generate and compare on one pinned platform (usually the CI Linux image). Font rendering differs on\n  macOS and Windows. Skip or tag goldens locally: `flutter test --exclude-tags golden`.\n- Load real fonts in goldens, or text renders as boxes and hides layout bugs.\n\n## Integration tests\n\n```dart\n// integration_test/pairing_test.dart\nvoid main() {\n  IntegrationTestWidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();\n\n  testWidgets('user pairs a device', (tester) async {\n    app.main();\n    await tester.pumpAndSettle();\n    await tester.tap(find.text('Add device'));\n    // ...\n  });\n}\n```\n\n- Run: `flutter test integration_test --flavor staging`.\n- Point at a test backend through `--dart-define-from-file`. Never at production with real user data.\n- Native permission dialogs are outside Flutter; pre-grant permissions in the test setup or use a\n  dedicated driver tool.\n\n## CI commands\n\n```bash\ndart format --output=none --set-exit-if-changed .\nflutter analyze\nflutter test --coverage\n```\n\n- Fail the build on analyzer warnings, not only errors.\n- Coverage is a signal, not a goal. Uncovered branches in notifiers and blocs matter more than widgets.\n",
  },
  "mobile-java": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-java\ndescription: \"Writes, reviews and modernizes Java in Android apps: Java 17 and 21 features that D8 supports, immutability, JSpecify nullability for Kotlin interop, leak-free threading without static Context or inner-class Handlers, ExecutorService, LiveData, ViewBinding, ListAdapter and DiffUtil, tests with JUnit, Mockito and Espresso, and incremental migration to Kotlin. Use when editing, reviewing or migrating .java files in an Android project.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Java on Android\n\nKeep Java Android code correct, leak-free and Kotlin-friendly while it lives, and move it to Kotlin in\nsmall safe steps when the team decides to.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Editing, fixing or reviewing `.java` files in an Android app or library.\n- Bumping the Java language level, or using a JDK API that may not exist on the app's `minSdk`.\n- Java code that Kotlin code calls (or will call) and needs nullability and interop annotations.\n- Leaks, ANRs or crashes caused by Handlers, executors, Fragments, adapters or unclosed resources.\n- Planning or executing an incremental Java-to-Kotlin migration.\n\nNot for: Kotlin-first code (use `mobile-kotlin`), architecture choices (use `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Read the module's build file: `compileOptions` source and target level, `minSdk`, and whether\n   `isCoreLibraryDesugaringEnabled` is on. These decide which features and APIs are safe.\n2. Load the reference that matches the task: [Threading](references/threading.md),\n   [Migration to Kotlin](references/migration-to-kotlin.md), [Testing](references/testing.md).\n3. If the project already builds Kotlin and the team has not said otherwise, write new classes in Kotlin.\n   Change existing Java minimally and in place.\n4. Add nullability annotations to every public signature you touch.\n5. Apply the rules below. Prefer fixing leaks and threading over stylistic rewrites.\n6. Run lint (`./gradlew lint`) for `NewApi`, threading annotation and resource-leak checks, then the tests.\n7. Keep conversion or modernization commits separate from behaviour changes.\n\n## Toolchain: what Java you can use\n\n| Feature | Java | On Android |\n|---|---|---|\n| Lambdas, method references, default and static interface methods | 8 | Desugared by D8 for every API level |\n| `var`, switch expressions, text blocks, `instanceof` patterns | 10-16 | Compile-time features, every API level |\n| Records | 16 | Supported by current AGP (D8 desugars them). Check reflection-based JSON libraries |\n| Sealed classes and interfaces | 17 | Only on recent AGP / D8. Verify before relying on them |\n| Pattern matching for `switch`, record patterns | 21 | Needs Java 21 source level and AGP / D8 support. Verify first |\n| `java.time` (API 26), `java.util.stream`, `Optional`, `java.util.function` (API 24) | library | Below those API levels only with core library desugaring |\n| Newer JDK methods (`List.of`, `List.getFirst()`, `Stream.toList()`) | library | Tied to newer API levels. Trust lint `NewApi`; desugaring covers only part |\n\nThe JDK that runs Gradle (17+ for current AGP) is separate from the language level and from the APIs\navailable at runtime. The runtime APIs come from the device's Android version.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Language level is free, library APIs are not\n\n```java\n// BAD: compiles against a recent compileSdk, crashes on older devices (added to Android in API 35)\nDevice first = devices.getFirst();\n\n// GOOD\nDevice first = devices.get(0);\n```\n\n```kotlin\n// GOOD: java.time on minSdk below 26 through core library desugaring (app/build.gradle.kts)\nandroid {\n    compileOptions {\n        isCoreLibraryDesugaringEnabled = true\n        sourceCompatibility = JavaVersion.VERSION_17\n        targetCompatibility = JavaVersion.VERSION_17\n    }\n}\ndependencies { coreLibraryDesugaring(libs.desugar.jdk.libs) }\n```\n\nNever suppress `NewApi` without a `Build.VERSION.SDK_INT` check or confirmed desugaring.\n\n### 2. Immutable by default\n\n```java\n// BAD: public mutable fields, internal list shared with callers\npublic class Device {\n    public String name;\n    public List<String> tags;\n}\n\n// GOOD: record with a defensive copy (works on every API level)\npublic record Device(String id, String name, List<String> tags) {\n    public Device {\n        Objects.requireNonNull(id);\n        tags = Collections.unmodifiableList(new ArrayList<>(tags));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- No records? `final` class, `private final` fields, no setters, copies in and unmodifiable views out.\n- `static final` constants only for truly immutable values. A `static final List` is still mutable.\n\n### 3. Nullability is declared for Kotlin callers\n\n```java\n// package-info.java: everything in the package is non-null unless marked\n@NullMarked\npackage com.example.devices;\n\nimport org.jspecify.annotations.NullMarked;\n```\n\n```java\n// BAD: Kotlin sees String! (platform type) and skips null checks, NPE at runtime\npublic String findName(String id) { return names.get(id); }\n\n// GOOD: inside @NullMarked, only nullable values are annotated\npublic @Nullable String findName(String id) { return names.get(id); }\n```\n\n- JSpecify `@Nullable` is a type-use annotation: `List<@Nullable String>`, `String @Nullable []`.\n- Packages not yet `@NullMarked` use AndroidX `@NonNull` / `@Nullable` on every public parameter and return.\n- Recent Kotlin compilers report JSpecify violations as errors, so wrong annotations break the Kotlin build.\n\n### 4. Nothing long-lived references an Activity, Fragment or View\n\n```java\n// BAD: static field keeps the Activity alive for the process lifetime\nprivate static Context context;\n\n// BAD: non-static inner class holds the outer Activity while queued or running\nprivate class Poller implements Runnable {\n    @Override public void run() { statusView.setText(read()); }\n}\n\n// GOOD: application context where a Context must be stored; work moved to a ViewModel\npublic final class StatusRepository {\n    private final Context appContext;\n    public StatusRepository(Context context) { this.appContext = context.getApplicationContext(); }\n}\n```\n\n- Anonymous classes and lambdas that use outer members capture the outer instance too.\n- `WeakReference` is a last resort for legacy callbacks, not a design.\n\n### 5. Main-thread work goes through an explicit Looper; no AsyncTask\n\n```java\n// BAD: deprecated, binds to the calling thread's Looper\nHandler handler = new Handler();\n\n// BAD: AsyncTask is deprecated (API 30); leaks the Activity and loses results on rotation\nnew LoadTask().execute();\n\n// GOOD\nprivate final Handler main = new Handler(Looper.getMainLooper());\n\n@Override protected void onDestroy() {\n    main.removeCallbacksAndMessages(null);\n    super.onDestroy();\n}\n```\n\nReplace `AsyncTask` with an `ExecutorService` in a ViewModel that publishes through `LiveData`\n(see [Threading](references/threading.md)).\n\n### 6. Every executor has an owner that shuts it down; every Future can be cancelled\n\n```java\n// BAD: new thread pool per call, never shut down\nExecutors.newFixedThreadPool(4).execute(this::sync);\n\n// GOOD: owned by the ViewModel, cancelled with it\npublic final class DevicesViewModel extends ViewModel {\n    private final ExecutorService io = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();\n    private @Nullable Future<?> loading;\n\n    public void load() {\n        if (loading != null) loading.cancel(true);\n        loading = io.submit(this::loadBlocking);\n    }\n\n    @Override protected void onCleared() { io.shutdownNow(); }\n}\n```\n\nIf the executor is injected and shared app-wide, cancel the `Future`s in `onCleared()` instead of shutting\nthe executor down.\n\n### 7. LiveData: right setter, read-only exposure, view lifecycle owner\n\n```java\n// BAD: setValue from a worker thread throws; mutable type exposed\npublic MutableLiveData<UiState> state = new MutableLiveData<>();\nio.execute(() -> state.setValue(load()));\n\n// GOOD\nprivate final MutableLiveData<UiState> state = new MutableLiveData<>(UiState.loading());\npublic LiveData<UiState> getState() { return state; }\nio.execute(() -> state.postValue(load()));\n```\n\n```java\n// BAD (Fragment): observers pile up after each view recreation\nviewModel.getState().observe(this, this::render);\n\n// GOOD\nviewModel.getState().observe(getViewLifecycleOwner(), this::render);\n```\n\n`postValue` keeps only the latest pending value. Use it for state, not for events that must all arrive.\n\n### 8. ViewBinding is cleared in onDestroyView\n\n```java\n// BAD: binding outlives the Fragment's view and leaks the whole view tree\nprivate FragmentDevicesBinding binding;\n\n// GOOD\nprivate @Nullable FragmentDevicesBinding binding;\n\n@Override\npublic void onViewCreated(@NonNull View view, @Nullable Bundle savedInstanceState) {\n    binding = FragmentDevicesBinding.bind(view);\n}\n\n@Override\npublic void onDestroyView() {\n    binding = null;\n    super.onDestroyView();\n}\n```\n\nUse the `Fragment(R.layout.fragment_devices)` constructor so `onCreateView` is not needed.\n\n### 9. Lists use ListAdapter and DiffUtil\n\n```java\n// BAD: full rebind, no animations, scroll state jumps\nadapter.setItems(items);\nadapter.notifyDataSetChanged();\n\n// GOOD\npublic final class DeviceAdapter extends ListAdapter<Device, DeviceAdapter.Holder> {\n    private static final DiffUtil.ItemCallback<Device> DIFF = new DiffUtil.ItemCallback<>() {\n        @Override public boolean areItemsTheSame(@NonNull Device a, @NonNull Device b) { return a.id().equals(b.id()); }\n        @Override public boolean areContentsTheSame(@NonNull Device a, @NonNull Device b) { return a.equals(b); }\n    };\n\n    public DeviceAdapter() { super(DIFF); }\n    // onCreateViewHolder / onBindViewHolder use getItem(position)\n}\n\nadapter.submitList(items); // new list instance each time; never mutate a submitted list\n```\n\n### 10. Resources are closed with try-with-resources\n\n```java\n// BAD: leaks on exception, StrictMode reports it\nInputStream in = resolver.openInputStream(uri);\nbyte[] data = readAll(in);\nin.close();\n\n// GOOD\ntry (InputStream in = resolver.openInputStream(uri)) {\n    if (in == null) throw new FileNotFoundException(uri.toString());\n    return readAll(in);\n}\n```\n\nApplies to `Cursor`, streams, `ParcelFileDescriptor`, `TypedArray` (API 31+ implements `AutoCloseable`;\ncall `recycle()` below that).\n\n### 11. equals and hashCode are overridden together\n\n```java\n// BAD: equals without hashCode breaks HashSet, HashMap and DiffUtil\n@Override public boolean equals(Object o) { return o instanceof Device d && id.equals(d.id); }\n\n// GOOD: both, over the same fields (or use a record)\n@Override public boolean equals(@Nullable Object o) {\n    return o instanceof Device d && id.equals(d.id) && name.equals(d.name);\n}\n@Override public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(id, name); }\n```\n\n### 12. Exceptions and interrupts are never swallowed\n\n```java\n// BAD\ntry { worker.join(); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }\ntry { sync(); } catch (Exception e) { e.printStackTrace(); }\n\n// GOOD\ntry {\n    worker.join();\n} catch (InterruptedException e) {\n    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();\n    return;\n}\ntry {\n    sync();\n} catch (IOException e) {\n    state.postValue(UiState.offline());\n}\n```\n\n### 13. Interop annotations follow the direction of calls\n\n```kotlin\n// Kotlin code called from Java\nclass Units {\n    companion object {\n        @JvmStatic fun celsius(f: Double): Double = (f - 32) / 1.8 // Units.celsius(..) from Java\n        const val MAX_MTU = 517                                     // compile-time constant\n    }\n}\nclass ScanSpec @JvmOverloads constructor(val timeoutMs: Long = 10_000, val lowLatency: Boolean = false)\n```\n\n`@JvmField` exposes a non-`const` property as a field. Details and ordering are in\n[Migration to Kotlin](references/migration-to-kotlin.md).\n\n### 14. Tests are deterministic\n\n```java\n// BAD: real threads and sleeps\nviewModel.load();\nThread.sleep(500);\n\n// GOOD: inject a direct executor; InstantTaskExecutorRule runs LiveData synchronously\n@Rule public InstantTaskExecutorRule liveData = new InstantTaskExecutorRule();\n\nDevicesViewModel vm = new DevicesViewModel(fakeRepo, Runnable::run);\n```\n\nSee [Testing](references/testing.md) for Mockito, Espresso and `IdlingResource`.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Rewriting a working Java class in a review that was about a bug fix.\n- `static` fields holding `Context`, `Activity`, `View`, `Fragment`, adapters or listeners.\n- `new Handler()`, `AsyncTask`, `Executors.new...` inside an Activity or Fragment.\n- `observe(this, ...)` in a Fragment; ViewBinding fields never cleared.\n- `notifyDataSetChanged()` for list updates; mutating a list already passed to `submitList`.\n- Public signatures without nullability annotations in code Kotlin calls.\n- `catch (Exception e) { e.printStackTrace(); }` and empty `InterruptedException` handlers.\n- Raising the Java level and assuming the JDK library on the device matches.\n- Converting a file to Kotlin and changing its behaviour in the same commit.\n- Logging tokens, device identifiers or personal data with `Log.d` (escalate to `mobile-security`).\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Language level, `minSdk` and core library desugaring checked before using a JDK API\n- [ ] Lint `NewApi` clean; no blanket suppressions\n- [ ] Value types immutable (records or final fields, defensive copies)\n- [ ] Package `@NullMarked` or public signatures annotated with `@Nullable` / `@NonNull`\n- [ ] No static or long-lived references to Activity, Fragment, View or non-application Context\n- [ ] Handlers use `Looper.getMainLooper()` and are cleared; no `AsyncTask` in new code\n- [ ] Executors shut down by their owner; Futures cancelled in `onCleared()`\n- [ ] `setValue` on main thread, `postValue` from workers; `LiveData` exposed read-only\n- [ ] Fragments observe with `getViewLifecycleOwner()`; binding nulled in `onDestroyView()`\n- [ ] RecyclerView uses `ListAdapter` + `DiffUtil` with id-based `areItemsTheSame`\n- [ ] Closeables in try-with-resources\n- [ ] `equals` and `hashCode` overridden together\n- [ ] No swallowed exceptions; interrupt status restored\n- [ ] Tests use direct executors and `InstantTaskExecutorRule`; Espresso uses `IdlingResource`, not sleeps\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-kotlin` -- target idioms when migrating, and Kotlin-side interop rules\n- `mobile-mvvm` -- ViewModel and LiveData or StateFlow architecture\n- `mobile-design-patterns` -- Builder, Factory and Strategy where Java needs them\n- `mobile-code-review` -- review workflow and severity levels\n- `mobile-security` -- storage, logging and network rules for legacy Java code\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- lint and test gates in the build pipeline\n- `mobile-testing` -- cross-platform test strategy\n",
    "references/migration-to-kotlin.md":
        "# Incremental Java to Kotlin migration\n\nLoad this when planning or doing a Java-to-Kotlin conversion in an Android codebase. The goal is a\ncodebase that builds and ships at every step, not a big-bang rewrite.\n\n## Order of work\n\n1. Make the module build Kotlin: the Kotlin Android plugin, or the built-in Kotlin support of newer AGP\n   versions. Versions go in `libs.versions.toml`.\n2. Move annotation processors that Kotlin classes will need (Room, Hilt, Glide) from `annotationProcessor`\n   to KSP. Java-only `annotationProcessor` does not see Kotlin sources.\n3. Annotate nullability in the Java code first (`@NullMarked` per package, `@Nullable` where needed).\n   The converter then produces correct nullable types instead of guesses.\n4. Write or extend tests around the class being converted, if they are missing.\n5. Convert in this order:\n   - Tests (low risk, fast feedback on Kotlin setup).\n   - Leaf classes: models, mappers, utilities with no Android dependencies.\n   - Repositories and data sources.\n   - ViewModels.\n   - Activities, Fragments and adapters last, usually together with a move to Compose or a rewrite of the screen.\n6. One class or small cluster per merge request. Conversion commit separate from cleanup and behaviour changes.\n\nDo not convert code that is scheduled for deletion, or a large class nobody can test.\n\n## Converting a file\n\n1. Use the IDE converter (\"Convert Java File to Kotlin File\" in the Code menu).\n2. Build and run tests before touching anything else. Commit.\n3. Clean up in a second commit, using the list below.\n\n## Cleanup after the converter\n\n| Converter output | Replace with |\n|---|---|\n| `!!` everywhere | Correct nullable types, `?.`, `?:`, `requireNotNull(x) { \"reason\" }` |\n| `lateinit var` for constructor-provided values | `val` constructor properties |\n| `var` fields that never change | `val` |\n| Getter / setter pairs | Properties |\n| `companion object` with only constants | Top-level `const val` or `private const val` |\n| Static utility class turned into `object` | Top-level functions or extension functions |\n| Java-style builders | Default and named arguments; delete the builder |\n| `equals` / `hashCode` / `toString` written by hand | `data class` (when all fields are part of identity) |\n| Listener interfaces with one method | `fun interface` or function types |\n| `Executor` + callback code | `suspend` functions and `Flow` (only once callers are Kotlin) |\n| `if (x != null) { x.foo() }` | `x?.foo()` or smart casts |\n\nBehaviour to double-check after conversion:\n\n- `==` in Kotlin calls `equals`. Java `==` on objects compared references.\n- Integer division, `Long` / `Int` literals and overflow in arithmetic the converter rewrote.\n- `when` without `else` over a Java enum or int constant.\n- Platform types from remaining Java code (`String!`) that the converter treated as non-null.\n\n## Keeping Java callers working\n\n```kotlin\n// Static access from Java: DeviceIds.parse(...) instead of DeviceIds.Companion.parse(...)\nclass DeviceIds {\n    companion object {\n        @JvmStatic fun parse(raw: String): DeviceId? = ...\n        const val PREFIX = \"dev-\"         // primitives and String: const val\n        @JvmField val DEFAULT_TAGS = listOf(\"new\") // other types: @JvmField exposes a field\n    }\n}\n\n// Default arguments: Java sees only the full signature without @JvmOverloads\nclass ScanSpec @JvmOverloads constructor(\n    val timeoutMs: Long = 10_000,\n    val lowLatency: Boolean = false,\n)\n\n// Checked exceptions Java callers must catch\n@Throws(IOException::class)\nfun readConfig(file: File): Config = ...\n```\n\n```kotlin\n// Bytes.kt: name the class generated for top-level functions (must precede the package line)\n@file:JvmName(\"Bytes\")\n\npackage com.example.ble\n\nfun ByteArray.toHex(): String = joinToString(\"\") { \"%02x\".format(it) } // Java: Bytes.toHex(array)\n```\n\n- Callbacks for Java callers: `fun interface` instead of Kotlin function types. A Java lambda for\n  `(String) -> Unit` must `return Unit.INSTANCE`.\n- `suspend` functions are awkward from Java. While Java callers remain, add a Java-facing wrapper that\n  returns a `Future` or takes a callback, and delete it with the last Java caller.\n- `internal` members get mangled names in Java. Do not rely on calling them from Java.\n- `object` singletons are `Foo.INSTANCE` from Java; `@JvmStatic` on their members avoids that.\n- Sealed classes and `data class` work from Java, but Java callers do not get Kotlin's exhaustive `when`\n  checks. Java branches over them end with a default that throws.\n\n## Java code that Kotlin calls\n\n- Every public signature carries nullability: JSpecify `@NullMarked` packages or AndroidX `@Nullable`\n  / `@NonNull`. Without them Kotlin sees platform types and null checks disappear.\n- Getters and setters named `getX` / `setX` / `isX` appear as properties in Kotlin.\n- Java `List` returns are seen as `(Mutable)List` in Kotlin; return unmodifiable collections so Kotlin's\n  read-only view is honest.\n- Lombok-generated members are not visible to Kotlin in the same module without the Kotlin Lombok compiler\n  plugin. Delombok or convert Lombok classes early.\n\n## Tracking progress\n\n- Measure the Java / Kotlin line ratio per module, not repo-wide, and convert module by module so a module\n  can eventually drop Java-specific setup.\n- Lint and detekt or ktlint run on converted files from the first commit, so style debt does not accumulate.\n- Avoid long-lived migration branches. Small merges to the main line keep conflicts manageable.\n",
    "references/testing.md":
        "# Testing Java Android code\n\nLoad this when writing or fixing tests for Java classes: ViewModels with `LiveData`, repositories with\nexecutors, and Espresso UI tests.\n\n## Setup\n\n| Test type | Runs on | Typical libraries |\n|---|---|---|\n| Local unit (`src/test`) | Desktop JVM | JUnit 4 or 5, Mockito, `androidx.arch.core:core-testing`, Robolectric when framework classes are needed |\n| Instrumented (`src/androidTest`) | Device or emulator | AndroidX Test (`AndroidJUnit4`), Espresso, `mockito-android`, Hilt testing |\n\n- JUnit 4 is the default for both. JUnit 5 (Jupiter) for local tests requires the Gradle test task to use the\n  JUnit Platform. Instrumented tests stay on JUnit 4 unless a third-party plugin is added.\n- `InstantTaskExecutorRule` is a JUnit 4 rule. With Jupiter, keep LiveData tests on JUnit 4 through the\n  vintage engine, or write an equivalent extension.\n- Dependency versions live in `libs.versions.toml`.\n\n## ViewModel with LiveData and an executor\n\nProduction code takes its executor as a constructor parameter, so tests can run work synchronously.\n\n```java\n/** Runs every task on the calling thread. AbstractExecutorService.submit wraps tasks and calls execute. */\nfinal class DirectExecutorService extends AbstractExecutorService {\n    private volatile boolean shutdown;\n\n    @Override public void execute(Runnable command) { command.run(); }\n    @Override public void shutdown() { shutdown = true; }\n    @Override public List<Runnable> shutdownNow() { shutdown = true; return Collections.emptyList(); }\n    @Override public boolean isShutdown() { return shutdown; }\n    @Override public boolean isTerminated() { return shutdown; }\n    @Override public boolean awaitTermination(long timeout, TimeUnit unit) { return true; }\n}\n```\n\n```java\npublic class DevicesViewModelTest {\n\n    @Rule public InstantTaskExecutorRule liveData = new InstantTaskExecutorRule();\n\n    private final DeviceRepository repo = mock(DeviceRepository.class);\n\n    @Test\n    public void load_success_emitsLoaded() throws Exception {\n        List<Device> devices = Collections.singletonList(new Device(\"1\", \"Gateway\", Collections.emptyList()));\n        when(repo.devices()).thenReturn(devices);\n        DevicesViewModel vm = new DevicesViewModel(repo, new DirectExecutorService());\n\n        vm.load();\n\n        assertEquals(UiState.loaded(devices), vm.getState().getValue());\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    public void load_ioError_emitsError() throws Exception {\n        when(repo.devices()).thenThrow(new IOException(\"offline\"));\n        DevicesViewModel vm = new DevicesViewModel(repo, new DirectExecutorService());\n\n        vm.load();\n\n        assertEquals(UiState.error(), vm.getState().getValue());\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- With `InstantTaskExecutorRule`, `postValue` delivers immediately, so `getValue()` is up to date.\n- To assert a sequence of states, record them with an observer:\n\n```java\nList<UiState> seen = new ArrayList<>();\nObserver<UiState> observer = seen::add;\nvm.getState().observeForever(observer);\ntry {\n    vm.load();\n    assertEquals(Arrays.asList(UiState.loading(), UiState.loaded(devices)), seen);\n} finally {\n    vm.getState().removeObserver(observer);\n}\n```\n\n`UiState` needs `equals` / `hashCode` (a record works) for these assertions.\n\n## Mockito\n\n- `mockito-core` for local tests, `mockito-android` for instrumented tests. Mockito 5 uses the inline mock\n  maker by default, so final classes and records can be mocked; prefer not to.\n- Fakes beat mocks for repositories and data sources: a small in-memory implementation is easier to read\n  and does not break on refactors.\n- Mock only what the class under test talks to directly. Never mock value types.\n- `verify(repo).refresh()` for interactions that are the behaviour under test; otherwise assert on state.\n- `ArgumentCaptor` for arguments built inside the class under test.\n\n## Framework classes in local tests\n\n- Code that touches `Context`, resources or `SharedPreferences` runs under Robolectric with\n  `@RunWith(AndroidJUnit4.class)` and `unitTests.isIncludeAndroidResources = true` in the Gradle test options.\n- Better still, keep framework calls at the edges so most logic needs no Android classes at all.\n\n## Espresso\n\n```java\n@RunWith(AndroidJUnit4.class)\npublic class DevicesScreenTest {\n\n    @Rule public ActivityScenarioRule<MainActivity> activity = new ActivityScenarioRule<>(MainActivity.class);\n\n    @Test\n    public void refresh_showsDevices() {\n        onView(withId(R.id.refresh)).perform(click());\n        onView(withText(\"Gateway\")).check(matches(isDisplayed()));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Espresso waits for the main thread and `AsyncTask` pool only. Work on your own executors needs an\n  `IdlingResource`, or Espresso asserts before the data arrives.\n- Never `Thread.sleep` in UI tests. It is slow on fast devices and flaky on slow ones.\n\n```java\n// Debug or test source set: counts in-flight background work\npublic final class BackgroundWork {\n    public static final CountingIdlingResource IDLING = new CountingIdlingResource(\"background-work\");\n    private BackgroundWork() {}\n}\n\n// Around asynchronous work in the code under test\nBackgroundWork.IDLING.increment();\nio.execute(() -> {\n    try {\n        state.postValue(UiState.loaded(repo.devices()));\n    } catch (IOException e) {\n        state.postValue(UiState.error());\n    } finally {\n        BackgroundWork.IDLING.decrement();\n    }\n});\n\n// Test\n@Before public void registerIdling() { IdlingRegistry.getInstance().register(BackgroundWork.IDLING); }\n@After public void unregisterIdling() { IdlingRegistry.getInstance().unregister(BackgroundWork.IDLING); }\n```\n\n- Cleaner alternative with Hilt: replace the production executor module in tests (`@TestInstallIn` or\n  `@UninstallModules`) with one whose executor Espresso can track.\n- Lists: `RecyclerViewActions` from `espresso-contrib` to scroll to or click items by position or matcher.\n- Fragments in isolation: `FragmentScenario` from `fragment-testing`. Hilt fragments need a Hilt-enabled\n  host activity in the debug source set.\n\n## Hilt in instrumented tests\n\n```java\n@HiltAndroidTest\npublic class SyncFlowTest {\n    @Rule public HiltAndroidRule hilt = new HiltAndroidRule(this);\n\n    @Before public void setUp() { hilt.inject(); }\n}\n```\n\nThe instrumentation runner must start `HiltTestApplication` (a custom `AndroidJUnitRunner` that overrides\n`newApplication`).\n\n## What to test first in legacy Java\n\n1. Pure logic hidden in Activities and Fragments: extract it into plain classes, then unit test.\n2. Parsers and mappers (DTOs, byte frames, CRC checks) with table-driven tests including malformed input.\n3. ViewModel state transitions for success, error and cancellation.\n4. One Espresso smoke test per critical flow, not per screen detail.\n",
    "references/threading.md":
        "# Threading in Java Android code\n\nLoad this when Java code starts background work, touches the main thread, handles callbacks from\nsystem services (Bluetooth, location, sensors) or replaces `AsyncTask`.\n\n## Quick scan\n\n| Pattern | Why | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| `new Thread(...).start()` in UI code | Unowned, no cancellation, leaks the Activity | Owned executor in a ViewModel or repository |\n| `AsyncTask` | Deprecated, leaks, loses results on rotation | `ExecutorService` + `LiveData` |\n| `new Handler()` | Deprecated, implicit Looper | `new Handler(Looper.getMainLooper())` |\n| `Executors.new...` without `shutdown()` | Thread leak | Owner shuts it down, or inject a shared one |\n| `future.get()` on the main thread | ANR | Callback, `LiveData`, or `get` only on a worker |\n| `CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(task)` with no executor | Runs on the common ForkJoin pool | Pass an explicit executor |\n| Work inside a `BluetoothGattCallback` method | Blocks a binder thread | Hand off to a single-thread executor |\n| Check-then-act on a shared `HashMap` | Race | `ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent` or a lock |\n\n## Replacing AsyncTask\n\n```java\n// BAD\nprivate class LoadTask extends AsyncTask<Void, Void, List<Device>> {\n    @Override protected List<Device> doInBackground(Void... v) { return repo.devices(); }\n    @Override protected void onPostExecute(List<Device> d) { adapter.submitList(d); }\n}\n\n// GOOD: ViewModel owns the work, the Fragment only observes\n@HiltViewModel\npublic final class DevicesViewModel extends ViewModel {\n    private final DeviceRepository repo;\n    private final ExecutorService io;\n    private final MutableLiveData<UiState> state = new MutableLiveData<>(UiState.loading());\n    private @Nullable Future<?> pending;\n\n    @Inject\n    public DevicesViewModel(DeviceRepository repo, @IoExecutor ExecutorService io) {\n        this.repo = repo;\n        this.io = io;\n    }\n\n    public LiveData<UiState> getState() { return state; }\n\n    public void load() {\n        if (pending != null) pending.cancel(true);\n        pending = io.submit(() -> {\n            try {\n                state.postValue(UiState.loaded(repo.devices()));\n            } catch (IOException e) {\n                state.postValue(UiState.error());\n            }\n        });\n    }\n\n    @Override protected void onCleared() {\n        if (pending != null) pending.cancel(true); // shared executor: cancel work, do not shut it down\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`@IoExecutor` is a project-defined qualifier. Tests pass an `ExecutorService` that runs tasks synchronously\n(see [Testing](testing.md)).\n\n## Executors\n\n- One shared, injected pool for IO (`@Singleton` in DI). Per-feature pools only when work must be serialized,\n  usually `Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor()`.\n- Owners that create an executor call `shutdown()` (finish queued work) or `shutdownNow()` (interrupt) when\n  their lifecycle ends.\n- `Future.cancel(true)` only interrupts. Blocking code must respond: check\n  `Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()` in loops and do not swallow `InterruptedException`.\n- Main-thread executor without a Context dependency on the call site: `ContextCompat.getMainExecutor(context)`.\n- Name threads with a `ThreadFactory` so stack traces and profilers show what each pool is for.\n\n```java\nThreadFactory named = r -> new Thread(r, \"ble-io\");\nExecutorService bleIo = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor(named);\n```\n\n## Futures\n\n- `CompletableFuture` (API 24+) chains with explicit executors:\n\n```java\nCompletableFuture\n    .supplyAsync(repo::loadConfig, io)\n    .thenApplyAsync(ConfigMapper::toDomain, io)\n    .whenComplete((config, error) -> state.postValue(error == null ? UiState.ready(config) : UiState.error()));\n```\n\n- Libraries that expect `ListenableFuture` (WorkManager, CameraX) can be bridged with\n  `CallbackToFutureAdapter` from `androidx.concurrent:concurrent-futures`.\n\n## Main thread and Handlers\n\n```java\n// BAD: inner class Handler keeps the Activity alive while messages are queued\nprivate final Handler handler = new Handler() {\n    @Override public void handleMessage(Message msg) { title.setText(\"done\"); }\n};\n\n// GOOD\nprivate final Handler main = new Handler(Looper.getMainLooper());\nprivate final Runnable refresh = this::refresh; // field, so it can be removed\n\n@Override protected void onStart() {\n    super.onStart();\n    main.postDelayed(refresh, 1_000);\n}\n\n@Override protected void onStop() {\n    main.removeCallbacks(refresh);\n    super.onStop();\n}\n```\n\n- Annotate methods with `@MainThread`, `@WorkerThread` or `@AnyThread` (`androidx.annotation`); lint checks calls\n  between them.\n\n## Shared state\n\n- Immutable snapshots shared between threads are safe without locks. Prefer replacing a reference over\n  mutating shared collections.\n- `volatile` for a single flag or reference written by one thread and read by others.\n- `AtomicInteger`, `AtomicReference` for counters and compare-and-set updates.\n- `synchronized` blocks for invariants spanning several fields. Never call out to unknown code while holding a lock.\n\n```java\n// BAD: two threads both miss and both create\nif (!cache.containsKey(id)) cache.put(id, load(id));\n\n// GOOD\nSession session = sessions.computeIfAbsent(id, this::open); // ConcurrentHashMap\n```\n\n## System callbacks (Bluetooth, location, sensors)\n\n- `BluetoothGattCallback` methods run on binder threads, not the main thread. Do not block them, and do not\n  touch views or call `setValue` from them.\n- Hand each callback to one single-thread executor, so state updates and the next GATT operation are\n  serialized in callback order.\n- Android GATT allows one outstanding operation per connection. Queue reads and writes; issue the next one\n  only after the previous callback.\n- Copy byte arrays received in callbacks before handing them to another thread if the source may be reused.\n\n```java\nprivate final ExecutorService gattThread = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();\n\n@Override\npublic void onCharacteristicChanged(@NonNull BluetoothGatt gatt,\n                                    @NonNull BluetoothGattCharacteristic characteristic,\n                                    @NonNull byte[] value) {\n    byte[] copy = value.clone();\n    gattThread.execute(() -> frames.onFrame(copy)); // CRC check and parsing happen off the binder thread\n}\n```\n\nThe `value` parameter variant of `onCharacteristicChanged` is the API 33+ callback; older devices call the\ndeprecated variant, so implement both when `minSdk` is below 33.\n\n## Background work that must survive the screen\n\n- Deferrable or guaranteed work: WorkManager. A Java `Worker.doWork()` runs synchronously on WorkManager's\n  background executor; return `Result.retry()` for transient failures.\n- Long-running user-visible work: a foreground service with the correct `foregroundServiceType`\n  (required when targeting Android 14+).\n\n## Debug checks\n\n```java\nif (BuildConfig.DEBUG) {\n    StrictMode.setThreadPolicy(new StrictMode.ThreadPolicy.Builder().detectAll().penaltyLog().build());\n    StrictMode.setVmPolicy(new StrictMode.VmPolicy.Builder().detectAll().penaltyLog().build());\n}\n```\n\nStrictMode flags disk and network access on the main thread and leaked closeables. LeakCanary in debug\nbuilds catches retained Activities and Fragments.\n",
  },
  "mobile-kmp": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-kmp\ndescription: \"Builds and reviews Kotlin Multiplatform and Compose Multiplatform code for Android and iOS: source sets and the default hierarchy template, interfaces with DI over expect/actual, shared AndroidX ViewModels, Ktor, Room KMP or SQLDelight, kotlinx.serialization, Flow and suspend from Swift via SKIE, Objective-C and Swift export limits, XCFramework and SPM publishing, commonTest. Use when setting up, migrating to or reviewing a KMP shared module.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Kotlin Multiplatform\n\nShare business logic between Android and iOS without making either app worse. A good result has a\n`commonMain` that compiles without platform imports, platform services behind interfaces, a Swift API that\nfeels native, a build on the current Gradle plugins, and tests that run on every target.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Setting up a shared KMP module, or adding iOS to an existing Android codebase.\n- Moving repositories, ViewModels, networking or persistence from Android code into `commonMain`.\n- Choosing between expect/actual and interfaces, Room KMP and SQLDelight, SKIE and KMP-NativeCoroutines,\n  shared Compose UI and native UI.\n- Fixing Swift-side pain: lost generics, `KotlinInt` boxes, non-exhaustive `switch`, crashes from Kotlin\n  exceptions, Flows that SwiftUI cannot observe.\n- Upgrading to AGP 9, where the KMP plugin no longer works with `com.android.application` or\n  `com.android.library` in the same module.\n- Publishing the shared code to an iOS team as an XCFramework or Swift package.\n\nNot for: Android-only Kotlin style (use `mobile-kotlin`), Swift-only code (use `mobile-swift`), CI pipelines\n(use `mobile-ci-cd`), or Flutter (use `mobile-flutter`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Read the build before writing code.\n   - `gradle/libs.versions.toml`: Kotlin, AGP, Compose Multiplatform, coroutines, Ktor, Room or SQLDelight,\n     SKIE or KMP-NativeCoroutines versions.\n   - `settings.gradle.kts` and each module's plugins: where `org.jetbrains.kotlin.multiplatform` is applied\n     and which Android plugin sits next to it.\n   - Declared targets, framework `baseName`, `export(...)` calls, and how Xcode consumes the framework\n     (build-phase script, local package, CocoaPods, remote XCFramework).\n2. Check compatibility before adding or upgrading anything. SKIE, KSP, Room and Compose Multiplatform each\n   support a specific Kotlin range. Do not bump Kotlin, AGP, `compileSdk` or the iOS deployment target\n   without asking.\n3. Load the reference for the area being touched:\n   [Structure and Gradle](references/structure-and-gradle.md), [iOS interop](references/ios-interop.md),\n   [Data and networking](references/data-and-networking.md), [Testing](references/testing.md).\n4. Decide what is shared. Default: domain, data, networking, persistence and ViewModels in `commonMain`;\n   UI per Rule 13. Hardware stacks (BLE, camera, location, push) stay platform code behind an interface.\n5. Write common code first, then platform implementations, then the Swift-facing facade.\n6. Add `commonTest` tests for new shared logic; run them on the JVM and the iOS simulator target.\n7. Build both apps: `./gradlew :androidApp:assembleDebug` and the iOS app in Xcode (or\n   `./gradlew :shared:linkDebugFrameworkIosSimulatorArm64`). A green Android build says nothing about iOS.\n8. Report what moved to common code, new dependencies, Swift API changes, and every `Info.plist`,\n   entitlement, Gradle property or Xcode build-phase change. These need explicit sign-off.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Use the default hierarchy template; never wire `dependsOn` by hand\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: manual intermediate source set, disables the default template with a warning\nval iosMain by creating { dependsOn(commonMain.get()) }\niosArm64Main.get().dependsOn(iosMain)\n\n// GOOD: iosMain, appleMain and nativeMain exist automatically for the declared targets\nkotlin {\n    iosArm64()\n    iosSimulatorArm64()\n    sourceSets {\n        commonMain.dependencies { implementation(libs.kotlinx.coroutines.core) }\n        iosMain.dependencies { implementation(libs.ktor.client.darwin) }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Put iOS code in `iosMain`, not `iosArm64Main`. Per-target source sets are for rare ABI differences.\n- `iosX64` (Intel simulator) is Tier 3. Declare it only if the team still builds on Intel Macs, and check\n  that every dependency still publishes it.\n\n### 2. The shared module is a library; app entry points live in app modules\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: KMP plugin and the Android application plugin in one module (unsupported from AGP 9)\nplugins {\n    alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.multiplatform)\n    alias(libs.plugins.android.application)\n}\n\n// GOOD: shared/build.gradle.kts\nplugins {\n    alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.multiplatform)\n    alias(libs.plugins.android.kotlin.multiplatform.library)\n}\nkotlin {\n    android {\n        namespace = \"com.example.shared\"\n        compileSdk = 36\n        minSdk = 26\n    }\n    iosArm64()\n    iosSimulatorArm64()\n}\n```\n\n- `com.android.kotlin.multiplatform.library` has one variant and no top-level `android {}` block. From\n  AGP 8.12 the target is `kotlin { android { } }`; older AGP uses the now-deprecated `androidLibrary { }`.\n- `MainActivity` and the app manifest move to an `androidApp` module that depends on `projects.shared`.\n  Layout and AGP 9 migration: [Structure and Gradle](references/structure-and-gradle.md).\n\n### 3. Platform services are interfaces bound in DI; `commonMain` stays platform-free\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: expect class for a service, Android types in shared signatures, nothing to fake in tests\nexpect class SecureStore() { fun put(key: String, value: String) }\nclass ReadingRepository(private val context: android.content.Context) {\n    fun now(): Long = System.currentTimeMillis()\n}\n\n// GOOD: interfaces in commonMain, implementations in androidMain / iosMain, bound per platform\ninterface SecureStore {\n    suspend fun put(key: String, value: ByteArray)\n    suspend fun get(key: String): ByteArray?\n}\ninterface Clock { fun nowMillis(): Long }\nclass ReadingRepository(private val clock: Clock, private val store: SecureStore)\n\n// GOOD: expect/actual for a tiny, stateless platform fact\nexpect val platformName: String\n```\n\n- Expect/actual classes are Beta and need `-Xexpect-actual-classes`. Prefer an interface plus an\n  `expect fun` factory, or bind the implementation in each platform's DI module.\n- An implementation can be written in Swift and passed in from the iOS app (a CoreBluetooth or Keychain\n  wrapper), which keeps Apple APIs in the language the iOS team owns.\n- `java.*`, `android.*` and `platform.*` imports belong in platform source sets only. Use multiplatform\n  libraries (coroutines, serialization, Ktor, kotlinx-datetime, kotlinx-io) instead of wrapping JVM APIs.\n- Hilt does not run in `commonMain`. Use the KMP DI library the project already has (Koin, kotlin-inject,\n  Metro) or constructor injection with a small composition root per platform.\n\n### 4. Share the ViewModel with AndroidX lifecycle; scopes and dispatchers stay structured\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: Hilt and Android types in a shared ViewModel; a scope nobody cancels\n@HiltViewModel\nclass DevicesViewModel @Inject constructor(app: Application) : AndroidViewModel(app)\nprivate val scope = CoroutineScope(Dispatchers.Default)\n\n// GOOD: commonMain, androidx.lifecycle:lifecycle-viewmodel\nclass DevicesViewModel(private val repo: DeviceRepository) : ViewModel() {\n    private val _state = MutableStateFlow<DevicesUiState>(DevicesUiState.Loading)\n    val state: StateFlow<DevicesUiState> = _state.asStateFlow()\n    fun refresh() = viewModelScope.launch { _state.value = repo.load().toUiState() }\n}\n\nval devicesViewModelFactory = viewModelFactory {\n    initializer { DevicesViewModel(repo = AppGraph.deviceRepository) }\n}\n```\n\n- Non-JVM targets have no reflection: in Compose Multiplatform call `viewModel { DevicesViewModel(...) }`,\n  never a bare `viewModel()`.\n- `viewModelScope` runs on `Dispatchers.Main.immediate`, the main queue on iOS. Inject IO and Default\n  dispatchers into repositories; rethrow `CancellationException` exactly as on Android (`mobile-kotlin`).\n- SwiftUI needs a `ViewModelStoreOwner` held as `@StateObject` so `clear()` runs on `deinit`:\n  [iOS interop](references/ios-interop.md).\n\n### 5. One Ktor client per app, an explicit engine per platform, TLS only\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: new client per call, logging prints the bearer token, cleartext URL\nsuspend fun fetch() = HttpClient { install(Logging) { level = LogLevel.ALL } }\n    .get(\"http://api.example.com/readings\").body<List<ReadingDto>>()\n\n// GOOD: engine comes from androidMain (OkHttp) or iosMain (Darwin)\nfun createHttpClient(engine: HttpClientEngine, json: Json) = HttpClient(engine) {\n    expectSuccess = true\n    install(ContentNegotiation) { json(json) }\n    install(HttpTimeout) { requestTimeoutMillis = 15_000 }\n    install(Logging) {\n        level = LogLevel.HEADERS\n        sanitizeHeader { it == HttpHeaders.Authorization }\n    }\n    defaultRequest { url(\"https://api.example.com/\") }\n}\n```\n\n- Each client owns a connection pool; create it once in the app graph.\n- Telemetry and device identifiers are personal data: no `LogLevel.BODY` in release builds.\n\n### 6. DTOs are `@Serializable` and mapped at the data edge\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: domain model doubles as the wire format; a new server field breaks old clients\n@Serializable data class Device(val id: String, val rssi: Int)\n\n// GOOD\n@Serializable\ndata class DeviceDto(@SerialName(\"device_id\") val id: String, val rssi: Int? = null)\n\nval appJson = Json { ignoreUnknownKeys = true }\nfun DeviceDto.toDomain() = Device(id = DeviceId(id), rssi = rssi)\n```\n\n- One injected `Json` instance. Apply `org.jetbrains.kotlin.plugin.serialization`, versioned with Kotlin.\n- Engines, error mapping and polymorphic payloads: [Data and networking](references/data-and-networking.md).\n\n### 7. Pick the database by team and schema, not by habit\n\n| Choose | When |\n|---|---|\n| Room KMP (`androidx.room3`, or Room 2.7+ already in use) | Android team knows Room, annotation-defined entities, migrating an existing Room database |\n| SQLDelight | SQL-first schema reviewed by both teams, compile-time checked queries, `.sqm` migrations, no KSP per target |\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: blocking DAO and LiveData, neither works in commonMain\n@Query(\"SELECT * FROM reading\") fun all(): LiveData<List<ReadingEntity>>\n\n// GOOD: Room in commonMain, bundled SQLite driver on every platform\n@Query(\"SELECT * FROM reading ORDER BY ts DESC\") fun observeAll(): Flow<List<ReadingEntity>>\nfun buildDb(builder: RoomDatabase.Builder<AppDatabase>) =\n    builder.setDriver(BundledSQLiteDriver()).build()\n```\n\n- Room 3 is a new package (`androidx.room3`), KSP-only and coroutines-only, with no `SupportSQLite` types.\n  Room 2.x and 3.x can coexist during a migration.\n- Either option needs real migrations and a migration test.\n\n### 8. Design the Swift-facing API; do not ship the raw Kotlin surface\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: generic interface (type lost in Objective-C), default arg Swift cannot omit, value class param\ninterface Store<T> { suspend fun load(id: DeviceId, refresh: Boolean = false): T }\n\n// GOOD: small concrete facade with explicit overloads\nclass DeviceFacade internal constructor(private val repo: DeviceRepository) {\n    @Throws(DeviceException::class, CancellationException::class)\n    suspend fun load(id: String): DeviceSnapshot = repo.load(DeviceId(id), refresh = false)\n\n    @Throws(DeviceException::class, CancellationException::class)\n    suspend fun refresh(id: String): DeviceSnapshot = repo.load(DeviceId(id), refresh = true)\n}\n```\n\n- Objective-C export keeps generics on classes only, maps `enum class` to a class, drops default\n  arguments and exports inline classes as their underlying type or `id`. Mapping table:\n  [iOS interop](references/ios-interop.md).\n- `@ObjCName(swiftName = ...)` fixes names, `@HiddenFromObjC` hides Kotlin-only API, and\n  `@ShouldRefineInSwift` lets the iOS team wrap an API in Swift.\n\n### 9. Every function Swift calls that can fail declares `@Throws`\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: an exception reaching Swift terminates the app\nfun parse(bytes: ByteArray): Frame = decoder.decode(bytes) // throws on malformed input\n\n// GOOD: becomes `throws` in Swift; suspend functions must also list CancellationException\n@Throws(FrameException::class)\nfun parse(bytes: ByteArray): Frame = decoder.decode(bytes)\n\n@Throws(IOException::class, CancellationException::class)\nsuspend fun upload(frame: Frame) { api.post(frame) }\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD: the Kotlin exception arrives as a Swift Error\ndo { try await facade.upload(frame: frame) } catch { state = .failed(error) }\n```\n\n- A non-suspend function without `@Throws` propagates no exception, and any Kotlin exception reaching\n  Swift is unhandled and terminates the process. A suspend function without it propagates only\n  `CancellationException`.\n- Return a sealed result for expected failures; keep exceptions for the unexpected.\n\n### 10. Flow, suspend and sealed types reach Swift through SKIE; one bridge per project\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: Swift receives a Flow with no element type and needs a hand-written collector\nval readings: Flow<List<Reading>> = repo.observe()\n\n// GOOD with SKIE: no annotation, StateFlow becomes a typed AsyncSequence\nval readings: StateFlow<List<Reading>> = repo.observe()\n    .stateIn(viewModelScope, SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5_000), emptyList())\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD: cast chain over a sealed class, silently wrong when a case is added\nif let c = state as? DevicesUiStateContent { show(c.devices) } else { showSpinner() }\n\n// GOOD: collection cancelled with the view; exhaustive switch, no default branch\n.task { for await list in viewModel.readings { self.readings = list } }\n\nswitch onEnum(of: state) {\ncase .loading: showSpinner()\ncase .content(let c): show(c.devices)\ncase .error(let e): showError(e.reason)\n}\n```\n\n- Recommendation: SKIE for app projects where one framework is consumed by the team's own app. It covers\n  suspend, Flow, sealed classes and enums with no Kotlin-side annotations.\n- Choose KMP-NativeCoroutines when Kotlin must move to a release SKIE does not support yet, when\n  coroutines are the only gap, or when shipping an SDK to third parties. Never apply both. Trade-offs and\n  setup: [iOS interop](references/ios-interop.md).\n- Swift export (Alpha) maps suspend to `async`, Flow to `AsyncSequence` and sealed types to Swift enums,\n  but it works only with direct integration and breaking changes are expected. Do not ship it yet.\n\n### 11. Export only what Swift uses\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: every transitive API in the header, dead-code elimination disabled for all of it\nbinaries.framework {\n    export(libs.ktor.client.core)\n    transitiveExport = true\n}\n\n// GOOD: export only modules Swift calls directly; they must be `api` dependencies\niosMain.dependencies { api(projects.core.model) }\nbinaries.framework {\n    baseName = \"Shared\"\n    export(projects.core.model)\n}\n```\n\n- Mark helpers `internal`. Every public declaration in exported modules becomes Swift API and binary size.\n- One umbrella framework per app. Two Kotlin frameworks in one app duplicate the runtime and types.\n\n### 12. Distribute by repository layout\n\n| Layout | Integration |\n|---|---|\n| Monorepo, one team | Direct integration: Xcode build phase runs `embedAndSignAppleFrameworkForXcode` |\n| Separate iOS repo or external consumers | Zipped XCFramework consumed via SPM `binaryTarget(url:checksum:)` |\n| CocoaPods dependencies used from Kotlin | Kotlin CocoaPods Gradle plugin |\n\n```kotlin\n// GOOD: produces build/XCFrameworks/release/Shared.xcframework\nval xcf = XCFramework(\"Shared\")\nlistOf(iosArm64(), iosSimulatorArm64()).forEach {\n    it.binaries.framework { baseName = \"Shared\"; xcf.add(this) }\n}\n```\n\n- Publish `Package.swift` from its own repository with semantic version tags. Commands and manifest:\n  [Structure and Gradle](references/structure-and-gradle.md).\n- The app target embeds and signs the framework. Flag any change to build phases, `Embed Frameworks` or\n  signing settings for the iOS owner.\n\n### 13. Share UI with Compose Multiplatform only when it pays off\n\n| Share UI (Compose Multiplatform) | Keep native UI (SwiftUI on iOS) |\n|---|---|\n| Internal, B2B or tooling apps where consistency beats platform feel | Consumer apps where users expect native navigation, text input and gestures |\n| Team is mostly Kotlin developers | Strong iOS team that owns the app |\n| Complex custom drawing or charts duplicated today | Heavy use of widgets, Live Activities, App Intents |\n| New app, no existing iOS UI | Mature SwiftUI app: share logic first, screens later |\n\n```kotlin\n// GOOD: iosMain entry point; SwiftUI hosts it with UIViewControllerRepresentable\nfun MainViewController(): UIViewController = ComposeUIViewController { App() }\n```\n\n- Compose Multiplatform for iOS is Stable. Screens can mix both ways (`UIKitViewController` with a\n  `UIHostingController` puts SwiftUI inside Compose).\n- Info.plist change: add `CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone` set to `true`, or the app crashes at\n  runtime. Call it out in the summary.\n- Use direct library coordinates in the catalog; the `compose.ui`-style plugin aliases are deprecated\n  since Compose Multiplatform 1.10.\n\n### 14. Shared logic is tested in `commonTest` with fakes, on every target\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: MockK in commonTest is JVM-only, so the iOS test compilation fails\nval repo = mockk<DeviceRepository>()\n\n// GOOD: kotlin.test, kotlinx-coroutines-test, Turbine, hand-written fake\nclass DevicesViewModelTest {\n    @BeforeTest fun setUp() = Dispatchers.setMain(StandardTestDispatcher())\n    @AfterTest fun tearDown() = Dispatchers.resetMain()\n\n    @Test fun refreshEmitsContent() = runTest {\n        val vm = DevicesViewModel(FakeDeviceRepository(devices = listOf(sensor)))\n        vm.state.test {\n            assertEquals(DevicesUiState.Loading, awaitItem())\n            vm.refresh()\n            assertEquals(DevicesUiState.Content(listOf(sensor)), awaitItem())\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Run `./gradlew :shared:allTests` so iOS simulator tests run too: [Testing](references/testing.md).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- `commonMain` code that only \"works\" because the iOS target was never built.\n- Manual `dependsOn` wiring or `by creating` source sets that disable the default hierarchy template.\n- Keeping the KMP and Android application plugins in one module on AGP 9, or treating\n  `android.enableLegacyVariantApi=true` as a permanent fix.\n- Raw `Flow`, generic interfaces or default arguments exposed to Swift for the iOS team to cope with.\n- SKIE and KMP-NativeCoroutines together, `transitiveExport = true`, or two Kotlin frameworks in one app.\n- An `HttpClient` per request; `LogLevel.ALL` or `BODY` in release; `http://` endpoints.\n- `runBlocking` on the iOS main thread to make a Swift call look synchronous.\n- BLE, camera or location stacks forced into common code. Share protocol parsing and state machines,\n  keep the radio APIs native (see `mobile-ble`).\n- Moving a mature SwiftUI app to Compose Multiplatform without the iOS team agreeing.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Kotlin, AGP, Compose Multiplatform, KSP and bridge versions confirmed compatible\n- [ ] Targets `iosArm64` and `iosSimulatorArm64` (`iosX64` only if needed); no manual `dependsOn`\n- [ ] Shared module uses `com.android.kotlin.multiplatform.library`; app entry points in app modules\n- [ ] `commonMain` free of `java.*`, `android.*`, `platform.*`; platform services are interfaces in DI\n- [ ] Shared ViewModels extend AndroidX `ViewModel`, built via initializer or factory, no Hilt\n- [ ] Scopes owned, dispatchers injected, `CancellationException` rethrown\n- [ ] One Ktor client, platform engine, timeouts, `expectSuccess`, sanitized logs, HTTPS only\n- [ ] DTOs `@Serializable` and mapped; one `Json` with `ignoreUnknownKeys`\n- [ ] Database choice justified; bundled driver; migrations and a migration test\n- [ ] Swift API reviewed: concrete types, overloads instead of defaults, clear names\n- [ ] `@Throws` on every Swift-callable throwing function (suspend ones include `CancellationException`)\n- [ ] One coroutine bridge; Swift collects Flows with cancellation; sealed types switched exhaustively\n- [ ] Only needed modules exported; one framework per app; signing and build-phase changes flagged\n- [ ] Compose Multiplatform decision recorded; `CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone` set if used\n- [ ] `commonTest` covers new shared logic with fakes; `allTests` passes; both apps build\n\n## Output format\n\nUse the review report for an existing KMP codebase and the migration plan for moving Android code to KMP.\n\n~~~markdown\n## KMP review: <module or branch>\n\nTargets: <android, iosArm64, iosSimulatorArm64>. Versions: Kotlin <x>, AGP <x>, CMP <x | none>,\nbridge <SKIE x | KMP-NativeCoroutines x | none>. iOS integration: <direct | SPM | CocoaPods>.\n\n### Findings\n1. [Blocker] shared/src/commonMain/.../FrameParser.kt:31 -- `parse` throws to Swift without `@Throws`\n   Why: any malformed frame terminates the iOS app.\n   Fix:\n   ```kotlin\n   @Throws(FrameException::class)\n   fun parse(bytes: ByteArray): Frame\n   ```\n2. [Major] ... -- ...\n\n### Swift API\n- <names, generics, default args, Flows, sealed types that need work>\n\n### Platform impact\n- Info.plist / entitlements / Xcode build phases / Gradle properties: <list or \"none\">\n\n### Verdict\nRequest changes -- 1 Blocker, 1 Major.\n~~~\n\n~~~markdown\n## KMP migration plan: <app>\n\nGoal: <what is shared and why>. UI: <native | Compose Multiplatform for screens X, Y>.\n\n| Phase | Scope | Exit criteria |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 Build | AGP 9 module split, Android-KMP library plugin, iOS integration | Both apps build in CI with an empty shared module |\n| 1 Models | DTOs, domain types, serialization | `commonTest` green on JVM and iOS simulator |\n| 2 Data | Ktor, database, repositories behind interfaces | Android on the shared data layer; one iOS feature reads it |\n| 3 State | Shared ViewModels, coroutine bridge | One iOS screen driven by a shared ViewModel |\n| 4 UI (optional) | Compose Multiplatform screens | Agreed with the iOS team; accessibility checked |\n\nRisks: <version lock-in, binary size, iOS ramp-up, database migration>.\nPlatform changes: <Info.plist keys, entitlements, build phases>.\n~~~\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-kotlin` -- Kotlin, coroutine and Compose rules that also apply in `commonMain`\n- `mobile-swift` -- Swift concurrency and SwiftUI on the consuming side\n- `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi` -- state and event design for shared ViewModels\n- `mobile-ble` -- keeping radio code native while sharing protocol logic\n- `mobile-testing` -- test strategy beyond `commonTest`\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- building the XCFramework and running iOS simulator tests in CI\n- `mobile-security` -- secure storage, TLS and logging for shared networking code\n- `mobile-code-review` -- general review dimensions applied to KMP diffs\n",
    "references/data-and-networking.md":
        "# Data and networking reference\n\nLoad this when writing the shared Ktor client, serialization models, Room KMP or SQLDelight databases,\nor moving an Android data layer into `commonMain`.\n\n## Ktor client\n\n### Engines\n\n| Platform | Engine | Artifact |\n|---|---|---|\n| Android | OkHttp (or the `Android` engine) | `io.ktor:ktor-client-okhttp` |\n| iOS | Darwin (uses `NSURLSession`) | `io.ktor:ktor-client-darwin` |\n\n```kotlin\n// commonMain: the client is built from an injected engine\nfun createHttpClient(engine: HttpClientEngine, json: Json, tokens: TokenProvider) = HttpClient(engine) {\n    expectSuccess = true\n    install(ContentNegotiation) { json(json) }\n    install(HttpTimeout) {\n        connectTimeoutMillis = 10_000\n        requestTimeoutMillis = 15_000\n    }\n    install(Auth) {\n        bearer {\n            loadTokens { tokens.current()?.let { BearerTokens(it.access, it.refresh) } }\n            refreshTokens { tokens.refresh()?.let { BearerTokens(it.access, it.refresh) } }\n        }\n    }\n    install(Logging) {\n        level = if (isDebugBuild) LogLevel.HEADERS else LogLevel.NONE\n        sanitizeHeader { it == HttpHeaders.Authorization }\n    }\n    defaultRequest { url(\"https://api.example.com/\") }\n}\n\n// androidMain\nfun platformEngine(): HttpClientEngine = OkHttp.create()\n\n// iosMain\nfun platformEngine(): HttpClientEngine = Darwin.create()\n```\n\n- Pass the engine through DI (or an `expect fun platformEngine()`) so tests can use `MockEngine` from\n  `io.ktor:ktor-client-mock`.\n- Without an engine argument, `HttpClient()` picks one from the engine dependencies on the classpath.\n  Recent Ktor 3 releases also ship `ktor-client-engine-defaults` for `commonMain`; check the Ktor version\n  before relying on it. Explicit engines are easier to configure and review.\n- `isDebugBuild` is the project's own flag (for example from an `expect val` or injected config).\n- The `Auth` plugin needs `io.ktor:ktor-client-auth`.\n\n### Transport security\n\n- HTTPS only. On iOS, App Transport Security blocks cleartext unless `Info.plist` has exceptions; never\n  add `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads`. On Android, keep `usesCleartextTraffic` off in the app manifest.\n- Certificate pinning is configured per engine: OkHttp `CertificatePinner` in the OkHttp engine config,\n  and the Darwin engine's challenge handling or certificate pinner on iOS. Pin keys, not leaf certificates,\n  and ship a backup pin. Escalate pinning decisions to `mobile-security`.\n- Tokens live in Keystore-backed storage on Android and the Keychain on iOS, behind a common interface.\n  Never in `commonMain` constants, `BuildConfig` or `Info.plist`.\n\n### Errors\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: swallows cancellation and hides the failure type\nsuspend fun readings(): List<Reading> = runCatching { api.readings() }.getOrDefault(emptyList())\n\n// GOOD: map transport errors to domain errors in one place\nsuspend fun readings(): ReadingsResult = try {\n    ReadingsResult.Ok(client.get(\"readings\").body<List<ReadingDto>>().map { it.toDomain() })\n} catch (e: CancellationException) {\n    throw e\n} catch (e: ClientRequestException) {      // 4xx with expectSuccess = true\n    ReadingsResult.Rejected(e.response.status.value)\n} catch (e: ServerResponseException) {     // 5xx\n    ReadingsResult.ServerError\n} catch (e: HttpRequestTimeoutException) {\n    ReadingsResult.Timeout\n}\n```\n\n- Network failure types differ between OkHttp and Darwin. Map the remaining platform IO failures in one\n  shared helper so callers and Swift see a sealed result, not engine-specific exceptions.\n- Anything that still escapes to Swift needs `@Throws` on the Swift-facing function.\n\n## kotlinx.serialization\n\n```kotlin\nval appJson = Json {\n    ignoreUnknownKeys = true   // new server fields do not break old clients\n    coerceInputValues = true   // unknown enum value or null for non-null field falls back to the default\n}\n\n@Serializable\ndata class ReadingDto(\n    @SerialName(\"device_id\") val deviceId: String,\n    val value: Double,\n    val unit: UnitDto = UnitDto.UNKNOWN,\n)\n\n@Serializable\nenum class UnitDto { @SerialName(\"c\") CELSIUS, @SerialName(\"f\") FAHRENHEIT, UNKNOWN }\n\n@Serializable\nsealed interface EventDto {\n    @Serializable @SerialName(\"reading\") data class Reading(val value: Double) : EventDto\n    @Serializable @SerialName(\"alarm\") data class Alarm(val code: Int) : EventDto\n}\n```\n\n- The `type` field is the default class discriminator for sealed hierarchies; change it with\n  `classDiscriminator` in the `Json` builder if the API uses another name.\n- DTOs stay in the data layer. Map to domain types with value classes for IDs.\n- The serialization plugin generates serializers at compile time. Still run a minified Android release\n  build before shipping, since custom keep rules elsewhere can strip generated classes.\n- Payloads with device identifiers or location are personal data. Do not log bodies.\n\n## Room KMP\n\nRoom 3 (`androidx.room3`) is the KMP-focused major version: KSP-only, coroutines-only, backed by\n`SQLiteDriver`. Projects already on Room 2.7+ in KMP can stay there and migrate later; both can coexist.\n\n~~~toml\n[versions]\nroom3 = \"<room3>\"\nsqlite = \"<androidx sqlite>\"\nksp = \"<ksp matching kotlin>\"\n\n[libraries]\nandroidx-room3-runtime = { module = \"androidx.room3:room3-runtime\", version.ref = \"room3\" }\nandroidx-room3-compiler = { module = \"androidx.room3:room3-compiler\", version.ref = \"room3\" }\nandroidx-sqlite-bundled = { module = \"androidx.sqlite:sqlite-bundled\", version.ref = \"sqlite\" }\n\n[plugins]\nksp = { id = \"com.google.devtools.ksp\", version.ref = \"ksp\" }\nandroidx-room3 = { id = \"androidx.room3\", version.ref = \"room3\" }\n~~~\n\n```kotlin\n// shared/build.gradle.kts\nplugins {\n    alias(libs.plugins.ksp)\n    alias(libs.plugins.androidx.room3)\n}\nkotlin {\n    sourceSets.commonMain.dependencies {\n        implementation(libs.androidx.room3.runtime)\n        implementation(libs.androidx.sqlite.bundled)\n    }\n}\ndependencies {\n    add(\"kspAndroid\", libs.androidx.room3.compiler)\n    add(\"kspIosArm64\", libs.androidx.room3.compiler)\n    add(\"kspIosSimulatorArm64\", libs.androidx.room3.compiler)\n}\nroom {\n    schemaDirectory(\"\$projectDir/schemas\")\n}\n```\n\n```kotlin\n// commonMain\n@Database(entities = [ReadingEntity::class], version = 1)\n@ConstructedBy(AppDatabaseConstructor::class)\nabstract class AppDatabase : RoomDatabase() {\n    abstract fun readingDao(): ReadingDao\n}\n\n// The Room compiler generates the actual implementations\n@Suppress(\"KotlinNoActualForExpect\")\nexpect object AppDatabaseConstructor : RoomDatabaseConstructor<AppDatabase> {\n    override fun initialize(): AppDatabase\n}\n\n@Dao\ninterface ReadingDao {\n    @Upsert suspend fun upsert(reading: ReadingEntity)\n    @Query(\"SELECT * FROM ReadingEntity ORDER BY ts DESC\") fun observeAll(): Flow<List<ReadingEntity>>\n}\n\nfun buildDatabase(builder: RoomDatabase.Builder<AppDatabase>): AppDatabase =\n    builder.setDriver(BundledSQLiteDriver()).build()\n```\n\n```kotlin\n// androidMain\nfun databaseBuilder(context: Context): RoomDatabase.Builder<AppDatabase> {\n    val appContext = context.applicationContext\n    return Room.databaseBuilder<AppDatabase>(\n        context = appContext,\n        name = appContext.getDatabasePath(\"app.db\").absolutePath,\n    )\n}\n\n// iosMain\nfun databaseBuilder(): RoomDatabase.Builder<AppDatabase> {\n    val dir = NSFileManager.defaultManager.URLForDirectory(\n        directory = NSDocumentDirectory,\n        inDomain = NSUserDomainMask,\n        appropriateForURL = null,\n        create = false,\n        error = null,\n    )\n    return Room.databaseBuilder<AppDatabase>(name = requireNotNull(dir?.path) + \"/app.db\")\n}\n```\n\n- DAO functions in common code are `suspend` or return `Flow`. No `LiveData`, no blocking DAOs.\n- `BundledSQLiteDriver` gives the same SQLite version everywhere. `NativeSQLiteDriver` uses the system\n  SQLite on iOS and needs `-lsqlite3` linker options.\n- The default query context is `Dispatchers.IO`. Configure another through the builder only with a reason.\n- Transactions use `useWriterConnection { it.immediateTransaction { ... } }`; migrations and callbacks\n  receive an `SQLiteConnection`, not `SupportSQLiteDatabase`.\n- Not available in KMP: pre-packaged databases (`createFromAsset` and friends), query callbacks, auto-close\n  timeout, multi-instance invalidation. Plan around them before migrating.\n- Minified Android builds need `-keep class * extends androidx.room3.RoomDatabase { <init>(); }`.\n- Personal data at rest: the iOS file lands in Documents (backed up to iCloud by default). Decide on file\n  protection and backup exclusion with `mobile-security`.\n\n## SQLDelight\n\n```kotlin\n// shared/build.gradle.kts\nplugins {\n    alias(libs.plugins.sqldelight) // app.cash.sqldelight\n}\nkotlin {\n    sourceSets {\n        commonMain.dependencies { implementation(libs.sqldelight.coroutines.extensions) }\n        androidMain.dependencies { implementation(libs.sqldelight.android.driver) }\n        iosMain.dependencies { implementation(libs.sqldelight.native.driver) }\n    }\n}\nsqldelight {\n    databases {\n        register(\"AppDatabase\") {\n            packageName.set(\"com.example.shared.db\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```sql\n-- src/commonMain/sqldelight/com/example/shared/db/Reading.sq\nCREATE TABLE reading (\n    id TEXT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY,\n    device_id TEXT NOT NULL,\n    value REAL NOT NULL,\n    ts INTEGER NOT NULL\n);\n\nselectByDevice:\nSELECT * FROM reading WHERE device_id = ? ORDER BY ts DESC;\n\nupsert:\nINSERT OR REPLACE INTO reading VALUES ?;\n```\n\n```kotlin\n// androidMain\nfun sqlDriver(context: Context): SqlDriver = AndroidSqliteDriver(AppDatabase.Schema, context, \"app.db\")\n// iosMain\nfun sqlDriver(): SqlDriver = NativeSqliteDriver(AppDatabase.Schema, \"app.db\")\n\n// commonMain\nfun observeReadings(db: AppDatabase, deviceId: String, io: CoroutineDispatcher): Flow<List<Reading>> =\n    db.readingQueries.selectByDevice(deviceId).asFlow().mapToList(io)\n```\n\n- Schema changes go in numbered `.sqm` migration files next to the `.sq` files. Turn on migration\n  verification in the database block (check the property name for the SQLDelight version in use) and\n  run it in CI.\n- SQL is checked at compile time, and both teams can review the schema in plain SQL.\n\n## Choosing between them\n\n| Question | Room KMP | SQLDelight |\n|---|---|---|\n| Team background | Android, Room annotations | SQL-first, mixed teams |\n| Existing Android Room database | Keep schema and migrations | Rewrite schema in `.sq`, port migrations |\n| Code generation | KSP, one entry per target | Gradle plugin, no KSP |\n| Query style | Annotated DAO methods | Named SQL statements generate Kotlin APIs |\n| Pre-packaged database | Not available in KMP | Check driver support before relying on it |\n\nEither way: one database instance per process, created in the app graph, and a migration test for every\nversion bump.\n",
    "references/ios-interop.md":
        "# iOS interop reference\n\nLoad this when designing the API Swift sees, choosing or configuring the coroutine bridge, consuming a\nshared ViewModel from SwiftUI, or evaluating Swift export.\n\n## How Kotlin reaches Swift today\n\nThe default path is Objective-C export: the Kotlin compiler produces an Apple framework with an\nObjective-C header, and Swift imports that header. Swift export (direct Kotlin to Swift) is Alpha.\n\n| Kotlin | Seen from Swift through Objective-C export | What to do |\n|---|---|---|\n| `suspend fun` | Completion handler; `async` from Swift 5.5 is \"highly experimental\" | Use SKIE (or KMP-NativeCoroutines) |\n| `Flow<T>`, `StateFlow<T>` | `Flow` interface, element type lost | Use SKIE (or KMP-NativeCoroutines) |\n| Generic class `Box<T>` | Objective-C generic class, some information lost | Use `T : Any` bound to keep non-null |\n| Generic interface or function | Generics dropped | Concrete facade types |\n| `enum class` | Class; `switch` needs `default` | SKIE turns it into a Swift enum |\n| `sealed class` / `sealed interface` | Class hierarchy; cast with `as?` | SKIE `onEnum(of:)` |\n| Default arguments | Not exported; every argument required | Explicit overloads or a parameter object |\n| `value class` / inline class | Underlying primitive type or `id` | Keep out of Swift-facing signatures |\n| `object`, `companion object` | Class with `shared` / `companion` property | `MyObject.shared.x` |\n| Top-level function in `Devices.kt` | Static on `DevicesKt` | Group Swift-facing API in classes |\n| `Int`, `Long` | `Int32`, `Int64`; `KotlinInt` when nullable or generic | Avoid nullable primitives in Swift API |\n| `List`, `Map`, `Set` | `Array`, `Dictionary`, `Set` | Do not subclass collection interfaces |\n| Function type `(Int) -> Unit` | Block returning `KotlinUnit` | Prefer `suspend` or a small interface |\n| Kotlin exception | Terminates the process unless declared with `@Throws` | See below |\n\n## Exceptions\n\n```kotlin\n// GOOD: non-suspend function, declared exception types become a Swift `throws`\n@Throws(FrameException::class)\nfun decode(bytes: ByteArray): Frame\n\n// GOOD: suspend functions must include CancellationException (or a supertype) in the list\n@Throws(DeviceException::class, CancellationException::class)\nsuspend fun connect(id: String): DeviceSession\n```\n\n- Exceptions not listed in `@Throws` that reach Swift are unhandled and terminate the app.\n- Non-suspend functions without `@Throws` propagate no exception at all.\n- Suspend functions without `@Throws` propagate only `CancellationException` as an `NSError`.\n- Swift errors thrown from Swift code are not translated into Kotlin exceptions. A Swift implementation\n  of a Kotlin interface reports failure through a return value or callback.\n- Keep `@Throws` on Swift-facing functions even with SKIE; it documents the contract and keeps\n  behaviour correct if the bridge changes.\n\n## Naming and visibility\n\n```kotlin\n@ObjCName(swiftName = \"DeviceClient\")\nclass KmpDeviceClient {\n    @ObjCName(\"connect\")\n    fun connectTo(@ObjCName(\"id\") deviceId: String) { /* ... */ }\n}\n\n@HiddenFromObjC          // visible to other Kotlin modules, hidden from Swift\nfun internalDebugDump(): String = \"\"\n\n@ShouldRefineInSwift     // exported with a __ prefix so Swift can wrap it in a nicer API\nfun rawReadings(): List<Long> = emptyList()\n```\n\n- `@ObjCName` needs the `kotlin.experimental.ExperimentalObjCName` opt-in.\n- Prefer `internal` for anything Swift does not call. Every public declaration in an exported module\n  becomes part of the framework header.\n\n## Interfaces implemented in Swift\n\nPassing a Swift implementation into Kotlin is a clean way to keep CoreBluetooth, Keychain or HealthKit\ncode in Swift.\n\n```kotlin\n// commonMain: callback-based, no generics, no suspend, easy to conform to from Swift\ninterface KeyValueVault {\n    fun write(key: String, value: ByteArray): Boolean\n    fun read(key: String): ByteArray?\n}\n```\n\n```swift\nfinal class KeychainVault: KeyValueVault {\n    func write(key: String, value: KotlinByteArray) -> Bool { /* SecItemAdd / SecItemUpdate */ }\n    func read(key: String) -> KotlinByteArray? { /* SecItemCopyMatching */ }\n}\n```\n\n- Kotlin interfaces become Objective-C protocols. Suspend members become completion-handler\n  requirements, which are awkward to implement; bridge to `suspend` on the Kotlin side with\n  `suspendCancellableCoroutine` instead.\n- `ByteArray` arrives as `KotlinByteArray`. For large payloads, convert once at the boundary rather\n  than per element.\n- Threading: the Kotlin caller decides the thread. Swift implementations must be safe to call off the\n  main thread, or the Kotlin side switches to `Dispatchers.Main` first.\n\n## Coroutine bridge: SKIE vs KMP-NativeCoroutines\n\n| | SKIE | KMP-NativeCoroutines |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mechanism | Compiler plugin that modifies the produced framework and adds generated Swift | Compiler plugin plus Swift packages; generates native wrappers for annotated declarations |\n| Kotlin-side changes | None | `@NativeCoroutines` / `@NativeCoroutinesState` on each exposed declaration |\n| Suspend | Native Swift `async`, two-way cancellation, callable from any thread | `asyncFunction(for:)` wrapper, cancellation via the Swift `Task` |\n| Flow | `SkieSwiftFlow`, `SkieSwiftStateFlow`: typed `AsyncSequence` | `asyncSequence(for:)`, Combine publisher or RxSwift observable |\n| Sealed, enums | Exhaustive Swift switch (`onEnum(of:)`), Swift enums | Not covered |\n| Default arguments | Opt-in, at most 5 per function, interface methods unsupported | Not covered |\n| Kotlin version support | Each SKIE release supports a fixed Kotlin range; can lag new Kotlin releases | Also version-matched; releases have tracked new Kotlin versions closely |\n| Swift side dependency | None | `KMPNativeCoroutinesAsync` (or Combine, RxSwift) via SPM or CocoaPods |\n| Swift export | Not documented | Compatibility mode exists; native wrappers not supported under Swift export |\n\nRecommendation: SKIE for app projects. It fixes suspend, Flow, sealed classes and enums for the whole\nframework with no Kotlin annotations, so shared code stays idiomatic and the iOS team gets native\ntypes. The cost is a hard dependency on SKIE supporting the project's Kotlin version.\n\nPick KMP-NativeCoroutines when:\n- the project must adopt a Kotlin release SKIE does not support yet, and that cannot wait;\n- only coroutines need bridging and the team wants explicit, opt-in API exposure;\n- the framework is an SDK for external apps that may not accept a framework-modifying plugin.\n\nNever use both in one framework. Switching later touches every Swift call site, so decide once.\n\n## SKIE setup and usage\n\n```kotlin\n// Only in the module that builds the Xcode framework\nplugins {\n    alias(libs.plugins.skie) // id \"co.touchlab.skie\"\n}\n```\n\n- SKIE processes everything exported from that framework, including exported dependencies.\n- Before every Kotlin upgrade, check the supported Kotlin range in the SKIE documentation.\n\n```swift\n// suspend: plain async call, Task cancellation cancels the coroutine\nlet session = try await facade.connect(id: deviceId)\n\n// Flow / StateFlow: typed AsyncSequence\nfor await readings in viewModel.readings {\n    self.readings = readings\n}\n\n// sealed class: exhaustive switch over the generated enum, no default branch\n@ViewBuilder\nfunc content(for state: DevicesUiState) -> some View {\n    switch onEnum(of: state) {\n    case .loading: ProgressView()\n    case .content(let content): DeviceList(devices: content.devices)\n    case .error(let error): ErrorView(reason: error.reason)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- A cancelled Kotlin suspend function surfaces as `CancellationError` in Swift.\n- Swift `async` functions may run off the main thread. Mark the Swift model `@MainActor` and update UI\n  state there.\n- Generic Kotlin classes need SKIE's `skie(_:)` wrapper for their suspend members.\n\n## KMP-NativeCoroutines setup and usage\n\n```kotlin\nplugins {\n    id(\"com.rickclephas.kmp.nativecoroutines\") version \"<version matching kotlin>\"\n}\nkotlin.sourceSets.all {\n    languageSettings.optIn(\"kotlin.experimental.ExperimentalObjCName\")\n}\n\nclass DevicesViewModel(private val repo: DeviceRepository) : ViewModel() {\n    @NativeCoroutinesState\n    val state: StateFlow<DevicesUiState> = repo.observe()\n        .stateIn(viewModelScope, SharingStarted.Eagerly, DevicesUiState.Loading)\n\n    @NativeCoroutines\n    suspend fun refresh(): Unit = repo.refresh()\n}\n```\n\n```swift\nimport KMPNativeCoroutinesAsync\n\nlet task = Task {\n    do {\n        for try await state in asyncSequence(for: viewModel.stateFlow) { self.state = state }\n    } catch {\n        self.failure = error\n    }\n}\n// cancelling the task cancels collection\ntry await asyncFunction(for: viewModel.refresh())\n```\n\n- Keep the Swift package version identical to the Kotlin plugin version.\n- Unannotated exposed suspend functions and Flows produce a warning; raise it with\n  `nativeCoroutines { exposedSeverity = ExposedSeverity.ERROR }`.\n\n## Shared ViewModel from SwiftUI\n\nThe AndroidX ViewModel needs a `ViewModelStore` on iOS so `onCleared` runs and `viewModelScope` is\ncancelled.\n\n```kotlin\n// iosMain\n@BetaInteropApi\n@Throws(IllegalArgumentException::class)\nfun ViewModelStore.resolveViewModel(\n    modelClass: ObjCClass,\n    factory: ViewModelProvider.Factory,\n    key: String?,\n    extras: CreationExtras? = null,\n): ViewModel {\n    @Suppress(\"UNCHECKED_CAST\")\n    val vmClass = getOriginalKotlinClass(modelClass) as? KClass<ViewModel>\n    require(vmClass != null) { \"The modelClass parameter must be a ViewModel type.\" }\n    val provider = ViewModelProvider.Companion.create(this, factory, extras ?: CreationExtras.Empty)\n    return key?.let { provider[key, vmClass] } ?: provider[vmClass]\n}\n```\n\n```swift\nfinal class IosViewModelStoreOwner: ObservableObject, ViewModelStoreOwner {\n    let viewModelStore = ViewModelStore()\n\n    func viewModel<T: ViewModel>(\n        key: String? = nil,\n        factory: ViewModelProviderFactory,\n        extras: CreationExtras? = nil\n    ) -> T {\n        do {\n            return try viewModelStore.resolveViewModel(\n                modelClass: T.self, factory: factory, key: key, extras: extras\n            ) as! T\n        } catch {\n            fatalError(\"Failed to create ViewModel of type \\(T.self)\")\n        }\n    }\n\n    deinit { viewModelStore.clear() }\n}\n\nstruct DevicesScreen: View {\n    @StateObject private var owner = IosViewModelStoreOwner()\n\n    var body: some View {\n        let vm: DevicesViewModel = owner.viewModel(factory: DevicesViewModelKt.devicesViewModelFactory)\n        DevicesContent(viewModel: vm)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Add `api(libs.androidx.lifecycle.viewmodel)` and `export(libs.androidx.lifecycle.viewmodel)` so\n  Swift sees `ViewModel`, `ViewModelStore` and `ViewModelStoreOwner` without package prefixes.\n- The `as!` and `fatalError` mirror the documented sample: a wrong type is a programming error. Keep\n  them out of any code path driven by user input.\n- One store owner per screen. A store held at app level keeps every ViewModel alive forever.\n\n## Swift export (Alpha)\n\n```kotlin\nkotlin {\n    iosArm64()\n    iosSimulatorArm64()\n    swiftExport {\n        moduleName = \"Shared\"\n        flattenPackage = \"com.example.shared\"\n    }\n}\n```\n\nXcode build phase: `./gradlew :shared:embedSwiftExportForXcode` instead of\n`embedAndSignAppleFrameworkForXcode`.\n\n- Maps suspend to `async`, Flow to `AsyncSequence` (`asAsyncSequence()`), sealed hierarchies to Swift\n  enums, Kotlin enums to Swift enums.\n- Limits: direct integration only; generics erased to upper bounds; only final classes that inherit\n  directly from `Any`; types inheriting `List`, `Set` or `Map` are ignored; breaking changes expected.\n- KMP-NativeCoroutines native wrappers do not work under Swift export.\n- Use it in a spike branch to track progress. Do not ship production apps on it until it leaves Alpha.\n",
    "references/structure-and-gradle.md":
        "# Structure and Gradle reference\n\nLoad this when creating or editing `settings.gradle.kts`, `libs.versions.toml`, the shared module's\n`build.gradle.kts`, the Android app module, the Xcode build phase, or XCFramework and Swift package\npublishing.\n\n## Module layout\n\n~~~text\nroot/\n  settings.gradle.kts\n  gradle/libs.versions.toml\n  shared/                     # KMP library: com.android.kotlin.multiplatform.library\n    src/commonMain/kotlin\n    src/commonTest/kotlin\n    src/androidMain/kotlin\n    src/androidHostTest/kotlin\n    src/iosMain/kotlin\n    src/iosTest/kotlin\n  androidApp/                 # com.android.application, MainActivity, manifest, app DI root\n  iosApp/                     # Xcode project, SwiftUI app, iOS DI root\n~~~\n\n- Larger codebases split `shared` into feature and core modules and keep one umbrella module that\n  produces the iOS framework and `export`s what Swift needs.\n- From AGP 9 the KMP plugin cannot share a module with `com.android.application` or\n  `com.android.library`. The Android entry point always lives in its own app module.\n\n## Source sets from the default hierarchy template\n\nThe Kotlin Gradle plugin applies the default hierarchy template automatically. With\n`iosArm64()` and `iosSimulatorArm64()` declared, these source sets exist and have type-safe accessors:\n\n| Source set | Compiles for | Use for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `commonMain` | every target | domain, data, ViewModels, interfaces |\n| `nativeMain` | all Kotlin/Native targets | rarely needed in a mobile app |\n| `appleMain` | all Apple targets | code shared with macOS or watchOS targets |\n| `iosMain` | `iosArm64`, `iosSimulatorArm64` | iOS implementations, `ComposeUIViewController` |\n| `androidMain` | Android target | Android implementations |\n\n- Explicit `dependsOn` calls cancel the template and produce a warning. Remove them instead of adding\n  `kotlin.mpp.applyDefaultHierarchyTemplate=false`.\n- `iosX64` is Tier 3 (not in active development). Add it only with a reason.\n\n## Version catalog\n\n~~~toml\n# gradle/libs.versions.toml\n[versions]\nkotlin = \"<kotlin>\"\nagp = \"<agp>\"\nandroid-compileSdk = \"36\"\nandroid-minSdk = \"26\"\ncoroutines = \"<kotlinx-coroutines>\"\nserialization = \"<kotlinx-serialization>\"\nktor = \"<ktor 3.x>\"\nlifecycle = \"<androidx lifecycle>\"\ncompose-multiplatform = \"<compose-multiplatform>\"\nskie = \"<skie>\"\nturbine = \"<turbine>\"\n\n[libraries]\nkotlinx-coroutines-core = { module = \"org.jetbrains.kotlinx:kotlinx-coroutines-core\", version.ref = \"coroutines\" }\nkotlinx-coroutines-test = { module = \"org.jetbrains.kotlinx:kotlinx-coroutines-test\", version.ref = \"coroutines\" }\nkotlinx-serialization-json = { module = \"org.jetbrains.kotlinx:kotlinx-serialization-json\", version.ref = \"serialization\" }\nktor-client-core = { module = \"io.ktor:ktor-client-core\", version.ref = \"ktor\" }\nktor-client-okhttp = { module = \"io.ktor:ktor-client-okhttp\", version.ref = \"ktor\" }\nktor-client-darwin = { module = \"io.ktor:ktor-client-darwin\", version.ref = \"ktor\" }\nktor-client-content-negotiation = { module = \"io.ktor:ktor-client-content-negotiation\", version.ref = \"ktor\" }\nktor-client-logging = { module = \"io.ktor:ktor-client-logging\", version.ref = \"ktor\" }\nktor-serialization-kotlinx-json = { module = \"io.ktor:ktor-serialization-kotlinx-json\", version.ref = \"ktor\" }\nandroidx-lifecycle-viewmodel = { module = \"androidx.lifecycle:lifecycle-viewmodel\", version.ref = \"lifecycle\" }\nkotlin-test = { module = \"org.jetbrains.kotlin:kotlin-test\", version.ref = \"kotlin\" }\nturbine = { module = \"app.cash.turbine:turbine\", version.ref = \"turbine\" }\n\n[plugins]\nkotlin-multiplatform = { id = \"org.jetbrains.kotlin.multiplatform\", version.ref = \"kotlin\" }\nkotlin-serialization = { id = \"org.jetbrains.kotlin.plugin.serialization\", version.ref = \"kotlin\" }\nkotlin-compose = { id = \"org.jetbrains.kotlin.plugin.compose\", version.ref = \"kotlin\" }\ncompose-multiplatform = { id = \"org.jetbrains.compose\", version.ref = \"compose-multiplatform\" }\nandroid-application = { id = \"com.android.application\", version.ref = \"agp\" }\nandroid-kotlin-multiplatform-library = { id = \"com.android.kotlin.multiplatform.library\", version.ref = \"agp\" }\nskie = { id = \"co.touchlab.skie\", version.ref = \"skie\" }\n~~~\n\n- Keep the project's existing versions. Never invent or bump a version without saying so.\n- Declare every plugin in the root `build.gradle.kts` with `apply false`, including\n  `alias(libs.plugins.android.kotlin.multiplatform.library) apply false`.\n- Typed project accessors (`projects.shared`) need\n  `enableFeaturePreview(\"TYPESAFE_PROJECT_ACCESSORS\")` in `settings.gradle.kts`.\n\n## Shared module\n\n```kotlin\n// shared/build.gradle.kts\nimport org.jetbrains.kotlin.gradle.plugin.mpp.apple.XCFramework\n\nplugins {\n    alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.multiplatform)\n    alias(libs.plugins.android.kotlin.multiplatform.library)\n    alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.serialization)\n    alias(libs.plugins.skie)\n}\n\nkotlin {\n    android {\n        namespace = \"com.example.shared\"\n        compileSdk = libs.versions.android.compileSdk.get().toInt()\n        minSdk = libs.versions.android.minSdk.get().toInt()\n        withHostTest { }\n    }\n\n    val xcf = XCFramework(\"Shared\")\n    listOf(iosArm64(), iosSimulatorArm64()).forEach { target ->\n        target.binaries.framework {\n            baseName = \"Shared\"\n            isStatic = true\n            export(libs.androidx.lifecycle.viewmodel)\n            xcf.add(this)\n        }\n    }\n\n    sourceSets {\n        commonMain.dependencies {\n            implementation(libs.kotlinx.coroutines.core)\n            implementation(libs.kotlinx.serialization.json)\n            implementation(libs.ktor.client.core)\n            implementation(libs.ktor.client.content.negotiation)\n            implementation(libs.ktor.client.logging)\n            implementation(libs.ktor.serialization.kotlinx.json)\n            api(libs.androidx.lifecycle.viewmodel)\n        }\n        androidMain.dependencies { implementation(libs.ktor.client.okhttp) }\n        iosMain.dependencies { implementation(libs.ktor.client.darwin) }\n        commonTest.dependencies {\n            implementation(libs.kotlin.test)\n            implementation(libs.kotlinx.coroutines.test)\n            implementation(libs.turbine)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- The Android-KMP library plugin has a single variant: no build types or flavors in the shared module.\n  Tests are opt-in (`withHostTest`, `withDeviceTest`), creating `androidHostTest` and\n  `androidDeviceTest` source sets.\n- Without build variants, `debugImplementation(...)` in the shared module becomes\n  `androidRuntimeClasspath(...)`.\n- On AGP older than 8.12 the block is `androidLibrary { }` instead of `android { }`.\n- `export(...)` only works for `api` dependencies. Export is non-transitive by default; keep it that way.\n- Expect/actual classes print a Beta warning. Only if the project deliberately uses them:\n  `compilerOptions { freeCompilerArgs.add(\"-Xexpect-actual-classes\") }`.\n\n## Android app module\n\n```kotlin\n// androidApp/build.gradle.kts\nplugins {\n    alias(libs.plugins.android.application)\n    alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.compose) // only if the app uses Compose\n}\n\nandroid {\n    namespace = \"com.example.app\"\n    compileSdk = libs.versions.android.compileSdk.get().toInt()\n    defaultConfig {\n        applicationId = \"com.example.app\"\n        minSdk = libs.versions.android.minSdk.get().toInt()\n        targetSdk = 36\n    }\n}\n\ndependencies {\n    implementation(projects.shared)\n}\n```\n\n- AGP 9 has built-in Kotlin support: do not apply `org.jetbrains.kotlin.android` in the app module.\n- The shared module's `namespace` must differ from the app's.\n- Hilt, if the Android app uses it, stays in this module and binds the shared interfaces.\n\n## AGP 9 migration for an existing KMP project\n\n1. Create `androidApp/` with its own `build.gradle.kts`; add `include(\":androidApp\")`.\n2. Move the Android entry point (`src/androidMain` of the old app module) to `androidApp/src/main`. Keep\n   expect/actual declarations and shared Android implementations in the shared module.\n3. In the shared module replace `com.android.library` (or `com.android.application`) with\n   `com.android.kotlin.multiplatform.library`, and replace `androidTarget { }` plus the top-level\n   `android { }` block with `kotlin { android { } }`.\n4. Remove `org.jetbrains.kotlin.android` from the app module.\n5. Build both apps and run all tests.\n\n- `android.enableLegacyVariantApi=true` in `gradle.properties` keeps the old APIs working until AGP 10.\n  It is a temporary bridge only; call it out if added.\n- Gradle and IDE minimums change with AGP 9. Check them before starting and ask before upgrading.\n\n## Compose Multiplatform\n\n```kotlin\n// shared or composeApp module that holds shared UI\nplugins {\n    alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.multiplatform)\n    alias(libs.plugins.android.kotlin.multiplatform.library)\n    alias(libs.plugins.compose.multiplatform)\n    alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.compose)\n}\n\nkotlin {\n    android { /* namespace, compileSdk, minSdk */ androidResources { enable = true } }\n    sourceSets {\n        commonMain.dependencies {\n            implementation(libs.compose.runtime)     // org.jetbrains.compose.runtime:runtime\n            implementation(libs.compose.foundation)  // org.jetbrains.compose.foundation:foundation\n            implementation(libs.compose.material3)   // org.jetbrains.compose.material3:material3\n            implementation(libs.lifecycle.viewmodel.compose)\n            // org.jetbrains.androidx.lifecycle:lifecycle-viewmodel-compose\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- The `compose.runtime`-style aliases from the Compose Gradle plugin are deprecated since 1.10. Use direct\n  coordinates; the deprecation warnings name the replacement for each alias.\n- Material3 for Compose Multiplatform is versioned separately from runtime and foundation. Check both.\n- Android resolves the JetBrains artifacts to the matching `androidx.compose` artifacts through Gradle\n  module metadata.\n- Info.plist: `CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone` = `true` is required by Compose Multiplatform\n  rendering on iOS. Without it the app crashes at runtime.\n\n## Xcode direct integration (monorepo)\n\nRun Script build phase in the iOS app target, placed before Compile Sources:\n\n```sh\nif [ \"YES\" = \"\$OVERRIDE_KOTLIN_BUILD_IDE_SUPPORTED\" ]; then\n  echo \"Skipping Gradle build task invocation due to OVERRIDE_KOTLIN_BUILD_IDE_SUPPORTED environment variable set to \\\"YES\\\"\"\n  exit 0\nfi\ncd \"\$SRCROOT/..\"\n./gradlew :shared:embedAndSignAppleFrameworkForXcode\n```\n\n- The task exists only when `binaries.framework` is declared for the iOS targets.\n- Turn off \"Based on dependency analysis\" for the phase and disable User Script Sandboxing in Build\n  Options. If a build ran sandboxed, stop the Gradle daemon (`./gradlew --stop`).\n- Custom build configurations need a user-defined `KOTLIN_FRAMEWORK_BUILD_TYPE` set to `Debug` or\n  `Release`.\n- These are Xcode project changes the iOS owner must review. List them in the summary.\n\n## XCFramework as a remote Swift package\n\n```sh\n./gradlew :shared:assembleSharedXCFramework\n# output: shared/build/XCFrameworks/release/Shared.xcframework\n# per build type: assembleSharedDebugXCFramework, assembleSharedReleaseXCFramework\n\ncd shared/build/XCFrameworks/release\nzip -r Shared.xcframework.zip Shared.xcframework\nswift package compute-checksum Shared.xcframework.zip\n```\n\nUpload the zip to storage with a direct download URL (for example a release asset), then publish this\nmanifest from a separate repository and tag it with a semantic version:\n\n```swift\n// swift-tools-version:5.3\nimport PackageDescription\n\nlet package = Package(\n    name: \"Shared\",\n    platforms: [.iOS(.v14)],\n    products: [.library(name: \"Shared\", targets: [\"Shared\"])],\n    targets: [\n        .binaryTarget(\n            name: \"Shared\",\n            url: \"<direct link to Shared.xcframework.zip>\",\n            checksum: \"<output of swift package compute-checksum>\"\n        )\n    ]\n)\n```\n\n- Validate before tagging: `swift package reset && swift package show-dependencies --format json`.\n- Kotlin 2.5.0 preview releases generate `Package.swift` next to the XCFramework. Check the Kotlin\n  version in use before relying on it.\n- Importing Swift packages into Kotlin code (`swiftPMDependencies {}`) is Alpha and needs Kotlin 2.5.0\n  previews. Do not adopt it in production builds without agreement.\n- The XCFramework zip contains compiled code only. Do not bundle API keys or endpoints that differ per\n  customer into it.\n",
    "references/testing.md":
        "# Testing reference\n\nLoad this when writing tests for shared code, choosing between fakes and mocks in KMP, or running tests on\nthe Android and iOS targets.\n\n## Source sets and dependencies\n\n| Source set | Runs on | Use for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `commonTest` | every target (JVM for Android host tests, Kotlin/Native runner for iOS) | domain, repositories, ViewModels, serialization |\n| `androidHostTest` | JVM (enable with `withHostTest { }` in `kotlin { android { } }`) | Android implementations, JVM-only tools such as MockK |\n| `androidDeviceTest` | device or emulator (`withDeviceTest { }`) | Android-specific database or platform tests |\n| `iosTest` | iOS simulator | iOS implementations, Kotlin/Native behaviour |\n\n```kotlin\nkotlin {\n    sourceSets {\n        commonTest.dependencies {\n            implementation(libs.kotlin.test)\n            implementation(libs.kotlinx.coroutines.test)\n            implementation(libs.turbine)\n            implementation(libs.ktor.client.mock)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `kotlin.test` gives `@Test`, `@BeforeTest`, `@AfterTest` and assertions on every target.\n- Turbine (`app.cash.turbine:turbine`) is multiplatform, including iOS targets.\n- MockK is JVM-only. It may be used in `androidHostTest`, never in `commonTest`.\n\n## Fakes over mocks\n\n```kotlin\n// commonTest\nclass FakeDeviceRepository(\n    devices: List<Device> = emptyList(),\n    private val failWith: DeviceError? = null,\n) : DeviceRepository {\n    private val state = MutableStateFlow(devices)\n    val refreshCalls = mutableListOf<Unit>()\n\n    override fun observe(): Flow<List<Device>> = state\n    override suspend fun refresh(): DeviceResult {\n        refreshCalls += Unit\n        return failWith?.let { DeviceResult.Failed(it) } ?: DeviceResult.Ok(state.value)\n    }\n    fun emit(devices: List<Device>) { state.value = devices }\n}\n```\n\n- A fake is a working, in-memory implementation of the interface the production code depends on. It\n  runs on every target and makes the interface design honest.\n- Because platform services are interfaces (see Rule 3 in the skill), every platform dependency in\n  shared code can be faked this way.\n\n## Coroutines and ViewModels\n\n```kotlin\nclass DevicesViewModelTest {\n    private val dispatcher = StandardTestDispatcher()\n\n    @BeforeTest fun setUp() = Dispatchers.setMain(dispatcher)\n    @AfterTest fun tearDown() = Dispatchers.resetMain()\n\n    @Test fun refreshFailureShowsError() = runTest {\n        val repo = FakeDeviceRepository(failWith = DeviceError.Offline)\n        val vm = DevicesViewModel(repo)\n\n        vm.state.test {\n            assertEquals(DevicesUiState.Loading, awaitItem())\n            vm.refresh()\n            assertEquals(DevicesUiState.Error(DeviceError.Offline), awaitItem())\n            cancelAndIgnoreRemainingEvents()\n        }\n        assertEquals(1, repo.refreshCalls.size)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `viewModelScope` uses `Dispatchers.Main.immediate`. Set a test Main dispatcher on every target, or the\n  iOS test can hang or fail with no main loop.\n- With a `TestDispatcher` set as Main, `runTest` shares its scheduler, so virtual time covers the\n  ViewModel's coroutines.\n- Inject the IO dispatcher into repositories and pass `StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler)` in tests.\n- Never `delay` for real or sleep in a test. Use `advanceTimeBy`, `advanceUntilIdle` or Turbine.\n\n## Ktor with MockEngine\n\n```kotlin\n@Test fun mapsServerErrorToResult() = runTest {\n    val engine = MockEngine { request ->\n        assertEquals(\"/readings\", request.url.encodedPath)\n        respond(\n            content = \"\"\"{\"error\":\"down\"}\"\"\",\n            status = HttpStatusCode.ServiceUnavailable,\n            headers = headersOf(HttpHeaders.ContentType, \"application/json\"),\n        )\n    }\n    val api = ReadingsApi(createHttpClient(engine, appJson, FakeTokenProvider()))\n\n    assertEquals(ReadingsResult.ServerError, api.readings())\n}\n```\n\n- The client factory takes an engine parameter so the same configuration (timeouts, auth, JSON) is under\n  test, not a different client.\n- Add serialization tests for every DTO with a captured real payload, including unknown fields and\n  unknown enum values.\n\n## Databases\n\n- Room KMP: build an in-memory database per platform through a test-only `expect fun` in `commonTest`\n  with actuals in `androidDeviceTest` (or host tests set up for Room) and `iosTest`. Use the bundled\n  driver so behaviour matches production.\n- Every schema version bump ships a migration test that opens the old schema, migrates and checks the\n  data.\n- SQLDelight: JVM tests can use `JdbcSqliteDriver(JdbcSqliteDriver.IN_MEMORY)` from\n  `app.cash.sqldelight:sqlite-driver`; iOS tests use `NativeSqliteDriver`.\n\n## Running\n\n```sh\n./gradlew :shared:allTests                  # every target, combined report for common and iOS tests\n./gradlew :shared:iosSimulatorArm64Test     # iOS simulator only\n./gradlew :shared:tasks --group verification # find the Android host test task for the project\n```\n\n- iOS tests need a macOS host with Xcode. CI must run them on a macOS runner; a Linux-only pipeline\n  silently skips the iOS target (see `mobile-ci-cd`).\n- The `allTests` report does not include Android test reports; check both.\n- Swift-side tests (Swift Testing or XCTest in the iOS app) cover the bridge: a Flow is collected and\n  cancelled, a `@Throws` error arrives as a Swift `Error`, a sealed type switch is exhaustive.\n\n## Checklist for a shared change\n\n- [ ] New domain and data logic has `commonTest` tests with fakes\n- [ ] ViewModel tests set a test Main dispatcher and use Turbine\n- [ ] Network code tested through `MockEngine` with the production client configuration\n- [ ] DTO round-trip and unknown-field tests\n- [ ] Migration test for every database version bump\n- [ ] `allTests` passes, iOS simulator included\n",
  },
  "mobile-kotlin": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-kotlin\ndescription: \"Writes idiomatic modern Kotlin for Android: Kotlin 2.x, null safety, sealed and value classes, structured coroutines with injected dispatchers, Flow and StateFlow, Jetpack Compose state and stability, Hilt, Room, KSP, Gradle version catalogs, R8 and tests with MockK and Turbine, plus Android 15 and 16 changes. Use when writing or refactoring Kotlin ViewModels, composables, repositories, DAOs, DI modules or build.gradle.kts files.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Kotlin for Android\n\nWrite Kotlin the way a senior Android engineer would in a current codebase: null-safe, immutable by default,\nstructured concurrency everywhere, Compose that skips work it does not need to do, and a build that is\nreproducible from the version catalog. Prefer the boring, documented API over a clever one.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Writing new Kotlin code in an Android app: ViewModels, use cases, repositories, composables, DAOs, workers.\n- Refactoring Java, callback or RxJava code to coroutines and Flow.\n- Migrating XML screens to Jetpack Compose, kapt to KSP, Groovy Gradle to Kotlin DSL.\n- Fixing recomposition, lifecycle, coroutine-leak or nullability bugs.\n- Updating code for a new `targetSdk` (edge-to-edge, predictive back, foreground service rules).\n\nNot for: architecture choice (use `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`), a security audit (use `mobile-security`),\nlegacy Java maintenance (use `mobile-java`), or reviewing someone else's diff (use `mobile-code-review`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Read the project before writing code.\n   - `gradle/libs.versions.toml`: Kotlin, AGP, Compose BOM, coroutines, Hilt, Room versions.\n   - Module `build.gradle.kts`: `minSdk`, `targetSdk`, `compileSdk`, KSP or kapt, Compose enabled.\n   - Existing conventions: package layout, base classes, dispatcher qualifiers, error types, test style.\n2. Match the existing stack. Do not add a library the project does not use without saying so.\n   Do not change `minSdk`, `targetSdk` or `compileSdk` without asking.\n3. Load the reference for the area being touched:\n   [Coroutines and Flow](references/coroutines-flow.md), [Compose](references/compose.md),\n   [Hilt and Room](references/hilt-room.md), [Build and tooling](references/build.md),\n   [Platform changes](references/platform.md), [Testing](references/testing.md).\n4. Model first: domain types, UI state, events, errors as sealed or data types. Then data layer, then\n   ViewModel, then UI.\n5. Write the code following the Rules below. Keep functions small, `private` by default, and public API\n   explicit about types.\n6. Add or update unit tests for new domain and data logic, and a Compose test for new interactive UI.\n7. Run the build, the tests and the linters (`./gradlew detekt ktlintCheck testDebugUnitTest` or the\n   project's equivalents). Report failures honestly; do not declare done on a red build.\n8. Summarize what changed, any new dependency, and any manifest, permission or SDK impact.\n\n## Baseline stack\n\n| Area | Default | Avoid in new code |\n|---|---|---|\n| Language | Kotlin 2.x, K2 compiler | Kotlin 1.x language versions, synthetic view imports |\n| UI | Jetpack Compose + Material 3 | New XML layouts, `findViewById` |\n| State | `StateFlow` + immutable data class | `LiveData` in new ViewModels, mutable public state |\n| Async | Coroutines + Flow | `AsyncTask`, raw threads, new RxJava |\n| DI | Hilt with constructor injection | Manual singletons, service locators |\n| Persistence | Room (suspend + Flow), DataStore | `SharedPreferences` for new code |\n| Serialization | kotlinx.serialization | Reflection-based parsers without keep rules |\n| Codegen | KSP | kapt where a KSP processor exists |\n| Build | Kotlin DSL + `libs.versions.toml` | Hardcoded versions in module files |\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. No `!!`. Fail with a message or handle the null\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: NullPointerException with no context\nval id = savedStateHandle.get<String>(\"id\")!!\n\n// GOOD: invariant violation explains itself\nval id = requireNotNull(savedStateHandle.get<String>(\"id\")) { \"id nav argument missing\" }\n\n// GOOD: absence is a normal case\nval name = user?.displayName ?: context.getString(R.string.anonymous)\n```\n\n- `checkNotNull` for state invariants, `requireNotNull` for arguments. Java platform types are nullable.\n- `lateinit` only for framework-injected fields (`@Inject lateinit var` in an entry point), never for data.\n\n### 2. State is immutable; variants are sealed\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: mutable fields, impossible states (loading and error at once)\nclass ProfileState { var loading = false; var error: String? = null; var user: User? = null }\n\n// GOOD\nsealed interface ProfileUiState {\n    data object Loading : ProfileUiState\n    data class Content(val user: User, val isRefreshing: Boolean = false) : ProfileUiState\n    data class Error(val reason: UiError) : ProfileUiState\n}\n```\n\n- `when` over a sealed type has no `else`, so a new variant is a compile error, not a silent fallthrough.\n- Use `val` and read-only collection types in data classes. Change state with `copy`.\n- Guard conditions in `when` (stable since Kotlin 2.2) replace nested `if` inside a branch:\n  `is ProfileUiState.Content if it.isRefreshing -> ...`.\n\n### 3. Typed IDs use value classes\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: easy to swap arguments, compiler cannot help\nfun assign(deviceId: String, userId: String)\nassign(user.id, device.id) // compiles, wrong\n\n// GOOD: zero-cost wrapper on the JVM\n@JvmInline value class DeviceId(val value: String)\n@JvmInline value class UserId(val value: String)\nfun assign(deviceId: DeviceId, userId: UserId)\n```\n\n- Keep value classes in the domain layer. Map to primitives at DTO and entity boundaries unless the\n  library supports them (kotlinx.serialization does).\n- Validate in `init` when the wrapper carries an invariant: `init { require(value.isNotBlank()) }`.\n\n### 4. Every coroutine has an owner. No `GlobalScope`\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: outlives the screen, cannot be cancelled or tested\nGlobalScope.launch { repository.sync() }\n\n// GOOD: tied to the ViewModel\nviewModelScope.launch { repository.sync() }\n\n// GOOD: work that must outlive a screen uses an injected application scope or WorkManager\nclass SyncTrigger @Inject constructor(@ApplicationScope private val scope: CoroutineScope)\n```\n\n- Repositories and use cases expose `suspend` functions or `Flow`. They do not launch and return `Unit`.\n- `runBlocking` only in `main` functions and legacy bridges, never on the main thread.\n\n### 5. Suspend functions are main-safe; dispatchers are injected\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: hardcoded dispatcher, caller must remember to switch threads\nclass FileStore { fun read(): ByteArray = file.readBytes() }\n\n// GOOD\nclass FileStore @Inject constructor(\n    @IoDispatcher private val io: CoroutineDispatcher,\n) {\n    suspend fun read(): ByteArray = withContext(io) { file.readBytes() }\n}\n```\n\n- The function that does the blocking work switches context. Callers never wrap it again.\n- `Dispatchers.Default` for CPU work, IO dispatcher for blocking IO. Main is only for UI.\n\n### 6. `CancellationException` is always rethrown\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: runCatching and catch-all both swallow cancellation\nval result = runCatching { api.fetch() }\ntry { api.fetch() } catch (e: Exception) { log(e) }\n\n// GOOD\ntry {\n    api.fetch()\n} catch (e: CancellationException) {\n    throw e\n} catch (e: IOException) {\n    _state.update { it.copy(error = UiError.Network) }\n}\n```\n\nCatch the narrowest exception that can be handled. Use `ensureActive()` in long loops.\n\n### 7. Use `supervisorScope` when children fail independently\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: one failed widget cancels all the others\nsuspend fun loadDashboard() = coroutineScope {\n    val a = async { api.weather() }\n    val b = async { api.news() }\n    Dashboard(a.await(), b.await())\n}\n\n// GOOD: each child failure is handled at its await\nsuspend fun loadDashboard() = supervisorScope {\n    val a = async { api.weather() }\n    val b = async { api.news() }\n    Dashboard(weather = a.awaitOrNull(), news = b.awaitOrNull())\n}\n```\n\n`awaitOrNull` is a local helper that catches the expected exception types and rethrows cancellation.\nUse `coroutineScope` when the result is all-or-nothing.\n\n### 8. Mutable state is private; updates are atomic\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: public mutable state and a read-modify-write race\nval state = MutableStateFlow(UiState())\nfun select(id: ItemId) { state.value = state.value.copy(selected = id) }\n\n// GOOD\nprivate val _state = MutableStateFlow(UiState())\nval state: StateFlow<UiState> = _state.asStateFlow()\nfun select(id: ItemId) = _state.update { it.copy(selected = id) }\n```\n\n### 9. Cold flows become state once, and UI collects with the lifecycle\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: new upstream per collector, keeps running in the background\nval items: Flow<List<Item>> get() = repository.observeItems()\nval items by viewModel.items.collectAsState(initial = emptyList())\n\n// GOOD\nval items: StateFlow<List<Item>> = repository.observeItems()\n    .stateIn(viewModelScope, SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5_000), emptyList())\n\nval items by viewModel.items.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()\n```\n\n- `WhileSubscribed(5_000)` keeps the upstream alive across configuration changes, stops it in background.\n- Views and Fragments collect inside `repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED)`.\n- Details on `flowOn`, `catch`, `combine` and SharedFlow: [Coroutines and Flow](references/coroutines-flow.md).\n\n### 10. Composables are stateless; state is hoisted\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: ViewModel passed deep, no modifier, not previewable\n@Composable\nfun DeviceRow(vm: DevicesViewModel, id: DeviceId) { /* reads vm.state, calls vm.connect */ }\n\n// GOOD\n@Composable\nfun DeviceRow(\n    device: DeviceUi,\n    onConnect: (DeviceId) -> Unit,\n    modifier: Modifier = Modifier,\n) {\n    Row(modifier.clickable { onConnect(device.id) }) { Text(device.name) }\n}\n```\n\n- Only the screen-level composable touches the ViewModel. Below it: state in, lambdas out.\n- `modifier: Modifier = Modifier` is the first optional parameter and is applied to the root only.\n- `rememberSaveable` for user input that must survive rotation and process death.\n\n### 11. Parameters are stable so composables can skip\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: List is an interface the compiler treats as unstable; a new list instance\n// every emission forces recomposition of every row\ndata class FeedUi(val posts: List<PostUi>)\n\n// GOOD\n@Immutable\ndata class FeedUi(val posts: ImmutableList<PostUi>)\n// built with posts.map(::toUi).toImmutableList()\n```\n\n- Strong skipping is the default since Kotlin 2.0.20: unstable parameters are compared by instance,\n  stable ones by `equals`. Recreating objects on every emission still defeats skipping.\n- Do not sprinkle `@Stable` to silence reports. Annotate only types that keep the contract.\n\n### 12. Effects are keyed by what they read\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: keeps the first deviceId forever\nLaunchedEffect(Unit) { viewModel.connect(deviceId) }\n\n// GOOD: restarts when deviceId changes\nLaunchedEffect(deviceId) { viewModel.connect(deviceId) }\n\n// GOOD: long-lived effect that must call the latest lambda without restarting\nval currentOnTimeout by rememberUpdatedState(onTimeout)\nLaunchedEffect(Unit) { delay(30_000); currentOnTimeout() }\n```\n\n- Listeners and observers registered in composition go in `DisposableEffect` with `onDispose`.\n- No side effects in the composable body. More in [Compose](references/compose.md).\n\n### 13. Dependencies come through the constructor\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: Activity context in a singleton, hidden dependency\nobject Prefs { lateinit var context: Context }\n\n// GOOD\n@HiltViewModel\nclass SettingsViewModel @Inject constructor(\n    private val settings: SettingsRepository,\n    private val savedStateHandle: SavedStateHandle,\n) : ViewModel()\n\n@Singleton\nclass SettingsRepository @Inject constructor(\n    @ApplicationContext private val context: Context,\n)\n```\n\n- Bind interfaces with `@Binds` in an abstract module; `@Provides` only for third-party types.\n- Scope annotations match lifetime. Details: [Hilt and Room](references/hilt-room.md).\n\n### 14. Room DAOs are suspend or Flow, and schemas migrate\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: blocking DAO, and user data wiped on the next schema change\n@Query(\"SELECT * FROM device\") fun all(): List<DeviceEntity>\nRoom.databaseBuilder(ctx, AppDb::class.java, \"app.db\").fallbackToDestructiveMigration()\n\n// GOOD\n@Query(\"SELECT * FROM device ORDER BY name\") fun observeAll(): Flow<List<DeviceEntity>>\n@Upsert suspend fun upsert(device: DeviceEntity)\n\n@Database(entities = [DeviceEntity::class], version = 3, exportSchema = true)\nabstract class AppDb : RoomDatabase()\n```\n\n- Every version bump ships a `Migration` or `AutoMigration` and a migration test.\n\n### 15. KSP, version catalog, and the Compose compiler plugin\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: kapt and hardcoded versions\nplugins { kotlin(\"kapt\") }\ndependencies { kapt(\"com.google.dagger:hilt-compiler:2.51\") }\n\n// GOOD\nplugins {\n    alias(libs.plugins.ksp)\n    alias(libs.plugins.hilt)\n    alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.compose)\n}\ndependencies { ksp(libs.hilt.compiler) }\n```\n\n- `libs.plugins.kotlin.compose` maps to `org.jetbrains.kotlin.plugin.compose`, versioned with Kotlin.\n- Release builds enable R8 and add keep rules for reflection. Details: [Build and tooling](references/build.md).\n\n### 16. Code is ready for the current `targetSdk`\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: content under the status bar when targeting SDK 35+, and onBackPressed is deprecated\n// and no longer called when targeting SDK 36 with predictive back\nsetContent { LazyColumn { items(rows, key = { it.id }) { RowItem(it) } } }\noverride fun onBackPressed() { if (editing) cancelEdit() else super.onBackPressed() }\n\n// GOOD\nenableEdgeToEdge()\nsetContent {\n    Scaffold { innerPadding ->\n        LazyColumn(contentPadding = innerPadding) { items(rows, key = { it.id }) { RowItem(it) } }\n    }\n    BackHandler(enabled = editing) { cancelEdit() }\n}\n```\n\nForeground service types, large-screen orientation rules and more: [Platform changes](references/platform.md).\n\n### 17. New logic ships with deterministic tests\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: real dispatcher and sleep, slow and flaky\n@Test fun loads() { vm.load(); Thread.sleep(1000); assertEquals(3, vm.state.value.items.size) }\n\n// GOOD: virtual time, Turbine for the flow\n@Test fun loads() = runTest {\n    coEvery { repo.items() } returns listOf(a, b, c)\n    val vm = ItemsViewModel(repo)\n    vm.state.test {\n        assertEquals(ItemsUiState.Loading, awaitItem())\n        assertEquals(3, (awaitItem() as ItemsUiState.Content).items.size)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nMain dispatcher rule, fakes vs mocks and Compose tests: [Testing](references/testing.md).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- `!!` \"because it cannot be null here\". If it truly cannot, the type should say so.\n- `GlobalScope`, `runBlocking` on the main thread, or a `CoroutineScope(...)` field nobody cancels.\n- `runCatching` or `catch (e: Exception)` around suspend calls without rethrowing cancellation.\n- `MutableStateFlow` or `MutableList` exposed from a ViewModel.\n- One-off events kept in state that the UI never acknowledges, so the last navigation or toast replays after\n  rotation. Clear the event once handled (see `mobile-mvvm`).\n- ViewModels importing `android.view`, `Context` (non-application), or Compose UI types.\n- Passing a ViewModel into leaf composables, or `hiltViewModel()` called below the screen level.\n- `LaunchedEffect(Unit)` reading a parameter that changes.\n- `derivedStateOf` wrapping a value that changes as often as its input. It adds cost, not savings.\n- Lazy lists without `key`, or keyed by index.\n- New kapt processors, versions hardcoded in module files, `kotlinOptions {}` instead of `compilerOptions {}`.\n- `fallbackToDestructiveMigration()` in release builds.\n- Secrets, tokens or API keys in `BuildConfig`, `strings.xml` or source. Escalate to `mobile-security`.\n- Logging device identifiers, emails or tokens with `Log.d`. They are personal data.\n- Silently bumping SDK levels or adding manifest permissions without calling it out.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Project versions, SDK levels and conventions read before writing code\n- [ ] No `!!`; nullable inputs handled or failed with `requireNotNull` and a message\n- [ ] UI state is an immutable data class or sealed interface; `when` is exhaustive without `else`\n- [ ] Typed IDs are value classes where mix-ups are possible\n- [ ] Every coroutine has an owner scope; no `GlobalScope`\n- [ ] Dispatchers injected; blocking work wrapped in `withContext` at the source\n- [ ] `CancellationException` rethrown in every catch around suspend code\n- [ ] Mutable flows private; updates use `update {}`\n- [ ] Cold flows converted with `stateIn(..., WhileSubscribed(5_000), ...)`; UI uses `collectAsStateWithLifecycle`\n- [ ] Composables stateless below the screen; `modifier` parameter first optional and applied to root\n- [ ] Composable parameters stable; immutable collections where lists are passed\n- [ ] Effects keyed correctly; `rememberUpdatedState` for long-lived lambdas; listeners in `DisposableEffect`\n- [ ] Lazy lists have stable `key` and `contentType` for mixed rows\n- [ ] Hilt constructor injection; `@ApplicationContext` only; scopes match lifetimes\n- [ ] Room DAOs suspend or Flow; migrations and `exportSchema = true`\n- [ ] KSP instead of kapt; new dependencies in `libs.versions.toml`\n- [ ] R8 keep rules for reflection or serialization added and release build verified\n- [ ] Edge-to-edge insets and predictive back handled for the current `targetSdk`\n- [ ] Unit tests for new domain and data logic; tests and linters pass\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi` -- where ViewModels, state and events fit in the architecture\n- `mobile-design-patterns` -- repository, use case, adapter and factory patterns in Kotlin\n- `mobile-code-review` -- reviewing Kotlin changes against these rules\n- `mobile-security` -- storage, network, crypto and logging rules for the same code\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- running detekt, ktlint, tests and release builds in CI\n- `mobile-java` -- legacy Java code and Java-Kotlin interop\n- `mobile-kmp` -- sharing Kotlin code with iOS\n- `mobile-ble` -- Android Bluetooth Low Energy rules\n- `mobile-testing` -- cross-platform test strategy and screenshot tests\n- `mobile-performance` -- Baseline Profiles, Macrobenchmark and Android vitals\n",
    "references/build.md":
        "# Build and tooling reference\n\nLoad this when editing `build.gradle.kts`, `settings.gradle.kts`, `libs.versions.toml`, R8 rules, or lint\nconfiguration.\n\n## Version catalog\n\n~~~toml\n# gradle/libs.versions.toml\n[versions]\nkotlin = \"<kotlin>\"\nksp = \"<ksp>\"\nagp = \"<agp>\"\nhilt = \"<hilt>\"\nroom = \"<room>\"\ncompose-bom = \"<bom>\"\n\n[libraries]\ncompose-bom = { group = \"androidx.compose\", name = \"compose-bom\", version.ref = \"compose-bom\" }\ncompose-material3 = { group = \"androidx.compose.material3\", name = \"material3\" }\nhilt-android = { group = \"com.google.dagger\", name = \"hilt-android\", version.ref = \"hilt\" }\nhilt-compiler = { group = \"com.google.dagger\", name = \"hilt-compiler\", version.ref = \"hilt\" }\nroom-runtime = { group = \"androidx.room\", name = \"room-runtime\", version.ref = \"room\" }\nroom-ktx = { group = \"androidx.room\", name = \"room-ktx\", version.ref = \"room\" }\nroom-compiler = { group = \"androidx.room\", name = \"room-compiler\", version.ref = \"room\" }\n\n[plugins]\nandroid-application = { id = \"com.android.application\", version.ref = \"agp\" }\nkotlin-compose = { id = \"org.jetbrains.kotlin.plugin.compose\", version.ref = \"kotlin\" }\nkotlin-serialization = { id = \"org.jetbrains.kotlin.plugin.serialization\", version.ref = \"kotlin\" }\nksp = { id = \"com.google.devtools.ksp\", version.ref = \"ksp\" }\nhilt = { id = \"com.google.dagger.hilt.android\", version.ref = \"hilt\" }\nroom = { id = \"androidx.room\", version.ref = \"room\" }\n~~~\n\n- Use the project's existing versions. Do not invent or bump versions without saying so.\n- Compose libraries take their version from the BOM: `implementation(platform(libs.compose.bom))`.\n- The Compose compiler plugin and the serialization plugin are versioned with Kotlin. Since Kotlin 2.0 there is\n  no separate `kotlinCompilerExtensionVersion` to manage.\n- Check KSP compatibility with the Kotlin version whenever either one is upgraded.\n\n## Module build file\n\n```kotlin\nplugins {\n    alias(libs.plugins.android.application)\n    alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.compose)\n    alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.serialization)\n    alias(libs.plugins.ksp)\n    alias(libs.plugins.hilt)\n    alias(libs.plugins.room)\n}\n\nandroid {\n    namespace = \"com.example.app\"\n    compileSdk = 36\n    defaultConfig {\n        minSdk = 26\n        targetSdk = 36\n    }\n    buildFeatures { compose = true }\n    buildTypes {\n        release {\n            isMinifyEnabled = true\n            isShrinkResources = true\n            proguardFiles(getDefaultProguardFile(\"proguard-android-optimize.txt\"), \"proguard-rules.pro\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nkotlin {\n    jvmToolchain(17)\n}\n\nroom {\n    schemaDirectory(\"\$projectDir/schemas\")\n}\n\ndependencies {\n    implementation(platform(libs.compose.bom))\n    implementation(libs.compose.material3)\n    implementation(libs.hilt.android)\n    ksp(libs.hilt.compiler)\n    implementation(libs.room.runtime)\n    implementation(libs.room.ktx)\n    ksp(libs.room.compiler)\n}\n```\n\n- SDK numbers above are an example. Never change the project's `compileSdk`, `targetSdk` or `minSdk`\n  without asking; a `targetSdk` bump opts into behavior changes (see [Platform changes](platform.md)).\n- Compiler options go in `kotlin { compilerOptions { } }`. `kotlinOptions {}` is deprecated.\n- Recent AGP versions include built-in Kotlin support, so a separate `org.jetbrains.kotlin.android` plugin may\n  not be needed. Follow what the project's AGP version requires rather than adding plugins by habit.\n- Shared configuration across many modules belongs in convention plugins (`build-logic`), not copied blocks.\n\n## kapt to KSP\n\n- Replace `kapt(...)` with `ksp(...)` for Room, Hilt (Dagger), Moshi codegen and other processors that ship a\n  KSP implementation. Remove the kapt plugin once nothing uses it.\n- KSP is faster and works with K2. kapt runs in a compatibility mode and slows every build.\n- Keep any processor that has no KSP version on kapt, and say so.\n\n## Compose compiler options\n\n```kotlin\ncomposeCompiler {\n    reportsDestination = layout.buildDirectory.dir(\"compose_compiler\")\n    metricsDestination = layout.buildDirectory.dir(\"compose_compiler\")\n}\n```\n\n- Enable reports in a local or CI diagnostic build, not permanently in every build.\n- Strong skipping is on by default; do not add flags to enable it.\n- A stability configuration file (also set in `composeCompiler {}`) marks external types as stable. Use it\n  only for types that really are immutable.\n\n## R8\n\n- `isMinifyEnabled = true` and `isShrinkResources = true` for release. Test the release build, not only debug.\n- Keep rules are needed for code reached only by reflection, JNI, or class names held in strings.\n  Classes referenced from the manifest and XML layouts are kept by AGP-generated rules.\n\n```proguard\n# Reflection-based JSON models (Gson and similar): keep field names\n-keepclassmembers class com.example.app.data.remote.dto.** { <fields>; }\n\n# Classes loaded by name\n-keep class com.example.app.plugin.** implements com.example.app.plugin.Plugin { <init>(); }\n```\n\n- kotlinx.serialization uses generated serializers, not reflection, and its artifacts ship R8 rules for\n  standard usage. Verify the release build when serializers are looked up dynamically.\n- Retrofit, OkHttp, Room and Hilt ship consumer rules. Do not copy old blanket rules for them.\n- `-keep class com.example.** { *; }` disables shrinking for the whole app. Never add it to fix one crash.\n- `@Keep` (androidx.annotation) on a single class is acceptable for a narrow reflection case.\n- Upload the mapping file (`build/outputs/mapping/release/mapping.txt`) with each release for readable\n  stack traces.\n\n## Static analysis\n\n- detekt for code smells and complexity; ktlint (directly or through Spotless) for formatting.\n- Add the Compose lint rule set for detekt or ktlint to catch missing `modifier` parameters, unstable\n  parameters, and effects without keys.\n- Commit a baseline when adopting on legacy code; never grow the baseline for new code.\n- Run both in CI on every merge request, alongside `lint` and unit tests.\n- Android Lint warnings about new API usage, missing permissions and `RECEIVER_EXPORTED` flags are real\n  defects, not noise.\n\n## Secrets and build config\n\n- No API keys, tokens or signing passwords in `build.gradle.kts`, `BuildConfig`, `gradle.properties` in VCS,\n  or `strings.xml`. Anything in the APK can be extracted.\n- Signing configs read from CI secrets or a local untracked file. See `mobile-ci-cd` and\n  `mobile-security`.\n- `buildConfig = true` only in modules that actually read `BuildConfig`.\n\n## Native libraries\n\n- Apps with `.so` files (own or from dependencies) must support 16 KB memory page sizes for current Google\n  Play submissions targeting Android 15+. Check with the APK Analyzer and rebuild native code with a current\n  NDK.\n",
    "references/compose.md":
        "# Jetpack Compose reference\n\nLoad this when writing or changing composables, screen state wiring, effects, lists or previews.\n\n## Screen and content split\n\n```kotlin\n@Composable\nfun DevicesRoute(\n    onOpenDevice: (DeviceId) -> Unit,\n    viewModel: DevicesViewModel = hiltViewModel(),\n) {\n    val state by viewModel.state.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()\n    DevicesScreen(\n        state = state,\n        onQueryChange = viewModel::onQueryChange,\n        onDeviceClick = onOpenDevice,\n    )\n}\n\n@Composable\nfun DevicesScreen(\n    state: DevicesUiState,\n    onQueryChange: (String) -> Unit,\n    onDeviceClick: (DeviceId) -> Unit,\n    modifier: Modifier = Modifier,\n) { /* pure UI, previewable, testable without a ViewModel */ }\n```\n\n- The route composable is the only one that knows about the ViewModel and navigation.\n- Parameter order: required data, event lambdas, `modifier`, other optional parameters, trailing content\n  lambda.\n- Event lambdas are named for what happened (`onDeviceClick`), not what the parent will do.\n\n## State\n\n| Need | API |\n|---|---|\n| Survive recomposition | `remember { }` |\n| Survive rotation and process death | `rememberSaveable { }` (Bundle-able types, `@Parcelize`, or a `Saver`) |\n| Value computed from other state that changes less often than its inputs | `remember { derivedStateOf { } }` |\n| Recompute when an input changes | `remember(input) { }` |\n| Turn Compose state into a Flow | `snapshotFlow { }` |\n| Load into state from a suspend source | `produceState` |\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: recomposes on every scroll pixel\nval showButton = listState.firstVisibleItemIndex > 0\n\n// GOOD: recomposes only when the boolean flips\nval showButton by remember { derivedStateOf { listState.firstVisibleItemIndex > 0 } }\n\n// BAD: derivedStateOf adds overhead, output changes as often as input\nval fullName by remember { derivedStateOf { \"\$first \$last\" } }\n\n// GOOD\nval fullName = \"\$first \$last\"\n```\n\n- `remember` keys include every input of the calculation, or the cached value goes stale.\n- Read fast-changing state as late as possible: lambda modifiers (`Modifier.offset { }`,\n  `Modifier.graphicsLayer { }`, `drawBehind { }`) read in layout or draw, skipping composition.\n\n## Stability and skipping\n\n- Strong skipping is on by default since Kotlin 2.0.20. Restartable composables skip when every argument\n  is equal: stable types by `equals`, unstable types by instance (`===`).\n- Lambdas are remembered automatically under strong skipping. Do not wrap them in `remember` by hand.\n- What still breaks skipping: creating new list or object instances on every emission or recomposition,\n  and mapping inside the composable body.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: new list each recomposition, child never skips\nDeviceList(devices = state.devices.filter { it.online })\n\n// GOOD: filter in the ViewModel, pass an ImmutableList\nDeviceList(devices = state.onlineDevices)\n```\n\n- `kotlinx.collections.immutable`: `ImmutableList`, `persistentListOf()`, `toImmutableList()`.\n- `@Immutable`: all public properties are `val` and deeply immutable.\n- `@Stable`: mutable, but changes are observable through Compose state and `equals` is consistent.\n- Types from modules without the Compose compiler (domain, third-party) are unstable. Either map to a UI\n  model in the UI module or list them in a stability configuration file in the `composeCompiler {}` block.\n- Measure before optimizing: Layout Inspector recomposition counts and the compiler reports\n  (`composeCompiler { reportsDestination = ...; metricsDestination = ... }`).\n\n## Side effects\n\n| API | Use |\n|---|---|\n| `LaunchedEffect(key)` | Suspend work tied to composition, restarted when keys change |\n| `rememberCoroutineScope()` | Launch from event callbacks (click, swipe) |\n| `DisposableEffect(key)` | Register and unregister listeners, observers, callbacks |\n| `rememberUpdatedState(value)` | Latest value inside a long-lived effect without restarting it |\n| `SideEffect` | Publish Compose state to non-Compose code after every successful recomposition |\n| `LifecycleStartEffect`, `LifecycleResumeEffect` | Work paired with lifecycle start/stop or resume/pause |\n\n```kotlin\n@Composable\nfun ConnectionObserver(deviceId: DeviceId, onStateChange: (LinkState) -> Unit) {\n    val currentOnStateChange by rememberUpdatedState(onStateChange)\n    val monitor = LocalLinkMonitor.current\n    DisposableEffect(deviceId, monitor) {\n        val listener = LinkListener { currentOnStateChange(it) }\n        monitor.register(deviceId, listener)\n        onDispose { monitor.unregister(deviceId, listener) }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Never call ViewModel functions, start navigation or log analytics directly in the composable body.\n- `LaunchedEffect(Unit)` and `LaunchedEffect(true)` are only correct when the effect reads nothing that\n  can change.\n\n## Lazy lists\n\n```kotlin\nLazyColumn(\n    modifier = modifier,\n    contentPadding = contentPadding,\n) {\n    item(key = \"header\", contentType = \"header\") { Header() }\n    items(\n        items = state.messages,\n        key = { it.id.value },\n        contentType = { it.kind },\n    ) { message ->\n        MessageRow(message, Modifier.animateItem())\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `key` is stable and unique (database or server id, never index). It preserves item state and scroll\n  position across updates.\n- `contentType` lets the list reuse compositions between rows of the same type.\n- Keys must be Bundle-able types (String, Int, Long, Parcelable) if they are saved with the list state.\n- No nested scrollable in the same direction without a fixed height.\n\n## Modifiers\n\n- Modifier order matters: `Modifier.padding(16.dp).clickable { }` has a smaller touch area than\n  `Modifier.clickable { }.padding(16.dp)`.\n- Apply the incoming `modifier` once, to the root, first in the chain:\n  `modifier.fillMaxWidth().padding(...)`.\n- Do not create `Modifier` chains in a loop body when they are constant; hoist them.\n\n## Accessibility and semantics\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: tappable icon, no label, 24dp target\nIcon(Icons.Default.Refresh, null, Modifier.clickable { onRefresh() })\n\n// GOOD\nIconButton(onClick = onRefresh) {\n    Icon(Icons.Default.Refresh, contentDescription = stringResource(R.string.refresh))\n}\n```\n\n- `Modifier.semantics(mergeDescendants = true) { }` for rows read as one item.\n- `Modifier.testTag(\"...\")` only where text or content description cannot identify a node.\n- Text sizes in `sp`, no fixed heights on text containers.\n\n## Previews\n\n```kotlin\n@PreviewLightDark\n@PreviewFontScale\n@Composable\nprivate fun DevicesScreenPreview(\n    @PreviewParameter(DevicesStateProvider::class) state: DevicesUiState,\n) {\n    AppTheme { DevicesScreen(state = state, onQueryChange = {}, onDeviceClick = {}) }\n}\n```\n\n- Previews take state, not a ViewModel. If a screen cannot be previewed, it is doing too much.\n- Keep previews `private` and in the same file or a `debug` source set.\n\n## Navigation\n\n- Pass IDs between destinations, never whole objects. Load the object in the destination ViewModel.\n- Navigation callbacks are lambdas from the route composable. Screens do not hold a `NavController`.\n- With Navigation 3, the back stack is state the app owns; with Navigation 2.8+, routes are type-safe\n  `@Serializable` classes and the ViewModel reads arguments from `SavedStateHandle`.\n",
    "references/coroutines-flow.md":
        "# Coroutines and Flow reference\n\nLoad this when writing or changing suspend functions, scopes, dispatchers, Flow pipelines or ViewModel state.\n\n## Scopes and ownership\n\n| Work | Scope |\n|---|---|\n| Screen-bound loading, user actions | `viewModelScope` (runs on `Dispatchers.Main.immediate`) |\n| UI-only work in a composable | `rememberCoroutineScope()` or `LaunchedEffect` |\n| Activity or Fragment UI collection | `lifecycleScope` + `repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED)` |\n| Must finish even if the screen closes (short) | Injected application scope |\n| Must survive process death, constraints, retries | WorkManager (`CoroutineWorker`) |\n\nApplication scope, provided once:\n\n```kotlin\n@Qualifier @Retention(AnnotationRetention.BINARY) annotation class ApplicationScope\n@Qualifier @Retention(AnnotationRetention.BINARY) annotation class IoDispatcher\n@Qualifier @Retention(AnnotationRetention.BINARY) annotation class DefaultDispatcher\n\n@Module\n@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)\nobject CoroutinesModule {\n    @Provides @IoDispatcher fun io(): CoroutineDispatcher = Dispatchers.IO\n    @Provides @DefaultDispatcher fun default(): CoroutineDispatcher = Dispatchers.Default\n\n    @Provides @Singleton @ApplicationScope\n    fun appScope(@DefaultDispatcher dispatcher: CoroutineDispatcher): CoroutineScope =\n        CoroutineScope(SupervisorJob() + dispatcher)\n}\n```\n\n- `SupervisorJob` so one failed child does not cancel the whole app scope.\n- A class that creates its own `CoroutineScope(...)` must also own a `close()` or `cancel()` path.\n\n## Structured concurrency\n\n```kotlin\n// Parallel, all-or-nothing: first failure cancels the siblings and is rethrown\nsuspend fun loadProfile(id: UserId): Profile = coroutineScope {\n    val user = async { userApi.get(id) }\n    val stats = async { statsApi.get(id) }\n    Profile(user.await(), stats.await())\n}\n\n// Fan-out over a list\nsuspend fun uploadAll(files: List<File>) = coroutineScope {\n    files.map { async { uploader.upload(it) } }.awaitAll()\n}\n\n// Bounded parallelism for many items\nprivate val uploads = io.limitedParallelism(4)\n```\n\n- `supervisorScope` when children fail independently; handle each child's failure at its `await` or\n  inside the child.\n- `CoroutineExceptionHandler` works only on root coroutines (`launch` in a scope). It does nothing on\n  `async` or on child coroutines.\n- `withTimeout` throws `TimeoutCancellationException`; `withTimeoutOrNull` returns null. Prefer the latter\n  when timeout is an expected outcome.\n- Long CPU loops call `ensureActive()` or `yield()` so cancellation can take effect.\n- `NonCancellable` only for short cleanup in `finally`:\n\n```kotlin\ntry {\n    session.stream()\n} finally {\n    withContext(NonCancellable) { session.close() }\n}\n```\n\n## Error handling\n\n```kotlin\n// A suspend-safe replacement for runCatching\nsuspend inline fun <T> suspendRunCatching(block: () -> T): Result<T> = try {\n    Result.success(block())\n} catch (e: CancellationException) {\n    throw e\n} catch (e: Exception) {\n    Result.failure(e)\n}\n```\n\n- Map exceptions to a domain error type in the data layer. The UI renders `UiError`, not `IOException`.\n- Do not mix `Result`/sealed results and thrown exceptions for the same failure in one layer.\n\n## Cold vs hot\n\n| Type | Starts | Holds value | Use for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `flow {}`, Room `Flow`, `callbackFlow` | On each collect | No | Data streams from a source |\n| `StateFlow` | Always active | Latest value, conflated, `distinctUntilChanged` | UI state |\n| `SharedFlow` | Always active | Configurable replay | Broadcast events to many collectors |\n| `Channel` | n/a | Buffered until received | Single-consumer events |\n\n## ViewModel state patterns\n\n```kotlin\n@HiltViewModel\nclass DevicesViewModel @Inject constructor(\n    repository: DeviceRepository,\n    private val connect: ConnectDeviceUseCase,\n) : ViewModel() {\n\n    private val query = MutableStateFlow(\"\")\n    private val transient = MutableStateFlow(Transient())\n\n    val state: StateFlow<DevicesUiState> = combine(\n        repository.observeDevices(),\n        query,\n        transient,\n    ) { devices, q, t ->\n        DevicesUiState(\n            devices = devices.filter { it.name.contains(q, ignoreCase = true) }\n                .map { it.toUi() }\n                .toImmutableList(),\n            query = q,\n            connectingId = t.connectingId,\n        )\n    }\n        .catch { emit(DevicesUiState(error = UiError.Storage)) }\n        .stateIn(viewModelScope, SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5_000), DevicesUiState())\n\n    fun onQueryChange(value: String) { query.value = value }\n\n    fun onConnect(id: DeviceId) {\n        viewModelScope.launch {\n            transient.update { it.copy(connectingId = id) }\n            try {\n                connect(id)\n            } finally {\n                transient.update { it.copy(connectingId = null) }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `stateIn` once per property, not in a getter.\n- `combine` emits only after every source has emitted once. Give sources an initial value.\n- `catch` handles upstream errors only. An exception in `collect` is not caught by it.\n- After `catch`, the flow completes. For a stream that must continue, handle errors inside a\n  `flatMapLatest` or `retryWhen`.\n\n## Operators that matter\n\n```kotlin\n// flowOn changes the context of everything ABOVE it\nrepository.rawReadings()\n    .map { parse(it) }          // runs on io\n    .flowOn(io)\n    .filter { it.isValid }      // runs on the collector's context\n\n// Search: cancel the previous request on each new query\nquery\n    .debounce(300)\n    .distinctUntilChanged()\n    .flatMapLatest { q -> repository.search(q) }\n```\n\n- `flatMapLatest` requires `@OptIn(ExperimentalCoroutinesApi::class)`; `debounce` requires\n  `@OptIn(FlowPreview::class)`.\n- `conflate()` or `sample()` (`FlowPreview`) for high-frequency sensor or BLE notifications that the UI cannot render at\n  full rate.\n- `distinctUntilChanged()` is redundant on a `StateFlow`.\n\n## Wrapping callbacks\n\n```kotlin\n// Caller holds BLUETOOTH_SCAN; ScanFailedException is an app-defined type\nfun BluetoothLeScanner.scanFlow(filters: List<ScanFilter>, settings: ScanSettings): Flow<ScanResult> =\n    callbackFlow {\n        val callback = object : ScanCallback() {\n            override fun onScanResult(callbackType: Int, result: ScanResult) {\n                trySend(result) // called on a binder thread, never assume main\n            }\n            override fun onScanFailed(errorCode: Int) {\n                close(ScanFailedException(errorCode))\n            }\n        }\n        startScan(filters, settings, callback)\n        awaitClose { stopScan(callback) }\n    }\n```\n\n- `awaitClose` is mandatory. Without it the flow completes immediately and the listener leaks.\n- `trySend` from callbacks; `send` only inside the producer coroutine.\n- Pick a buffer policy deliberately: `.buffer(capacity, BufferOverflow.DROP_OLDEST)` for latest-wins data.\n- One-shot callbacks use `suspendCancellableCoroutine` and unregister in `invokeOnCancellation`.\n\n## One-off events\n\nPrefer modelling the event as state the UI consumes and acknowledges:\n\n```kotlin\ndata class EditUiState(val saved: Boolean = false)\n// UI: LaunchedEffect(state.saved) { if (state.saved) { onSaved(); viewModel.onSavedHandled() } }\n```\n\nIf a `Channel` is used, expose it with `receiveAsFlow()` and collect it in `LaunchedEffect` or\n`repeatOnLifecycle`, so events are not delivered to a stopped UI.\n\n## Views and Fragments\n\n```kotlin\nviewLifecycleOwner.lifecycleScope.launch {\n    viewLifecycleOwner.repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED) {\n        launch { viewModel.state.collect(::render) }\n        launch { viewModel.events.collect(::handle) }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Fragments use `viewLifecycleOwner`, not the Fragment itself.\n- `launchWhenStarted` and `launchWhenResumed` are deprecated. Replace with `repeatOnLifecycle`.\n",
    "references/hilt-room.md":
        "# Hilt and Room reference\n\nLoad this when adding injected classes, DI modules, entities, DAOs, migrations or serialization models.\n\n## Hilt setup\n\n```kotlin\n@HiltAndroidApp\nclass App : Application()\n\n@AndroidEntryPoint\nclass MainActivity : ComponentActivity()\n\n@HiltViewModel\nclass DeviceViewModel @Inject constructor(\n    private val savedStateHandle: SavedStateHandle,\n    private val repository: DeviceRepository,\n) : ViewModel()\n```\n\n- Constructor injection for every class the app owns. Modules exist only for interfaces and third-party types.\n- Compose obtains ViewModels with `hiltViewModel()` at the route level only.\n\n## Modules\n\n```kotlin\n// Interface to implementation: @Binds in an abstract module, no body to maintain\n@Module\n@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)\nabstract class DataModule {\n    @Binds abstract fun deviceRepository(impl: OfflineFirstDeviceRepository): DeviceRepository\n}\n\n// Third-party types: @Provides\n@Module\n@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)\nobject NetworkModule {\n    @Provides @Singleton\n    fun json(): Json = Json { ignoreUnknownKeys = true }\n\n    @Provides @Singleton\n    fun database(@ApplicationContext context: Context): AppDb =\n        Room.databaseBuilder(context, AppDb::class.java, \"app.db\")\n            .addMigrations(MIGRATION_1_2, MIGRATION_2_3)\n            .build()\n\n    @Provides\n    fun deviceDao(db: AppDb): DeviceDao = db.deviceDao()\n}\n```\n\n## Scopes\n\n| Annotation | Component | Lives as long as |\n|---|---|---|\n| `@Singleton` | `SingletonComponent` | The process |\n| `@ActivityRetainedScoped` | `ActivityRetainedComponent` | Activity, across configuration changes |\n| `@ViewModelScoped` | `ViewModelComponent` | One ViewModel |\n| `@ActivityScoped` | `ActivityComponent` | One Activity instance |\n\n- Unscoped is the default and the right choice for stateless classes. Scope only when the instance holds\n  shared state or is expensive to create.\n- A `@Singleton` must never hold an Activity, View, or screen state.\n- `@ApplicationContext` for any `Context` in a singleton. Never inject an Activity context into it.\n- Use `@Qualifier` annotations for two bindings of the same type (dispatchers, scopes, base URLs).\n- `Lazy<T>` or `Provider<T>` to defer expensive construction off the startup path.\n\n## Room entities and DAOs\n\n```kotlin\n@Entity(tableName = \"device\", indices = [Index(\"serial\", unique = true)])\ndata class DeviceEntity(\n    @PrimaryKey val id: String,\n    val serial: String,\n    val name: String,\n    @ColumnInfo(defaultValue = \"0\") val lastSeenEpochMs: Long,\n)\n\n@Dao\ninterface DeviceDao {\n    @Query(\"SELECT * FROM device ORDER BY name\")\n    fun observeAll(): Flow<List<DeviceEntity>>\n\n    @Query(\"SELECT * FROM device WHERE id = :id\")\n    suspend fun byId(id: String): DeviceEntity?\n\n    @Upsert\n    suspend fun upsert(devices: List<DeviceEntity>)\n\n    @Query(\"DELETE FROM device\")\n    suspend fun clear()\n\n    @Transaction\n    suspend fun replaceAll(devices: List<DeviceEntity>) {\n        clear()\n        upsert(devices)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `Flow` queries for observed data, `suspend` for one-shot reads and writes. Room runs them off the main\n  thread; no `withContext` needed around DAO calls.\n- Flow queries re-emit on any write to the observed tables. Add `distinctUntilChanged()` when the UI only\n  cares about actual changes.\n- Multi-step writes in one `@Transaction`.\n- Entities stay in the data layer. Map to domain models (with value-class IDs) in the repository.\n- Never `allowMainThreadQueries()` outside tests.\n\n## Migrations\n\n```kotlin\n@Database(\n    entities = [DeviceEntity::class],\n    version = 3,\n    exportSchema = true,\n    autoMigrations = [AutoMigration(from = 1, to = 2)],\n)\nabstract class AppDb : RoomDatabase() {\n    abstract fun deviceDao(): DeviceDao\n}\n\nval MIGRATION_2_3 = object : Migration(2, 3) {\n    override fun migrate(db: SupportSQLiteDatabase) {\n        db.execSQL(\"ALTER TABLE device ADD COLUMN lastSeenEpochMs INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0\")\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `exportSchema = true` and the schema directory committed to version control. Auto-migrations and\n  migration tests need the exported JSON.\n- Schema location with the Room Gradle plugin: `room { schemaDirectory(\"\$projectDir/schemas\") }`.\n- Renames and deletes in auto-migrations need an `AutoMigrationSpec` (`@RenameColumn`, `@DeleteColumn`).\n- `fallbackToDestructiveMigration` deletes user data. Do not ship it in release builds.\n- Each migration has a test with `MigrationTestHelper` (see [Testing](testing.md)).\n\n## kotlinx.serialization\n\n```kotlin\n@Serializable\ndata class DeviceDto(\n    val id: String,\n    @SerialName(\"display_name\") val name: String,\n    val firmware: String? = null,\n)\n\n// One configured Json instance, injected\nval json = Json {\n    ignoreUnknownKeys = true   // tolerate new server fields\n    explicitNulls = false      // omit nulls when encoding\n}\n```\n\n- Default values make fields optional on decode. A missing non-default field throws.\n- DTOs are separate from entities and domain models. Map at the edge.\n- Retrofit uses the official kotlinx.serialization converter with the same injected `Json` instance.\n- Payloads with device identifiers are personal data: no full-body logging in release builds.\n",
    "references/platform.md":
        "# Platform changes reference\n\nLoad this when touching Activities, window insets, back handling, services, permissions, or when the\nproject's `targetSdk` changes. Only behavior tied to `targetSdk` is listed; bumping it opts the app in.\n\nFor an SDK level not listed here, read the official behavior-changes page for that release before changing\ncode. Do not assume.\n\n## Edge-to-edge (targetSdk 35+)\n\n- On Android 15+ devices, apps targeting SDK 35 are drawn edge-to-edge. Status and navigation bar color APIs\n  no longer have an effect.\n- The `windowOptOutEdgeToEdgeEnforcement` opt-out is deprecated and ignored when targeting SDK 36. Do not rely\n  on it.\n- Call `enableEdgeToEdge()` in `onCreate` before `setContent` so older versions behave the same way.\n\nCompose:\n\n```kotlin\noverride fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {\n    enableEdgeToEdge()\n    super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)\n    setContent { AppTheme { AppRoot() } }\n}\n\n@Composable\nfun SettingsScreen(modifier: Modifier = Modifier) {\n    Scaffold(modifier = modifier, topBar = { TopAppBar(title = { Text(\"Settings\") }) }) { innerPadding ->\n        LazyColumn(contentPadding = innerPadding) { /* ... */ }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Material 3 `Scaffold`, `TopAppBar` and `NavigationBar` consume insets. Pass `innerPadding` to scrolling\n  content as `contentPadding` so items scroll under the bars but end above them.\n- Without a `Scaffold`: `Modifier.safeDrawingPadding()`, `Modifier.windowInsetsPadding(WindowInsets.safeDrawing)`,\n  and `Modifier.imePadding()` for text fields above the keyboard.\n- Backgrounds draw behind the bars; touch targets and text stay inside the insets.\n\nViews:\n\n```kotlin\nViewCompat.setOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(binding.list) { view, insets ->\n    val bars = insets.getInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.systemBars())\n    view.updatePadding(bottom = bars.bottom)\n    insets\n}\n```\n\n## Predictive back\n\n- `onBackPressed()` is deprecated. For apps targeting SDK 36 on Android 16+, predictive back is on by default\n  and `onBackPressed()` is no longer called, nor is `KEYCODE_BACK` dispatched.\n- Compose: `BackHandler(enabled = condition) { }`. For a custom animation driven by the gesture,\n  `PredictiveBackHandler { progress -> }` collects the back events.\n- Views: `onBackPressedDispatcher.addCallback(owner) { }` and toggle `isEnabled` as state changes.\n\n```kotlin\n// GOOD: enabled only while there is something to intercept\nBackHandler(enabled = state.hasUnsavedChanges) { showDiscardDialog = true }\n```\n\n- A callback that is always enabled breaks back-to-home. Enable it only while it has work to do.\n- Navigation libraries (Navigation Compose, Navigation 3) already handle back for their back stack.\n\n## Foreground services\n\n- Targeting SDK 34+: every foreground service declares `android:foregroundServiceType` in the manifest,\n  holds the matching `FOREGROUND_SERVICE_<TYPE>` permission, and passes the type when starting.\n\n```kotlin\nServiceCompat.startForeground(\n    this,\n    NOTIFICATION_ID,\n    notification,\n    ServiceInfo.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_TYPE_CONNECTED_DEVICE,\n)\n```\n\n- A BLE gateway service usually fits `connectedDevice`. Pick the type that matches what the service actually\n  does; a wrong type is a Play policy problem, not only a crash risk.\n- Targeting SDK 35+: `dataSync` and `mediaProcessing` services have a daily time limit. Implement\n  `Service.onTimeout(startId, fgsType)` and stop the service there.\n- Targeting SDK 35+: some types (including `dataSync`, `camera`, `microphone`, `mediaPlayback`) can no longer\n  be started from a `BOOT_COMPLETED` receiver.\n- Deferrable or retryable work belongs in WorkManager. Prefer it over a foreground service whenever the user\n  does not need to see ongoing work.\n\n## Large screens and orientation (targetSdk 36+)\n\n- On displays with smallest width 600dp or more, manifest orientation, resizability and aspect-ratio\n  restrictions are ignored for apps targeting SDK 36. Games are excluded.\n- Code must handle any size and orientation: state in ViewModels and `rememberSaveable`, layouts adapting to\n  window size classes, no hardcoded portrait assumptions in camera or scanning screens.\n\n## Other target-dependent rules still often missed\n\n- Runtime-registered broadcast receivers (targetSdk 34+) pass `RECEIVER_EXPORTED` or `RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED`.\n  Use `ContextCompat.registerReceiver` for backward compatibility.\n- `PendingIntent` (targetSdk 31+) must specify `FLAG_IMMUTABLE` or `FLAG_MUTABLE`. Default to immutable.\n- Bluetooth (targetSdk 31+): `BLUETOOTH_SCAN` and `BLUETOOTH_CONNECT` are runtime permissions. Add\n  `android:usesPermissionFlags=\"neverForLocation\"` to `BLUETOOTH_SCAN` only if scan results are never used to\n  derive location.\n- Notifications (targetSdk 33+): `POST_NOTIFICATIONS` is a runtime permission. Request it in context.\n- Exported components (`exported=\"true\"`) are an attack surface. See `mobile-security`.\n\n## Manifest and permission changes\n\n- Every new permission, service type, or exported component is called out explicitly in the change summary.\n- Request runtime permissions at the point of use with `rememberLauncherForActivityResult(\n  ActivityResultContracts.RequestPermission())` or `RequestMultiplePermissions()`, and handle denial.\n",
    "references/testing.md":
        "# Testing reference\n\nLoad this when writing unit tests for ViewModels, use cases, repositories and flows, or UI tests for\ncomposables and Room migrations.\n\n## What to test where\n\n| Code | Test type | Source set |\n|---|---|---|\n| Use cases, mappers, validators | Plain JUnit | `test` |\n| ViewModels, repositories, flows | JUnit + `kotlinx-coroutines-test` + Turbine | `test` |\n| Composables (behavior) | Compose UI test (instrumented, or JVM with Robolectric) | `androidTest` or `test` |\n| Room DAOs and migrations | Instrumented with an in-memory DB and `MigrationTestHelper` | `androidTest` |\n\n## Main dispatcher rule\n\n`viewModelScope` uses `Dispatchers.Main`, which does not exist on the JVM. Replace it per test:\n\n```kotlin\nclass MainDispatcherRule(\n    val testDispatcher: TestDispatcher = StandardTestDispatcher(),\n) : TestWatcher() {\n    override fun starting(description: Description) = Dispatchers.setMain(testDispatcher)\n    override fun finished(description: Description) = Dispatchers.resetMain()\n}\n```\n\n- With `StandardTestDispatcher`, launched coroutines run only when the scheduler advances\n  (`advanceUntilIdle()`, `runCurrent()`, or a suspending call such as `awaitItem()`).\n- `UnconfinedTestDispatcher` runs eagerly. Use it when a test needs `stateIn` collectors to start immediately,\n  not as a default to hide ordering problems.\n- Inject the same scheduler into code under test:\n  `Repo(io = StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler))` inside `runTest`, so virtual time is shared.\n\n## ViewModel test with a fake and Turbine\n\n```kotlin\nclass DevicesViewModelTest {\n    @get:Rule val mainRule = MainDispatcherRule()\n\n    private val repository = FakeDeviceRepository()\n\n    @Test\n    fun `filters devices by query`() = runTest {\n        repository.emit(listOf(device(\"Gateway A\"), device(\"Sensor B\")))\n        val vm = DevicesViewModel(repository, ConnectDeviceUseCase(repository))\n\n        vm.state.test {\n            assertEquals(DevicesUiState(), awaitItem()) // stateIn initial value\n            assertEquals(2, awaitItem().devices.size)\n            vm.onQueryChange(\"gate\")\n            assertEquals(listOf(\"Gateway A\"), awaitItem().devices.map { it.name })\n            cancelAndIgnoreRemainingEvents()\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- The first `awaitItem()` on a `StateFlow` may be the initial value. Assert it or skip it explicitly; do not\n  rely on timing.\n- `expectNoEvents()` to prove nothing else was emitted; `awaitError()` for failing flows.\n- `turbineScope { }` with `testIn(backgroundScope)` when asserting several flows together.\n\n## Fakes vs mocks\n\n- Prefer fakes for repositories and data sources: a small class backed by a `MutableStateFlow`. They test\n  behavior and survive refactors.\n- Use MockK for collaborators that are hard to fake, or to verify an interaction that is the behavior itself.\n\n```kotlin\nprivate val api = mockk<DeviceApi>()\n\n@Test\nfun `maps network failure to domain error`() = runTest {\n    coEvery { api.devices() } throws IOException(\"offline\")\n    val repo = NetworkDeviceRepository(api, io = StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler))\n\n    val result = repo.refresh()\n\n    assertEquals(RefreshResult.Failure(DeviceError.Network), result)\n    coVerify(exactly = 1) { api.devices() }\n}\n```\n\n- `coEvery` / `coVerify` for suspend functions; `every` / `verify` for regular ones.\n- `relaxed = true` hides missing stubs. Use it only for collaborators whose return value does not matter.\n- Do not mock types you own just to return data. Write a fake.\n- Do not mock data classes or value classes.\n\n## Time and delays\n\n```kotlin\n@Test\nfun `retries after backoff`() = runTest {\n    val api = FlakyApi(failures = 1)\n    val repo = Repo(api, io = StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler))\n\n    val result = async { repo.loadWithRetry() }\n    advanceTimeBy(1_000)\n    runCurrent()\n\n    assertEquals(expected, result.await())\n}\n```\n\n- `runTest` skips `delay` with virtual time. Never `Thread.sleep` in a test.\n- `runTest` waits for every child coroutine. A collector that never completes makes the test fail on timeout,\n  which surfaces leaked work; launch such collectors in `backgroundScope`.\n\n## Compose UI tests\n\nUse the v2 test APIs (`androidx.compose.ui.test.junit4.v2`, ui-test 1.11 and later). The v1 factories in\n`androidx.compose.ui.test.junit4` are deprecated.\n\n```kotlin\nimport androidx.compose.ui.test.junit4.v2.createComposeRule\n\nclass DevicesScreenTest {\n    @get:Rule val composeRule = createComposeRule()\n\n    @Test\n    fun clickingDeviceReportsId() {\n        var clicked: DeviceId? = null\n        composeRule.setContent {\n            AppTheme {\n                DevicesScreen(\n                    state = DevicesUiState(devices = persistentListOf(deviceUi(\"Gateway A\"))),\n                    onQueryChange = {},\n                    onDeviceClick = { clicked = it },\n                )\n            }\n        }\n\n        composeRule.onNodeWithText(\"Gateway A\").performClick()\n\n        assertEquals(DeviceId(\"gateway-a\"), clicked)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Test the stateless screen with plain state and lambdas. No ViewModel, no Hilt, no network.\n- Find nodes by semantics: `onNodeWithText`, `onNodeWithContentDescription`, `onNodeWithTag` as a last resort.\n- Assertions: `assertIsDisplayed()`, `assertIsEnabled()`, `assertTextEquals()`, `assertCountEquals()` on\n  `onAllNodes...`.\n- `createAndroidComposeRule<MainActivity>()` only when the test needs the Activity (resources, Hilt entry\n  point). With Hilt, add `@HiltAndroidTest` and `HiltAndroidRule` ordered before the compose rule.\n- The rule waits for idle. Use `waitUntil { }` for asynchronous conditions instead of sleeps.\n- v2 runs composition coroutines on `StandardTestDispatcher`: launched work is queued, not run immediately.\n  Call `composeRule.waitForIdle()` or assert inside `runOnIdle { }` after triggering work. For tests that\n  also need coroutine control, prefer `runComposeUiTest { }` over mixing the rule with `runTest`, which\n  creates two clocks.\n\n## Room\n\n```kotlin\n@get:Rule\nval helper = MigrationTestHelper(\n    InstrumentationRegistry.getInstrumentation(),\n    AppDb::class.java,\n)\n\n@Test\nfun migrate2To3() {\n    helper.createDatabase(TEST_DB, 2).use { db ->\n        db.execSQL(\"INSERT INTO device (id, serial, name) VALUES ('1', 'S1', 'A')\")\n    }\n    helper.runMigrationsAndValidate(TEST_DB, 3, true, MIGRATION_2_3)\n}\n```\n\n- DAO tests use `Room.inMemoryDatabaseBuilder(...)` and close the database in `@After`.\n- Migration tests need the exported schema JSON files available to the test APK.\n\n## Test hygiene\n\n- Test names describe behavior: `` `emits error when token expired` ``.\n- One behavior per test; arrange, act, assert separated by blank lines.\n- No shared mutable state between tests; build fresh fakes per test.\n- New domain and data logic ships with tests in the same change.\n",
  },
  "mobile-merge-request": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-merge-request\ndescription: \"Guides the merge request and pull request workflow for mobile teams: branch naming, Conventional Commits, small and stacked MRs, self-review, rebase vs merge, safe conflict resolution for lockfiles, project.pbxproj and version catalogs, MR descriptions with screenshots and rollback, and reviewer etiquette. Use when creating branches, writing commits or MR descriptions, rebasing, resolving conflicts or answering review comments.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile merge request\n\nGet mobile changes from a branch into main with a clean history, a description a reviewer can act on,\nand no lost work along the way. Applies to GitLab merge requests and GitHub pull requests alike;\n\"MR\" below means either.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Creating a branch, writing or cleaning up commit messages before review.\n- Opening an MR or PR, writing or improving its title and description.\n- Splitting a large change into smaller or stacked MRs.\n- Rebasing onto main, resolving conflicts, or force-pushing after review fixes.\n- Reviewing someone else's MR, or replying to review comments on your own.\n- Checking MR hygiene: branch name, commit messages, size, description, checklist.\n\nNot for: reviewing the code itself for bugs (use `mobile-code-review`), or pipeline configuration\n(use `mobile-ci-cd`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Gather facts before writing anything.\n   - Branch and target: `git branch --show-current`, `git fetch origin`.\n   - Commits: `git log --oneline origin/main..HEAD`.\n   - Size and files: `git diff --stat --merge-base origin/main`.\n   - Ticket key from the branch name or the user. Never invent a ticket key; leave a placeholder.\n2. Check hygiene against the Rules below: branch name, commit messages, size, unrelated changes,\n   generated files, secrets in the diff.\n3. If the history needs cleanup, propose the commands (fixup, autosquash, rebase) and wait for approval.\n   The agent never rewrites history, force-pushes or merges without explicit user consent.\n4. Self-review the full diff as a reviewer would. Remove debug logs, commented-out code, stray TODOs.\n5. Run the project's build, lint and tests for every affected platform. Report failures honestly.\n6. Write the MR title and description from [the MR template](references/mr-template.md).\n   Fill only what the diff and the user support; mark unknowns as `TODO` for the author.\n7. For conflicts, follow [conflict resolution](references/conflicts.md). Re-run build and tests after.\n8. For exact command sequences (stacking, retargeting, recovery), use\n   [git commands](references/git-commands.md).\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Branch names carry type, owner and ticket\n\n```text\n# BAD\nahmad-changes\nfix\nfeature/new-stuff\n\n# GOOD\nfeature/AH/PROJ-123-ble-reconnect-backoff\nfix/AH/PROJ-456-crash-on-empty-scan\nchore/AH/PROJ-789-bump-agp\n```\n\n- Pattern: `<type>/<initials>/<TICKET>-<kebab-short-title>`. Types: `feature`, `fix`, `chore`,\n  `refactor`, `docs`, `test`, `release`, `hotfix`.\n- Lowercase after the ticket key, no spaces, under about 60 characters.\n- Follow the repository's own convention if it already has one.\n\n```bash\ngit fetch origin\ngit switch -c feature/AH/PROJ-123-ble-reconnect-backoff origin/main\n```\n\n### 2. Commits follow Conventional Commits\n\n```text\n# BAD\nfixed stuff\nWIP\nUpdate MainActivity.kt\n\n# GOOD\nfix(ble): retry GATT connect with exponential backoff\nfeat(onboarding): add QR pairing screen\nrefactor(settings): move formatting out of SettingsViewController\n```\n\n- Format: `<type>(<optional scope>): <imperative summary>`, summary under about 72 characters, no period.\n- Types: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `docs`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`, `style`, `revert`.\n- Body explains why, not what the diff already shows. Wrap at 72.\n- Footers: `Refs: PROJ-123`, `BREAKING CHANGE: <what breaks and how to migrate>`.\n\nBreaking changes:\n\n```text\n# BAD: breaking change hidden in a fix\nfix(api): rename deviceId field\n\n# GOOD\nfeat(api)!: rename deviceId to gatewayId in telemetry payload\n\nBREAKING CHANGE: backend must accept gatewayId; app versions before 3.2 send deviceId.\nRefs: PROJ-321\n```\n\n- No tool-generated footers, sign-offs or co-author trailers unless the project requires them.\n\n### 3. One concern per MR, small enough to review in one sitting\n\n```text\n# BAD: one MR\nfeat: QR pairing + Gradle 9 upgrade + rename package + fix crash in settings\n\n# GOOD: four MRs\nchore(build): upgrade Gradle wrapper\nrefactor: rename package com.acme.old to com.acme.gateway\nfeat(onboarding): add QR pairing screen\nfix(settings): crash on empty device name\n```\n\n- Rough guidance: under about 400 changed lines of hand-written code. Lockfiles, generated code and\n  snapshots do not count but must be listed.\n- Mechanical changes (renames, formatting, dependency bumps) go in their own MR, never mixed with logic.\n- If it cannot be small, stack it (rule 4) or put it behind a feature flag and land it in slices.\n\n### 4. Stacked MRs target their parent, then get retargeted\n\n```bash\n# BAD: second MR targets main and shows the first MR's commits too\ngit switch -c feature/AH/PROJ-124-pairing-ui feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api\n# ...opened against main\n\n# GOOD: target the parent branch; after the parent merges, rebase and retarget to main\ngit switch -c feature/AH/PROJ-124-pairing-ui feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api\n# open MR: feature/AH/PROJ-124-pairing-ui -> feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api\n```\n\n- Say \"Depends on !123\" (GitLab) or \"Depends on #123\" (GitHub) at the top of the description.\n- Keep stacks shallow: two or three levels.\n- After the parent is squash-merged, replay only your own commits with `git rebase --onto` (see\n  [git commands](references/git-commands.md)) and retarget the MR to main.\n\n### 5. Rebase your own branch; never rewrite shared branches\n\n```bash\n# BAD\ngit push --force                  # overwrites whatever is on the remote, including others' commits\ngit rebase origin/main            # on main, develop or a branch a colleague also pushes to\n\n# GOOD: own branch only, refuses if the remote moved since your last fetch\ngit fetch origin\ngit rebase origin/main\ngit push --force-with-lease\n```\n\n- Shared branches (`main`, `develop`, `release/*`, a branch two people push to) only get merge commits\n  or new commits, never rewrites.\n- Do not rebase in the middle of an active review round without telling reviewers; it resets their\n  \"changes since last review\" view on some platforms. Prefer fixup commits until approval.\n\n### 6. Review fixes are fixup commits, squashed before merge\n\n```bash\n# BAD: noise that lands in main\ngit commit -m \"address review comments\"\ngit commit -m \"fix typo\"\n\n# GOOD\ngit commit --fixup=a1b2c3d\n# after approval, fold fixups into their targets\ngit rebase -i --autosquash origin/main\ngit range-diff origin/main @{u} HEAD   # confirm only intended changes before pushing\ngit push --force-with-lease\n```\n\n### 7. Squash policy is explicit\n\n- One logical change per MR: squash-merge. The MR title becomes the commit, so it must be a valid\n  Conventional Commit.\n- Several atomic, well-written commits (for example a refactor then a feature): rebase-merge or\n  fast-forward, no squash, so `git bisect` and `git revert` stay precise.\n- Never squash an MR that mixes a dependency bump with a behaviour change; split it instead.\n\n```text\n# BAD squash-merge title\nFeature/AH/PROJ-123 ble reconnect backoff\n\n# GOOD\nfix(ble): retry GATT connect with exponential backoff (PROJ-123)\n```\n\n### 8. Conflicts are resolved by understanding both sides, then verified by a build\n\n```bash\n# BAD: pick a side blindly for every file\ngit checkout --theirs .\ngit add . && git rebase --continue\n\n# GOOD: inspect, resolve per file, regenerate what is generated, then build and test\ngit status\ngit diff --name-only --diff-filter=U\n# resolve each file; regenerate lockfiles and generated code instead of hand-merging\ngit add <file>\ngit rebase --continue\n./gradlew assembleDebug testDebugUnitTest\n```\n\n- During a rebase `--ours` is the branch being rebased onto (main) and `--theirs` is your commit.\n  The opposite of a merge.\n- Lockfiles, `project.pbxproj` and version catalogs have specific procedures:\n  [conflict resolution](references/conflicts.md).\n\n### 9. The description lets a reviewer review without asking\n\n```markdown\n<!-- BAD -->\nFixes the bug.\n\n<!-- GOOD (short form) -->\n## Summary\nReconnect to the gateway with exponential backoff after GATT error 133 instead of failing at once.\n\n## Ticket\nPROJ-123\n\n## Testing\n- Unit: BackoffPolicyTest (new), ConnectionManagerTest (updated)\n- Manual: Pixel 8 Android 16, iPhone 15 iOS 26; power-cycled gateway 10 times, reconnected every time\n```\n\n- Every user-visible change has Android and iOS screenshots or recordings, before and after.\n- Call out explicitly: new permissions, manifest or `Info.plist` keys, entitlements, `minSdk`,\n  `targetSdk` or deployment target changes, database migrations, analytics or telemetry changes.\n- Full template: [MR template](references/mr-template.md).\n\n### 10. Review comments are labelled and actionable\n\n```text\n# BAD\nThis is wrong.\nWhy would you do it like this?\n\n# GOOD\nissue: `onCharacteristicChanged` runs on a binder thread; `_state.value = ...` here races with the\ncollector. Use `_state.update {}` or hop to the main dispatcher.\n\nnit (non-blocking): `deviceList` -> `devices` to match the repository naming.\n\nquestion: Is the 10 s timeout from the firmware spec, or a guess? If a guess, add a constant with a comment.\n```\n\n- Labels: `issue:` (blocking), `suggestion:`, `question:`, `nit:` (never blocks), `praise:`, `todo:`.\n- Comment on the code, not the person. Explain why, and propose a fix or a suggestion block.\n- See [reviewer etiquette](references/mr-template.md#reviewer-etiquette) for both sides of a review.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- \"WIP\", \"fix\", \"changes\" as the final commit or squash title.\n- Force-pushing with plain `--force`, or rewriting `main`, `develop` or a colleague's branch.\n- Rebasing and force-pushing during review without saying so, so reviewers lose their place.\n- One MR containing a feature, a dependency bump and a mass reformat.\n- Hand-merging `pubspec.lock`, `Podfile.lock`, `Package.resolved` or generated code.\n- Resolving `project.pbxproj` conflicts by taking one side wholesale and losing the other side's files.\n- Marking every reviewer thread resolved without replying or changing anything.\n- Screenshots for one platform only on a cross-platform UI change.\n- Descriptions that repeat the diff file by file instead of explaining why.\n- Merging with a red pipeline \"because it is flaky\".\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Branch follows `<type>/<initials>/<TICKET>-<short-title>` and is based on current main\n- [ ] Every commit is a valid Conventional Commit; breaking changes use `!` and a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer\n- [ ] MR has one concern and is reviewable in one sitting (or is split or stacked)\n- [ ] Stacked MRs target their parent and say \"Depends on\"\n- [ ] Self-reviewed: no debug logs, commented-out code, secrets, personal data or unrelated files\n- [ ] Build, lint and tests pass for every affected platform after the last rebase or conflict fix\n- [ ] Description has Summary, Ticket, Changes, screenshots (Android and iOS), Testing, Risk and rollback\n- [ ] Permissions, manifest, `Info.plist`, entitlements, SDK levels and migrations are called out\n- [ ] Review fixes are fixup commits; history is cleaned before merge\n- [ ] Force pushes use `--force-with-lease`, on the author's own branch only\n- [ ] Every review thread has a reply; resolved by the agreed party\n- [ ] Squash or rebase-merge chosen deliberately; final commit title is a valid Conventional Commit\n\n## Output format\n\nWhen asked to write an MR or PR description, produce the title and the filled template:\n\n~~~markdown\nTitle: fix(ble): retry GATT connect with exponential backoff (PROJ-123)\n\n## Summary\n<one or two sentences: what changes for the user or the codebase, and why>\n\n## Ticket\nPROJ-123\n\n## Changes\n- <grouped by intent, not by file>\n\n## Screenshots / recordings\n| Android | iOS |\n|---|---|\n| TODO | TODO |\n\n## Testing\n- <tests added or updated, devices and OS versions, manual steps>\n\n## Risk and rollback\n- Risk: <what could break, who is affected>\n- Rollback: <revert MR | disable flag X | server config>\n\n## Checklist\n- [x] <items verified from the diff>\n- [ ] <items the author must confirm>\n~~~\n\nWhen asked to check MR hygiene, produce findings in this shape:\n\n~~~markdown\n## MR hygiene: <branch name>\n\nTarget: main. Commits: 5. Changed: 612 lines in 14 files (180 in lockfiles and generated code).\n\n### Findings\n1. [Must fix] Commit \"wip\" (a1b2c3d) -- not a Conventional Commit.\n   Fix: `git commit --fixup=<target>` or reword before merge.\n2. [Must fix] Description has no iOS screenshots for a shared UI change.\n3. [Should fix] Gradle wrapper bump mixed with feature code -- split into its own MR.\n4. [Note] `Info.plist` gains `NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription` -- call it out under Risk.\n\n### Suggested commands\n```bash\ngit rebase -i --autosquash origin/main\ngit push --force-with-lease\n```\n\n### Verdict\nNot ready -- 2 Must fix.\n~~~\n\nSeverity: Must fix blocks requesting review; Should fix is expected before merge; Note is informational.\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-code-review` -- reviewing the code inside the MR\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- pipelines, required checks, release branches and signing\n- `mobile-security` -- when the diff touches secrets, permissions, network or storage\n- `mobile-kotlin`, `mobile-swift`, `mobile-flutter` -- build and test commands per platform\n- `mobile-release` -- release notes and readiness before merging a release branch\n",
    "references/conflicts.md":
        "# Conflict resolution for mobile projects\n\nLoad this when a rebase or merge stops on conflicts. The goal is a result that contains both sides'\nintent and builds, not a result with no conflict markers.\n\n## General procedure\n\n1. See what conflicted and why.\n\n```bash\ngit status\ngit diff --name-only --diff-filter=U            # only the conflicted files\ngit log --oneline --merge -- <file>             # during a merge: commits on both sides touching the file\n```\n\n2. Know which side is which.\n\n| Operation | `--ours` / HEAD | `--theirs` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `git merge origin/main` | your branch | main |\n| `git rebase origin/main` | main (plus your already-replayed commits) | the commit being replayed |\n\n3. Resolve per file using the rules below. Never `git checkout --ours .` or `--theirs .` for the\n   whole tree.\n4. Check nothing is left behind:\n\n```bash\ngit diff --check                                # reports leftover conflict markers\ngit add <file>\ngit rebase --continue                           # or: git merge --continue\n```\n\n5. Rebuild and retest every affected platform before pushing. A clean merge can still be broken.\n\n```bash\n./gradlew assembleDebug testDebugUnitTest lint\nxcodebuild -scheme App -destination 'platform=iOS Simulator,name=<simulator name>' build test\nflutter analyze && flutter test\n```\n\n6. If the conflict is large or unclear, `git rebase --abort` and ask the other author, rather than guess.\n\n## Source files (Kotlin, Java, Swift, Dart)\n\n- Read both versions and the commit messages that produced them. Merge the intent, not the lines.\n- Watch for semantic conflicts that git does not flag: a function renamed on main and called by its old\n  name in your branch, a new required parameter, a changed default. The build catches most; tests catch\n  the rest.\n- Imports: keep the union, then let the formatter or IDE remove unused ones.\n\n## Lockfiles\n\nNever hand-merge a lockfile. Resolve the manifest, take main's lockfile, regenerate.\n\n| Lockfile | Manifest to resolve first | Regenerate with |\n|---|---|---|\n| `pubspec.lock` | `pubspec.yaml` | `flutter pub get` (or `dart pub get`) |\n| `Podfile.lock` | `Podfile` | `pod install` (not `pod update`, which bumps everything) |\n| `Package.resolved` | Xcode project or `Package.swift` | `xcodebuild -resolvePackageDependencies`, or resolve packages in Xcode |\n| `gradle.lockfile` (dependency locking) | `libs.versions.toml`, `build.gradle.kts` | the project's lock task, for example `./gradlew dependencies --write-locks` per affected module |\n\n```bash\n# during a rebase, --ours is main's version\ngit checkout --ours pubspec.lock\nflutter pub get\ngit add pubspec.lock\n```\n\nReview the regenerated lockfile diff: only the packages your branch intended to change should move.\n\n## Generated code\n\nDo not hand-merge generated output. Resolve the sources, then regenerate.\n\n- Dart `*.g.dart`, `*.freezed.dart`, mocks: `dart run build_runner build --delete-conflicting-outputs`.\n- Flutter localizations: `flutter gen-l10n` after merging the `.arb` files.\n- Protobuf or OpenAPI generated clients: rerun the generator from the merged schema.\n- Room exported schemas (`schemas/<Database>/<version>.json`): a conflict here usually means both\n  branches bumped the database to the same version. This is a real conflict: renumber one branch's\n  migration to the next version, then rebuild so the schema is exported again.\n\nIf generated files should not be in git at all, raise it as a separate MR.\n\n## Xcode `project.pbxproj`\n\n- Each added file appears in several sections (file reference, build file, group children, build\n  phase). Keep both sides' entries in every section; dropping one side silently removes files from\n  the target.\n- Remove only the conflict markers and true duplicates (same object ID listed twice).\n- Validate the file and then build:\n\n```bash\nplutil -lint App.xcodeproj/project.pbxproj\nxcodebuild -scheme App -destination 'platform=iOS Simulator,name=<simulator name>' build\n```\n\n- Open the project in Xcode and confirm the files from both sides appear in the right targets.\n- If the file is badly mangled, abort, take main's version and re-add your files in Xcode.\n- Prevention: synchronized folder groups (Xcode 16 and later) stop most per-file entries; project\n  generators (XcodeGen, Tuist) avoid committing the project file altogether.\n- Do not set `merge=union` for `*.pbxproj` in `.gitattributes`; it produces duplicate or dangling\n  entries that only fail later.\n\n## Gradle version catalog (`libs.versions.toml`)\n\n- New entries from both sides: keep both.\n- Same alias, different versions: choose deliberately, usually the newer one, and check compatibility\n  (Kotlin and Compose compiler, AGP and Gradle, BOM-managed libraries).\n- Same library added under two different aliases: keep one alias and update usages.\n- Keep each table sorted alphabetically; it makes future conflicts rare and trivial.\n- Run a Gradle sync and a full build after resolving; a catalog mistake fails at configuration time.\n\n## Android resources and manifest\n\n- `strings.xml` and translations: keep both sides' keys; make sure no key was renamed on one side.\n- `AndroidManifest.xml`: keep both sides' components and permissions, remove duplicates, and recheck\n  `android:exported` on every component either side added.\n- Navigation graphs and XML layouts: resolve by hand and open the editor preview to confirm.\n\n## iOS resources\n\n- `Localizable.xcstrings` is JSON: keep both sides' keys, then confirm it still parses by building.\n- `Info.plist` and entitlements: keep both sides' keys and call out the result in the MR description.\n- Asset catalogs (`Contents.json`): keep both entries; duplicated asset names fail the build.\n\n## After resolving\n\n- [ ] No conflict markers (`git diff --check`)\n- [ ] Lockfiles and generated code regenerated, not hand-edited\n- [ ] Build and tests green for every affected platform\n- [ ] `git range-diff origin/main @{u} HEAD` reviewed before `git push --force-with-lease`\n- [ ] Non-trivial resolutions mentioned in an MR comment so reviewers can check them\n",
    "references/git-commands.md":
        "# Git commands for the MR workflow\n\nLoad this for exact command sequences. Everything here assumes the remote is `origin` and the\ntarget branch is `main`; substitute the project's names. Commands that rewrite history are for the\nauthor's own, unshared branch only, and the agent runs them only after the user agrees.\n\n## One-time setup\n\n```bash\ngit config --global push.autoSetupRemote true   # first push of a new branch sets upstream\ngit config --global rebase.autoSquash true      # fixup! commits are folded automatically in rebase -i\ngit config --global rerere.enabled true         # remember conflict resolutions across rebases\ngit config --global pull.rebase true            # pulling your own branch rebases instead of merging\n```\n\n## Start a branch\n\n```bash\ngit fetch origin\ngit switch -c feature/AH/PROJ-123-ble-reconnect-backoff origin/main\n```\n\n## Inspect before opening the MR\n\n```bash\ngit log --oneline origin/main..HEAD             # commits that will be in the MR\ngit diff --stat --merge-base origin/main        # size and touched files\ngit diff --merge-base origin/main               # full diff, as the reviewer will see it\ngit diff --check --merge-base origin/main       # whitespace errors and leftover conflict markers\n```\n\n## Commit and amend\n\n```bash\ngit add -p                                      # stage hunk by hunk, keeps commits focused\ngit commit -m \"fix(ble): retry GATT connect with exponential backoff\" -m \"Refs: PROJ-123\"\ngit commit --amend                              # last commit only, before it is pushed or reviewed\n```\n\n## Review fixes as fixups\n\n```bash\ngit commit --fixup=a1b2c3d                      # content fix for commit a1b2c3d\ngit commit --fixup=reword:a1b2c3d               # message-only fix for commit a1b2c3d\ngit rebase -i --autosquash origin/main          # after approval: folds fixups into their targets\n```\n\n## Update a branch with main\n\n```bash\ngit fetch origin\ngit rebase origin/main                          # own branch only\ngit range-diff origin/main @{u} HEAD            # compare pushed commits with rebased commits\ngit push --force-with-lease                     # refuses if someone else pushed since your fetch\n```\n\n- `git range-diff` shows per-commit differences between the old and new series. Use it before every\n  force push to confirm the rebase did not drop or alter anything unintended.\n- `--force-with-lease` protects against overwriting commits you have not fetched. Run it right after\n  your own `fetch`, not after an automated background fetch that may have updated the lease.\n\nFor shared branches, merge instead of rebasing:\n\n```bash\ngit switch release/3.2\ngit merge --no-ff origin/main\ngit push\n```\n\n## Open the MR from the command line\n\n```bash\n# GitLab push options: create the MR on push\ngit push -o merge_request.create -o merge_request.target=main\n\n# GitHub CLI\ngh pr create --base main --title \"fix(ble): retry GATT connect with exponential backoff (PROJ-123)\" \\\n  --body-file pr-description.md\ngh pr create --draft --base main --fill\n\n# GitLab CLI\nglab mr create --target-branch main --draft\n```\n\n## Stacked MRs\n\nCreate the stack:\n\n```bash\ngit switch -c feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api origin/main\n# ...commits, push, MR 1 targets main\ngit switch -c feature/AH/PROJ-124-pairing-ui\n# ...commits, push, MR 2 targets feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api\n```\n\nParent changed during review (new fixups on PROJ-123), update the child:\n\n```bash\ngit switch feature/AH/PROJ-124-pairing-ui\ngit rebase feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api\ngit push --force-with-lease\n```\n\nOr rebase the whole stack at once from the top branch; intermediate branches move with it:\n\n```bash\ngit switch feature/AH/PROJ-124-pairing-ui\ngit rebase --update-refs origin/main\ngit push --force-with-lease origin feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api feature/AH/PROJ-124-pairing-ui\n```\n\nParent was squash-merged into main. The child still contains the parent's original commits, which\nmain no longer has. Replay only the child's own commits:\n\n```bash\ngit fetch origin\n# local feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api still points at the parent's pre-merge tip\ngit rebase --onto origin/main feature/AH/PROJ-123-pairing-api feature/AH/PROJ-124-pairing-ui\ngit push --force-with-lease\n```\n\nThen retarget MR 2 to `main` in the MR settings. Both GitLab and GitHub can retarget dependent MRs\nautomatically when the parent branch is deleted on merge; check the MR target afterwards anyway.\n\n## Undo and recover\n\n```bash\ngit rebase --abort                              # back to before the rebase started\ngit merge --abort                               # back to before the merge started\ngit reflog                                      # find the commit you had before a bad rebase\ngit switch -c rescue/PROJ-123 HEAD@{2}          # recover it on a new branch, nothing is lost\ngit revert <sha>                                # undo a merged commit on a shared branch\ngit revert -m 1 <merge-sha>                     # undo a merged MR that landed as a merge commit\n```\n\nPrefer recovery onto a new branch over moving the current branch, so nothing is overwritten.\n\n## Commands the agent does not run without explicit approval\n\n- `git push --force-with-lease` or any push that rewrites remote history.\n- `git rebase` of a branch that has an open MR under review.\n- Merging an MR, deleting a branch, or changing branch protection.\n- Plain `git push --force` is never suggested.\n",
    "references/mr-template.md":
        "# MR template and reviewer etiquette\n\nLoad this when writing or improving an MR or PR description, or when reviewing or answering review\ncomments. Copy the template into `.gitlab/merge_request_templates/Default.md` (GitLab) or\n`.github/pull_request_template.md` (GitHub) to make it the project default.\n\n## Title\n\n- Same rules as a commit subject: `<type>(<scope>): <imperative summary> (<TICKET>)`.\n- It becomes the squash commit, so it must stand alone in `git log --oneline`.\n- Draft work: prefix `Draft:` on GitLab, or open as a draft PR on GitHub (`gh pr create --draft`).\n\n```text\n# BAD\nPROJ-123\nFixes\nFeature/AH/PROJ-123 pairing\n\n# GOOD\nfeat(onboarding): add QR pairing screen (PROJ-123)\n```\n\n## Full template\n\n~~~markdown\n## Summary\n<!-- One or two sentences: what changes for the user or the codebase, and why now. -->\n\n## Ticket\n<!-- PROJ-123. Add \"Closes #123\" if the tracker should close it on merge. -->\n<!-- Stacked: \"Depends on !122\" (GitLab) or \"Depends on #122\" (GitHub). -->\n\n## Changes\n<!-- Grouped by intent, not by file. Mention what reviewers should look at first. -->\n- \n- \n\n## Screenshots / recordings\n<!-- Required for any user-visible change. Before and after. Light and dark if theming is touched. -->\n| | Android | iOS |\n|---|---|---|\n| Before | | |\n| After | | |\n\n## Testing\n<!-- What proves this works. -->\n- Unit tests:\n- UI / instrumentation tests:\n- Manual: device, OS version, steps, result\n- Not tested (and why):\n\n## Risk and rollback\n- Risk: <!-- what could break, which users, which app versions -->\n- Migration: <!-- database, preferences, keychain, payload format; none if none -->\n- Rollback: <!-- revert this MR | turn off flag X | server-side switch -->\n\n## Platform and compliance notes\n<!-- Delete lines that do not apply. -->\n- New permissions / manifest entries:\n- `Info.plist` keys / entitlements / background modes:\n- `minSdk` / `targetSdk` / `compileSdk` / deployment target:\n- Privacy manifest (`PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`) / data safety form impact:\n- Telemetry or analytics payload changes (personal data?):\n- New third-party dependency (license, size, maintenance):\n\n## Checklist\n- [ ] Self-reviewed the full diff\n- [ ] Commits follow Conventional Commits; breaking changes marked\n- [ ] Build, lint and tests pass locally for Android and iOS (or the affected platform)\n- [ ] New logic in domain and data layers has unit tests\n- [ ] No secrets, tokens, keys or personal data in code, logs or fixtures\n- [ ] Strings localized; accessibility labels added for new controls\n- [ ] Screenshots for both platforms attached\n- [ ] Docs, changelog or release notes updated if needed\n~~~\n\n## Filling it in well\n\n- Summary is about behaviour, not files. \"Users can pair by scanning a QR code\" beats\n  \"Added PairingFragment.kt\".\n- Changes lists decisions a reviewer might question: \"Kept the old pairing flow behind\n  `legacy_pairing` flag until 3.3\".\n- Testing names real devices and OS versions. \"Tested on emulator\" alone is weak for BLE, camera,\n  background work or push notifications.\n- Risk is never \"none\" for a change that touches persistence, networking, BLE or auth. Say what was\n  considered.\n- Remove empty sections rather than leaving template comments in the final description.\n\n## Reviewer etiquette\n\n### When reviewing\n\n- Respond within one working day, even if only to say when a full review will happen.\n- Read the description and ticket first; review against intent.\n- Review the whole change before commenting, so early comments are not invalidated by later code.\n- Label every comment so the author knows what blocks:\n\n| Label | Meaning | Blocks merge |\n|---|---|---|\n| `issue:` | A defect or a broken requirement | Yes |\n| `suggestion:` | A better approach, with reason | Only if marked `(blocking)` |\n| `question:` | Need information to judge | Until answered |\n| `nit:` | Preference or polish | Never |\n| `praise:` | Something done well, say why | Never |\n| `todo:` | Small required follow-up | Yes |\n\n- Propose the fix. Both GitLab and GitHub support suggestion blocks the author can apply in one click:\n\n~~~markdown\nsuggestion: keep the scan timeout in one place.\n```suggestion\nprivate const val SCAN_TIMEOUT_MS = 10_000L\n```\n~~~\n\n- Comment on the code, not the person: \"this call can run on a binder thread\", not \"you forgot threading\".\n- Out-of-scope problems: mention once, suggest a ticket, do not block.\n- Approve when remaining items are nits. Do not hold an MR hostage for preferences.\n\n### When being reviewed\n\n- Reply to every thread. A silent resolve looks like the comment was ignored.\n- Short replies that point to the change: \"Done in a1b2c3d\", \"Moved to `BackoffPolicy`, see fixup e4f5a6b\".\n- Disagree with reasons and evidence, once. If still unresolved, agree on a decision maker or a\n  follow-up ticket, not a long thread.\n- Push fixes as fixup commits during review so reviewers can see what changed since their last pass.\n- Re-request review after a round of fixes; do not assume reviewers watch the MR.\n\n### Resolving threads\n\n- Blocking threads (`issue:`, `todo:`, `question:`) are resolved by the reviewer who opened them,\n  after checking the fix.\n- Nits and applied suggestions can be resolved by the author with a reply.\n- Turn on \"all threads must be resolved\" (GitLab) or \"require conversation resolution\" (GitHub branch\n  protection) so the rule is enforced, not remembered.\n",
  },
  "mobile-mvc": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-mvc\ndescription: \"Fixes controller-based mobile code: UIKit MVC and Massive View Controllers (child view controllers, coordinators, separate data sources), legacy Android Activity-as-controller and MVP, and Flutter controllers, plus step-by-step migration to MVVM one screen at a time. Use when working on UIViewController, Activity, Fragment or presenter code, splitting a god class, or deciding whether MVC is still fine.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile MVC and MVP\n\nKeep controller-based screens small, testable and leak-free, and move them to MVVM safely when that pays\noff. Covers UIKit MVC, legacy Android Activity-as-controller and MVP, and Flutter controllers.\n\n## When to use\n\n- A `UIViewController`, `Activity` or `Fragment` has grown past a few hundred lines and mixes\n  networking, formatting, navigation and view code.\n- Adding a feature to a legacy MVC or MVP screen without rewriting it.\n- Reviewing presenter code for leaks, or a Flutter widget whose `State` holds all the logic.\n- Deciding whether a screen should stay MVC or move to MVVM.\n- Planning or executing a screen-by-screen migration to ViewModels.\n\nNot for: designing new screens in MVVM or MVI from scratch (use `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Identify the platform and pattern actually in use, and load the matching reference:\n   [UIKit](references/uikit.md), [Android legacy and MVP](references/android-legacy.md),\n   [Flutter controllers](references/flutter.md).\n2. Map the controller's responsibilities. For each method, tag it: view setup, layout, data source,\n   networking, persistence, formatting, validation, navigation, lifecycle.\n3. Decide: keep, slim down, or migrate (see \"When MVC is still fine\" below). Prefer the smallest change\n   that fixes the actual pain.\n4. Before moving code, add characterization tests around behaviour that will move (formatting,\n   validation, state transitions). If logic is untestable in place, extract it as a pure function first.\n5. Extract one responsibility at a time, in this order: formatting, networking and persistence behind a\n   service, data source objects, navigation into a coordinator or navigator, child controllers for\n   independent regions.\n6. Fix lifecycle on the way: presenters detach, observers are removed, tasks are cancelled, controllers\n   are disposed.\n7. If migrating, follow [migration to MVVM](references/migration-to-mvvm.md): one screen per MR,\n   ViewModel introduced behind the existing controller, state moved field by field.\n8. Build, run the tests, and exercise the screen on device, including rotation (Android) and\n   background and foreground.\n\n## When MVC is still fine\n\n- Static or nearly static screens: settings lists, about pages, legal text, simple forms.\n- Thin wrappers around system controllers (pickers, mail compose, share sheets, web views).\n- Screens with no business rules and no state that must survive configuration change.\n- Code scheduled for deletion. Do not migrate what is about to be removed.\n- A team or codebase where MVC is consistent and screens stay small. Consistency beats a half-migrated mix.\n\nMigrate when: logic needs unit tests, state is lost on rotation or process death, the same logic is\nduplicated across screens, or the controller cannot be changed without regressions.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. The controller owns views, not networking or persistence\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: view controller builds requests and decodes JSON\noverride func viewDidLoad() {\n    super.viewDidLoad()\n    URLSession.shared.dataTask(with: URL(string: \"https://api.example.com/devices\")!) { data, _, _ in\n        let devices = try! JSONDecoder().decode([Device].self, from: data!)\n        DispatchQueue.main.async { self.devices = devices; self.tableView.reloadData() }\n    }.resume()\n}\n\n// GOOD: injected service, the controller only renders\nfinal class DevicesViewController: UIViewController {\n    private let service: DeviceService\n    init(service: DeviceService) {\n        self.service = service\n        super.init(nibName: nil, bundle: nil)\n    }\n    required init?(coder: NSCoder) { fatalError(\"init(coder:) is not supported\") }\n}\n```\n\nJava:\n```java\n// BAD: Activity opens sockets and parses responses\nnew Thread(() -> {\n    String json = httpGet(\"https://api.example.com/devices\");\n    runOnUiThread(() -> adapter.submit(parse(json)));\n}).start();\n\n// GOOD: presenter asks a repository; the Activity only renders\npresenter.loadDevices();\n```\n\n### 2. Navigation is decided outside the screen\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: the screen knows the next screen and how to build it\nlet detail = DeviceDetailViewController(service: DeviceServiceImpl(), device: device)\nnavigationController?.pushViewController(detail, animated: true)\n\n// GOOD: the screen reports intent; a coordinator decides\nvar onSelectDevice: ((Device.ID) -> Void)?\nfunc tableView(_ tableView: UITableView, didSelectRowAt indexPath: IndexPath) {\n    guard let id = dataSource.itemIdentifier(for: indexPath) else { return }\n    onSelectDevice?(id)\n}\n```\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: presenter starts Activities\nclass DevicesPresenter(private val activity: Activity) {\n    fun onDeviceClicked(id: String) = activity.startActivity(Intent(activity, DetailActivity::class.java))\n}\n\n// GOOD: presenter calls a view method; the Activity or a navigator performs it\ninterface DevicesView { fun openDetail(deviceId: String) }\n```\n\n### 3. Data sources and delegates are separate objects\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: index-based data source in the controller, reloadData on every change\nextension DevicesViewController: UITableViewDataSource {\n    func tableView(_ t: UITableView, numberOfRowsInSection s: Int) -> Int { devices.count }\n    func tableView(_ t: UITableView, cellForRowAt i: IndexPath) -> UITableViewCell { ... }\n}\n\n// GOOD: diffable data source keyed by stable ids, owned by the controller, configured once\nprivate lazy var dataSource = UITableViewDiffableDataSource<Section, Device.ID>(tableView: tableView) {\n    [weak self] tableView, indexPath, id in\n    self?.cellProvider.cell(in: tableView, at: indexPath, for: id)\n}\n```\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: notifyDataSetChanged on every update\nadapter.items = devices; adapter.notifyDataSetChanged()\n\n// GOOD: ListAdapter with DiffUtil, stable ids in areItemsTheSame\nadapter.submitList(devices)\n```\n\n### 4. Formatting lives in formatters, not in cells or controllers\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: new formatter per cell, logic untestable\ncell.detail.text = { let f = DateFormatter(); f.dateFormat = \"dd.MM.yyyy\"; return f.string(from: d.lastSeen) }()\n\n// GOOD: a testable row model built outside the cell\nstruct DeviceRow { let title: String; let lastSeen: String }\nextension DeviceRow {\n    init(_ d: Device) {\n        title = d.name\n        lastSeen = d.lastSeen.formatted(date: .abbreviated, time: .shortened)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD: formatting inside build\nText('\${(device.battery * 100).round()}% - \${device.lastSeen.toLocal()}')\n\n// GOOD: controller or row model exposes display strings, tested without widgets\nText(row.batteryLabel)\n```\n\n### 5. A presenter never outlives its view\n\nJava:\n```java\n// BAD: presenter keeps the Activity after onDestroy; async callback touches a dead view\n@Override public void onDevicesLoaded(List<Device> devices) { view.showDevices(devices); }\n\n// GOOD: attach and detach with the lifecycle; callbacks check the view\npublic void attach(DevicesContract.View view) { this.view = view; }\npublic void detach() { this.view = null; cancelPending(); }\n\n@Override public void onDevicesLoaded(List<Device> devices) {\n    if (view != null) view.showDevices(devices);\n}\n// Activity: presenter.detach() in onDestroy()\n```\n\n- `WeakReference<View>` hides the leak but keeps the work running. Cancel the work too.\n\n### 6. Presenters and controllers are free of framework types\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: presenter needs Context and a Looper, so it cannot run in a plain JUnit test\nclass LoginPresenter(private val context: Context) {\n    fun onLogin() = Toast.makeText(context, context.getString(R.string.error), Toast.LENGTH_SHORT).show()\n}\n\n// GOOD: presenter depends on interfaces; the view decides how to show an error\nclass LoginPresenter(private val auth: AuthRepository, private val scope: CoroutineScope) {\n    private var view: LoginContract.View? = null\n    fun onLogin(email: String, password: String) {\n        scope.launch {\n            val result = auth.login(email, password)\n            view?.render(if (result.isSuccess) LoginState.Done else LoginState.Error(LoginError.InvalidCredentials))\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nSwift: controllers' helpers (formatters, validators, services) import `Foundation`, not `UIKit`.\n\n### 7. Flutter controllers are owned by State and disposed\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD: controller created in build, logic in State, never disposed\n@override\nWidget build(BuildContext context) {\n  final controller = PairingController(repo);\n  return ListenableBuilder(listenable: controller, builder: (_, __) => Text(controller.status));\n}\n\n// GOOD\nclass _PairingScreenState extends State<PairingScreen> {\n  late final PairingController _controller = PairingController(widget.repo);\n\n  @override\n  void dispose() {\n    _controller.dispose();\n    super.dispose();\n  }\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) => ListenableBuilder(\n        listenable: _controller,\n        builder: (context, _) => Text(_controller.status),\n      );\n}\n```\n\n### 8. Async results check that the screen is still there\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: retains the controller and updates it after it was popped\nloadTask = Task { self.render(try await service.devices()) }\n\n// GOOD: weak self, capture only the service, cancel on disappear\nloadTask = Task { [weak self, service] in\n    do {\n        let devices = try await service.devices()\n        self?.render(devices)\n    } catch {\n        self?.showError(error)\n    }\n}\noverride func viewDidDisappear(_ animated: Bool) {\n    super.viewDidDisappear(animated)\n    loadTask?.cancel()\n}\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD\nawait _controller.pair();\nNavigator.of(context).pop();\n\n// GOOD\nawait _controller.pair();\nif (!mounted) return;\nNavigator.of(context).pop();\n```\n\n### 9. Dependencies come in through the initializer or constructor\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: hidden singletons, impossible to fake\nlet devices = try await APIClient.shared.devices()\n\n// GOOD: storyboard screens can still use init injection via a custom init?(coder:service:)\nlet vc = storyboard.instantiateViewController(identifier: \"Devices\") { coder in\n    DevicesViewController(coder: coder, service: service)\n}\n```\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD\nprivate val repo = DeviceRepository.getInstance()\n\n// GOOD: presenter constructed with its dependencies (Hilt or a manual factory)\nclass DevicesPresenter @Inject constructor(private val repo: DeviceRepository)\n```\n\n### 10. Migrate one screen at a time, tests first\n\n```text\n# BAD\nrefactor: migrate whole app to MVVM (214 files)\n\n# GOOD: one screen, three small MRs\ntest(devices): characterization tests for DevicesPresenter\nrefactor(devices): add DevicesViewModel behind DevicesActivity\nrefactor(devices): move device list state to DevicesViewModel, remove presenter\n```\n\nDetails and code: [migration to MVVM](references/migration-to-mvvm.md).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Massive View Controller: networking, parsing, formatting, data source, delegate and navigation in one\n  class with a dozen `extension`s that only hide the size.\n- \"Manager\" or \"Helper\" singletons that every controller calls, replacing one god class with a global one.\n- Presenter holding `Activity` or `Context` and never detached; work keeps running after `onDestroy`.\n- Using `AsyncTask` (deprecated) or Loaders (deprecated) in maintained code.\n- Child view controllers added as subviews without `addChild` and `didMove(toParent:)`, so appearance\n  callbacks and trait changes do not propagate.\n- Coordinators holding strong references to the view controllers they push and never releasing them.\n- Flutter `State` classes with hundreds of lines of business logic and `setState` calls from async code.\n- Big-bang migration of every screen in one MR, or migrating screens that work and are not changing.\n- Introducing a ViewModel but leaving the old state in the controller too, so there are two sources of truth.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Controller code is view setup, binding and lifecycle only\n- [ ] Networking and persistence behind injected services or repositories\n- [ ] Formatting and validation in testable types, not in cells, widgets or controllers\n- [ ] Table and collection data sources are separate objects, diffable or DiffUtil based\n- [ ] Navigation goes through a coordinator, navigator or view callback\n- [ ] Child view controllers use the full containment calls\n- [ ] Presenters attach and detach with the lifecycle and cancel pending work\n- [ ] Presenters and helpers do not import `android.*` or `UIKit`\n- [ ] Flutter controllers created in `initState` or a `late final` field and disposed in `dispose`\n- [ ] Async callbacks check `mounted`, a non-null view or `[weak self]` before updating UI\n- [ ] Characterization tests exist before logic is moved\n- [ ] Migration is one screen per MR, with one source of truth for each piece of state\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-mvvm` -- target architecture when migrating\n- `mobile-mvi` -- alternative target for complex, event-heavy screens\n- `mobile-design-patterns` -- coordinator, adapter, strategy and facade patterns used here\n- `mobile-swift`, `mobile-java`, `mobile-kotlin`, `mobile-flutter` -- language rules for the code being moved\n- `mobile-code-review` -- reviewing the refactoring MRs\n- `mobile-testing` -- characterization tests before a migration\n",
    "references/android-legacy.md":
        "# Android legacy: Activity as controller and MVP\n\nLoad this when the screen is a Java or Kotlin `Activity` or `Fragment` with logic in it, or uses an MVP\npresenter. New Android screens should use MVVM with Compose; this reference is for maintaining and\nmigrating what exists.\n\n## Symptoms of a god Activity\n\n- `onCreate` longer than one screen; network calls, SQL and string formatting inline.\n- State in fields that is lost on rotation, or saved field by field in `onSaveInstanceState`.\n- `AsyncTask`, raw `Thread`, or `Handler` callbacks that touch views after `onDestroy`.\n- `onActivityResult` with a switch over request codes for camera, permissions and other screens.\n\n## MVP contract\n\nOne contract per screen. The view interface is what the presenter may ask of the UI; the presenter\ninterface is what the UI reports.\n\nJava:\n```java\npublic interface DevicesContract {\n    interface View {\n        void showLoading(boolean loading);\n        void showDevices(List<DeviceRow> rows);\n        void showError(@StringRes int message);\n        void openDetail(String deviceId);\n    }\n\n    interface Presenter {\n        void attach(View view);\n        void detach();\n        void loadDevices();\n        void onDeviceClicked(String deviceId);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`@StringRes` is an annotation from `androidx.annotation`, which is acceptable in a presenter; avoid\nanything that needs a `Context`.\n\n## Presenter with lifecycle-safe callbacks\n\nJava:\n```java\npublic final class DevicesPresenter implements DevicesContract.Presenter {\n    private final DeviceRepository repository;\n    @Nullable private DevicesContract.View view;\n    @Nullable private Cancellable pending;\n\n    public DevicesPresenter(DeviceRepository repository) {\n        this.repository = repository;\n    }\n\n    @Override public void attach(DevicesContract.View view) { this.view = view; }\n\n    @Override public void detach() {\n        view = null;\n        if (pending != null) pending.cancel();\n    }\n\n    @Override public void loadDevices() {\n        if (view != null) view.showLoading(true);\n        pending = repository.loadDevices(new Callback<List<Device>>() {\n            @Override public void onSuccess(List<Device> devices) {\n                if (view == null) return;\n                view.showLoading(false);\n                view.showDevices(DeviceRow.from(devices));\n            }\n            @Override public void onError(Throwable error) {\n                if (view == null) return;\n                view.showLoading(false);\n                view.showError(R.string.devices_error);\n            }\n        });\n    }\n\n    @Override public void onDeviceClicked(String deviceId) {\n        if (view != null) view.openDetail(deviceId);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`Cancellable` and `Callback` stand for whatever the project's repository returns. The point is that\ndetach both drops the view and cancels the work.\n\nCallbacks from a repository may arrive on a background thread. Either the repository posts to the main\nthread, or the presenter does; never call view methods from a worker thread.\n\nKotlin presenter with coroutines:\n```kotlin\nclass DevicesPresenter(\n    private val repository: DeviceRepository,\n    mainDispatcher: CoroutineDispatcher,\n) : DevicesContract.Presenter {\n    private val scope = CoroutineScope(SupervisorJob() + mainDispatcher)\n    private var view: DevicesContract.View? = null\n\n    override fun attach(view: DevicesContract.View) { this.view = view }\n\n    override fun detach() {\n        view = null\n        scope.coroutineContext.cancelChildren()\n    }\n\n    override fun loadDevices() {\n        scope.launch {\n            view?.showLoading(true)\n            val result = runCatchingNonCancellable { repository.devices() }\n            view?.showLoading(false)\n            result.onSuccess { view?.showDevices(DeviceRow.from(it)) }\n                .onFailure { view?.showError(R.string.devices_error) }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`runCatchingNonCancellable` stands for a project helper that rethrows `CancellationException`; plain\n`runCatching` around suspend calls swallows cancellation.\n\n## Wiring in the Activity and Fragment\n\nJava Activity:\n```java\n@Override protected void onCreate(@Nullable Bundle savedInstanceState) {\n    super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);\n    binding = ActivityDevicesBinding.inflate(getLayoutInflater());\n    setContentView(binding.getRoot());\n    presenter.attach(this);\n    presenter.loadDevices();\n}\n\n@Override protected void onDestroy() {\n    presenter.detach();\n    super.onDestroy();\n}\n```\n\nFragment: attach in `onViewCreated`, detach in `onDestroyView`, and clear the view binding there too.\nThe fragment instance outlives its view, so `onDestroy` is too late.\n\nLimitations to state in reviews: an MVP presenter created in `onCreate` is recreated on rotation, so\nin-flight work and loaded data are lost. This is the most common reason to migrate the screen to a\nViewModel (see [migration to MVVM](migration-to-mvvm.md)).\n\n## Deprecated APIs to replace while touching legacy screens\n\n| Legacy | Replacement |\n|---|---|\n| `AsyncTask` | Coroutines in a ViewModel, or an executor owned by a lifecycle-aware component |\n| Loaders (`LoaderManager`) | ViewModel with `StateFlow` or `LiveData` |\n| `startActivityForResult` / `onActivityResult` | `registerForActivityResult` with an `ActivityResultContract` |\n| `requestPermissions` / `onRequestPermissionsResult` | `ActivityResultContracts.RequestPermission` |\n| `onBackPressed()` override | `OnBackPressedCallback` on `onBackPressedDispatcher` |\n| `Handler()` with no Looper | `Handler(Looper.getMainLooper())`, or coroutines |\n| Kotlin synthetics (`kotlin-android-extensions`) | View Binding |\n| `ProgressDialog` | An inline progress indicator in the layout |\n\n- Back handling: with predictive back enabled (the default when targeting Android 16), `onBackPressed()`\n  is not called. Legacy screens that override it must move to `OnBackPressedCallback`.\n- Orientation: when targeting Android 16, large-screen devices ignore orientation locks and\n  resizability restrictions. A legacy Activity that locked portrait to avoid losing state on rotation\n  will now lose it on tablets and foldables. Fix the state handling, do not rely on the lock.\n\n## Testing presenters\n\n```kotlin\n@Test fun `shows devices after load`() = runTest {\n    val view = mockk<DevicesContract.View>(relaxed = true)\n    val presenter = DevicesPresenter(FakeDeviceRepository(listOf(device)), StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler))\n    presenter.attach(view)\n\n    presenter.loadDevices()\n    advanceUntilIdle()\n\n    verifyOrder {\n        view.showLoading(true)\n        view.showLoading(false)\n        view.showDevices(DeviceRow.from(listOf(device)))\n    }\n}\n\n@Test fun `no view calls after detach`() = runTest {\n    val view = mockk<DevicesContract.View>(relaxed = true)\n    val presenter = DevicesPresenter(SlowDeviceRepository(), StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler))\n    presenter.attach(view)\n    presenter.loadDevices()\n    presenter.detach()\n    advanceUntilIdle()\n\n    verify(exactly = 0) { view.showDevices(any()) }\n}\n```\n\nThe second test is the leak guard: it fails if detach does not cancel the work.\n",
    "references/flutter.md":
        "# Flutter controller pattern\n\nLoad this when a Flutter screen keeps its logic in a `State` class, or uses `ChangeNotifier` or\n`ValueNotifier` controllers. The model is the framework's own controllers (`TextEditingController`,\n`ScrollController`): a plain object that holds state and logic, notifies listeners, and is owned and\ndisposed by a `State`.\n\n## Before: logic in State\n\n```dart\n// BAD: networking, validation and formatting in the State; untestable without pumping widgets\nclass _PairingScreenState extends State<PairingScreen> {\n  bool _loading = false;\n  String? _error;\n\n  Future<void> _pair(String code) async {\n    if (code.length != 8) {\n      setState(() => _error = 'Invalid code');\n      return;\n    }\n    setState(() => _loading = true);\n    final response = await http.post(Uri.parse('https://api.example.com/pair'), body: code);\n    setState(() => _loading = false);\n    if (response.statusCode != 200) setState(() => _error = 'Pairing failed');\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## After: controller plus thin widget\n\n```dart\nenum PairingStatus { idle, pairing, paired, failed }\n\nclass PairingController extends ChangeNotifier {\n  PairingController(this._repository);\n\n  final PairingRepository _repository;\n  PairingStatus _status = PairingStatus.idle;\n  String? _errorKey;\n  bool _disposed = false;\n\n  PairingStatus get status => _status;\n  String? get errorKey => _errorKey;\n  bool get canSubmit => _status != PairingStatus.pairing;\n\n  static bool isValidCode(String code) => RegExp(r'^[A-Z0-9]{8}\$').hasMatch(code);\n\n  Future<void> pair(String code) async {\n    if (!isValidCode(code)) {\n      _set(PairingStatus.failed, 'invalidCode');\n      return;\n    }\n    _set(PairingStatus.pairing, null);\n    try {\n      await _repository.pair(code);\n      _set(PairingStatus.paired, null);\n    } on PairingException {\n      _set(PairingStatus.failed, 'pairingFailed');\n    }\n  }\n\n  void _set(PairingStatus status, String? errorKey) {\n    if (_disposed) return;\n    _status = status;\n    _errorKey = errorKey;\n    notifyListeners();\n  }\n\n  @override\n  void dispose() {\n    _disposed = true;\n    super.dispose();\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- State is private; the widget reads getters and calls methods.\n- The controller does not import `material.dart` widgets or hold a `BuildContext`. It exposes error keys\n  or enums; the widget maps them to localized strings.\n- `notifyListeners()` after `dispose()` throws, which is why async methods check `_disposed`.\n\n```dart\nclass _PairingScreenState extends State<PairingScreen> {\n  late final PairingController _controller = PairingController(widget.repository);\n  final _codeField = TextEditingController();\n\n  @override\n  void dispose() {\n    _controller.dispose();\n    _codeField.dispose();\n    super.dispose();\n  }\n\n  Future<void> _submit() async {\n    await _controller.pair(_codeField.text);\n    if (!mounted) return;\n    if (_controller.status == PairingStatus.paired) Navigator.of(context).pop(true);\n  }\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) {\n    return ListenableBuilder(\n      listenable: _controller,\n      builder: (context, child) => Column(\n        children: [\n          child!,\n          if (_controller.errorKey != null) Text(localizeError(context, _controller.errorKey!)),\n          FilledButton(\n            onPressed: _controller.canSubmit ? _submit : null,\n            child: const Text('Pair'),\n          ),\n        ],\n      ),\n      child: TextField(controller: _codeField),\n    );\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- `ListenableBuilder` rebuilds only its builder. Static subtrees go in `child` so they are not rebuilt.\n- Navigation and snack bars stay in the widget, after a `mounted` check.\n\n## ValueNotifier for a single value\n\n```dart\n// BAD: whole screen rebuilds for a counter\nsetState(() => _secondsLeft--);\n\n// GOOD\nfinal _secondsLeft = ValueNotifier<int>(60);\n\nValueListenableBuilder<int>(\n  valueListenable: _secondsLeft,\n  builder: (context, value, _) => Text('\$value s'),\n)\n// dispose: _secondsLeft.dispose();\n```\n\n`ValueNotifier` notifies only when the new value is not `==` to the old one. Mutating a list inside a\n`ValueNotifier<List<T>>` does not notify; assign a new list.\n\n## Ownership rules\n\n- Whoever creates a controller disposes it. A widget that receives a controller from its parent never\n  disposes it.\n- Optional external controller, like `TextField`:\n\n```dart\nclass DeviceList extends StatefulWidget {\n  const DeviceList({super.key, this.controller});\n  final DeviceListController? controller;\n  @override\n  State<DeviceList> createState() => _DeviceListState();\n}\n\nclass _DeviceListState extends State<DeviceList> {\n  DeviceListController? _internal;\n  DeviceListController get _effective => widget.controller ?? (_internal ??= DeviceListController());\n\n  @override\n  void didUpdateWidget(DeviceList oldWidget) {\n    super.didUpdateWidget(oldWidget);\n    if (widget.controller != null && _internal != null) {\n      _internal!.dispose();\n      _internal = null;\n    }\n  }\n\n  @override\n  void dispose() {\n    _internal?.dispose();\n    super.dispose();\n  }\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) => ListenableBuilder(\n        listenable: _effective,\n        builder: (context, _) => Text('\${_effective.count} devices'),\n      );\n}\n```\n\n- Never create a controller in `build`. It is recreated on every rebuild and never disposed.\n- Manual `addListener` calls have a matching `removeListener` in `dispose`, and in `didUpdateWidget`\n  when the listened object changes.\n\n## Testing controllers\n\nControllers are plain Dart, so they are tested with `test`, not `testWidgets`.\n\n```dart\ntest('invalid code fails without calling the repository', () async {\n  final repo = FakePairingRepository();\n  final controller = PairingController(repo);\n  var notifications = 0;\n  controller.addListener(() => notifications++);\n\n  await controller.pair('abc');\n\n  expect(controller.status, PairingStatus.failed);\n  expect(controller.errorKey, 'invalidCode');\n  expect(repo.calls, 0);\n  expect(notifications, 1);\n});\n```\n\n## When to go further\n\nA `ChangeNotifier` controller with an injected repository is already a small ViewModel. When several\nwidgets need it, or it must survive navigation, provide it through the project's state management\n(provider, Riverpod, Bloc) instead of passing it by hand. See `mobile-mvvm` and `mobile-mvi`.\n",
    "references/migration-to-mvvm.md":
        "# Migrating MVC or MVP screens to MVVM\n\nLoad this when moving an existing controller-based screen to a ViewModel. The approach is a strangler:\nthe old screen keeps working while state and logic move behind it, one screen and one slice at a time.\nEach step is a separate, shippable MR.\n\n## Choose the screen\n\n- Start with the screen that changes most often or produces the most bugs, not the easiest one.\n- Skip screens that are stable, simple or scheduled for removal (see \"When MVC is still fine\" in the\n  skill).\n- One screen per migration. Shared base classes (`BaseActivity`, `BaseViewController`) are migrated last.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Characterization tests. Pin current behaviour before touching it: formatting output, validation\n   results, state after each user action, error messages. If the logic cannot be tested in place,\n   extract it into a pure function with no other change, and test that.\n2. Extract pure logic. Formatting, validation and mapping move into plain types with unit tests. The\n   controller calls them. Behaviour is unchanged.\n3. Introduce the ViewModel behind the existing controller. Empty or nearly empty, injected, created by\n   the platform's mechanism. The controller holds a reference; nothing reads from it yet.\n4. Move state one piece at a time. For each field: add it to the ViewModel's UI state, make the\n   controller render from the ViewModel, delete the controller's field. Never keep both.\n5. Move actions. Controller event handlers call ViewModel methods instead of services.\n6. Move navigation. The ViewModel exposes navigation as state or a one-off event; the controller or a\n   coordinator performs it.\n7. Delete the presenter, the contract and dead code. Remove the old tests only after the new ones cover\n   the same behaviour.\n8. Optionally replace the view layer later (XML to Compose, UIKit to SwiftUI) as its own project. The\n   ViewModel does not change for that.\n\nEach step keeps the app releasable. If a step cannot be shipped alone, it is too big.\n\n## Android: MVP or god Activity to ViewModel\n\nStep 3, ViewModel behind the Activity:\n\n```kotlin\ndata class DevicesUiState(\n    val loading: Boolean = false,\n    val rows: List<DeviceRow> = emptyList(),\n    val error: DevicesError? = null,\n)\n\n@HiltViewModel\nclass DevicesViewModel @Inject constructor(\n    private val repository: DeviceRepository,\n) : ViewModel() {\n    private val _state = MutableStateFlow(DevicesUiState())\n    val state: StateFlow<DevicesUiState> = _state.asStateFlow()\n\n    fun load() {\n        viewModelScope.launch {\n            _state.update { it.copy(loading = true, error = null) }\n            try {\n                val rows = DeviceRow.from(repository.devices())\n                _state.update { it.copy(loading = false, rows = rows) }\n            } catch (e: IOException) {\n                _state.update { it.copy(loading = false, error = DevicesError.Network) }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nStep 4, the Kotlin Activity renders from the ViewModel:\n\n```kotlin\n@AndroidEntryPoint\nclass DevicesActivity : AppCompatActivity() {\n    private val viewModel: DevicesViewModel by viewModels()\n\n    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {\n        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)\n        setContentView(binding.root)\n        if (savedInstanceState == null) viewModel.load()\n        lifecycleScope.launch {\n            repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED) {\n                viewModel.state.collect(::render)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe same screen in a Java Activity, while the Activity itself is not yet converted:\n\n```java\n// Kotlin side: val stateLiveData = state.asLiveData()  (lifecycle-livedata-ktx)\nDevicesViewModel viewModel = new ViewModelProvider(this).get(DevicesViewModel.class);\nviewModel.getStateLiveData().observe(this, this::render);\n```\n\nNotes:\n- The ViewModel survives rotation, so the \"load in onCreate\" call is guarded or moved into the\n  ViewModel's `init`. The rotation bug that MVP had is gone after step 4.\n- State that must survive process death (form input, selected tab) goes in `SavedStateHandle`.\n- During the migration a Java Activity can host a Kotlin ViewModel; convert the Activity to Kotlin\n  separately.\n- Do not wrap the old presenter inside a ViewModel to \"make it survive rotation\". The presenter still\n  holds a view reference that is now retained across Activity instances: a leak.\n\n## iOS: UIKit MVC to a ViewModel\n\nStep 3, an observable ViewModel the view controller owns:\n\n```swift\n@MainActor @Observable\nfinal class DevicesViewModel {\n    private(set) var rows: [DeviceRow] = []\n    private(set) var isLoading = false\n    private(set) var error: DevicesError?\n    private let service: DeviceService\n\n    init(service: DeviceService) { self.service = service }\n\n    func load() async {\n        isLoading = true\n        defer { isLoading = false }\n        do {\n            rows = try await service.devices().map(DeviceRow.init)\n            error = nil\n        } catch is CancellationError {\n        } catch {\n            self.error = .network\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nStep 4, binding the view controller. Pick the option that fits the deployment target:\n\n- iOS 26 and later: UIKit tracks `@Observable` properties read in `updateProperties()` and re-runs it\n  when they change.\n\n```swift\noverride func updateProperties() {\n    super.updateProperties()\n    apply(viewModel.rows)\n    rootView.spinner.isHidden = !viewModel.isLoading\n}\n```\n\n- Earlier deployment targets: keep the ViewModel as an `ObservableObject` with `@Published` state and\n  bind with Combine, or render explicitly after each ViewModel call.\n\n```swift\nviewModel.\$state\n    .receive(on: DispatchQueue.main)\n    .sink { [weak self] state in self?.render(state) }\n    .store(in: &cancellables)\n```\n\nNotes:\n- The ViewModel does not import UIKit and does not hold the view controller.\n- Navigation leaves through closures to a coordinator, as in [UIKit](uikit.md).\n- A migrated screen can later be rendered with SwiftUI inside a `UIHostingController` while the\n  coordinator and ViewModel stay the same.\n\n## Flutter: State logic to controller to ViewModel\n\n1. Move logic from the `State` into a `ChangeNotifier` controller, as in [Flutter controllers](flutter.md).\n2. Inject the repository instead of constructing clients inside the controller.\n3. When the project uses provider, Riverpod or Bloc, expose the controller through it so widgets stop\n   owning it. At that point it is a ViewModel; follow `mobile-mvvm` or `mobile-mvi`.\n\n## MR plan for one screen\n\n```text\ntest(devices): characterization tests for device list formatting and states\nrefactor(devices): extract DeviceRow formatting from DevicesActivity\nrefactor(devices): add DevicesViewModel behind DevicesActivity\nrefactor(devices): render device list from DevicesViewModel state\nrefactor(devices): route device selection through DevicesViewModel\nrefactor(devices): remove DevicesPresenter and DevicesContract\n```\n\n## Definition of done per screen\n\n- [ ] Characterization tests passed before and after every step\n- [ ] Each piece of state has one owner: the ViewModel\n- [ ] Controller or Activity contains only view binding, rendering and lifecycle\n- [ ] Rotation and process death tested (Android); background and foreground tested (iOS)\n- [ ] Old presenter, contract and dead code removed\n- [ ] No behaviour change shipped inside a refactor MR\n",
    "references/uikit.md":
        "# UIKit MVC reference\n\nLoad this when the screen is a `UIViewController` written in Swift. Assumes Swift 6 language mode, where\n`UIViewController` and `UIView` are `@MainActor`.\n\n## Where each responsibility goes\n\n| Found in the view controller | Move it to |\n|---|---|\n| `URLSession`, request building, JSON decoding | A service or repository protocol, injected through `init` |\n| Core Data, SwiftData, file or Keychain access | A store type behind a protocol |\n| Date, number, measurement formatting | Row or display models built from domain models |\n| Input validation | A pure validator type with unit tests |\n| `UITableViewDataSource`, `UICollectionViewDataSource` | A diffable data source plus a cell provider |\n| `pushViewController`, `present`, building the next screen | A coordinator |\n| Large view hierarchy built in `viewDidLoad` | A custom `UIView` subclass returned from `loadView()` |\n| Independent regions (header, map, list) | Child view controllers |\n\nWhat stays: owning the root view, binding data to views, forwarding user actions, lifecycle hooks.\n\n## Custom root view\n\n```swift\n// BAD: 150 lines of constraints in viewDidLoad\noverride func viewDidLoad() {\n    super.viewDidLoad()\n    let stack = UIStackView()\n    // ...\n}\n\n// GOOD: the view owns its layout; the controller binds\nfinal class DevicesView: UIView {\n    let tableView = UITableView(frame: .zero, style: .insetGrouped)\n    let emptyLabel = UILabel()\n    // layout in init\n}\n\nfinal class DevicesViewController: UIViewController {\n    private var rootView: DevicesView { view as! DevicesView }\n    override func loadView() { view = DevicesView() }\n}\n```\n\nThe forced cast is safe here because `loadView()` sets the type in the same class.\n\n## Child view controllers\n\nEvery containment step is required. Skipping `addChild` or `didMove(toParent:)` breaks appearance\ncallbacks, trait propagation and safe-area handling.\n\n```swift\n// BAD\nheaderContainer.addSubview(header.view)\n\n// GOOD\nfunc embed(_ child: UIViewController, in container: UIView) {\n    addChild(child)\n    child.view.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false\n    container.addSubview(child.view)\n    NSLayoutConstraint.activate([\n        child.view.topAnchor.constraint(equalTo: container.topAnchor),\n        child.view.bottomAnchor.constraint(equalTo: container.bottomAnchor),\n        child.view.leadingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: container.leadingAnchor),\n        child.view.trailingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: container.trailingAnchor),\n    ])\n    child.didMove(toParent: self)\n}\n\nfunc remove(_ child: UIViewController) {\n    child.willMove(toParent: nil)\n    child.view.removeFromSuperview()\n    child.removeFromParent()\n}\n```\n\n- Children talk to the parent through closures or a delegate, not by reaching into `parent`.\n- Each child has its own dependencies and can be tested and previewed alone.\n\n## Coordinators\n\nThe coordinator builds screens, injects their dependencies and performs navigation. Screens expose\nintent through closures or a delegate.\n\n```swift\n@MainActor\nprotocol Coordinator: AnyObject {\n    func start()\n}\n\n@MainActor\nfinal class DevicesCoordinator: Coordinator {\n    private let navigationController: UINavigationController\n    private let service: DeviceService\n    private var children: [Coordinator] = []\n\n    init(navigationController: UINavigationController, service: DeviceService) {\n        self.navigationController = navigationController\n        self.service = service\n    }\n\n    func start() {\n        let list = DevicesViewController(service: service)\n        list.onSelectDevice = { [weak self] id in self?.showDetail(id) }\n        navigationController.pushViewController(list, animated: false)\n    }\n\n    private func showDetail(_ id: Device.ID) {\n        let detail = DeviceDetailViewController(deviceID: id, service: service)\n        navigationController.pushViewController(detail, animated: true)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Closures from screens to coordinators capture the coordinator `[weak self]`.\n- The coordinator holds the navigation controller, not the screens. Screens are owned by UIKit.\n- Child coordinators are kept in `children` and removed when their flow finishes. Detect a user-driven\n  back navigation with `UINavigationControllerDelegate.navigationController(_:didShow:animated:)`,\n  or with a callback from the screen's `viewDidDisappear` when `isMovingFromParent` is true.\n- Deep links and push notifications enter through the app coordinator, which routes to the right flow.\n\n## Diffable data sources and cell registration\n\n```swift\n// BAD: model array and index paths drift apart; full reload loses animation and selection\ndevices.remove(at: indexPath.row)\ntableView.reloadData()\n\n// GOOD: identifiers in the snapshot, models looked up by id\nenum Section { case main }\n\nprivate lazy var dataSource = makeDataSource()\n\nprivate func makeDataSource() -> UICollectionViewDiffableDataSource<Section, Device.ID> {\n    let registration = UICollectionView.CellRegistration<UICollectionViewListCell, Device.ID> {\n        [weak self] cell, _, id in\n        guard let row = self?.rows[id] else { return }\n        var content = cell.defaultContentConfiguration()\n        content.text = row.title\n        content.secondaryText = row.lastSeen\n        cell.contentConfiguration = content\n    }\n    return UICollectionViewDiffableDataSource(collectionView: collectionView) { collectionView, indexPath, id in\n        collectionView.dequeueConfiguredReusableCell(using: registration, for: indexPath, item: id)\n    }\n}\n\nprivate func apply(_ devices: [Device]) {\n    rows = Dictionary(uniqueKeysWithValues: devices.map { (\$0.id, DeviceRow(\$0)) })\n    var snapshot = NSDiffableDataSourceSnapshot<Section, Device.ID>()\n    snapshot.appendSections([.main])\n    snapshot.appendItems(devices.map(\\.id))\n    dataSource.apply(snapshot, animatingDifferences: true)\n}\n```\n\n- Use stable identifiers as item identifiers, not whole models; duplicate identifiers crash at apply time.\n- When content changes but identity does not, use `snapshot.reconfigureItems(_:)` instead of a reload.\n- Create cell registrations once, outside the cell provider closure.\n- For a large or reused data source, move `makeDataSource` and `apply` into a dedicated\n  `DevicesListAdapter` object the controller owns.\n\n## Async work in a view controller\n\n```swift\n// BAD: Task lives on after the screen is popped and retains it\noverride func viewDidLoad() {\n    super.viewDidLoad()\n    Task { self.apply(try await service.devices()) }\n}\n\n// GOOD\nprivate var loadTask: Task<Void, Never>?\n\noverride func viewWillAppear(_ animated: Bool) {\n    super.viewWillAppear(animated)\n    loadTask = Task { [weak self, service] in\n        do {\n            let devices = try await service.devices()\n            self?.apply(devices)\n        } catch is CancellationError {\n        } catch {\n            self?.showError(error)\n        }\n    }\n}\n\noverride func viewDidDisappear(_ animated: Bool) {\n    super.viewDidDisappear(animated)\n    loadTask?.cancel()\n}\n```\n\n- Notification observers: store the token from block-based `addObserver(forName:object:queue:using:)`\n  and remove it, or iterate `NotificationCenter.default.notifications(named:)` inside a stored task.\n- Delegates are `weak`.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Unit-test row models, validators, services and coordinators without UIKit rendering.\n- For controller tests, call `loadViewIfNeeded()` and drive it through its public API with fake services.\n- Coordinators are tested by giving them a `UINavigationController` and asserting on `viewControllers`.\n",
  },
  "mobile-mvi": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-mvi\ndescription: \"Designs, implements and tests screens with MVI on Android, iOS and Flutter: intents as sealed types, one immutable state, a pure reducer and side effects fed back as actions, using Kotlin StateFlow stores, Swift @Observable stores (TCA as an option) and Dart Bloc or Cubit. Use for screens with many interacting events, debounced search or racing requests, or when adopting or reviewing Bloc or Redux-style code.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# MVI for mobile apps\n\nBuild screens where every change goes through one loop: the UI sends an intent, a pure reducer computes\nthe next state, effects do the IO outside the reducer and report back as new actions. State transitions\nbecome plain functions that are trivial to test, replay and reason about under concurrency.\n\n## When to use\n\n- A screen has many events that interact: typing, retry, refresh, pagination, selection, live updates.\n- Requests race each other (debounced search, rapid toggles, BLE or socket updates arriving mid-request).\n- State transitions must be auditable or tested exhaustively (payments, onboarding wizards, editors).\n- The codebase already uses Bloc, TCA, Orbit, Mavericks or a hand-rolled store, and new code must fit.\n- Reviewing an MVI store for impure reducers, lost effects or oversized state.\n\nNot for: simple read-only or CRUD screens; MVVM with a single `UiState` is enough there\n(use `mobile-mvvm`). Do not convert a whole app to MVI to fix one complex screen.\n\n## The loop\n\n```\n          +----------------------- State -----------------------+\n          v                                                      |\n   UI renders State --- Intent ---> Store --- (State, Action) ---> Reducer (pure)\n                                      |                              ^\n                                      | effect for this action       | Result action\n                                      v                              |\n                                   Effect runner (IO, timers, navigation)\n```\n\n| Part | What it is | Kotlin | Swift | Dart |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Intent | What the user did | `sealed interface SearchIntent` | `enum SearchAction` cases | `sealed class SearchEvent` |\n| Result | What an effect produced | `sealed interface SearchResult` | more `SearchAction` cases | internal result classes |\n| State | Everything the screen renders | `data class` | `struct: Equatable` | `@immutable` class or `Equatable` |\n| Reducer | `(State, Action) -> State`, no IO | top-level or `object` function | `static func` | top-level function |\n| Effect | IO, timers, navigation, analytics | coroutine in `viewModelScope` | `Task` owned by the store | code in a Bloc handler |\n| Store | Holds state, runs the loop | `ViewModel` + `StateFlow` | `@MainActor @Observable` class | `Bloc` |\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the screen needs MVI (see When to use). If not, use `mobile-mvvm`.\n2. Load the reference for the platform: [Kotlin and Compose](references/kotlin.md),\n   [Swift and SwiftUI](references/swift.md), [Dart with Bloc](references/dart.md).\n3. Write the `State`: the minimum the UI renders. Derive the rest with computed properties.\n4. List intents from the UI as a sealed type, named after what happened (`QueryChanged`, `RetryClicked`).\n5. List results the effects can produce (`Loaded`, `Failed`), each carrying what the reducer needs\n   to reject stale data (for example the query it was for).\n6. Write the reducer and its tests first. It is pure, so the tests need no mocks or dispatchers.\n7. Write the store: apply the reducer, then start the effect for the action with an explicit\n   concurrency policy (cancel previous, drop new, or queue).\n8. Decide how one-off effects reach the UI: state field first, effect channel only if needed.\n9. Build the UI: render `State`, send intents, never mutate state directly.\n10. Write effect tests with fakes and virtual time. Run the existing test suite and report failures.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Intents are one sealed type named after what happened\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: commands named after implementation, open to anything\nfun setLoading(value: Boolean)\nfun callSearchApi(query: String)\n\n// GOOD\nsealed interface SearchIntent {\n    data class QueryChanged(val query: String) : SearchIntent\n    data object RetryClicked : SearchIntent\n    data class ResultClicked(val id: String) : SearchIntent\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\nenum SearchAction: Equatable, Sendable {\n    case queryChanged(String)                  // from the view\n    case retryTapped\n    case resultTapped(Repo.ID)\n    case detailsDismissed\n    case loaded(query: String, [Repo])         // from effects\n    case failed(query: String)\n}\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\nsealed class SearchEvent { const SearchEvent(); }\nfinal class QueryChanged extends SearchEvent { const QueryChanged(this.query); final String query; }\nfinal class RetryPressed extends SearchEvent { const RetryPressed(); }\n```\n\n- Exhaustive `when` / `switch` over a sealed type makes the compiler flag every unhandled intent.\n- Kotlin can split public intents and internal results into two sealed sub-interfaces of one `SearchAction`,\n  so the UI cannot send a result. Swift enums cannot; mark effect-only cases with a comment.\n\n### 2. One immutable State; derive instead of duplicating\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: flags that can contradict each other, list duplicated for filtering\ndata class SearchState(\n    val isLoading: Boolean, val isError: Boolean, val isEmpty: Boolean,\n    val items: List<Repo>, val filteredItems: List<Repo>,\n)\n\n// GOOD\ndata class SearchState(\n    val query: String = \"\",\n    val items: List<Repo> = emptyList(),\n    val isLoading: Boolean = false,\n    val hasError: Boolean = false,\n) {\n    val showEmpty: Boolean get() = query.isNotBlank() && !isLoading && !hasError && items.isEmpty()\n}\n```\n\n- When states are mutually exclusive (loading, content, error), model them as a sealed type or enum\n  instead of booleans; keep shared fields like `query` outside it.\n\n### 3. The reducer is pure\n\nSame state and action in, same state out. No IO, no clock, no random ids, no logging, no launching work.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: IO and a launched coroutine inside the reducer\nfun reduce(state: SearchState, intent: SearchIntent): SearchState = when (intent) {\n    is SearchIntent.QueryChanged -> {\n        scope.launch { repository.search(intent.query) }\n        state.copy(query = intent.query, isLoading = true)\n    }\n    ...\n}\n\n// GOOD: only computes the next state\nfun reduce(state: SearchState, action: SearchAction): SearchState = when (action) {\n    is SearchIntent.QueryChanged ->\n        state.copy(query = action.query, isLoading = action.query.isNotBlank(), hasError = false)\n    is SearchResult.Loaded ->\n        if (action.query != state.query) state else state.copy(items = action.items, isLoading = false)\n    ...\n}\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD: a static function on a value type; `inout` is equally pure for structs\nenum SearchReducer {\n    static func reduce(_ state: SearchState, _ action: SearchAction) -> SearchState {\n        var state = state\n        switch action {\n        case .queryChanged(let query):\n            state.query = query\n            state.isLoading = !query.isEmpty\n        case let .loaded(query, items):\n            guard query == state.query else { return state }\n            state.items = items\n            state.isLoading = false\n        // ...\n        }\n        return state\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Kotlin: `MutableStateFlow.update` may run its lambda more than once under contention. A pure reducer\n  makes that harmless; one with side effects would run them twice.\n- Values the reducer needs from the outside world (current time, generated id) arrive inside the action.\n\n### 4. Effects run outside the reducer and report back as actions\n\n```kotlin\nfun onIntent(intent: SearchIntent) {\n    dispatch(intent)                                   // 1. reduce\n    when (intent) {                                    // 2. start the effect for this intent\n        is SearchIntent.QueryChanged -> search(intent.query, debounce = true)\n        SearchIntent.RetryClicked -> search(state.value.query, debounce = false)\n        is SearchIntent.ResultClicked -> _effects.trySend(SearchEffect.OpenDetails(intent.id))\n    }\n}\n\nprivate fun dispatch(action: SearchAction) = _state.update { reduce(it, action) }\n\nprivate fun search(query: String, debounce: Boolean) {\n    searchJob?.cancel()\n    if (query.isBlank()) return\n    searchJob = viewModelScope.launch {\n        if (debounce) delay(300)\n        val result = try {\n            SearchResult.Loaded(query, repository.search(query))\n        } catch (e: IOException) {\n            SearchResult.Failed(query)\n        }\n        dispatch(result)                               // 3. result goes back through the reducer\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- The effect never writes state directly. Every change is visible as an action in logs and tests.\n- Swift: the store keeps a `Task` per effect and calls `send(.loaded(...))` when it finishes.\n- Dart (Bloc): the handler awaits the repository, then `emit(reduce(state, result))`.\n\n### 5. Effect concurrency is a decision, not an accident\n\n| Policy | Use for | Kotlin | Swift | Dart (Bloc) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cancel previous | Search, filters, latest-wins loads | `job?.cancel()` before `launch` | `task?.cancel()` before `Task {}` | `restartable()` |\n| Drop new while busy | Submit, pay, pull to refresh | `if (job?.isActive == true) return` | `guard task == nil` | `droppable()` |\n| Queue | Ordered writes, BLE command sequences | `Channel` + one consumer, or `Mutex` | `AsyncStream` + one consumer | `sequential()` |\n\n- Results carry their input (query, page, request id); the reducer drops results that no longer match.\n  Cancellation alone is not enough: a result can arrive between completion and cancel.\n- Dart: after an `await` in a `restartable()` handler, check `emit.isDone` before doing more IO;\n  cancellation stops `emit`, not your `Future`.\n\n### 6. One-off effects: state first, channel when it is truly fire-and-forget\n\n```kotlin\n// GOOD (preferred): navigation as state, cleared by an intent after the UI acts\ndata class SearchState(val openDetailsId: String? = null, ...)\n\n// ACCEPTABLE: effect channel, buffered, collected with the lifecycle on Main.immediate\nprivate val _effects = Channel<SearchEffect>(Channel.BUFFERED)\nval effects: Flow<SearchEffect> = _effects.receiveAsFlow()\n\n// BAD: SharedFlow without replay loses effects while the UI is stopped or rotating\nval effects = MutableSharedFlow<SearchEffect>()\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD: SwiftUI navigation driven by state\n.navigationDestination(item: Binding(\n    get: { store.state.selectedRepo },\n    set: { if \$0 == nil { store.send(.detailsDismissed) } }\n)) { id in\n    RepoDetailView(id: id)\n}\n```\n\n```dart\n// GOOD: Bloc state field plus BlocListener with listenWhen\nBlocListener<SearchBloc, SearchState>(\n  listenWhen: (previous, current) => previous.selectedRepoId != current.selectedRepoId,\n  listener: (context, state) { /* navigate, then add(const DetailsOpened()) */ },\n  child: ...,\n)\n```\n\n- A `Channel` keeps effects while nobody collects, but an effect can still be lost if the collector is\n  cancelled between receive and handling. Collect on `Dispatchers.Main.immediate` inside\n  `repeatOnLifecycle(STARTED)`; if losing one is unacceptable, use state.\n\n### 7. The store is the only writer; the UI only renders and sends\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: UI writes state and calls store internals, bypassing the reducer\nviewModel.mutableState.value = state.copy(isLoading = true)\nviewModel.searchNow(\"x\")\n\n// GOOD: one entry point\n@Composable fun SearchScreen(state: SearchState, onIntent: (SearchIntent) -> Unit, modifier: Modifier = Modifier)\n```\n\n- Swift: `private(set) var state` on the store; the view calls `store.send(_:)`.\n- Dart: widgets call `context.read<SearchBloc>().add(...)`; they never call `emit`.\n- Text fields: reduce the text change synchronously on the main thread, otherwise the cursor jumps.\n\n### 8. Keep state lean and render slices\n\nEvery action produces a new state and every observer compares it.\n\n- `StateFlow` and Bloc skip equal states using `equals` / `==`; on large lists that comparison is O(n)\n  per action. Keep big collections out of state that changes on every keystroke, or page them.\n- Compose: pass each child only the fields it reads, not the whole `State`; with strong skipping,\n  unchanged arguments let children skip.\n- SwiftUI: `@Observable` tracks `store.state` as one property, so any change re-evaluates every reader.\n  Pass child views plain values, or split rarely changing parts into separate store properties.\n- Flutter: `BlocSelector` or `context.select` for slices; `buildWhen` on `BlocBuilder`.\n- High-frequency streams (BLE notifications, sensor data) are sampled or throttled before they become\n  actions; one action per packet will flood the reducer and the UI.\n\n### 9. Test the reducer as a pure function, the store with fakes\n\n```kotlin\n@Test fun `stale result for an older query is ignored`() {\n    val state = SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", isLoading = true)\n    assertEquals(state, reduce(state, SearchResult.Loaded(\"kot\", listOf(repo))))\n}\n```\n\n```swift\n@Test func queryChangeStartsLoading() {\n    let next = SearchReducer.reduce(SearchState(hasError: true), .queryChanged(\"swift\"))\n    #expect(next == SearchState(query: \"swift\", isLoading: true))\n}\n```\n\n```dart\ntest('failure for an old query is ignored', () {\n  const state = SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.loading);\n  expect(reduce(state, const SearchFailed('flut')), state);\n});\n```\n\n- Reducer tests: one test per transition, including the stale-result and no-op cases. No mocks.\n- Store tests: fake repository, virtual time (`runTest`), Turbine for state and effect sequences,\n  `bloc_test` for Blocs, `await store.send(...)?.value` for Swift stores.\n- Assert whole states, not single fields, so unexpected changes fail the test.\n\n### 10. Use the platform's established MVI tools when the team has them\n\n- Swift: The Composable Architecture (TCA) gives reducers, effects, dependency injection and an exhaustive\n  test store. Worth it for large apps that want enforced structure; it is a significant dependency with its\n  own learning curve and API changes between versions. Follow its current documentation and do not mix\n  it with hand-rolled stores inside one feature.\n- Dart: `Bloc` for event-driven screens (named events, transformers, event traceability in `onTransition`);\n  `Cubit` for simple screens, with methods instead of events and `emit` directly.\n\n```dart\n// Cubit: fine for a counter-sized screen\nclass FilterCubit extends Cubit<bool> {\n  FilterCubit() : super(false);\n  void toggle() => emit(!state);\n}\n```\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Giant state: one `State` for a whole tab or app. Each screen owns its state; share data through\n  repositories.\n- Reducers doing IO, logging, reading the clock or generating ids.\n- Effects writing state directly instead of returning a result action.\n- Losing effects: `SharedFlow(replay = 0)`, collecting outside the lifecycle, or `trySend` on a full\n  `RENDEZVOUS` channel without checking the result.\n- State explosion: a sealed state per combination (`LoadingWithError`, `RefreshingEmpty`). Use a few\n  orthogonal fields plus one sealed sub-state for truly exclusive cases.\n- Over-engineering: intents, reducer, effects and a store for a settings toggle or a static detail page.\n- Booleans that can contradict each other (`isLoading` and `hasError` both true with no meaning defined).\n- One action per BLE packet or scroll pixel, flooding reducer and UI.\n- A generic \"base MVI\" framework written in-house before two screens need it.\n- Tests that mock the reducer or assert on private functions instead of states.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] The screen really needs MVI (interacting events, races or auditable transitions)\n- [ ] Intents are one sealed type, named after user actions; results carry their input\n- [ ] One immutable `State`, minimal, derived values computed\n- [ ] Reducer is pure: no IO, clock, random, logging or launching\n- [ ] Effects run outside the reducer and report back through actions\n- [ ] Concurrency policy chosen per effect (cancel previous, drop, queue); stale results ignored\n- [ ] One-off effects modelled as state, or a buffered channel collected with the lifecycle\n- [ ] UI renders state and sends intents only; children receive slices\n- [ ] High-frequency inputs throttled before they become actions\n- [ ] Reducer tests per transition; store tests with fakes and virtual time; existing tests pass\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-mvvm` -- the simpler default for screens that do not need a reducer\n- `mobile-mvc` -- legacy UIKit and Activity-centric screens\n- `mobile-design-patterns` -- state, command and observer patterns behind MVI\n- `mobile-kotlin`, `mobile-swift`, `mobile-flutter` -- language rules for each platform\n- `mobile-code-review` -- reviewing stores, reducers and effects\n- `mobile-testing` -- where reducer and store tests sit in the overall test strategy\n- `mobile-kmp` -- sharing a store between Android and iOS\n",
    "references/dart.md":
        "# MVI with Dart, Flutter and Bloc\n\nLoad this when building or reviewing a Bloc or Cubit feature. Assumes Dart 3, Flutter stable, `bloc`,\n`flutter_bloc`, `bloc_concurrency`, `equatable`, and `bloc_test` in tests.\n\nBloc maps onto MVI directly: events are intents, the handler runs effects, `emit` publishes the next state.\nTo keep the reducer pure, handlers call a top-level `reduce` function and do IO only around it.\n\nThe feature: repository search with debounce, latest-query-wins, retry, and navigation to details\ndriven by state.\n\n## Contract\n\n```dart\nclass Repo extends Equatable {\n  const Repo({required this.id, required this.name});\n\n  final String id;\n  final String name;\n\n  @override\n  List<Object?> get props => [id, name];\n}\n\nclass SearchException implements Exception {}\n\nabstract interface class RepoRepository {\n  Future<List<Repo>> search(String query);\n}\n```\n\n## Actions and state\n\n```dart\nsealed class SearchAction {\n  const SearchAction();\n}\n\n/// Events: sent by the UI.\nsealed class SearchEvent extends SearchAction {\n  const SearchEvent();\n}\n\nfinal class QueryChanged extends SearchEvent {\n  const QueryChanged(this.query);\n  final String query;\n}\n\nfinal class RetryPressed extends SearchEvent {\n  const RetryPressed();\n}\n\nfinal class ResultTapped extends SearchEvent {\n  const ResultTapped(this.id);\n  final String id;\n}\n\nfinal class DetailsOpened extends SearchEvent {\n  const DetailsOpened();\n}\n\n/// Results: produced inside the Bloc by effects. Not events, so the UI cannot add them.\nsealed class SearchResult extends SearchAction {\n  const SearchResult();\n}\n\nfinal class SearchSucceeded extends SearchResult {\n  const SearchSucceeded(this.query, this.items);\n  final String query;\n  final List<Repo> items;\n}\n\nfinal class SearchFailed extends SearchResult {\n  const SearchFailed(this.query);\n  final String query;\n}\n\nenum SearchStatus { idle, loading, success, failure }\n\nfinal class SearchState extends Equatable {\n  const SearchState({\n    this.query = '',\n    this.items = const [],\n    this.status = SearchStatus.idle,\n    this.selectedRepoId,\n  });\n\n  final String query;\n  final List<Repo> items;\n  final SearchStatus status;\n  final String? selectedRepoId;\n\n  bool get showEmpty => status == SearchStatus.success && items.isEmpty;\n\n  SearchState copyWith({\n    String? query,\n    List<Repo>? items,\n    SearchStatus? status,\n    String? Function()? selectedRepoId,\n  }) =>\n      SearchState(\n        query: query ?? this.query,\n        items: items ?? this.items,\n        status: status ?? this.status,\n        selectedRepoId: selectedRepoId != null ? selectedRepoId() : this.selectedRepoId,\n      );\n\n  @override\n  List<Object?> get props => [query, items, status, selectedRepoId];\n}\n```\n\n- `Equatable` gives value equality. Bloc skips emitting a state equal to the current one, and\n  `bloc_test` compares emitted states with `==`.\n\n## Reducer\n\n```dart\nSearchState reduce(SearchState state, SearchAction action) => switch (action) {\n      QueryChanged(:final query) when query.trim().isEmpty => SearchState(query: query),\n      QueryChanged(:final query) => state.copyWith(query: query, status: SearchStatus.loading),\n      RetryPressed() => state.copyWith(status: SearchStatus.loading),\n      ResultTapped(:final id) => state.copyWith(selectedRepoId: () => id),\n      DetailsOpened() => state.copyWith(selectedRepoId: () => null),\n      SearchSucceeded(:final query, :final items) => query != state.query\n          ? state\n          : state.copyWith(items: items, status: SearchStatus.success),\n      SearchFailed(:final query) =>\n        query != state.query ? state : state.copyWith(status: SearchStatus.failure),\n    };\n```\n\n- A switch expression over the sealed `SearchAction` is exhaustive; a new event or result does not\n  compile until handled.\n\n## Bloc (store and effects)\n\n```dart\nclass SearchBloc extends Bloc<SearchEvent, SearchState> {\n  SearchBloc(this._repository, {this.debounce = const Duration(milliseconds: 300)})\n      : super(const SearchState()) {\n    on<QueryChanged>(_onQueryChanged, transformer: restartable());\n    on<RetryPressed>(_onRetry, transformer: droppable());\n    on<ResultTapped>((event, emit) => emit(reduce(state, event)));\n    on<DetailsOpened>((event, emit) => emit(reduce(state, event)));\n  }\n\n  final RepoRepository _repository;\n  final Duration debounce;\n\n  Future<void> _onQueryChanged(QueryChanged event, Emitter<SearchState> emit) async {\n    emit(reduce(state, event));\n    if (event.query.trim().isEmpty) return;\n    await Future<void>.delayed(debounce);\n    if (emit.isDone) return; // a newer QueryChanged cancelled this handler\n    await _search(event.query, emit);\n  }\n\n  Future<void> _onRetry(RetryPressed event, Emitter<SearchState> emit) async {\n    emit(reduce(state, event));\n    await _search(state.query, emit);\n  }\n\n  Future<void> _search(String query, Emitter<SearchState> emit) async {\n    try {\n      final items = await _repository.search(query);\n      emit(reduce(state, SearchSucceeded(query, items)));\n    } on SearchException {\n      emit(reduce(state, SearchFailed(query)));\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n- `restartable()` cancels the previous `QueryChanged` handler. Cancelling stops its `emit` calls but not\n  its `Future`, so the handler checks `emit.isDone` before starting the request.\n- `droppable()` ignores retries while one is running.\n- A result for an old query (for example a retry that finishes after the user typed) is dropped by the\n  reducer's query check.\n- Handlers never build state inline; every state comes from `reduce`, so the reducer tests cover it.\n\n## Screen\n\n```dart\nclass SearchPage extends StatelessWidget {\n  const SearchPage({super.key});\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) => BlocProvider(\n        create: (context) => SearchBloc(context.read<RepoRepository>()),\n        child: const SearchView(),\n      );\n}\n\nclass SearchView extends StatelessWidget {\n  const SearchView({super.key});\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) {\n    return BlocListener<SearchBloc, SearchState>(\n      listenWhen: (previous, current) => previous.selectedRepoId != current.selectedRepoId,\n      listener: (context, state) {\n        final id = state.selectedRepoId;\n        if (id == null) return;\n        context.read<SearchBloc>().add(const DetailsOpened());\n        Navigator.of(context).push(MaterialPageRoute<void>(builder: (_) => RepoDetailPage(id: id)));\n      },\n      child: Scaffold(\n        appBar: AppBar(\n          title: TextField(\n            onChanged: (query) => context.read<SearchBloc>().add(QueryChanged(query)),\n            decoration: InputDecoration(hintText: context.l10n.searchHint), // app's AppLocalizations\n          ),\n        ),\n        body: BlocBuilder<SearchBloc, SearchState>(\n          builder: (context, state) => switch (state.status) {\n            SearchStatus.idle => const SizedBox.shrink(),\n            SearchStatus.loading when state.items.isEmpty => const Center(child: CircularProgressIndicator()),\n            SearchStatus.failure => RetryView(\n                onRetry: () => context.read<SearchBloc>().add(const RetryPressed()),\n              ),\n            _ when state.showEmpty => const EmptyResultsView(),\n            _ => ListView.builder(\n                itemCount: state.items.length,\n                itemBuilder: (context, index) {\n                  final repo = state.items[index];\n                  return ListTile(\n                    key: ValueKey(repo.id),\n                    title: Text(repo.name),\n                    onTap: () => context.read<SearchBloc>().add(ResultTapped(repo.id)),\n                  );\n                },\n              ),\n          },\n        ),\n      ),\n    );\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- The text field owns its text; the Bloc receives every change as an event. Do not feed `state.query`\n  back into a controller on every build, or the cursor jumps.\n- `BlocListener` performs navigation once per selection and acknowledges it with `DetailsOpened`.\n- For large screens, use `BlocSelector` or `context.select` so widgets rebuild only for their slice.\n\n## Reducer tests (pure)\n\n```dart\nvoid main() {\n  const repo = Repo(id: '1', name: 'flutter');\n\n  group('reduce', () {\n    test('query change starts loading and clears failure', () {\n      expect(\n        reduce(const SearchState(status: SearchStatus.failure), const QueryChanged('flu')),\n        const SearchState(query: 'flu', status: SearchStatus.loading),\n      );\n    });\n\n    test('blank query resets the screen', () {\n      expect(reduce(const SearchState(query: 'f', items: [repo]), const QueryChanged('')), const SearchState());\n    });\n\n    test('stale success is ignored', () {\n      const state = SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.loading);\n      expect(reduce(state, const SearchSucceeded('flut', [repo])), state);\n    });\n\n    test('failure for the current query shows the error', () {\n      const state = SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.loading);\n      expect(\n        reduce(state, const SearchFailed('flutter')),\n        const SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.failure),\n      );\n    });\n  });\n}\n```\n\n## Bloc and effect tests\n\n```dart\nclass FakeRepoRepository implements RepoRepository {\n  final queries = <String>[];\n  Map<String, List<Repo>> results = {};\n  bool failNext = false;\n\n  @override\n  Future<List<Repo>> search(String query) async {\n    queries.add(query);\n    if (failNext) {\n      failNext = false;\n      throw SearchException();\n    }\n    return results[query] ?? const [];\n  }\n}\n\nvoid main() {\n  const repo = Repo(id: '1', name: 'flutter');\n  late FakeRepoRepository repository;\n\n  setUp(() => repository = FakeRepoRepository()..results = {'flutter': [repo]});\n\n  blocTest<SearchBloc, SearchState>(\n    'typing quickly searches only the last query',\n    build: () => SearchBloc(repository, debounce: const Duration(milliseconds: 10)),\n    act: (bloc) => bloc\n      ..add(const QueryChanged('flu'))\n      ..add(const QueryChanged('flutter')),\n    wait: const Duration(milliseconds: 50),\n    expect: () => const [\n      SearchState(query: 'flu', status: SearchStatus.loading),\n      SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.loading),\n      SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.success, items: [repo]),\n    ],\n    verify: (_) => expect(repository.queries, ['flutter']),\n  );\n\n  blocTest<SearchBloc, SearchState>(\n    'failure then retry recovers',\n    build: () => SearchBloc(repository..failNext = true, debounce: Duration.zero),\n    act: (bloc) async {\n      bloc.add(const QueryChanged('flutter'));\n      await Future<void>.delayed(const Duration(milliseconds: 10));\n      bloc.add(const RetryPressed());\n    },\n    wait: const Duration(milliseconds: 10),\n    expect: () => const [\n      SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.loading),\n      SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.failure),\n      SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.loading),\n      SearchState(query: 'flutter', status: SearchStatus.success, items: [repo]),\n    ],\n  );\n\n  blocTest<SearchBloc, SearchState>(\n    'tapping a result selects it for navigation',\n    build: () => SearchBloc(repository),\n    act: (bloc) => bloc.add(const ResultTapped('1')),\n    expect: () => const [SearchState(selectedRepoId: '1')],\n  );\n}\n```\n\n- `blocTest` runs `build`, `act`, waits, then compares every emitted state in order.\n- Short real waits are needed because the debounce uses a real `Future.delayed`. Keep the injected\n  debounce small in tests; for heavier time logic, wrap the test in `fakeAsync` and elapse time manually.\n- Fakes over mocks: the fake records queries, so the test verifies behaviour without interaction mocks.\n\n## Cubit instead of Bloc\n\nA `Cubit` exposes methods and calls `emit` directly. It suits screens without debounce, cancellation or\nevent ordering needs:\n\n```dart\nclass FavoritesFilterCubit extends Cubit<FavoritesFilterState> {\n  FavoritesFilterCubit() : super(const FavoritesFilterState());\n\n  void toggleOnlineOnly() => emit(state.copyWith(onlineOnly: !state.onlineOnly));\n}\n```\n\n- Switch a Cubit to a Bloc when you need event transformers (`restartable`, `droppable`, `sequential`)\n  or a log of named events (`onTransition`, a `BlocObserver`).\n- Keep the reducer-style discipline in a Cubit too: compute the next state in a pure function, do IO\n  around it, and test the function without the Cubit.\n",
    "references/kotlin.md":
        "# MVI with Kotlin, Jetpack Compose and StateFlow\n\nLoad this when building or reviewing an MVI store in Kotlin. Assumes Kotlin 2.x, AndroidX Lifecycle 2.8\nor newer, Hilt, and Turbine in tests. No MVI library is required; the store is a plain `ViewModel`.\n\nThe feature: repository search with a 300 ms debounce, latest-query-wins, retry after failure, and\nopening a result's details as a one-off effect.\n\n## Contract\n\n```kotlin\ndata class Repo(val id: String, val name: String)\n\ninterface RepoRepository {\n    suspend fun search(query: String): List<Repo> // throws IOException on network failure\n}\n```\n\n## Actions, state and effects\n\n```kotlin\nsealed interface SearchAction\n\n/** Sent by the UI. */\nsealed interface SearchIntent : SearchAction {\n    data class QueryChanged(val query: String) : SearchIntent\n    data object RetryClicked : SearchIntent\n    data class ResultClicked(val id: String) : SearchIntent\n}\n\n/** Produced by effects only. Carries the query so stale results can be rejected. */\nsealed interface SearchResult : SearchAction {\n    data class Loaded(val query: String, val items: List<Repo>) : SearchResult\n    data class Failed(val query: String) : SearchResult\n}\n\ndata class SearchState(\n    val query: String = \"\",\n    val items: List<Repo> = emptyList(),\n    val isLoading: Boolean = false,\n    val hasError: Boolean = false,\n) {\n    val showEmpty: Boolean get() = query.isNotBlank() && !isLoading && !hasError && items.isEmpty()\n}\n\nsealed interface SearchEffect {\n    data class OpenDetails(val id: String) : SearchEffect\n}\n```\n\n- `onIntent` accepts `SearchIntent` only, so the UI cannot fake a `SearchResult`.\n\n## Reducer\n\n```kotlin\nfun reduce(state: SearchState, action: SearchAction): SearchState = when (action) {\n    is SearchIntent.QueryChanged ->\n        if (action.query.isBlank()) {\n            SearchState(query = action.query)\n        } else {\n            state.copy(query = action.query, isLoading = true, hasError = false)\n        }\n\n    SearchIntent.RetryClicked -> state.copy(isLoading = true, hasError = false)\n\n    is SearchIntent.ResultClicked -> state\n\n    is SearchResult.Loaded ->\n        if (action.query != state.query) state\n        else state.copy(items = action.items, isLoading = false, hasError = false)\n\n    is SearchResult.Failed ->\n        if (action.query != state.query) state\n        else state.copy(isLoading = false, hasError = true)\n}\n```\n\n- Exhaustive `when` without `else`: adding an action breaks the build until it is handled.\n- Previous items stay visible while a new query loads; the UI shows a progress bar over them.\n\n## Store\n\n```kotlin\n@HiltViewModel\nclass SearchViewModel @Inject constructor(\n    private val repository: RepoRepository,\n) : ViewModel() {\n\n    private val _state = MutableStateFlow(SearchState())\n    val state: StateFlow<SearchState> = _state.asStateFlow()\n\n    private val _effects = Channel<SearchEffect>(Channel.BUFFERED)\n    val effects: Flow<SearchEffect> = _effects.receiveAsFlow()\n\n    private var searchJob: Job? = null\n\n    fun onIntent(intent: SearchIntent) {\n        dispatch(intent)\n        when (intent) {\n            is SearchIntent.QueryChanged -> search(intent.query, debounce = true)\n            SearchIntent.RetryClicked -> search(_state.value.query, debounce = false)\n            is SearchIntent.ResultClicked -> _effects.trySend(SearchEffect.OpenDetails(intent.id))\n        }\n    }\n\n    private fun dispatch(action: SearchAction) {\n        _state.update { reduce(it, action) }\n    }\n\n    private fun search(query: String, debounce: Boolean) {\n        searchJob?.cancel()\n        if (query.isBlank()) return\n        searchJob = viewModelScope.launch {\n            if (debounce) delay(DEBOUNCE_MS)\n            val result = try {\n                SearchResult.Loaded(query, repository.search(query))\n            } catch (e: IOException) {\n                SearchResult.Failed(query)\n            }\n            dispatch(result)\n        }\n    }\n\n    companion object {\n        const val DEBOUNCE_MS = 300L\n    }\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n- Concurrency policy: cancel previous. The reducer's query check covers the window where a result\n  completes just before the cancel.\n- `CancellationException` is not caught: only `IOException` becomes a result.\n- `ResultClicked` could also navigate directly from the UI. It goes through the store here because real\n  screens usually record it (recent searches, analytics) before navigating.\n\n## Screen\n\n```kotlin\n@Composable\nfun SearchRoute(\n    onOpenDetails: (String) -> Unit,\n    viewModel: SearchViewModel = hiltViewModel(),\n) {\n    val state by viewModel.state.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()\n    val lifecycleOwner = LocalLifecycleOwner.current // androidx.lifecycle.compose.LocalLifecycleOwner\n    val currentOnOpenDetails by rememberUpdatedState(onOpenDetails)\n\n    LaunchedEffect(viewModel, lifecycleOwner) {\n        lifecycleOwner.repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED) {\n            withContext(Dispatchers.Main.immediate) {\n                viewModel.effects.collect { effect ->\n                    when (effect) {\n                        is SearchEffect.OpenDetails -> currentOnOpenDetails(effect.id)\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    SearchScreen(state = state, onIntent = viewModel::onIntent)\n}\n\n@Composable\nfun SearchScreen(\n    state: SearchState,\n    onIntent: (SearchIntent) -> Unit,\n    modifier: Modifier = Modifier,\n) {\n    Column(modifier) {\n        OutlinedTextField(\n            value = state.query,\n            onValueChange = { onIntent(SearchIntent.QueryChanged(it)) },\n            label = { Text(stringResource(R.string.search_label)) },\n            singleLine = true,\n        )\n        if (state.isLoading) LinearProgressIndicator(Modifier.fillMaxWidth())\n        if (state.hasError) {\n            ErrorRow(onRetry = { onIntent(SearchIntent.RetryClicked) })\n        }\n        if (state.showEmpty) Text(stringResource(R.string.search_empty))\n        LazyColumn {\n            items(state.items, key = { it.id }) { repo ->\n                RepoRow(repo = repo, onClick = { onIntent(SearchIntent.ResultClicked(repo.id)) })\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `rememberUpdatedState` keeps the long-lived effect collector from calling a stale `onOpenDetails`.\n- The text field round trip is synchronous (`update` on the main thread), so the cursor does not jump.\n- `SearchScreen` takes state and one callback, so previews pass any `SearchState`.\n\n## Reducer tests (pure, no coroutines)\n\n```kotlin\nclass SearchReducerTest {\n    private val repo = Repo(\"1\", \"kotlinx.coroutines\")\n\n    @Test\n    fun `query change starts loading and clears error`() {\n        val next = reduce(SearchState(query = \"ko\", hasError = true), SearchIntent.QueryChanged(\"kot\"))\n        assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"kot\", isLoading = true), next)\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `blank query resets results`() {\n        val state = SearchState(query = \"k\", items = listOf(repo))\n        assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"\"), reduce(state, SearchIntent.QueryChanged(\"\")))\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `loaded result for current query shows items`() {\n        val state = SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", isLoading = true)\n        val next = reduce(state, SearchResult.Loaded(\"kotlin\", listOf(repo)))\n        assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", items = listOf(repo)), next)\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `stale result for an older query is ignored`() {\n        val state = SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", isLoading = true)\n        assertEquals(state, reduce(state, SearchResult.Loaded(\"kot\", listOf(repo))))\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `failure shows error and stops loading`() {\n        val state = SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", isLoading = true)\n        assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", hasError = true), reduce(state, SearchResult.Failed(\"kotlin\")))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Store and effect tests\n\n```kotlin\nclass FakeRepoRepository : RepoRepository {\n    val queries = mutableListOf<String>()\n    var failNext = false\n    var results: Map<String, List<Repo>> = emptyMap()\n\n    override suspend fun search(query: String): List<Repo> {\n        queries += query\n        if (failNext) {\n            failNext = false\n            throw IOException(\"offline\")\n        }\n        return results[query].orEmpty()\n    }\n}\n\nclass SearchViewModelTest {\n    // StandardTestDispatcher, not Unconfined: each launched effect runs as its own step,\n    // so Turbine sees every intermediate state instead of a conflated one.\n    @get:Rule val mainDispatcherRule = MainDispatcherRule(StandardTestDispatcher())\n\n    private val repo = Repo(\"1\", \"kotlin\")\n    private val repository = FakeRepoRepository().apply { results = mapOf(\"kotlin\" to listOf(repo)) }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `typing quickly searches only the last query`() = runTest {\n        val viewModel = SearchViewModel(repository)\n\n        viewModel.onIntent(SearchIntent.QueryChanged(\"k\"))\n        viewModel.onIntent(SearchIntent.QueryChanged(\"ko\"))\n        viewModel.onIntent(SearchIntent.QueryChanged(\"kotlin\"))\n        advanceUntilIdle()\n\n        assertEquals(listOf(\"kotlin\"), repository.queries)\n        assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", items = listOf(repo)), viewModel.state.value)\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `failure then retry recovers`() = runTest {\n        repository.failNext = true\n        val viewModel = SearchViewModel(repository)\n\n        viewModel.state.test {\n            assertEquals(SearchState(), awaitItem())\n\n            viewModel.onIntent(SearchIntent.QueryChanged(\"kotlin\"))\n            assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", isLoading = true), awaitItem())\n            assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", hasError = true), awaitItem())\n\n            viewModel.onIntent(SearchIntent.RetryClicked)\n            assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", isLoading = true), awaitItem())\n            assertEquals(SearchState(query = \"kotlin\", items = listOf(repo)), awaitItem())\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `clicking a result emits OpenDetails`() = runTest {\n        val viewModel = SearchViewModel(repository)\n\n        viewModel.effects.test {\n            viewModel.onIntent(SearchIntent.ResultClicked(\"42\"))\n            assertEquals(SearchEffect.OpenDetails(\"42\"), awaitItem())\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `MainDispatcherRule` is the same rule as in the `mobile-mvvm` Kotlin reference. Because `Dispatchers.Main`\n  is a `TestDispatcher`, `runTest` shares its scheduler: `delay(300)` is skipped in virtual time and\n  `advanceUntilIdle()` drives `viewModelScope`.\n- `StateFlow` conflates. With an unconfined Main dispatcher and a fake that never suspends, the loading\n  state of a retry can be overwritten before the collector sees it; the standard dispatcher avoids that.\n- Assert whole `SearchState` values. A stray field change fails the test instead of slipping through.\n- Effects are tested on their own flow; they never appear in state assertions.\n",
    "references/swift.md":
        "# MVI with Swift, SwiftUI and a small Store\n\nLoad this when building or reviewing an MVI-style feature in Swift. Assumes Swift 6 language mode,\niOS 17 or newer, and Swift Testing. The store below is hand-rolled and dependency-free; see the end of\nthis file for when to use The Composable Architecture (TCA) instead.\n\nThe feature: repository search with debounce, latest-query-wins, retry, and navigation to details\ndriven by state.\n\n## Contract\n\n```swift\nstruct Repo: Identifiable, Equatable, Sendable {\n    let id: String\n    let name: String\n}\n\nprotocol SearchClient: Sendable {\n    func search(_ query: String) async throws -> [Repo]\n}\n```\n\n## Actions and state\n\n```swift\nenum SearchAction: Equatable, Sendable {\n    // From the view\n    case queryChanged(String)\n    case retryTapped\n    case resultTapped(Repo.ID)\n    case detailsDismissed\n    // From effects only\n    case loaded(query: String, [Repo])\n    case failed(query: String)\n}\n\nstruct SearchState: Equatable {\n    var query = \"\"\n    var items: [Repo] = []\n    var isLoading = false\n    var hasError = false\n    var selectedRepo: Repo.ID?\n\n    var showEmpty: Bool { !query.isEmpty && !isLoading && !hasError && items.isEmpty }\n}\n```\n\n- Navigation is state (`selectedRepo`), so it survives view re-creation and is visible in tests.\n\n## Reducer\n\n```swift\nenum SearchReducer {\n    static func reduce(_ state: SearchState, _ action: SearchAction) -> SearchState {\n        var state = state\n        switch action {\n        case .queryChanged(let query):\n            state.query = query\n            state.hasError = false\n            state.isLoading = !query.isEmpty\n            if query.isEmpty { state.items = [] }\n\n        case .retryTapped:\n            state.isLoading = true\n            state.hasError = false\n\n        case .resultTapped(let id):\n            state.selectedRepo = id\n\n        case .detailsDismissed:\n            state.selectedRepo = nil\n\n        case let .loaded(query, items):\n            guard query == state.query else { return state }\n            state.items = items\n            state.isLoading = false\n\n        case .failed(let query):\n            guard query == state.query else { return state }\n            state.isLoading = false\n            state.hasError = true\n        }\n        return state\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `switch` without `default`: a new action is a compile error until handled.\n- `var state = state` mutates a local copy of a value type; the function stays pure.\n\n## Store\n\n```swift\n@MainActor\n@Observable\nfinal class SearchStore {\n    private(set) var state = SearchState()\n\n    @ObservationIgnored private let client: any SearchClient\n    @ObservationIgnored private let debounce: Duration\n    @ObservationIgnored private var searchTask: Task<Void, Never>?\n\n    init(client: any SearchClient, debounce: Duration = .milliseconds(300)) {\n        self.client = client\n        self.debounce = debounce\n    }\n\n    /// Reduces the action, then starts its effect. Returns the effect's task so tests can await it.\n    @discardableResult\n    func send(_ action: SearchAction) -> Task<Void, Never>? {\n        state = SearchReducer.reduce(state, action)\n        switch action {\n        case .queryChanged(let query):\n            return search(query, after: debounce)\n        case .retryTapped:\n            return search(state.query, after: .zero)\n        case .resultTapped, .detailsDismissed, .loaded, .failed:\n            return nil\n        }\n    }\n\n    private func search(_ query: String, after delay: Duration) -> Task<Void, Never>? {\n        searchTask?.cancel()\n        guard !query.isEmpty else { return nil }\n        let task = Task { [client] in\n            do {\n                try await Task.sleep(for: delay)\n                let items = try await client.search(query)\n                guard !Task.isCancelled else { return }\n                send(.loaded(query: query, items))\n            } catch {\n                guard !Task.isCancelled else { return }\n                send(.failed(query: query))\n            }\n        }\n        searchTask = task\n        return task\n    }\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n- The store is `@MainActor`, and the `Task` inherits it, so `send` from the effect is a main-actor call.\n- `Task.sleep` throws when cancelled, which is the debounce. The `Task.isCancelled` checks stop a cancelled\n  request from reporting `failed` (for example `URLError(.cancelled)`) after a newer query started.\n- Effects never assign `state`; they call `send` with a result action.\n- For time-heavy logic, inject `any Clock<Duration>` instead of a `Duration` and sleep on the clock.\n\n## View\n\n```swift\nstruct SearchView: View {\n    @State private var store: SearchStore\n\n    init(client: any SearchClient) {\n        _store = State(initialValue: SearchStore(client: client))\n    }\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack {\n            List(store.state.items) { repo in\n                Button(repo.name) { store.send(.resultTapped(repo.id)) }\n            }\n            .overlay {\n                if store.state.isLoading {\n                    ProgressView()\n                } else if store.state.hasError {\n                    ContentUnavailableView {\n                        Label(\"Search failed\", systemImage: \"wifi.exclamationmark\")\n                    } actions: {\n                        Button(\"Retry\") { store.send(.retryTapped) }\n                    }\n                } else if store.state.showEmpty {\n                    ContentUnavailableView.search(text: store.state.query)\n                }\n            }\n            .searchable(text: Binding(\n                get: { store.state.query },\n                set: { store.send(.queryChanged(\$0)) }\n            ))\n            .navigationDestination(item: Binding(\n                get: { store.state.selectedRepo },\n                set: { if \$0 == nil { store.send(.detailsDismissed) } }\n            )) { id in\n                RepoDetailView(id: id)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Bindings route writes through `send`; the view never assigns state.\n- Every reader of `store.state` re-evaluates on any state change. Large screens pass child views plain\n  values (`RepoRow(repo: repo)`) so SwiftUI can skip unchanged children.\n\n## Reducer tests (pure)\n\n```swift\nimport Testing\n@testable import Search\n\nextension Repo {\n    static let swiftRepo = Repo(id: \"1\", name: \"swift\")\n}\n\nstruct SearchReducerTests {\n    @Test func queryChangeStartsLoadingAndClearsError() {\n        let next = SearchReducer.reduce(SearchState(hasError: true), .queryChanged(\"swift\"))\n        #expect(next == SearchState(query: \"swift\", isLoading: true))\n    }\n\n    @Test func emptyQueryClearsResults() {\n        let state = SearchState(query: \"s\", items: [.swiftRepo])\n        #expect(SearchReducer.reduce(state, .queryChanged(\"\")) == SearchState())\n    }\n\n    @Test func staleResultIsIgnored() {\n        let state = SearchState(query: \"swift\", isLoading: true)\n        #expect(SearchReducer.reduce(state, .loaded(query: \"sw\", [.swiftRepo])) == state)\n    }\n\n    @Test func failureShowsError() {\n        let state = SearchState(query: \"swift\", isLoading: true)\n        #expect(SearchReducer.reduce(state, .failed(query: \"swift\")) == SearchState(query: \"swift\", hasError: true))\n    }\n\n    @Test func tappingResultSelectsIt() {\n        let next = SearchReducer.reduce(SearchState(), .resultTapped(\"1\"))\n        #expect(next.selectedRepo == \"1\")\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Store and effect tests\n\n```swift\nactor FakeSearchClient: SearchClient {\n    private(set) var queries: [String] = []\n    private let results: [String: [Repo]]\n    private let error: (any Error)?\n\n    init(results: [String: [Repo]] = [:], error: (any Error)? = nil) {\n        self.results = results\n        self.error = error\n    }\n\n    func search(_ query: String) async throws -> [Repo] {\n        queries.append(query)\n        if let error { throw error }\n        return results[query] ?? []\n    }\n}\n\n@MainActor\nstruct SearchStoreTests {\n    @Test func searchLoadsResults() async {\n        let store = SearchStore(client: FakeSearchClient(results: [\"swift\": [.swiftRepo]]), debounce: .zero)\n\n        await store.send(.queryChanged(\"swift\"))?.value\n\n        #expect(store.state == SearchState(query: \"swift\", items: [.swiftRepo]))\n    }\n\n    @Test func newQueryCancelsPreviousSearch() async {\n        let client = FakeSearchClient()\n        let store = SearchStore(client: client, debounce: .milliseconds(20))\n\n        let first = store.send(.queryChanged(\"s\"))\n        let second = store.send(.queryChanged(\"sw\"))\n        await first?.value\n        await second?.value\n\n        #expect(await client.queries == [\"sw\"])\n    }\n\n    @Test func failureShowsError() async {\n        let client = FakeSearchClient(error: URLError(.notConnectedToInternet))\n        let store = SearchStore(client: client, debounce: .zero)\n\n        await store.send(.queryChanged(\"swift\"))?.value\n\n        #expect(store.state == SearchState(query: \"swift\", hasError: true))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `send` returns the effect task, so tests `await` it instead of sleeping.\n- The cancellation test waits a short real delay (20 ms). If the suite grows time-dependent tests,\n  inject a clock and drive it from the test.\n- The fake is an `actor`, so recording queries is data-race free under Swift 6 checking.\n\n## The Composable Architecture (TCA)\n\nConsider TCA instead of a hand-rolled store when:\n- Several teams build features that must compose (parent and child features, shared dependencies).\n- Exhaustive testing of every state change and effect is a requirement.\n- The team accepts a large third-party dependency and its migration cost between major versions.\n\nWhen the project uses TCA, follow its current documentation for reducers, effects, dependencies and its\ntest store. Keep the same principles: pure state changes, effects returned rather than performed inline,\nresults sent back as actions. Do not mix TCA features and hand-rolled stores inside one feature.\n",
  },
  "mobile-mvvm": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-mvvm\ndescription: \"Designs, implements and tests screens with MVVM on Android (Compose, ViewModel, StateFlow, Hilt; legacy LiveData), iOS (SwiftUI, @Observable, @MainActor) and Flutter (Riverpod, ChangeNotifier): single immutable UI state, one-off events, lifecycle-aware collection and ViewModel tests. Use when building or refactoring a screen's state handling, or choosing between MVVM, MVI and MVC.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# MVVM for mobile apps\n\nBuild screens where the UI is a plain function of one immutable state object, the ViewModel owns that state\nand the screen-scoped work, and data comes from repositories that the ViewModel receives by injection.\nThe result survives rotation and process death, renders the same state the same way every time, and is\ntestable without a device.\n\n## When to use\n\n- A new screen or feature needs a ViewModel, state holder or presentation layer.\n- An existing screen has state bugs: lost input on rotation or process death, double loads, stale UI,\n  snackbars shown twice or never.\n- Migrating Java + LiveData + XML screens to Kotlin + StateFlow + Compose, `ObservableObject` to\n  `@Observable`, or `setState` screens to Riverpod or provider.\n- Reviewing a ViewModel for layer leaks, lifecycle issues or untestable code.\n- Choosing between MVVM, MVI and MVC for a screen or a codebase.\n\nNot for: screens with many interacting events, strict event ordering or debounced concurrent requests\n(use `mobile-mvi`), or legacy UIKit and Activity-centric code you only maintain (use `mobile-mvc`).\n\n## When to choose MVVM vs MVI vs MVC\n\n| | MVVM | MVI | MVC |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| State | One `UiState` per screen (or a few observable properties) | One immutable `State`, changed only by a reducer | Spread across controller and views |\n| How UI talks to logic | Calls named ViewModel functions (`onRefresh()`) | Sends one sealed `Intent` type | Controller handles view callbacks directly |\n| Side effects | Inside ViewModel functions | Explicit effects outside a pure reducer | Inside the controller |\n| Boilerplate | Low | Medium to high | Lowest at first, grows fast |\n| Testing | ViewModel tests with fakes | Pure reducer tests plus effect tests | Hard, logic bound to views |\n| Good fit | Most screens: lists, details, forms, settings | Search with debounce, editors, wizards, chat, real-time device dashboards | Prototypes, small UIKit or Activity code already in that style |\n| Platform fit | Android Architecture guidance, SwiftUI `@Observable`, Riverpod, provider | Compose + `StateFlow` store, TCA, Bloc | UIKit `UIViewController`, legacy Activities |\n\nRules of thumb:\n- Default to MVVM with a single `UiState`. It already gives unidirectional data flow.\n- Move a screen to MVI when ViewModel functions start racing each other or the state transitions need\n  to be auditable and tested as pure functions.\n- Do not mix patterns inside one screen. Mixing across screens is fine.\n\n## Layers\n\n```\nUI (Composable, SwiftUI View, Widget)  renders UiState, forwards user events\n   | events                ^ UiState\n   v                       |\nViewModel                  owns screen state and screen-scoped work, maps domain data to UiState\n   |\n   v\nUse cases (optional)       business rules reused by several ViewModels, or too complex for one\n   |\n   v\nRepository                 single source of truth, merges sources, maps DTOs and entities to domain models\n   |\n   v\nData sources               network, database, BLE, files, platform APIs\n```\n\n- Dependencies point down only. Lower layers never import UI or ViewModel types.\n- Use cases are optional. Add one when the logic is shared or non-trivial, not as a pass-through.\n- The ViewModel lives as long as the screen (navigation destination), not as long as the view instance.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Confirm MVVM fits the screen using the table above.\n2. Load the reference for the platform before writing code:\n   [Kotlin and Compose](references/kotlin.md), [Java with LiveData and ViewBinding](references/java.md),\n   [Swift and SwiftUI](references/swift.md), [Dart with Riverpod or provider](references/dart.md).\n3. Write the `UiState`: everything the screen renders, with defaults. Derive values (filtered lists,\n   button enabled flags) instead of storing them twice.\n4. List the user events and name one ViewModel function per event (`onRefresh`, `onFavoriteClick`).\n5. Define the repository interface in domain types, plus a fake for tests and previews.\n6. Implement the ViewModel: combine sources into `UiState`, handle events, map errors to state.\n7. Wire DI and ownership (Hilt, `@State`, Riverpod provider, `ChangeNotifierProvider`).\n8. Build the UI as a stateless function of `UiState` plus callbacks. Preview it with fake states.\n9. Save small user input that must survive process death (filters, query, selected id).\n10. Write ViewModel tests with fakes, run the existing test suite and report failures.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. One immutable UiState per screen, exposed read-only\n\nSeveral independent mutable properties drift out of sync and let the UI write state.\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: public mutable state, three flows that can disagree\nval devices = MutableStateFlow<List<Device>>(emptyList())\nval isLoading = MutableStateFlow(false)\nval error = MutableStateFlow<String?>(null)\n\n// GOOD\ndata class DeviceListUiState(\n    val devices: List<Device> = emptyList(),\n    val isLoading: Boolean = false,\n    val userMessage: UserMessage? = null,\n)\n\nprivate val _uiState = MutableStateFlow(DeviceListUiState())\nval uiState: StateFlow<DeviceListUiState> = _uiState.asStateFlow()\n\nfun onRefresh() = _uiState.update { it.copy(isLoading = true) }\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: the view can write any of these\n@Observable final class DeviceListModel { var devices: [Device] = []; var isLoading = false }\n\n// GOOD\n@MainActor @Observable\nfinal class DeviceListModel {\n    private(set) var state = DeviceListState()\n}\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD: mutable fields, callers mutate the list in place\nclass DeviceListController extends ChangeNotifier { List<Device> devices = []; }\n\n// GOOD: immutable state, replaced as a whole\nclass DeviceListNotifier extends Notifier<DeviceListState> {\n  @override\n  DeviceListState build() => const DeviceListState();\n}\n```\n\nJava:\n```java\n// BAD\npublic final MutableLiveData<List<Device>> devices = new MutableLiveData<>();\n\n// GOOD\nprivate final MediatorLiveData<DeviceListUiState> uiState = new MediatorLiveData<>();\npublic LiveData<DeviceListUiState> getUiState() { return uiState; }\n```\n\n- Swift: separate `private(set)` properties also work and `@Observable` tracks them individually.\n\n### 2. Events go up, state comes down\n\nThe UI calls a named function for each user event and renders whatever state comes back.\nComposables, Views and Widgets below the screen level receive state and lambdas, not the ViewModel.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: leaf composable owns the ViewModel, cannot be previewed or reused\n@Composable fun DeviceRow(id: String, vm: DeviceListViewModel = hiltViewModel()) { ... }\n\n// GOOD\n@Composable fun DeviceRow(device: Device, onFavoriteClick: () -> Unit, modifier: Modifier = Modifier) { ... }\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD: view mutates model internals, no place for validation or side effects\nToggle(\"Online only\", isOn: \$model.onlineOnly)\n\n// GOOD: the change goes through the model\nToggle(\"Online only\", isOn: Binding(get: { model.state.onlineOnly }, set: { model.setOnlineOnly(\$0) }))\n```\n\n- A direct `@Bindable` binding to a model property is acceptable for plain form fields with no logic.\n\n### 3. The ViewModel knows no UI framework types\n\n- No `Context`, `Activity`, `View`, `NavController`, `UIViewController` or `BuildContext` in a ViewModel.\n- No resolved strings. Expose an enum or id; the UI maps it to localized text.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: leaks the Activity, breaks on locale change, needs Robolectric to test\nclass DeviceListViewModel(private val context: Context) : ViewModel() {\n    fun error() = context.getString(R.string.refresh_failed)\n}\n\n// GOOD\nenum class UserMessage { RefreshFailed }\n// UI: UserMessage.RefreshFailed -> stringResource(R.string.refresh_failed)\n```\n\n- Flutter: notifier methods never take a `BuildContext`. Set state (`saved: true`) and let the widget\n  navigate from `ref.listen`.\n\n### 4. Expose observed data as hot state, started by the UI\n\nKotlin: convert repository flows once with `stateIn` and `WhileSubscribed(5_000)`. Collection stops five\nseconds after the last subscriber leaves (survives rotation, stops in the background).\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: loads in init, ignores lifecycle, hard to test the initial state\ninit { viewModelScope.launch { _uiState.value = DeviceListUiState(devices = repo.load()) } }\n\n// GOOD\nval uiState: StateFlow<DeviceListUiState> = repository.observeDevices()\n    .map { DeviceListUiState(devices = it) }\n    .stateIn(viewModelScope, SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5_000), DeviceListUiState(isLoading = true))\n```\n\nSwift: start loading from `.task`, which cancels when the view disappears, not from `init`.\nDart: load in `AsyncNotifier.build`; use `autoDispose` providers for screen state.\n\n### 5. Collect state with the lifecycle\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: keeps collecting while the app is in the background\nval state by viewModel.uiState.collectAsState()\n\n// GOOD\nval state by viewModel.uiState.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()\n```\n\n```java\n// BAD: Fragment lifecycle outlives its view, observers pile up after back navigation\nviewModel.getUiState().observe(this, this::render);\n\n// GOOD\nviewModel.getUiState().observe(getViewLifecycleOwner(), this::render);\n```\n\n- Fragments with flows: `viewLifecycleOwner.lifecycleScope` plus `repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED)`.\n- SwiftUI: async work in `.task` / `.task(id:)`; `@Observable` reads in `body` are tracked automatically.\n- Flutter: `ref.watch` in `build`, `ref.read` in callbacks, `ref.listen` for side effects.\n\n### 6. One-off events are state that the UI acknowledges\n\nSnackbars, navigation after save and dialogs are modelled as nullable state. The UI performs the effect,\nthen tells the ViewModel it is done. Nothing is lost if the UI is not collecting at that moment.\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: SharedFlow without replay drops events when nobody collects (rotation, background)\nprivate val _events = MutableSharedFlow<String>()\n\n// GOOD\nfun onUserMessageShown() = userMessage.update { null }\n\n// UI\nstate.userMessage?.let { message ->\n    val text = message.toText()\n    LaunchedEffect(message) {\n        snackbarHostState.showSnackbar(text)\n        viewModel.onUserMessageShown()\n    }\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n.alert(model.state.userMessage?.title ?? \"\", isPresented: Binding(\n    get: { model.state.userMessage != nil },\n    set: { if !\$0 { model.userMessageShown() } }\n)) { Button(\"OK\", role: .cancel) {} }\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\nref.listen(deviceListProvider.select((s) => s.value?.userMessage), (_, message) {\n  if (message == null) return;\n  ScaffoldMessenger.of(context).showSnackBar(SnackBar(content: Text(message.text(context))));\n  ref.read(deviceListProvider.notifier).userMessageShown();\n});\n```\n\nChannel trade-offs, if the team still prefers an event stream:\n- `Channel(Channel.BUFFERED)` + `receiveAsFlow()` keeps events while no one collects, but an event can\n  still be lost if the collector is cancelled between receiving and handling it.\n- Collect it in `repeatOnLifecycle(STARTED)` on `Dispatchers.Main.immediate` to shrink that window.\n- Never use `SingleLiveEvent` in new code; it supports one observer and hides the same race.\n\n### 7. Dependencies come in through the constructor\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: hidden dependency, cannot be faked\nclass DeviceListViewModel : ViewModel() { private val repo = DeviceRepositoryImpl(ApiClient.instance) }\n\n// GOOD\n@HiltViewModel\nclass DeviceListViewModel @Inject constructor(\n    private val repository: DeviceRepository,\n    private val savedStateHandle: SavedStateHandle,\n) : ViewModel()\n```\n\n- Swift: the owning view creates the model once with its dependencies:\n  `_model = State(initialValue: DeviceListModel(repository: repository))` in the view's `init`.\n- Dart: notifiers read dependencies with `ref.watch(deviceRepositoryProvider)`; tests override that provider.\n- Screen state is never held by a `@Singleton`, a global or an app-wide provider.\n- Navigation arguments reach the ViewModel through `SavedStateHandle` or assisted injection, not a setter\n  called from the UI after creation.\n\n### 8. Small user input survives process death\n\nRotation is handled by the ViewModel. Process death is not: the ViewModel is recreated empty.\n\n| Platform | ViewModel-owned input | View-only state |\n|---|---|---|\n| Android | `SavedStateHandle` (`getStateFlow`, `set`) | `rememberSaveable` |\n| iOS | Restore from the view on appear | `@SceneStorage` |\n| Flutter | Pass restored values in from the widget | `RestorationMixin`, `RestorableInt`, `RestorableTextEditingController` |\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: filter resets after process death\nprivate val onlineOnly = MutableStateFlow(false)\n\n// GOOD\nprivate val onlineOnly = savedStateHandle.getStateFlow(KEY_ONLINE_ONLY, false)\nfun onToggleOnlineOnly() { savedStateHandle[KEY_ONLINE_ONLY] = !onlineOnly.value }\n```\n\n- Save ids, queries, filters and draft text. Never save lists, images or anything the repository can reload.\n- Saved state is not a secure store. Never put tokens or personal data in it.\n\n### 9. The repository is the single source of truth\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: ViewModel talks to Retrofit and Room, maps DTOs, decides caching\nclass DeviceListViewModel(private val api: DeviceApi, private val dao: DeviceDao) : ViewModel()\n\n// GOOD: ViewModel sees domain models only\nclass DeviceListViewModel(private val repository: DeviceRepository) : ViewModel()\n```\n\n- Repositories expose domain models, not network DTOs or database entities.\n- Data that changes over time is observed (`Flow`, `AsyncSequence`, `Stream`), so writes elsewhere update\n  the screen without a manual reload.\n\n### 10. Errors become state; cancellation is not an error\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: runCatching swallows CancellationException; catch-all hides bugs\nrunCatching { repository.refresh() }\n\n// GOOD\ntry {\n    repository.refresh()\n} catch (e: IOException) {\n    userMessage.value = UserMessage.RefreshFailed\n}\n```\n\n```swift\ndo {\n    state.devices = try await repository.fetchDevices()\n} catch is CancellationError {\n    return\n} catch {\n    state.userMessage = .loadFailed\n}\n```\n\n- Dart: catch specific types (`on DeviceException`). In `AsyncNotifier`, `AsyncValue.guard` turns a thrown\n  error into `AsyncError` for the screen to render.\n\n### 11. Test the ViewModel with fakes and virtual time\n\n```kotlin\n@get:Rule val mainDispatcherRule = MainDispatcherRule()\n\n@Test fun `refresh failure shows message until acknowledged`() = runTest {\n    repository.refreshError = IOException(\"offline\")\n    val viewModel = DeviceListViewModel(repository, SavedStateHandle())\n    viewModel.uiState.test {\n        viewModel.onRefresh()\n        advanceUntilIdle()\n        assertEquals(UserMessage.RefreshFailed, expectMostRecentItem().userMessage)\n        viewModel.onUserMessageShown()\n        assertNull(awaitItem().userMessage)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Swift: a `@MainActor` Swift Testing suite awaits the model's async functions directly and uses `#expect`.\n- Dart: a `ProviderContainer` with the repository provider overridden by a fake; `container.listen` keeps\n  `autoDispose` providers alive. Full examples are in each reference file.\n- Fakes over mocks for repositories. Mocks only to verify an interaction that has no visible state.\n- Test state transitions and messages, not private functions.\n- No `Thread.sleep`, `Task.sleep` or real timers in ViewModel tests.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- A ViewModel per composable or per row. One per screen or navigation destination.\n- Sharing one ViewModel across unrelated screens as a global state bag. Share a repository instead.\n- `LiveData` or `ObservableObject` in new code without a reason (interop with legacy screens is a reason).\n- UI state copied into local `remember` or `@State` and then edited there, so two sources of truth exist.\n- Business logic in composables, `body` or `build` (sorting, filtering, validation).\n- Use cases that only forward one repository call.\n- Calling ViewModel functions from the composable body or `body` instead of an effect or event handler.\n- Hardcoded `Dispatchers.IO` in the ViewModel. Repositories own their dispatchers.\n- `@ObservedObject var model = Model()`: a new model on every parent redraw. `@State var model = Model()`\n  is correct, but its initializer still runs on every parent redraw (only the first instance is kept),\n  so keep `init` cheap and start work in `.task`.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] MVVM chosen deliberately (not a screen that needs `mobile-mvi`)\n- [ ] One immutable `UiState` per screen, exposed read-only\n- [ ] Derived values computed, not stored twice\n- [ ] UI below screen level receives state and callbacks, not the ViewModel\n- [ ] No `Context`, `View`, `NavController`, `BuildContext` or resolved strings in the ViewModel\n- [ ] Repository flows converted with `stateIn(viewModelScope, WhileSubscribed(5_000), initial)`\n- [ ] Collected with `collectAsStateWithLifecycle`, `getViewLifecycleOwner()`, `.task` or `ref.watch`\n- [ ] One-off events modelled as state and acknowledged by the UI\n- [ ] Dependencies injected by constructor; screen state not held in singletons\n- [ ] Filters, query, selected id and draft input survive process death; no secrets in saved state\n- [ ] Repository is the source of truth; DTOs mapped at the data layer\n- [ ] Errors mapped to user-facing state; cancellation rethrown or ignored, never reported\n- [ ] ViewModel tests with fakes and virtual time; existing tests pass\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-mvi` -- when the screen needs a reducer, explicit intents and effects\n- `mobile-mvc` -- maintaining UIKit or Activity-centric screens\n- `mobile-design-patterns` -- repository, factory, adapter and other building blocks used here\n- `mobile-kotlin`, `mobile-java`, `mobile-swift`, `mobile-flutter` -- language rules for each platform\n- `mobile-code-review` -- reviewing the resulting ViewModels and screens\n- `mobile-testing` -- where ViewModel tests sit in the overall test strategy\n- `mobile-kmp` -- sharing ViewModels between Android and iOS\n",
    "references/dart.md":
        "# MVVM with Dart and Flutter (Riverpod, or provider)\n\nLoad this when building or reviewing a Flutter screen and its state holder. Assumes Dart 3, Flutter\nstable and Riverpod 3. In Riverpod the \"ViewModel\" is a `Notifier` or `AsyncNotifier`; with the simpler\n`provider` package it is a `ChangeNotifier`.\n\nThe feature: a device list loaded from a repository, pull to refresh, an \"online only\" filter, favorites\nwith optimistic update and rollback, and a snackbar when something fails.\n\n## Domain and data contract\n\n```dart\n@immutable\nclass Device {\n  const Device({required this.id, required this.name, required this.isOnline, this.isFavorite = false});\n\n  final String id;\n  final String name;\n  final bool isOnline;\n  final bool isFavorite;\n\n  Device copyWith({bool? isFavorite}) =>\n      Device(id: id, name: name, isOnline: isOnline, isFavorite: isFavorite ?? this.isFavorite);\n}\n\nclass DeviceException implements Exception {}\n\nabstract interface class DeviceRepository {\n  Future<List<Device>> fetchDevices();\n  Future<void> setFavorite(String id, {required bool favorite});\n}\n\nfinal deviceRepositoryProvider = Provider<DeviceRepository>(\n  (ref) => HttpDeviceRepository(ref.watch(httpClientProvider)), // data layer\n);\n```\n\n- Use `freezed` or `equatable` for value equality and `copyWith` in real code; it is written by hand\n  here to keep the example dependency-free.\n\n## State\n\n```dart\nenum UserMessage { refreshFailed, favoriteFailed }\n\n@immutable\nclass DeviceListState {\n  const DeviceListState({this.devices = const [], this.onlineOnly = false, this.userMessage});\n\n  final List<Device> devices;\n  final bool onlineOnly;\n  final UserMessage? userMessage;\n\n  List<Device> get visibleDevices => onlineOnly ? devices.where((d) => d.isOnline).toList() : devices;\n\n  DeviceListState copyWith({\n    List<Device>? devices,\n    bool? onlineOnly,\n    ValueGetter<UserMessage?>? userMessage, // a getter, so callers can set the field back to null\n  }) =>\n      DeviceListState(\n        devices: devices ?? this.devices,\n        onlineOnly: onlineOnly ?? this.onlineOnly,\n        userMessage: userMessage != null ? userMessage() : this.userMessage,\n      );\n}\n```\n\n## Notifier (the ViewModel)\n\n```dart\nfinal deviceListProvider =\n    AsyncNotifierProvider.autoDispose<DeviceListNotifier, DeviceListState>(DeviceListNotifier.new);\n\nclass DeviceListNotifier extends AsyncNotifier<DeviceListState> {\n  DeviceRepository get _repository => ref.read(deviceRepositoryProvider);\n\n  @override\n  Future<DeviceListState> build() async {\n    final devices = await ref.watch(deviceRepositoryProvider).fetchDevices();\n    return DeviceListState(devices: devices);\n  }\n\n  Future<void> refresh() async {\n    try {\n      final devices = await _repository.fetchDevices();\n      if (!ref.mounted) return;\n      _update((s) => s.copyWith(devices: devices));\n    } on DeviceException {\n      if (!ref.mounted) return;\n      _update((s) => s.copyWith(userMessage: () => UserMessage.refreshFailed));\n    }\n  }\n\n  void setOnlineOnly(bool value) => _update((s) => s.copyWith(onlineOnly: value));\n\n  Future<void> toggleFavorite(String id) async {\n    final current = state.value;\n    if (current == null) return;\n    final newValue = !current.devices.firstWhere((d) => d.id == id).isFavorite;\n    _update((s) => s.copyWith(devices: _withFavorite(s.devices, id, newValue)));\n    try {\n      await _repository.setFavorite(id, favorite: newValue);\n    } on DeviceException {\n      if (!ref.mounted) return;\n      _update((s) => s.copyWith(\n            devices: _withFavorite(s.devices, id, !newValue),\n            userMessage: () => UserMessage.favoriteFailed,\n          ));\n    }\n  }\n\n  void userMessageShown() => _update((s) => s.copyWith(userMessage: () => null));\n\n  void _update(DeviceListState Function(DeviceListState) transform) {\n    final current = state.value;\n    if (current != null) state = AsyncData(transform(current));\n  }\n}\n\nList<Device> _withFavorite(List<Device> devices, String id, bool favorite) => [\n      for (final d in devices) d.id == id ? d.copyWith(isFavorite: favorite) : d,\n    ];\n```\n\nNotes:\n- `build` loads the first time the screen watches the provider; `autoDispose` frees it when the screen leaves.\n- `ref.watch` in `build` for dependencies, `ref.read` in methods.\n- After every `await`, check `ref.mounted` before touching `state`; the screen may be gone.\n- `_update` always transforms the latest state, so a filter toggled during a refresh is not overwritten.\n- A failed refresh keeps the data and shows a message instead of replacing the list with an error page.\n- With code generation (`@riverpod` annotations) the provider is generated; the class body is the same.\n\n## Screen\n\n```dart\nclass DeviceListScreen extends ConsumerWidget {\n  const DeviceListScreen({super.key});\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context, WidgetRef ref) {\n    ref.listen(deviceListProvider.select((s) => s.value?.userMessage), (_, message) {\n      if (message == null) return;\n      ScaffoldMessenger.of(context).showSnackBar(SnackBar(content: Text(message.text(context))));\n      ref.read(deviceListProvider.notifier).userMessageShown();\n    });\n\n    final asyncState = ref.watch(deviceListProvider);\n\n    return Scaffold(\n      appBar: AppBar(\n        title: Text(context.l10n.devicesTitle), // context.l10n: the app's AppLocalizations extension\n        actions: [\n          Switch(\n            value: asyncState.value?.onlineOnly ?? false,\n            onChanged: (value) => ref.read(deviceListProvider.notifier).setOnlineOnly(value),\n          ),\n        ],\n      ),\n      body: switch (asyncState) {\n        AsyncData(:final value) => RefreshIndicator(\n            onRefresh: () => ref.read(deviceListProvider.notifier).refresh(),\n            child: ListView.builder(\n              itemCount: value.visibleDevices.length,\n              itemBuilder: (context, index) {\n                final device = value.visibleDevices[index];\n                return DeviceTile(\n                  key: ValueKey(device.id),\n                  device: device,\n                  onFavoriteTap: () => ref.read(deviceListProvider.notifier).toggleFavorite(device.id),\n                );\n              },\n            ),\n          ),\n        AsyncError() => ErrorView(onRetry: () => ref.invalidate(deviceListProvider)),\n        _ => const Center(child: CircularProgressIndicator()),\n      },\n    );\n  }\n}\n\nextension on UserMessage {\n  String text(BuildContext context) => switch (this) {\n        UserMessage.refreshFailed => context.l10n.devicesRefreshFailed,\n        UserMessage.favoriteFailed => context.l10n.devicesFavoriteFailed,\n      };\n}\n```\n\n- `ref.listen` performs the one-off effect and acknowledges it; `ref.watch` only renders.\n- `DeviceTile` is a separate `StatelessWidget` with a `const` constructor, not a helper method.\n\n## Saved state\n\nRiverpod state is lost on process death. For small widget-owned input, use Flutter state restoration\n(requires `restorationScopeId` on `MaterialApp`):\n\n```dart\nclass _SearchFieldState extends State<SearchField> with RestorationMixin {\n  final _query = RestorableTextEditingController();\n\n  @override\n  String? get restorationId => 'device_search';\n\n  @override\n  void restoreState(RestorationBucket? oldBucket, bool initialRestore) {\n    registerForRestoration(_query, 'query');\n  }\n\n  @override\n  void dispose() {\n    _query.dispose();\n    super.dispose();\n  }\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) => TextField(controller: _query.value);\n}\n```\n\n## Tests\n\n```dart\nclass FakeDeviceRepository implements DeviceRepository {\n  FakeDeviceRepository(this.devices);\n\n  List<Device> devices;\n  Exception? fetchError;\n  Exception? favoriteError;\n\n  @override\n  Future<List<Device>> fetchDevices() async {\n    if (fetchError case final error?) throw error;\n    return devices;\n  }\n\n  @override\n  Future<void> setFavorite(String id, {required bool favorite}) async {\n    if (favoriteError case final error?) throw error;\n  }\n}\n\nvoid main() {\n  const online = Device(id: '1', name: 'Gateway', isOnline: true);\n  const offline = Device(id: '2', name: 'Sensor', isOnline: false);\n\n  late FakeDeviceRepository repository;\n  late ProviderContainer container;\n\n  setUp(() {\n    repository = FakeDeviceRepository([online, offline]);\n    container = ProviderContainer(overrides: [deviceRepositoryProvider.overrideWithValue(repository)]);\n    addTearDown(container.dispose);\n    container.listen(deviceListProvider, (_, _) {}); // keeps the autoDispose provider alive\n  });\n\n  DeviceListNotifier notifier() => container.read(deviceListProvider.notifier);\n  DeviceListState current() => container.read(deviceListProvider).value!;\n\n  test('loads devices', () async {\n    final state = await container.read(deviceListProvider.future);\n    expect(state.devices.map((d) => d.id), ['1', '2']);\n  });\n\n  test('online only hides offline devices', () async {\n    await container.read(deviceListProvider.future);\n    notifier().setOnlineOnly(true);\n    expect(current().visibleDevices.map((d) => d.id), ['1']);\n  });\n\n  test('refresh failure keeps data and shows message until acknowledged', () async {\n    await container.read(deviceListProvider.future);\n    repository.fetchError = DeviceException();\n\n    await notifier().refresh();\n\n    expect(current().devices, hasLength(2));\n    expect(current().userMessage, UserMessage.refreshFailed);\n    notifier().userMessageShown();\n    expect(current().userMessage, isNull);\n  });\n\n  test('favorite failure rolls back', () async {\n    await container.read(deviceListProvider.future);\n    repository.favoriteError = DeviceException();\n\n    await notifier().toggleFavorite('1');\n\n    expect(current().devices.first.isFavorite, isFalse);\n    expect(current().userMessage, UserMessage.favoriteFailed);\n  });\n}\n```\n\n- Override the repository provider, not the notifier; the notifier is the unit under test.\n- `container.listen` keeps an `autoDispose` provider alive between reads in a test.\n\n## Simpler option: ChangeNotifier with provider\n\nFine for small apps or teams new to Flutter. Same layering, more manual work.\n\n```dart\nclass DeviceListController extends ChangeNotifier {\n  DeviceListController(this._repository);\n\n  final DeviceRepository _repository;\n  DeviceListState _state = const DeviceListState();\n  bool _isLoading = false;\n  bool _disposed = false;\n\n  DeviceListState get state => _state;\n  bool get isLoading => _isLoading;\n\n  Future<void> load() async {\n    _isLoading = true;\n    notifyListeners();\n    try {\n      final devices = await _repository.fetchDevices();\n      _state = _state.copyWith(devices: devices);\n    } on DeviceException {\n      _state = _state.copyWith(userMessage: () => UserMessage.refreshFailed);\n    } finally {\n      _isLoading = false;\n      if (!_disposed) notifyListeners();\n    }\n  }\n\n  @override\n  void dispose() {\n    _disposed = true;\n    super.dispose();\n  }\n}\n\n// Wiring\nChangeNotifierProvider(\n  create: (context) => DeviceListController(context.read<DeviceRepository>())..load(),\n  child: const DeviceListScreen(),\n);\n\n// In build: context.watch<DeviceListController>().state\n// In callbacks: context.read<DeviceListController>().load()\n```\n\n- `notifyListeners()` after `dispose()` throws, so async methods guard it.\n- Test it as a plain Dart object: construct with a fake, call `load()`, assert `state`.\n",
    "references/java.md":
        "# MVVM with Java, LiveData, XML and ViewBinding (legacy)\n\nLoad this when maintaining Java screens. New screens should be Kotlin + Compose (see\n[the Kotlin reference](kotlin.md)); this file shows how to keep legacy code correct until it is migrated.\n\nThe feature is the same device list: observe devices, refresh, \"online only\" filter that survives process\ndeath, and a message when refresh fails.\n\n## Contract\n\n```java\npublic interface DeviceRepository {\n    LiveData<List<Device>> observeDevices();          // for example a Room DAO returning LiveData\n\n    /** Callbacks are delivered on the main thread. */\n    Cancellable refresh(Callback callback);\n\n    interface Callback {\n        void onSuccess();\n        void onError(@NonNull IOException error);\n    }\n\n    interface Cancellable {\n        void cancel();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- The repository runs IO on its own `ExecutorService` and posts results to the main thread, so the\n  ViewModel only uses `setValue` and never races on read-modify-write.\n\n## UI state\n\n```java\npublic enum UserMessage { REFRESH_FAILED }\n\npublic final class DeviceListUiState {\n    public final List<Device> devices;\n    public final boolean onlineOnly;\n    public final boolean isRefreshing;\n    @Nullable public final UserMessage userMessage;\n\n    public DeviceListUiState(List<Device> devices, boolean onlineOnly, boolean isRefreshing,\n                             @Nullable UserMessage userMessage) {\n        this.devices = Collections.unmodifiableList(new ArrayList<>(devices));\n        this.onlineOnly = onlineOnly;\n        this.isRefreshing = isRefreshing;\n        this.userMessage = userMessage;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Immutable fields and an unmodifiable list. Override `equals` and `hashCode` if the state is compared\n  (tests, `distinctUntilChanged`).\n\n## ViewModel\n\n```java\n@HiltViewModel\npublic final class DeviceListViewModel extends ViewModel {\n\n    static final String KEY_ONLINE_ONLY = \"online_only\";\n\n    private final DeviceRepository repository;\n    private final SavedStateHandle savedStateHandle;\n    private final MediatorLiveData<DeviceListUiState> uiState = new MediatorLiveData<>();\n\n    private List<Device> allDevices = Collections.emptyList();\n    private boolean onlineOnly;\n    private boolean isRefreshing;\n    @Nullable private UserMessage userMessage;\n    @Nullable private DeviceRepository.Cancellable inFlight;\n\n    @Inject\n    public DeviceListViewModel(DeviceRepository repository, SavedStateHandle savedStateHandle) {\n        this.repository = repository;\n        this.savedStateHandle = savedStateHandle;\n        uiState.addSource(repository.observeDevices(), devices -> {\n            allDevices = devices;\n            publish();\n        });\n        uiState.addSource(savedStateHandle.getLiveData(KEY_ONLINE_ONLY, false), value -> {\n            onlineOnly = value;\n            publish();\n        });\n    }\n\n    public LiveData<DeviceListUiState> getUiState() {\n        return uiState;\n    }\n\n    public void refresh() {\n        if (isRefreshing) return;\n        isRefreshing = true;\n        publish();\n        inFlight = repository.refresh(new DeviceRepository.Callback() {\n            @Override public void onSuccess() {\n                isRefreshing = false;\n                publish();\n            }\n\n            @Override public void onError(@NonNull IOException error) {\n                isRefreshing = false;\n                userMessage = UserMessage.REFRESH_FAILED;\n                publish();\n            }\n        });\n    }\n\n    public void setOnlineOnly(boolean value) {\n        if (value != onlineOnly) savedStateHandle.set(KEY_ONLINE_ONLY, value);\n    }\n\n    public void userMessageShown() {\n        userMessage = null;\n        publish();\n    }\n\n    private void publish() {\n        List<Device> visible = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (Device device : allDevices) {\n            if (!onlineOnly || device.isOnline()) visible.add(device);\n        }\n        uiState.setValue(new DeviceListUiState(visible, onlineOnly, isRefreshing, userMessage));\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    protected void onCleared() {\n        if (inFlight != null) inFlight.cancel();\n    }\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n- `MediatorLiveData` observes its sources only while it has an active observer, so it follows the\n  Fragment's view lifecycle automatically.\n- The anonymous callback holds the ViewModel, not the Fragment, and is cancelled in `onCleared()`.\n- No `SingleLiveEvent`: the message is state, cleared by `userMessageShown()`.\n\n## Fragment\n\n```java\n@AndroidEntryPoint\npublic final class DeviceListFragment extends Fragment {\n\n    @Nullable private FragmentDeviceListBinding binding;\n    private DeviceListViewModel viewModel;\n    private final DeviceAdapter adapter = new DeviceAdapter(); // ListAdapter with a DiffUtil.ItemCallback\n\n    @Override\n    public View onCreateView(@NonNull LayoutInflater inflater, @Nullable ViewGroup container,\n                             @Nullable Bundle savedInstanceState) {\n        binding = FragmentDeviceListBinding.inflate(inflater, container, false);\n        return binding.getRoot();\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void onViewCreated(@NonNull View view, @Nullable Bundle savedInstanceState) {\n        viewModel = new ViewModelProvider(this).get(DeviceListViewModel.class);\n        binding.list.setAdapter(adapter);\n        binding.swipeRefresh.setOnRefreshListener(viewModel::refresh);\n        binding.onlineOnly.setOnCheckedChangeListener((button, checked) -> viewModel.setOnlineOnly(checked));\n        viewModel.getUiState().observe(getViewLifecycleOwner(), this::render);\n    }\n\n    private void render(DeviceListUiState state) {\n        adapter.submitList(state.devices);\n        binding.swipeRefresh.setRefreshing(state.isRefreshing);\n        if (binding.onlineOnly.isChecked() != state.onlineOnly) {\n            binding.onlineOnly.setChecked(state.onlineOnly);\n        }\n        if (state.userMessage == UserMessage.REFRESH_FAILED) {\n            Snackbar.make(binding.getRoot(), R.string.devices_refresh_failed, Snackbar.LENGTH_SHORT).show();\n            viewModel.userMessageShown();\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void onDestroyView() {\n        binding = null;\n        super.onDestroyView();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `getViewLifecycleOwner()`, not `this`, so observers do not pile up after back navigation.\n- `render` is idempotent: it sets every view from state, guarding listeners that would echo back.\n- The binding is cleared in `onDestroyView` to avoid leaking the view hierarchy.\n\n## Tests\n\n```java\npublic class DeviceListViewModelTest {\n\n    @Rule public InstantTaskExecutorRule instantTaskExecutorRule = new InstantTaskExecutorRule();\n\n    private final FakeDeviceRepository repository = new FakeDeviceRepository();\n    private final Observer<DeviceListUiState> observer = state -> { };\n    private DeviceListViewModel viewModel;\n\n    @Before\n    public void setUp() {\n        viewModel = new DeviceListViewModel(repository, new SavedStateHandle());\n        viewModel.getUiState().observeForever(observer); // MediatorLiveData needs an active observer\n    }\n\n    @After\n    public void tearDown() {\n        viewModel.getUiState().removeObserver(observer);\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    public void refreshFailureShowsMessageUntilAcknowledged() {\n        repository.nextRefreshError = new IOException(\"offline\");\n\n        viewModel.refresh();\n\n        DeviceListUiState state = viewModel.getUiState().getValue();\n        assertEquals(UserMessage.REFRESH_FAILED, state.userMessage);\n        assertFalse(state.isRefreshing);\n\n        viewModel.userMessageShown();\n        assertNull(viewModel.getUiState().getValue().userMessage);\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    public void onlineOnlyHidesOfflineDevices() {\n        repository.devices.setValue(Arrays.asList(Devices.ONLINE, Devices.OFFLINE));\n\n        viewModel.setOnlineOnly(true);\n\n        assertEquals(Collections.singletonList(Devices.ONLINE), viewModel.getUiState().getValue().devices);\n    }\n}\n\nfinal class FakeDeviceRepository implements DeviceRepository {\n    final MutableLiveData<List<Device>> devices = new MutableLiveData<>(Collections.emptyList());\n    @Nullable IOException nextRefreshError;\n\n    @Override public LiveData<List<Device>> observeDevices() { return devices; }\n\n    @Override public Cancellable refresh(Callback callback) {\n        if (nextRefreshError != null) callback.onError(nextRefreshError); else callback.onSuccess();\n        return () -> { };\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `Devices.ONLINE` and `Devices.OFFLINE` are test fixtures (one online, one offline `Device`).\n- `InstantTaskExecutorRule` (from `androidx.arch.core:core-testing`) runs LiveData work synchronously.\n- The fake calls back synchronously, matching the \"main thread\" contract without real executors.\n\n## Migration path to Kotlin\n\n1. Convert the state class to a Kotlin `data class`; Java callers keep working.\n2. Convert the ViewModel; expose `StateFlow` and, for remaining Java Fragments, `uiState.asLiveData()`.\n3. Replace the Fragment with a Compose screen hosted in the same navigation graph.\n4. Delete the `LiveData` bridge when no Java observer is left.\n",
    "references/kotlin.md":
        "# MVVM with Kotlin, Jetpack Compose and Hilt\n\nLoad this when building or reviewing a Kotlin ViewModel and Compose screen. Assumes Kotlin 2.x,\nCompose with strong skipping, AndroidX Lifecycle 2.8 or newer, Hilt and Turbine in tests.\n\nThe feature: a device list that observes devices from a repository, supports pull to refresh,\nan \"online only\" filter that survives process death, favorites, and a snackbar when refresh fails.\n\n## Domain and data contract\n\n```kotlin\ndata class Device(\n    val id: String,\n    val name: String,\n    val isOnline: Boolean,\n    val isFavorite: Boolean,\n)\n\ninterface DeviceRepository {\n    fun observeDevices(): Flow<List<Device>>\n    suspend fun refresh()\n    suspend fun setFavorite(id: String, favorite: Boolean)\n}\n```\n\n- The repository implementation owns its dispatcher, caching and DTO mapping. The ViewModel never sees them.\n\n## UI state\n\n```kotlin\nenum class UserMessage { RefreshFailed, FavoriteFailed }\n\ndata class DeviceListUiState(\n    val devices: List<Device> = emptyList(),\n    val onlineOnly: Boolean = false,\n    val isLoading: Boolean = false,\n    val isRefreshing: Boolean = false,\n    val userMessage: UserMessage? = null,\n) {\n    val isEmpty: Boolean get() = !isLoading && devices.isEmpty()\n}\n```\n\n- `List<Device>` is unstable for the Compose compiler. With strong skipping it is compared by instance,\n  which is fine because each state copy is a new object only when something changed. Use\n  `kotlinx.collections.immutable` if profiling shows extra recompositions.\n\n## ViewModel\n\n```kotlin\n@HiltViewModel\nclass DeviceListViewModel @Inject constructor(\n    private val repository: DeviceRepository,\n    private val savedStateHandle: SavedStateHandle,\n) : ViewModel() {\n\n    private val onlineOnly = savedStateHandle.getStateFlow(KEY_ONLINE_ONLY, false)\n    private val isRefreshing = MutableStateFlow(false)\n    private val userMessage = MutableStateFlow<UserMessage?>(null)\n\n    val uiState: StateFlow<DeviceListUiState> = combine(\n        repository.observeDevices(),\n        onlineOnly,\n        isRefreshing,\n        userMessage,\n    ) { devices, onlineOnly, refreshing, message ->\n        DeviceListUiState(\n            devices = if (onlineOnly) devices.filter { it.isOnline } else devices,\n            onlineOnly = onlineOnly,\n            isRefreshing = refreshing,\n            userMessage = message,\n        )\n    }.stateIn(\n        scope = viewModelScope,\n        started = SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5_000),\n        initialValue = DeviceListUiState(isLoading = true),\n    )\n\n    fun onRefresh() {\n        if (isRefreshing.value) return\n        viewModelScope.launch {\n            isRefreshing.value = true\n            try {\n                repository.refresh()\n            } catch (e: IOException) {\n                userMessage.value = UserMessage.RefreshFailed\n            } finally {\n                isRefreshing.value = false\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    fun onToggleOnlineOnly() {\n        savedStateHandle[KEY_ONLINE_ONLY] = !onlineOnly.value\n    }\n\n    fun onFavoriteClick(device: Device) {\n        viewModelScope.launch {\n            try {\n                repository.setFavorite(device.id, !device.isFavorite)\n            } catch (e: IOException) {\n                userMessage.value = UserMessage.FavoriteFailed\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    fun onUserMessageShown() {\n        userMessage.value = null\n    }\n\n    companion object {\n        internal const val KEY_ONLINE_ONLY = \"online_only\"\n    }\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n- Local UI flags (`isRefreshing`, `userMessage`) are small private flows combined with repository data,\n  so there is still exactly one public state.\n- The favorite toggle writes to the repository; the observed flow updates the list. No manual copy of the list.\n- `IOException` stands in for the domain error type the repository throws. Never catch `Exception`\n  around suspend calls without rethrowing `CancellationException`.\n\n## Passing navigation arguments\n\n- With type-safe Navigation Compose routes, read them from `SavedStateHandle` (`savedStateHandle.toRoute<T>()`).\n- With Navigation 3, where the app holds the back stack and the key, pass the key into the ViewModel with\n  Hilt assisted injection (`@AssistedInject`, `@AssistedFactory`, `@HiltViewModel(assistedFactory = ...)`)\n  and create it with `hiltViewModel<VM, VM.Factory> { factory -> factory.create(key) }`.\n- Never pass arguments through a setter the UI calls after creation; it runs again on recomposition and\n  is skipped after process death.\n\n## Screen\n\n```kotlin\n@Composable\nfun DeviceListRoute(\n    onDeviceClick: (String) -> Unit,\n    viewModel: DeviceListViewModel = hiltViewModel(),\n) {\n    val uiState by viewModel.uiState.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()\n    DeviceListScreen(\n        state = uiState,\n        onRefresh = viewModel::onRefresh,\n        onToggleOnlineOnly = viewModel::onToggleOnlineOnly,\n        onFavoriteClick = viewModel::onFavoriteClick,\n        onDeviceClick = onDeviceClick,\n        onUserMessageShown = viewModel::onUserMessageShown,\n    )\n}\n\n@Composable\nfun DeviceListScreen(\n    state: DeviceListUiState,\n    onRefresh: () -> Unit,\n    onToggleOnlineOnly: () -> Unit,\n    onFavoriteClick: (Device) -> Unit,\n    onDeviceClick: (String) -> Unit,\n    onUserMessageShown: () -> Unit,\n    modifier: Modifier = Modifier,\n) {\n    val snackbarHostState = remember { SnackbarHostState() }\n\n    state.userMessage?.let { message ->\n        val text = message.toText()\n        LaunchedEffect(message) {\n            snackbarHostState.showSnackbar(text)\n            onUserMessageShown()\n        }\n    }\n\n    Scaffold(modifier = modifier, snackbarHost = { SnackbarHost(snackbarHostState) }) { padding ->\n        PullToRefreshBox(\n            isRefreshing = state.isRefreshing,\n            onRefresh = onRefresh,\n            modifier = Modifier.padding(padding),\n        ) {\n            when {\n                state.isLoading -> CircularProgressIndicator()\n                state.isEmpty -> Text(stringResource(R.string.devices_empty))\n                else -> LazyColumn {\n                    item {\n                        FilterRow(checked = state.onlineOnly, onCheckedChange = { onToggleOnlineOnly() })\n                    }\n                    items(state.devices, key = { it.id }) { device ->\n                        DeviceRow(\n                            device = device,\n                            onClick = { onDeviceClick(device.id) },\n                            onFavoriteClick = { onFavoriteClick(device) },\n                        )\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n@Composable\nprivate fun UserMessage.toText(): String = when (this) {\n    UserMessage.RefreshFailed -> stringResource(R.string.devices_refresh_failed)\n    UserMessage.FavoriteFailed -> stringResource(R.string.devices_favorite_failed)\n}\n```\n\n- `DeviceListScreen` has no ViewModel parameter, so previews and screenshot tests pass fake states.\n- The snackbar is cleared only after it was shown. Rotation while it is visible shows it again instead\n  of losing it.\n- Navigation on click needs no ViewModel round trip when there is no logic; the route receives a lambda.\n\n## Tests\n\n```kotlin\nclass MainDispatcherRule(\n    private val dispatcher: TestDispatcher = UnconfinedTestDispatcher(),\n) : TestWatcher() {\n    override fun starting(description: Description) = Dispatchers.setMain(dispatcher)\n    override fun finished(description: Description) = Dispatchers.resetMain()\n}\n\nclass FakeDeviceRepository : DeviceRepository {\n    val devices = MutableStateFlow<List<Device>>(emptyList())\n    var refreshError: IOException? = null\n\n    override fun observeDevices(): Flow<List<Device>> = devices\n    override suspend fun refresh() {\n        refreshError?.let { throw it }\n    }\n    override suspend fun setFavorite(id: String, favorite: Boolean) {\n        devices.update { list -> list.map { if (it.id == id) it.copy(isFavorite = favorite) else it } }\n    }\n}\n\nclass DeviceListViewModelTest {\n    @get:Rule val mainDispatcherRule = MainDispatcherRule()\n\n    private val repository = FakeDeviceRepository()\n    private val online = Device(\"1\", \"Gateway\", isOnline = true, isFavorite = false)\n    private val offline = Device(\"2\", \"Sensor\", isOnline = false, isFavorite = false)\n\n    @Test\n    fun `online only filter hides offline devices and is saved`() = runTest {\n        val handle = SavedStateHandle()\n        val viewModel = DeviceListViewModel(repository, handle)\n        backgroundScope.launch(UnconfinedTestDispatcher(testScheduler)) { viewModel.uiState.collect() }\n        repository.devices.value = listOf(online, offline)\n\n        viewModel.onToggleOnlineOnly()\n\n        assertEquals(listOf(online), viewModel.uiState.value.devices)\n        assertEquals(true, handle.get<Boolean>(DeviceListViewModel.KEY_ONLINE_ONLY))\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `filter is restored after process death`() = runTest {\n        val handle = SavedStateHandle(mapOf(DeviceListViewModel.KEY_ONLINE_ONLY to true))\n        val viewModel = DeviceListViewModel(repository, handle)\n        backgroundScope.launch(UnconfinedTestDispatcher(testScheduler)) { viewModel.uiState.collect() }\n        repository.devices.value = listOf(online, offline)\n\n        assertTrue(viewModel.uiState.value.onlineOnly)\n        assertEquals(listOf(online), viewModel.uiState.value.devices)\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `refresh failure shows message until acknowledged`() = runTest {\n        repository.refreshError = IOException(\"offline\")\n        val viewModel = DeviceListViewModel(repository, SavedStateHandle())\n\n        viewModel.uiState.test {\n            viewModel.onRefresh()\n            advanceUntilIdle()\n            val failed = expectMostRecentItem()\n            assertEquals(UserMessage.RefreshFailed, failed.userMessage)\n            assertFalse(failed.isRefreshing)\n\n            viewModel.onUserMessageShown()\n            assertNull(awaitItem().userMessage)\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    fun `favorite click updates the observed list`() = runTest {\n        val viewModel = DeviceListViewModel(repository, SavedStateHandle())\n        backgroundScope.launch(UnconfinedTestDispatcher(testScheduler)) { viewModel.uiState.collect() }\n        repository.devices.value = listOf(online)\n\n        viewModel.onFavoriteClick(online)\n\n        assertTrue(viewModel.uiState.value.devices.single().isFavorite)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `stateIn` with `WhileSubscribed` does nothing until someone collects. Either collect in `backgroundScope`\n  and read `.value`, or use Turbine's `test {}`.\n- When `Dispatchers.Main` is a `TestDispatcher`, `runTest` shares its scheduler, so `advanceUntilIdle()`\n  also drives `viewModelScope`.\n- `SavedStateHandle()` and `SavedStateHandle(map)` work in plain JVM tests; no Robolectric needed.\n",
    "references/swift.md":
        "# MVVM with Swift, SwiftUI and Observation\n\nLoad this when building or reviewing a SwiftUI screen and its view model. Assumes Swift 6 language mode,\niOS 17 or newer (`@Observable`), and Swift Testing.\n\nThe feature: a device list loaded from a repository, pull to refresh, an \"online only\" filter restored\nper scene, favorites with optimistic update and rollback, and an alert when something fails.\n\n## Domain and data contract\n\n```swift\nstruct Device: Identifiable, Equatable, Sendable {\n    let id: String\n    var name: String\n    var isOnline: Bool\n    var isFavorite: Bool\n}\n\nprotocol DeviceRepository: Sendable {\n    func fetchDevices() async throws -> [Device]\n    func setFavorite(_ favorite: Bool, for id: Device.ID) async throws\n}\n```\n\n- The protocol is the seam for fakes. The real implementation owns `URLSession`, decoding and caching.\n\n## State\n\n```swift\nenum UserMessage: Equatable {\n    case loadFailed\n    case favoriteFailed\n\n    var title: String {\n        switch self {\n        case .loadFailed: String(localized: \"Could not load devices\")\n        case .favoriteFailed: String(localized: \"Could not update favorite\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct DeviceListState: Equatable {\n    var devices: [Device] = []\n    var onlineOnly = false\n    var isLoading = false\n    var userMessage: UserMessage?\n\n    var visibleDevices: [Device] {\n        onlineOnly ? devices.filter(\\.isOnline) : devices\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `visibleDevices` is derived, so it can never disagree with `devices` and `onlineOnly`.\n\n## View model\n\n```swift\n@MainActor\n@Observable\nfinal class DeviceListModel {\n    private(set) var state = DeviceListState()\n\n    @ObservationIgnored private let repository: any DeviceRepository\n\n    init(repository: any DeviceRepository) {\n        self.repository = repository\n    }\n\n    func load() async {\n        state.isLoading = true\n        defer { state.isLoading = false }\n        do {\n            state.devices = try await repository.fetchDevices()\n        } catch is CancellationError {\n            return\n        } catch {\n            state.userMessage = .loadFailed\n        }\n    }\n\n    func setOnlineOnly(_ value: Bool) {\n        state.onlineOnly = value\n    }\n\n    func toggleFavorite(_ id: Device.ID) async {\n        guard let index = state.devices.firstIndex(where: { \$0.id == id }) else { return }\n        let newValue = !state.devices[index].isFavorite\n        state.devices[index].isFavorite = newValue\n        do {\n            try await repository.setFavorite(newValue, for: id)\n        } catch {\n            if let i = state.devices.firstIndex(where: { \$0.id == id }) {\n                state.devices[i].isFavorite = !newValue\n            }\n            state.userMessage = .favoriteFailed\n        }\n    }\n\n    func userMessageShown() {\n        state.userMessage = nil\n    }\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n- `@MainActor` makes every state mutation main-thread safe; repository calls suspend off it.\n- `@ObservationIgnored` on dependencies, so reading them does not register view dependencies.\n- After `await`, the array may have changed. The rollback looks the index up again instead of reusing it.\n- `URLSession` reports cancellation as `URLError(.cancelled)`, not `CancellationError`. Map it in the\n  repository or check `Task.isCancelled` before reporting an error.\n\n## View\n\n```swift\nstruct DeviceListView: View {\n    @State private var model: DeviceListModel\n    @SceneStorage(\"deviceList.onlineOnly\") private var storedOnlineOnly = false\n\n    init(repository: any DeviceRepository) {\n        _model = State(initialValue: DeviceListModel(repository: repository))\n    }\n\n    var body: some View {\n        List(model.state.visibleDevices) { device in\n            DeviceRow(device: device) {\n                Task { await model.toggleFavorite(device.id) }\n            }\n        }\n        .overlay {\n            if model.state.isLoading && model.state.devices.isEmpty {\n                ProgressView()\n            }\n        }\n        .refreshable { await model.load() }\n        .toolbar {\n            Toggle(\"Online only\", isOn: Binding(\n                get: { model.state.onlineOnly },\n                set: { model.setOnlineOnly(\$0) }\n            ))\n        }\n        .alert(model.state.userMessage?.title ?? \"\", isPresented: Binding(\n            get: { model.state.userMessage != nil },\n            set: { if !\$0 { model.userMessageShown() } }\n        )) {\n            Button(\"OK\", role: .cancel) {}\n        }\n        .task {\n            model.setOnlineOnly(storedOnlineOnly)\n            await model.load()\n        }\n        .onChange(of: model.state.onlineOnly) { _, newValue in\n            storedOnlineOnly = newValue\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct DeviceRow: View {\n    let device: Device\n    let onFavoriteTap: () -> Void\n\n    var body: some View {\n        HStack {\n            Text(device.name)\n            Spacer()\n            Button(action: onFavoriteTap) {\n                Image(systemName: device.isFavorite ? \"star.fill\" : \"star\")\n            }\n            .accessibilityLabel(device.isFavorite ? \"Remove favorite\" : \"Add favorite\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `@State` owns the model; the view that creates it is the only owner. Child views get values and closures,\n  or the model as a plain property (`@Bindable` when they need bindings).\n- `State(initialValue:)` in `init` is evaluated on every parent redraw but only the first instance is kept.\n  Keep the model `init` cheap; start work in `.task`.\n- `.task` cancels `load()` when the view disappears.\n- `@SceneStorage` restores the filter per window after the app is terminated in the background.\n  It is not encrypted; store only non-sensitive UI values.\n- Dependencies can also come from a custom `EnvironmentValues` entry instead of the initializer.\n\n## Tests (Swift Testing)\n\n```swift\nimport Testing\n@testable import Devices\n\nstruct FakeDeviceRepository: DeviceRepository {\n    var devices: [Device] = []\n    var loadError: (any Error)?\n    var favoriteError: (any Error)?\n\n    func fetchDevices() async throws -> [Device] {\n        if let loadError { throw loadError }\n        return devices\n    }\n\n    func setFavorite(_ favorite: Bool, for id: Device.ID) async throws {\n        if let favoriteError { throw favoriteError }\n    }\n}\n\nextension Device {\n    static let online = Device(id: \"1\", name: \"Gateway\", isOnline: true, isFavorite: false)\n    static let offline = Device(id: \"2\", name: \"Sensor\", isOnline: false, isFavorite: false)\n}\n\n@MainActor\nstruct DeviceListModelTests {\n    @Test func loadPublishesDevices() async {\n        let model = DeviceListModel(repository: FakeDeviceRepository(devices: [.online, .offline]))\n\n        await model.load()\n\n        #expect(model.state.devices == [.online, .offline])\n        #expect(model.state.isLoading == false)\n    }\n\n    @Test func onlineOnlyFiltersDevices() async {\n        let model = DeviceListModel(repository: FakeDeviceRepository(devices: [.online, .offline]))\n        await model.load()\n\n        model.setOnlineOnly(true)\n\n        #expect(model.state.visibleDevices == [.online])\n    }\n\n    @Test func loadFailureShowsMessageUntilAcknowledged() async {\n        let model = DeviceListModel(repository: FakeDeviceRepository(loadError: URLError(.notConnectedToInternet)))\n\n        await model.load()\n        #expect(model.state.userMessage == .loadFailed)\n\n        model.userMessageShown()\n        #expect(model.state.userMessage == nil)\n    }\n\n    @Test func favoriteFailureRollsBack() async throws {\n        let repository = FakeDeviceRepository(devices: [.online], favoriteError: URLError(.timedOut))\n        let model = DeviceListModel(repository: repository)\n        await model.load()\n\n        await model.toggleFavorite(Device.online.id)\n\n        let device = try #require(model.state.devices.first)\n        #expect(device.isFavorite == false)\n        #expect(model.state.userMessage == .favoriteFailed)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- The suite is `@MainActor` because the model is. Tests `await` the model's async functions directly;\n  no sleeps, no expectations with timeouts.\n- `Error` is `Sendable`, so a struct fake holding `(any Error)?` is `Sendable` without annotations.\n- For time-based logic (debounce, polling), inject `any Clock<Duration>` into the model.\n",
  },
  "mobile-performance": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-performance\ndescription: \"Measures and fixes Android, iOS and Flutter app performance: startup (Baseline Profiles, App Startup, iOS launch time), jank (Compose recomposition, SwiftUI body cost, Flutter UI vs raster thread), memory leaks, app size, battery and network use. Uses Macrobenchmark, Perfetto, LeakCanary, Instruments, MetricKit, Flutter DevTools and Android vitals. Use when an app is slow to start, janky, leaking, too big or draining battery, or when asked to profile or benchmark.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile performance\n\nFind and fix the performance problems users actually feel: slow launch, dropped frames, hangs, memory\ngrowth, large downloads and battery drain. The agent measures first on a release-like build on a real\nlow or mid-range device, changes one thing, measures again under the same conditions, and reports the\nnumbers, not impressions.\n\n## When to use\n\n- \"The app is slow to start\", \"scrolling stutters\", \"the UI freezes\", \"memory keeps growing\".\n- Android vitals, the Xcode Organizer or MetricKit show slow starts, ANRs, hangs, hitches or high memory.\n- The APK, AAB or IPA grew, or users complain about battery or mobile data use.\n- Setting up Macrobenchmark, Baseline Profiles, XCTest performance tests or Flutter profiling.\n- Reviewing a change that touches the startup path, a scrolling list, images, background work or sync.\n\nNot for: general code review (use `mobile-code-review`), Compose, SwiftUI or widget API usage without a\nmeasured problem (use `mobile-kotlin`, `mobile-swift`, `mobile-flutter`), BLE throughput and connection\ntuning (use `mobile-ble`), or CI pipeline setup (use `mobile-ci-cd`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Name the symptom and the one metric that captures it (see the table below). \"Feels slow\" is not a\n   metric; \"cold start TTID median 1 850 ms on a Galaxy A15\" is.\n2. Fix the test conditions before measuring, and keep them for the \"after\" run.\n   - Build: release-like. Android `benchmark` build type (non-debuggable, `profileable`, R8 on). iOS\n     Release configuration through Profile. Flutter `--profile`.\n   - Device: a real low or mid-range phone your users own. Never an emulator or simulator for numbers.\n   - State: charged above 50 %, not hot, other apps closed, same OS version, same network.\n   - Details in [references/measuring.md](references/measuring.md).\n3. Measure the baseline with enough runs to trust it (startup at least 10 iterations, frames over a\n   scripted scroll of at least 5 runs). Record median and P90, not the best run.\n4. Find the cause with a trace (Perfetto, Instruments, DevTools), not by reading code alone. Load the\n   platform reference: [Android](references/android.md), [iOS](references/ios.md),\n   [Flutter](references/flutter.md). Flutter apps also need the host tools for native startup.\n5. Change one thing. Several changes at once make it impossible to tell which one helped or hurt.\n6. Re-measure with the same build type, device, iterations and statistic. Keep the change only if the\n   difference is larger than the run-to-run noise.\n7. Lock the gain in: a benchmark or performance test in CI, and field monitoring (Android vitals,\n   Organizer, MetricKit) to confirm it in production.\n8. Report with the output format below. If a number could not be measured, say so; never estimate it.\n\n## Metrics and thresholds\n\n| Area | Lab metric | Field source | Reference values |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Startup (Android) | `timeToInitialDisplayMs`, `timeToFullDisplayMs` (`StartupTimingMetric`) | Android vitals | Vitals flags slow cold start at 5 s or more, warm 2 s or more, hot 1 s or more |\n| Startup (iOS) | `XCTApplicationLaunchMetric`, App Launch template | Organizer Launch Time, `MXAppLaunchMetric` | Time to first frame after the launch screen; compare release over release |\n| Startup (Flutter) | `--trace-startup` `timeToFirstFrameMicros` | Host platform vitals | Same as the host platform |\n| Jank (Android) | `frameDurationCpuMs`, `frameOverrunMs` (`FrameTimingMetric`) | Vitals slow and frozen frames | Slow session: more than 50 % of frames over 16 ms. Frozen: more than 0.1 % over 700 ms |\n| Jank (iOS) | `XCTOSSignpostMetric.scrollingAndDecelerationMetric`, Animation Hitches | Organizer hitch rate | Hitch rate at or below 10 ms/s is good. Main-thread work per frame under about 5 ms |\n| Hangs | Main thread blocked | ANR rate, Organizer hangs, `MXHangDiagnostic` | ANR core vital 0.47 % overall, 8 % per phone model. iOS: delays over 100 ms are noticeable |\n| Frames (Flutter) | UI and raster time per frame in DevTools | Host platform vitals | About 16 ms per frame at 60 Hz, 8 ms at 120 Hz |\n| Memory | Heap and leaks in LeakCanary, Allocations, DevTools | Vitals memory metrics, `memoryMetrics` | No retained screens after navigating back |\n| Battery | Wake locks, wakeups, background network | Vitals battery metrics, `cpuMetrics` | Vitals: background network over 50 MB per day, Wi-Fi scans over 4 per hour |\n| Size | Download and install size per variant | Play Console, App Store Connect | Track every release; investigate any jump |\n\nThresholds change. Check the current Android vitals and Apple pages before quoting them to a team.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Numbers come only from release-like builds on real devices\n\nDebug builds run with JIT, assertions and debugger hooks. Their numbers are meaningless.\n\nKotlin (Gradle):\n```kotlin\n// BAD: benchmarking the debug variant on an emulator\n// GOOD: release-like and debug-signed; add <profileable android:shell=\"true\"/> to the manifest\ncreate(\"benchmark\") {\n    initWith(getByName(\"release\"))\n    signingConfig = signingConfigs.getByName(\"debug\")\n    matchingFallbacks += listOf(\"release\")\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: timing launch with print(Date()) in a Debug run from Xcode\n// GOOD: XCTest performance test, Release configuration, physical device\nfunc testLaunch() {\n    measure(metrics: [XCTApplicationLaunchMetric()]) { XCUIApplication().launch() }\n}\n```\n\nDart:\n```sh\nflutter run            # BAD: debug, JIT, asserts\nflutter run --profile  # GOOD: AOT, with tracing for DevTools\n```\n\n### 2. Android ships a Baseline Profile and a startup profile\n\nWithout one, a fresh install interprets and JIT-compiles the startup path. Google reports about 30 %\nfaster execution of covered code from the first launch.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: no profile, first launches after every update run cold code\n// GOOD: generated by the Baseline Profile Gradle plugin (androidx.baselineprofile)\n@get:Rule val rule = BaselineProfileRule()\n\n@Test fun generate() = rule.collect(\n    packageName = \"com.example.app\",\n    includeInStartupProfile = true,\n) {\n    pressHome()\n    startActivityAndWait()\n}\n```\n\n- Profiles are installed only for release builds. Regenerate them when the startup path or main user\n  journeys change. Full setup in [references/android.md](references/android.md).\n- Flutter apps: the Dart code is already AOT-compiled; a profile helps only the Kotlin and Java side.\n\n### 3. Nothing non-critical runs before the first frame\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: every SDK initialized on the main thread before any UI\nclass App : Application() {\n    override fun onCreate() {\n        super.onCreate()\n        Analytics.init(this); RemoteConfig.fetchBlocking(); Maps.init(this)\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: only what the first screen needs; the rest is lazy or deferred\n@HiltAndroidApp\nclass App : Application() {\n    @Inject lateinit var analytics: Lazy<Analytics> // dagger.Lazy, created on first use\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: synchronous network and database work in didFinishLaunchingWithOptions or App.init\ninit() { try? database.migrateAndSyncNow() }\n// GOOD: show cached content, refresh after the first frame\n.task { await model.refreshInBackground() }\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD: await analytics.init(); await remoteConfig.fetchAndActivate(); before runApp\n// GOOD: await only what the first frame needs\nFuture<void> main() async {\n  WidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();\n  final settings = await Settings.load();\n  runApp(App(settings: settings));\n  unawaited(analytics.init());\n}\n```\n\n- App Startup (`androidx.startup`) replaces many library content providers with one, and lets you turn\n  off automatic initialization and call `AppInitializer` later. It does not make slow init fast by itself.\n- iOS: fewer embedded dynamic frameworks, no `+load` or static initializers, and mergeable libraries\n  (Xcode 15+) reduce pre-`main` time.\n\n### 4. Report when the screen is really usable\n\nTTID ends at the first frame, often a spinner. Mark the point where real content is shown.\n\nKotlin (Compose):\n```kotlin\n// BAD: no signal, TTFD is never reported\n// GOOD\nReportDrawnWhen { state.items.isNotEmpty() }\n```\n\nSwift (iOS 16+):\n```swift\n// GOOD: extended launch counted in MetricKit until content is ready\nlet id = MXLaunchTaskID(\"load-feed\")\ntry MXMetricManager.extendLaunchMeasurement(forTaskID: id) // main thread, before the first scene is active\nawait model.loadFeed()\ntry MXMetricManager.finishExtendedLaunchMeasurement(forTaskID: id)\n```\n\nFlutter: mark the moment with a `dart:developer` `Timeline` event for lab traces; field launch numbers\ncome from the host platform ([references/flutter.md](references/flutter.md)).\n\n### 5. Frame work stays inside the frame budget\n\nParsing, sorting, image decoding and disk access belong off the main thread or UI isolate.\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: 2 MB JSON parsed on the main thread during scroll\nval items = json.decodeFromString<List<Item>>(body)\n// GOOD\nval items = withContext(defaultDispatcher) { json.decodeFromString<List<Item>>(body) }\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: Task inherits the main actor, the parse still blocks the UI\nButton(\"Load\") { Task { items = parse(data) } }\n// GOOD: @concurrent (Swift 6.2) always leaves the caller's actor\n@concurrent nonisolated func parse(_ data: Data) async -> [Item] { /* ... */ }\nButton(\"Load\") { Task { items = await parse(data) } }\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD\nfinal items = parseItems(body);\n// GOOD\nfinal items = await Isolate.run(() => parseItems(body));\n```\n\n### 6. Update only what changed\n\nCompose, SwiftUI and Flutter all redo work for every reader of changed state. Narrow the readers.\n\nKotlin (Compose):\n```kotlin\n// BAD: reads scroll offset in composition, recomposes every pixel\nBox(Modifier.offset(y = (scroll.value / 2).dp))\n// GOOD: read in the layout phase, no recomposition\nBox(Modifier.offset { IntOffset(0, scroll.value / 2) })\n```\n\nSwift (SwiftUI):\n```swift\n// BAD: ObservableObject, every @Published change re-evaluates every observing body\nfinal class Feed: ObservableObject { @Published var items = [Item](); @Published var clock = Date() }\n// GOOD: @Observable tracks only the properties each body reads\n@Observable final class Feed { var items = [Item](); var clock = Date() }\n```\n\nDart (Flutter):\n```dart\n// BAD: whole screen rebuilds when the counter changes\nfinal state = context.watch<CartModel>();\n// GOOD: only this widget rebuilds, and only when count changes\nfinal count = context.select((CartModel c) => c.count);\n```\n\nMore patterns: `mobile-kotlin` references/compose.md, `mobile-swift` references/swiftui.md,\n`mobile-flutter` references/performance.md.\n\n### 7. Images are decoded at display size\n\nA 4000x3000 photo is about 48 MB of pixels, whatever size it is drawn at.\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD\nval bmp = BitmapFactory.decodeFile(path)\n// GOOD: let the image loader size it to the view, or downsample yourself\nAsyncImage(model = url, contentDescription = null, modifier = Modifier.size(48.dp)) // Coil\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD\nlet image = UIImage(contentsOfFile: path)\n// GOOD: ImageIO thumbnail at the target pixel size (48 pt at 3x)\nlet source = CGImageSourceCreateWithURL(url as CFURL, nil)!\nlet options = [kCGImageSourceCreateThumbnailFromImageAlways: true,\n               kCGImageSourceThumbnailMaxPixelSize: 144] as CFDictionary\nlet thumb = CGImageSourceCreateThumbnailAtIndex(source, 0, options)\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD\nImage.file(file, width: 48);\n// GOOD\nImage.file(file, width: 48, cacheWidth: (48 * MediaQuery.devicePixelRatioOf(context)).round());\n```\n\n### 8. Leaks are found with a tool, then fixed at the reference\n\nNavigate into and out of a screen five times. If its objects are still alive, something holds them.\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: singleton keeps the listener, and through it the Activity\noverride fun onStart() { super.onStart(); DeviceHub.addListener(listener) }\n// GOOD: paired registration\noverride fun onStop() { DeviceHub.removeListener(listener); super.onStop() }\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: infinite async loop keeps self alive forever\nTask { for await value in stream { self.render(value) } }\n// GOOD: owned by the view lifecycle, cancelled when it disappears\n.task { for await value in model.stream { model.render(value) } }\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD: controller never disposed, ticker and listeners leak\nlate final _controller = AnimationController(vsync: this, duration: _d);\n// GOOD\n@override\nvoid dispose() { _controller.dispose(); super.dispose(); }\n```\n\nTools: LeakCanary (debug builds only), Xcode Memory Graph and Instruments Leaks and Allocations\ngenerations, DevTools Memory view Diff Snapshots.\n\n### 9. Background work is batched, constrained and deferrable\n\nEvery wakeup and radio power-up costs battery. Let the OS schedule work when it is cheap.\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: own timer, wakes the device every minute on any network\nhandler.postDelayed(syncRunnable, 60_000)\n// GOOD: one unique periodic job, unmetered network, battery not low\nval request = PeriodicWorkRequestBuilder<SyncWorker>(6, TimeUnit.HOURS)\n    .setConstraints(Constraints.Builder()\n        .setRequiredNetworkType(NetworkType.UNMETERED)\n        .setRequiresBatteryNotLow(true)\n        .build())\n    .build()\nWorkManager.getInstance(context)\n    .enqueueUniquePeriodicWork(\"sync\", ExistingPeriodicWorkPolicy.KEEP, request)\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: large upload started on a default session over cellular\n// GOOD: background session the system schedules, or a BGProcessingTask\nlet config = URLSessionConfiguration.background(withIdentifier: \"com.example.upload\")\nconfig.isDiscretionary = true\nlet request = BGProcessingTaskRequest(identifier: \"com.example.cleanup\")\nrequest.requiresExternalPower = true\nrequest.requiresNetworkConnectivity = true\ntry BGTaskScheduler.shared.submit(request)\n```\n\n- iOS: `BGTaskScheduler` identifiers must be listed under `BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers` in\n  Info.plist, with the Background Modes capability. Call out that Info.plist and capability change.\n- Android 15+: `dataSync` and `mediaProcessing` foreground services are limited to 6 hours per 24 hours.\n  Android 16 applies job runtime quotas by standby bucket, also to jobs started in the foreground.\n- Test with Doze and standby forced (commands in [references/android.md](references/android.md)).\n\n### 10. Network traffic is small, cached and not polled\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: no cache, the same 300 KB list downloaded on every screen open\nval client = OkHttpClient()\n// GOOD: HTTP cache honours Cache-Control and ETag from the server\nval client = OkHttpClient.Builder()\n    .cache(Cache(File(context.cacheDir, \"http\"), 20L * 1024 * 1024))\n    .build()\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: prefetching on a default session over cellular and in Low Data Mode\n// GOOD: discretionary traffic waits for a cheap network\nconfig.allowsConstrainedNetworkAccess = false\nconfig.allowsExpensiveNetworkAccess = false\nconfig.waitsForConnectivity = true\n```\n\n- Paginate; request only the fields the screen shows; let the server compress responses.\n- Replace polling with push (FCM, APNs) or with server-driven `Cache-Control` and conditional requests.\n- Coalesce telemetry into batches. Device identifiers in payloads are personal data (see\n  `mobile-eu-compliance`).\n\n### 11. App size is measured per release and kept down\n\n- Android: `isMinifyEnabled = true` and `isShrinkResources = true` in release (BAD: both off).\n  Ship an AAB; inspect with APK Analyzer (Build > Analyze APK, Compare with previous APK).\n- iOS: export with \"All compatible device variants\" and read `App Thinning Size Report.txt`;\n  App Store Connect shows the real per-device sizes.\n- Flutter: `flutter build appbundle --analyze-size` or `flutter build ios --analyze-size`, then the\n  DevTools app size tool. `--split-debug-info` removes symbols from the binary; keep them for crashes.\n\n### 12. Regressions are caught before users see them\n\n- Run Macrobenchmark or XCTest performance tests on a fixed device in CI, nightly at least.\n- iOS: set XCTest baselines per device; the test fails when a metric regresses past the baseline.\n- Subscribe to MetricKit and watch Android vitals after each staged rollout step.\n- Flutter: `flutter drive --profile` with `traceAction` gives a frame summary per run\n  ([references/flutter.md](references/flutter.md)). MetricKit subscriber: [references/ios.md](references/ios.md).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Optimizing without a baseline number, or claiming an improvement without an \"after\" number.\n- Measuring a debug build, an emulator, a simulator or a flagship phone only.\n- Reporting the single best run. Use median and P90 over repeated runs.\n- Changing several things, then crediting the one you expected to help.\n- Adding `remember`, `@Stable`, `RepaintBoundary`, `const` or caching everywhere \"for performance\"\n  without a trace showing the problem.\n- Treating a splash screen as a startup fix. It hides TTID; it does not shorten it.\n- Moving startup work to a background thread that the first screen then waits on.\n- Keeping LeakCanary, StrictMode penalties or verbose tracing in release builds.\n- Requesting a battery optimization exemption instead of using WorkManager, FCM or BGTaskScheduler.\n- Polling a server every few seconds from the foreground or a foreground service.\n- Comparing app sizes between a universal APK or IPA and a thinned or Play-delivered download.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Symptom expressed as one metric, with device, OS version and build type written down\n- [ ] Release-like build (non-debuggable, `profileable`, R8 on / Release / `--profile`) on a real device\n- [ ] Baseline measured with enough iterations; median and P90 recorded\n- [ ] Cause located in a trace (Perfetto, Instruments, DevTools), not guessed from code\n- [ ] One change at a time; same conditions for the \"after\" run\n- [ ] Android: Baseline Profile and startup profile generated and checked with `CompilationMode`\n- [ ] Startup path free of non-critical SDK init, network and disk work; TTFD or extended launch reported\n- [ ] No parsing, decoding or I/O on the main thread or UI isolate during scroll or animation\n- [ ] Images decoded at display size\n- [ ] Repeated navigation leaves no retained screens (LeakCanary, Memory Graph, DevTools diff)\n- [ ] Background work uses WorkManager or BGTaskScheduler with constraints; no polling; HTTP cache on\n- [ ] Size measured per variant and compared with the previous release\n- [ ] Regression guard added: benchmark or performance test, plus field metric to watch\n\n## Output format\n\n~~~markdown\n## Performance report: <scope>\n\nDevice: <model, OS version>. Build: <benchmark variant | Release via Profile | --profile>.\nTool: <Macrobenchmark | Instruments App Launch | DevTools | ...>. Runs: <n per side>. Statistic: <median, P90>.\n\n| Issue | Metric before | Change | Metric after |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Cold start blocked by SDK init | TTID median 1 850 ms, P90 2 100 ms | Lazy analytics, Baseline Profile | TTID median 1 120 ms, P90 1 290 ms |\n| Feed scroll jank | frameOverrunMs P90 9.8 | Moved date formatting out of composition | frameOverrunMs P90 -2.1 |\n\n### Evidence\n- <trace file or screenshot name, and what it shows>\n\n### Not measured\n- <anything that could not be measured, and why>\n\n### Follow-up\n- <regression guard added, field metric to watch after release>\n~~~\n\nRules for the report: every \"after\" number uses the same device, build, tool and run count as its\n\"before\" number. A change that did not beat the run-to-run noise is listed as \"no measurable effect\".\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-kotlin`, `mobile-swift`, `mobile-flutter` -- Compose, SwiftUI and widget rules behind most jank fixes\n- `mobile-code-review` -- reviewing performance-sensitive changes before merge\n- `mobile-testing` -- benchmark and performance test setup alongside unit and UI tests\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- running benchmarks on devices in CI and storing results\n- `mobile-ble` -- BLE throughput, connection intervals and background scanning cost\n- `mobile-release` -- staged rollouts and watching vitals after release\n- `mobile-eu-compliance` -- telemetry payloads that contain device identifiers\n",
    "references/android.md":
        "# Android performance reference\n\nLoad this for startup, jank, memory, battery, network and size work in Kotlin or Java apps, and for the\nhost side of Flutter apps. Use version catalog aliases for the coordinates below and take current\nversions from the Jetpack release notes.\n\n## Benchmark and Baseline Profile modules\n\nAndroid Studio: File > New > New Module > Baseline Profile Generator (AGP 8.2+). It creates a\n`com.android.test` module, applies the Baseline Profile Gradle plugin and adds a generator and a startup\nbenchmark. Manual setup:\n\n```kotlin\n// :baselineprofile/build.gradle.kts\nplugins {\n    alias(libs.plugins.android.test)\n    alias(libs.plugins.androidx.baselineprofile) // id \"androidx.baselineprofile\"\n}\nandroid { targetProjectPath = \":app\" }\ndependencies {\n    implementation(libs.androidx.benchmark.macro.junit4) // androidx.benchmark:benchmark-macro-junit4\n    implementation(libs.androidx.test.ext.junit)\n}\n\n// :app/build.gradle.kts\nplugins {\n    alias(libs.plugins.android.application)\n    alias(libs.plugins.androidx.baselineprofile)\n}\ndependencies {\n    implementation(libs.androidx.profileinstaller) // androidx.profileinstaller:profileinstaller\n    baselineProfile(project(\":baselineprofile\"))\n}\n```\n\n```xml\n<!-- app AndroidManifest.xml, inside <application>: lets the shell profile a release build -->\n<profileable android:shell=\"true\" tools:targetApi=\"29\" />\n```\n\nGenerator, covering startup and the main journeys:\n\n```kotlin\n@RunWith(AndroidJUnit4::class)\n@LargeTest\nclass BaselineProfileGenerator {\n    @get:Rule val rule = BaselineProfileRule()\n\n    @Test fun generate() = rule.collect(\n        packageName = \"com.example.app\",\n        includeInStartupProfile = true,\n    ) {\n        pressHome()\n        startActivityAndWait()\n        device.findObject(By.res(\"device_list\")).fling(Direction.DOWN)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Generate: `./gradlew :app:generateBaselineProfile` (or `generate<Variant>BaselineProfile`), on a\n  physical device or a Gradle managed device.\n- Output: `src/<variant>/generated/baselineProfiles/baseline-prof.txt` and `startup-prof.txt`. Commit\n  them. Startup profiles need R8 (`isMinifyEnabled = true`); DEX layout optimization is on by default\n  from AGP 8.3.\n- Profiles are installed only for release builds. On Android 7 to 8.1 `profileinstaller` installs them\n  on first run; from Android 9 Play also uses them at install time.\n- Libraries can ship their own Baseline Profiles; startup profiles cannot come from libraries.\n\n## Startup benchmark\n\n```kotlin\n@RunWith(AndroidJUnit4::class)\n@LargeTest\nclass StartupBenchmark {\n    @get:Rule val rule = MacrobenchmarkRule()\n\n    @Test fun startupNone() = startup(CompilationMode.None())\n\n    @Test fun startupBaselineProfile() =\n        startup(CompilationMode.Partial(baselineProfileMode = BaselineProfileMode.Require))\n\n    private fun startup(mode: CompilationMode) = rule.measureRepeated(\n        packageName = \"com.example.app\",\n        metrics = listOf(StartupTimingMetric()),\n        compilationMode = mode,\n        iterations = 10,\n        startupMode = StartupMode.COLD,\n        setupBlock = { pressHome() },\n    ) {\n        startActivityAndWait()\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `None` is the worst case (fresh install, no profile). `Partial(BaselineProfileMode.Require)` fails if\n  no profile is present, so it also guards against a lost profile. `Full` is not what users get.\n- Output: `timeToInitialDisplayMs` and `timeToFullDisplayMs` as min, median and max. Compare medians.\n- Macrobenchmark refuses debuggable or non-profileable targets and emulators by default. Do not silence\n  this with `androidx.benchmark.suppressErrors` for numbers you report.\n- Results and Perfetto traces land in the connected device output folder\n  (`build/outputs/connected_android_test_additional_output/`). Open traces in Android Studio or\n  ui.perfetto.dev.\n\n## Frame benchmark\n\n```kotlin\n@Test fun scrollFeed() = rule.measureRepeated(\n    packageName = \"com.example.app\",\n    metrics = listOf(FrameTimingMetric()),\n    iterations = 5,\n    startupMode = StartupMode.WARM,\n    setupBlock = { pressHome(); startActivityAndWait() },\n) {\n    val list = device.findObject(By.res(\"feed\"))\n    list.setGestureMargin(device.displayWidth / 5)\n    list.fling(Direction.DOWN)\n    device.waitForIdle()\n}\n```\n\n- `frameDurationCpuMs`: CPU time per frame (UI thread and RenderThread), P50 to P99.\n- `frameOverrunMs` (API 31+): how late each frame was. Positive values are janky frames. Track P90 and P99.\n- Expose test tags as resource ids for UI Automator: `Modifier.semantics { testTagsAsResourceId = true }`\n  on the root composable.\n\n## Startup path\n\n- Read the system log line `Displayed <package>/<activity>: +<time>` (TTID) for a quick check; it is\n  logged by the system, so disable Logcat filters. Never use it as the reported number.\n- Report TTFD: `ComponentActivity.reportFullyDrawn()`, or in Compose `ReportDrawn()`,\n  `ReportDrawnWhen { }`, `ReportDrawnAfter { }`. Calling it too early makes TTFD equal TTID.\n- Lazy initialization: inject `dagger.Lazy<T>` or `Provider<T>`, create singletons on first use, and\n  defer non-critical SDKs until after the first frame.\n\nApp Startup (`androidx.startup:startup-runtime`):\n\n```kotlin\nclass LoggerInitializer : Initializer<Logger> {\n    override fun create(context: Context): Logger = Logger.init(context)\n    override fun dependencies(): List<Class<out Initializer<*>>> = emptyList()\n}\n```\n\n```xml\n<provider\n    android:name=\"androidx.startup.InitializationProvider\"\n    android:authorities=\"\${applicationId}.androidx-startup\"\n    android:exported=\"false\"\n    tools:node=\"merge\">\n    <!-- turn off automatic init for one component, then call it later -->\n    <meta-data android:name=\"com.example.LoggerInitializer\" tools:node=\"remove\" />\n</provider>\n```\n\n```kotlin\n// later, off the startup path\nAppInitializer.getInstance(context).initializeComponent(LoggerInitializer::class.java)\n```\n\n- Audit the merged manifest (APK Analyzer) for library `ContentProvider`s; each one runs before\n  `Application.onCreate`.\n- `StrictMode` with `detectDiskReads()` and `detectNetwork()` in debug builds finds I/O on the main thread.\n\n## Tracing\n\n- Custom sections: `trace(\"loadFeed\") { ... }` from `androidx.tracing`. They appear in Perfetto,\n  Android Studio System Trace and Macrobenchmark traces. The app must be `profileable` or debuggable.\n- On-device capture: Developer options > System Tracing > Record trace (Quick Settings tile available).\n  Traces are `.perfetto-trace` files on Android 10+; open them at ui.perfetto.dev.\n- Compose composition tracing: add `androidx.compose.runtime:runtime-tracing` (API 30+ device); system\n  traces then show composable names. Strip the strings from production builds with the ProGuard rule\n  from the Compose tracing guide.\n- What to look for in a startup trace: `bindApplication`, `activityStart`, `Choreographer#doFrame`,\n  long `inflate` or composition slices, and main-thread state \"Runnable\" or \"Sleeping\" while waiting on a\n  lock or binder call.\n\n## Compose jank\n\nThe coding rules live in `mobile-kotlin`, references/compose.md. Diagnosis:\n\n- Layout Inspector shows recomposition and skip counts per composable. Count, scroll, count again.\n- Compose compiler reports (release build) show which classes are unstable and which composables are\n  not skippable:\n\n```kotlin\ncomposeCompiler {\n    reportsDestination = layout.buildDirectory.dir(\"compose_compiler\")\n    metricsDestination = layout.buildDirectory.dir(\"compose_compiler\")\n}\n```\n\n- Use reports only when a trace shows recomposition cost. Strong skipping already handles most lambdas.\n\n## Memory and leaks\n\n```kotlin\ndependencies {\n    debugImplementation(libs.leakcanary.android) // com.squareup.leakcanary:leakcanary-android\n}\n```\n\n- No code needed. It watches destroyed Activities, Fragments and their Views, cleared ViewModels and\n  destroyed Services, and prints the reference chain (Logcat tag `LeakCanary`).\n- Fix the first reference in the chain that should not exist (singleton, static, registered listener,\n  coroutine in a global scope), not the leaked object.\n- Android Studio Memory Profiler: heap dump after forcing GC, filter by your package, look for several\n  instances of one Activity or Fragment.\n- Bitmaps: decode with the image loader at view size; Play vitals now reports bitmap memory as well.\n\n## Battery and background work\n\n- WorkManager for deferrable work; constraints: `setRequiredNetworkType`, `setRequiresCharging`,\n  `setRequiresBatteryNotLow`, `setRequiresDeviceIdle`, `setRequiresStorageNotLow`.\n- Periodic work: minimum interval 15 minutes. Use `enqueueUniquePeriodicWork` so restarts do not stack\n  duplicate jobs. Backoff defaults to exponential, 30 seconds; the minimum is 10 seconds.\n- Doze suspends network, ignores wake locks, defers alarms and jobs until maintenance windows. Use\n  `setAndAllowWhileIdle()` only for alarms that must fire; they fire at most once per 9 minutes.\n- Push instead of polling: FCM high priority only for user-visible notifications.\n- Android 15+: `dataSync` and `mediaProcessing` foreground services get 6 hours per 24 hours;\n  `Service.onTimeout(int, int)` is called and the service must stop within seconds.\n- Android 16: regular and expedited jobs follow runtime quotas by standby bucket, including jobs started\n  while the app is visible and jobs running beside a foreground service. Log\n  `WorkInfo.getStopReason()` (`stopReason` in Kotlin) to see why a job stopped.\n- Do not ask for `REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS` unless the core function needs it (Play policy\n  lists cases such as companion apps for peripheral devices that keep a persistent connection).\n\nTest commands:\n\n```sh\nadb shell dumpsys deviceidle force-idle        # enter Doze\nadb shell dumpsys deviceidle unforce\nadb shell dumpsys battery reset\nadb shell am set-standby-bucket com.example.app rare\nadb shell am get-standby-bucket com.example.app\nadb shell am compat enable FGS_INTRODUCE_TIME_LIMITS com.example.app   # FGS timeouts on Android 15\n```\n\n- Power Profiler (Android Studio, inside System Trace): per-rail power from ODPM on Pixel 6 and later,\n  Android 10+. Device-level, so close other apps.\n- `PowerMetric` in Macrobenchmark (experimental, Pixel 6 or newer) gives energy per subsystem per run.\n\n## Network\n\n- One `OkHttpClient` per app (shared connection pool and cache). OkHttp requests gzip transparently.\n- HTTP cache plus server `Cache-Control` and `ETag` turns repeat loads into 304 responses.\n- Paging 3 for long lists; request only fields the screen needs.\n- Batch telemetry and BLE logs into one upload job with constraints; never one request per event.\n\n## App size\n\n```kotlin\nandroid {\n    buildTypes {\n        release {\n            isMinifyEnabled = true\n            isShrinkResources = true\n            proguardFiles(getDefaultProguardFile(\"proguard-android-optimize.txt\"), \"proguard-rules.pro\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Ship an AAB. Play generates per-device APKs; the compressed download limit for bundles is 200 MB.\n- APK Analyzer: Build > Analyze APK, or drag the APK or AAB into the editor. Compare with previous APK\n  shows what grew. Command line: `apkanalyzer` in the SDK command-line tools.\n- Play Console app size report shows real download sizes per device configuration.\n- WebP for raster images, vector drawables for small icons, and the locale filter of your AGP version\n  (`resourceConfigurations` in the size guide) to drop unused translations from libraries.\n\n## Android vitals\n\n| Metric | Bad behavior threshold | Core vital |\n|---|---|---|\n| User-perceived crash rate | 1.09 % overall, 8 % per phone model | Yes |\n| User-perceived ANR rate | 0.47 % overall, 8 % per phone model | Yes |\n| Excessive partial wake locks | 5 % of battery sessions (partial wake locks over 3 hours in total) | Yes (beta) |\n| Slow cold / warm / hot start | Start of 5 s / 2 s / 1 s or more | No |\n| Slow frames | Sessions with more than 50 % of frames over 16 ms | No |\n| Frozen frames | Sessions with more than 0.1 % of frames over 700 ms | No |\n| Background Wi-Fi scans | More than 4 per hour | No |\n| Background network usage | More than 50 MB per day | No |\n\n- Play evaluates over 28 days. Memory and bitmap memory metrics by RAM tier are being added with\n  store visibility impact announced for February 2027; read the current vitals overview for the numbers.\n- Verify all values on the current Android vitals pages before using them in a report; they change.\n",
    "references/flutter.md":
        "# Flutter performance reference\n\nLoad this for measuring Flutter apps. Widget-level fixes (rebuild scope, lists, images, effects) are in\n`mobile-flutter`, references/performance.md; this file covers how to measure, read the tools, and where\nthe host platform tools take over.\n\n## Build mode and device\n\n- Profile mode for all performance work: `flutter run --profile`. It is AOT-compiled like release, with\n  tracing for DevTools. Debug mode (JIT, asserts) numbers are meaningless.\n- Physical device only, and the slowest one your users reasonably own. Simulators and emulators do not\n  represent release behaviour.\n- Release mode for size measurements and final confirmation; it has no DevTools connection.\n\n## Frames: UI thread vs raster thread\n\nEach frame has a UI thread part (Dart: build, layout, paint into a layer tree) and a raster thread part\n(the engine draws the layer tree with Impeller or Skia on the GPU). Either can miss the budget of about\n16 ms at 60 Hz (8 ms at 120 Hz).\n\n| Slow part | Usual cause | Check |\n|---|---|---|\n| UI thread | Large rebuilds, heavy `build`, sync parsing or I/O, layout of long non-lazy lists | Track Widget Builds, CPU profiler |\n| Raster thread | `saveLayer` from opacity, clips, shader masks, blur; large images; many layers | More debugging options toggles |\n| Both, only on first run of an animation | Shader compilation (marked dark red in the frames chart) | Usually gone with Impeller |\n\nDevTools Performance view:\n- Flutter frames chart: one bar pair per frame (UI, raster). Frames over budget are red.\n- Frame analysis tab: hints for the selected janky frame.\n- Timeline events: builds, layouts, paints, GC, HTTP, and your own `Timeline` / `TimelineTask` events\n  from `dart:developer`.\n- Enhance tracing: Track Widget Builds, Track Layouts, Track Paints. They slow frames down; use them to\n  find what runs, not to measure how long it takes.\n- More debugging options: turn off Render Clip layers, Render Opacity layers, Render Physical Shape\n  layers. If raster time drops sharply, that effect is the cost.\n\nPerformance overlay: Performance Overlay button in DevTools, `P` in the `flutter run` terminal, or\n`showPerformanceOverlay: true` on `MaterialApp`. Top graph is raster (labelled GPU), bottom is UI. View\nit only in profile mode.\n\n## Repeatable frame measurements\n\n```dart\n// integration_test/scroll_perf_test.dart\nimport 'package:flutter/widgets.dart';\nimport 'package:flutter_test/flutter_test.dart';\nimport 'package:integration_test/integration_test.dart';\nimport 'package:my_app/main.dart' as app;\n\nvoid main() {\n  final binding = IntegrationTestWidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();\n\n  testWidgets('feed scroll', (tester) async {\n    app.main();\n    await tester.pumpAndSettle();\n    final list = find.byKey(const ValueKey('feed'));\n    await binding.traceAction(() async {\n      await tester.fling(list, const Offset(0, -2000), 3000);\n      await tester.pumpAndSettle();\n    }, reportKey: 'feed_scroll');\n  });\n}\n```\n\n```dart\n// test_driver/perf_driver.dart\nimport 'package:flutter_driver/flutter_driver.dart' as driver;\nimport 'package:integration_test/integration_test_driver.dart';\n\nFuture<void> main() => integrationDriver(\n      responseDataCallback: (data) async {\n        if (data == null) return;\n        final timeline = driver.Timeline.fromJson(data['feed_scroll'] as Map<String, dynamic>);\n        final summary = driver.TimelineSummary.summarize(timeline);\n        await summary.writeTimelineToFile('feed_scroll', pretty: true, includeSummary: true);\n      },\n    );\n```\n\n```sh\nflutter drive --driver=test_driver/perf_driver.dart \\\n  --target=integration_test/scroll_perf_test.dart --profile\n```\n\n- Output in `build/`: `feed_scroll.timeline_summary.json` with `average_frame_build_time_millis`,\n  `worst_frame_build_time_millis`, `missed_frame_build_budget_count`,\n  `average_frame_rasterizer_time_millis`, `worst_frame_rasterizer_time_millis`,\n  `missed_frame_rasterizer_budget_count`, `frame_count`.\n- Compare `missed_frame_*_budget_count` and the worst times across runs; report the median of the runs.\n- The Flutter docs suggest adding `--no-dds` when running on a mobile device or emulator.\n\n## Startup\n\n```sh\nflutter run --profile --trace-startup\n```\n\n- Writes `build/start_up_info.json` with timings in microseconds, including `timeToFirstFrameMicros`\n  and `timeToFrameworkInitMicros`. Check the current Flutter docs for the full key list.\n- The user-visible launch also includes native startup. Measure it with the host tools: Macrobenchmark\n  `StartupTimingMetric` on Android and `XCTApplicationLaunchMetric` or Instruments App Launch on iOS\n  (see [android.md](android.md) and [ios.md](ios.md)).\n- Keep `main()` minimal; defer plugin and SDK init that the first frame does not need.\n- Android Baseline Profiles cover only the Kotlin and Java part (embedding, plugins). Dart code is\n  already AOT-compiled in profile and release builds.\n\n## Memory\n\nDevTools Memory view (profile mode):\n- Profile Memory: current allocation by class; toggle Refresh on GC.\n- Diff Snapshots: snapshot, open and close the screen several times, snapshot again, diff. A growing\n  count of a `State`, controller or model class is the leak.\n- Trace Instances: which call stacks allocate instances of selected classes.\n\nLeak causes in Dart:\n- Controllers, `FocusNode`, `AnimationController`, `ScrollController` and stream subscriptions not\n  disposed or cancelled.\n- Closures that capture a short-lived object (often `BuildContext`) stored in a long-lived object.\n  Capture the value you need (`Theme.of(context)`) instead of the context.\n- Native memory (images, platform views) does not show in the Dart heap; use Android Studio Memory\n  Profiler or Instruments Allocations for the host process.\n\n## Battery and network\n\n- Background work runs on the host: the `workmanager` plugin maps to WorkManager on Android and\n  `BGTaskScheduler` on iOS; the constraint and Info.plist rules in the main skill apply.\n- Timers and periodic `Stream`s keep running while the app is in the background until the OS suspends\n  it. Pause them in `AppLifecycleListener` (`onHide`, `onPause`) and resume on `onResume`.\n- Use one HTTP client instance, cache responses where the server allows, paginate, and batch telemetry.\n- Decode large JSON in `Isolate.run` so the UI isolate keeps its frame budget.\n\n## App size\n\n```sh\nflutter build appbundle --analyze-size\nflutter build apk --analyze-size\nflutter build ios --analyze-size\n```\n\n- Each prints a summary and writes a `*-code-size-analysis_*.json` file. Open it with `dart devtools` >\n  Open app size tool; load two files to diff releases.\n- On iOS, `--analyze-size` builds a `.app` for relative comparison. For real download size, use the\n  Xcode App Thinning Size Report (see [ios.md](ios.md)), starting from\n  `flutter build ipa --export-method development`.\n- `--split-debug-info=<dir>` moves Dart symbols out of the binary and can reduce code size a lot. Keep\n  the symbol files per release for crash symbolication.\n- Remove unused packages, fonts and assets; check large transitive native SDKs in the host tools (APK\n  Analyzer, size report).\n\n## Impeller\n\n- Impeller is the only renderer on iOS; there is no switch back to Skia.\n- On Android it is the default on API 29+ and falls back to OpenGL on devices without Vulkan support.\n  Opt out for debugging with `flutter run --no-enable-impeller`, or for a release with the\n  `io.flutter.embedding.android.EnableImpeller` meta-data set to `false` in `AndroidManifest.xml`. Treat\n  an opt-out as temporary and file the rendering bug.\n- Shader warm-up steps written for Skia are not needed with Impeller. Re-measure before keeping them.\n",
    "references/ios.md":
        "# iOS performance reference\n\nLoad this for launch time, hangs, hitches, SwiftUI update cost, memory, energy, network and size work\nin Swift apps, and for the host side of Flutter apps on iOS.\n\n## Ground rules for iOS numbers\n\n- Profile with Product > Profile, which builds the Release configuration by default. Check the scheme.\n- Use a physical device, ideally the oldest model you support. The simulator never issues memory\n  warnings or out-of-memory terminations and runs on Mac hardware.\n- Launch varies with device state (cold after reboot or memory pressure, warm after a force quit).\n  Measure several runs per state and say which state you measured.\n\n## Launch time\n\nField data:\n- Xcode Organizer > Launch Time: milliseconds from icon tap to first frame after the launch screen,\n  50th and 90th percentile, per device and release.\n- Organizer > Launches: the slowest functions during launch, with stack traces.\n- MetricKit `MXAppLaunchMetric`: `histogrammedTimeToFirstDraw`, `histogrammedOptimizedTimeToFirstDraw`,\n  `histogrammedApplicationResumeTime`, and `histogrammedExtendedLaunch` (iOS 16+).\n\nLab:\n- Instruments App Launch template: time profile plus thread states during launch. Blocked main thread\n  time shows waits on locks, I/O or IPC.\n- Time Profiler template: the App Life Cycle track splits launch into process initialization, UIKit\n  initialization, initial scene rendering and initial frame rendering.\n- dyld Activity instrument: time spent loading libraries and running static initializers.\n\n```swift\nfinal class LaunchTests: XCTestCase {\n    func testColdLaunch() {\n        measure(metrics: [XCTApplicationLaunchMetric()]) {\n            XCUIApplication().launch()\n        }\n    }\n\n    func testLaunchUntilResponsive() {\n        measure(metrics: [XCTApplicationLaunchMetric(waitUntilResponsive: true)]) {\n            XCUIApplication().launch()\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWhat makes launch slow, in the order to check:\n1. Many embedded dynamic frameworks. Merge them (mergeable libraries, Xcode 15+), link statically, or\n   drop the dependency.\n2. Static initializers: C++ static constructors, Objective-C `+load`, `__attribute__((constructor))`.\n3. Work in `application(_:didFinishLaunchingWithOptions:)`, `App.init` or the first view's `init` and\n   `body`: database migration, network, SDK setup, large file reads.\n4. A complex first screen. Show cached or placeholder content and fill it in after the first frame.\n\nExtended launch (content actually ready), iOS 16+:\n\n```swift\n@MainActor\nfunc loadInitialContent() async {\n    let id = MXLaunchTaskID(\"initial-content\")\n    try? MXMetricManager.extendLaunchMeasurement(forTaskID: id)\n    defer { try? MXMetricManager.finishExtendedLaunchMeasurement(forTaskID: id) }\n    await feed.load()\n}\n```\n\n- Start the first task before the first scene becomes active; at most 16 tasks.\n- For your own lab view of post-launch work, wrap it in an `OSSignposter` interval on a log with the\n  `.pointsOfInterest` category; it appears in the Points of Interest track in Instruments.\n\n## Hangs\n\n- A delay over about 100 ms in a discrete interaction (tap, key press) is noticeable. Assume the main\n  thread has under half of that.\n- On device: Settings > Developer > Hang Detection reports hangs in development and TestFlight builds.\n- Instruments: Time Profiler and the Hitches template include the Hangs instrument (iOS 16+).\n- Field: Organizer Hangs, and `MXHangDiagnostic` (`hangDuration`, `callStackTree`) in\n  `MXDiagnosticPayload.hangDiagnostics`, delivered immediately on iOS 15+.\n\n```swift\n// BAD: synchronous work in a Task created in a view inherits the main actor\nButton(\"Export\") { Task { let file = export(items); share(file) } }\n\n// GOOD: CPU work in a function that leaves the caller's actor\n@concurrent nonisolated func export(_ items: [Item]) async -> URL { /* ... */ }\nButton(\"Export\") { Task { share(await export(items)) } }\n```\n\n`@concurrent` is Swift 6.2. With the `NonisolatedNonsendingByDefault` feature on, a plain\n`nonisolated async` function runs on the caller's actor; see `mobile-swift`, references/concurrency.md.\n\n## Hitches and SwiftUI update cost\n\n- Hitch: a frame not ready by its deadline (8.3 ms at 120 Hz, 16.7 ms at 60 Hz). Commit hitches come\n  from main-thread work; render hitches from a scene too complex for the render server.\n- Organizer hitch rate in ms of pause per second: at or below 10 ms/s is good; Apple lists 25 and 50\n  ms/s as the warning and critical levels.\n- Instruments Animation Hitches template; record on an older device while scrolling.\n- Keep per-frame main-thread work, including table and collection data source calls, under about 5 ms.\n\nSwiftUI template in Instruments (Product > Profile > SwiftUI):\n- View Body Updates lanes per module show long `body` evaluations; select one and read the Time\n  Profiler call tree for that range.\n- Update Groups and the cause-and-effect graph (\"Show Causes\") show why a view updated. Frequent\n  updates from an `ObservableObject` with many `@Published` properties usually disappear after moving to\n  `@Observable`.\n- `Self._printChanges()` inside `body` prints which input changed. It is an underscored debugging aid;\n  never ship it.\n\n```swift\n// BAD: sorting and formatter creation on every body evaluation\nvar body: some View {\n    let sorted = readings.sorted { \$0.date > \$1.date }\n    List(sorted) { Text(DateFormatter().string(from: \$0.date)) }\n}\n\n// GOOD: sorted once in the model, formatting by format style\nvar body: some View {\n    List(model.sortedReadings) { Text(\$0.date, format: .dateTime.hour().minute()) }\n}\n```\n\nScrolling performance test:\n\n```swift\nfunc testFeedScrolling() {\n    let app = XCUIApplication()\n    app.launch()\n    let options = XCTMeasureOptions()\n    options.invocationOptions = [.manuallyStop]\n    measure(metrics: [XCTOSSignpostMetric.scrollingAndDecelerationMetric], options: options) {\n        app.collectionViews.firstMatch.swipeUp(velocity: .fast)\n        stopMeasuring()\n        app.collectionViews.firstMatch.swipeDown(velocity: .fast)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- iOS 26 adds `XCTHitchMetric(application:)` for hitch counts in performance tests.\n- `XCTMeasureOptions.iterationCount`: the block runs `iterationCount + 1` times; the first run is\n  discarded as warm-up.\n\n## Memory and leaks\n\n- Xcode Debug Memory Graph: shows objects and who references them; leaked cycles are flagged. Turn on\n  Malloc Stack Logging in the scheme Diagnostics to get allocation stack traces.\n- Instruments Allocations: use Mark Generation before and after a feature (open and close a screen five\n  times). Objects surviving every generation are the leak or the cache to bound.\n- Instruments Leaks: finds memory nothing references any more, including retain cycles. Abandoned\n  memory that is still referenced (a screen held by a singleton, an unbounded cache) needs Allocations\n  generations or the Memory Graph.\n- `XCTMemoryMetric` measures physical memory change across a test block.\n- Common causes: closures capturing `self` stored on `self`, timers, `NotificationCenter` block observers\n  without removal, delegates declared `strong`, unstructured `Task` loops over infinite sequences.\n- Jetsam (out-of-memory) terminations show up in Organizer and as jetsam event reports, not as crashes.\n\n## Energy and background work\n\n- MetricKit battery-related metrics: `cpuMetrics`, `gpuMetrics`, `displayMetrics`,\n  `locationActivityMetrics`, `networkTransferMetrics`, `cellularConditionMetrics`.\n- Organizer Energy reports show energy issues reported from the field.\n- Background work: `BGAppRefreshTaskRequest` for short refresh, `BGProcessingTaskRequest` with\n  `requiresExternalPower` and `requiresNetworkConnectivity` for heavy work. Identifiers must be in\n  `BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers` (Info.plist) and the Background Modes capability must be on.\n- Large transfers: `URLSessionConfiguration.background(withIdentifier:)` with `isDiscretionary = true`;\n  the system may wait for Wi-Fi and power.\n- Location: the lowest accuracy that works, stop updates when not needed, significant-change or visits\n  monitoring instead of continuous GPS.\n\n## Network\n\n- `URLCache` with a disk capacity sized for your content; send and honour `Cache-Control` and `ETag`.\n- For discretionary traffic: `allowsConstrainedNetworkAccess = false` (Low Data Mode),\n  `allowsExpensiveNetworkAccess = false`, `waitsForConnectivity = true`. Test with Settings > Developer\n  > Network Override, and Network Link Conditioner for slow networks.\n- Instruments Network template shows connections and transfer sizes per request.\n\n## MetricKit subscriber\n\n```swift\nimport MetricKit\n\nfinal class MetricsCollector: NSObject, MXMetricManagerSubscriber {\n    func start() { MXMetricManager.shared.add(self) } // safe to call during launch\n\n    func didReceive(_ payloads: [MXMetricPayload]) {\n        for payload in payloads { send(payload.jsonRepresentation()) } // daily\n    }\n\n    func didReceive(_ payloads: [MXDiagnosticPayload]) {\n        for payload in payloads { send(payload.jsonRepresentation()) } // hangs, crashes, launch\n    }\n\n    private func send(_ json: Data) { /* queue for batched upload */ }\n}\n```\n\n- Metrics arrive at most once per day per source, only on physical devices; diagnostics arrive\n  immediately on iOS 15+. `pastPayloads` and `pastDiagnosticPayloads` return earlier reports.\n- Payloads include device and OS metadata. If you upload them linked to a user or device identifier,\n  treat them as personal data and reflect that in the App Store privacy details.\n\n## App size\n\n- Archive, export as Ad Hoc, Development or Enterprise with \"All compatible device variants\" for app\n  thinning. The export folder contains `App Thinning Size Report.txt` with compressed (download) and\n  uncompressed (install) size per variant.\n- Automate: `xcodebuild -exportArchive -archivePath App.xcarchive -exportPath out -exportOptionsPlist\n  Options.plist`, with the `thinning` key set to `<thin-for-all-variants>` in the plist.\n- The `.app`, `.xcarchive` and uploaded `.ipa` are not valid size measurements. App Store Connect shows\n  the real per-device sizes.\n- Common wins: asset catalogs (enables thinning and compression), remove unused assets and fonts, ODR\n  or Background Assets for large optional content, fewer third-party SDKs.\n",
    "references/measuring.md":
        "# Measuring reference\n\nLoad this before taking any number that will go into a report. Bad method produces numbers that move\nby more than the change being measured.\n\n## Lab vs field\n\n| | Lab | Field |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tools | Macrobenchmark, Perfetto, Instruments, XCTest metrics, DevTools | Android vitals, Play Console, Xcode Organizer, MetricKit |\n| Good for | Finding the cause, comparing two builds, blocking regressions in CI | Knowing which problem users actually have, confirming a fix |\n| Weakness | One device, one state, synthetic journey | Days of delay, aggregated, no traces for most metrics |\n\nStart from field data to pick the problem, use lab data to fix it, and go back to field data to confirm.\n\n## Device choice\n\n- Real hardware. Emulators and simulators share the host CPU, GPU and memory; their numbers are not\n  representative, and Macrobenchmark refuses to run on an emulator by default.\n- Pick the device from your field data: a common low or mid-range model in your user base (check the\n  device list in Android vitals or the Organizer filters). A flagship hides most problems.\n- Keep one dedicated benchmark device per platform for CI so results stay comparable over months.\n\n## Build\n\n| Platform | Measure on | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Android | `benchmark` build type: `initWith(release)`, non-debuggable, `<profileable android:shell=\"true\"/>`, R8 on | Debuggable builds disable ART optimizations |\n| iOS | Release configuration via Product > Profile or a performance test scheme | Debug builds add checks and no optimization |\n| Flutter | `--profile` (frames, startup trace), release for size | Debug is JIT-compiled with asserts |\n\nSame build for \"before\" and \"after\", except for the one change under test. Note the commit hashes.\n\n## Conditions\n\n- Battery above 50 % and not charging during the run unless charging is the scenario. Low battery\n  enables power saving; Macrobenchmark errors on low battery by default.\n- Let the device cool down between long runs; thermal throttling makes later runs slower.\n- Close other apps, disable auto-updates, keep the same network (or airplane mode if the journey allows).\n- Same OS version, same language and font scale, same account and data set. A feed with 20 items and\n  one with 2 000 are different benchmarks.\n- Android: say which compilation state you measured (`CompilationMode.None()`, `Partial` with a\n  Baseline Profile, or `DEFAULT`). First launch after install and after an update is the\n  user-relevant worst case.\n- iOS: say whether the launch was after a reboot, after a force quit, or after other heavy apps; Apple\n  treats warm and cold launch as a spectrum.\n\n## How many runs and which statistic\n\n- Startup: at least 10 iterations per variant; report median and P90 (or median and max if only\n  min/median/max are available, as with `StartupTimingMetric`).\n- Frames: at least 5 iterations of the same scripted journey; report P50, P90 and P99 of\n  `frameOverrunMs` or `frameDurationCpuMs`, the hitch metric, or `missed_frame_*_budget_count`.\n- XCTest: the block runs `iterationCount + 1` times and the first run is discarded as warm-up; raise\n  `iterationCount` for noisy metrics. Set a baseline per device in Xcode so regressions fail the test.\n- Never report a single run or the best run.\n- Noise check: run the unchanged build twice. If the two medians differ by more than the improvement\n  you are claiming, you need more runs or calmer conditions, not a conclusion.\n\n## Startup states\n\n| State | Android | iOS |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cold | Process not running; `StartupMode.COLD` kills it between iterations | Process not running and dependencies paged out (after reboot or memory pressure) |\n| Warm | Process alive, Activity recreated; `StartupMode.WARM` | Process start with system caches warm (for example right after a force quit) |\n| Hot | Activity brought to front; `StartupMode.HOT` | Resume of a suspended process, reported separately as resume time |\n\nAndroid vitals and Macrobenchmark use TTID (first frame) and TTFD (`reportFullyDrawn`). The iOS launch\nmetric is time to first frame after the launch screen; extended launch covers content loaded after it.\n\n## Reading a trace\n\n1. Find the region: startup from process start to first frame, or one janky frame in the frames track.\n2. Look at the main (UI) thread state, not only CPU samples: Running is your code, Runnable means the\n   thread waits for a CPU, Sleeping or Blocked means it waits on a lock, I/O, binder or IPC.\n3. Expand the longest slices first. Add custom trace sections (`trace(\"...\")` on Android,\n   `OSSignposter` on iOS, `Timeline.timeSync` in Dart) around suspects and record again.\n4. Write down the slice name and duration; that is the evidence in the report.\n\nAndroid quick capture without Android Studio: Developer options > System Tracing > Record trace, then\nopen the `.perfetto-trace` file at ui.perfetto.dev. Pull from `/data/local/traces/` with `adb pull` if\nsharing from the device is not possible.\n\n## Report\n\nUse the table from the main skill. Example with full context:\n\n~~~markdown\nDevice: Samsung Galaxy A15, Android 15. Build: benchmark variant, commit a1b2c3d vs d4e5f6a.\nTool: Macrobenchmark StartupTimingMetric, COLD, CompilationMode.Partial(Require). Runs: 15 per side.\n\n| Issue | Metric before | Change | Metric after |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Analytics SDK init on main thread | TTID median 1 640 ms, max 1 910 ms | Deferred init with App Startup + AppInitializer after first frame | TTID median 1 210 ms, max 1 380 ms |\n| No startup profile | TTID median 1 210 ms | includeInStartupProfile = true | TTID median 1 150 ms (within noise of 70 ms, kept for field check) |\n~~~\n\n- Every \"after\" uses the same device, build type, tool, mode and run count as its \"before\".\n- Say \"within noise\" when the change is smaller than the noise check; do not claim it as a win.\n- Attach or name the trace files that show the cause.\n- List what was not measured and why (no device available, metric requires API 31+, field data not\n  yet in).\n",
  },
  "mobile-release": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-release\ndescription: \"Checks store readiness of Android, iOS and Flutter apps for Google Play and the App Store: target API level, Data safety, sensitive permissions, account deletion, Play App Signing, privacy manifest and required-reason APIs, App Review rejections, ATT, TestFlight, versioning, staged and phased rollout, crash gates and rollback. Use before submitting a build, for a store rejection, or to plan a release.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile release\n\nDecide whether a build can ship to Google Play and the App Store, and plan how it ships: check the build\nand the project against current store requirements, collect evidence for each item, list the blockers, and\nlay out the rollout with stop conditions and a rollback path.\n\nStore policies change several times a year. The agent uses the verified facts below as a starting point,\nsays when a fact needs re-checking, and never invents a deadline, threshold or guideline number. Anything\nnot in the table is phrased as \"check the current policy\" and linked to the console page or guideline name.\n\n## When to use\n\n- \"Is this build ready for the Play Store / App Store?\" or a release readiness review before a submission.\n- A rejection or policy email: Play policy violation, App Review rejection, ITMS-91053 missing API declaration.\n- Raising `targetSdk`, adding a sensitive permission or foreground service, or adding a tracking or ad SDK.\n- Planning a release: tracks, TestFlight groups, staged or phased rollout, crash gates, rollback.\n- Versioning questions: `versionCode`, `CFBundleVersion`, Flutter `version:` in `pubspec.yaml`.\n\nNot for: building the upload pipeline (use `mobile-ci-cd`), GDPR or CRA assessment of the data flows\nbehind the store forms (use `mobile-eu-compliance`), or a security audit (use `mobile-security`).\n\n## Verified key facts\n\nChecked on 2026-10-15 against the official pages named in the Source column. Re-check before quoting a\ndate to a customer or planning a release around it.\n\n| Topic | Fact | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Play target API, new apps and updates | Must target Android 16 (API 36) or higher since 31 August 2026. Wear OS and Android Automotive OS: API 35. Android TV and Android XR: API 34 | Play Console Help \"Target API level requirements for Google Play apps\"; developer.android.com \"Meet Google Play's target API level requirement\" |\n| Play target API, extension | Extension available on request until 1 November 2026 (form in Play Console) | Same pages |\n| Play target API, existing apps | Must target API 35 or higher to stay available to new users on devices running a newer Android version | Same pages |\n| Play target API, next deadline | No official announcement found for API 37. Check the current policy | Same pages |\n| Play App Bundle | Apps created after August 2021 publish app bundles; only legacy apps may still use APKs | Play Console Help \"Android App Bundles\" |\n| Play new personal accounts | Personal accounts created after 13 November 2023 must run a closed test with at least 12 testers opted in for at least 14 consecutive days before production access | Play Console Help \"App testing requirements for new personal developer accounts\" |\n| Android vitals bad behavior thresholds | User-perceived crash rate 1.09% overall, 8% per phone model. User-perceived ANR rate 0.47% overall, 8% per phone model. Excessive partial wake locks 5%. Above a threshold, Play may reduce visibility and show a warning on the listing | developer.android.com \"Android vitals\" |\n| Play staged rollout | Updates only, not the first release. Halting stops new users from getting it; users who have it stay on it. The percentage never increases automatically | Play Console Help \"Release app updates with staged rollouts\" |\n| App Store build requirement | Since 28 April 2026: built with Xcode 26 or later using the iOS 26 (or matching platform 26) SDK | Apple \"Upcoming Requirements\" |\n| App Store minimum deployment target | Since 9 September 2026: iOS and iPadOS apps uploaded must target iOS 13 or later | Apple \"Upcoming Requirements\" |\n| Required reason APIs | Since 1 May 2024, uploads that use a required reason API without an approved reason in the privacy manifest are not accepted | Apple \"Describing use of required reason API\" |\n| Third-party SDK manifests | Since 12 February 2025, submissions must contain a valid privacy manifest for listed commonly used SDKs; binary SDKs on the list also need a signature | Apple \"Adding a privacy manifest to your app or third-party SDK\"; \"Third-party SDK requirements\" |\n| App Review 4.8 Login Services | Third-party or social login for the primary account requires an equivalent login option that limits data to name and email, lets the user keep the email private, and does not collect interactions for ads without consent. Listed exceptions apply | App Store Review Guidelines 4.8 |\n| App Review 5.1.1(v) | Apps that support account creation must offer account deletion within the app | App Store Review Guidelines 5.1.1(v) |\n| App Store phased release | 7 days: 1%, 2%, 5%, 10%, 20%, 50%, 100% of users with automatic updates. Pause for up to 30 days in total. Anyone can still download the update manually | App Store Connect Help \"Release a version update in phases\" |\n| TestFlight | Up to 100 internal testers, up to 10,000 external testers. Builds expire after 90 days. The first build for external testers goes to review | App Store Connect Help \"TestFlight overview\" |\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Scope the release.** Platforms, flavors or schemes, store accounts, current live version and build\n   number on each store, target date, and who approves. Ask; do not guess store tracks or groups.\n2. **Read the build configuration.** Android: `targetSdk`, `minSdk`, `versionCode`, `versionName`,\n   signing config, merged manifest permissions and services. iOS: deployment target, Xcode version in CI,\n   `CFBundleShortVersionString`, `CFBundleVersion`, `Info.plist` purpose strings, entitlements,\n   `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`. Flutter: `version:` in `pubspec.yaml`, plus both native projects.\n3. **Inventory SDKs and data.** From `libs.versions.toml`, Gradle dependencies, `Package.resolved`,\n   `Podfile.lock`, `pubspec.lock`. For each SDK: what it collects, whether it tracks, its privacy manifest.\n   This inventory drives the Data safety form, App Privacy details and the privacy manifest.\n4. **Check Google Play items** with [Google Play](references/google-play.md): target API, Data safety,\n   permission and foreground service declarations, account deletion, app bundle, Play App Signing, tracks,\n   pre-launch report.\n5. **Check App Store items** with [App Store](references/app-store.md): Xcode and SDK, privacy manifest and\n   required reasons, SDK manifests and signatures, App Privacy details, ATT, the common rejection list,\n   review notes and demo account, TestFlight.\n6. **Plan the rollout** with [Versioning and rollout](references/versioning-and-rollout.md): version\n   numbers, release notes, track progression, staged or phased percentages, crash and ANR gates, and the\n   rollback plan, including server-side kill switches.\n7. **Mark every item** Pass, Fail or N/A with evidence (file and line, console screenshot reference,\n   command output). Console-only items the agent cannot see are marked \"Not verified\" with an owner.\n8. **Report** in the output format below. Blockers first. Do not change code, store settings or consoles\n   unless asked.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Target API level meets the current Play requirement\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: build.gradle.kts -- uploads rejected for new apps and updates since 31 August 2026\nandroid {\n    defaultConfig { targetSdk = 35 }\n}\n\n// GOOD: target API 36 or higher, from the version catalog; review behavior changes before raising it\nandroid {\n    compileSdk = libs.versions.compileSdk.get().toInt()\n    defaultConfig { targetSdk = libs.versions.targetSdk.get().toInt() } // 36\n}\n```\n\n- Raising `targetSdk` changes runtime behavior (edge-to-edge, foreground services, permissions). Treat it\n  as a feature with its own test pass, not a release-day edit. Ask before bumping it.\n- Flutter: check `android/app/build.gradle.kts`; `flutter.targetSdkVersion` follows the Flutter SDK\n  version, so pin it explicitly if the SDK default lags the Play requirement.\n\n### 2. iOS builds use the required Xcode and SDK\n\n```yaml\n# BAD: CI pinned to an old Xcode; App Store Connect rejects the upload\n- run: sudo xcode-select -s /Applications/Xcode_16.4.app\n\n# GOOD: Xcode 26 or later (exact path depends on the runner image), checked in the job log\n- run: sudo xcode-select -s /Applications/Xcode_26.0.app\n- run: xcodebuild -version\n```\n\n- `IPHONEOS_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET` of 13.0 or later for uploads since 9 September 2026.\n\n### 3. Build numbers only go up; marketing versions follow semantic versioning\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: same versionCode as the last upload, or a lower one for a hotfix branch\ndefaultConfig { versionCode = 41; versionName = \"2.3\" }\n\n// GOOD: versionCode from CI, strictly increasing; versionName is MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH from the tag\ndefaultConfig {\n    versionCode = providers.gradleProperty(\"versionCode\").get().toInt()\n    versionName = providers.gradleProperty(\"versionName\").get()\n}\n```\n\n```yaml\n# Flutter pubspec.yaml: build-name+build-number feeds versionName/versionCode and\n# CFBundleShortVersionString/CFBundleVersion\nversion: 2.4.1+20401\n```\n\n- Play rejects a `versionCode` already used; the maximum is 2100000000.\n- `CFBundleShortVersionString` is three period-separated integers. `CFBundleVersion` must be unique and\n  higher for each upload of the same version.\n\n### 4. Sensitive permissions are removed unless they are core and declared\n\n```xml\n<!-- BAD: broad permissions \"just in case\"; each one needs a Play declaration and a review -->\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES\" />\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION\" />\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.READ_CALL_LOG\" />\n\n<!-- GOOD: name the packages you need; BLE scanning without location when no location is derived -->\n<queries>\n    <package android:name=\"com.example.vendor.app\" />\n</queries>\n<uses-permission\n    android:name=\"android.permission.BLUETOOTH_SCAN\"\n    android:usesPermissionFlags=\"neverForLocation\" />\n```\n\n- Background location: core feature only, Play Console declaration, prominent in-app disclosure before\n  the runtime prompt, demo video. Details in [Google Play](references/google-play.md).\n- SMS and Call Log: only for the default SMS, Phone or Assistant handler, or a listed exception.\n- Check the merged manifest (`app/build/intermediates/merged_manifests/`) and every track: a permission\n  pulled in by a library counts, and so does one left in a closed testing release.\n\n### 5. Foreground services declare a type in the manifest and in Play Console\n\n```xml\n<!-- BAD: targeting Android 14+ without a type; startForeground() fails at runtime -->\n<service android:name=\".GatewaySyncService\" />\n\n<!-- GOOD: type and matching permission; connectedDevice needs a prerequisite such as BLUETOOTH_CONNECT -->\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE\" />\n<uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_CONNECTED_DEVICE\" />\n<service\n    android:name=\".GatewaySyncService\"\n    android:foregroundServiceType=\"connectedDevice\"\n    android:exported=\"false\" />\n```\n\n- Each type used also needs a declaration on the App content page: feature description, user impact if\n  deferred, a video of the user triggering it, and the use case.\n\n### 6. Account deletion is in the app, and on Play also on the web\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: deactivation only, or \"email support to delete\"\nfun onDeleteAccount() = api.deactivate(userId)\n\n// GOOD: in-app path in account settings; backend deletes account and associated data\nsuspend fun deleteAccount() {\n    api.deleteAccount()          // server deletes the account record and associated data\n    session.clear()\n}\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD: iOS -- same path; revoke Sign in with Apple tokens on the server (REST API) when used\nfunc deleteAccount() async throws {\n    try await api.deleteAccount()\n    await session.clear()\n}\n```\n\n- Play: also a web link where users can request deletion without the app, entered in the Data safety form.\n- App Store: temporary deactivation alone is insufficient. A link to the website is acceptable only if the\n  user must finish deletion there, and it must go directly to that page.\n\n### 7. Store privacy forms match the SDK inventory\n\n```text\nBAD:  Data safety \"No data collected\" while Crashlytics and an analytics SDK ship in the bundle.\nGOOD: Each SDK in the inventory mapped to data types, purpose, shared or not, optional or not;\n      the same answers drive Data safety (Play), App Privacy details (App Store) and PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy.\n```\n\n- Both stores hold the developer responsible for third-party SDK collection.\n- App Privacy answers can be updated without a new build; Data safety inaccuracies can block updates.\n\n### 8. The privacy manifest declares every required reason API with an approved reason\n\n```xml\n<!-- BAD: app uses UserDefaults and file timestamps, no PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy in the target -->\n\n<!-- GOOD: PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy in the app target's resources -->\n<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>\n<false/>\n<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>\n<array>\n    <dict>\n        <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>\n        <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>\n        <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>\n        <array><string>CA92.1</string></array>\n    </dict>\n    <dict>\n        <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>\n        <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>\n        <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>\n        <array><string>C617.1</string></array>\n    </dict>\n</array>\n```\n\n- Pick reasons that match what the code does; the reason table is in [App Store](references/app-store.md).\n- Flutter plugins such as `shared_preferences` wrap `UserDefaults`; use plugin versions that ship their\n  own manifest, and declare the app's own use in the Runner target.\n- Generate the privacy report from the archive in Xcode Organizer and review it before each submission.\n\n### 9. Third-party SDKs ship their own manifests and signatures\n\n```swift\n// BAD: Package.resolved pins an SDK version from before it shipped a privacy manifest\n.package(url: \"https://github.com/example/analytics-ios\", exact: \"3.1.0\")\n\n// GOOD: version with a bundled PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy (and a signature for binary SDKs on Apple's list)\n.package(url: \"https://github.com/example/analytics-ios\", from: \"5.2.0\")\n```\n\n- An SDK cannot rely on the app's manifest for its own required reason API use, and the app cannot fix a\n  missing SDK manifest by declaring it for the SDK. Upgrade or replace the SDK.\n\n### 10. No tracking before App Tracking Transparency authorization\n\n```swift\n// BAD: ad SDK started and IDFA read at launch, before any prompt\nAds.start()\n\n// GOOD: ask in context, start tracking features only when authorized\nlet status = await ATTrackingManager.requestTrackingAuthorization()\nif status == .authorized { Ads.enablePersonalizedAds() } else { Ads.enableNonPersonalizedAds() }\n```\n\n```xml\n<!-- Info.plist: required when requesting authorization; explain the actual use -->\n<key>NSUserTrackingUsageDescription</key>\n<string>Allows ads based on your activity in other companies' apps and websites.</string>\n```\n\n- Do not gate features or offer rewards for allowing tracking (5.1.2(i)). Domains in\n  `NSPrivacyTrackingDomains` fail to load until the user grants permission.\n- ATT is an Apple requirement on top of, not a replacement for, GDPR consent (see `mobile-eu-compliance`).\n\n### 11. Third-party login comes with an equivalent privacy-preserving option (4.8)\n\n```swift\n// BAD: only \"Continue with Google\" for the primary account\nLoginButtons(providers: [.google])\n\n// GOOD: an equivalent option meeting 4.8 (Sign in with Apple meets it), or own accounts only\nLoginButtons(providers: [.apple, .google, .email])\n```\n\n- Not required for: company-own accounts only, enterprise or education accounts, government eID, or a\n  client for a specific third-party service. Quote the guideline exception in the review notes if relied on.\n\n### 12. The submission is complete and reviewable\n\n```text\nBAD:  Review notes empty; login required; staging backend down; \"Lorem ipsum\" in a settings screen;\n      screenshots show only the splash screen.\nGOOD: Demo account with sample data (or an approved demo mode), backend up for the review window,\n      notes explaining hardware-dependent features (BLE device, gateway) with a demo video,\n      no placeholder text, screenshots of the app in use, privacy policy URL in metadata and in the app.\n```\n\n- Hardware companion apps: reviewers rarely have the device. Explain what works without it and attach a video.\n- Play: provide the same test credentials for the pre-launch report and app access.\n\n### 13. Releases go through tracks and a staged or phased rollout with gates\n\n```ruby\n# BAD: straight to 100% production from CI\nupload_to_play_store(track: \"production\")\n\n# GOOD: staged rollout fraction; iOS phased release; a human raises the percentage after the gate\nupload_to_play_store(track: \"production\", rollout: \"0.05\")\nupload_to_app_store(phased_release: true, submit_for_review: true)\n```\n\n- Gate each increase on crash and ANR rates against the previous release and the vitals thresholds.\n- Plan in [Versioning and rollout](references/versioning-and-rollout.md).\n\n### 14. A rollback plan exists before the release starts\n\n```kotlin\n// GOOD: risky features behind remote flags with a safe default; a minimum-version gate for emergencies\nval newSyncEnabled = remoteConfig.getBoolean(\"new_ble_sync_enabled\") // default false in app defaults\nif (BuildConfig.VERSION_CODE < remoteConfig.getLong(\"min_supported_version_code\")) showForceUpdate()\n```\n\n```dart\n// GOOD: Flutter -- same pattern; the flag defaults to the old path if config cannot be fetched\nfinal useNewSync = remoteConfig.getBool('new_ble_sync_enabled');\n```\n\n- Android: halt the staged rollout, fix, ship a higher `versionCode`. There is no downgrade for users who\n  already updated.\n- iOS: no rollback. Pause the phased release, submit a fix, request an expedited review for a critical bug.\n- Server-side kill switches are the only instant rollback on both stores.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Declaring a build \"store ready\" without checking the merged manifest and the archived bundle.\n- Copying Data safety or App Privacy answers from another app, or answering \"no data collected\" with\n  crash reporting or analytics SDKs in the build.\n- Requesting `ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION` for BLE scanning that does not derive location.\n- Using `QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES` to check for one companion app.\n- Writing a privacy manifest with reasons that sound acceptable instead of the ones that match the code.\n- Hand-editing `versionCode` or `CFBundleVersion`, or reusing build numbers across flavors that share a listing.\n- 100% production releases with no staged rollout, no gates and no owner watching vitals.\n- Assuming an App Store release can be rolled back, or that a halted Play rollout removes the update\n  from users who already have it.\n- Shipping a risky BLE or firmware-update change without a remote flag to turn it off.\n- Quoting a store deadline, threshold or guideline number from memory instead of the current page.\n\n## Checklist\n\nGoogle Play:\n- [ ] `targetSdk` meets the current requirement for the app's form factor (or an extension is granted)\n- [ ] Data safety form matches the SDK inventory; data deletion questions answered\n- [ ] Every sensitive permission in the merged manifest is core and declared; none left in test tracks\n- [ ] Foreground service types in the manifest and declared in Play Console with a video\n- [ ] In-app account deletion and a web deletion link (if accounts exist)\n- [ ] App bundle upload; Play App Signing enrolled; upload key stored outside the repo with a reset plan\n- [ ] Release tested on internal or closed track; pre-launch report reviewed with test credentials\n- [ ] Crash and ANR rates of the current release below the vitals thresholds\n\nApp Store:\n- [ ] Built with Xcode 26 or later and the 26 SDK; deployment target iOS 13 or later\n- [ ] `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` in the app target; every required reason API declared with a correct reason\n- [ ] Listed third-party SDKs at versions with manifests (and signatures for binaries); privacy report reviewed\n- [ ] App Privacy details match the inventory; tracking declared consistently with ATT use\n- [ ] ATT prompt before any tracking; `NSUserTrackingUsageDescription` present if requested\n- [ ] Login options meet 4.8; in-app account deletion meets 5.1.1(v); privacy policy linked in app and metadata\n- [ ] Review notes, demo account, live backend, no placeholder content, accurate screenshots\n- [ ] TestFlight build tested by internal testers; external group approved if used\n\nBoth:\n- [ ] Marketing version is semver; build numbers strictly higher than anything uploaded\n- [ ] Release notes written for each store language\n- [ ] Staged or phased rollout with gates, owner and stop conditions\n- [ ] Rollback plan: kill switches for risky features, minimum-version gate, hotfix path\n\n## Output format\n\n~~~markdown\n## Release readiness: <app> <version> (<build numbers>) -- <date>\n\nFacts checked against store pages on <date>. Items marked \"Not verified\" need the console owner.\n\n| Item | Platform | Pass / Fail / N/A | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Target API 36 | Android | Fail | `app/build.gradle.kts:14` targetSdk = 35 |\n| Background location declared | Android | N/A | Not in merged manifest (`./gradlew :app:processReleaseMainManifest`) |\n| FGS type connectedDevice declared | Android | Not verified | Manifest OK (`AndroidManifest.xml:42`); Play Console declaration not visible to the agent |\n| Account deletion (in-app + web) | Both | Pass | `SettingsScreen.kt:88`, `AccountView.swift:51`; web link https://example.com/delete |\n| Privacy manifest and required reasons | iOS | Fail | `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` missing UserDefaults (`CA92.1`) used in `Settings.swift:12` |\n| 4.8 Login Services | iOS | Pass | Sign in with Apple offered next to Google (`LoginView.swift:30`) |\n| Build numbers increase | Both | Pass | versionCode 20401 > live 20307; CFBundleVersion 20401 > 20307 |\n\n### Blockers\n1. <item> -- why it blocks (store rejects / policy violation), fix, owner\n\n### Rollout plan\n- Android: internal, closed, production 5%, 20%, 50%, 100%. Gate: crash and ANR rate vs previous release\n  and vitals thresholds. Owner <name>\n- iOS: TestFlight internal, external, phased release. Pause condition <metric>. Owner <name>\n- Rollback: <flags>, <min-version gate>, hotfix path\n\n### Not verified / check the current policy\n- <console-only items and policy points that could not be confirmed>\n~~~\n\nStatus values: Pass, Fail, N/A, Not verified. \"Pass\" always has evidence; a blocker is any Fail that\ncauses rejection, policy enforcement or user harm.\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- signing, upload automation, version numbers from CI\n- `mobile-eu-compliance` -- GDPR, consent and deletion behind the store privacy forms\n- `mobile-security` -- permissions, storage and network findings that also affect store review\n- `mobile-ble` -- BLE permissions, foreground services and background behavior of companion apps\n- `mobile-testing` -- test pass before a release and for `targetSdk` changes\n- `mobile-performance` -- crash, ANR and startup work behind the vitals gates\n- `mobile-merge-request` -- describing release and policy changes in the MR\n",
    "references/app-store.md":
        "# App Store release readiness\n\nLoad this when checking an iOS or Flutter build for the App Store. Facts were checked on 2026-10-15 against\ndeveloper.apple.com pages named in each section. Guideline numbers and wording change; quote the current\nApp Store Review Guidelines in any reply to App Review.\n\n## Build requirements\n\nSource: Apple \"Upcoming Requirements\" (developer.apple.com/news/upcoming-requirements).\n\n| Since | Requirement |\n|---|---|\n| 28 April 2026 | Uploads built with Xcode 26 or later using the iOS 26, iPadOS 26, tvOS 26, visionOS 26 or watchOS 26 SDK |\n| 9 September 2026 | iOS and iPadOS apps uploaded must target iOS 13 or later |\n| 31 January 2026 | Answers to the updated age rating questions required to keep submitting updates |\n| 1 May 2024 | Approved reasons required for required reason APIs, including in third-party SDKs |\n| 16 October 2024 | EU: trader status required to submit updates for apps on the EU App Store (DSA) |\n\nNo newer future-dated requirement was listed on 2026-10-15. Check the page before each major release and\nbefore pinning the CI Xcode version.\n\n## Privacy manifest\n\nSources: Apple \"Privacy manifest files\", \"Adding a privacy manifest to your app or third-party SDK\",\n\"Describing use of required reason API\".\n\n- File name `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`, added to the target's resources (File, New File, App Privacy File).\n- App bundles place it at the bundle root on iOS. Swift packages declare it as a resource explicitly.\n- Top-level keys:\n\n| Key | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `NSPrivacyTracking` | Whether the app or SDK uses data for tracking as defined for App Tracking Transparency |\n| `NSPrivacyTrackingDomains` | Domains used for tracking; requests to them fail until the user grants tracking permission. Requires `NSPrivacyTracking` true |\n| `NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes` | Data types collected, linked or not, used for tracking or not, purposes |\n| `NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes` | Required reason API categories used, each with approved reasons |\n\n- App Store Connect rejects submissions with invalid manifests (unexpected keys or values) and emails the\n  path of the invalid file. If it comes from an SDK, update the SDK.\n- Generate the privacy report: archive, open Organizer, select the archive, Generate Privacy Report. Review\n  it against the App Privacy answers before submission.\n\n## Required reason API reasons\n\nSource: Apple \"NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType\". Declare only reasons that match the code. Values as of 2026-10-15:\n\n| Category | Reason | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| `NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults` | `CA92.1` | Read and write data only accessible to the app itself |\n| | `1C8F.1` | Data shared within the same App Group |\n| | `C56D.1` | Third-party SDK wrapper, only when the app calls it |\n| | `AC6B.1` | Managed app configuration and feedback keys (MDM) |\n| `NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp` | `C617.1` | Metadata of files in the app, App Group or CloudKit container |\n| | `DDA9.1` | Display timestamps to the user; not sent off-device |\n| | `3B52.1` | Files the user granted access to (document picker) |\n| | `0A2A.1` | Third-party SDK wrapper |\n| `NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime` | `35F9.1` | Elapsed time between in-app events, timers |\n| | `8FFB.1` | Absolute timestamps for in-app events |\n| | `3D61.1` | Optional user-submitted bug report |\n| `NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryDiskSpace` | `85F4.1` | Display disk space to the user |\n| | `E174.1` | Check space before writing, or clean up when low |\n| | `7D9E.1` | Optional user-submitted bug report |\n| | `B728.1` | Health research apps |\n| `NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryActiveKeyboards` | `3EC4.1` | Custom keyboard apps |\n| | `54BD.1` | Customize UI based on active keyboards |\n\nWhere these come from in practice:\n- `UserDefaults` / `@AppStorage`: the app, and plugins such as Flutter `shared_preferences`.\n- File timestamps: `FileManager.attributesOfItem`, `stat`, `URLResourceValues` with dates.\n- Boot time: `mach_absolute_time()`, `ProcessInfo.systemUptime` (often inside analytics and crash SDKs).\n- Disk space: `volumeAvailableCapacityKey`, `statfs`.\n\nSearch the code before writing the manifest:\n\n```sh\ngrep -rnE \"UserDefaults|@AppStorage|systemUptime|mach_absolute_time|attributesOfItem|creationDate|contentModificationDate|volumeAvailableCapacity|statfs|activeInputModes\" --include=*.swift --include=*.m .\n```\n\nAn upload missing a reason gets an email naming the API (ITMS-91053 \"Missing API declaration\").\n\n## Third-party SDK manifests and signatures\n\nSource: Apple \"Third-party SDK requirements\".\n\n- Apple lists commonly used SDKs (examples: Alamofire, Firebase modules, FBSDKCoreKit, GoogleSignIn,\n  Flutter, `path_provider`, `url_launcher`, `share_plus`, OneSignal, RealmSwift, Lottie). Check the current\n  list for every dependency.\n- New apps that include a listed SDK, or updates that add one, must include its privacy manifest. Binary\n  dependencies on the list must also be signed. Any version, and SDKs that repackage listed ones, count.\n- Since 12 February 2025, submissions must contain a valid privacy manifest for these SDKs.\n- Xcode validates that a new version of a signed SDK comes from the same developer.\n- The app developer remains responsible for all code an SDK brings in.\n\n## App Privacy details\n\nSource: Apple \"App privacy details on the App Store\".\n\n- Required to submit new apps and updates. Includes data collected by third-party SDKs.\n- A data type may be omitted only if it meets all the optional disclosure criteria (not used for tracking,\n  not for advertising or other purposes, infrequent, optional, user-provided each time with clear disclosure).\n- Data is presumed linked to the user unless de-identified before collection.\n- Answers can be updated at any time without a new build, and must stay accurate.\n\n## App Tracking Transparency\n\nSources: Apple \"User privacy and data use\"; AppTrackingTransparency framework documentation.\n\n- Tracking: linking user or device data from the app with other companies' data for targeted ads or ad\n  measurement, or sharing it with data brokers.\n- Ask through ATT before tracking or reading the advertising identifier. Without permission the identifier\n  is all zeros; tracking with another identifier (for example a hashed email) is not allowed.\n- Not tracking: on-device-only linking, sharing solely for fraud prevention or security, consumer reporting\n  for credit decisions.\n- `NSUserTrackingUsageDescription` in `Info.plist` explains the use. No incentives and no gating (5.1.2(i)).\n- Fingerprinting is not allowed regardless of ATT.\n\n## Common App Review rejections\n\nSource: App Store Review Guidelines (developer.apple.com/app-store/review/guidelines).\n\n| Guideline | What it says (short) | Check |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2.1(a) App Completeness | Final versions, no placeholder text or temporary content, tested on device, demo account and live backend if login exists; crashing binaries are rejected | Demo account in review notes, backend up, no \"TODO\" or lorem ipsum strings |\n| 2.3 Accurate Metadata | Metadata, including privacy information, matches the app's core experience | Description, screenshots, App Privacy answers current |\n| 2.3.3 | Screenshots show the app in use, not only title art, login or splash screen | Screenshot set per device size |\n| 2.5.4 | Background services only for intended purposes (VoIP, audio, location, task completion, notifications) | `UIBackgroundModes` justified, BLE background modes used for real features |\n| 4.2 Minimum Functionality | More than a repackaged website | WebView-only apps |\n| 4.8 Login Services | Third-party or social login for the primary account needs an equivalent option limiting data to name and email, private email, no ad tracking without consent | Sign in with Apple or another compliant option, or a listed exception |\n| 5.1.1(i) Privacy Policies | Privacy policy link in App Store Connect metadata and easily accessible in the app | Link in settings or about screen |\n| 5.1.1(ii) Permission | Purpose strings clearly and completely describe the use | Every `NS*UsageDescription` specific to the feature |\n| 5.1.1(iv) Access | No manipulating or forcing consent; offer alternatives when a permission is declined | App works with location denied (manual entry) |\n| 5.1.1(v) Account Sign-In | Apps that support account creation offer account deletion in the app; no login if no significant account features | Deletion path in account settings |\n| 5.1.2(i) Data Use and Sharing | ATT permission required to track; no requiring tracking, notifications or location to use the app | ATT flow and feature gating |\n\n4.8 exceptions (no extra login option needed): only the company's own accounts; alternative app marketplace\nlogin; education or enterprise accounts; government or industry citizen ID; a client for a specific\nthird-party service where users sign in to that service to access their content.\n\n## Account deletion (5.1.1(v))\n\nSource: Apple \"Offering account deletion in your app\".\n\n- Easy to find, typically in account settings. Delete the entire account record and associated personal data.\n- Temporary deactivation alone is insufficient. Extra confirmation steps are allowed; obstruction is not.\n- If deletion must finish on a website, link directly to that page.\n- Customer service flows (phone, email) only for highly regulated industries (5.1.1(ix)).\n- Manual deletion that takes time is acceptable if the user is told how long and gets a confirmation.\n- Sign in with Apple: revoke user tokens with the REST API and handle server-to-server notifications.\n- Applies to everyone regardless of location, and to automatically created guest accounts.\n\n## Review notes for hardware companion apps\n\n```text\nDemo account: reviewer@example.com / (password in the App Review Information field, not here)\nHardware: the app controls the <model> gateway over Bluetooth LE. Without the device, the reviewer can\nsign in, browse the demo site and its recorded history. Pairing, live data and firmware update are shown\nin the attached video: <link>.\nBackground mode bluetooth-central: keeps the connection to the paired gateway to receive alarms.\n```\n\n## TestFlight\n\nSource: App Store Connect Help \"TestFlight overview\".\n\n- Internal testers: up to 100 App Store Connect users with access to the app.\n- External testers: up to 10,000, by email or public link. The first build added to an external group goes\n  to App Review; later builds may not need a full review.\n- Builds are available for 90 days.\n- Use TestFlight internal for every release candidate; external groups for field testing with devices.\n\n## Phased release and expedited review\n\nSources: App Store Connect Help \"Release a version update in phases\"; Apple \"App Review\".\n\n- Phased release: 7 days at 1%, 2%, 5%, 10%, 20%, 50%, 100% of users with automatic updates. Anyone can\n  still update manually from the App Store. Pause for up to 30 days in total, any number of times.\n  \"Release to All Users\" ends it early.\n- Removing the app from sale stops the phased release for that version.\n- There is no rollback of a released version. Fix forward with a new build.\n- Expedited review: request for a critical bug fix (include steps to reproduce on the current version) or a\n  time-sensitive event. It is a request, not a guarantee. Apple states it reviews at least 50% of\n  submissions in under 24 hours and 90% in under 48 hours.\n\n## Crash monitoring\n\n- Xcode Organizer shows crashes, hangs, energy and launch metrics from users who share analytics.\n- MetricKit delivers daily metric reports and diagnostic reports in the app (`MXMetricManager`\n  subscriber; iOS 27 adds `MetricManager` with async sequences). Forward them to your backend or crash\n  reporter, without personal data.\n- App Store Connect has no published crash threshold equivalent to Android vitals; set the team's own gate.\n",
    "references/google-play.md":
        "# Google Play release readiness\n\nLoad this when checking an Android or Flutter build for Google Play. Facts were checked on 2026-10-15 against\nthe Play Console Help and developer.android.com pages named in each section. Play policies change often:\nif a date below is in the past or close, re-check the page before relying on it.\n\n## Target API level\n\nSource: Play Console Help \"Target API level requirements for Google Play apps\" and developer.android.com\n\"Meet Google Play's target API level requirement\".\n\n| Form factor | New apps and updates must target | Since |\n|---|---|---|\n| Phone and tablet | Android 16 (API 36) or higher | 31 August 2026 |\n| Wear OS | Android 15 (API 35) or higher | 31 August 2026 |\n| Android Automotive OS | Android 15 (API 35) or higher | 31 August 2026 |\n| Android TV, Android XR | Android 14 (API 34) or higher | Check the current policy (the Help page lists different dates in two places) |\n\n- Extension: can be requested in Play Console until 1 November 2026.\n- Existing apps below API 35 (phones) are not offered to new users on devices running a newer Android\n  version than the app targets.\n- Exception: permanently private apps for internal distribution in one organization.\n- Next deadline: no official announcement for API 37 was found on 2026-10-15. Check the current policy\n  before planning the next `targetSdk` raise.\n\nWhat to check:\n- `targetSdk` in every application module and flavor that ships, not only `app/`.\n- Behavior changes for the new level (foreground services, background starts, edge-to-edge, permissions,\n  intents). Run the full test pass on a device with that Android version.\n\n## Data safety section\n\nSource: Play Console Help \"Provide information for Google Play's Data safety section\".\n\n- Every published app must complete the form, including apps that collect no user data.\n- Data collected or shared by third-party code (SDKs) must be included.\n- Building advertising or user profiles cannot be declared as ephemeral processing.\n- Encryption in transit: the form asks about it; the Help page points to TLS or HTTPS. Answer from what\n  the code and SDKs actually do, and check the form's current wording.\n- Data deletion questions are mandatory for all developers.\n- Inaccurate declarations lead to policy enforcement such as blocked updates or removal.\n- The independent security review badge is optional and does not verify the declarations.\n\nMapping procedure:\n1. List SDKs from the dependency graph (`./gradlew :app:dependencies --configuration releaseRuntimeClasspath`).\n2. For each SDK, take the vendor's Data safety guidance and confirm it against how the app configures it.\n3. Add the app's own collection: account data, telemetry, crash logs, device identifiers, location.\n4. Mark each type: collected or shared, required or optional, purpose. Keep the sheet in the repo next\n   to the iOS App Privacy sheet so both stores stay consistent.\n\n## Sensitive permissions and declarations\n\n### Background location\n\nSource: Play Console Help \"Requesting access to location in the background\".\n\n- Only for core functionality. Never solely for ads or analytics.\n- Declaration under App content, Sensitive app permissions, Location permissions. Describe one feature.\n- Prominent disclosure in normal app use, before the runtime prompt, containing the word \"location\",\n  describing the data and use, saying it works \"when the app is closed\" or \"not in use\", and listing every\n  feature that uses it. Template from the page: \"[This app] collects location data to enable [\"feature\"]\n  even when the app is closed or not in use.\"\n- Video (30 seconds or less recommended) showing the disclosure, the runtime prompt and the feature.\n- If not needed, remove the permission from the manifest in all tracks, including test tracks.\n\nBLE companion apps: on Android 12 and later, `BLUETOOTH_SCAN` with `neverForLocation` avoids location\npermissions entirely when the app does not derive location from scan results. Ask the firmware or product\nowner before setting it if beacons or location features exist.\n\n### SMS and Call Log\n\nSource: Play Console Help \"Use of SMS or Call Log permission groups\".\n\n- Only for the app registered as the default SMS, Phone or Assistant handler, or a listed temporary exception\n  when no alternative exists.\n- Declared through the Permissions Declaration Form in Play Console. Apps without it may be removed.\n\n### QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES\n\nSource: Play Console Help \"Use of the broad package (App) visibility (QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES) permission\".\n\n- Applies to apps targeting API 30 or later.\n- Permitted only when the core purpose needs all installed apps (device search, antivirus, file managers,\n  browsers). Not allowed if a less broad method works.\n- Declared through the Permissions Declaration Form.\n\n```xml\n<!-- Prefer package visibility filtering -->\n<queries>\n    <package android:name=\"com.example.gateway.setup\" />\n    <intent>\n        <action android:name=\"android.intent.action.VIEW\" />\n        <data android:scheme=\"https\" />\n    </intent>\n</queries>\n```\n\n### Foreground service types\n\nSources: Play Console Help \"Understanding Google Play's foreground service requirements\";\ndeveloper.android.com \"Foreground service types\".\n\n- Apps targeting Android 14 and above declare a type for each foreground service in the manifest, with\n  the matching `FOREGROUND_SERVICE_*` permission.\n- Each type used is declared on the App content page with: a description of the feature, the user impact\n  if the task is deferred or interrupted, a video showing how the user triggers it, and the use case.\n- `connectedDevice` requires one of: a network or NFC manifest permission, a granted Bluetooth or UWB\n  runtime permission (for example `BLUETOOTH_CONNECT`), or USB permission.\n- Apps targeting Android 15 or higher cannot start a `dataSync` foreground service from `BOOT_COMPLETED`.\n- `specialUse` is for cases no other type covers and is reviewed.\n\nOther declarations to look for in App content: full-screen intent, exact alarms, accessibility services,\nphoto and video permissions, health permissions. Check the current policy for each one the app uses.\n\n## Account deletion\n\nSource: Play Console Help \"Understanding Google Play's app account deletion requirements\".\n\n- Apps that allow account creation anywhere in the app must provide an in-app path to delete the account\n  and associated data, easy to find (for example account settings).\n- They must also provide a web link where users can request deletion without reinstalling the app. The\n  link is entered in the Data safety form.\n- Deleting the account means deleting associated user data. Data kept for security, fraud prevention or\n  legal reasons must be disclosed.\n- Wear OS and Android TV apps need no in-app option but still need the web link.\n\n## App bundle and Play App Signing\n\nSources: Play Console Help \"Android App Bundles\" and \"Use Play App Signing\".\n\n- Apps created after August 2021 publish Android App Bundles (`.aab`). Only legacy apps may still use APKs.\n- New apps are enrolled in Play App Signing by default.\n- Upload key: held by the developer (RSA 2048 or larger), signs the bundle before upload. If lost or\n  leaked, request an upload key reset in Play Console (Manage Play app signing, Request upload key reset)\n  with the new certificate exported as PEM.\n- App signing key: held by Google, signs what users install. It can be upgraded through Play Console.\n- API keys, Firebase, Google Sign-In and `assetlinks.json` use the app signing key fingerprints from Play\n  Console, not the local upload key. If the console shows several fingerprints, register all of them.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: release signed with a keystore committed to the repo\nsigningConfigs { create(\"release\") { storeFile = file(\"release.jks\"); storePassword = \"changeit\" } }\n\n// GOOD: upload key from the CI environment (see mobile-ci-cd)\nsigningConfigs {\n    create(\"release\") {\n        storeFile = file(System.getenv(\"ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PATH\"))\n        storePassword = System.getenv(\"ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD\")\n        keyAlias = System.getenv(\"ANDROID_KEY_ALIAS\")\n        keyPassword = System.getenv(\"ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD\")\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Release tracks\n\nSource: Play Console Help \"Set up an open, closed, or internal test\".\n\n| Track | Who | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Internal testing | Up to 100 testers by email | Available within minutes; use for every release candidate |\n| Closed testing | Email lists (up to 2,000 users each) or Google Groups | Test link can take hours the first time |\n| Open testing | Anyone who joins; visible on Play | Optional cap of at least 1,000 |\n| Production | All users | Test-track users get production if it has a higher versionCode |\n\n- New personal developer accounts (created after 13 November 2023): a closed test with at least 12 testers\n  opted in for at least 14 consecutive days before applying for production (Play Console Help \"App testing\n  requirements for new personal developer accounts\").\n- Managed publishing lets the team control when an approved release goes live. Check the current Play\n  Console Help for how it interacts with review times.\n\n## Pre-launch report\n\nSources: Play Console Help \"Use a pre-launch report to identify issues\" and \"Pre-launch report overview\".\n\n- Runs automatically after a bundle upload, subject to device lab capacity. Results usually within an hour.\n- Reports stability (crashes, ANRs, restricted API use), performance, accessibility and screenshots.\n- Provide a dedicated test account under Pre-launch report, Settings, Test account credentials. Login\n  automation works with standard Android widgets, not WebView or OpenGL sign-in.\n- Robo scripts recorded in Android Studio guide the crawler; up to three deep links can be added.\n- Crashes on test devices do not count toward the app's vitals. The report cannot find every issue.\n\n## Android vitals\n\nSource: developer.android.com \"Android vitals\".\n\n| Metric | Overall | Per phone model |\n|---|---|---|\n| User-perceived crash rate | 1.09% | 8% |\n| User-perceived ANR rate | 0.47% | 8% |\n| Excessive partial wake locks | 5% | - |\n\n- Above a threshold, Play may reduce the app's visibility and show a warning on the store listing.\n- Check the evaluation window and the per-watch-model thresholds in Play Console before using them as gates.\n",
    "references/versioning-and-rollout.md":
        "# Versioning, rollout and rollback\n\nLoad this when choosing version numbers, writing release notes, planning a staged or phased rollout, or\npreparing a rollback plan. Store facts are from the pages named in the other two references, checked on\n2026-10-15.\n\n## Version numbers\n\nTwo numbers per platform. The marketing version is for people; the build number is for the store and the OS.\n\n| | Android | iOS | Flutter (`pubspec.yaml`) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Marketing version | `versionName` (string, shown to users) | `CFBundleShortVersionString` (three period-separated integers) | `version: 2.4.1+...` (build name) |\n| Build number | `versionCode` (positive integer, max 2100000000, never reused on Play) | `CFBundleVersion` (one to three period-separated integers) | `version: ...+20401` (build number) |\n\nRules:\n- Marketing version follows semantic versioning `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`. It comes from the release tag.\n- Build numbers strictly increase across every upload, on every track and branch, including hotfixes.\n  Android uses `versionCode` to block downgrades; Play rejects a reused code.\n- One build-number scheme for both platforms makes crash reports and support tickets easier to match.\n  A common scheme: `MAJOR * 10000 + MINOR * 100 + PATCH` with room for rebuilds, or a CI run number\n  with an offset (see `mobile-ci-cd`). Check the scheme cannot overflow or collide with past uploads.\n- Flavors that share one Play listing need disjoint `versionCode` ranges.\n- KMP and Flutter: one source of truth (tag or `pubspec.yaml`), passed to both native builds.\n\n```kotlin\n// GOOD: build.gradle.kts -- fail the build instead of uploading a wrong number\nval versionCodeProp = providers.gradleProperty(\"versionCode\").orNull?.toIntOrNull()\n    ?: error(\"Pass -PversionCode=<int>\")\nandroid {\n    defaultConfig {\n        versionCode = versionCodeProp\n        versionName = providers.gradleProperty(\"versionName\").get()\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```text\n# GOOD: iOS -- Config/Version.xcconfig, overridden from CI\nMARKETING_VERSION = 2.4.1\nCURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION = 20401\n```\n\n## Release notes\n\n- Written per release, per store language, from the merged changes. User-facing changes only; no ticket\n  numbers or internal names.\n- Mention security fixes without exploit details. Mention required actions (re-pair the device, update the\n  gateway firmware) first.\n- Play release notes and App Store \"What's New\" have length limits. Check the current limit in the console.\n- Keep the source text in the repo (for example `fastlane/metadata/`) so it is reviewed with the release.\n\n```text\nBAD:  \"Bug fixes and performance improvements. JIRA-1234, refactored BleManager.\"\nGOOD: \"Pairing with the Gateway 2 is faster and retries automatically if the connection drops.\n       Fixed a crash when opening history without a network connection.\"\n```\n\n## Rollout plan\n\n### Android (Play)\n\n1. Upload to internal testing. Smoke test on real devices, including the oldest supported Android version\n   and the newest one.\n2. Promote to closed (and optionally open) testing. Review the pre-launch report.\n3. Production staged rollout. Suggested steps: 1% or 5%, 20%, 50%, 100%. Staged rollout is for updates\n   only; the first production release goes to everyone.\n4. Raise the percentage manually after each gate passes. It never increases automatically.\n\n### iOS (App Store)\n\n1. TestFlight internal for every release candidate; external groups for field tests.\n2. Submit with phased release on. Seven days at 1%, 2%, 5%, 10%, 20%, 50%, 100% of users with automatic\n   updates; manual updates are not limited.\n3. Pause (up to 30 days in total) if a gate fails. \"Release to All Users\" only after the gate passes.\n\n### Gates\n\nDecide the numbers before the rollout starts, and write them in the release ticket.\n\n| Gate | Android source | iOS source | Example stop condition |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Crash rate | Play Console vitals, Crashlytics | Xcode Organizer, MetricKit, crash reporter | Crash-free users below previous release, or user-perceived crash rate approaching 1.09% |\n| ANR or hang rate | Play Console vitals | Organizer hangs, MetricKit hang diagnostics | ANR rate approaching 0.47%, or hangs up vs previous release |\n| Per-device spikes | Vitals per phone model (8% threshold) | Organizer by device | One model far above the rest |\n| Feature health | Backend metrics, BLE connect success rate, firmware update success rate | Same | Pairing or update success drops |\n| Support signals | Reviews, tickets | Same | New issue cluster |\n\nThe 1.09%, 0.47% and 8% figures are Play's bad behavior thresholds (developer.android.com \"Android\nvitals\"). A release gate should trigger well below them; a threshold breach is already a store penalty.\n\n## Rollback\n\nNeither store can put an older build back on devices that already updated. Plan for fixing forward, and\nmake risky features switchable from the server.\n\n### Android\n\n1. Halt the staged rollout in Play Console. No new users get the version; users who have it keep it.\n2. Turn off the faulty feature with its remote flag if one exists.\n3. Build the fix with a higher `versionCode` (a rebuild of the previous code also needs a higher\n   `versionCode`). Release it, staged again.\n4. Resuming a halted rollout targets the same set of users, so only resume a version you trust.\n\n### iOS\n\n1. Pause the phased release. Users with automatic updates stop receiving it; manual updates still work.\n2. Turn off the faulty feature remotely.\n3. Submit a fix. For a critical bug, request an expedited review with steps to reproduce on the current\n   version. It is not guaranteed.\n4. If the release is harmful and no fix is ready, removing the app from sale is a last resort with\n   side effects (the phased release cannot be restarted for that version). Check the current App Store\n   Connect Help before doing it.\n\n### Kill switches and minimum version\n\n- Every risky change (new BLE stack, firmware update flow, sync engine, payment) ships behind a remote flag\n  with an in-app default that keeps the old behavior when the config cannot be fetched.\n- A minimum supported version from the backend forces an update when a build is unsafe (Play In-App\n  Updates immediate flow on Android, a blocking screen with the App Store link on iOS).\n- Flags are configuration, not secrets. Do not put keys or credentials in remote config.\n- Remove flags after the rollout completes, or they become permanent forks of the code.\n\n```kotlin\n// GOOD: flag with safe default; evaluated once per session, off the main thread\nclass FeatureFlags @Inject constructor(private val remote: RemoteConfigSource) {\n    suspend fun newBleSyncEnabled(): Boolean =\n        runCatching { remote.getBoolean(\"new_ble_sync_enabled\") }.getOrDefault(false)\n}\n```\n\n```swift\n// GOOD: same on iOS\nfunc newBleSyncEnabled() async -> Bool {\n    (try? await remoteConfig.bool(for: \"new_ble_sync_enabled\")) ?? false\n}\n```\n\n```dart\n// GOOD: Flutter\nFuture<bool> newBleSyncEnabled() async {\n  try {\n    return await remoteConfig.getBool('new_ble_sync_enabled');\n  } catch (_) {\n    return false;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Release ticket template\n\n```text\nRelease: <app> <version> (<versionCode> / <CFBundleVersion>)\nOwner: <name>          Approver: <name>\nScope: <merged MRs>    Risky changes and their flags: <list>\nGates: crash <x>, ANR/hang <y>, feature metric <z>, checked at <times>\nAndroid: internal <date>, closed <date>, production 5% <date>, 20%, 50%, 100%\niOS: TestFlight <date>, submitted <date>, phased release start <date>\nRollback: halt / pause, flags to turn off, hotfix branch, expedited review criteria\n```\n",
  },
  "mobile-security": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-security\ndescription: \"Audits Android, iOS and Flutter apps against OWASP MASVS v2 and the Mobile Top 10 2024: hardcoded secrets, insecure storage (Keystore, Keychain), TLS and pinning, exported components, deep links, WebViews, biometrics, logging of personal data and supply chain. Reports findings with MASVS IDs, severity and evidence. Use for security reviews, pentest prep, or changes to auth, crypto, storage, network or IPC code.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile security audit\n\nAudit a mobile app the way a mobile pentester and a compliance reviewer would together: find what an\nattacker on the network, a malicious app on the device, or someone holding an unlocked or rooted phone can\nactually exploit, map it to an OWASP MASVS v2 control, and show the smallest fix that closes it.\n\n## When to use\n\n- The user asks for a security audit, security review, MASVS or OWASP Mobile Top 10 check, or pentest prep.\n- A change touches credentials, tokens, crypto, local storage, networking, TLS, pinning, IPC, deep links,\n  WebViews, biometrics, logging or third-party SDKs.\n- An IoT companion app talks to devices over BLE, MQTT or HTTPS and handles device identifiers.\n- Preparing evidence for GDPR, Cyber Resilience Act, RED Delegated Act (EN 18031) or ETSI EN 303 645 reviews.\n\nNot for: general code quality or concurrency review (use `mobile-code-review`), or CI pipeline setup\n(use `mobile-ci-cd`, though this skill flags missing dependency scanning and SBOM steps).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Define scope and threat model before reading code.\n   - What is sensitive: credentials, tokens, keys, health or location data, device identifiers, payment data.\n   - Which attackers matter: network attacker (public Wi-Fi), malicious app on the same device, lost or\n     stolen unlocked device, user with a rooted or jailbroken device, reverse engineer of the binary.\n   - Which backends and devices are under the developer's control (relevant for pinning and attestation).\n2. Inventory the attack surface and load the matching references:\n   [Android](references/android.md), [iOS](references/ios.md), [Flutter](references/flutter.md),\n   [MASVS controls and Top 10 map](references/masvs-map.md).\n   For CRA, RED delegated act / EN 18031 or GDPR questions, hand the findings to `mobile-eu-compliance`.\n   - Android: merged release manifest, network security config, backup rules, release build type, catalogs.\n   - iOS: `Info.plist`, entitlements, `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`, `.xcconfig`, lock files. Flutter: both of these.\n3. Run the quick-scan patterns from the references. Each hit is a candidate, not a finding.\n4. Walk the MASVS categories in order: STORAGE, CRYPTO, AUTH, NETWORK, PLATFORM, CODE, RESILIENCE, PRIVACY.\n   Read the full code path for every candidate, not just the matching line.\n5. Judge the release build: check flavor manifests, `debug-overrides`, `#if DEBUG` and `kDebugMode` guards.\n   A debug-only issue is Low at most, unless it can ship.\n6. Verify each finding: reachable in release? Mitigated elsewhere (server check, interceptor, OS default)?\n   If static review cannot confirm it, list it under \"Needs dynamic verification\", not as a finding.\n7. Rate severity, assign one MASVS v2 control ID and the matching Mobile Top 10 2024 item, and write a fix.\n   Only use control IDs listed in [MASVS controls](references/masvs-map.md).\n8. Report using the output format, including what was not checked. Do not edit files unless asked.\n\n## Severity levels\n\n| Severity | Definition | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| Critical | Exploitable remotely or at scale with no special access; compromises accounts, keys or many users' data | Backend admin key in the binary, trust-all `TrustManager`, cleartext login, MQTT credentials over port 1883 |\n| High | Exploitable with realistic preconditions (same network, malicious app installed, brief physical access); exposes sensitive data or bypasses auth | Exported component leaking tokens, unvalidated deep link loading any URL in a WebView with a JS bridge, boolean-only biometric gate on secrets |\n| Medium | Needs strong preconditions (root, jailbreak, backup extraction) or weakens defense in depth | Tokens in plaintext app storage, sensitive screens without screenshot protection, `allowBackup` with tokens |\n| Low | Hardening gap with little direct impact | No obfuscation, verbose non-sensitive logs, missing CT awareness, debug-only issue |\n\nRate by impact and likelihood in this app's threat model. A finding without a realistic attacker is not\nHigh because a checklist says so.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. No secrets in the app package (MASVS-CRYPTO-2, MASVS-AUTH-1, M1)\n\nEverything in an APK, IPA or Flutter bundle is readable: `BuildConfig`, `strings.xml`, `Info.plist`,\n`.xcconfig`, assets, `--dart-define` values. Obfuscation does not change that.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: ends up as a constant in the APK\nbuildConfigField(\"String\", \"PAYMENTS_SECRET\", \"\\\"sk_live_...\\\"\")\n\n// GOOD: app gets a short-lived, user-scoped token from your backend after login\nval token = authRepository.sessionToken()\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD: Info.plist and xcconfig values ship in plain text\nlet key = Bundle.main.object(forInfoDictionaryKey: \"BROKER_PASSWORD\") as? String\n```\n\n- Restricted public identifiers (OAuth client ID, Maps key locked to package and signing cert) are fine.\n  Anything that grants privilege on its own (server keys, signing keys, shared broker passwords) is not.\n- Check git history and CI config too; a secret ever committed must be rotated, not just deleted.\n\n### 2. Sensitive data at rest uses platform key stores (MASVS-STORAGE-1, MASVS-CRYPTO-2, M9)\n\n`androidx.security:security-crypto` (`EncryptedSharedPreferences`, `EncryptedFile`, `MasterKey`) is\ndeprecated. Do not add it; plan migration for existing users. Replacement: an Android Keystore key, either\ndirectly with `Cipher` or via Tink AEAD, with ciphertext stored in DataStore or a file.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: plaintext token in SharedPreferences or DataStore\nprefs.edit { putString(\"refresh_token\", token) }\n\n// GOOD: Tink AEAD, keyset wrapped by an Android Keystore key (setup in references/android.md)\nval ciphertext = aead.encrypt(token.encodeToByteArray(), \"refresh_token\".encodeToByteArray())\ndataStore.edit { it[REFRESH_TOKEN] = Base64.encodeToString(ciphertext, Base64.NO_WRAP) }\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD\nUserDefaults.standard.set(token, forKey: \"refreshToken\")\n\n// GOOD: Keychain, not migrated to other devices or backups\nlet query: [String: Any] = [\n    kSecClass as String: kSecClassGenericPassword,\n    kSecAttrService as String: \"com.example.auth\",\n    kSecAttrAccount as String: \"refreshToken\",\n    kSecValueData as String: Data(token.utf8),\n    kSecAttrAccessible as String: kSecAttrAccessibleAfterFirstUnlockThisDeviceOnly,\n]\nlet status = SecItemAdd(query as CFDictionary, nil)\n```\n\n```dart\n// BAD\nawait prefs.setString('refresh_token', token);\n\n// GOOD\nconst storage = FlutterSecureStorage(\n  iOptions: IOSOptions(accessibility: KeychainAccessibility.first_unlock_this_device),\n);\nawait storage.write(key: 'refresh_token', value: token);\n```\n\n- Pick the strictest Keychain accessibility class the feature allows; background refresh needs `AfterFirstUnlock`.\n- Never roll custom crypto: no ECB, no static IVs, no keys derived from device IDs, no `Random` for secrets.\n\n### 3. Sensitive data does not leak through side channels (MASVS-STORAGE-2, MASVS-PLATFORM-3, M9)\n\nLogs, backups, screenshots, the app switcher snapshot, clipboard and keyboard caches all leak.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: Log.d survives in release unless stripped\nLog.d(\"Auth\", \"login ok token=\$token user=\$email\")\n\n// GOOD: no secrets or personal data in logs; strip debug logs in release with R8 rules\nLog.d(\"Auth\", \"login ok\")\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD: explicitly public in the unified log\nlogger.info(\"token \\(token, privacy: .public)\")\n\n// GOOD\nlogger.info(\"login ok for user \\(userId, privacy: .private(mask: .hash))\")\n```\n\n- Android: `window.setFlags(FLAG_SECURE, FLAG_SECURE)` on screens with credentials, OTPs, payment or health data.\n- iOS has no API to block screenshots; cover sensitive content when `scenePhase` leaves `.active`.\n- Android backup: exclude token and keyset files via `dataExtractionRules` and `fullBackupContent`, or disable\n  backup. Keystore keys never leave the device, so restored ciphertext cannot be decrypted; handle that path.\n\n### 4. All traffic uses TLS with platform validation (MASVS-NETWORK-1, M5)\n\n```xml\n<!-- BAD -->\n<application android:usesCleartextTraffic=\"true\" ... >\n\n<!-- GOOD: res/xml/network_security_config.xml -->\n<network-security-config>\n    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted=\"false\" />\n</network-security-config>\n```\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: disables certificate and hostname validation for everything\nOkHttpClient.Builder()\n    .sslSocketFactory(trustAllFactory, trustAllManager)\n    .hostnameVerifier { _, _ -> true }\n\n// GOOD: platform defaults\nOkHttpClient.Builder().build()\n```\n\n- iOS: no `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads`; no `.useCredential` for a server trust that skipped `SecTrustEvaluateWithError`.\n- Dart: no `badCertificateCallback` returning `true`. `dart:io` does not apply ATS or the Android network config.\n\nIoT and MQTT: TLS always, including on \"internal\" networks. Never cleartext brokers.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD\nMqttClient(\"tcp://broker.example.com:1883\", clientId, MemoryPersistence())\n\n// GOOD: TLS, per-device or per-user credentials, broker-side topic ACLs\nMqttClient(\"ssl://broker.example.com:8883\", clientId, MemoryPersistence())\n```\n\n- BLE: CRC16 or CRC32 detects corruption, not tampering. Commands that change device state need an\n  authenticated channel (LE Secure Connections pairing plus app-layer authentication, or a MAC/AEAD).\n\n### 5. Pinning only with a rotation plan (MASVS-NETWORK-2, M5)\n\nDefault: standard CA validation, which Certificate Transparency enforcement strengthens where the platform\napplies it. Pin only when the threat model requires it (high-value targets, controlled backends, IoT\nprovisioning endpoints) and the team owns key rotation.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: one pin, no backup; the app stops connecting when the server key rotates\n.add(\"api.example.com\", \"sha256/<current-spki>\")\n\n// GOOD: SPKI pins for current key and a pre-generated backup key, with monitoring and a release plan\nCertificatePinner.Builder()\n    .add(\"api.example.com\", \"sha256/<current-spki>\")\n    .add(\"api.example.com\", \"sha256/<backup-spki>\")\n    .build()\n```\n\n- Android `<pin-set>` needs an `expiration` and a backup pin; iOS uses `NSPinnedDomains`. Never pin\n  third-party endpoints you do not control (analytics, CDNs, identity providers).\n- Pinning is bypassable on a rooted device; it protects users from network attackers, not the app from its user.\n\n### 6. Biometrics unlock a key, not a boolean (MASVS-AUTH-2, MASVS-AUTH-3, M3)\n\nA success callback alone can be hooked on a rooted or jailbroken device. Bind the secret to authentication.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: boolean gate\nbiometricPrompt.authenticate(promptInfo)\n// onAuthenticationSucceeded { showVault() }\n\n// GOOD: Keystore key with setUserAuthenticationRequired(true); only an authenticated Cipher decrypts\nbiometricPrompt.authenticate(promptInfo, BiometricPrompt.CryptoObject(cipher))\n// onAuthenticationSucceeded { result -> result.cryptoObject?.cipher?.doFinal(encryptedToken) }\n```\n\n```swift\n// BAD: boolean gate\nif try await LAContext().evaluatePolicy(.deviceOwnerAuthenticationWithBiometrics, localizedReason: \"Unlock\") {\n    showVault()\n}\n\n// GOOD: Keychain item protected by access control; the OS releases it only after biometry\nlet access = SecAccessControlCreateWithFlags(\n    nil, kSecAttrAccessibleWhenPasscodeSetThisDeviceOnly, .biometryCurrentSet, nil)\n// add with kSecAttrAccessControl: access; read with kSecUseAuthenticationContext off the main thread\n```\n\n- `BIOMETRIC_STRONG` for crypto-bound auth. `setInvalidatedByBiometricEnrollment(true)` and `.biometryCurrentSet`\n  invalidate the key on new enrollment. iOS requires `NSFaceIDUsageDescription` in `Info.plist`.\n\n### 7. Sessions are controlled by the server (MASVS-AUTH-1, M3)\n\n- OAuth / OIDC uses the system browser (Custom Tabs, `ASWebAuthenticationSession`) with PKCE, not an\n  embedded WebView. Passkeys via Credential Manager or `AuthenticationServices` where supported.\n- Short-lived access tokens, refresh tokens in secure storage, server-side revocation on logout.\n- Authorization is enforced by the backend. A hidden button or a local `isAdmin` flag is not access control.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: role decided on the client\nif (prefs.getBoolean(\"is_admin\", false)) showAdminPanel()\n\n// GOOD: UI hint only; every admin API call is authorized server-side\nif (session.claims.roles.contains(\"admin\")) showAdminPanel()\n```\n\n### 8. IPC entry points are minimal and validate input (MASVS-PLATFORM-1, MASVS-CODE-4, M4, M8)\n\n```xml\n<!-- BAD: internal screen reachable by any app -->\n<activity android:name=\".DebugMenuActivity\" android:exported=\"true\" />\n\n<!-- GOOD -->\n<activity android:name=\".DebugMenuActivity\" android:exported=\"false\" />\n```\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: deep link parameter goes straight into a privileged action\nwebView.loadUrl(intent.data?.getQueryParameter(\"url\").orEmpty())\n\n// GOOD: allowlist scheme and host\nval target = intent.data?.getQueryParameter(\"url\")?.toUri()\nif (target?.scheme == \"https\" && target.host in trustedHosts) webView.loadUrl(target.toString())\n```\n\n- iOS `onOpenURL` / `application(_:continue:)` and Flutter routers validate the same way before routing.\n- Custom URL schemes can be claimed by any app. Use verified App Links (`android:autoVerify=\"true\"` plus\n  `assetlinks.json`) and Universal Links (Associated Domains) for anything carrying tokens or codes.\n- Never launch an `Intent` taken from another app's extras (intent redirection).\n- `PendingIntent` uses `FLAG_IMMUTABLE` unless mutability is required, with an explicit target component.\n- Content providers: `exported=\"false\"` or signature-level permissions; no raw path joins on `openFile`.\n\n### 9. WebViews load trusted content and expose minimal bridges (MASVS-PLATFORM-2, M4)\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: JS bridge on any page the WebView navigates to\nwebView.settings.javaScriptEnabled = true\nwebView.addJavascriptInterface(TokenBridge(session), \"Android\")\nwebView.loadUrl(urlFromServerOrDeepLink)\n\n// GOOD: origin-scoped message listener, navigation restricted to an allowlist\nif (WebViewFeature.isFeatureSupported(WebViewFeature.WEB_MESSAGE_LISTENER)) {\n    WebViewCompat.addWebMessageListener(webView, \"bridge\", setOf(\"https://app.example.com\"), listener)\n}\n```\n\n- iOS: every `WKScriptMessageHandler` checks `message.frameInfo.securityOrigin` and `isMainFrame`.\n- Keep `allowFileAccess`, `allowFileAccessFromFileURLs` and `allowUniversalAccessFromFileURLs` off.\n  Serve local content through `WebViewAssetLoader`.\n- External links open in the browser, not inside the app WebView.\n\n### 10. Dependencies are known and scanned (MASVS-CODE-3, M2)\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: dynamic version, unreproducible and unscannable\nimplementation(\"com.example:sdk:+\")\n\n// GOOD\nimplementation(libs.example.sdk)\n```\n\n- Commit lock files (`Package.resolved`, `pubspec.lock`, `Podfile.lock`); scan them in CI (OSV-Scanner,\n  OWASP Dependency-Check, Dependabot or Renovate) and fail on Critical.\n- Generate a CycloneDX or SPDX SBOM per release and archive it with the build.\n- MASVS-CODE-1: no unjustified support for unpatched OS versions. MASVS-CODE-2: a forced-update path\n  exists (Play In-App Updates immediate flow, or a server-driven minimum version).\n\n### 11. Obfuscation and attestation raise cost; the server decides (MASVS-RESILIENCE-1 to 4, M7)\n\nR8, Swift symbol stripping and Flutter `--obfuscate` slow reverse engineering. None is a security boundary.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: local check is the only control, patched out in minutes\nif (RootChecker.isRooted()) finish()\n\n// GOOD: attestation token sent with the sensitive request; backend verifies and decides\nval token = integrityTokenProvider.request(\n    StandardIntegrityTokenRequest.builder().setRequestHash(requestHash).build()).await()\napi.transfer(request, integrityToken = token.token())\n```\n\n- Android: Play Integrity API, verdict decrypted and checked on the server. SafetyNet Attestation is gone.\n- iOS: App Attest (`DCAppAttestService`) with server-side attestation and assertion verification;\n  DeviceCheck for per-device bits.\n- Release builds: `isMinifyEnabled = true`, `android:debuggable` not set, no `get-task-allow` in distribution.\n\n### 12. Collect the minimum and treat device identifiers as personal data (MASVS-PRIVACY-1 to 4, M6)\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: telemetry carries direct identifiers it does not need\nTelemetry(mac = device.macAddress, serial = device.serial, email = user.email, rssi = rssi)\n\n// GOOD: only what the feature needs, pseudonymous ID rotated per installation\nTelemetry(installId = installId, firmware = device.firmwareVersion, rssi = rssi)\n```\n\n- MAC addresses, serials, IMEI, advertising IDs and IP addresses are personal data under GDPR when linkable\n  to a person; pseudonymised data is still personal data.\n- Request runtime permissions at point of use; prefer coarse location, `neverForLocation` for BLE scans\n  on Android 12+, and the photo picker over broad media access.\n- iOS: `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` declares collected data and required-reason APIs, consistent with the\n  App Store privacy labels. Android: the Play Data safety form matches what the code does.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Reporting checklist items without an attacker and a path. \"No root detection\" is not a finding by itself.\n- Auditing the debug build or a single file and calling the app secure.\n- Recommending pinning by default without a rotation, backup-pin and monitoring plan.\n- Treating R8, obfuscation, root or jailbreak detection as a fix for a server-side authorization flaw.\n- \"Encrypting\" with a key hardcoded next to the ciphertext, or moving a secret to native code and calling it hidden.\n- Suggesting `EncryptedSharedPreferences` for new code, or CRC as protection against an attacker.\n- Inventing MASVS control IDs, CVE numbers or legal deadlines. If unsure, say so.\n- Dumping real secrets found during the audit into the report. Show the location and a redacted prefix only.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Threat model and data classification written down before findings\n- [ ] Release manifest, `Info.plist`, entitlements, network config and build types reviewed\n- [ ] No privileged secrets in code, resources, `BuildConfig`, `Info.plist`, `--dart-define` or git history\n- [ ] Tokens and keys in Keystore / Keychain-backed storage; no new `security-crypto` usage\n- [ ] Keychain accessibility class and Android backup rules checked\n- [ ] No secrets or personal data in release logs; `FLAG_SECURE` or snapshot cover on sensitive screens\n- [ ] Cleartext disabled; no trust-all managers, hostname verifiers or `badCertificateCallback`\n- [ ] MQTT and other IoT channels use TLS; BLE commands authenticated, not only CRC-checked\n- [ ] Pinning, if present, has backup pins, expiry and a rotation plan\n- [ ] Biometric auth bound to a `CryptoObject` or Keychain access control\n- [ ] OAuth via system browser with PKCE; authorization enforced server-side\n- [ ] Every exported component, deep link and App / Universal Link validated\n- [ ] WebView bridges origin-scoped; file access off; untrusted URLs not loaded\n- [ ] Dependencies pinned and scanned in CI, SBOM per release, attestation verified server-side if used\n- [ ] Telemetry minimised; device identifiers handled as personal data\n- [ ] \"Not checked\" section lists everything outside static review\n\n## Output format\n\n~~~markdown\n## Security audit: <app / module / MR>\n\nScope: <files, modules, commit>. Platforms: <Android | iOS | Flutter>. Build reviewed: <release variant>.\nThreat model: <assets, attackers considered>.\n\n### Findings\n\n1. [Critical] MASVS-NETWORK-1 / M5 -- Trust-all TrustManager in production client\n   Evidence: app/src/main/java/com/example/net/HttpModule.kt:42\n   Impact: any network attacker can read and modify all API traffic, including login credentials.\n   Fix:\n   ```kotlin\n   OkHttpClient.Builder().build() // remove custom sslSocketFactory and hostnameVerifier\n   ```\n\n2. [High] MASVS-AUTH-2 / M3 -- Biometric unlock is a boolean gate\n   Evidence: ios/App/Vault/VaultUnlock.swift:88\n   Impact: ...\n   Fix: ...\n\n### Needs dynamic verification\n- <items that static review could not confirm, for example \"pinning behaviour on proxy interception\">\n\n### Summary\n\n| MASVS category | Critical | High | Medium | Low | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| STORAGE | 0 | 0 | 1 | 0 | |\n| CRYPTO | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | |\n| AUTH | 0 | 1 | 0 | 0 | |\n| NETWORK | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | |\n| PLATFORM | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | |\n| CODE | 0 | 0 | 0 | 1 | |\n| RESILIENCE | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | |\n| PRIVACY | 0 | 0 | 1 | 0 | |\n\n### Not checked\n- Backend authorization and API security\n- Runtime behaviour (proxy interception, Frida, rooted / jailbroken device testing)\n- Compiled binary (only source reviewed)\n- <modules, flavors or SDKs out of scope>\n\n### Compliance notes (if EU market or IoT)\n- <findings relevant to the CRA, EN 18031 or GDPR; map them with `mobile-eu-compliance`>\n~~~\n\nRules for the report:\n- One root cause is one finding; list extra locations under Evidence.\n- Every Critical and High has a concrete fix snippet or config change.\n- Redact any real secret found: show the file, line and first four characters only, and say \"rotate\".\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-eu-compliance` -- maps these findings to CRA, RED / EN 18031 and GDPR requirements\n- `mobile-code-review` -- general correctness, concurrency and lifecycle review\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- wiring dependency scanning, SBOM generation and signing into the pipeline\n- `mobile-kotlin`, `mobile-java`, `mobile-swift`, `mobile-flutter` -- platform idioms behind the fixes\n- `mobile-merge-request` -- describing security fixes and permission or entitlement changes in the MR\n- `mobile-ble` -- pairing, command authentication and firmware update on the device link\n- `mobile-release` -- privacy manifests, Data safety and store privacy declarations\n",
    "references/android.md":
        "# Android security reference\n\nLoad this for Kotlin, Java, `AndroidManifest.xml`, `res/xml/*` and Gradle files. Always audit the merged\nrelease manifest (`app/build/intermediates/merged_manifests/release/...` or the APK Analyzer view), since\nlibraries add components and permissions.\n\n## Quick scan\n\n| Pattern | Why | Usual severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| `buildConfigField(` with key, secret, password, token | Secret in the APK | Critical if privileged |\n| `usesCleartextTraffic=\"true\"`, `cleartextTrafficPermitted=\"true\"` | Cleartext HTTP | High, Critical for credentials |\n| `<certificates src=\"user\"` outside `debug-overrides` | Trusts user-installed CAs in release | High |\n| `X509TrustManager` with empty `checkServerTrusted` | Trust-all | Critical |\n| `hostnameVerifier { _, _ -> true }`, `ALLOW_ALL_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER` | Hostname check disabled | Critical |\n| `tcp://` broker URI, port `1883` | Cleartext MQTT | Critical |\n| `EncryptedSharedPreferences`, `EncryptedFile`, `MasterKey` | Deprecated library | Low (migrate), not a vulnerability by itself |\n| `getSharedPreferences(` / DataStore keys named token, password, pin | Plaintext secrets | Medium |\n| `MODE_WORLD_READABLE`, `MODE_WORLD_WRITEABLE` | Other apps read the file | High (throws on modern targets) |\n| `getExternalStorageDirectory`, `getExternalFilesDir` with sensitive data | Shared or removable storage | Medium |\n| `android:exported=\"true\"` | Entry point for other apps | Review each |\n| `android:allowBackup=\"true\"` without `dataExtractionRules` | Tokens restorable to another device | Medium |\n| `android:debuggable=\"true\"` in a release manifest | Debugger and data access | High |\n| `addJavascriptInterface(` | JS bridge | High if untrusted content can load |\n| `setAllowFileAccess(true)`, `allowUniversalAccessFromFileURLs` | File theft via WebView | High |\n| `PendingIntent.FLAG_MUTABLE`, implicit base intent | Hijackable PendingIntent | High |\n| `getParcelableExtra<Intent>` then `startActivity` | Intent redirection | High |\n| `Log.` / `println(` / `Timber.` with token, password, email, mac, serial | Leak via logcat or crash reports | Medium to High |\n| `Cipher.getInstance(\"AES\")`, `\"AES/ECB/...\"`, `\"DES\"` | Weak mode or default ECB | High |\n| `MessageDigest.getInstance(\"MD5\")` / `\"SHA-1\"` for security | Broken hash | Medium |\n| `java.util.Random` for tokens, nonces, keys | Predictable | High |\n| `isMinifyEnabled = false` in release | No shrinking or obfuscation | Low |\n\n## Storage and keys\n\n`androidx.security:security-crypto` is deprecated. Existing users need a one-time migration: read with the\nold API, write with the new scheme, delete the old file, keep the old dependency only until migration ships.\n\nDirect Android Keystore key (no extra library):\n\n```kotlin\nfun getOrCreateKey(alias: String): SecretKey {\n    val ks = KeyStore.getInstance(\"AndroidKeyStore\").apply { load(null) }\n    (ks.getKey(alias, null) as? SecretKey)?.let { return it }\n    val spec = KeyGenParameterSpec.Builder(\n        alias, KeyProperties.PURPOSE_ENCRYPT or KeyProperties.PURPOSE_DECRYPT,\n    )\n        .setBlockModes(KeyProperties.BLOCK_MODE_GCM)\n        .setEncryptionPaddings(KeyProperties.ENCRYPTION_PADDING_NONE)\n        .setKeySize(256)\n        .build()\n    return KeyGenerator.getInstance(KeyProperties.KEY_ALGORITHM_AES, \"AndroidKeyStore\")\n        .apply { init(spec) }\n        .generateKey()\n}\n\n// Encrypt: let the Keystore generate the IV, store iv + ciphertext together\nval cipher = Cipher.getInstance(\"AES/GCM/NoPadding\").apply { init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, key) }\nval blob = cipher.iv + cipher.doFinal(plaintext)\n```\n\nTink AEAD (keyset stored in its own preferences file, encrypted by an Android Keystore master key):\n\n```kotlin\nAeadConfig.register()\nval aead: Aead = AndroidKeysetManager.Builder()\n    .withSharedPref(context, \"auth_keyset\", \"auth_keyset_prefs\")\n    .withKeyTemplate(KeyTemplates.get(\"AES256_GCM\"))\n    .withMasterKeyUri(\"android-keystore://auth_master_key\")\n    .build()\n    .keysetHandle\n    .getPrimitive(RegistryConfiguration.get(), Aead::class.java)\n\n// Associated data binds the ciphertext to its slot, so it cannot be swapped into another key\nval ciphertext = aead.encrypt(token.encodeToByteArray(), \"refresh_token\".encodeToByteArray())\n```\n\n- Older Tink versions use `keysetHandle.getPrimitive(Aead::class.java)`; match the pinned Tink version.\n- Build the `Aead` once (it is thread-safe) and off the main thread; keyset loading touches disk and Keystore.\n- `setIsStrongBoxBacked(true)` throws `StrongBoxUnavailableException` on devices without StrongBox; fall back.\n- Keystore operations can be slow and can fail after OS updates or lock-screen changes; handle\n  `KeyPermanentlyInvalidatedException` by re-authenticating the user, not by crashing.\n- Room / SQLite with sensitive data: encrypt the fields that matter or use an encrypted database library\n  with a Keystore-wrapped key.\n\n## Backup\n\n```xml\n<!-- AndroidManifest.xml -->\n<application\n    android:allowBackup=\"true\"\n    android:dataExtractionRules=\"@xml/data_extraction_rules\"\n    android:fullBackupContent=\"@xml/backup_rules\" ... >\n```\n\n```xml\n<!-- res/xml/data_extraction_rules.xml (API 31+) -->\n<data-extraction-rules>\n    <cloud-backup>\n        <exclude domain=\"sharedpref\" path=\"auth_keyset_prefs.xml\" />\n        <exclude domain=\"file\" path=\"datastore/session.preferences_pb\" />\n    </cloud-backup>\n    <device-transfer>\n        <exclude domain=\"sharedpref\" path=\"auth_keyset_prefs.xml\" />\n        <exclude domain=\"file\" path=\"datastore/session.preferences_pb\" />\n    </device-transfer>\n</data-extraction-rules>\n```\n\n`fullBackupContent` covers API 30 and below. Restored Tink keysets whose Keystore master key did not\ntravel cannot be decrypted; exclude them rather than crash on restore.\n\n## Network\n\n```xml\n<!-- res/xml/network_security_config.xml -->\n<network-security-config>\n    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted=\"false\">\n        <trust-anchors>\n            <certificates src=\"system\" />\n        </trust-anchors>\n    </base-config>\n\n    <!-- Optional pinning, only with a rotation plan -->\n    <domain-config>\n        <domain includeSubdomains=\"true\">api.example.com</domain>\n        <pin-set expiration=\"2027-06-30\">\n            <pin digest=\"SHA-256\">CURRENT_SPKI_BASE64=</pin>\n            <pin digest=\"SHA-256\">BACKUP_SPKI_BASE64=</pin>\n        </pin-set>\n    </domain-config>\n\n    <!-- Debug builds only: allow a proxy CA for testing -->\n    <debug-overrides>\n        <trust-anchors>\n            <certificates src=\"user\" />\n        </trust-anchors>\n    </debug-overrides>\n</network-security-config>\n```\n\n- After the `pin-set` expiration date, pins are no longer enforced; the app keeps working on normal validation.\n- OkHttp `CertificatePinner` pins apply in addition to platform validation; same backup-pin rules apply.\n- MQTT (Paho, HiveMQ client): `ssl://` / TLS on 8883, hostname verification on, credentials per device or\n  per user, never a shared broker password compiled into the app. Prefer client certificates or short-lived\n  tokens issued by the backend.\n\n## IPC\n\n- Since Android 12 every component with an intent filter must declare `android:exported`. Audit each `true`.\n- Exported components that need to be reachable only by your own apps use a `signature` protection level\n  permission.\n- Read extras defensively: `IntentCompat.getParcelableExtra(intent, key, Foo::class.java)`, validate every field.\n- Never forward an `Intent` received from another app to `startActivity` or `sendBroadcast`.\n- Runtime receivers: `ContextCompat.registerReceiver(..., ContextCompat.RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED)` unless other\n  apps must send to it.\n- App Links: `<intent-filter android:autoVerify=\"true\">` with `https` scheme and a matching\n  `/.well-known/assetlinks.json` listing the release signing certificate fingerprint.\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: mutable, implicit PendingIntent\nPendingIntent.getActivity(ctx, 0, Intent(\"com.example.OPEN\"), PendingIntent.FLAG_MUTABLE)\n\n// GOOD\nPendingIntent.getActivity(\n    ctx, 0, Intent(ctx, DetailActivity::class.java), PendingIntent.FLAG_IMMUTABLE,\n)\n```\n\n## WebView\n\n- `javaScriptEnabled` only when needed. No `addJavascriptInterface` on a WebView that can reach\n  third-party or user-controlled URLs; every `@JavascriptInterface` method is callable by any frame.\n- Prefer `WebViewCompat.addWebMessageListener` with an explicit allowed-origin set.\n- `shouldOverrideUrlLoading` allowlists hosts and sends everything else to the browser.\n- Local content through `WebViewAssetLoader` over `https://appassets.androidplatform.net`, with file access off.\n- No `onReceivedSslError { handler.proceed() }`.\n\n## Biometrics\n\n```kotlin\nval spec = KeyGenParameterSpec.Builder(\"vault\", KeyProperties.PURPOSE_ENCRYPT or KeyProperties.PURPOSE_DECRYPT)\n    .setBlockModes(KeyProperties.BLOCK_MODE_GCM)\n    .setEncryptionPaddings(KeyProperties.ENCRYPTION_PADDING_NONE)\n    .setUserAuthenticationRequired(true)\n    .setUserAuthenticationParameters(0, KeyProperties.AUTH_BIOMETRIC_STRONG) // API 30+\n    .setInvalidatedByBiometricEnrollment(true)\n    .build()\n\nval promptInfo = BiometricPrompt.PromptInfo.Builder()\n    .setTitle(getString(R.string.unlock_title))\n    .setAllowedAuthenticators(BiometricManager.Authenticators.BIOMETRIC_STRONG)\n    .setNegativeButtonText(getString(R.string.cancel))\n    .build()\n\nbiometricPrompt.authenticate(promptInfo, BiometricPrompt.CryptoObject(cipher))\n```\n\n- Timeout `0` means every use of the key needs a fresh authentication.\n- Decrypt or sign inside `onAuthenticationSucceeded` with `result.cryptoObject`; the GATT or network call\n  that follows must not run on the assumption that a flag was set.\n\n## UI and side channels\n\n- `FLAG_SECURE` on windows showing credentials, OTPs, payment or health data. In Compose apps set it on the\n  Activity window or the dialog window.\n- `Activity.setRecentsScreenshotEnabled(false)` (API 33+) keeps the recents thumbnail blank.\n- Clipboard: mark copied secrets sensitive with `ClipDescription.EXTRA_IS_SENSITIVE` (API 33+).\n- Notifications with sensitive content use `VISIBILITY_PRIVATE` or a public version.\n\n## Release build\n\n```kotlin\nandroid {\n    buildTypes {\n        release {\n            isMinifyEnabled = true\n            isShrinkResources = true\n            proguardFiles(getDefaultProguardFile(\"proguard-android-optimize.txt\"), \"proguard-rules.pro\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```proguard\n# Strip debug and verbose logging from release\n-assumenosideeffects class android.util.Log {\n    public static int v(...);\n    public static int d(...);\n}\n```\n\n- Stripping log calls does not remove string building done before the call in all cases; do not log\n  secrets in the first place.\n- Play Integrity standard request: prepare a token provider once, request a token per sensitive action with\n  a `requestHash` of the action, send it to the backend, decrypt and evaluate the verdict there.\n- SBOM: the CycloneDX Gradle plugin (`org.cyclonedx.bom`) generates a CycloneDX SBOM from resolved\n  dependencies. Gradle dependency verification (`gradle/verification-metadata.xml`) and dependency locking\n  protect against tampered or drifting artifacts.\n",
    "references/flutter.md":
        "# Flutter security reference\n\nLoad this when the project has `pubspec.yaml`. A Flutter app is also an Android app and an iOS app: audit\n`android/` with [Android](android.md) and `ios/` with [iOS](ios.md) as well. Most platform findings\n(manifest, ATS, entitlements, backup, `FLAG_SECURE`) live there, not in Dart.\n\n## Quick scan\n\n| Pattern | Why | Usual severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| `--dart-define` / `String.fromEnvironment` holding secrets | Compiled into the binary | Critical if privileged |\n| `.env` files bundled as assets (`flutter_dotenv` and similar) | Plain text in the app bundle | Critical if privileged |\n| `SharedPreferences` / `Hive` box (unencrypted) with tokens | Plaintext storage | Medium |\n| `badCertificateCallback = (...) => true` | Trust-all | Critical |\n| `http://` base URLs, `Uri.http(` | Cleartext | High |\n| `MqttServerClient` with `secure` not set, port 1883 | Cleartext MQTT | Critical |\n| `print(` / `debugPrint(` / logger calls with token, email, mac, serial | Leak via device logs in release | Medium |\n| `JavaScriptMode.unrestricted` plus `addJavaScriptChannel` on untrusted URLs | Bridge exposed to any page | High |\n| `launchUrl(Uri.parse(param))` with deep link input | Open redirect, scheme abuse | Medium |\n| `local_auth` `authenticate()` result as the only gate | Hookable boolean | High for secrets |\n| Packages with `any` or wide version ranges, `pubspec.lock` not committed for the app | Unreproducible builds | Low to Medium |\n\n## Secrets and configuration\n\n```dart\n// BAD: anyone can extract this from libapp.so or App.framework\nconst brokerPassword = String.fromEnvironment('BROKER_PASSWORD');\n\n// GOOD: the backend issues a short-lived, per-user credential after login\nfinal creds = await authApi.mqttCredentials();\n```\n\n- `--dart-define` and `--dart-define-from-file` are build configuration, not secret storage.\n- `--obfuscate --split-debug-info=<dir>` renames Dart symbols in release builds. It does not hide string\n  literals and is not a security control. Keep the symbol files private for crash symbolication.\n\n## Secure storage\n\n```dart\nconst storage = FlutterSecureStorage(\n  iOptions: IOSOptions(accessibility: KeychainAccessibility.first_unlock_this_device),\n);\nawait storage.write(key: 'refresh_token', value: token);\n```\n\n- iOS backend is the Keychain; choose the accessibility class per [iOS](ios.md).\n- Android backend uses an Android Keystore-protected key. Check the pinned plugin version and its changelog:\n  older Android options were built on the deprecated `androidx.security:security-crypto` library, and\n  major versions have changed the Android storage format, which needs a tested migration path.\n- Exclude the plugin's Android preferences file from backup, or restored data will fail to decrypt.\n- Large or structured sensitive data: encrypt with a key kept in secure storage, not in Dart constants.\n\n## Network\n\n```dart\n// BAD\nfinal client = HttpClient()..badCertificateCallback = (cert, host, port) => true;\n\n// GOOD: default validation\nfinal client = HttpClient();\n```\n\nPinning with `dart:io` (only with a rotation plan):\n\n```dart\nSecurityContext pinnedContext(List<int> caPem) {\n  final context = SecurityContext(withTrustedRoots: false);\n  context.setTrustedCertificatesBytes(caPem); // pinned CA, ship current and backup\n  return context;\n}\n\nfinal client = HttpClient(context: pinnedContext(caBytes));\n```\n\n- Dio on mobile uses `IOHttpClientAdapter`; apply the same `HttpClient` configuration through its\n  `createHttpClient` callback rather than disabling checks.\n- `network_security_config.xml` and ATS govern platform networking (WebViews, native plugins). `dart:io`\n  `HttpClient` uses its own TLS stack and does not consult them, so Dart code must enforce `https` itself.\n- MQTT (`mqtt_client`): set `secure = true`, port 8883, and keep certificate validation on.\n\n```dart\n// BAD\nfinal client = MqttServerClient.withPort('broker.example.com', clientId, 1883);\n\n// GOOD\nfinal client = MqttServerClient.withPort('broker.example.com', clientId, 8883)\n  ..secure = true;\n```\n\n## Local authentication\n\n- `local_auth` returns a boolean. For secrets, store them behind platform key protection instead: Keychain\n  access control on iOS and a `CryptoObject`-bound Keystore key on Android, exposed through a platform\n  channel or a plugin that implements the crypto binding.\n- Do not treat `canCheckBiometrics` or `isDeviceSupported` as proof that the user authenticated.\n\n## WebView (`webview_flutter`)\n\n```dart\ncontroller\n  ..setJavaScriptMode(JavaScriptMode.unrestricted)\n  ..setNavigationDelegate(NavigationDelegate(\n    onNavigationRequest: (request) {\n      final uri = Uri.parse(request.url);\n      return uri.scheme == 'https' && trustedHosts.contains(uri.host)\n          ? NavigationDecision.navigate\n          : NavigationDecision.prevent;\n    },\n  ))\n  ..addJavaScriptChannel('Bridge', onMessageReceived: _onBridgeMessage)\n  ..loadRequest(Uri.parse('https://app.example.com/help'));\n```\n\n- JavaScript channels are reachable by every page the controller loads; the navigation allowlist is the\n  control that makes the bridge safe.\n- Validate every message received on a channel as untrusted input.\n\n## Deep links\n\n- Routes from `go_router` or another router receive path and query parameters from other apps. Validate\n  them before triggering actions (pairing a device, opening a URL, changing settings).\n- Use verified App Links and Universal Links for anything carrying codes or tokens; configure them in the\n  native projects.\n\n## Logging\n\n- `print` and `debugPrint` reach logcat and the device console in release builds.\n- Route logs through one logger that drops debug output and redacts identifiers when `kReleaseMode` is true.\n\n```dart\n// BAD\ndebugPrint('connected to \${device.mac} with token \$token');\n\n// GOOD\nappLog.info('connected to device ref=\${device.pseudonymousId}'); // project logger, redacts in release\n```\n\n## Supply chain\n\n- Commit `pubspec.lock` for apps. Review new packages for maintenance status, publisher and native code.\n- Scan `pubspec.lock` together with the Android and iOS lock files; plugins pull native dependencies\n  (Gradle artifacts, CocoaPods or SwiftPM packages) that also belong in the SBOM.\n",
    "references/ios.md":
        "# iOS security reference\n\nLoad this for Swift, Objective-C, `Info.plist`, `.entitlements`, `.xcconfig` and `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`.\nCall out every `Info.plist` and entitlement change explicitly in the report; they affect App Review,\nsigning and provisioning.\n\n## Quick scan\n\n| Pattern | Why | Usual severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| Keys like `API_SECRET`, `PASSWORD`, `TOKEN` in `Info.plist` or `.xcconfig` | Secret in the IPA | Critical if privileged |\n| `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads` = YES | ATS disabled globally | High, needs App Review justification |\n| `NSExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads`, `NSExceptionMinimumTLSVersion` lowered | Cleartext or weak TLS for a domain | High |\n| `.useCredential` with `serverTrust` and no `SecTrustEvaluateWithError` | Trust-all | Critical |\n| `UserDefaults` / `@AppStorage` with token, password, PIN | Plaintext plist | Medium |\n| `kSecAttrAccessibleAlways` / `kSecAttrAccessibleAlwaysThisDeviceOnly` | Deprecated, readable while locked | Medium |\n| `evaluatePolicy(` result used as the only gate | Hookable boolean | High for secrets or payments |\n| `print(`, `NSLog(`, `privacy: .public` with personal data | Leak via device logs | Medium |\n| `WKScriptMessageHandler` without origin check | Any frame calls native code | High |\n| `loadFileURL(_:allowingReadAccessTo:)` with a broad directory | Web content reads app files | Medium |\n| `open(url)` or routing from `onOpenURL` without validation | Deep link abuse | Medium to High |\n| `CC_MD5`, `Insecure.MD5`, `Insecure.SHA1` for security | Broken hash | Medium |\n| `get-task-allow` = true in a distribution entitlement | Debuggable release | High |\n| CocoaMQTT / other MQTT clients with SSL disabled or port 1883 | Cleartext MQTT | Critical |\n\n## Keychain accessibility classes\n\n| Class | Readable when | Migrates to new device | Use for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `kSecAttrAccessibleWhenPasscodeSetThisDeviceOnly` | Unlocked, only while a passcode is set | No | Highest-value secrets, biometric-bound items |\n| `kSecAttrAccessibleWhenUnlockedThisDeviceOnly` | Unlocked | No | Secrets used only in the foreground |\n| `kSecAttrAccessibleWhenUnlocked` | Unlocked | Yes | Default; foreground secrets that may migrate |\n| `kSecAttrAccessibleAfterFirstUnlockThisDeviceOnly` | After first unlock until reboot | No | Tokens needed by background refresh, BLE restoration, push handling |\n| `kSecAttrAccessibleAfterFirstUnlock` | After first unlock until reboot | Yes | Background secrets that may migrate |\n\n- `ThisDeviceOnly` items are not restored onto a different device from a backup.\n- Keychain items can survive app deletion. Clear stale credentials on first launch after install\n  (detect with a flag in `UserDefaults`, which is removed on uninstall).\n- Shared access across your apps uses a Keychain access group entitlement; keep it as narrow as possible.\n\n## Biometric-bound Keychain item\n\n```swift\nfunc storeVaultKey(_ secret: Data) throws {\n    var error: Unmanaged<CFError>?\n    guard let access = SecAccessControlCreateWithFlags(\n        nil, kSecAttrAccessibleWhenPasscodeSetThisDeviceOnly, .biometryCurrentSet, &error\n    ) else { throw error!.takeRetainedValue() as Error }\n\n    let query: [String: Any] = [\n        kSecClass as String: kSecClassGenericPassword,\n        kSecAttrService as String: \"com.example.vault\",\n        kSecAttrAccount as String: \"vaultKey\",\n        kSecAttrAccessControl as String: access,\n        kSecValueData as String: secret,\n    ]\n    let status = SecItemAdd(query as CFDictionary, nil)\n    guard status == errSecSuccess else { throw KeychainError(status: status) }\n}\n\n// Blocks while the system prompt is shown: never call on the main thread.\nfunc readVaultKey() throws -> Data {\n    let context = LAContext()\n    context.localizedReason = String(localized: \"Unlock your vault\")\n    let query: [String: Any] = [\n        kSecClass as String: kSecClassGenericPassword,\n        kSecAttrService as String: \"com.example.vault\",\n        kSecAttrAccount as String: \"vaultKey\",\n        kSecReturnData as String: true,\n        kSecUseAuthenticationContext as String: context,\n    ]\n    var result: CFTypeRef?\n    let status = SecItemCopyMatching(query as CFDictionary, &result)\n    guard status == errSecSuccess, let data = result as? Data else { throw KeychainError(status: status) }\n    return data\n}\n```\n\n- `.biometryCurrentSet` invalidates the item when enrolled biometrics change; `.biometryAny` does not.\n- `.userPresence` allows passcode fallback; choose deliberately and document it.\n- For signing use cases, create a Secure Enclave key (`kSecAttrTokenIDSecureEnclave`) with the same access\n  control and have the server verify signatures.\n- `NSFaceIDUsageDescription` is required in `Info.plist` when Face ID is used.\n\n## Files and data protection\n\n```swift\ntry data.write(to: url, options: [.atomic, .completeFileProtection])\n```\n\n- Default file protection is `completeUntilFirstUserAuthentication`. Use `.complete` for sensitive files\n  that are not needed in the background.\n- Exclude caches and regenerable data from backup with `isExcludedFromBackup` on `URLResourceValues`.\n- Core Data / SwiftData stores inherit file protection; set it on the store file when it holds sensitive data.\n\n## Network\n\n```xml\n<!-- Info.plist: ATS on by default; pin only domains you control -->\n<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>\n<dict>\n    <key>NSPinnedDomains</key>\n    <dict>\n        <key>api.example.com</key>\n        <dict>\n            <key>NSIncludesSubdomains</key>\n            <true/>\n            <key>NSPinnedCAIdentities</key>\n            <array>\n                <dict>\n                    <key>SPKI-SHA256-BASE64</key>\n                    <string>CURRENT_CA_SPKI_BASE64=</string>\n                </dict>\n                <dict>\n                    <key>SPKI-SHA256-BASE64</key>\n                    <string>BACKUP_CA_SPKI_BASE64=</string>\n                </dict>\n            </array>\n        </dict>\n    </dict>\n</dict>\n```\n\n- Custom trust evaluation must call `SecTrustEvaluateWithError` and only then compare pins.\n- Apple platforms require Certificate Transparency for publicly trusted TLS certificates, so standard\n  validation already rejects many mis-issued certificates.\n- ATS exceptions in `Info.plist` need a justification at App Review; local-network IoT devices are a valid\n  case only for the specific local domain or `NSAllowsLocalNetworking`, never a global exception.\n- Local network access to IoT devices needs `NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription`.\n\n## WebView\n\n```swift\nlet config = WKWebViewConfiguration()\nconfig.defaultWebpagePreferences.allowsContentJavaScript = needsJavaScript\nconfig.userContentController.add(handler, name: \"bridge\")\n\nfunc webView(_ webView: WKWebView, decidePolicyFor action: WKNavigationAction) async -> WKNavigationActionPolicy {\n    guard let host = action.request.url?.host, trustedHosts.contains(host) else {\n        if let url = action.request.url { _ = await UIApplication.shared.open(url) }\n        return .cancel\n    }\n    return .allow\n}\n```\n\n- Every `WKScriptMessage` handler checks `frameInfo.isMainFrame` and `frameInfo.securityOrigin` before acting.\n- OAuth and SSO use `ASWebAuthenticationSession`, not a `WKWebView` the app can read.\n\n## Logging\n\n- `os.Logger` redacts dynamic interpolations by default in release logs. Never mark tokens, emails, device\n  identifiers or locations `privacy: .public`.\n- `print` and `NSLog` output reaches the device console; remove or guard them.\n- Crash reporters and analytics SDKs: check breadcrumbs and custom keys for personal data.\n\n## UI\n\n- Hide sensitive content when the scene is inactive, so the app switcher snapshot is clean:\n\n```swift\n.overlay { if scenePhase != .active { PrivacyCover() } }\n```\n\n- Secure text fields (`SecureField`) for passwords and PINs; set `textContentType` so autofill works and\n  the keyboard does not learn secrets.\n- `UIPasteboard`: use `setItems(_:options:)` with `.localOnly` and `.expirationDate` for copied secrets.\n\n## Integrity and attestation\n\n- App Attest: `DCAppAttestService.shared.isSupported`, `generateKey()`, `attestKey(_:clientDataHash:)` once,\n  then `generateAssertion(_:clientDataHash:)` per sensitive request. The server verifies the attestation,\n  stores the public key and checks assertion counters.\n- Requires the App Attest entitlement (`com.apple.developer.devicecheck.appattest-environment`); flag it as\n  an entitlement and provisioning change.\n- Jailbreak detection is advisory only; never rely on it as the sole control.\n\n## Privacy\n\n- `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` lists collected data types and required-reason API usage, and third-party SDKs ship\n  their own manifests. Check consistency with the App Store privacy label.\n- Bluetooth needs `NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription`; ask only when the user starts a BLE feature.\n- `identifierForVendor` is still an identifier; treat it as personal data in telemetry.\n",
    "references/masvs-map.md":
        "# MASVS v2 controls and Mobile Top 10 map\n\nLoad this before assigning control IDs. Use only the IDs in this table. MASVS v2 removed the old v1\nlevels (L1, L2, R); use MAS testing profiles from the MASTG if the user asks for levels.\n\n## MASVS v2 controls\n\n| Control | Statement (short) | Typical findings |\n|---|---|---|\n| MASVS-STORAGE-1 | The app securely stores sensitive data | Tokens in SharedPreferences, UserDefaults, plain files, SQLite without protection |\n| MASVS-STORAGE-2 | The app prevents leakage of sensitive data | Secrets in logs, backups, screenshots, app switcher snapshot, clipboard, keyboard cache, crash reports |\n| MASVS-CRYPTO-1 | The app employs current strong cryptography and uses it according to industry best practices | ECB, static IV, MD5 or SHA-1 for security, `java.util.Random` for secrets, custom crypto |\n| MASVS-CRYPTO-2 | The app performs key management according to industry best practices | Hardcoded keys, keys derived from device IDs, keys outside Keystore / Keychain |\n| MASVS-AUTH-1 | The app uses secure authentication and authorization protocols and follows the relevant best practices | Client-side role checks, long-lived tokens, OAuth in embedded WebView, no PKCE, shared credentials in app |\n| MASVS-AUTH-2 | The app performs local authentication securely according to the platform best practices | Boolean-only biometric gate, weak fallback, key not invalidated on enrollment change |\n| MASVS-AUTH-3 | The app secures sensitive operations with additional authentication | Payments, device unlock commands or credential changes without step-up auth |\n| MASVS-NETWORK-1 | The app secures all network traffic according to the current best practices | Cleartext, trust-all managers, disabled hostname checks, ATS exceptions, cleartext MQTT |\n| MASVS-NETWORK-2 | The app performs identity pinning for all remote endpoints under the developer's control | Pinning missing where the threat model needs it, or pinning without backup pins |\n| MASVS-PLATFORM-1 | The app uses IPC mechanisms securely | Exported components, intent redirection, mutable PendingIntents, unvalidated deep links, providers |\n| MASVS-PLATFORM-2 | The app uses WebViews securely | JS bridges on untrusted content, file access enabled, loading arbitrary URLs |\n| MASVS-PLATFORM-3 | The app uses the user interface securely | No screenshot protection on sensitive screens, overlay or tapjacking, sensitive data in notifications |\n| MASVS-CODE-1 | The app requires an up-to-date platform version | Very old `minSdk` or deployment target without justification |\n| MASVS-CODE-2 | The app has a mechanism for enforcing app updates | No way to block a known-vulnerable version |\n| MASVS-CODE-3 | The app only uses software components without known vulnerabilities | Vulnerable or unmaintained dependencies, no scanning, dynamic versions |\n| MASVS-CODE-4 | The app validates and sanitizes all untrusted inputs | Deep link params, IPC extras, server or BLE payloads used without validation, SQL or path injection |\n| MASVS-RESILIENCE-1 | The app validates the integrity of the platform | No attestation where the threat model needs it |\n| MASVS-RESILIENCE-2 | The app implements anti-tampering mechanisms | No signature or integrity verification for high-value apps |\n| MASVS-RESILIENCE-3 | The app implements anti-static analysis mechanisms | No obfuscation in release, debug symbols shipped |\n| MASVS-RESILIENCE-4 | The app implements anti-dynamic analysis techniques | Debuggable release build, no debugger or instrumentation awareness where required |\n| MASVS-PRIVACY-1 | The app minimizes access to sensitive data and resources | Over-broad permissions, collecting identifiers the feature does not need |\n| MASVS-PRIVACY-2 | The app prevents identification of the user | Stable cross-app identifiers, linkable telemetry, fingerprinting |\n| MASVS-PRIVACY-3 | The app is transparent about data collection and usage | Privacy manifest or Data safety form inconsistent with code, undisclosed SDK collection |\n| MASVS-PRIVACY-4 | The app offers user control over their data | No account or data deletion path, no opt-out for optional telemetry |\n\nRESILIENCE controls apply only when the threat model includes the device owner or reverse engineers\n(banking, DRM, anti-cheat, high-value IoT control). Do not report them as gaps for every app.\n\n## OWASP Mobile Top 10 (2024)\n\n| ID | Risk | Main MASVS controls |\n|---|---|---|\n| M1 | Improper Credential Usage | MASVS-AUTH-1, MASVS-CRYPTO-2, MASVS-STORAGE-1 |\n| M2 | Inadequate Supply Chain Security | MASVS-CODE-3 |\n| M3 | Insecure Authentication/Authorization | MASVS-AUTH-1, MASVS-AUTH-2, MASVS-AUTH-3 |\n| M4 | Insufficient Input/Output Validation | MASVS-CODE-4, MASVS-PLATFORM-1, MASVS-PLATFORM-2 |\n| M5 | Insecure Communication | MASVS-NETWORK-1, MASVS-NETWORK-2 |\n| M6 | Inadequate Privacy Controls | MASVS-PRIVACY-1 to MASVS-PRIVACY-4, MASVS-STORAGE-2 |\n| M7 | Insufficient Binary Protections | MASVS-RESILIENCE-1 to MASVS-RESILIENCE-4 |\n| M8 | Security Misconfiguration | MASVS-PLATFORM-1, MASVS-NETWORK-1, MASVS-CODE-1 |\n| M9 | Insecure Data Storage | MASVS-STORAGE-1, MASVS-STORAGE-2 |\n| M10 | Insufficient Cryptography | MASVS-CRYPTO-1, MASVS-CRYPTO-2 |\n\nThe mapping is a guide for the report, not an official one-to-one table. Pick the single control that\nbest describes the root cause.\n",
  },
  "mobile-swift": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-swift\ndescription: \"Writes idiomatic modern Swift for iOS: Swift 6 strict concurrency (async/await, actors, @MainActor, Sendable, cancellation), value types, some vs any, typed throws, SwiftUI with @Observable, NavigationStack and stable identity, SwiftData, SPM modules and Swift Testing. Use when writing or refactoring Swift views, view models, services or packages, or fixing concurrency warnings and Swift 6 migration errors.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Swift for iOS\n\nWrite Swift the way a senior iOS engineer would in Swift 6: data-race safe by construction, value types\nfirst, no crashes on unexpected input, SwiftUI views that stay small and cheap, and code split into\npackages with tests next to it.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Writing new Swift, SwiftUI or SwiftData code, or generating a feature from a ticket.\n- Refactoring or migrating older code: completion handlers, `ObservableObject`, `NavigationView`,\n  Core Data, XCTest unit tests, Swift 5 language mode.\n- Fixing compiler errors such as \"sending risks causing data races\", \"non-Sendable type\",\n  \"main actor-isolated property can not be referenced\".\n- Adding a local Swift package, a test target, a lint config, or a privacy manifest.\n\nNot for: reviewing someone else's diff (use `mobile-code-review`), a security audit (use\n`mobile-security`), or choosing an app architecture (use `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Read the project settings before writing code.\n   - Swift language mode, strict concurrency level, default actor isolation, minimum iOS version.\n   - `Package.swift` files, existing lint and format configs, test framework in use.\n2. Match the deployment target. `@Observable` and SwiftData need iOS 17, `Mutex` needs iOS 18.\n   Gate newer APIs with `if #available` or ask before raising the target.\n3. Load the reference for the area being touched:\n   [Concurrency](references/concurrency.md), [SwiftUI](references/swiftui.md),\n   [SwiftData](references/swiftdata.md), [Testing](references/testing.md),\n   [Packages and tooling](references/packages.md).\n4. Model data first: value types, explicit `Sendable` where it crosses isolation, typed errors where the\n   set is closed.\n5. Decide isolation per type: `@MainActor` for UI-facing state, an `actor` for shared mutable state,\n   `nonisolated` value types for everything else.\n6. Write the code, then the tests in the same change. Swift Testing for unit tests, XCTest only for UI\n   and performance tests.\n7. Build with zero concurrency warnings, run the tests, run SwiftLint and the formatter.\n8. List every `Info.plist` key, entitlement, background mode and privacy manifest entry the change adds.\n   These have signing and App Review consequences and must be called out explicitly.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Structs by default, `final class` only when identity matters\n\n```swift\n// BAD: reference semantics for plain data, shared mutation, not Sendable\nclass Reading { var value: Double; var date: Date }\n\n// GOOD\nstruct Reading: Sendable, Equatable { let value: Double; let date: Date }\n\n// GOOD: identity and lifetime matter (connection, cache, model observed by UI)\n@MainActor @Observable\nfinal class DeviceSession { var state: ConnectionState = .idle }\n```\n\n- Classes are `final` unless subclassing is designed for.\n- `let` over `var`. Mutation happens through methods with clear names, not public setters.\n\n### 2. No force unwraps, force casts or `try!` in app code\n\n```swift\n// BAD\nlet url = URL(string: base + path)!\nlet cell = tableView.dequeueReusableCell(withIdentifier: \"c\") as! DeviceCell\n\n// GOOD\nguard let url = URL(string: base + path) else { throw APIError.invalidURL(path) }\nlet name = device.name ?? String(localized: \"Unknown device\")\n```\n\n- In tests use `try #require(value)` instead of `!`, so a nil fails the test instead of crashing the run.\n- A static literal URL may use a `guard` with `preconditionFailure(\"...\")` and a message, never a bare `!`.\n\n### 3. `some` when one concrete type is fixed, `any` only when it must vary at runtime\n\n```swift\n// BAD: existential box and dynamic dispatch for no reason\nfunc render(_ items: any Collection<Reading>) -> any View\n\n// GOOD: generic, specialised by the compiler\nfunc render(_ items: some Collection<Reading>) -> some View\n\n// GOOD: heterogeneous storage really needs an existential\nvar sinks: [any TelemetrySink]\n```\n\n- Write `any` explicitly for every existential even where the compiler does not yet require it.\n- Primary associated types (`some Collection<Reading>`, `any AsyncSequence<Int, Never>`) instead of\n  type-erasing wrappers.\n\n### 4. Structured concurrency before unstructured `Task {}`\n\n```swift\n// BAD: unstructured tasks, no cancellation propagation, errors lost\nTask { profile = try await api.profile() }\nTask { devices = try await api.devices() }\n\n// GOOD: both cancelled with the parent, errors propagate\nasync let profile = api.profile()\nasync let devices = api.devices()\nstate = .loaded(try await profile, try await devices)\n```\n\n- Dynamic fan-out uses `withThrowingTaskGroup`; bound the width for large inputs.\n- `Task {}` only at an entry point from synchronous code (button action, delegate callback), and its\n  handle is stored and cancelled when the owner goes away.\n- `Task.detached` needs a written reason.\n\n### 5. UI-facing state is `@MainActor`, data crossing isolation is `Sendable`\n\n```swift\n// BAD: silences the checker, data race still possible\nfinal class TokenStore: @unchecked Sendable { var token: String? }\n\n// GOOD: an actor owns the mutable state\nactor TokenStore {\n    private var token: String?\n    func update(_ new: String?) { token = new }\n}\n```\n\n- View models: `@MainActor @Observable final class`.\n- `@unchecked Sendable` and `nonisolated(unsafe)` only with a lock guarding every field and a comment\n  explaining why an actor does not fit.\n- No `DispatchQueue.main.async` in `async` code; isolate the function or type to `@MainActor` instead.\n\n### 6. Actor state is re-checked after every `await`\n\n```swift\n// Inside an actor or a @MainActor class.\n// BAD: reentrancy, two callers both pass the check and both connect\nfunc connect() async throws {\n    guard state == .idle else { return }\n    try await transport.open()\n    state = .connected\n}\n\n// GOOD: claim the state before suspending\nfunc connect() async throws {\n    guard state == .idle else { return }\n    state = .connecting\n    do { try await transport.open(); state = .connected }\n    catch { state = .idle; throw error }\n}\n```\n\n### 7. Cancellation is checked in loops and forwarded to callback APIs\n\n```swift\n// BAD: keeps uploading after the user left the screen\nfor chunk in chunks { try await upload(chunk) }\n\n// GOOD\nfor chunk in chunks {\n    try Task.checkCancellation()\n    try await upload(chunk)\n}\n```\n\n- Wrap callback APIs that support cancelling in `withTaskCancellationHandler`. Details in\n  [Concurrency](references/concurrency.md).\n- Do not catch `CancellationError` and turn it into a user-facing error state.\n\n### 8. Callbacks are bridged once, at the edge\n\n```swift\n// BAD: continuation resumed for every discovery, traps on the second one\nfunc scan() async -> Peripheral {\n    await withCheckedContinuation { c in\n        scanner.onDiscover = { c.resume(returning: \$0) }\n    }\n}\n\n// GOOD: repeated events are a stream, cleaned up when the consumer stops\n// scanner: a Sendable wrapper around the platform delegate API\nfunc discoveries() -> AsyncStream<Peripheral> {\n    let (stream, continuation) = AsyncStream.makeStream(of: Peripheral.self)\n    scanner.onDiscover = { continuation.yield(\$0) }\n    continuation.onTermination = { [scanner] _ in scanner.stop() }\n    scanner.start()\n    return stream\n}\n```\n\n- One-shot callback: `withCheckedThrowingContinuation`, resumed exactly once on every path.\n- Repeated events: `AsyncStream` / `AsyncThrowingStream` with `onTermination`.\n\n### 9. Typed throws only for closed, local error domains\n\n```swift\n// BAD: public API promises a single error type forever, callers gain nothing\npublic func sync() async throws(SyncError)\n\n// GOOD: closed set, callers switch exhaustively\nfunc decode(_ frame: Data) throws(FrameError) -> Packet\nenum FrameError: Error { case tooShort(Int), badChecksum, unknownOpcode(UInt8) }\n```\n\n- Use `throws(E)` for parsers, validation and module-internal code. Use untyped `throws` at module and\n  network boundaries where new failure kinds will appear.\n- Typed throws does not replace `Result` in stored properties.\n\n### 10. SwiftUI: the view that creates a model owns it in `@State`\n\n```swift\n// BAD: legacy pattern, model recreated on every parent redraw\n@ObservedObject var model = DeviceListModel()\n\n// GOOD\nstruct DeviceListScreen: View {\n    @State private var model = DeviceListModel()\n    var body: some View {\n        DeviceList(model: model)\n            .task { await model.load() }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Passed-in `@Observable` models are plain `let`/`var` properties; use `@Bindable` only to get bindings.\n- `@Binding` for a value the parent owns. `@Environment` for app-wide dependencies.\n- `.task(id: query)` for work that restarts when an input changes; it cancels the previous run.\n\n### 11. SwiftUI identity is stable and views stay small\n\n```swift\n// BAD: index identity, AnyView erasure, heavy body\nForEach(items.indices, id: \\.self) { i in AnyView(row(items[i])) }\n\n// GOOD\nForEach(items) { item in DeviceRow(device: item) }\n```\n\n- Items are `Identifiable` with an id from the data, never `UUID()` created in `body`.\n- Conditional content uses `@ViewBuilder` or `Group`, not `AnyView`.\n- Extract subviews when `body` stops fitting on a screen; no formatters, sorting or I/O inside `body`.\n\n### 12. Capture lists break cycles, and only where a cycle exists\n\n```swift\n// BAD: self -> task -> self, the loop keeps the object alive forever\nmonitor = Task { for await r in readings { self.append(r) } }\n\n// GOOD\nmonitor = Task { [weak self] in\n    for await r in readings {\n        guard let self else { return }\n        append(r)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Escaping closures stored by `self` (timers, observers, stored tasks) capture `[weak self]`.\n- `unowned` only when the referenced object provably outlives the closure.\n- SwiftUI views are structs: no `[weak self]` in `.task` or button actions.\n- Delegates are `weak var delegate: (any SomeDelegate)?` with an `AnyObject`-constrained protocol.\n\n### 13. SwiftData contexts stay on their actor\n\n```swift\n// BAD: ModelContext and model objects are not Sendable\nTask.detached { context.insert(entity) }\n\n// GOOD: background work in a model actor, identifiers cross the boundary\n@ModelActor\nactor ReadingImporter {\n    func importAll(_ rows: [ReadingDTO]) throws {\n        for row in rows { modelContext.insert(ReadingEntity(row)) }\n        try modelContext.save()\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- UI reads with `@Query`; writes go through `@Environment(\\.modelContext)` or a model actor.\n- Pass `PersistentIdentifier` between actors, not model instances. See [SwiftData](references/swiftdata.md).\n\n### 14. Features are local packages with explicit, minimal APIs\n\n```swift\n// BAD: public only so a sibling target can call it, now visible to the whole app\npublic func makeDecoder() -> JSONDecoder\n\n// GOOD: visible to other targets of the same Swift package, hidden from everyone else\npackage func makeDecoder() -> JSONDecoder\n```\n\n- One local package (or one target) per feature plus shared core modules; features depend on core,\n  never on each other.\n- Default to `internal`. `package` spans targets of one Swift package only; `public` is for what\n  other packages and the app target import.\n- Every dependency is declared in `Package.swift`; nothing relies on a transitive import.\n\n### 15. Swift Testing for unit tests, XCTest where it is still required\n\n```swift\n// BAD: a nil or a thrown error crashes the whole test run\nfunc testParse() { XCTAssertEqual(try! parse(frame).battery!, 87) }\n\n// GOOD: failures are reported, the run continues\n@Test func parsesBatteryLevel() throws {\n    let packet = try parse(frame)\n    let battery = try #require(packet.battery)\n    #expect(battery == 87)\n}\n```\n\n- XCTest stays for UI tests (`XCUIApplication`) and performance tests (`measure`).\n- No `sleep` in tests; inject a clock. Details in [Testing](references/testing.md).\n\n## Platform and project changes\n\nCall these out in the change summary every time, with the exact key or entitlement name.\n\n- `Info.plist` usage strings: `NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription`, `NSCameraUsageDescription`,\n  `NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription` and others. For Bluetooth, camera and microphone a missing\n  string terminates the app on first access.\n- `UIBackgroundModes` entries such as `bluetooth-central` or `location`. App Review asks for justification.\n- Entitlements (push, App Groups, Keychain sharing, associated domains) change the provisioning profile.\n- `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`: required-reason APIs (for example `UserDefaults`, file timestamps, system boot\n  time, disk space) need a declared reason; collected data types and tracking domains must match reality.\n  See [Packages and tooling](references/packages.md).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Sprinkling `@MainActor`, `@unchecked Sendable` or `nonisolated(unsafe)` until the compiler is quiet.\n- `Task { }` inside `onAppear` instead of `.task`.\n- `DispatchSemaphore` or `DispatchGroup.wait()` to make async code synchronous.\n- `ObservableObject` and `@Published` in new code on iOS 17+.\n- Giant views with `AnyView` and computed `some View` properties instead of real subviews.\n- `UUID()` as the `id` of items created during rendering.\n- Singletons (`static let shared`) reached from deep inside views and models instead of injection.\n- `try?` that silently drops an error the user should see.\n- One giant app target with every file `public` \"just in case\".\n- Adding a third-party SDK without checking it ships its own privacy manifest and signature.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Language mode, strict concurrency and deployment target checked before writing\n- [ ] Value types by default; classes are `final`\n- [ ] No `!`, `as!`, `try!` in app code; tests use `#require`\n- [ ] UI-facing types `@MainActor`; shared mutable state in actors; no unjustified `@unchecked Sendable`\n- [ ] Actor state re-validated after each `await`\n- [ ] Long loops check cancellation; continuations resume exactly once; streams have `onTermination`\n- [ ] Escaping closures stored by `self` use `[weak self]`; delegates are `weak`\n- [ ] SwiftUI models owned with `@State`; async work in `.task` / `.task(id:)`; stable `ForEach` ids\n- [ ] No `AnyView` or heavy work in `body`\n- [ ] SwiftData contexts not shared across actors; identifiers passed instead of models\n- [ ] New code in the right package with the narrowest access level\n- [ ] Swift Testing unit tests added; XCTest only for UI and performance\n- [ ] Zero warnings, tests pass, SwiftLint and formatter clean\n- [ ] `Info.plist`, entitlements, background modes and privacy manifest changes listed explicitly\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-code-review` -- review checklist that applies these rules to a diff\n- `mobile-security` -- Keychain, ATS, data protection and MASVS checks for the code written here\n- `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi` -- where view models, state and intents live\n- `mobile-design-patterns` -- repository, coordinator and dependency injection patterns in Swift\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- Xcode Cloud, signing and test plans in the pipeline\n- `mobile-kotlin`, `mobile-flutter` -- the equivalent rules for Android and Flutter\n- `mobile-kmp` -- consuming a Kotlin Multiplatform framework from Swift\n- `mobile-ble` -- CoreBluetooth rules\n- `mobile-testing` -- cross-platform test strategy and snapshot tests\n- `mobile-performance` -- Instruments, launch time and MetricKit\n",
    "references/concurrency.md":
        "# Swift concurrency reference\n\nLoad this when writing `async` code, actors, `Sendable` types, or bridging callback and delegate APIs.\nAssumes Swift 6 language mode with complete data-race checking.\n\n## Isolation map\n\n| Kind of code | Isolation | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| View models, routers, anything SwiftUI reads | `@MainActor final class` | UI reads and writes happen on main |\n| Shared mutable state (caches, connection registry, token store) | `actor` | Serialised access without locks |\n| Plain data, DTOs, parsers, pure functions | `nonisolated` value types | Usable from any isolation |\n| Legacy class that must be shared and cannot be an actor | `final class` + `Mutex` (iOS 18+) | Synchronous access with a real lock |\n\nCheck the project's default actor isolation before adding annotations. Since Swift 6.2 a module can\ndefault to `@MainActor` (Xcode build setting \"Default Actor Isolation\", or\n`.defaultIsolation(MainActor.self)` in `Package.swift`). In such a module, mark parsers, DTOs and\nbackground services `nonisolated` explicitly.\n\n## async let vs task groups\n\n- `async let`: a fixed, small number of child tasks known at compile time.\n- `withThrowingTaskGroup`: a dynamic number of children, results collected in completion order.\n- `withDiscardingTaskGroup` / `withThrowingDiscardingTaskGroup`: many children whose results are not needed.\n\n```swift\n// Collect results keyed by input, completion order does not matter\nlet readings = try await withThrowingTaskGroup(of: (Device.ID, Reading).self) { group in\n    for id in ids {\n        group.addTask { (id, try await api.latestReading(for: id)) }\n    }\n    var result: [Device.ID: Reading] = [:]\n    for try await (id, reading) in group { result[id] = reading }\n    return result\n}\n```\n\nBound the width when the input can be large, so 500 inputs do not open 500 connections:\n\n```swift\nfunc uploadAll(_ files: [URL], maxConcurrent: Int = 4) async throws {\n    try await withThrowingTaskGroup(of: Void.self) { group in\n        var pending = files.makeIterator()\n        for _ in 0..<maxConcurrent {\n            guard let file = pending.next() else { break }\n            group.addTask { try await upload(file) }\n        }\n        while try await group.next() != nil {\n            if let file = pending.next() {\n                group.addTask { try await upload(file) }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- When one child throws, the group cancels the remaining children and rethrows.\n- Never let a child task escape the group closure.\n\n## Actors and reentrancy\n\nEvery `await` inside an actor is a suspension point where other callers can run. Rules:\n\n- Read state, mutate it, then suspend. Not read, suspend, mutate.\n- Re-check invariants after each `await`.\n- Deduplicate concurrent requests by caching the in-flight `Task`, not only the result.\n\n```swift\nactor FirmwareCatalog {\n    private var inFlight: [String: Task<Manifest, Error>] = [:]\n\n    func manifest(for model: String) async throws -> Manifest {\n        if let task = inFlight[model] { return try await task.value }\n        let task = Task { try await api.manifest(model) }\n        inFlight[model] = task\n        defer { inFlight[model] = nil }\n        return try await task.value\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Keep actor methods short. Long synchronous work inside an actor blocks every other caller.\n- `nonisolated` on actor members that touch only immutable `let` state of `Sendable` type.\n\n## Sendable\n\n- Non-public structs and enums whose stored properties are all `Sendable` are inferred `Sendable`.\n  Public types need an explicit conformance.\n- Classes are `Sendable` only if `final` with immutable `Sendable` stored properties, or main-actor\n  isolated, or protected by a lock.\n- Closures crossing isolation are `@Sendable` and capture only `Sendable` values.\n\n```swift\nimport Synchronization\n\n// Synchronous, thread-safe counter for code that cannot await\nfinal class PacketStats: Sendable {\n    private let dropped = Mutex(0)\n    func recordDrop() { dropped.withLock { \$0 += 1 } }\n    var droppedCount: Int { dropped.withLock { \$0 } }\n}\n```\n\n- `sending` on a parameter or result lets a non-`Sendable` value move across isolation when the caller\n  no longer uses it. Prefer making the type `Sendable`; reach for `sending` when it cannot be.\n- `@preconcurrency import SomeModule` for dependencies not yet annotated. Remove it once they are.\n\n## Swift 6.2 isolation of async functions\n\nWith the upcoming feature `NonisolatedNonsendingByDefault` (on in new Xcode 26 projects with\n\"Approachable Concurrency\"), a `nonisolated async` function runs on the caller's actor instead of\nhopping to the global executor.\n\n- To force work off the caller's actor (CPU-heavy parsing, compression), mark the function `@concurrent`.\n- Check which mode the project uses before reasoning about where an `async` function runs.\n\n```swift\n// CPU-bound work explicitly leaves the main actor\n@concurrent\nnonisolated func decodeLog(_ data: Data) async throws -> [LogEntry] {\n    try LogDecoder().decode(data)\n}\n```\n\n## Cancellation\n\nCancellation is cooperative. Nothing stops unless the code checks.\n\n- `try Task.checkCancellation()` in loops and before expensive steps.\n- `Task.isCancelled` when cleanup is needed instead of throwing.\n- `Task.sleep(for:)` and `URLSession` async APIs throw `CancellationError` on cancel.\n- Callback APIs with their own cancel method use `withTaskCancellationHandler`:\n\n```swift\nfunc fetch(_ request: LegacyRequest) async throws -> Data {\n    let operation = LegacyOperation(request) // Sendable; cancel() is thread-safe\n    return try await withTaskCancellationHandler {\n        try await withCheckedThrowingContinuation { continuation in\n            operation.start { result in continuation.resume(with: result) }\n        }\n    } onCancel: {\n        operation.cancel()\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `onCancel` runs immediately and concurrently, possibly before `start` is called. The operation must\n  tolerate cancel-before-start.\n- The cancelled operation must still complete its callback so the continuation resumes.\n\n## Continuations\n\n- `withCheckedThrowingContinuation` in all code. The unsafe variants only after profiling proves a need.\n- Resume exactly once on every path, including error and timeout paths.\n- A checked continuation that is never resumed leaks the task and logs a warning; resuming twice traps.\n\n## AsyncStream\n\n```swift\nfunc notifications(from characteristic: CharacteristicID) -> AsyncThrowingStream<Data, Error> {\n    let (stream, continuation) = AsyncThrowingStream.makeStream(\n        of: Data.self,\n        bufferingPolicy: .bufferingNewest(64)\n    )\n    let token = transport.subscribe(characteristic) { event in\n        switch event {\n        case .value(let data): continuation.yield(data)\n        case .failed(let error): continuation.finish(throwing: error)\n        case .ended: continuation.finish()\n        }\n    }\n    continuation.onTermination = { _ in token.cancel() }\n    return stream\n}\n```\n\n- Choose a buffering policy on purpose. The default is unbounded; high-rate sensor or BLE notifications\n  should use `.bufferingNewest(n)` so memory cannot grow without limit.\n- `onTermination` releases the underlying subscription when the consumer cancels or the stream ends.\n- `AsyncStream` supports one consumer. For several consumers, give each its own stream.\n- Consume in `.task` in SwiftUI, or in a stored `Task` that is cancelled in the owner's teardown.\n\n## Time\n\n- `try await Task.sleep(for: .seconds(2))`, not `Task.sleep(nanoseconds:)`.\n- Inject `any Clock<Duration>` into types that wait, time out or debounce, so tests control time.\n\n```swift\nfinal class Reconnector {\n    private let clock: any Clock<Duration>\n    init(clock: any Clock<Duration> = ContinuousClock()) { self.clock = clock }\n\n    func backoff(attempt: Int) async throws {\n        try await clock.sleep(for: .seconds(min(1 << attempt, 30)))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Migrating older code\n\n- Convert completion handlers at the lowest layer first, then move up. Keep the callback version only\n  if other modules still depend on it.\n- Replace `DispatchQueue` serial queues that guard state with an `actor`.\n- Replace `DispatchGroup` fan-out with a task group.\n- Move one module at a time to Swift 6 language mode; fix warnings in Swift 5 mode with complete\n  checking first.\n- Never fix a data-race error with `@unchecked Sendable` without a lock covering every stored property.\n",
    "references/packages.md":
        "# Packages and tooling reference\n\nLoad this when creating or changing Swift packages, access levels, lint and format configuration,\n`Info.plist`, entitlements or the privacy manifest.\n\n## Layout\n\n```\nApp/                      Xcode app target: composition root, App struct, assets, entitlements\nPackages/\n  Core/                   Package.swift with targets: CoreModels, CoreNetworking, CoreBLE, CoreTesting\n  DeviceFeature/          Package.swift: DeviceFeature, DeviceFeatureTests\n  FirmwareFeature/        Package.swift: FirmwareFeature, FirmwareFeatureTests\n```\n\n- The app target is thin: it wires dependencies and hosts the root view.\n- Features depend on core modules, never on other features. Cross-feature navigation goes through\n  routes or closures provided by the app.\n- Shared fakes live in a testing target (for example `CoreTesting`) that only test targets depend on.\n\n## Package.swift\n\n```swift\n// swift-tools-version: 6.0\nimport PackageDescription\n\nlet package = Package(\n    name: \"DeviceFeature\",\n    platforms: [.iOS(.v17)],\n    products: [\n        .library(name: \"DeviceFeature\", targets: [\"DeviceFeature\"]),\n    ],\n    dependencies: [\n        .package(path: \"../Core\"),\n    ],\n    targets: [\n        .target(\n            name: \"DeviceFeature\",\n            dependencies: [\n                .product(name: \"CoreModels\", package: \"Core\"),\n                .product(name: \"CoreBLE\", package: \"Core\"),\n            ],\n            resources: [.process(\"Resources\")]\n        ),\n        .testTarget(\n            name: \"DeviceFeatureTests\",\n            dependencies: [\n                \"DeviceFeature\",\n                .product(name: \"CoreTesting\", package: \"Core\"),\n            ]\n        ),\n    ],\n    swiftLanguageModes: [.v6]\n)\n```\n\n- Declare every module the target imports. Do not rely on something being linked transitively.\n- Resources are loaded with `Bundle.module`, never `Bundle.main`, inside a package.\n- Remote dependencies use a version requirement (`from: \"2.1.0\"`), not a branch. Commit\n  `Package.resolved` for the app.\n- Opt into upcoming features per target with `swiftSettings: [.enableUpcomingFeature(\"ExistentialAny\")]`.\n- With tools version 6.2 or later, `swiftSettings: [.defaultIsolation(MainActor.self)]` makes a UI\n  module main-actor by default. Do not use it for networking, parsing or BLE transport modules.\n\n## Access control\n\n| Level | Visible to | Use for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `private` | Enclosing declaration and its extensions in the same file | Implementation details |\n| `fileprivate` | Same file | Rare; helpers shared by several types in one file |\n| `internal` (default) | Same module | Almost everything |\n| `package` | All targets in the same Swift package | Helpers shared between targets of one package |\n| `public` | Any importing module | The module's API |\n| `open` | Any importing module, subclassable | Only for classes designed for subclassing |\n\n- Start `internal`, widen only when a caller in another module needs it.\n- A `public` type needs a `public init` if other modules construct it; the memberwise init is internal.\n- Public types that cross isolation declare `Sendable` explicitly; it is not inferred for public types.\n- `@testable import` in tests only. Production code never relies on it.\n\n## SwiftLint\n\n```yaml\n# .swiftlint.yml\nincluded:\n  - App\n  - Packages\nexcluded:\n  - \"**/.build\"\nopt_in_rules:\n  - force_unwrapping\n  - implicitly_unwrapped_optional\n  - unowned_variable_capture\n  - empty_count\n  - first_where\n  - fatal_error_message\nline_length:\n  warning: 140\n  error: 200\n```\n\n- `force_cast` and `force_try` are on by default; keep them on.\n- Run `swiftlint --strict` in CI so warnings fail the build.\n- Disable a rule inline with `// swiftlint:disable:next <rule>` and a reason, never file-wide.\n\n## Formatting\n\n- Pick one formatter for the repository: SwiftFormat (`.swiftformat` config) or the `swift-format` tool\n  shipped with the Swift toolchain. Running both causes churn.\n- SwiftFormat example:\n\n```\n# .swiftformat\n--swiftversion 6.0\n--indent 4\n--maxwidth 140\n--exclude **/.build\n```\n\n- CI runs the formatter in lint mode (`swiftformat --lint .`) and fails on differences.\n- Formatting-only changes go in their own commit.\n\n## Info.plist and entitlements\n\nEvery addition here must be listed in the change summary because it affects signing, App Review or\nuser prompts.\n\n- Usage descriptions explain the user benefit in plain language:\n  `NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription`, `NSCameraUsageDescription`,\n  `NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription`, `NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription`.\n  Accessing Bluetooth, camera or microphone without the key terminates the app.\n- `UIBackgroundModes` (`bluetooth-central`, `location`, `remote-notification`, `processing`) must match\n  real behaviour; App Review rejects unused modes.\n- Entitlements such as Push Notifications, App Groups, Keychain Sharing and Associated Domains require\n  the capability on the App ID and a regenerated provisioning profile. Flag this for whoever owns signing.\n- App Transport Security exceptions (`NSAppTransportSecurity`) need a written justification. Never add\n  `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads` for production traffic.\n\n## Privacy manifest (PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy)\n\nThe app and every SDK that uses required-reason APIs or collects data ships a `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy`.\n\n```xml\n<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>\n<false/>\n<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>\n<array/>\n<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>\n<array/>\n<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>\n<array>\n    <dict>\n        <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>\n        <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>\n        <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>\n        <array>\n            <string>CA92.1</string>\n        </array>\n    </dict>\n</array>\n```\n\n- Required-reason API categories include `UserDefaults`, file timestamps, system boot time, disk space\n  and active keyboards. Adding code that calls one of these means adding or updating an entry with an\n  approved reason code from Apple's documentation. Do not guess a reason code.\n- Collected data types (device IDs, location, diagnostics, telemetry) must match what the app and its\n  SDKs actually send, including whether it is linked to the user and used for tracking.\n- A Swift package that needs its own manifest includes it as a resource:\n  `resources: [.copy(\"PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy\")]`.\n- Generate the privacy report from an archive in the Xcode Organizer before release and review it.\n",
    "references/swiftdata.md":
        "# SwiftData reference\n\nLoad this when adding or changing persistence with SwiftData. Requires iOS 17. For Core Data projects,\ndo not mix in SwiftData without a migration plan agreed with the team.\n\n## Models\n\n```swift\nimport SwiftData\n\n@Model\nfinal class DeviceEntity {\n    @Attribute(.unique) var serial: String\n    var name: String\n    var lastSeen: Date?\n    @Relationship(deleteRule: .cascade, inverse: \\ReadingEntity.device)\n    var readings: [ReadingEntity] = []\n\n    init(serial: String, name: String) {\n        self.serial = serial\n        self.name = name\n    }\n}\n\n@Model\nfinal class ReadingEntity {\n    var value: Double\n    var date: Date\n    var device: DeviceEntity?\n\n    init(value: Double, date: Date) {\n        self.value = value\n        self.date = date\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `@Model` types are classes and are not `Sendable`. Keep them in the persistence and UI layers.\n- Map to plain `struct` domain models at the repository boundary if the domain layer must stay\n  framework-free or cross actors.\n- Store secrets and tokens in the Keychain, not in SwiftData. Device serials and telemetry can be\n  personal data; apply a file protection class and a retention policy.\n\n## Container\n\n```swift\n@main\nstruct GatewayApp: App {\n    var body: some Scene {\n        WindowGroup { RootView() }\n            .modelContainer(for: [DeviceEntity.self, ReadingEntity.self])\n    }\n}\n```\n\nFor tests and previews use an in-memory store:\n\n```swift\nlet container = try ModelContainer(\n    for: DeviceEntity.self, ReadingEntity.self,\n    configurations: ModelConfiguration(isStoredInMemoryOnly: true)\n)\n```\n\n## Reading\n\n```swift\nstruct DeviceList: View {\n    @Query(sort: \\DeviceEntity.name) private var devices: [DeviceEntity]\n    var body: some View { List(devices) { DeviceRow(device: \$0) } }\n}\n```\n\nFilter that depends on input: build the query in `init`.\n\n```swift\nstruct ReadingList: View {\n    @Query private var readings: [ReadingEntity]\n\n    init(serial: String) {\n        _readings = Query(\n            filter: #Predicate<ReadingEntity> { \$0.device?.serial == serial },\n            sort: \\ReadingEntity.date,\n            order: .reverse\n        )\n    }\n\n    var body: some View { List(readings) { ReadingRow(reading: \$0) } }\n}\n```\n\nOutside views, fetch with a descriptor and a limit:\n\n```swift\nvar descriptor = FetchDescriptor<ReadingEntity>(\n    sortBy: [SortDescriptor(\\.date, order: .reverse)]\n)\ndescriptor.fetchLimit = 100\nlet latest = try context.fetch(descriptor)\n```\n\n## Writing\n\n```swift\n@Environment(\\.modelContext) private var context\n\nfunc add(_ device: DeviceEntity) throws {\n    context.insert(device)\n    try context.save()\n}\n```\n\n- The main context autosaves, but call `save()` explicitly after user-visible writes so errors surface.\n- Bulk imports and sync run in a `@ModelActor`, never on the main context.\n\n```swift\n@ModelActor\nactor ReadingImporter {\n    func importBatch(_ rows: [ReadingDTO], for deviceID: PersistentIdentifier) throws {\n        guard let device = self[deviceID, as: DeviceEntity.self] else { return }\n        for row in rows {\n            let reading = ReadingEntity(value: row.value, date: row.date)\n            reading.device = device\n            modelContext.insert(reading)\n        }\n        try modelContext.save()\n    }\n}\n\n// Caller\nlet importer = ReadingImporter(modelContainer: container)\ntry await importer.importBatch(rows, for: device.persistentModelID)\n```\n\n- Pass `PersistentIdentifier` (which is `Sendable`) across actors, then re-fetch in the target context.\n- One context per actor. Never capture a `ModelContext` or `@Model` instance in a `Task.detached`.\n\n## Schema changes and migration\n\nAny change to a shipped `@Model` needs a versioned schema so existing user data migrates.\n\n```swift\nenum SchemaV1: VersionedSchema {\n    static var versionIdentifier: Schema.Version { Schema.Version(1, 0, 0) }\n    static var models: [any PersistentModel.Type] { [DeviceEntity.self] }\n\n    @Model final class DeviceEntity {\n        var serial: String\n        init(serial: String) { self.serial = serial }\n    }\n}\n\nenum SchemaV2: VersionedSchema {\n    static var versionIdentifier: Schema.Version { Schema.Version(2, 0, 0) }\n    static var models: [any PersistentModel.Type] { [DeviceEntity.self] }\n\n    @Model final class DeviceEntity {\n        var serial: String\n        var firmwareVersion: String?\n        init(serial: String) { self.serial = serial }\n    }\n}\n\nenum GatewayMigrationPlan: SchemaMigrationPlan {\n    static var schemas: [any VersionedSchema.Type] { [SchemaV1.self, SchemaV2.self] }\n    static var stages: [MigrationStage] {\n        [.lightweight(fromVersion: SchemaV1.self, toVersion: SchemaV2.self)]\n    }\n}\n\n// The rest of the app refers to the current version only\ntypealias DeviceEntity = SchemaV2.DeviceEntity\n\nlet container = try ModelContainer(\n    for: DeviceEntity.self,\n    migrationPlan: GatewayMigrationPlan.self\n)\n```\n\n- Lightweight stages cover added optional properties, renames with `@Attribute(originalName:)`, and\n  removed properties. Data transformations need a `.custom` stage.\n- Test migration on a store created by the previous release build, not only on a fresh install.\n- Never ship a change that deletes and recreates the store to \"fix\" a migration error.\n",
    "references/swiftui.md":
        "# SwiftUI reference\n\nLoad this when writing or refactoring SwiftUI views and their models. Assumes iOS 17+ and the Observation\nframework. For older targets keep `ObservableObject` and `@StateObject` and say so.\n\n## Property wrapper choice\n\n| Situation | Use |\n|---|---|\n| View creates and owns an `@Observable` model | `@State private var model = Model()` |\n| Model passed in, view only reads it | `let model: Model` (or `var`) |\n| Model passed in, view needs `\$model.field` bindings | `@Bindable var model: Model` |\n| Simple value owned by this view | `@State private var isExpanded = false` |\n| Value owned by the parent, edited here | `@Binding var isOn: Bool` |\n| App-wide dependency or model | `@Environment(Store.self) private var store` |\n| System or custom environment value | `@Environment(\\.dismiss) private var dismiss` |\n| Legacy `ObservableObject` | `@StateObject` (owner) / `@ObservedObject` (passed in) |\n\n`@State` is always `private`. A value that comes from outside is not `@State`.\n\n## Observable models\n\n```swift\n@MainActor @Observable\nfinal class DeviceListModel {\n    private(set) var devices: [Device] = []\n    private(set) var error: LoadError?\n    var filter = \"\"\n\n    @ObservationIgnored private let repository: DeviceRepository\n\n    init(repository: DeviceRepository) { self.repository = repository }\n\n    func load() async {\n        do { devices = try await repository.devices() }\n        catch is CancellationError { }\n        catch { self.error = LoadError(error) }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Views re-render only when a property they actually read changes.\n- `@ObservationIgnored` for dependencies and caches the UI never reads.\n- Keep `init` cheap. `@State private var model = Model()` evaluates the initializer each time the\n  parent re-creates the view value; the extra instances are discarded but their init cost is paid.\n  Load data in `.task`, not in `init`.\n\n## Bindings from an observable model\n\n```swift\nstruct DeviceSettingsView: View {\n    @Bindable var settings: DeviceSettings\n    var body: some View {\n        Form {\n            Toggle(\"Auto reconnect\", isOn: \$settings.autoReconnect)\n            Stepper(\"Interval: \\(settings.interval)s\", value: \$settings.interval, in: 5...60)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWhen the model comes from the environment, create the bindable locally:\n\n```swift\n@Environment(DeviceSettings.self) private var settings\nvar body: some View {\n    @Bindable var settings = settings\n    Toggle(\"Auto reconnect\", isOn: \$settings.autoReconnect)\n}\n```\n\n## Environment\n\n```swift\n// Inject an observable model\nContentView().environment(store)\n\n// Read it\n@Environment(Store.self) private var store\n```\n\nCustom values use the `@Entry` macro:\n\n```swift\n// TelemetryClient is a Sendable protocol, so the default value is safe to share\nextension EnvironmentValues {\n    @Entry var telemetryClient: any TelemetryClient = NoopTelemetryClient()\n}\n\n// Inject and read\nRootView().environment(\\.telemetryClient, LiveTelemetryClient())\n@Environment(\\.telemetryClient) private var telemetry\n```\n\n- `@Environment(Store.self)` crashes at runtime if no ancestor injected it. Inject at the root and in\n  every `#Preview`.\n- The environment is for dependencies and app-wide state, not for passing one value two levels down.\n\n## Async work\n\n```swift\n// Restarts on every query change, previous run is cancelled\n.task(id: query) {\n    try? await Task.sleep(for: .milliseconds(300))\n    guard !Task.isCancelled else { return }\n    await model.search(query)\n}\n```\n\n- `.task` starts before the view appears and is cancelled when it disappears.\n- `.refreshable { await model.reload() }` for pull to refresh.\n- Consume `AsyncSequence` values in `.task` with `for await`; the loop ends when the view disappears.\n- `.onChange(of: value) { oldValue, newValue in }` for synchronous reactions; avoid chains of `onChange`\n  that feed each other.\n\n## Navigation\n\n```swift\nenum Route: Hashable {\n    case device(Device.ID)\n    case firmware(Device.ID)\n    case settings\n}\n\nstruct RootView: View {\n    @State private var path: [Route] = []\n\n    var body: some View {\n        NavigationStack(path: \$path) {\n            DeviceList(onSelect: { path.append(.device(\$0)) })\n                .navigationDestination(for: Route.self) { route in\n                    switch route {\n                    case .device(let id): DeviceDetail(id: id)\n                    case .firmware(let id): FirmwareUpdate(id: id)\n                    case .settings: SettingsView()\n                    }\n                }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Routes carry identifiers, not models, so they are cheap, `Hashable` and restorable.\n- One `navigationDestination(for:)` per type, attached inside the stack, not inside lazy containers.\n- `NavigationPath` only when the stack really holds unrelated types.\n- `NavigationSplitView` for iPad and Mac layouts. `NavigationView` is deprecated.\n\n## Identity and performance\n\n```swift\n// BAD: id regenerated on every render, every row is new every time\nForEach(readings.map { RowItem(id: UUID(), reading: \$0) }) { RowView(item: \$0) }\n\n// GOOD\nForEach(readings) { reading in ReadingRow(reading: reading) } // Reading.id comes from the data\n```\n\n- `.id(value)` on a view discards its state when `value` changes. Use it only to reset on purpose.\n- Prefer `if` / `switch` inside a `@ViewBuilder` over `AnyView`. Same-type branches can use modifiers\n  (`.opacity`, `.disabled`) instead of swapping views, which keeps identity.\n- `body` does no I/O, sorting of large arrays, or formatter creation. Precompute in the model or use\n  `Text(date, format: .dateTime.hour().minute())`.\n- `LazyVStack` / `List` for long or unbounded lists; `VStack` only for short fixed content.\n- Split views at natural boundaries. A subview re-renders only when its inputs change.\n\n## Previews\n\n```swift\n#Preview {\n    DeviceListScreen()\n        .environment(Store.preview)\n}\n\n#Preview(\"Toggle states\") {\n    @Previewable @State var isOn = true\n    Toggle(\"Auto reconnect\", isOn: \$isOn)\n}\n```\n\n- Previews use in-memory or fake dependencies, never live network or BLE.\n\n## Accessibility\n\n- Meaningful images get `.accessibilityLabel`; decorative ones use `Image(decorative:)`.\n- Use text styles (`.font(.body)`), not fixed point sizes, so Dynamic Type works.\n- Combine row content with `.accessibilityElement(children: .combine)` when a row reads as one item.\n- Tap targets at least 44x44 points.\n",
    "references/testing.md":
        "# Testing reference\n\nLoad this when adding or changing tests. Swift Testing for unit and integration tests; XCTest for UI\ntests and performance tests. Both frameworks can live in the same test target.\n\n## Swift Testing basics\n\n```swift\nimport Testing\n@testable import DeviceFeature\n\n@Suite(\"Frame decoder\")\nstruct FrameDecoderTests {\n    let decoder = FrameDecoder() // fresh instance per test, no shared state\n\n    @Test func decodesBatteryLevel() throws {\n        let packet = try decoder.decode(Fixtures.batteryFrame)\n        let battery = try #require(packet.battery)\n        #expect(battery == 87)\n    }\n\n    @Test func rejectsBadChecksum() {\n        #expect(throws: FrameError.badChecksum) {\n            try decoder.decode(Fixtures.corruptedFrame)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- `#expect` records a failure and continues. `try #require` stops the test; use it for preconditions and\n  to unwrap optionals.\n- A `struct` suite gets a new instance per test. Setup goes in `init`, teardown in `deinit` of a\n  `final class` suite only when cleanup is required.\n- `#expect(throws: SomeError.self)` checks the type; passing an `Equatable` error value checks the case.\n- Write the expectation as a plain expression. `#expect(a == b)` already reports both sides on failure.\n\n## Parameterized tests\n\n```swift\n@Test(arguments: [\n    (Data([0x01, 0x00]), Opcode.ping),\n    (Data([0x02, 0x10]), Opcode.status),\n])\nfunc decodesOpcode(frame: Data, expected: Opcode) throws {\n    #expect(try FrameDecoder().decode(frame).opcode == expected)\n}\n\n@Test(arguments: [0, 1, 19, 20])\nfunc rejectsFramesShorterThanHeader(length: Int) {\n    #expect(throws: FrameError.self) {\n        try FrameDecoder().decode(Data(count: length))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Each argument runs as its own test case and can be re-run alone.\n- Two argument collections produce every combination. Use `zip(inputs, outputs)` for pairs.\n- Prefer parameterized tests over a `for` loop inside one test.\n\n## Async code\n\n```swift\n@MainActor\n@Test func loadPublishesDevices() async {\n    let model = DeviceListModel(repository: FakeDeviceRepository(stubbedDevices: [.sample]))\n    await model.load()\n    #expect(model.devices == [.sample])\n}\n```\n\n- Mark tests or suites `@MainActor` when they touch main-actor types.\n- `await` the operation under test. Never `sleep` and hope.\n\nCallbacks and events that must fire use `confirmation`:\n\n```swift\n@Test func emitsConnectedEvent() async {\n    let session = DeviceSession(transport: FakeTransport())\n    await confirmation(\"connected event\") { connected in\n        session.onEvent = { event in\n            if event == .connected { connected() }\n        }\n        await session.connect()\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- The confirmation must be confirmed before the closure returns. It does not wait for later callbacks.\n- `confirmation(expectedCount: 3)` for an exact count; `expectedCount: 0` asserts something never happens.\n\n## Time and dependencies\n\n- Inject `any Clock<Duration>` and use a manual test clock (own implementation or a library) so backoff,\n  timeouts and debounce run instantly and deterministically.\n- Put protocols at boundaries (network, BLE transport, storage, keychain) and inject fakes. Avoid mocking\n  frameworks that rely on runtime tricks.\n- SwiftData tests use `ModelConfiguration(isStoredInMemoryOnly: true)`.\n\n```swift\nprotocol DeviceRepository: Sendable {\n    func devices() async throws -> [Device]\n}\n\nstruct FakeDeviceRepository: DeviceRepository {\n    var stubbedDevices: [Device] = []\n    var stubbedError: (any Error)?\n    func devices() async throws -> [Device] {\n        if let stubbedError { throw stubbedError }\n        return stubbedDevices\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Traits and organisation\n\n```swift\nextension Tag {\n    @Tag static var ble: Self\n    @Tag static var parsing: Self\n}\n\n@Suite(.tags(.ble), .timeLimit(.minutes(1)))\nstruct ReconnectTests { /* tests */ }\n\n@Test(.disabled(\"Flaky on CI simulator, see TICKET-123\"))\nfunc scansInBackground() async { /* test body */ }\n```\n\n- `.serialized` on a suite whose tests share an external resource. Tests run in parallel by default.\n- `.enabled(if:)` for environment-dependent tests.\n- `.bug(\"https://...\")` links a known issue.\n\n## XCTest: what still needs it\n\nUI tests:\n\n```swift\nfinal class PairingUITests: XCTestCase {\n    func testPairingFlow() throws {\n        let app = XCUIApplication()\n        app.launchArguments = [\"-ui-testing\", \"-fake-ble\"]\n        app.launch()\n\n        app.buttons[\"pair-device\"].tap()\n        XCTAssertTrue(app.staticTexts[\"paired-status\"].waitForExistence(timeout: 5))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Query by `accessibilityIdentifier`, not by visible text that changes with localisation.\n- Launch arguments switch the app to fake transports and fixed data. UI tests never depend on real\n  devices, servers or BLE hardware.\n\nPerformance tests:\n\n```swift\nfunc testDecodePerformance() {\n    measure(metrics: [XCTClockMetric(), XCTMemoryMetric()]) {\n        _ = try? FrameDecoder().decodeAll(Fixtures.largeCapture)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Set a baseline in Xcode and run on a fixed device class; simulator numbers vary.\n- Async XCTest waits use `await fulfillment(of: [expectation], timeout: 2)`, not `wait(for:)`.\n\n## Running\n\n- Packages that build on macOS: `swift test`.\n- iOS-only packages and the app: `xcodebuild test -scheme <Scheme> -destination '<simulator>'` or a\n  test plan.\n- Keep fast unit tests in package test targets so they run without launching the simulator app.\n",
  },
  "mobile-testing": {
    "SKILL.md":
        "---\nname: mobile-testing\ndescription: \"Plans, writes and reviews test strategies for Android, iOS and Flutter apps: mobile test pyramid, fakes over mocks, virtual time, Compose UI tests, XCUITest, Flutter integration_test, screenshot tests with Roborazzi, Paparazzi, swift-snapshot-testing and goldens, flaky-test quarantine, coverage that matters and CI sharding. Use when deciding what to test where, writing a test plan, fixing flaky or slow suites, or reviewing tests.\"\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  author: Ahmad Hussain\n  version: \"1.0.0\"\n  pack: mobile-dev-kit\n---\n\n# Mobile testing\n\nDecide what to test at which layer, keep every test deterministic, and give the team a suite that is fast\non every pull request and trusted when it fails. A good result is a test plan or review that names the\nlayers, what each one covers, the gaps, and how CI runs it.\n\n## When to use\n\n- Planning tests for a new feature or app, or auditing an existing suite.\n- Choosing between a unit test, a screen test, a screenshot test and an end-to-end test.\n- Adding screenshot tests (Roborazzi, Paparazzi, swift-snapshot-testing, Flutter goldens).\n- Writing Compose UI tests, XCUITests or Flutter `integration_test` flows.\n- Flaky, slow or order-dependent tests; deciding on retries, quarantine and CI sharding.\n- Setting a coverage policy, or reviewing tests in a merge request.\n\nNot for: framework syntax for one language (use `mobile-kotlin`, `mobile-java`, `mobile-swift`,\n`mobile-flutter`; each has a references/testing.md), pipeline YAML, signing and caching details\n(use `mobile-ci-cd`).\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Map the feature: entry points, state holders, data sources, platform boundaries (network, BLE,\n   storage, permissions, clock) and the user journeys that cost money or support tickets when they break.\n2. Read the existing tests and CI config. Note frameworks in use, test run time, known flaky tests and\n   coverage reports. Extend what exists before adding a new framework.\n3. Assign every behavior to the lowest layer that can catch its bug (see the table below). Write the\n   list down before writing code.\n4. Put seams at boundaries: interfaces or protocols for data sources, injected dispatchers and clocks,\n   launch flags for UI tests. Write fakes once per boundary.\n5. Write the tests. For language-level syntax, load the matching language skill's reference by name:\n   `mobile-kotlin`, references/testing.md (coroutines, Turbine, MockK, Room, Compose);\n   `mobile-java`, references/testing.md (LiveData, Mockito, Espresso, IdlingResource);\n   `mobile-swift`, references/testing.md (Swift Testing, confirmations, XCTest UI and performance);\n   `mobile-flutter`, references/testing.md (mocktail, fake_async, Riverpod, Bloc, widget tests).\n   For ViewModel and reducer tests, `mobile-mvvm` rule 11 and `mobile-mvi` rule 9.\n6. Screen and journey tests: load [ui-tests.md](references/ui-tests.md).\n7. Visual regressions: load [screenshot-tests.md](references/screenshot-tests.md).\n8. CI layout, sharding, retries and the flaky-test process: load [ci-and-flakes.md](references/ci-and-flakes.md).\n9. Run the suite twice, once in random order where the runner supports it. Report failures honestly; never\n   mark the work done with a red or skipped-without-ticket test.\n10. Deliver the result in the output format below.\n\n## Test layers\n\n| Layer | Catches | Android | iOS | Flutter | Runs |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Unit | Logic: validation, mapping, parsing, CRC, reducers, ViewModels | JUnit, coroutines-test, Turbine | Swift Testing | `package:test`, `flutter_test` | Every PR, seconds |\n| Integration | Real collaborators together: repository + DB, serializer + fixtures, DI graph | Room in-memory, Robolectric | Swift Testing with in-memory SwiftData | `flutter_test` with in-memory fakes | Every PR |\n| Screen (UI behavior) | One screen renders state and sends events | Compose UI test on Robolectric or device | XCUITest on a screen with fixed data | `testWidgets` | Every PR (JVM, widget), merge (device) |\n| Screenshot | Layout, theme, dark mode, large fonts, RTL | Roborazzi or Paparazzi | swift-snapshot-testing | `matchesGoldenFile` | Every PR, pinned image |\n| End-to-end | A critical journey across screens and process boundaries | Compose UI or Espresso on emulator | XCUITest | `integration_test` | Merge or nightly |\n\nMost tests sit in the first two rows. End-to-end tests are few, one per critical journey (sign-in,\npairing, purchase, firmware update), never one per screen detail.\n\n## Rules\n\n### 1. Test each behavior at the lowest layer that can catch its bug\n\nA UI test that checks a validation rule is slow, flaky and points nowhere when it fails.\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: an instrumented UI test to prove one validation rule\ncomposeRule.onNodeWithTag(\"pin\").performTextInput(\"12\")\ncomposeRule.onNodeWithText(\"PIN too short\").assertIsDisplayed()\n\n// GOOD: the rule is a plain function, tested on the JVM in milliseconds\n@Test fun `rejects pin shorter than six digits`() {\n    assertEquals(PinError.TooShort, validatePin(\"12\"))\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// BAD: XCUITest launches the app to check a parser\nfunc testBatteryFrame() { app.launch(); /* navigate to a screen that shows the parsed value */ }\n\n// GOOD\n@Test func decodesBatteryLevel() throws {\n    #expect(try FrameDecoder().decode(Fixtures.batteryFrame).battery == 87)\n}\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// BAD: an integration_test on a device for a mapper\n// GOOD\ntest('maps 0xFF to unknown battery', () {\n  expect(Battery.fromRaw(0xFF), Battery.unknown);\n});\n```\n\n- The screen test then checks once that an error state is rendered, not every rule behind it.\n\n### 2. Fakes at boundaries you own; mocks only for interactions\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: a mock of your own repository, re-stubbed in every test, breaks on every refactor\nval repo = mockk<DeviceRepository>()\nevery { repo.devices } returns flowOf(listOf(gateway))\n\n// GOOD: one fake per boundary, reused by every test of the feature\nclass FakeDeviceRepository : DeviceRepository {\n    private val state = MutableStateFlow<List<Device>>(emptyList())\n    var refreshError: Throwable? = null\n    override val devices: Flow<List<Device>> = state\n    override suspend fun refresh() { refreshError?.let { throw it } }\n    fun emit(list: List<Device>) { state.value = list }\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n// GOOD: protocol at the boundary, fake in the test target\nstruct FakeTransport: BLETransport {\n    var responses: [Data] = []\n    func write(_ frame: Data) async throws -> Data { responses.first ?? Data() }\n}\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// GOOD: implements the interface; no per-test stubbing\nclass FakeDeviceRepository implements DeviceRepository {\n  List<Device> devices = [];\n  @override\n  Future<List<Device>> fetch() async => devices;\n}\n```\n\n- Mock when the interaction is the behavior (an analytics event sent exactly once, a write never issued).\n- Wrap third-party clients (HTTP, BLE stacks, billing) behind your interface; fake the interface.\n- Never mock value types, data classes or the class under test.\n\n### 3. Time, dispatchers and randomness are injected\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\n// BAD: hardcoded dispatcher and real delay; the test waits 30 seconds or races\nclass Poller(private val api: Api) {\n    suspend fun run() = withContext(Dispatchers.IO) { while (true) { api.poll(); delay(30_000) } }\n}\n\n// GOOD: same class with `io: CoroutineDispatcher` injected and used in withContext(io)\n@Test fun `polls every 30 seconds`() = runTest {\n    val api = FakeApi()\n    backgroundScope.launch { Poller(api, StandardTestDispatcher(testScheduler)).run() }\n    advanceTimeBy(60_001)\n    assertEquals(3, api.pollCount)\n}\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\ntry await Task.sleep(for: .seconds(30)) // BAD: real time, no seam\n// GOOD: the clock is a dependency; tests pass a manual test clock\nstruct Poller {\n    let clock: any Clock<Duration>\n    func waitForNextPoll() async throws { try await clock.sleep(for: .seconds(30)) }\n}\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\nfinal expiresAt = DateTime.now().add(ttl); // BAD\n// GOOD: inject the clock; drive timers with fakeAsync or tester.pump(duration)\nclass TokenCache {\n  TokenCache({DateTime Function()? now}) : _now = now ?? DateTime.now;\n  final DateTime Function() _now;\n}\n```\n\n- Same for UUIDs, random backoff jitter, locale and time zone: pass them in, fix them in tests.\n\n### 4. Screen tests drive a stateless screen with plain state\n\nKotlin (Compose, v2 test APIs):\n```kotlin\n// BAD: real ViewModel, Hilt graph and network inside a screen test\ncomposeRule.setContent { DevicesRoute(viewModel = hiltViewModel()) }\n\n// GOOD\nimport androidx.compose.ui.test.junit4.v2.createComposeRule\n@get:Rule val composeRule = createComposeRule()\n\n@Test fun clickingDeviceReportsId() {\n    var clicked: DeviceId? = null\n    composeRule.setContent {\n        AppTheme { DevicesScreen(state = DevicesUiState(devices = listOf(gateway)), onDeviceClick = { clicked = it }) }\n    }\n    composeRule.onNodeWithText(\"Gateway A\").performClick()\n    assertEquals(gateway.id, clicked)\n}\n```\n\nSwift (SwiftUI):\n```swift\n// BAD: one XCUITest per screen state. GOOD: model test for logic, snapshot per visual state\n@MainActor @Test func emptyListShowsPairPrompt() async {\n    let model = DeviceListModel(repository: FakeDeviceRepository(stubbedDevices: []))\n    await model.load()\n    #expect(model.state == .empty)\n}\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// GOOD: widget test with the repository overridden by a fake\nawait tester.pumpWidget(ProviderScope(\n  overrides: [deviceRepositoryProvider.overrideWithValue(FakeDeviceRepository())],\n  child: const MaterialApp(home: DevicesScreen()),\n));\n```\n\n- The v1 rule factories (`androidx.compose.ui.test.junit4.createComposeRule`) are deprecated. v2 runs\n  composition on `StandardTestDispatcher`; queued work runs after `waitForIdle()` or `runOnIdle`.\n\n### 5. Find elements the way a user or assistive technology does\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\ncomposeRule.onAllNodesWithTag(\"row\")[3].performClick()                    // BAD: tree position\ncomposeRule.onNodeWithContentDescription(\"Delete Gateway A\").performClick() // GOOD: what a user hears\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\napp.buttons[\"Pair\"].tap()        // BAD: visible text breaks in every locale\napp.buttons[\"pair-device\"].tap() // GOOD: accessibilityIdentifier\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\nawait tester.tap(find.byType(IconButton).at(2));       // BAD\nawait tester.tap(find.byTooltip('Delete Gateway A')); // GOOD\n```\n\n### 6. Wait on conditions, never on wall-clock time\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\nThread.sleep(2_000) // BAD\ncomposeRule.waitUntilAtLeastOneExists(hasText(\"Connected\"), timeoutMillis = 5_000) // GOOD\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\nsleep(3) // BAD\nXCTAssertTrue(app.staticTexts[\"paired-status\"].waitForExistence(timeout: 5)) // GOOD\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\nawait Future<void>.delayed(const Duration(seconds: 2)); // BAD\n// GOOD: pump fake time; pumpAndSettle throws on endless animations such as spinners\nawait tester.pump(const Duration(milliseconds: 300));\n```\n\n- Background work the test framework cannot see gets an idling resource, not a sleep (details in\n  [ui-tests.md](references/ui-tests.md)).\n\n### 7. Screenshot tests pin everything that changes pixels\n\nDevice size, density, locale, font scale, theme, fonts, OS or simulator version and renderer.\n\nKotlin (Paparazzi):\n```kotlin\n@get:Rule val paparazzi = Paparazzi() // BAD: locale comes from the machine running the test\n// GOOD\n@get:Rule val paparazzi = Paparazzi(\n    deviceConfig = DeviceConfig.PIXEL_5.copy(locale = \"en\", nightMode = NightMode.NIGHT),\n)\n```\n\nSwift:\n```swift\nassertSnapshot(of: DeviceCard(device: .sample), as: .image) // BAD: size depends on the host\n// GOOD: fixed device layout, and the same simulator model and OS on every machine\nassertSnapshot(of: DeviceCard(device: .sample), as: .image(layout: .device(config: .iPhone13)))\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\n// GOOD: fixed surface; real fonts loaded in flutter_test_config.dart, else text renders as boxes\ntester.view.physicalSize = const Size(1080, 2400);\ntester.view.devicePixelRatio = 3.0;\naddTearDown(tester.view.reset);\n```\n\n### 8. Reference images are recorded on the CI image and reviewed as diffs\n\n```bash\n# BAD: references recorded on a developer laptop, CI records missing ones and passes\n# GOOD: CI only verifies; a labelled job on the same image records and uploads for review\n./gradlew verifyRoborazziDebug          # or verifyPaparazziDebug\n# TEST_RUNNER_ prefix passes SNAPSHOT_TESTING_RECORD=never to the test runner process\nTEST_RUNNER_SNAPSHOT_TESTING_RECORD=never xcodebuild test ...\nflutter test --tags golden               # --update-goldens only in the recording job\n```\n\n- A changed reference image is reviewed in the merge request like code. Unexplained diffs block.\n\n### 9. End-to-end tests run against a controlled world\n\nSwift:\n```swift\napp.launch() // BAD: real backend, real BLE device, shared test account\n\n// GOOD: launch flags switch the app to fakes and fixed data\napp.launchArguments = [\"-ui-testing\", \"-fake-ble\"]\napp.launch()\n```\n\n- Android: replace modules with test doubles (`@TestInstallIn`) or use a test flavor. Flutter: a test\n  entry point or `--dart-define-from-file` pointing at a test backend.\n- Never production endpoints or real user data. BLE hardware tests live in a separate, manual or\n  rig-based suite with its own reporting.\n\n### 10. Every test is isolated and order-independent\n\nKotlin:\n```kotlin\ncompanion object { val repo = FakeDeviceRepository() } // BAD: shared, mutated by earlier tests\nprivate val repo = FakeDeviceRepository()               // GOOD: new per test instance\n```\n\n- Swift Testing: a `struct` suite gets a fresh instance per test, and tests run in parallel by default.\n- Dart: build state in `setUp`; run with `--test-randomize-ordering-seed random` to expose order\n  dependence.\n- Android instrumented: Android Test Orchestrator with `clearPackageData` when app state leaks between\n  tests (slower; use where needed).\n\n### 11. A flaky test is a bug: quarantine with a ticket, retry only with tracking\n\nSwift:\n```swift\n@Test func scansInBackground() async { } // BAD: fails one run in ten, everyone re-runs CI\n// GOOD: out of the blocking gate, owned, with a deadline\n@Test(.disabled(\"Flaky on CI simulator, MOB-412, fix by 2026-11-01\"))\nfunc scansInBackground() async { }\n```\n\nDart:\n```dart\ntestWidgets('reconnects after drop', (tester) async { /* ... */ }, tags: 'quarantine');\n// Blocking job: flutter test --exclude-tags quarantine\n```\n\n- Kotlin: a team annotation or category excluded from the blocking job, with the ticket in the reason.\n- Retries (Gradle test-retry plugin, `xcodebuild -retry-tests-on-failure`) are allowed only when the\n  report records which tests passed on retry and someone reviews that list. Never retry silently.\n\n### 12. Coverage gates protect critical logic, not a vanity number\n\n```kotlin\n// BAD: one global 90 percent gate over generated code, DI modules and composables\n// GOOD: a gate on the modules that hold logic, plus a review of uncovered branches there\nkover { reports { verify { rule { minBound(80) } } } } // in the domain and data modules\n```\n\n- Exclude generated code (DI, serializers, build config) from reports. Gate domain, data and parsing\n  modules; read the uncovered-branch report for ViewModels, reducers and protocol code.\n- iOS: `xcodebuild test -enableCodeCoverage YES`. Flutter: `flutter test --coverage`.\n- A test that executes code without asserting on it adds coverage and no safety. Look for it in reviews.\n\n### 13. The pull request gate stays fast; slow layers are sharded\n\n- PR: unit, integration, JVM screen, widget and screenshot-verify tests, under 10 minutes. Merge or\n  nightly: device journeys, sharded. Android `numShards`/`shardIndex` or managed device shards; iOS\n  `build-for-testing` then `test-without-building` per shard; Flutter `--total-shards`/`--shard-index`.\n  Commands in [ci-and-flakes.md](references/ci-and-flakes.md).\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- An inverted pyramid: many end-to-end tests, few unit tests, a suite nobody runs locally.\n- Testing private functions or implementation details instead of observable state and output.\n- Mocking every collaborator, then asserting the mocks were called in the order the code calls them.\n- `Thread.sleep`, `sleep`, `Task.sleep` or `Future.delayed` in tests to \"wait for\" anything.\n- `UnconfinedTestDispatcher` everywhere to hide ordering problems.\n- Snapshot references recorded on mixed machines, or CI that records missing snapshots and passes.\n- Hundreds of screenshot tests of whole screens with live data; nobody reviews the diffs.\n- UI tests that depend on real servers, BLE hardware, network quality or a shared account.\n- Retrying the whole job until green; `@Ignore` or `.disabled` with no ticket.\n- A coverage target raised by tests that assert nothing.\n- Test fixtures containing real device serials, MAC addresses or user data. Use synthetic identifiers.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [ ] Critical journeys listed; each has exactly one end-to-end test\n- [ ] Every behavior assigned to the lowest layer that can catch it\n- [ ] Fakes exist for each boundary (network, storage, BLE, clock); mocks only for interactions\n- [ ] Dispatchers, clocks, randomness, locale and time zone injected; no real delays in tests\n- [ ] Screen tests use stateless screens with plain state; Compose tests use the v2 rule factories\n- [ ] Selectors use text, semantics or accessibility identifiers, not tree position\n- [ ] No sleeps; waits are condition-based; background work has idling resources\n- [ ] Screenshot tests pin device, locale, font scale, theme, fonts and the CI image\n- [ ] CI verifies snapshots and never records; reference changes reviewed in the MR\n- [ ] Tests pass in random order and in isolation\n- [ ] Flaky tests quarantined with a ticket and owner; retried tests reported and reviewed\n- [ ] Coverage gated on logic modules, generated code excluded\n- [ ] PR gate under 10 minutes; slow layers sharded; reports uploaded on failure\n- [ ] Fixtures contain no real personal data or device identifiers\n- [ ] Suite run locally and in CI; failures reported, not hidden\n\n## Output format\n\nFor a plan:\n\n~~~markdown\n## Test plan: <feature or app>\n\nPlatforms: <Android | iOS | Flutter>. Existing frameworks: <list>. Current PR suite time: <n> min.\n\n### Critical journeys\n1. <journey> -- end-to-end test: <name>, layer runs on <merge | nightly>\n\n### Coverage by layer\n| Behavior | Layer | Test (file or name) | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| PIN validation | Unit | PinValidatorTest | New |\n| Device list renders empty state | Screenshot | DeviceListSnapshots | Exists |\n\n### Seams and fakes\n- <boundary> -- <fake or launch flag to add>\n\n### Gaps and risks\n- <untested behavior> -- <risk> -- <proposed test>\n\n### CI plan\n- PR: <jobs, target time>\n- Merge / nightly: <jobs, shards, devices>\n- Flake policy: <quarantine tag, retry setting, report location>\n~~~\n\nFor a review of existing tests: list findings as `[Blocker|Major|Minor] path:line -- issue`, then\n`Why` and a minimal `Fix`, in the same style as `mobile-code-review`, followed by the gaps table above.\nMissing tests for new logic or a sleep-based wait is Major; a test that can never fail is Major.\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `mobile-kotlin`, `mobile-java`, `mobile-swift`, `mobile-flutter` -- language-level test syntax in each\n  skill's references/testing.md\n- `mobile-mvvm`, `mobile-mvi` -- ViewModel, store and reducer tests\n- `mobile-ci-cd` -- pipeline jobs, caching and report upload that run this plan\n- `mobile-code-review` -- review of the tests inside a merge request\n- `mobile-accessibility` -- semantics and labels that also make UI tests stable\n- `mobile-ble` -- transport fakes and hardware-in-the-loop suites\n- `mobile-security` -- tests for storage, network and crypto controls\n",
    "references/ci-and-flakes.md":
        "# CI layout, sharding and flaky tests\n\nLoad this when deciding which tests run where in CI, splitting slow suites, or handling flaky tests. For\nworkflow YAML, SHA-pinned actions, caching keys and report upload, see `mobile-ci-cd`.\n\n## Stages\n\n| Stage | Runs | Target time | Blocks |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Pull request | Lint, unit, integration, JVM screen tests, widget tests, screenshot verify | Under 10 min | Merge |\n| Merge to main | Instrumented and XCUITest journeys on emulators and simulators, sharded | Under 30 min | Release |\n| Nightly | Full journey suite, extra devices and OS versions, quarantined tests, flake hunting | Any | Nothing; reports to the team |\n| Release candidate | Device farm or physical devices, BLE rig, manual exploratory checks | Planned | Store upload |\n\n- Upload test reports and failure artifacts on every run, pass or fail, so a red build can be diagnosed\n  without re-running it.\n- Cache dependencies and build outputs keyed on lockfiles (Gradle, SwiftPM, pub). Never cache test results\n  across commits.\n\n## Android\n\nLocal tests in parallel on one machine:\n\n```kotlin\ntasks.withType<Test>().configureEach {\n    maxParallelForks = (Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors() / 2).coerceAtLeast(1)\n}\n```\n\nInstrumented tests, sharded with the AndroidJUnitRunner arguments `numShards` and `shardIndex`\n(`shardIndex` is zero-based; run one job per index):\n\n```bash\nadb shell am instrument -w -e numShards 4 -e shardIndex 0 \\\n  com.example.app.test/androidx.test.runner.AndroidJUnitRunner\n```\n\nGradle managed devices give reproducible emulators defined in the build:\n\n```kotlin\nandroid {\n    testOptions {\n        managedDevices {\n            localDevices {\n                create(\"pixel6api34\") {\n                    device = \"Pixel 6\"\n                    apiLevel = 34\n                    systemImageSource = \"aosp\"\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```bash\n./gradlew pixel6api34DebugAndroidTest -Pandroid.testoptions.manageddevices.emulator.gpu=swiftshader_indirect\n```\n\n- The GPU flag is needed on CI hosts without hardware rendering (for example hosted GitHub Actions runners).\n- Shards per device: `android.experimental.androidTest.numManagedDeviceShards=<n>` in `gradle.properties`.\n  Each shard is a separate emulator; large counts can time out while provisioning.\n- Automated Test Device images (`systemImageSource = \"aosp-atd\"` or `\"google-atd\"`) start faster but disable\n  hardware rendering and support only some API levels. Do not run screenshot tests on them.\n- Groups (`groups { create(\"phoneAndTablet\") { ... } }`) run the same suite across several devices with\n  `phoneAndTabletGroupDebugAndroidTest`.\n\nIsolation between instrumented tests:\n\n```kotlin\nandroid {\n    defaultConfig {\n        testInstrumentationRunner = \"androidx.test.runner.AndroidJUnitRunner\"\n        testInstrumentationRunnerArguments[\"clearPackageData\"] = \"true\"\n    }\n    testOptions { execution = \"ANDROIDX_TEST_ORCHESTRATOR\" }\n}\ndependencies { androidTestUtil(libs.androidx.test.orchestrator) }\n```\n\nThe orchestrator restarts the app for every test, so it is slower. Use it where state leaks, not by default.\n\n## iOS\n\nBuild once, test many times:\n\n```bash\nxcodebuild build-for-testing -scheme App -destination 'platform=iOS Simulator,name=iPhone 16' \\\n  -derivedDataPath build\nxcodebuild test-without-building -xctestrun build/Build/Products/<name>.xctestrun \\\n  -destination 'platform=iOS Simulator,name=iPhone 16' -resultBundlePath results/shard-0.xcresult\n```\n\n- Parallel workers on one machine: `-parallel-testing-enabled YES` and `-parallel-testing-worker-count <n>`\n  (or `-maximum-parallel-testing-workers <n>`).\n- Several simulators in one job: pass multiple `-destination` values and\n  `-parallelize-tests-among-destinations` to split test classes among them instead of running everything\n  on each.\n- Across CI jobs: list tests with `-enumerate-tests -test-enumeration-style flat`, split the list, and give\n  each job its slice with repeated `-only-testing Target/Class` arguments.\n- Select a test plan with `-testPlan <name>`, or one configuration with `-only-test-configuration <name>`.\n- Timeouts: `-test-timeouts-enabled YES` with `-default-test-execution-time-allowance <seconds>`, so a hung\n  test fails instead of hanging the runner.\n- Coverage: `-enableCodeCoverage YES`. The result bundle holds coverage and attachments; upload it.\n- Variables for the test process need the `TEST_RUNNER_` prefix, for example\n  `TEST_RUNNER_SNAPSHOT_TESTING_RECORD=never`.\n- Use the test plan option to randomize execution order in at least one scheduled run.\n\n## Flutter\n\n```yaml\n# GitHub Actions matrix: one job per shard\nstrategy:\n  matrix:\n    shard: [0, 1, 2, 3]\nsteps:\n  - uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # vX.Y.Z\n  - uses: subosito/flutter-action@<sha> # vX.Y.Z\n    with:\n      cache: true\n  - run: flutter test --total-shards 4 --shard-index \${{ matrix.shard }} --exclude-tags quarantine\n```\n\n- `--shard-index` is zero-based and must be below `--total-shards`.\n- `--test-randomize-ordering-seed random` in one job finds order-dependent tests. To reproduce a failure,\n  re-run with the same numeric seed (a 32-bit unsigned integer); log the seed the job used.\n- `-j` / `--concurrency` sets parallel test processes for unit and widget tests and is ignored for\n  integration tests.\n- Goldens run only on the pinned platform job (`--tags golden`).\n\n## Flaky tests\n\nA flaky test passes and fails on the same code. It costs every engineer time and teaches the team to ignore\nred builds.\n\n### Causes, in order of how often they show up\n\n1. Real time: sleeps, real `delay`, `DateTime.now()`, timeouts tuned to one machine.\n2. Missing synchronization: asserting before background work finishes; work the framework cannot see.\n3. Shared state: static fakes, a database or preferences left by the previous test, test order.\n4. Environment: network, real backend, emulator animations, locale and time zone of the host.\n5. Concurrency bugs in production code. These are real defects; the test is doing its job.\n\n### Process\n\n1. Detect: track per-test pass and fail history from CI reports. A test that failed and then passed on\n   retry is flaky by definition.\n2. Quarantine within a day: move it out of the blocking job with a tag, category or disabled trait that\n   names a ticket and an owner. It keeps running in the nightly job.\n3. Fix or delete within an agreed time (for example two weeks). A quarantined test with no owner is\n   deleted, and the gap it leaves is recorded in the test plan.\n4. Reproduce locally by repetition: `xcodebuild test -only-testing <id> -test-iterations 50\n   -run-tests-until-failure`; Flutter with a fixed `--test-randomize-ordering-seed`; Android by running the\n   class in a loop on a managed device.\n5. Fix the cause from the list above. Do not raise timeouts as the fix.\n\n### Retries\n\nRetries are allowed only when they are visible.\n\n```kotlin\n// build.gradle.kts, JVM test tasks only; plugin id org.gradle.test-retry\ntasks.withType<Test>().configureEach {\n    retry {\n        maxRetries.set(2)\n        maxFailures.set(20)          // stop retrying when the build is genuinely broken\n        failOnPassedAfterRetry.set(false)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- The plugin augments Gradle `Test` tasks (JUnit 4, JUnit 5, Spock, TestNG). It does not cover\n  instrumented tests.\n- `failOnPassedAfterRetry.set(true)` fails the build on any flake; use it once the suite is clean.\n- iOS: `-retry-tests-on-failure` with `-test-iterations <max>` (default maximum 3). The result bundle shows\n  which tests needed a retry; publish that list.\n- Never wrap the whole CI job in a retry loop. It hides flakes and multiplies cost.\n",
    "references/screenshot-tests.md":
        "# Screenshot tests\n\nLoad this when adding or fixing screenshot tests (also called snapshot or golden tests). Pick one tool\nper platform and module. Check each library's README for the current version before adding it; do not\ncopy version numbers from this file.\n\n## What to snapshot\n\n- Design-system components in every variant: enabled, disabled, error, loading.\n- Key screen states with fixed fake data: empty, loaded, error, long text, many items.\n- The same states in dark theme, the largest supported font scale and one RTL locale.\n- Not: every screen with live data, animations mid-flight, or content with timestamps you did not fix.\n\nKeep the count small enough that every diff gets reviewed. A diff nobody looks at is a test that cannot fail.\n\n## Pin the environment\n\n| Input | Android | iOS | Flutter |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Device size and density | `@Config(qualifiers = ...)` (Roborazzi), `DeviceConfig` (Paparazzi) | `.image(on:)` or `layout: .device(config:)` | `tester.view.physicalSize`, `devicePixelRatio` |\n| Locale | Robolectric qualifiers, `DeviceConfig(locale = ...)` | Scheme or test plan language, or inject the locale | `MaterialApp(locale: ...)` |\n| Theme and font scale | `+night` qualifier, `DeviceConfig.copy(nightMode, fontScale)` | `traits:` with a `UITraitCollection` | `MediaQuery` or theme in the test tree |\n| Fonts | Bundled app fonts render on the JVM | Bundled fonts | Load with `FontLoader`; the default test font is Ahem (boxes) |\n| Host | Record and verify on the same CI image | Same simulator model and OS version | Same OS (usually the Linux CI image) |\n\nPaparazzi's `DeviceConfig.locale` defaults to a value detected from the environment. Set it explicitly.\n\n## Android: Roborazzi (Robolectric)\n\nRuns on the JVM as a local test, so it also captures screens after interactions.\n\n```kotlin\n// module build.gradle.kts\nplugins { id(\"io.github.takahirom.roborazzi\") }\n\nandroid {\n    testOptions { unitTests { isIncludeAndroidResources = true } }\n}\n\nroborazzi {\n    outputDir.set(file(\"src/screenshots\")) // default is build/outputs/roborazzi, which is not committed\n}\n\ndependencies {\n    testImplementation(libs.roborazzi)\n    testImplementation(libs.roborazzi.compose)\n    testImplementation(libs.roborazzi.junit.rule)\n    testImplementation(libs.robolectric)\n}\n```\n\n```kotlin\n@RunWith(AndroidJUnit4::class)\n@GraphicsMode(GraphicsMode.Mode.NATIVE)\n@Config(qualifiers = RobolectricDeviceQualifiers.Pixel5)\nclass DeviceCardScreenshotTest {\n\n    @Test fun deviceCard() {\n        captureRoboImage { AppTheme { DeviceCard(sampleDevice) } }\n    }\n\n    @Config(qualifiers = \"+night\")\n    @Test fun deviceCardDark() {\n        captureRoboImage { AppTheme { DeviceCard(sampleDevice) } }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- After interactions in a Compose UI test: `composeRule.onNodeWithTag(\"device-card\").captureRoboImage()`.\n- Tasks: `recordRoborazziDebug`, `compareRoborazziDebug`, `verifyRoborazziDebug`,\n  `verifyAndRecordRoborazziDebug`. Equivalent properties on `testDebugUnitTest`:\n  `-Proborazzi.test.record=true`, `-Proborazzi.test.compare=true`, `-Proborazzi.test.verify=true`.\n- A small tolerance is set through `RoborazziOptions(compareOptions = RoborazziOptions.CompareOptions(changeThreshold = 0.01F))`.\n  Keep it near zero; a large threshold hides real regressions.\n- The README states there is no guarantee of identical rendering across environments and recommends\n  recording and verifying on CI. It also needs Robolectric native graphics on API 28 or higher, and\n  documents the Robolectric `robolectric.pixelCopyRenderMode` option (`hardware`) for more accurate\n  captures; check the README for how to set it for your version.\n\n## Android: Paparazzi\n\nRenders composables and views without a device, emulator or Robolectric.\n\n```kotlin\nplugins { id(\"app.cash.paparazzi\") }\n```\n\n```kotlin\nimport com.android.resources.NightMode\n\nclass DeviceCardPaparazziTest {\n    @get:Rule val paparazzi = Paparazzi(\n        deviceConfig = DeviceConfig.PIXEL_5.copy(locale = \"en\", nightMode = NightMode.NIGHT, fontScale = 1.5f),\n        theme = \"android:Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar\",\n    )\n\n    @Test fun deviceCard() {\n        paparazzi.snapshot { AppTheme { DeviceCard(sampleDevice) } }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Tasks: `recordPaparazziDebug` writes references to `src/test/snapshots`; `verifyPaparazziDebug` compares\n  and writes diffs to `build/paparazzi/failures`; `testDebug` produces an HTML report under\n  `build/reports/paparazzi/`.\n- The project recommends Git LFS for snapshots: `git lfs track \"**/snapshots/**/*.png\"`.\n- Recent releases are 2.0 alphas; check the changelog before choosing a version.\n\n## Android: Compose Preview Screenshot Testing\n\nGoogle's tool turns `@Preview` functions annotated with `@PreviewTest` in a `screenshotTest` source set into\nscreenshot tests (`updateDebugScreenshotTest`, `validateDebugScreenshotTest`). It is alpha and experimental,\nneeds `android.experimental.enableScreenshotTest=true`, and newer AGP versions recommend configuring it\nthrough AGP test suites. Use it only if the team accepts alpha tooling; re-check the official page first.\n\n## iOS: swift-snapshot-testing\n\nAdd the package `https://github.com/pointfreeco/swift-snapshot-testing` to the test target only (product\n`SnapshotTesting`).\n\n```swift\nimport SnapshotTesting\nimport SwiftUI\nimport Testing\n@testable import DeviceFeature\n\n@MainActor\n@Suite(.snapshots(record: .missing))\nstruct DeviceCardSnapshotTests {\n    @Test func light() {\n        assertSnapshot(of: DeviceCard(device: .sample), as: .image(layout: .device(config: .iPhone13)))\n    }\n\n    @Test func dark() {\n        assertSnapshot(\n            of: DeviceCard(device: .sample),\n            as: .image(layout: .device(config: .iPhone13), traits: UITraitCollection(userInterfaceStyle: .dark))\n        )\n    }\n\n    @Test func accessibilityText() {\n        assertSnapshot(\n            of: DeviceCard(device: .sample),\n            as: .image(layout: .sizeThatFits, traits: UITraitCollection(preferredContentSizeCategory: .accessibilityExtraLarge))\n        )\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- UIKit: `assertSnapshot(of: viewController, as: .image(on: .iPhoneSe))`, also `.iPhoneSe(.landscape)`.\n- SwiftUI layouts: `.device(config:)`, `.fixed(width:height:)`, `.sizeThatFits` (default).\n- `precision` and `perceptualPrecision` default to 1. A `perceptualPrecision` slightly below 1 absorbs\n  anti-aliasing noise; never lower `precision` to make layout changes pass.\n- Record modes: `.all`, `.failed`, `.missing` (default: records missing references and fails), `.never`\n  (missing reference is a failure; the documented choice for CI). Set per assertion (`record:`), per scope\n  (`withSnapshotTesting(record:)`), per suite (`.snapshots(record:)`), or with the environment variable\n  `SNAPSHOT_TESTING_RECORD`. From `xcodebuild`, prefix it: `TEST_RUNNER_SNAPSHOT_TESTING_RECORD=never`.\n- References live in `__Snapshots__` next to the test file. Failure images go to the directory in\n  `SNAPSHOT_ARTIFACTS`, or the temporary directory; upload that directory from CI.\n- The README warns that snapshots must be compared on the exact simulator that recorded them. Pin the\n  simulator model and OS in the CI destination and in the team's recording instructions.\n\n## Flutter: golden files\n\n```dart\ntestWidgets('device card, dark, large text', (tester) async {\n  tester.view.physicalSize = const Size(1080, 2400);\n  tester.view.devicePixelRatio = 3.0;\n  addTearDown(tester.view.reset);\n\n  await tester.pumpWidget(MaterialApp(\n    theme: AppTheme.dark(),\n    home: MediaQuery(\n      data: const MediaQueryData(textScaler: TextScaler.linear(1.5)),\n      child: Scaffold(body: DeviceCard(device: sampleDevice)),\n    ),\n  ));\n\n  await expectLater(find.byType(DeviceCard), matchesGoldenFile('goldens/device_card_dark_large.png'));\n}, tags: 'golden');\n```\n\nLoad real fonts once for the whole directory tree. The test framework looks upward from each test file for\n`flutter_test_config.dart` and runs `testExecutable` with the test's `main`:\n\n```dart\n// test/flutter_test_config.dart\nimport 'dart:async';\n\nimport 'package:flutter/services.dart';\nimport 'package:flutter_test/flutter_test.dart';\n\nFuture<void> testExecutable(FutureOr<void> Function() testMain) async {\n  TestWidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();\n  final roboto = FontLoader('Roboto')..addFont(rootBundle.load('assets/fonts/Roboto-Regular.ttf'));\n  await roboto.load();\n  await testMain();\n}\n```\n\n- The family name passed to `FontLoader` must match the `fontFamily` the theme uses.\n- Update references with `flutter test --update-goldens`, only in the recording job or locally on the\n  pinned platform. The docs note custom fonts can render differently across platforms and Flutter versions,\n  so re-record after a Flutter upgrade in a dedicated merge request.\n- Declare the tag in `dart_test.yaml` (`tags: { golden: {} }`) and run goldens only on the pinned\n  platform: `flutter test --tags golden` there, `--exclude-tags golden` elsewhere.\n- `goldenFileComparator` can be replaced in `flutter_test_config.dart` (for example to add a tolerance).\n  Keep any tolerance near zero.\n\n## Reviewing diffs\n\n1. CI runs verify only and uploads the diff images as artifacts on failure.\n2. An intended UI change re-records in the same merge request, on the pinned image, through a labelled or\n   manual job, and commits the new references.\n3. The reviewer opens every changed reference. Unexplained changes block the merge.\n4. Re-record everything only in a dedicated merge request (tool upgrade, OS image change, font change).\n",
    "references/ui-tests.md":
        "# UI and end-to-end tests\n\nLoad this when writing screen tests or journey tests: Compose UI tests, XCUITest and Flutter\n`integration_test`. For Espresso and `IdlingResource` in Java or View-based code, see `mobile-java`,\nreferences/testing.md. For widget-test basics (`pump`, finders), see `mobile-flutter`, references/testing.md.\n\n## Choosing the runtime\n\n| Need | Android | iOS | Flutter |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Screen behavior, fast, every PR | Compose UI test on Robolectric (`src/test`) | Model test plus snapshot; XCUITest only if the screen logic is in the view | `testWidgets` |\n| Real rendering, IME, permissions, process death | Compose UI test on emulator (`src/androidTest`) | XCUITest on simulator | `integration_test` on emulator or simulator |\n| Journey across screens | Instrumented test on a Gradle managed device | XCUITest with launch flags | `integration_test` |\n\n## Compose UI tests\n\nDependencies (versions in `libs.versions.toml`):\n\n```kotlin\nandroidTestImplementation(libs.androidx.compose.ui.test.junit4)\ndebugImplementation(libs.androidx.compose.ui.test.manifest) // needed for createComposeRule()\n```\n\nUse the v2 rule factories. The v1 factories are deprecated; v2 runs composition on a\n`StandardTestDispatcher`, so launched coroutines are queued until the test clock advances.\n\n| Deprecated (v1) | Use (v2) |\n|---|---|\n| `androidx.compose.ui.test.junit4.createComposeRule` | `androidx.compose.ui.test.junit4.v2.createComposeRule` |\n| `androidx.compose.ui.test.junit4.createAndroidComposeRule` | `androidx.compose.ui.test.junit4.v2.createAndroidComposeRule` |\n| `androidx.compose.ui.test.runComposeUiTest` | `androidx.compose.ui.test.v2.runComposeUiTest` |\n\n```kotlin\nimport androidx.compose.ui.test.junit4.v2.createAndroidComposeRule\n\nclass PairingScreenTest {\n    // Empty host activity: setContent is allowed and string resources are reachable\n    @get:Rule val composeRule = createAndroidComposeRule<ComponentActivity>()\n\n    @Test fun showsErrorWhenPairingFails() {\n        composeRule.setContent {\n            AppTheme { PairingScreen(state = PairingUiState(error = PairingError.Timeout), onRetry = {}) }\n        }\n        val retry = composeRule.activity.getString(R.string.retry)\n        composeRule.onNodeWithText(retry).assertIsDisplayed().assertHasClickAction()\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Migrating to v2: a test that asserted state right after triggering work may now see the old state. Call\n  `composeRule.waitForIdle()` or wrap the assertion in `runOnIdle { }`.\n- Tests that combine a compose rule with `runTest` should move to `runComposeUiTest`, which runs the block in\n  its own `runTest` scope with a shared clock. Check whether your version still needs an opt-in annotation.\n- Hilt: `@HiltAndroidTest`, `HiltAndroidRule` ordered before the compose rule, production modules replaced\n  with `@TestInstallIn`. See `mobile-kotlin`, references/testing.md.\n\n### Synchronization\n\n```kotlin\n// Animations and time-based UI: drive the clock yourself\ncomposeRule.mainClock.autoAdvance = false\ncomposeRule.onNodeWithText(\"Scan\").performClick()\ncomposeRule.mainClock.advanceTimeBy(300)\ncomposeRule.onNodeWithTag(\"scan-progress\").assertIsDisplayed()\n\n// Work outside Compose (data loading, a fake transport callback)\ncomposeRule.waitUntilAtLeastOneExists(hasText(\"Gateway A\"), timeoutMillis = 5_000)\ncomposeRule.waitUntil(timeoutMillis = 5_000) { fakeTransport.connected }\n```\n\n- Also `waitUntilDoesNotExist`, `waitUntilExactlyOneExists`, `waitUntilNodeCount`.\n- Background work the rule cannot see: implement an `IdlingResource` and register it with\n  `composeRule.registerIdlingResource(...)`; unregister in teardown.\n- The docs warn that external latches (`CountDownLatch`) instead of `waitUntil` can misbehave because the\n  test clock does not advance.\n- Merged semantics: a `Row` with `Modifier.semantics(mergeDescendants = true)` hides child nodes; pass\n  `useUnmergedTree = true` to the finder when asserting on a child.\n\n### Permissions on a device\n\n```kotlin\n@get:Rule val permissions: GrantPermissionRule =\n    GrantPermissionRule.grant(Manifest.permission.BLUETOOTH_SCAN, Manifest.permission.BLUETOOTH_CONNECT)\n```\n\n- Grant only what the test needs. Test the denied path separately, at screen level, with a fake permission\n  checker rather than the system dialog.\n- Clear app state between tests with Android Test Orchestrator and `clearPackageData` when tests share\n  storage (see [ci-and-flakes.md](ci-and-flakes.md)).\n\n## XCUITest\n\nXCUITest is black-box: the test process drives the app through accessibility. Swift Testing does not run UI\ntests; UI test targets use `XCTestCase`.\n\n```swift\nfinal class PairingUITests: XCTestCase {\n    private var app: XCUIApplication!\n\n    override func setUp() {\n        continueAfterFailure = false\n        app = XCUIApplication()\n        app.launchArguments = [\"-ui-testing\", \"-fake-ble\"]\n        app.launchEnvironment = [\"FIXTURE\": \"one-gateway\"]\n        app.launch()\n    }\n\n    func testPairingHappyPath() {\n        let screen = PairingScreen(app: app)\n        screen.pairButton.tap()\n        XCTAssertTrue(screen.status.waitForExistence(timeout: 5))\n        XCTAssertEqual(screen.status.label, \"Paired\")\n    }\n}\n\n// Screen object: one place to update when identifiers change\nstruct PairingScreen {\n    let app: XCUIApplication\n    var pairButton: XCUIElement { app.buttons[\"pair-device\"] }\n    var status: XCUIElement { app.staticTexts[\"paired-status\"] }\n}\n```\n\nApp side:\n\n```swift\n#if DEBUG\nlet usesFakeBLE = ProcessInfo.processInfo.arguments.contains(\"-fake-ble\")\n#else\nlet usesFakeBLE = false\n#endif\n```\n\n- Compile test hooks into debug builds only. A launch argument that swaps transports or skips login in a\n  release build is a security hole.\n- Query by `accessibilityIdentifier`, which is not localized and not read by VoiceOver.\n- `waitForExistence(timeout:)` and, in current SDKs, `waitForNonExistence(timeout:)` for disappearing\n  spinners. No `sleep`.\n- System permission alerts: prefer avoiding them with fakes. When the alert is the subject, handle it\n  with `addUIInterruptionMonitor(withDescription:handler:)`, or reset state first with\n  `app.resetAuthorizationStatus(for:)`. On a simulator, `xcrun simctl privacy <device> grant location\n  <bundle id>` pre-grants supported services (Bluetooth is not one of them; the simulator has no BLE radio,\n  so use a fake transport).\n- iOS 17 and later: `try app.performAccessibilityAudit()` in one smoke test per main screen catches missing\n  labels and small hit targets.\n- Run locales and configurations from a test plan (one configuration per language or region) instead of\n  duplicating tests.\n\n## Flutter integration_test\n\n```yaml\n# pubspec.yaml\ndev_dependencies:\n  flutter_test:\n    sdk: flutter\n  integration_test:\n    sdk: flutter\n```\n\n```dart\n// integration_test/pairing_test.dart\nimport 'package:flutter_test/flutter_test.dart';\nimport 'package:integration_test/integration_test.dart';\nimport 'package:my_app/main_test.dart' as app; // test entry point wired to fakes\n\nvoid main() {\n  IntegrationTestWidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();\n\n  testWidgets('user pairs a gateway', (tester) async {\n    app.main();\n    await tester.pumpAndSettle();\n\n    await tester.tap(find.text('Add device'));\n    await tester.pumpAndSettle();\n    await tester.tap(find.text('Gateway A'));\n\n    await tester.pump(const Duration(seconds: 1));\n    expect(find.text('Paired'), findsOneWidget);\n  });\n}\n```\n\n- Run all: `flutter test integration_test`; one file on one device:\n  `flutter test integration_test/pairing_test.dart -d <device-id>`.\n- A separate entry point (`lib/main_test.dart`) or `--dart-define-from-file` selects fakes or a test\n  backend. Never production.\n- `pumpAndSettle` throws if an endless animation is running (indeterminate spinners). Use `pump(duration)`\n  around such screens.\n- `--concurrency` is ignored for integration tests; split files across CI jobs instead.\n- Native dialogs (permissions, system pickers) are outside Flutter. Pre-grant on the device\n  (`adb shell pm grant <package> <permission>`, `xcrun simctl privacy ...`) or fake the permission plugin\n  behind your own interface.\n- Device farms on Android: build `flutter build apk --debug`, then in `android/`\n  `./gradlew app:assembleAndroidTest` and `./gradlew app:assembleDebug -Ptarget=integration_test/<name>_test.dart`,\n  as described in the `integration_test` package README.\n\n## What a journey test asserts\n\n- The user-visible outcome (status text, navigation to the next screen, item in a list), not internal state.\n- One journey per test. Arrange the starting state through launch flags or fixtures, not by tapping through\n  onboarding in every test.\n- Failure artifacts: screenshot on failure, logs, and the result bundle or test report uploaded by CI.\n",
  },
};

/// Skill name -> description from the SKILL.md frontmatter.
const Map<String, String> embeddedSkillDescriptions = {
  "mobile-accessibility":
      "Audits and fixes accessibility in Android, iOS and Flutter apps against WCAG 2.2 level AA and EN 301 549, and checks whether the European Accessibility Act applies. Covers Compose semantics, TalkBack, SwiftUI modifiers, VoiceOver, Dynamic Type, Flutter Semantics, touch targets, contrast, font scaling and automated checks. Engineering guidance, not legal advice. Use for accessibility reviews, EAA readiness, screen reader bugs or a11y tests.",
  "mobile-ble":
      "Designs, implements and reviews Bluetooth Low Energy code in Android, iOS and Flutter apps: Android 12+ permissions, filtered scans, GATT operation queues, MTU, bonding, status 133, CoreBluetooth threading and state restoration, flutter_blue_plus, framing, CRC, safe firmware updates (DFU) and connection state machines. Use when writing or reviewing BLE scan, connect, read, write or notify code, reconnect logic or device protocols.",
  "mobile-ci-cd":
      "Writes and hardens CI/CD pipelines for Android, iOS and Flutter on GitHub Actions and GitLab CI: Gradle and xcodebuild builds, lint and tests, signing from secrets, fastlane, Play and TestFlight upload, Firebase App Distribution, caching and SHA-pinned actions. Use when setting up, fixing, speeding up or reviewing a mobile pipeline, workflow YAML, .gitlab-ci.yml or Fastfile.",
  "mobile-code-review":
      "Reviews Kotlin, Java, Swift and Dart code and diffs in Android, iOS and Flutter apps for correctness, concurrency, lifecycle leaks, UI performance, architecture, error handling, tests, accessibility and i18n. Reports Blocker, Major, Minor and Nit findings with file:line and fix snippets, then an Approve or Request changes verdict. Use when reviewing a pull request, merge request, diff, branch or file before merging mobile code.",
  "mobile-design-patterns":
      "Chooses and implements design patterns in Kotlin, Java, Swift and Dart mobile code: Repository, Factory, Strategy, Observer, Adapter, Decorator, Facade, State machine, Command, Coordinator, Result types and Delegation, with a selection table and over-engineering warnings. Use when picking a pattern, refactoring god classes or flag-driven state, wrapping an SDK, or when a design feels over-abstracted.",
  "mobile-eu-compliance":
      "Assesses Android, iOS, Flutter and connected-device companion apps against the EU Cyber Resilience Act, the Radio Equipment Directive delegated act with EN 18031-1, -2 and -3, and GDPR with ePrivacy: inventory, requirement mapping, evidence, gaps and a remediation plan in a gap report table. Engineering guidance, not legal advice. Use for CRA or EN 18031 readiness, SBOM and vulnerability handling, BLE or Wi-Fi companion apps, or GDPR reviews of mobile apps.",
  "mobile-flutter":
      "Writes idiomatic modern Dart 3 and Flutter: records, patterns and sealed classes, async and Streams, Isolate.run, small const widgets, keys, context.mounted after await, disposing controllers, Riverpod or Bloc, go_router, Pigeon platform channels, flavors, lints and unit, widget, golden and integration tests. Use when writing, refactoring or reviewing Dart or Flutter code, screens, providers, blocs, routes or tests.",
  "mobile-java":
      "Writes, reviews and modernizes Java in Android apps: Java 17 and 21 features that D8 supports, immutability, JSpecify nullability for Kotlin interop, leak-free threading without static Context or inner-class Handlers, ExecutorService, LiveData, ViewBinding, ListAdapter and DiffUtil, tests with JUnit, Mockito and Espresso, and incremental migration to Kotlin. Use when editing, reviewing or migrating .java files in an Android project.",
  "mobile-kmp":
      "Builds and reviews Kotlin Multiplatform and Compose Multiplatform code for Android and iOS: source sets and the default hierarchy template, interfaces with DI over expect/actual, shared AndroidX ViewModels, Ktor, Room KMP or SQLDelight, kotlinx.serialization, Flow and suspend from Swift via SKIE, Objective-C and Swift export limits, XCFramework and SPM publishing, commonTest. Use when setting up, migrating to or reviewing a KMP shared module.",
  "mobile-kotlin":
      "Writes idiomatic modern Kotlin for Android: Kotlin 2.x, null safety, sealed and value classes, structured coroutines with injected dispatchers, Flow and StateFlow, Jetpack Compose state and stability, Hilt, Room, KSP, Gradle version catalogs, R8 and tests with MockK and Turbine, plus Android 15 and 16 changes. Use when writing or refactoring Kotlin ViewModels, composables, repositories, DAOs, DI modules or build.gradle.kts files.",
  "mobile-merge-request":
      "Guides the merge request and pull request workflow for mobile teams: branch naming, Conventional Commits, small and stacked MRs, self-review, rebase vs merge, safe conflict resolution for lockfiles, project.pbxproj and version catalogs, MR descriptions with screenshots and rollback, and reviewer etiquette. Use when creating branches, writing commits or MR descriptions, rebasing, resolving conflicts or answering review comments.",
  "mobile-mvc":
      "Fixes controller-based mobile code: UIKit MVC and Massive View Controllers (child view controllers, coordinators, separate data sources), legacy Android Activity-as-controller and MVP, and Flutter controllers, plus step-by-step migration to MVVM one screen at a time. Use when working on UIViewController, Activity, Fragment or presenter code, splitting a god class, or deciding whether MVC is still fine.",
  "mobile-mvi":
